{"record_id":"D36-B001","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Mutable Default Argument","concept":"State leakage through mutable default argument","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a Python function unexpectedly retain values between calls when a list is used as a default argument?","answer":"Because default argument expressions are evaluated when the function is defined, not each time it is called. A mutable default list is therefore shared across calls.","explanation":"The observed accumulation of values across independent calls is explained by the function's single shared default list. The key diagnostic evidence is that the list changes after one invocation and retains that state during subsequent invocations without the caller supplying the list.","principle":"Mutable objects used as default arguments can unintentionally create persistent function-level state.","objective":"Diagnose state leakage caused by a mutable Python default argument.","requirements":["Each call should receive an independent collection unless shared state is explicitly intended."],"constraints":["Preserve normal function-call semantics."],"preconditions":["Python function uses a mutable default value."],"input":"add_item(\"a\"); add_item(\"b\")","output":"Observed output may be [\"a\",\"b\"] across separate calls when an independent list was expected.","expected_behavior":"Each invocation should start with a fresh collection when no collection argument is supplied.","incorrect_code":"def add_item(item, items=[]):\n    items.append(item)\n    return items","corrected_code":"def add_item(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items","failure_mode":"Unexpected state persistence across function calls.","expected_error":"","cause":"The default list object is created once at function definition time and reused.","root_cause":"A mutable object is used as the function's default argument, creating shared state between calls.","solution":"Use None as the default and allocate a new list inside the function, or explicitly pass an owned collection.","edge_case":"An explicitly supplied list should still be mutated if that behavior is part of the function contract.","security_implication":"Unexpected shared state can become a security concern when the retained data contains request-specific or user-specific information.","performance_implication":"Creating a small list per call has allocation cost but avoids unintended shared state.","test_case":"Call add_item twice without supplying items and verify that each independent call starts with an empty collection.","test_input":"add_item(\"a\"); add_item(\"b\")","expected_test_result":"The first result contains only \"a\" and the second contains only \"b\".","testing_guidance":"Test both omitted and explicitly supplied collection arguments.","verification_method":"Static inspection of default argument semantics plus runtime behavior test.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the default object identity is not shared across independent calls.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.x interpreter"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_BEHAVIOR","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B001-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python language documentation on function definition and default parameter evaluation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B002","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Async Error Handling","concept":"Promise rejection escaping synchronous try/catch","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does a synchronous try/catch fail to catch an error produced by an awaited operation when the await is omitted?","answer":"Because the asynchronous operation returns a Promise whose rejection occurs outside the synchronous execution path. A try/catch surrounding the call does not catch a later Promise rejection unless the Promise is awaited inside the try block or explicitly handled.","explanation":"The diagnostic distinction is between synchronous exceptions and asynchronous Promise rejection. If the function returns a rejected Promise and the caller does not await it within the try block, the catch handler is not observing the rejection.","principle":"Promise rejection must be observed through await or Promise rejection handling.","objective":"Diagnose an asynchronous JavaScript error that bypasses a synchronous catch block.","requirements":["The caller must observe and handle the rejected Promise."],"constraints":["Preserve asynchronous execution."],"preconditions":["An asynchronous function can reject."],"input":"try { doWork(); } catch (error) { handle(error); }","output":"The catch block may not execute when doWork() returns a rejected Promise.","expected_behavior":"The rejection should reach the intended error handler.","incorrect_code":"async function caller() {\n    try {\n        doWork();\n    } catch (error) {\n        handle(error);\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"async function caller() {\n    try {\n        await doWork();\n    } catch (error) {\n        handle(error);\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Unhandled Promise rejection or missed error handling.","expected_error":"Promise rejection rather than a synchronous throw reaching the catch block.","cause":"The asynchronous operation was started without awaiting or attaching a rejection handler.","root_cause":"The error-handling boundary does not encompass the asynchronous Promise rejection.","solution":"Await the Promise within the try block or attach explicit rejection handling.","edge_case":"Fire-and-forget work intentionally detached from the caller requires its own rejection handling strategy.","security_implication":"Missed failures can leave authorization, cleanup, or transaction operations in an unexpected state.","performance_implication":"Awaiting introduces sequencing where required; unnecessary serialization should be avoided when independent operations can run concurrently with Promise.all and explicit error handling.","test_case":"Use a function that returns Promise.reject and verify that the corrected caller invokes the error handler.","test_input":"doWork = async () => { throw new Error(\"failure\"); }","expected_test_result":"The error handler is invoked exactly once.","testing_guidance":"Test both rejection and successful resolution paths.","verification_method":"Promise rejection test and control-flow inspection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the rejected Promise is observed by the intended handler.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2017+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ES2017+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime supporting async/await"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_RUNTIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B002-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ECMAScript Promise and async function semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B003","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Nullability","concept":"Unchecked nullable value access","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a TypeScript runtime failure caused by accessing a property on an absent value be diagnosed?","answer":"The evidence should first establish whether the value can actually be absent at runtime. If the type permits undefined or null, blindly asserting its presence hides the actual precondition failure. The root cause is an unmet existence precondition rather than the property access itself.","explanation":"A debugger showing an absent object at the failing property access is evidence of a missing-value condition. Candidate causes include an incorrect lookup key, missing initialization, an empty result, or a violated API contract. The diagnostic process should distinguish these causes rather than simply adding a non-null assertion.","principle":"Runtime invariants must be established before dereferencing potentially absent values.","objective":"Trace an undefined-value property access to the violated existence invariant.","requirements":["The object must exist before property access."],"constraints":["Do not suppress the type checker solely to hide the condition."],"preconditions":["Lookup may return undefined."],"input":"const user = users.get(id); return user.name;","output":"Runtime failure when user is absent.","expected_behavior":"Absent data should produce an explicit controlled outcome.","incorrect_code":"const user = users.get(id)!;\nreturn user.name;","corrected_code":"const user = users.get(id);\nif (user === undefined) {\n    throw new Error(\"User not found\");\n}\nreturn user.name;","failure_mode":"Runtime dereference of an absent value.","expected_error":"TypeError or equivalent runtime property-access failure when the value is undefined.","cause":"The implementation assumes successful lookup without establishing the required invariant.","root_cause":"The code performs a property access without validating the lookup result.","solution":"Validate the lookup result and define the absent-value behavior explicitly.","edge_case":"An empty or deleted record must be distinguished from a valid record containing empty fields.","security_implication":"Missing authorization or identity objects must not silently become trusted defaults.","performance_implication":"A direct existence check is normally cheaper than recovering from an exception.","test_case":"Request a nonexistent identifier and verify controlled failure.","test_input":"id = \"missing-id\"","expected_test_result":"The function follows the defined not-found behavior instead of dereferencing undefined.","testing_guidance":"Test existing, missing, and malformed identifiers separately.","verification_method":"Static strict-null analysis plus runtime negative test.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that all dereference paths establish the required invariant.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"4.x+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_AND_TYPE_SYSTEM_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B003-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["TypeScript strict null checking documentation and ECMAScript runtime property access behavior"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B004","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Null Reference","concept":"Null dereference caused by missing invariant validation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a Java NullPointerException be distinguished from the deeper cause that produced the null value?","answer":"The dereference is the immediate failure point, but the root cause is often earlier: a lookup returned null, initialization was skipped, or a contract allowed absence without handling it. Diagnosis must trace the value backward to the point where the null became possible.","explanation":"The stack trace identifies the dereference location but does not necessarily identify why the reference was null. Candidate causes should be traced through the value's producer and control-flow paths.","principle":"The failure location is not necessarily the root-cause location.","objective":"Trace a Java null dereference backward to the producer of the null value.","requirements":["Required object must exist before use, or absence must be explicitly handled."],"constraints":["Preserve defined not-found semantics."],"preconditions":["A method may return null."],"input":"User user = repository.find(id); return user.getName();","output":"NullPointerException when repository.find returns null.","expected_behavior":"The absent-user condition is handled according to the application contract.","incorrect_code":"User user = repository.find(id);\nreturn user.getName();","corrected_code":"User user = repository.find(id);\nif (user == null) {\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"User not found: \" + id);\n}\nreturn user.getName();","failure_mode":"NullPointerException.","expected_error":"java.lang.NullPointerException when user is null.","cause":"repository.find returned null and the caller dereferenced the result without checking it.","root_cause":"The caller relied on an unstated non-null invariant that the repository contract did not guarantee.","solution":"Define the repository contract explicitly and handle absence before dereference.","edge_case":"If absence is normal business behavior, Optional or an equivalent explicit result contract may be more appropriate than exceptions.","security_implication":"Identity or authorization objects must not be treated as present when lookup fails.","performance_implication":"Explicit null handling avoids exception-driven normal control flow.","test_case":"Mock the repository to return null and verify the specified absent-result behavior.","test_input":"repository.find(\"missing\") -> null","expected_test_result":"The caller handles absence according to its contract and does not perform a null dereference.","testing_guidance":"Test both successful lookup and absent lookup.","verification_method":"Stack-trace inspection plus producer/consumer data-flow analysis and negative test.","verification_guidance":"Verify the producer contract rather than treating the stack-trace line as sufficient root-cause evidence.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 8+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Java runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_RUNTIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B004-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Java Language Specification and Java API/runtime behavior regarding null references"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B005","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Use-After-Free","concept":"Heap lifetime violation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a C use-after-free failure be diagnosed when the program sometimes appears to work?","answer":"The key evidence is that memory is accessed after its lifetime has ended. Apparent success is not evidence of correctness because the allocator may leave old bytes unchanged temporarily. The root cause is an ownership/lifetime violation, not merely corrupted data.","explanation":"A sanitizer report, allocation/free trace, or debugger observation can establish that the pointer outlives the allocation. Candidate causes include premature free, ownership confusion, double ownership, or stale aliases. The diagnosis should establish the lifetime graph before modifying the code.","principle":"An object must not be accessed after its storage lifetime ends.","objective":"Diagnose a C use-after-free through allocation lifetime evidence.","requirements":["Every dereference must occur while the referenced allocation remains alive."],"constraints":["Preserve ownership and cleanup responsibilities."],"preconditions":["A dynamically allocated object is referenced by multiple code paths."],"input":"int *p = malloc(sizeof(int)); *p = 42; free(p); return *p;","output":"Undefined behavior; observed output is not reliable evidence.","expected_behavior":"The object must be accessed only before free or transferred to a still-valid owner.","incorrect_code":"int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));\n*p = 42;\nfree(p);\nreturn *p;","corrected_code":"int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));\nif (p == NULL) return -1;\n*p = 42;\nint result = *p;\nfree(p);\nreturn result;","failure_mode":"Use-after-free / undefined behavior.","expected_error":"No deterministic language-level error is guaranteed; sanitizer tooling may report heap-use-after-free.","cause":"The pointer is dereferenced after the allocation has been released.","root_cause":"The implementation violates the allocation lifetime invariant.","solution":"Access the value before release or transfer ownership so that the consuming code controls the valid lifetime.","edge_case":"A pointer becoming NULL after free does not by itself repair other aliases to the released allocation.","security_implication":"Use-after-free can become memory corruption or a security vulnerability depending on program context.","performance_implication":"Correct lifetime management generally avoids repeated allocation work caused by defensive leaks or crashes.","test_case":"Run the failing implementation under an address-sanitizing runtime/toolchain where available.","test_input":"Single allocation followed by free and dereference.","expected_test_result":"The invalid implementation should be detected by the applicable memory-safety tooling.","testing_guidance":"Use sanitizers and ownership/lifetime analysis where available.","verification_method":"Memory-safety instrumentation plus source-level lifetime analysis.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer correctness from one successful native execution.","language":"C","language_version":"C11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C11+","dependencies":["C standard allocation APIs"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler","Runtime memory-safety instrumentation where available"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"C_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B006"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B005-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO C language rules concerning object lifetime and dynamic allocation; sanitizer documentation for applicable implementations"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B006","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Iterator Invalidation","concept":"Invalidated container iterator","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can inserting into a C++ vector make a previously obtained iterator invalid?","answer":"Operations that reallocate the vector's storage invalidate iterators and references into the old storage. The root cause is using an iterator whose validity guarantee was lost after a modifying operation.","explanation":"The diagnostic process must establish whether the operation can reallocate storage. A crash may occur immediately, later, or not at all because using an invalidated iterator is undefined behavior.","principle":"Iterator validity is part of a container operation's semantic contract.","objective":"Diagnose iterator invalidation after vector modification.","requirements":["An iterator must remain valid for every operation that uses it."],"constraints":["Container may reallocate when its size changes."],"preconditions":["An iterator is retained across a potentially invalidating vector operation."],"input":"auto it = values.begin(); values.push_back(10); use(*it);","output":"Undefined behavior if the push causes iterator invalidation and the iterator is subsequently dereferenced.","expected_behavior":"Obtain a valid iterator after the modifying operation or use a container/operation with appropriate validity guarantees.","incorrect_code":"std::vector<int> values{1,2,3};\nauto it = values.begin();\nvalues.push_back(4);\nint x = *it;","corrected_code":"std::vector<int> values{1,2,3};\nvalues.push_back(4);\nauto it = values.begin();\nint x = *it;","failure_mode":"Invalid iterator use.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior; no guaranteed runtime exception.","cause":"The iterator was retained across a potentially invalidating vector modification.","root_cause":"The code violates the iterator-validity guarantee after a container operation.","solution":"Reacquire iterators after potentially invalidating operations or use an appropriate stable-reference data structure when required.","edge_case":"If sufficient capacity is already reserved, a particular operation may avoid reallocation, but code should rely only on documented iterator invalidation guarantees rather than accidental capacity.","security_implication":"Undefined behavior in native code can contribute to memory-safety vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Choosing a stable-reference container can change memory locality and performance; reserve can reduce reallocations but does not universally eliminate all invalidation rules.","test_case":"Construct a vector with constrained capacity, modify it, and use the stale iterator under suitable sanitization/debug tooling.","test_input":"Vector iterator retained across push_back.","expected_test_result":"The diagnostic environment should identify invalid iterator use where supported.","testing_guidance":"Use compiler/library debug iterators or sanitizers where supported, but also validate against the documented container invalidation rules.","verification_method":"C++ standard container invalidation rules plus diagnostic tooling.","verification_guidance":"Do not use absence of a crash as evidence that the iterator remains valid.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++17+","dependencies":["C++ standard library"],"execution_requirements":["C++ compiler and standard library"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"C++_STANDARD_LIBRARY_CONTRACT","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B005"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B006-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ standard library vector iterator invalidation requirements"],"create