{"record_id":"D36-B007","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Ownership","concept":"Attempted use after move","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"How should a Rust compiler error about using a moved value be diagnosed?","answer":"The compiler is identifying an ownership violation: ownership of the value was transferred by a move, so the original binding can no longer be used unless the type or operation provides an appropriate copy, clone, borrow, or other ownership strategy.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is the compiler's move analysis and the operation that consumed the value. Candidate fixes include borrowing instead of moving, cloning when semantically justified, or restructuring ownership. The correct fix depends on the ownership requirement rather than merely suppressing the error.","principle":"Ownership transfer changes which binding may legally use a value.","objective":"Trace a Rust moved-value compilation failure to an ownership-transfer operation.","requirements":["Each use must respect the value's ownership state."],"constraints":["Avoid unnecessary cloning when borrowing satisfies the requirement."],"preconditions":["The value's type or operation permits ownership transfer."],"input":"let name = String::from(\"Ada\"); consume(name); println!(\"{}\", name);","output":"Compilation failure because name was moved into consume.","expected_behavior":"The value should either remain borrowed or be deliberately transferred and no longer used by the original binding.","incorrect_code":"fn consume(value: String) {}\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ada\");\n    consume(name);\n    println!(\"{}\", name);\n}","corrected_code":"fn consume(value: &str) {}\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ada\");\n    consume(&name);\n    println!(\"{}\", name);\n}","failure_mode":"Compile-time ownership violation.","expected_error":"Use of moved value.","cause":"The String is moved into consume and therefore is no longer owned by name.","root_cause":"The function signature requires ownership when only a borrow was needed.","solution":"Change the API to borrow when ownership transfer is unnecessary, or intentionally transfer ownership and stop using the original binding.","edge_case":"Cloning may be correct when independent ownership is genuinely required, but it changes allocation and ownership semantics.","security_implication":"Rust's ownership model can prevent classes of memory-lifetime errors when its guarantees are respected.","performance_implication":"Borrowing can avoid allocation/copying; cloning can introduce additional memory and CPU cost.","test_case":"Compile both implementations and inspect ownership behavior.","test_input":"String passed to a function accepting String versus &str.","expected_test_result":"The borrowed implementation compiles and preserves later use of name.","testing_guidance":"Compiler diagnostics are primary evidence for this class of failure.","verification_method":"Rust compiler type/borrow checking.","verification_guidance":"Treat successful compilation as evidence for the checked ownership constraints, not proof of all application-level correctness.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.x","dependencies":["Rust standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RUST_COMPILER_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B005"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B007-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["The Rust Reference and The Rust Programming Language ownership and borrowing documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B008","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Interface Nil Semantics","concept":"Typed nil stored inside a non-nil interface","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a Go interface comparison against nil unexpectedly evaluate to false when the underlying pointer is nil?","answer":"An interface value contains both dynamic type and dynamic value. If the dynamic type is a pointer type and the dynamic value is nil, the interface itself can still be non-nil because its dynamic type is present.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is obtained by inspecting the interface's dynamic type and underlying value separately. The apparent contradiction comes from confusing a nil underlying pointer with a nil interface value.","principle":"In Go, interface nilness depends on both dynamic type and dynamic value.","objective":"Diagnose unexpected non-nil interface behavior caused by a typed nil.","requirements":["API contracts must define whether nil interfaces or typed nil values are permitted."],"constraints":["Preserve interface semantics rather than relying on intuitive pointer-only nil behavior."],"preconditions":["A typed nil pointer is assigned to an interface."],"input":"var p *MyType = nil; var x interface{} = p","output":"x != nil can evaluate true.","expected_behavior":"Code must explicitly handle the interface/value contract.","incorrect_code":"func valid(x interface{}) bool {\n    return x == nil\n}","corrected_code":"func valid(x interface{}) bool {\n    if x == nil {\n        return false\n    }\n    // Additional type-specific nil handling may be required by the API contract.\n    return true\n}","failure_mode":"Unexpected nil comparison result.","expected_error":"","cause":"The interface contains a dynamic pointer type even though its underlying pointer value is nil.","root_cause":"The implementation treats interface nilness as equivalent to underlying pointer nilness.","solution":"Define and enforce an explicit API contract for nil values and inspect the dynamic value when typed nils are meaningful.","edge_case":"Reflection-based nil checks must themselves be used carefully because not every reflected kind can be compared to nil.","security_implication":"Incorrect nil handling can bypass validation or produce unexpected authorization/resource behavior if nil carries security meaning.","performance_implication":"Reflection-based generic nil detection can add complexity and overhead and should not replace clear type contracts unnecessarily.","test_case":"Assign a nil pointer to an interface and test interface equality with nil.","test_input":"var p *MyType = nil; var x interface{} = p; x == nil","expected_test_result":"The comparison demonstrates the distinction between a nil interface and an interface containing a typed nil.","testing_guidance":"Test nil interface, typed nil pointer in interface, and non-nil pointer cases separately.","verification_method":"Go language specification semantics plus focused runtime test.","verification_guidance":"Inspect both dynamic type and dynamic value when diagnosing interface nil behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.x","dependencies":["Go standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"GO_LANGUAGE_SPECIFICATION","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B003","D36-B004"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D36-B008-T01"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Go language specification on interface values and nil"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B009","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Database Transaction Boundary","concept":"Partial state update caused by missing transaction boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a partially applied multi-step database update be diagnosed?","answer":"The diagnostic evidence is that some related writes commit while a later required write fails. Candidate causes include separate transactions, premature commits, autocommit behavior, or an incomplete rollback path. The root cause is failure to establish the required atomicity boundary around the complete operation.","explanation":"The correct diagnosis requires tracing transaction ownership across every database operation. A failure in the second operation is not sufficient by itself; the key evidence is that the first operation remains committed despite the overall business operation failing.","principle":"A business operation requiring atomic state transition must have a transaction boundary that encompasses all required writes.","objective":"Diagnose partial persistence caused by an incomplete transaction boundary.","requirements":["All writes belonging to the atomic business operation must commit together or roll back together."],"constraints":["Transaction behavior depends on the database driver and transaction configuration."],"preconditions":["Multiple persistent changes form one logical operation."],"input":"Update account balance; insert ledger record; ledger insertion fails.","output":"Account balance remains changed while ledger record is absent.","expected_behavior":"Both changes commit together or neither change persists.","incorrect_code":"update_balance(account_id, amount)\ncommit()\ninsert_ledger(account_id, amount)\ncommit()","corrected_code":"begin_transaction()\ntry:\n    update_balance(account_id, amount)\n    insert_ledger(account_id, amount)\n    commit()\nexcept Exception:\n    rollback()\n    raise","failure_mode":"Partial database state after operation failure.","expected_error":"The second operation fails while the first operation has already committed.","cause":"The transaction was committed before the logical operation was complete.","root_cause":"The transaction boundary does not match the atomic business invariant.","solution":"Place all required writes inside one transaction and roll back when the operation cannot complete.","edge_case":"External side effects such as email or network calls may not participate in the database transaction and require an explicit consistency strategy.","security_implication":"Partial authorization, billing, or identity state can create serious security or integrity problems.","performance_implication":"Long transactions can increase lock duration and contention; transaction scope should be atomic but not unnecessarily broad.","test_case":"Force the second write to fail and inspect persistent state after rollback.","test_input":"First write succeeds; second write throws an error.","expected_test_result":"No partial database state remains after rollback.","testing_guidance":"Use failure injection and inspect the database after both success and failure paths.","verification_method":"Transaction trace plus database state inspection after forced failure.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual persistence behavior rather than assuming a framework transaction annotation or helper always covers every operation.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Database-driver dependent","dependencies":["Database driver","Transactional database"],"execution_requirements":["Transactional database environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DATABASE_TRANSACTION_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B010"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"c