{"record_id":"D36-B013","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Numeric Semantics","concept":"Floating-point representation causing exact equality failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can JavaScript exact equality fail for decimal arithmetic that appears mathematically exact?","answer":"JavaScript Number uses IEEE 754 binary floating-point representation for ordinary numeric values. Many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly in binary, so arithmetic results may differ slightly from the mathematical decimal value.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a calculated value that differs from the expected decimal value by a small representation error. The root cause is using binary floating-point equality for a domain that requires exact decimal semantics.","principle":"Numeric representation determines which equality and arithmetic guarantees are valid.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect financial or threshold logic caused by floating-point equality assumptions.","requirements":["Numeric representation must match the domain's precision requirements."],"constraints":["Approximate comparison is unsuitable for domains requiring exact decimal arithmetic."],"preconditions":["Decimal quantities are represented using JavaScript Number and compared exactly."],"input":"0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3","output":"The exact comparison evaluates to false.","expected_behavior":"Exact decimal domains should use an appropriate decimal or integer representation.","incorrect_code":"if (price * taxRate === expectedTax) {\n    applyTax();\n}","corrected_code":"const priceCents = 1000;\nconst taxRateBasisPoints = 500;\nconst taxCents = Math.round(priceCents * taxRateBasisPoints / 10000);","failure_mode":"Incorrect equality or accumulated rounding behavior.","expected_error":"","cause":"Binary floating-point representation is treated as exact decimal arithmetic.","root_cause":"The numeric representation does not satisfy the domain's precision invariant.","solution":"Use integer minor units, a decimal arithmetic implementation, or an explicitly defined tolerance strategy where approximation is acceptable.","edge_case":"Tolerance-based comparison must account for scale and domain semantics; a fixed arbitrary epsilon is not universally correct.","security_implication":"Incorrect monetary or threshold calculations can affect authorization limits, billing, fraud controls, or transaction integrity.","performance_implication":"Decimal arithmetic or arbitrary-precision representations can cost more than native floating-point operations.","test_case":"Test decimal arithmetic around equality, rounding boundaries, and repeated accumulation.","test_input":"Values such as 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 and domain-specific monetary amounts.","expected_test_result":"The selected representation produces results consistent with the documented precision model.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary values and cumulative operations rather than testing one arithmetic expression only.","verification_method":"Numeric representation analysis plus domain-specific arithmetic tests.","verification_guidance":"Verify the required precision before choosing exact or approximate comparison semantics.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ECMAScript","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ECMAScript implementations using Number semantics","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ECMASCRIPT_AND_IEEE_754_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ECMAScript Number semantics and IEEE 754 floating-point representation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B014","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Runtime Boundaries","concept":"Unsafe type assertion on external data","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can TypeScript code compile successfully while failing on malformed JSON at runtime?","answer":"A TypeScript type assertion changes the compiler's interpretation of a value but does not validate or transform the runtime value. External data must be validated before stronger application invariants are assumed.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a runtime value whose actual structure differs from the asserted type. The root cause is confusing compile-time type information with runtime validation.","principle":"Static types do not automatically establish runtime invariants at external trust boundaries.","objective":"Diagnose runtime failures caused by treating unvalidated external data as a trusted TypeScript type.","requirements":["External data must be validated before entering a trusted domain model."],"constraints":["Validation must cover all invariants required by downstream code."],"preconditions":["Data originates from JSON, a network response, database, JavaScript, or another dynamically typed source."],"input":"const user = JSON.parse(payload) as User;","output":"Malformed data can pass compilation and fail during property access.","expected_behavior":"Malformed external data is rejected at the boundary.","incorrect_code":"const user = JSON.parse(payload) as User;\nreturn user.profile.name;","corrected_code":"const value: unknown = JSON.parse(payload);\nif (!isUser(value)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid user payload\");\n}\nreturn value.profile.name;","failure_mode":"Runtime failure despite successful static compilation.","expected_error":"Runtime TypeError or domain validation failure depending on the malformed value.","cause":"A type assertion was incorrectly treated as a runtime validator.","root_cause":"The trust boundary establishes no runtime evidence for the asserted type.","solution":"Parse into unknown data, validate required invariants, and only then expose a trusted domain type.","edge_case":"A validator that checks only top-level properties may still permit invalid nested values.","security_implication":"Unvalidated external data can influence security-sensitive decisions under false type assumptions.","performance_implication":"Boundary validation adds processing cost but prevents downstream failures and invalid-state propagation.","test_case":"Supply valid JSON and malformed JSON with missing, null, and incorrectly typed nested properties.","test_input":"Multiple payload shapes.","expected_test_result":"Only payloads satisfying the complete domain contract are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Include adversarial malformed structures and unexpected additional fields where relevant.","verification_method":"Boundary validation tests plus static inspection of all type assertions.","verification_guidance":"Every type assertion crossing an external trust boundary should have an explicit justification or replacement validation strategy.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"4.x+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler","JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"TYPE_SYSTEM_AND_RUNTIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["TypeScript type assertions and unknown-type documentation; ECMAScript JSON runtime behavior"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B015","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Concurrency","concept":"Deadlock caused by inconsistent lock ordering","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can two Java threads deadlock even though each thread correctly releases its locks?","answer":"Deadlock can occur when two threads acquire multiple locks in opposite orders. Each thread can hold one lock while waiting indefinitely for the lock held by the other.","explanation":"The root cause is a circular wait created by inconsistent lock acquisition order. The diagnostic evidence is a thread dump showing blocked threads and the locks each thread owns and waits for.","principle":"When multiple locks must be acquired together, a consistent global acquisition order can prevent lock-order deadlocks.","objective":"Diagnose a deadlock caused by lock-order inversion.","requirements":["The lock acquisition protocol must prevent circular wait."],"constraints":["The chosen ordering must be consistently applied by all relevant code paths."],"preconditions":["At least two locks can be acquired by multiple threads."],"input":"Thread A acquires lockA then lockB; Thread B acquires lockB then lockA.","output":"Both threads can wait indefinitely.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent operations complete without circular lock dependency.","incorrect_code":"synchronized (lockA) {\n    synchronized (lockB) {\n        operation();\n    }\n}\n\n// Another path\nsynchronized (lockB) {\n    synchronized (lockA) {\n        operation();\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"synchronized (lockA) {\n    synchronized (lockB) {\n        operation();\n    }\n}\n\n// All paths use the same order\nsynchronized (lockA) {\n    synchronized (lockB) {\n        anotherOperation();\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Permanent or long-duration thread blocking due to circular lock dependency.","expected_error":"No mandatory exception; the JVM may show blocked threads in a thread dump.","cause":"Different code paths acquire the same locks in different orders.","root_cause":"No consistent lock-order invariant exists across the synchronization protocol.","solution":"Establish and enforce a consistent lock order or redesign ownership to avoid nested locking.","edge_case":"A globally consistent order must account for every path that can acquire the involved locks, not merely the two functions where the failure was observed.","security_implication":"Deadlocks can create availability failures and can become denial-of-service conditions when attacker-controlled operations trigger the blocked path.","performance_implication":"Excessive lock nesting increases contention and reduces concurrency even when deadlock does not occur.","test_case":"Execute competing operations that acquire the same locks through different code paths under controlled concurrency.","test_input":"Concurrent operations using reversed lock acquisition order.","expected_test_result":"The corrected implementation completes without circular waiting.","testing_guidance":"Use thread dumps and concurrency stress tests; avoid treating one successful run as proof of deadlock freedom.","verification_method":"Lock-order analysis, thread-dump inspection, and controlled concurrency testing.","verification_guidance":"Construct the lock dependency graph and check for cycles.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 8+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":["Java synchronization primitives"],"execution_requirements":["JVM","Concurrent test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"JAVA_CONCURRENCY_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B014"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Java Language Specification synchroni