{"record_id":"D36-B016","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Memory Safety","concept":"Off-by-one buffer access","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a one-position indexing mistake cause memory corruption in C?","answer":"C arrays are zero-indexed, so an array with N elements has valid indices from 0 through N-1. Accessing index N is outside the array and creates undefined behavior.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an index reaching the collection size rather than the final valid index. The visible symptom may be a crash, corrupted state, sanitizer report, or apparently correct execution because the behavior is undefined.","principle":"Memory bounds must be derived from the actual number of valid elements, not from the maximum index by assumption.","objective":"Diagnose an out-of-bounds access caused by an off-by-one loop condition.","requirements":["Every array access must use an index within the object's valid bounds."],"constraints":["Undefined behavior cannot be safely reasoned about after an invalid memory access occurs."],"preconditions":["A fixed-size C array is traversed with an incorrect loop bound."],"input":"int values[10]; for (int i = 0; i <= 10; ++i) use(values[i]);","output":"The iteration accesses values[10], which is outside the array.","expected_behavior":"Only indices 0 through 9 are accessed.","incorrect_code":"for (int i = 0; i <= 10; ++i) {\n    process(values[i]);\n}","corrected_code":"for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {\n    process(values[i]);\n}","failure_mode":"Out-of-bounds memory access.","expected_error":"No guaranteed runtime error; behavior is undefined. Memory sanitizers may report the invalid access.","cause":"The loop condition includes the element count as a valid index.","root_cause":"The implementation confuses the number of elements with the highest valid index.","solution":"Use a strict upper bound equal to the element count for zero-based indexing, or derive bounds from the actual object where possible.","edge_case":"The correct bound depends on the actual object extent; hard-coded bounds can themselves become stale after structural changes.","security_implication":"Out-of-bounds memory access can cause memory corruption and may become a security vulnerability.","performance_implication":"Bounds-safe implementations may introduce checks depending on compiler and design, but correctness and memory safety take precedence.","test_case":"Traverse arrays at sizes 0, 1, and several larger boundary values while enabling memory sanitization where available.","test_input":"Array lengths including the minimum and maximum expected sizes.","expected_test_result":"No invalid access occurs and all valid elements are processed exactly once.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer or equivalent instrumentation when available.","verification_method":"Static boundary reasoning plus sanitizer-assisted execution.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer memory safety solely from a successful normal execution.","language":"C","language_version":"C11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["C compiler","Runtime memory-safety instrumentation where available"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"C_LANGUAGE_UNDEFINED_BEHAVIOR","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO C language semantics regarding arrays, subscripting, and undefined behavior"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B017","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Integer Semantics","concept":"Signed and unsigned conversion causing incorrect boundary logic","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a C++ loop unexpectedly execute a huge number of iterations when comparing a signed integer with an unsigned size?","answer":"When signed and unsigned integers participate in the same comparison, the signed value may be converted to an unsigned type. A negative signed value can therefore become a very large positive value, changing the comparison result.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a comparison involving a potentially negative signed index and an unsigned container size. The root cause is implicit integer conversion rather than the loop condition alone.","principle":"Integer conversion rules are part of program semantics and must be considered when reasoning about boundary conditions.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect loop behavior caused by signed/unsigned comparison.","requirements":["Index and size types should be chosen and compared deliberately."],"constraints":["Container size APIs commonly use unsigned size types."],"preconditions":["A potentially negative signed value is compared against an unsigned size.","],"input":"int index = -1; if (index < values.size()) ...","output":"The comparison may evaluate differently from the programmer's intuitive signed comparison.","expected_behavior":"Boundary checks must correctly handle negative values before conversion to an unsigned representation.","incorrect_code":"int index = get_index();\nif (index < values.size()) {\n    use(values[index]);\n}","corrected_code":"int index = get_index();\nif (index >= 0 && static_cast<std::size_t>(index) < values.size()) {\n    use(values[static_cast<std::size_t>(index)]);\n}","failure_mode":"Incorrect boundary check followed by invalid indexing or unexpected control flow.","expected_error":"Potential out-of-bounds access, failed validation, or unexpectedly large iteration range depending on surrounding code.","cause":"Implicit conversion changes the signed value before comparison.","root_cause":"The implementation does not account for the language's integral conversion rules.","solution":"Validate signed values before converting to the container's size type or use compatible types deliberately.","edge_case":"Casting a negative value directly to an unsigned type before validating it reproduces the underlying problem.","security_implication":"Incorrect bounds checks can become memory-safety vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Unexpected large iteration ranges can cause severe CPU consumption or denial of service.","test_case":"Test negative, zero, maximum valid, and first invalid index values.","test_input":"index = -1, 0, last valid index, container size.","expected_test_result":"Negative and out-of-range indices are rejected before indexing.","testing_guidance":"Enable compiler warnings for sign-conversion and related diagnostics where practical.","verification_method":"Type-conversion analysis, compiler diagnostics, boundary tests, and sanitizer-assisted testing.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the types of both operands rather than reasoning only from their apparent numeric values.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["C++ compiler","Optional sanitizer tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CPLUSPLUS_INTEGRAL_CONVERSION_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B016"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ standard integral conversion rules and container size type semantics"],"created_at":"2026