{"record_id":"D36-B018","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Borrowing","concept":"Conflicting immutable and mutable borrows","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does Rust reject code that attempts to mutate a value while an active immutable borrow is still used?","answer":"Rust's borrow rules prevent mutable access from overlapping with an active incompatible immutable borrow. The compiler rejects the program because allowing both could permit invalid references or violate aliasing guarantees.","explanation":"Unlike a runtime race or memory-corruption bug, this failure is detected statically. The diagnostic evidence is the compiler's borrow-checking error and the source-level lifetime relationship between the references.","principle":"Rust enforces ownership and borrowing rules at compile time to prevent invalid aliasing and lifetime relationships.","objective":"Diagnose a borrow-checker failure involving overlapping incompatible references.","requirements":["References must obey Rust's ownership and aliasing rules."],"constraints":["The lifetime of the immutable borrow must end before conflicting mutable access occurs."],"preconditions":["A value is immutably borrowed and then mutably borrowed while the immutable reference remains required."],"input":"let first = &values[0]; values.push(1); use(first);","output":"Compilation fails because mutable access conflicts with the outstanding immutable borrow.","expected_behavior":"The code must end or restructure the immutable borrow before mutating the owning collection.","incorrect_code":"let first = &values[0];\nvalues.push(1);\nprintln!(\"{first}\");","corrected_code":"let first = values[0];\nvalues.push(1);\nprintln!(\"{first}\");","failure_mode":"Compile-time borrow-checking failure.","expected_error":"Rust compiler error describing conflicting borrow/use of the value.","cause":"A mutable operation occurs while an incompatible immutable reference remains live.","root_cause":"The reference lifetime overlaps a mutation that may invalidate or conflict with the reference.","solution":"Copy the required value when appropriate, shorten the borrow scope, or redesign the operation to avoid overlapping incompatible access.","edge_case":"Whether a reference remains live can depend on how it is subsequently used; lexical scope alone is not always the complete model.","security_implication":"The borrow checker prevents a class of memory-safety defects before execution.","performance_implication":"Avoiding unnecessary copies may matter for large values, but correctness of ownership relationships comes first.","test_case":"Compile variants where the immutable reference is used before mutation, after mutation, or replaced by a copied value.","test_input":"Borrowing and mutation combinations.","expected_test_result":"Only programs satisfying the ownership and borrowing rules compile.","testing_guidance":"Treat compiler diagnostics as primary evidence for this class of failure.","verification_method":"Compiler type/borrow checking and ownership-flow inspection.","verification_guidance":"Do not classify a compile-time borrow rejection as a runtime failure.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RUST_OWNERSHIP_AND_BORROWING_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B007"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["The Rust Reference and Rust Book ownership and borrowing documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B019","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Distributed Systems","concept":"Duplicate side effect after ambiguous request completion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can retrying a timed-out request create a duplicate side effect?","answer":"A client timeout establishes that the client did not receive confirmation, not that the server failed to execute the operation. If the server committed the first operation before the response was lost, a retry can perform the same side effect again.","explanation":"The critical diagnostic evidence is the distinction between operation completion and response delivery. The root cause is a retry strategy that treats ambiguous completion as confirmed failure for a non-idempotent operation.","principle":"Retry safety depends on operation idempotency and the semantics of ambiguous completion.","objective":"Diagnose duplicate effects caused by unsafe retries.","requirements":["Retries must preserve the intended business effect."],"constraints":["Network failures can occur after server-side commit but before client-side response receipt."],"preconditions":["A non-idempotent operation is retried after timeout or connection failure."],"input":"Create-order request times out after the server commits.","output":"A retry may create a second order.","expected_behavior":"Repeated attempts must either produce one logical effect or use an explicit reconciliation mechanism.","incorrect_code":"try:\n    create_order(order)\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    create_order(order)","corrected_code":"request_id = generate_request_id()\ntry:\n    create_order(order, idempotency_key=request_id)\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    reconcile_or_retry_with_same_key(request_id)","failure_mode":"Duplicate business side effect.","expected_error":"Client observes a timeout while the server may already have committed the operation.","cause":"The retry mechanism has no reliable operation identity or idempotency guarantee.","root_cause":"Transport failure is incorrectly interpreted as operation failure.","solution":"Use server-supported idempotency keys, deterministic request identity, reconciliation, or an operation designed to be safely repeatable.","edge_case":"An idempotency key only provides the intended guarantee when the receiving service actually implements and persists the corresponding semantics.","security_implication":"Duplicate financial, privilege, account, or state-changing operations can create integrity and security incidents.","performance_implication":"Retries increase load and can amplify outages; bounded retries and backoff reduce amplification but do not establish idempotency.","test_case":"Commit the first operation and intentionally drop its response before the client retries.","test_input":"First operation commits; response is lost; second request uses the same operation identity.","expected_test_result":"The server preserves the documented single-operation semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test timeout-before-commit, timeout-after-commit, connection reset, duplicate request, and idempotency-key reuse.","verification_method":"Failure injection plus server-side effect inspection and request-identity tracing.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual service behavior instead of assuming that a client retry library guarantees idempotency.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Protocol/API dependent","dependencies":["HTTP/API client","Server-side idempotency support or reconciliation mechanism"],"execution_requirements":["Controlled client/server environment","Failure-injection capability"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_FAILURE_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B010"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["HTTP request failure semantics and distributed-systems idempotency principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B020","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Version-Sensitive Semantics","concept":"Loop-variable capture behavior across Go versions","knowledge_type":"VERSION_BEHAVIOR","question":"Why can goroutine code involving loop variables behave differently between older Go versions and Go 1.22+?","answer":"Go changed loop-variable semantics in Go 1.22 so that variables declared by loop constructs can have per-iteration scope in the applicable language version/module context. Code and diagnostics involving closure capture must therefore record the Go version and module language version.","explanation":"The historical failure pattern involved closures or goroutines capturing a loop variable whose reused storage produced unexpected values. Go 1.22 changed the semantics for loop variables declared by affected loop constructs. Version information is therefore part of the root-cause analysis rather than incidental metadata.","principle":"Language-version changes can alter the semantics of previously problematic code and must be included in debugging evidence.","objective":"Diagnose a loop-variable capture bug while accounting for Go version semantics.","requirements":["The language version and module configuration must be recorded when analyzing version-sensitive behavior."],"constraints":["The exact semantics depend on the loop form and language version/module settings."],"preconditions":["A closure or goroutine captures a loop variable and behavior is being compared across Go versions."],"input":"for _, v := range values { go func() { use(v) }() }","output":"Behavior depends on the applicable Go language semantics and version context.","expected_behavior":"Each goroutine should observe the intended iteration value.","incorrect_code":"for _, v := range values {\n    go func() {\n        use(v)\n    }()\n}","corrected_code":"for _, v := range values {\n    value := v\n    go func() {\n        use(value)\n    }()\n}","failure_mode":"Unexpected captured loop value in affected historical language semantics.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime error; incorrect values may be observed.","cause":"Closure captures loop-variable state whose semantics differ by Go language version.","root_cause":"Debugging or code generation ignored version-specific loop-variable semantics.","solution":"Record the Go/module language version, understand the applicable loop semantics, and use explicit per-iteration binding when portability across versions is required.","edge_case":"Go 1.22 changed loop-variable behavior, but not every loop construct or compatibility configuration should be generalized without checking the applicable language specification.","security_implication":"Incorrectly captured identifiers can cause actions to target the wrong resource, tenant, or authorization context.","performance_implication":"Explicit variable binding normally has negligible conceptual overhead; goroutine creation and synchronization dominate typical costs.","test_case":"Compile and run equivalent closure-capture examples under the intended Go language versions and module configurations.","test_input":"Multiple loop iterations with delayed goroutine execution.","expected_test_result":"Observed behavior matches the semantics documented for the selected Go language version.","testing_guidance":"Record compiler version and go.mod language directive with the test result.","verification_method":"Version-aware compiler execution plus inspection of generated behavior.","verification_guidance":"Never generalize a version-sensitive language behavior without recording the language version and relevant module configuration.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.22+ and earlier version semantics","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Version-sensitive; Go 1.22 introduced changed loop-variable semantics","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler","Version-specific module configuration"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERSIONED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B011"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Go specification and official Go documentation on loop-variable semantics and the Go 1.22 loop-variable change"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B021","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Thread Interruption","concept":"Swallowed InterruptedException causing lost cancellation signal","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can catching InterruptedException without restoring interruption status cause a Java task to ignore cancellation?","answer":"InterruptedException is a cooperative cancellation signal. If code catches it and neither propagates the exception nor restores the thread's interrupted status, higher-level code may lose the signal and continue executing when cancellation was requested.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a thread that receives interruption but continues through a blocking or cancellable workflow. The root cause is not the interruption itself but the exception handler consuming the cancellation signal without preserving its meaning.","principle":"Cancellation signals must remain observable across abstraction boundaries unless the component intentionally completes the cancellation protocol.","objective":"Diagnose lost cancellation caused by incorrect InterruptedException handling.","requirements":["Interruption must be propagated or the interrupted status restored when the current layer cannot fully handle cancellation."],"constraints":["The correct policy depends on whether the current method owns the cancellation decision."],"preconditions":["A Java blocking or interruptible operation throws InterruptedException."],"input":"try { queue.take(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }","output":"The method exits without preserving the thread's interruption status.","expected_behavior":"Cancellation should remain observable to the caller or controlling executor.","incorrect_code":"try {\n    queue.take();\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    log(\"interrupted\");\n}","corrected_code":"try {\n    queue.take();\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n    throw new CancellationException(\"Operation interrupted\");\n}","failure_mode":"Cancellation signal is swallowed.","expected_error":"No mandatory runtime exception after the handler; the higher-level system may incorrectly continue normal execution.","cause":"InterruptedException is caught without propagation or restoration.","root_cause":"The interruption contract is broken at an exception boundary.","solution":"Propagate InterruptedException when possible or restore the interrupted status before returning when the API cannot propagate it.","edge_case":"A component that intentionally consumes interruption must have an explicit cancellation policy and should not silently convert cancellation into successful completion.","security_implication":"Ignoring cancellation can leave security-sensitive or privileged operations running beyond their intended lifetime.","performance_implication":"Cancelled work that continues executing consumes CPU, threads, I/O, and other resources.","test_case":"Interrupt a worker while it is blocked and verify that cancellation remains observable to the controlling layer.","test_input":"Worker blocked on an interruptible operation followed by Thread.interrupt().","expected_test_result":"The cancellation signal reaches the intended control boundary and the worker does not silently continue normal work.","testing_guidance":"Test interruption during every relevant blocking stage, including queues, sleeps, waits, and executor tasks.","verification_method":"Thread interruption analysis plus controlled cancellation tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect both exception handling and interrupted-status propagation.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 8+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":["Java concurrency APIs"],"execution_requirements":["JVM","Concurrent test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"JAVA_CONCURRENCY_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B014","D36-B015"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Java Thread interruption and InterruptedException documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B022","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Object Aliasing","concept":"Unexpected mutation through shared object references","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can changing one JavaScript object unexpectedly change another apparently independent object?","answer":"Objects are reference values. Assigning an object to another variable or storing the same object in multiple structures does not create an independent copy. Mutating one reference therefore changes the same underlying object observed through the other reference.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is identity equality or a shared nested reference between supposedly independent state objects. The root cause is an incorrect assumption that assignment performs deep copying.","principle":"Object ownership and aliasing must be explicit when mutable state is shared.","objective":"Diagnose state corruption caused by unintended object aliasing.","requirements":["Independent state must not share mutable object identity unless sharing is intentional."],"constraints":["Shallow copies do not recursively copy nested mutable objects."],"preconditions":["Two logical state holders reference the same mutable object or nested object."],"input":"const copy = original; copy.settings.enabled = false;","output":"original.settings.enabled also becomes false.","expected_behavior":"An independent copy should have independent mutable state.","incorrect_code":"const profile = { settings: { enabled: true } };\nconst backup = profile;\nbackup.settings.enabled = false;","corrected_code":"const profile = { settings: { enabled: true } };\nconst backup = structuredClone(profile);\nbackup.settings.enabled = false;","failure_mode":"Unexpected mutation propagates between logical state owners.","expected_error":"","cause":"Multiple variables retain references to the same mutable object.","root_cause":"The implementation confuses reference assignment with object copying.","solution":"Define ownership explicitly and use an appropriate copy strategy or immutable data design.","edge_case":"structuredClone has supported-value limitations; custom classes, functions, external resources, and special objects may require another strategy.","security_implication":"Shared mutable references can cause cross-request, cross-user, or cross-tenant state contamination when object ownership is incorrectly modeled.","performance_implication":"Deep copying can increase CPU and memory usage; immutable structures or controlled ownership may be more efficient for large data.","test_case":"Modify nested state through one reference and verify whether another logical state holder changes.","test_input":"Nested object with multiple references.","expected_test_result":"Independent state remains independent after mutation.","testing_guidance":"Test both shallow and nested mutations because shallow-copy tests can miss shared nested references.","verification_method":"Object identity inspection plus mutation-isolation tests.","verification_guidance":"Trace references rather than comparing only serialized values before mutation.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2021+ for structuredClone availability","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"JavaScript object-reference semantics; structuredClone availability is environment-dependent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime supporting the selected copy mechanism"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ECMASCRIPT_REFERENCE_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ECMAScript object reference semantics and structuredClone platform specification/documentation"],"created_a