{"record_id":"D36-B023","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Control Flow","concept":"Incorrect discriminated-union narrowing caused by an incomplete discriminator contract","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can TypeScript code incorrectly assume a union member when the runtime discriminator is not validated?","answer":"TypeScript can narrow a union based on static control-flow analysis, but runtime data can still contain unexpected discriminator values if it originated outside the type system. An incomplete validation boundary can therefore make apparently exhaustive logic unsafe at runtime.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an externally supplied discriminator value that is outside the declared union. The root cause is treating a compile-time union declaration as proof that runtime input belongs to that union.","principle":"Exhaustiveness at compile time does not establish validity of untrusted runtime input.","objective":"Diagnose runtime failures caused by assuming an external discriminator satisfies a TypeScript union.","requirements":["Runtime discriminators must be validated before entering a trusted discriminated-union model."],"constraints":["The validation contract must cover both discriminator and associated payload fields."],"preconditions":["External data is parsed into a TypeScript discriminated union without complete runtime validation."],"input":"const event = JSON.parse(payload) as Event;","output":"Unexpected event kinds can reach branches designed only for declared union members.","expected_behavior":"Unknown discriminator values are rejected or handled explicitly.","incorrect_code":"const event = JSON.parse(payload) as Event;\nif (event.kind === \"created\") {\n    handleCreated(event);\n} else {\n    handleDeleted(event);\n}","corrected_code":"const event: unknown = JSON.parse(payload);\nif (!isEvent(event)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid event\");\n}\nif (event.kind === \"created\") {\n    handleCreated(event);\n} else {\n    handleDeleted(event);\n}","failure_mode":"Runtime data enters an invalid union state.","expected_error":"Application-specific validation error or downstream runtime failure.","cause":"The discriminator was trusted without runtime validation.","root_cause":"The boundary between untrusted data and the discriminated domain model was not enforced.","solution":"Validate discriminator and member-specific fields before narrowing and processing the value.","edge_case":"Adding a default branch does not automatically make malformed payloads safe if the default path assumes a valid declared member.","security_implication":"Unexpected event types can reach incorrect authorization or state-transition logic.","performance_implication":"Boundary validation adds processing cost but prevents invalid state from propagating through multiple layers.","test_case":"Supply every valid discriminator plus unknown, missing, null, and wrong-type discriminator values.","test_input":"Valid and malformed event payloads.","expected_test_result":"Only structurally valid declared event variants are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Test discriminator validity independently from payload validity.","verification_method":"Static narrowing analysis plus runtime boundary validation tests.","verification_guidance":"Check that all externally reachable union states are explicitly represented in the validation contract.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"4.x+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler","JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"TYPE_SYSTEM_AND_RUNTIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B014"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["TypeScript control-flow analysis, discriminated unions, and type narrowing documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B024","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Integer Arithmetic","concept":"Signed integer overflow causing undefined behavior","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can arithmetic overflow in signed C produce behavior that cannot be diagnosed merely by inspecting the resulting number?","answer":"Signed integer overflow in C is undefined behavior. The implementation therefore cannot be safely reasoned about as simple modular arithmetic after the overflow occurs.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence may include incorrect comparisons, compiler-dependent behavior, sanitizer reports, or optimization-sensitive failures. The root cause is performing an operation whose mathematical result cannot be represented by the signed integer type.","principle":"Arithmetic validity must be established before performing operations whose result may exceed the representable range.","objective":"Diagnose undefined behavior caused by signed integer overflow.","requirements":["Arithmetic operations must respect the representable range of the selected type."],"constraints":["Exact integer range depends on the implementation and type."],"preconditions":["Signed integer arithmetic can exceed the representable range."],"input":"int result = a + b;","output":"If the mathematical sum is outside the representable range, behavior is undefined.","expected_behavior":"Overflow is detected or avoided before the operation.","incorrect_code":"int total = quantity * price;\nif (total < 0) {\n    report_error();\n}","corrected_code":"if (quantity != 0 && price > INT_MAX / quantity) {\n    report_error();\n}\nint total = quantity * price;","failure_mode":"Undefined behavior from signed integer overflow.","expected_error":"No guaranteed runtime error; sanitizers may detect the operation.","cause":"Operands produce a result outside the signed type's representable range.","root_cause":"The implementation performs unchecked arithmetic before establishing range safety.","solution":"Use checked arithmetic, wider types where sufficient, explicit range validation, or an arithmetic abstraction with defined overflow semantics.","edge_case":"A wider type is not automatically safe if the product or intermediate expression can exceed that wider type too.","security_implication":"Integer overflow can bypass bounds checks, corrupt allocation sizes, or enable memory-safety vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Checked arithmetic can add cost, while undefined behavior can permit compiler optimizations that make debugging difficult.","test_case":"Exercise minimum, maximum, near-boundary, zero, and negative operands.","test_input":"Values near the representable limits of the selected integer type.","expected_test_result":"Overflow is rejected or handled according to the defined arithmetic contract.","testing_guidance":"Use compiler sanitizers and boundary-focused tests.","verification_method":"Range analysis, compiler diagnostics, and sanitizer-assisted execution.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat observed wraparound on one platform as the language-level behavior of signed overflow.","language":"C","language_version":"C11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C11+","dependencies":["limits.h for implementation-defined integer limits"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler","Optional undefined-behavior sanitizer"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ISO_C_UNDEFINED_BEHAVIOR","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B016"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO C integer arithmetic and undefined-behavior rules"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B025","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Object Lifetime","concept":"Dangling reference after object lifetime ends","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a C++ reference remain syntactically valid while referring to an object whose lifetime has ended?","answer":"A reference does not extend the lifetime of every object it refers to. If the referenced object is destroyed, using the reference afterward produces undefined behavior.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an ownership/lifetime path showing destruction before reference use. The root cause is retaining an alias beyond the lifetime of its referent.","principle":"Reference validity depends on object lifetime, not merely on the reference variable remaining in scope.","objective":"Diagnose use of a dangling C++ reference.","requirements":["Every reference or pointer use must occur while the referenced object's lifetime is valid."],"constraints":["Lifetime rules vary with storage duration, ownership, temporaries, and container operations."],"preconditions":["A reference escapes the lifetime of the referenced object."],"input":"const std::string& get_name() {\n    std::string local = \"Alice\";\n    return local;\n}","output":"The returned reference refers to an object whose lifetime ended when the function returned.","expected_behavior":"Return an owning value or a reference whose referent is guaranteed to outlive the reference.","incorrect_code":"const std::string& get_name() {\n    std::string local = \"Alice\";\n    return local;\n}","corrected_code":"std::string get_name() {\n    std::string local = \"Alice\";\n    return local;\n}","failure_mode":"Use-after-lifetime / dangling reference.","expected_error":"No guaranteed runtime error; behavior is undefined.","cause":"A reference to an automatic local object escapes the object's lifetime.","root_cause":"The API exposes a non-owning reference without a lifetime guarantee for its referent.","solution":"Return an owning value or ensure the referenced object's lifetime exceeds every possible use.","edge_case":"Returning a reference to static storage or an externally owned object can be valid when the lifetime contract is explicit.","security_implication":"Dangling references can cause memory corruption and potentially exploitable behavior.","performance_implication":"Returning values can often be optimized through move semantics and copy elision; avoiding undefined behavior is more important than assuming reference return is faster.","test_case":"Compile with warnings and run lifetime-sensitive code under AddressSanitizer where applicable.","test_input":"Function returning a reference to a local object.","expected_test_result":"The corrected API returns a valid object whose lifetime is owned by the caller.","testing_guidance":"Use compiler warnings and lifetime analysis; sanitizers can provide runtime evidence but cannot prove absence of all lifetime bugs.","verification_method":"Ownership/lifetime analysis plus sanitizer-assisted execution.","verification_guidance":"Trace the complete lifetime of the referenced object, not just the lexical scope of the reference.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["C++ compiler","Optional AddressSanitizer"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CPLUSPLUS_OBJECT_LIFETIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B006","D36-B016"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ object lifetime, reference, and temporary-lifetime rules"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B026","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Error Handling","concept":"Panicking on recoverable operational failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can using unwrap on an expected operational failure turn a recoverable condition into process failure?","answer":"unwrap converts a Result::Err or Option::None into a panic. When the underlying failure is an expected operational condition such as missing configuration or unavailable input, unwrap incorrectly treats normal failure as an invariant violation.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a panic originating from unwrap at an operation whose failure is part of normal application behavior. The root cause is an incorrect error policy rather than the existence of the underlying error.","principle":"Error handling should distinguish violated programmer invariants from expected operational failures.","objective":"Diagnose inappropriate panic behavior caused by unwrap.","requirements":["Expected operational failures must be represented and handled according to the application's error contract."],"constraints":["unwrap can be appropriate when a condition is genuinely proven impossible by program invariants."],"preconditions":["An operation can legitimately return Err or None under normal circumstances."],"input":"let config = load_config().unwrap();","output":"A configuration failure causes panic instead of controlled error propagation.","expected_behavior":"Expected failure is propagated or transformed into an appropriate application error.","incorrect_code":"fn start() {\n    let config = load_config().unwrap();\n    run(config);\n}","corrected_code":"fn start() -> Result<(), ConfigError> {\n    let config = load_config()?;\n    run(config)\n}","failure_mode":"Unexpected process panic from recoverable error.","expected_error":"panic caused by unwrap when the Result is Err.","cause":"The implementation assumes success without establishing the required invariant.","root_cause":"An operational failure was incorrectly modeled as an impossible programmer error.","solution":"Propagate or explicitly handle the error unless success is guaranteed by a verified invariant.","edge_case":"unwrap can be valid after a prior check or when a static invariant makes failure impossible; the justification must be real rather than assumed.","security_implication":"Panics in request-handling or privileged services can create availability failures and may expose sensitive diagnostic information if panic output is mishandled.","performance_implication":"Error propagation generally avoids the broader operational cost of process recovery or restart caused by inappropriate panic.","test_case":"Force the underlying operation to return Err and verify controlled error propagation.","test_input":"Missing or invalid configuration.","expected_test_result":"The corrected implementation returns an error through the declared error contract without panic.","testing_guidance":"Test every documented failure variant rather than only the successful path.","verification_method":"Compiler type checking plus negative-path runtime tests.","verification_guidance":"Determine whether failure is an expected domain condition or a violated invariant before selecting panic semantics.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust 1.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler","Runtime test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RUST_ERROR_HANDLING_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B018"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Rust Result, Option, panic, and error-handling documentation"],"created_at":"2026-0