{"record_id":"D36-B027","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Error Handling","concept":"Ignored error return causing invalid state propagation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can ignoring a Go error return cause a later failure that appears unrelated to the original operation?","answer":"Go commonly represents operational failure through explicit error return values. Ignoring an error can allow an invalid, empty, partially initialized, or stale value to continue through the system until a later operation fails or produces incorrect behavior.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an earlier operation that returned an error followed by downstream use of its result. The root cause is failure information being discarded at the boundary where it was first available.","principle":"Errors should be handled at the point where sufficient context exists to decide whether recovery, propagation, or transformation is appropriate.","objective":"Trace a downstream failure back to an ignored Go error.","requirements":["Relevant error returns must be checked and handled according to the operation contract."],"constraints":["Some APIs intentionally permit ignored errors, but that decision must be explicit and justified."],"preconditions":["A function returns both a value and an error and the caller ignores the error."],"input":"data, _ := loadConfig(); use(data)","output":"Invalid or incomplete data may reach later processing.","expected_behavior":"The failure is handled before dependent processing proceeds.","incorrect_code":"data, _ := loadConfig()\nreturn process(data)","corrected_code":"data, err := loadConfig()\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"load config: %w\", err)\n}\nreturn process(data)","failure_mode":"Downstream failure caused by discarded upstream error.","expected_error":"The original error may be lost and replaced by a less informative later error.","cause":"The caller explicitly discards the returned error.","root_cause":"Failure information is removed before the system has completed the dependent operation's preconditions.","solution":"Handle, propagate, or intentionally classify the error at the acquisition boundary.","edge_case":"An error may legitimately be ignored when the API contract documents that it is non-actionable, but the discard should be deliberate.","security_implication":"Ignored authorization, validation, or integrity errors can allow invalid state to reach security-sensitive operations.","performance_implication":"Downstream processing of invalid data can waste resources and increase failure-recovery cost.","test_case":"Force the dependency to return an error and verify that dependent processing does not execute with invalid data.","test_input":"Injected configuration/load failure.","expected_test_result":"The original error is propagated or intentionally transformed with sufficient context.","testing_guidance":"Test each error branch and inspect wrapped error chains where applicable.","verification_method":"Static error-path analysis plus injected failure tests.","verification_guidance":"Locate the first point where the failure became observable rather than assigning root cause to the final crash alone.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime","Failure-injection test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"GO_ERROR_HANDLING_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B020"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Go language error-return conventions and standard library error-handling documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B028","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Class State","concept":"Shared mutable class attribute mistaken for instance state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can changing an attribute on one Python object unexpectedly affect another object of the same class?","answer":"A mutable class attribute is shared through the class unless an instance receives its own attribute. Mutating the shared object therefore changes the state observed by other instances.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is multiple instances observing the same mutable object identity. The root cause is placing per-instance mutable state at class scope.","principle":"State intended to be independent per instance must be owned by each instance rather than by the class.","objective":"Diagnose cross-instance state leakage caused by a mutable class attribute.","requirements":["Per-instance mutable state must be initialized per instance."],"constraints":["Class-level mutable state is valid when shared state is intentional."],"preconditions":["A mutable object is declared as a class attribute and later mutated through instances."],"input":"class Cart:\n    items = []\n\nfirst = Cart()\nsecond = Cart()\nfirst.items.append(\"A\")","output":"second.items also contains the appended value.","expected_behavior":"Each cart should have an independent items collection.","incorrect_code":"class Cart:\n    items = []","corrected_code":"class Cart:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []","failure_mode":"Cross-instance mutable-state leakage.","expected_error":"","cause":"Both instances resolve the same class-level mutable object.","root_cause":"Per-instance state was declared at class scope.","solution":"Initialize mutable state inside instance initialization or deliberately use a shared class-level object when sharing is required.","edge_case":"Immutable class attributes used as defaults do not have the same mutation-sharing problem, although assignment can still create instance attributes.","security_implication":"Shared state can leak information between users, sessions, tenants, or requests if instances represent security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Accidental shared state may reduce allocations but correctness and isolation requirements take precedence.","test_case":"Create two independent instances, mutate state through one, and assert that the other remains unchanged.","test_input":"Two Cart instances with one mutation.","expected_test_result":"Only the mutated instance contains the new item.","testing_guidance":"Test nested mutable members as well as direct attributes.","verification_method":"Object identity inspection plus isolation tests.","verification_guidance":"Check both class attribute lookup and instance attribute initialization.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.x interpreter"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PYTHON_ATTRIBUTE_LOOKUP_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B001","D36-B022"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python data model and attribute lookup documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B029","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Prototype and Property Semantics","concept":"Inherited property mistaken for own data property","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can JavaScript code incorrectly treat an inherited property as if it were an object's own property?","answer":"Property lookup normally traverses the prototype chain. Code that assumes every readable property belongs directly to the object can therefore process inherited values unexpectedly.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a property that is readable but is not an own property. The root cause is confusing property accessibility with ownership. This distinction becomes important when objects originate from external input, custom prototypes, or framework-managed objects.","principle":"Property existence and property ownership are separate concepts in JavaScript.","objective":"Diagnose logic that incorrectly accepts inherited properties as direct object data.","requirements":["Code requiring own data properties must explicitly test ownership."],"constraints":["The appropriate ownership check depends on the intended object contract."],"preconditions":["Application logic interprets property presence as proof that the object directly owns the property."],"input":"if (\"role\" in user) { authorize(user.role); }","output":"An inherited role property can satisfy the condition.","expected_behavior":"Authorization logic should use a validated own property or trusted domain representation.","incorrect_code":"if (\"role\" in user) {\n    authorize(user.role);\n}","corrected_code":"if (Object.hasOwn(user, \"role\")) {\n    authorize(user.role);\n}","failure_mode":"Unexpected inherited property influences application logic.","expected_error":"","cause":"The in operator checks the prototype chain as well as own properties.","root_cause":"The implementation uses property existence when the actual invariant requires own-property ownership.","solution":"Use an ownership-aware check and validate the complete input contract before security-sensitive processing.","edge_case":"Object.hasOwn requires an environment supporting the API; Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call can be used where compatibility requires it.","security_implication":"Property-prototype confusion can affect authorization, configuration, filtering, and input-processing logic.","performance_implication":"Property checks have runtime cost, but the relevant concern is correctness and security rather than micro-optimization.","test_case":"Create objects with own, inherited, missing, and null-prototype properties and verify the intended behavior.","test_input":"Objects with role defined directly and through the prototype chain.","expected_test_result":"Only values satisfying the intended ownership contract are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Include objects with unusual prototypes and null prototypes where relevant to the application boundary.","verification_method":"Prototype-chain inspection plus ownership-focused tests.","verification_guidance":"For security-sensitive data, combine ownership checks with schema validation rather than relying on property existence alone.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+ for Object.hasOwn","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ES2022+ for Object.hasOwn; equivalent ownership checks exist in earlier ECMAScript versions","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime supporting selected ownership API"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ECMASCRIPT_PROPERTY_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B013"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ECMAScript property access, prototype chain, an