{"record_id":"D36-B030","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Resource and Stream Lifecycle","concept":"Resource leak caused by incomplete cleanup in exceptional control flow","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can Java code leak a resource even though it closes the resource on the normal path?","answer":"Manual cleanup can be skipped when an exception occurs before the close operation. Java try-with-resources encodes deterministic cleanup into the language construct and ensures resources implementing AutoCloseable are closed when leaving the scope.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a control-flow path that exits before close is reached. The root cause is coupling cleanup to successful completion rather than the resource lifetime.","principle":"Resource cleanup must be guaranteed across normal completion and exceptional control flow.","objective":"Diagnose a Java resource leak caused by manual cleanup.","requirements":["Owned AutoCloseable resources must be closed on all relevant exit paths."],"constraints":["Resource ownership must be clear when multiple components share a resource."],"preconditions":["A method acquires an AutoCloseable resource and can throw before manual close.","],"input":"InputStream stream = open(); process(stream); stream.close();","output":"An exception from process can bypass close.","expected_behavior":"The resource closes when the owning scope exits regardless of processing success.","incorrect_code":"InputStream stream = open();\nprocess(stream);\nstream.close();","corrected_code":"try (InputStream stream = open()) {\n    process(stream);\n}","failure_mode":"Resource leak on exceptional path.","expected_error":"No mandatory immediate exception; repeated failures can exhaust descriptors or other finite resources.","cause":"Cleanup is located after an operation that can throw.","root_cause":"Resource lifetime is not structurally tied to the owning scope.","solution":"Use try-with-resources for AutoCloseable resources or otherwise guarantee cleanup on every exit path.","edge_case":"Cleanup itself can fail; try-with-resources defines suppression behavior that should be understood when diagnosing close-related failures.","security_implication":"Leaked files, sockets, or other resources can cause availability problems and may retain access to sensitive data longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Leaked resources reduce available capacity and can eventually cause cascading failures.","test_case":"Force process to throw after resource acquisition and verify the resource is closed.","test_input":"Injected processing exception after opening the resource.","expected_test_result":"The resource is closed despite the processing failure.","testing_guidance":"Test success, primary failure, and close failure paths separately.","verification_method":"Control-flow analysis plus resource-lifecycle tests.","verification_guidance":"Inspect ownership and exceptional paths rather than verifying only the successful execution path.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 7+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 7+ for try-with-resources","dependencies":["Java AutoCloseable/Closeable APIs"],"execution_requirements":["JVM","Resource-aware test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"JAVA_RESOURCE_MANAGEMENT_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B017"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Java try-with-resources and AutoCloseable documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B031","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Generator Consumption","concept":"One-shot iterator exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a Python generator produce correct results once and then appear empty on a later operation?","answer":"Generators are iterators with consumable state. Once iteration reaches exhaustion, subsequent iteration over the same generator does not recreate the previously yielded values.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is that the first consumer receives values while a later consumer receives none. The root cause is treating a stateful iterator as if it were a reusable collection.","principle":"Iterator consumption is stateful; reusable data requires a collection or a new iterator.","objective":"Diagnose missing data caused by accidental reuse of an exhausted generator.","requirements":["The data abstraction must match the required reuse semantics."],"constraints":["Materializing a generator changes memory and timing characteristics."],"preconditions":["A generator object is passed to multiple consumers or traversed multiple times."],"input":"items = (load(x) for x in source)\nfirst = list(items)\nsecond = list(items)","output":"The first consumer receives generated values; the second receives an empty sequence.","expected_behavior":"If multiple independent traversals are required, provide a reusable collection or create a fresh iterator.","incorrect_code":"items = (load(x) for x in source)\nprocess_a(items)\nprocess_b(items)","corrected_code":"items = [load(x) for x in source]\nprocess_a(items)\nprocess_b(items)","failure_mode":"Second consumer receives no data.","expected_error":"","cause":"The generator has already been advanced to exhaustion.","root_cause":"The API contract does not distinguish a consumable iterator from reusable collection data.","solution":"Document ownership/consumption semantics, materialize when repeated traversal is required, or create a fresh generator for each traversal.","edge_case":"Materializing a large or infinite stream may be inappropriate; in that case each consumer may need an independently produced stream or a controlled fan-out mechanism.","security_implication":"Exhausted iterators can cause incomplete validation, logging, or security checks if a second pass is assumed to contain the original data.","performance_implication":"Materialization increases memory usage but can avoid repeated expensive generation when multiple passes are required.","test_case":"Consume the iterator once and then attempt a second complete traversal.","test_input":"Finite generator consumed by two independent processing stages.","expected_test_result":"The implementation explicitly provides reusable data or independent iteration according to its contract.","testing_guidance":"Test single-pass and repeated-consumption requirements separately.","verification_method":"Iterator-state analysis plus multi-consumer tests.","verification_guidance":"Identify whether the API promises one-shot or reusable traversal before diagnosing behavior as a defect.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.x interpreter"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PYTHON_ITERATOR_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python iterator and generator documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B032","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Promise State","concept":"Stale state observed after asynchronous scheduling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can JavaScript code read an outdated value after scheduling an asynchronous state update?","answer":"An asynchronous update does not necessarily take effect before subsequent synchronous code executes. Code that assumes immediate visibility of a scheduled update can observe the previous state.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an ordering mismatch between the operation that schedules the update and the operation that reads the state. The root cause is confusing scheduling with immediate state mutation.","principle":"Asynchronous scheduling establishes future work, not immediate completion.","objective":"Diagnose stale state caused by incorrect assumptions about asynchronous ordering.","requirements":["Dependent logic must execute only after the state transition it depends on is actually complete."],"constraints":["Exact scheduling behavior depends on the API and runtime abstraction."],"preconditions":["A state-changing asynchronous operation is followed by immediate dependent logic."],"input":"updateStateAsync(); console.log(state);","output":"The log can observe the previous state.","expected_behavior":"Dependent logic observes the state only after the update contract guarantees completion.","incorrect_code":"saveAsync(data);\nconsole.log(currentData);","corrected_code":"await saveAsync(data);\nconsole.log(currentData);","failure_mode":"Read occurs before asynchronous state transition completes.","expected_error":"","cause":"The caller continues synchronously after scheduling asynchronous work.","root_cause":"The dependency relationship between update completion and state consumption is not represented in control flow.","solution":"Await or otherwise subscribe to the completion signal defined by the API before consuming dependent state.","edge_case":"Some APIs update state through events or external stores rather than returning a Promise; the correct completion signal must come from the API contract.","security_implication":"Stale authorization, configuration, or session state can cause incorrect security decisions.","performance_implication":"Waiting for required completion can increase latency, but racing ahead may create retries, inconsistent writes, or more expensive recovery.","test_case":"Delay completion of the asynchronous update and verify that dependent logic does not run prematurely.","test_input":"Controlled delayed asynchronous update.","expected_test_result":"Dependent logic executes after the documented completion point.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic deferred promises or test hooks rather than relying only on timing sleeps.","verification_method":"Event-order analysis plus controlled asynchronous tests.","verification_guidance":"Trace the actual completion signal instead of inferring ordering from source-code proximity.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2017+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ES2017+ for async/await syntax","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ECMASCRIPT_ASYNCHRONOUS_EXECUTION_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B002"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ECMAScript Promise and asynchronous execution semantics"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B033","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Module Boundaries","concept":"Runtime import/export mismatch hidden by declaration assumptions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can TypeScript compile successfully while a module fails at runtime because import/export behavior differs from the expected module format?","answer":"TypeScript's type checking and emitted module behavior are separate concerns. A source-level import can be type-correct while runtime module resolution, package exports, interop configuration, or emitted module format differs from the assumptions of the execution environment.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a successful type check followed by a runtime module-resolution or export-shape failure. The root cause must be traced through the emitted module format, package metadata, compiler configuration, runtime version, and actual module resolver.","principle":"Compile-time module typing does not guarantee runtime module-resolution compatibility.","objective":"Diagnose runtime import failures caused by module-system configuration mismatch.","requirements":["Compiler, package, and runtime module semantics must agree."],"constraints":["Exact behavior depends on TypeScript configuration, package metadata, runtime, and module system."],"preconditions":["A project mixes or transforms module formats."],"input":"import { feature } from \"pkg\";","output":"Compilation may succeed while runtime resolution fails or produces a different export shape.","expected_behavior":"The emitted module and runtime resolver must resolve the intended export.","incorrect_code":"import { feature } from \"legacy-commonjs-package\";","corrected_code":"import pkg from \"legacy-commonjs-package\";\nconst { feature } = pkg;","failure_mode":"Runtime module resolution or export-shape failure.","expected_error":"Environment-specific module resolution or export access error.","cause":"Type declarations and runtime module behavior do not represent the same interop contract.","root_cause":"Build-time and runtime module semantics are inconsistent.","solution":"Align package metadata, TypeScript module settings, emitted format, runtime resolution, and the package's actual exports.","edge_case":"A package can expose different conditions through package exports, so inspecting only one entry point may not establish runtime behavior for every environment.","security_implication":"Unexpected module resolution can load unintended code or incompatible dependency variants in poorly controlled environments.","performance_implication":"Incorrect module resolution can cause duplicate dependency copies or inefficient loading, depending on the environment.","test_case":"Build the project and execute the emitted artifact under the actual target runtime/module mode.","test_input":"Target package with declared and actual export formats.","expected_test_result":"The runtime resolves the same intended export shape that the application was compiled against.","testing_guidance":"Test the production build rather than only the TypeScript development environment.","verification_method":"Inspect emitted JavaScript, package metadata, resolver behavior, and runtime execution.","verification_guidance":"Record TypeScript version, compiler options, package version, runtime version, and module mode.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"Version-dependent","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Configuration and runtime dependent","dependencies":["Target package","JavaScript runtime/module loader"],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler","Target runtime","Installed dependency package"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MODULE_SYSTEM_AND_TOOLCHAIN_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrast