{"record_id":"D36-B035","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Pointer Ownership","concept":"Double free caused by unclear ownership transfer","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can C code free the same allocation twice when multiple functions believe they own it?","answer":"C has no built-in ownership system for heap allocations. If ownership responsibility is not explicitly defined, two code paths may both release the same allocation, causing undefined behavior.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an allocation with multiple cleanup paths that can both execute. The root cause is ambiguous ownership rather than the free operation itself.","principle":"Every heap allocation must have a clearly defined owner and exactly one responsible release path unless an explicit ownership protocol says otherwise.","objective":"Diagnose double free caused by ambiguous heap ownership.","requirements":["Allocation ownership and release responsibility must be explicit."],"constraints":["Shared access does not imply shared ownership."],"preconditions":["Multiple components can call free on the same pointer."],"input":"char *buffer = malloc(size); function_a(buffer); function_b(buffer);","output":"Both functions may release the same allocation.","expected_behavior":"Exactly one ownership path releases the allocation.","incorrect_code":"void function_a(char *p) {\n    free(p);\n}\n\nvoid function_b(char *p) {\n    free(p);\n}","corrected_code":"void function_a(char *p) {\n    /* borrows p; does not free it */\n    use(p);\n}\n\nvoid function_b(char *p) {\n    use(p);\n}\n\nvoid caller(void) {\n    char *p = malloc(100);\n    function_a(p);\n    function_b(p);\n    free(p);\n}","failure_mode":"Double free or use-after-free after competing cleanup.","expected_error":"No guaranteed runtime error; sanitizers may report allocator misuse.","cause":"Multiple functions assume ownership of the same allocation.","root_cause":"Ownership transfer and release responsibility are not represented in the API contract.","solution":"Define owner/borrower roles explicitly and centralize or transfer ownership deterministically.","edge_case":"A NULL pointer is safe for free, but that does not make repeated release of the same live allocation safe.","security_implication":"Double free is a memory-safety vulnerability and can lead to corruption or exploitable behavior.","performance_implication":"Allocator corruption can cause severe instability; ownership discipline prevents both correctness and allocator-level failures.","test_case":"Exercise all cleanup paths and run under AddressSanitizer or an equivalent allocator diagnostic.","test_input":"Allocation passed through multiple non-owning functions before one owner releases it.","expected_test_result":"Exactly one release occurs and no sanitizer reports allocator misuse.","testing_guidance":"Document ownership in function contracts and test exceptional cleanup paths.","verification_method":"Ownership-flow analysis plus sanitizer-assisted execution.","verification_guidance":"Trace every allocation, ownership transfer, release, and error-path cleanup.","language":"C","language_version":"C11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C11+","dependencies":["C standard allocation APIs"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler","AddressSanitizer or equivalent diagnostic tooling"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"C_MEMORY_MANAGEMENT_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B016","D36-B025"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["ISO C allocation semantics and established memory-ownership engineering practice"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B036","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Exception Safety","concept":"Partial object state after exception during multi-step mutation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a C++ operation leave an object logically inconsistent when an exception occurs halfway through a multi-step update?","answer":"If an operation mutates several related fields and an intermediate operation throws, the object may remain partially updated unless the operation provides an appropriate exception-safety guarantee.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is an object state that satisfies neither the old invariant nor the intended new invariant after an exception. The root cause is mutation without a transactional or rollback-safe state transition.","principle":"Operations that can throw during state mutation must preserve the required exception-safety guarantee.","objective":"Diagnose invariant corruption after partial C++ mutation.","requirements":["Object invariants must hold after both successful and exceptional exits."],"constraints":["Strong exception safety may require temporary state or rollback and can have additional cost."],"preconditions":["Multiple fields are updated and an intermediate operation may throw."],"input":"object.a = newA; operation_that_throws(); object.b = newB;","output":"The object can contain newA with old b after failure.","expected_behavior":"Either the entire operation succeeds or the object's prior invariant remains valid.","incorrect_code":"void update(State& s) {\n    s.a = compute_a();\n    risky_operation();\n    s.b = compute_b();\n}","corrected_code":"void update(State& s) {\n    State candidate = s;\n    candidate.a = compute_a();\n    risky_operation();\n    candidate.b = compute_b();\n    s = std::move(candidate);\n}","failure_mode":"Object invariant violated after exceptional exit.","expected_error":"The intermediate operation throws while the original object has already been partially modified.","cause":"Mutation is applied directly before all potentially throwing operations complete.","root_cause":"The implementation does not provide the required exception-safety guarantee.","solution":"Construct or validate a complete candidate state before committing, or implement a documented rollback strategy.","edge_case":"Copying the entire state may be expensive or impossible for non-copyable resources; a transaction-specific design may be required.","security_implication":"Partial security-policy, permission, or configuration updates can leave the system in an unintended state.","performance_implication":"Copy-and-commit strategies can increase memory and CPU use; the required exception-safety guarantee should drive the design.","test_case":"Inject an exception at every potentially throwing stage and verify the object's invariant afterward.","test_input":"Failures before each state commit point.","expected_test_result":"The object either reaches the complete new state or retains a valid prior state according to the documented guarantee.","testing_guidance":"Test every throwing operation, not merely the first known failure.","verification_method":"Exception-safety analysis plus failure-injection tests.","verification_guidance":"State invariants must be explicitly defined before testing exception behavior.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["C++ compiler","Failure-injection test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CPLUSPLUS_EXCEPTION_SAFETY","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B025"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["C++ exception-safety principles and standard library exception guarantees"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B037","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Async Cancellation","concept":"Cancellation-unsafe partial state transition","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can cancellation of a Rust asynchronous operation leave application state partially updated?","answer":"Cancellation can stop an asynchronous future before it completes. If state mutation is performed incrementally across await points without preserving an invariant, cancellation can leave partially applied state.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a state transition that can be interrupted between logically dependent mutations. The root cause is treating an async operation as atomic when its future can be dropped before completion.","principle":"Cancellation boundaries must be considered part of asynchronous state-machine design.","objective":"Diagnose inconsistent state after cancellation of an asynchronous operation.","requirements":["Every cancellation point must leave state in a valid invariant."],"constraints":["Exact cancellation semantics depend on the async runtime and operation design."],"preconditions":["An async operation mutates shared or persistent state across await points."],"input":"state.a = new_a;\nawait external_step();\nstate.b = new_b;","output":"Cancellation during external_step can leave a updated while b remains old.","expected_behavior":"Cancellation leaves a valid state or the operation uses transactional/rollback semantics.","incorrect_code":"async fn update(state: &mut State) {\n    state.a = new_a();\n    external_step().await;\n    state.b = new_b();\n}","corrected_code":"async fn update(state: &mut State) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    let candidate = prepare_state().await?;\n    commit_state(state, candidate);\n    Ok(())\n}","failure_mode":"Partial state mutation after cancellation.","expected_error":"No universal exception; the future may be dropped or cancellation may be propagated by the runtime.","cause":"State mutation occurs before an await that can prevent completion.","root_cause":"The implementation does not define a valid invariant at cancellation boundaries.","solution":"Prepare state before commit, use transactional semantics, or explicitly design every cancellation boundary to preserve invariants.","edge_case":"Some external operations cannot be rolled back; compensating actions or durable state machines may be required.","security_implication":"Partially applied authorization or policy changes can produce inconsistent security state.","performance_implication":"Transactional preparation can require additional memory or I/O but prevents costly recovery from inconsistent state.","test_case":"Inject cancellation at each await boundary and verify state invariants.","test_input":"Cancellation before, during, and after external asynchronous operations.","expected_test_result":"Every cancellation point leaves a valid state according to the documented contract.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic cancellation hooks rather than timing-dependent cancellation tests.","verification_method":"Async state-machine analysis plus controlled cancellation testing.","verification_guidance":"Record which operations are rollbackable, compensatable, or irreversible.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Async runtime dependent","dependencies":["Asynchronous runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler","Target async runtime","Deterministic cancellation test harness"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ASYNC_STATE_MACHINE_AND_CANCELLATION_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B016","D36-B026"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Rust async/future semantics and target runtime cancellation documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B038","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go HTTP Resource Management","concept":"Response-body leak causing connection-pool exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can failing to close a Go HTTP response body cause requests to degrade or eventually fail under load?","answer":"An HTTP response body represents a resource whose lifecycle must be completed according to the HTTP client's contract. Failing to close it can prevent connection reuse or retain resources, eventually exhausting available connections or file descriptors.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is increasing connection or descriptor usage correlated with requests whose response bodies are not closed. The root cause is incomplete response-resource lifecycle management.","principle":"Network resources must be released according to the client library's lifecycle contract even when the response status indicates failure.","objective":"Diagnose HTTP resource exhaustion caused by an unclosed response body.","requirements":["Every obtained response body must be closed according to the Go HTTP client contract."],"constraints":["Connection reuse behavior depends on how the response body is consumed and the client implementation."],"preconditions":["Code performs HTTP requests and receives response bodies."],"input":"resp, err := client.Do(req)\nif err != nil { return err }\nreturn process(resp)","output":"The response body may remain open after processing.","expected_behavior":"The body is closed on all relevant paths after the response is obtained.","incorrect_code":"resp, err := client.Do(req)\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\nreturn process(resp)","corrected_code":"resp, err := client.Do(req)\nif err != nil {\n    return err\n}\ndefer resp.Body.Close()\nreturn process(resp)","failure_mode":"HTTP resource leak and degraded connection reuse.","expected_error":"Under sustained load, the application may experience connection or descriptor exhaustion or increased latency.","cause":"Response.Body.Close is not guaranteed after a successful HTTP request.","root_cause":"The response resource lifetime is not tied to the request-processing scope.","solution":"Close the response body on every successful response path and verify client-specific reuse requirements.","edge_case":"A caller that transfers response-body ownership must document and enforce the transfer contract instead of closing it prematurely.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can create denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implication":"Failure to release response resources can reduce connection reuse, increase connection creation, and increase latency.","test_case":"Perform repeated requests while monitoring open connections and descriptors and verify cleanup after each response.","test_input":"High-volume HTTP requests including error-status responses.","expected_test_result":"Resource usage remains bounded according to the client and server configuration.","testing_guidance":"Test success, non-success status, processing failure, timeout, and cancellation paths.","verification_method":"Resource lifecycle analysis plus load/resource monitoring.","verification_guidance":"Interpret connection metrics together with client configuration and server behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.x","dependencies":["net/http"],"execution_requirements":["Go runtime","HTTP test server","Resource monitoring"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"GO_NET_HTTP_RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B017","D36-B030"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Go net/http Client and Response.Body documentation"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B039","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Cross-Platform Path Semantics","concept":"Platform-dependent path handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can manually constructed filesystem paths work on one operating system and fail on another?","answer":"Filesystem path syntax and separators are platform-dependent. Concatenating paths with hard-coded separators or assumptions about drive roots, case sensitivity, or path normalization can produce invalid or unintended paths on another platform.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a path that differs between the developer environment and the target environment. The root cause is encoding platform-specific path semantics directly into application logic instead of using the platform's path abstraction.","principle":"Filesystem paths should be constructed and normalized through the target language or platform's path abstraction.","objective":"Diagnose cross-platform file-access failures caused by manual path construction.","requirements":["Path construction must respect the target operating system's filesystem semantics."],"constraints":["Filesystem case sensitivity and path rules vary by platform and filesystem.","],"preconditions":["Application constructs filesystem paths manually using string concatenation."],"input":"path = base + \"/\" + filename","output":"The path can behave incorrectly on platforms with different path conventions or normalization requirements.","expected_behavior":"The application constructs paths using the platform-aware path API.","incorrect_code":"path = base + \"/\" + filename","corrected_code":"from pathlib import Path\npath = Path(base) / filename","failure_mode":"File-not-found, incorrect path resolution, or unintended path interpretation.","expected_error":"Environment-specific filesystem error such as FileNotFoundError.","cause":"Platform-specific path assumptions are encoded as string operations.","root_cause":"The application treats filesystem paths as ordinary strings rather than structured platform-dependent paths.","solution":"Use the language's path abstraction and validate path semantics at trust boundaries.","edge_case":"A path string intended for a remote protocol may require protocol-specific rules rather than local filesystem path handling.","security_implication":"Incorrect normalization can contribute to path traversal or access-control failures when paths are derived from untrusted input.","performance_implication":"Path abstraction overhead is generally minor; repeated filesystem operations dominate typical performance costs.","test_case":"Run path construction tests across the target operating systems and test absolute, relative, empty, nested, and unusual path components.","test_input":"Equivalent logical paths under multiple platform path conventions.","expected_test_result":"The intended filesystem location is resolved consistently according to each platform's documented semantics.","testing_guidance":"Include platform-specific CI when cross-platform support is a requirement.","verification_method":"Path API inspection plus multi-platform integration tests.","verification_guidance":"Do not assume one operating system's path behavior represents all deployment targets.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":["pathlib"],"execution_requirements":["Target operating systems/filesystems"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PLATFORM_FILESYSTEM_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B020"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python pathlib documentation and operating-system filesystem path semantics"],"created_at":"