{"record_id":"D36-B040","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Configuration and Defaults","concept":"Silent fallback to an unsafe default after configuration failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can silently replacing an invalid configuration value with a default create a defect that is difficult to diagnose?","answer":"A fallback can hide the original configuration failure and cause the application to operate under assumptions that were never explicitly approved. The root cause is treating invalid or missing configuration as equivalent to an intentional default.","explanation":"The diagnostic evidence is a mismatch between effective configuration and the expected deployment contract combined with absence of an explicit configuration error. The correct diagnosis distinguishes an optional default from an invalid configuration that should fail fast.","principle":"Defaults should be explicit and semantically safe; invalid required configuration should not silently become a different valid configuration.","objective":"Diagnose behavior caused by silent configuration fallback.","requirements":["Required configuration must be validated and invalid values must produce actionable diagnostics."],"constraints":["Some settings legitimately have documented safe defaults."],"preconditions":["Application uses fallback behavior when configuration is missing or malformed."],"input":"timeout = int(os.getenv(\"TIMEOUT\", \"30\"))","output":"A missing setting silently activates 30 seconds regardless of deployment expectations.","expected_behavior":"The application either uses an explicitly documented default or fails validation when the setting is required.","incorrect_code":"timeout = int(os.getenv(\"TIMEOUT\", \"30\"))","corrected_code":"raw_timeout = os.getenv(\"TIMEOUT\")\nif raw_timeout is None:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"TIMEOUT is required\")\ntry:\n    timeout = int(raw_timeout)\nexcept ValueError as exc:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"TIMEOUT must be an integer\") from exc\nif timeout <= 0:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"TIMEOUT must be positive\")","failure_mode":"Unexpected behavior caused by hidden configuration substitution.","expected_error":"The original deployment/configuration defect is suppressed.","cause":"Missing or invalid configuration is converted into a fallback value without explicit policy.","root_cause":"Configuration validation does not distinguish absent optional settings from required configuration failures.","solution":"Define configuration contracts, validate required settings at startup, and document intentional defaults.","edge_case":"A default can be correct when it is part of the documented contract and remains safe under all supported environments.","security_implication":"Unsafe defaults can disable security controls, weaken timeouts, expose services, or change authorization behavior.","performance_implication":"Incorrect defaults can cause excessive resource use, connection retention, queue growth, or latency.","test_case":"Test missing, malformed, zero, negative, boundary, and valid configuration values.","test_input":"Multiple configuration states.","expected_test_result":"Invalid required configuration fails explicitly; documented optional defaults behave as specified.","testing_guidance":"Test startup validation separately from runtime configuration changes if dynamic configuration is supported.","verification_method":"Configuration contract analysis plus startup and deployment tests.","verification_guidance":"Record the intended default policy as evidence before classifying fallback as a defect.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":["Environment/configuration system"],"execution_requirements":["Python runtime","Controlled configuration environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONFIGURATION_CONTRACT_SEMANTICS","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D36-B020"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":["Python environment-variable semantics and configuration-management engineering principles"],"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B041","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Concurrency","concept":"Deadlock caused by inconsistent lock ordering","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can two C++ threads deadlock even when each mutex is individually used correctly?","answer":"A deadlock can occur when different execution paths acquire the same multiple mutexes in different orders. Each thread can hold one mutex while waiting indefinitely for the mutex held by the other.","explanation":"Mutex correctness is not sufficient when multiple locks participate in one operation. If thread A acquires mutex_a then waits for mutex_b while thread B acquires mutex_b then waits for mutex_a, neither thread can progress. The failure is a lock-ordering violation rather than an individual mutex API failure.","principle":"Concurrent code that acquires multiple locks should establish and consistently follow a global lock-ordering rule.","objective":"Identify and repair deadlocks caused by inconsistent acquisition order.","requirements":"All paths that acquire the same lock set must use a consistent ordering policy.","constraints":"The example is illustrative and has not been executed in this record.","preconditions":"Two shared mutexes exist and multiple threads can execute operations requiring both.","input":"Two functions acquire mutex_a and mutex_b in opposite orders.","output":"A deterministic lock-ordering strategy that prevents cyclic waiting.","expected_behavior":"Threads may wait for locks, but the wait-for graph must not contain a cycle.","incorrect_code":"std::mutex mutex_a;\nstd::mutex mutex_b;\n\nvoid first() {\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> a(mutex_a);\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> b(mutex_b);\n}\n\nvoid second() {\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> b(mutex_b);\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> a(mutex_a);\n}","corrected_code":"std::mutex mutex_a;\nstd::mutex mutex_b;\n\nvoid first() {\n    std::scoped_lock lock(mutex_a, mutex_b);\n}\n\nvoid second() {\n    std::scoped_lock lock(mutex_a, mutex_b);\n}","failure_mode":"Deadlock / cyclic lock dependency","expected_error":"No language-level exception is required; affected threads can block indefinitely.","cause":"Different code paths acquire multiple mutexes in incompatible orders.","root_cause":"No single lock acquisition ordering or atomic multi-lock acquisition strategy was enforced.","solution":"Use std::scoped_lock for simultaneous acquisition where appropriate, or establish and enforce a global lock ordering.","edge_case":"A future code path that introduces another mutex can reintroduce the cycle if it violates the established ordering.","security_implication":"Deadlocks can become a denial-of-service condition when externally reachable operations consume worker threads or critical resources.","performance_implication":"Blocked threads consume scheduling and synchronization resources and can reduce throughput to zero for affected operations.","test_case":"Run concurrent operations that exercise every lock acquisition path and verify completion within a bounded timeout.","test_input":"Multiple threads repeatedly call first() and second().","expected_test_result":"All operations complete without indefinite blocking.","testing_guidance":"Use stress tests and a bounded completion timeout; inspect thread stacks when a timeout occurs.","verification_method":"Review every path acquiring the involved locks and verify a consistent order or safe multi-lock acquisition mechanism.","verification_guidance":"Construct a lock dependency graph and confirm that it contains no cycle.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17 or later","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++17+","dependencies":"C++ standard library","execution_requirements":"C++17 compiler and multithreaded runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.98,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ standard library synchronization facilities and established concurrency deadlock principles.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B042","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Concurrency","concept":"Race condition in check-then-act shared state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is a check followed by an update unsafe when multiple Java threads access the same mutable state?","answer":"The check and subsequent update can be interleaved with another thread's operation, allowing both threads to observe the same old state and perform an action that should have occurred only once.","explanation":"A sequence such as if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k,v) is not automatically atomic merely because each individual operation is thread-safe or executes without memory corruption. The correctness requirement applies to the combined check-and-act operation.","principle":"Atomicity must cover the complete logical invariant, not merely individual operations.","objective":"Detect and repair check-then-act races in shared Java state.","requirements":"The invariant must remain true under concurrent execution.","constraints":"The example assumes multiple threads share the same map.","preconditions":"At least two threads can execute the same logical check-and-update operation concurrently.","input":"Concurrent threads test whether a key exists and then insert it.","output":"An atomic operation that preserves the intended uniqueness invariant.","expected_behavior":"Only one logical initialization occurs for a key that must be initialized once.","incorrect_code":"if (!map.containsKey(key)) {\n    map.put(key, createValue());\n}","corrected_code":"map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> createValue());","failure_mode":"Race condition / lost invariant","expected_error":"Usually no exception; incorrect application state can result.","cause":"Another thread can modify the shared state between the check and the action.","root_cause":"The logical operation was split into separately interleavable steps without synchronization or an atomic map operation.","solution":"Use an atomic concurrent-map operation such as computeIfAbsent when its semantics match the requirement, or synchronize the complete critical section.","edge_case":"If value creation has external side effects, computeIfAbsent semantics and possible concurrent behavior must be considered before treating it as equivalent to arbitrary synchronized code.","security_implication":"Race conditions around authorization, quotas, uniqueness, or resource allocation can create security-relevant invariant violations.","performance_implication":"Overly broad synchronization can reduce concurrency; atomic concurrent collections can preserve more parallelism when applicable.","test_case":"Run many concurrent initializations for the same key and verify the invariant and resulting state.","test_input":"Hundreds of concurrent calls targeting the same absent key.","expected_test_result":"The final state satisfies the single-initialization invariant and no inconsistent state is observed.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated stress runs with synchronization barriers to increase the probability of problematic interleavings.","verification_method":"Identify the invariant, enumerate interleavings between check and action, and verify that the selected operation is atomic for that invariant.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer correctness merely because the collection itself is thread-safe.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 8+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":"Java Collections Framework / java.util.concurrent when applicable","execution_requirements":"Java runtime with multithreaded execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_AND_LIBRARY_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.98,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[