{"record_id":"D36-B043","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Concurrency","concept":"Goroutine leak caused by blocked send without cancellation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a Go goroutine remain permanently blocked when sending to a channel?","answer":"A goroutine can leak when it performs a blocking channel send and no receiver remains available, especially when the producer has no cancellation or shutdown path.","explanation":"Goroutines are lightweight but are not automatically reclaimed while blocked. A producer that sends indefinitely to a channel whose consumer has stopped can remain blocked and retain its stack and referenced resources.","principle":"Long-lived goroutines performing blocking operations should have an explicit lifecycle and cancellation strategy.","objective":"Detect and prevent goroutine leaks caused by abandoned channel operations.","requirements":"Producer and consumer lifecycles must be coordinated.","constraints":"The example focuses on cancellation rather than arbitrary channel ownership designs.","preconditions":"A goroutine can outlive or race with its channel consumer.","input":"A producer attempts a channel send while the consumer may terminate.","output":"A cancellation-aware send that terminates when the operation is no longer needed.","expected_behavior":"The goroutine either delivers the value or exits when cancellation occurs.","incorrect_code":"func produce(ch chan<- int) {\n    for i := 0; ; i++ {\n        ch <- i\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"func produce(ctx context.Context, ch chan<- int) {\n    for i := 0; ; i++ {\n        select {\n        case ch <- i:\n        case <-ctx.Done():\n            return\n        }\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Goroutine leak / blocked channel operation","expected_error":"No required runtime error; the goroutine can remain blocked indefinitely.","cause":"The send has no alternative cancellation or shutdown path.","root_cause":"Goroutine lifetime was not coupled to the lifecycle of the operation consuming its output.","solution":"Use context cancellation or another explicit shutdown protocol around potentially blocking operations.","edge_case":"Cancellation may occur immediately before or after a value becomes sendable; callers should treat cancellation as lifecycle control rather than assuming a specific select branch.","security_implication":"Leaked goroutines can retain sensitive data or resources longer than intended and can contribute to resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Repeated leaks increase goroutine count and memory usage and can eventually degrade or exhaust process resources.","test_case":"Start producers, cancel their context while consumers stop, and verify that producer goroutines terminate.","test_input":"Context cancellation with no active receiver.","expected_test_result":"All producer goroutines exit within a bounded interval.","testing_guidance":"Use goroutine-count instrumentation or leak-detection tooling in repeated tests.","verification_method":"Trace every blocking operation in the goroutine and identify the event that guarantees termination.","verification_guidance":"A shutdown test should cover consumer termination before producer termination.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.20+","dependencies":"Go standard library context and channels","execution_requirements":"Go runtime with concurrent goroutine execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Go language specification and official context/channel documentation; established goroutin