{"record_id":"D36-B044","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Integer Semantics","concept":"Integer overflow behavior differs by build profile","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can integer-overflow behavior appear different between Rust debug and release builds?","answer":"Rust arithmetic overflow checking depends on compilation settings and context. In ordinary integer operations, debug-oriented builds commonly check overflow and can panic, while optimized release builds generally use wrapping behavior unless overflow checks are explicitly enabled.","explanation":"Code that implicitly relies on one overflow behavior can therefore appear correct during one build configuration and behave differently in another. The root problem is relying on unspecified project-level assumptions rather than choosing explicit checked, saturating, or wrapping semantics.","principle":"When overflow semantics matter, express them explicitly rather than depending on build-profile defaults.","objective":"Identify arithmetic bugs caused by implicit overflow assumptions.","requirements":"The intended overflow behavior must be explicit and consistent across supported builds.","constraints":"Exact behavior can depend on compiler settings and operation context.","preconditions":"An integer calculation can exceed the representable range.","input":"An arithmetic operation whose result may overflow its integer type.","output":"Explicit checked, saturating, or wrapping behavior matching the domain requirement.","expected_behavior":"Overflow follows an intentionally selected semantic rather than an accidental build configuration.","incorrect_code":"let total = count * size;","corrected_code":"let total = count.checked_mul(size).ok_or(Error::Overflow)?;","failure_mode":"Arithmetic overflow / build-profile-dependent behavior","expected_error":"A checked overflow may panic in configurations where overflow checks are enabled; unchecked behavior can wrap according to Rust integer semantics.","cause":"The calculation relies on implicit overflow behavior.","root_cause":"The implementation does not encode what should happen when the mathematical result exceeds the integer range.","solution":"Use checked_mul, saturating_mul, wrapping_mul, or explicit wider arithmetic according to the domain invariant.","edge_case":"Boundary values near the maximum or minimum representable integer can expose the bug even when normal inputs never do.","security_implication":"Overflow in sizes, offsets, lengths, quotas, or resource calculations can cause incorrect bounds or allocation decisions.","performance_implication":"Checked operations add semantics that must be considered in hot paths, but correctness should take precedence where overflow is possible.","test_case":"Test values at, below, and above the maximum safe multiplication threshold under all supported build profiles.","test_input":"count and size values whose mathematical product exceeds the target integer type.","expected_test_result":"The program follows the explicitly documented overflow policy consistently.","testing_guidance":"Run debug and release configurations and include boundary-focused arithmetic tests.","verification_method":"Identify the mathematical range of every operand and verify the chosen overflow policy against the domain invariant.","verification_guidance":"Do not classify release-build wrapping as a bug when wrapping is explicitly required by the domain.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.70+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust stable","dependencies":"Rust standard library","execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain with debug and release build configurations","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_AND_TOOLCHAIN_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.97,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[]