{"record_id":"D36-B045","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Runtime Safety","concept":"any boundary hides invalid runtime data","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can TypeScript code compile successfully while failing at runtime because a value is not actually the declared type?","answer":"The any type disables substantial static checking at the boundary where it is introduced. If an external value is treated as a trusted object without runtime validation, TypeScript may accept operations that fail when the actual JavaScript value does not satisfy the assumed structure.","explanation":"TypeScript types are primarily compile-time constructs and do not automatically validate JSON, network responses, database results, or other runtime data. Casting or propagating any can conceal a contract violation until an operation requires a property or method that is absent or has the wrong type.","principle":"Untrusted runtime data should cross into typed application logic through explicit validation rather than unchecked assertions or any.","objective":"Diagnose runtime failures caused by unsafely typed external data.","requirements":"External data must be validated before being used as a trusted domain object.","constraints":"The example does not depend on a particular validation library.","preconditions":"A runtime source can provide malformed or unexpected data.","input":"An external object assumed to contain a numeric user field.","output":"A validated value with a trustworthy runtime shape.","expected_behavior":"Invalid input is rejected before typed business logic consumes it.","incorrect_code":"const payload: any = JSON.parse(raw);\nconst total = payload.price * payload.quantity;","corrected_code":"type OrderPayload = { price: number; quantity: number };\n\nfunction isOrderPayload(value: unknown): value is OrderPayload {\n    if (typeof value !== \"object\" || value === null) return false;\n    const v = value as Record<string, unknown>;\n    return typeof v.price === \"number\" && typeof v.quantity === \"number\";\n}\n\nconst payload: unknown = JSON.parse(raw);\nif (!isOrderPayload(payload)) throw new Error(\"Invalid order payload\");\nconst total = payload.price * payload.quantity;","failure_mode":"Runtime type contract violation","expected_error":"Possible TypeError, NaN, or incorrect application behavior depending on the malformed values.","cause":"Untrusted runtime data was treated as statically trusted data.","root_cause":"The type boundary did not enforce a runtime validation step.","solution":"Use unknown at untrusted boundaries and validate the actual runtime structure before narrowing to a domain type.","edge_case":"An object may contain correctly typed top-level fields but invalid nested structures, requiring recursive validation.","security_implication":"Unchecked external data can bypass validation assumptions and contribute to authorization, injection, business-logic, or data-integrity vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Runtime validation has a cost, but selective boundary validation prevents repeated downstream defensive checks and failed operations.","test_case":"Supply valid, missing-field, null, wrong-type, and nested-malformed payloads.","test_input":"JSON objects with price and quantity missing, null, strings, arrays, and valid numbers.","expected_test_result":"Only structurally valid payloads reach the arithmetic operation.","testing_guidance":"Test the validator independently and include adversarial malformed runtime inputs.","verification_method":"Trace every external-data boundary and verify that no unchecked any or assertion bypasses runtime validation.","verification_guidance":"Static compilation alone does not verify runtime data contracts.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":"TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime","execution_requirements":"TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime capable of JSON parsing","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TYPE_SYSTEM_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"TypeScript Handbook documentation on any, unknown, narrowing, and type erasure; ECMAScript JSON behavior.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B046","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Numeric Semantics","concept":"Floating-point equality failure from binary representation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can two JavaScript arithmetic expressions that are mathematically equal compare as unequal?","answer":"JavaScript Number uses IEEE 754 double-precision binary floating-point representation, so many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly. Independent arithmetic can therefore produce slightly different representable values.","explanation":"A direct equality comparison assumes exact representation. For calculations involving fractional decimal values, accumulated representation and rounding can make an expression such as 0.1 + 0.2 differ from the representable value of 0.3.","principle":"Floating-point results should be compared according to the numerical tolerance or representation requirements of the domain.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect equality checks involving floating-point calculations.","requirements":"The comparison method must match the required numerical precision.","constraints":"Tolerance selection is domain-specific and should not be treated as universally correct.","preconditions":"The program performs arithmetic using JavaScript Number values representing fractional quantities.","input":"Two independently computed floating-point values expected to be mathematically equal.","output":"A comparison strategy appropriate to the domain precision.","expected_behavior":"Values that differ only within the documented numerical tolerance are treated according to the domain rule.","incorrect_code":"if (0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3) {\n    process();\n}","corrected_code":"const actual = 0.1 + 0.2;\nconst expected = 0.3;\nconst tolerance = Number.EPSILON * Math.max(1, Math.abs(actual), Math.abs(expected));\n\nif (Math.abs(actual - expected) <= tolerance) {\n    process();\n}","failure_mode":"Floating-point precision / exact-equality failure","expected_error":"Usually no exception; the comparison evaluates to false despite mathematical expectations.","cause":"Decimal fractions are represented approximately in binary floating point.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes mathematical decimal equality from binary floating-point representation.","solution":"Use a domain-appropriate tolerance or a decimal/integer representation when exact decimal semantics are required.","edge_case":"For large magnitudes, a fixed epsilon can be inappropriate; relative and absolute tolerances may need domain-specific design.","security_implication":"Incorrect monetary, quota, threshold, or authorization calculations can create business-logic inconsistencies.","performance_implication":"Tolerance checks are inexpensive; specialized decimal arithmetic can have different performance characteristics.","test_case":"Test decimal fractions, values near zero, large magnitudes, and values around the accepted tolerance.","test_input":"0.1 + 0.2 compared with 0.3.","expected_test_result":"The comparison follows the explicitly defined numerical tolerance policy.","testing_guidance":"Avoid asserting exact equality for floating-point results unless the operation and representation guarantee it.","verification_method":"Identify the numeric representation, rounding behavior, and domain precision requirement before selecting the comparison strategy.","verification_guidance":"A tolerance is not automatically correct; its magnitude must be justified by the domain.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ECMAScript 2023+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ECMAScript 2015+","dependencies":"ECMAScript Number","execution_requirements":"JavaScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NUMERIC_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"spe