{"record_id":"D36-B049","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Type Conversion","concept":"Signed and unsigned conversion corrupts bounds logic","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can mixing signed and unsigned integers make a C++ bounds check behave incorrectly?","answer":"Usual arithmetic conversions can convert a signed value to an unsigned type when operands of different integer categories are compared or combined. A negative signed value can therefore become a large unsigned value and invalidate an intended bounds check.","explanation":"A condition such as index < container.size() may have surprising semantics when index is signed and container.size() is unsigned. If index is negative, the conversion can transform it into a large unsigned value before comparison.","principle":"Index and size comparisons should use compatible integer representations and explicitly handle negative values.","objective":"Detect bounds-check bugs caused by implicit signed-to-unsigned conversion.","requirements":"Negative indices must be rejected before conversion, or the program must use an intentionally compatible representation.","constraints":"Exact conversion rules depend on the operand types and their ranks.","preconditions":"A signed integer can contain negative values and is compared with an unsigned size.","input":"A negative signed index compared with an unsigned container size.","output":"A bounds check that explicitly rejects invalid negative values.","expected_behavior":"Negative indices are rejected before any unsigned conversion can obscure the invalid state.","incorrect_code":"int index = get_index();\nif (index < values.size()) {\n    use(values[index]);\n}","corrected_code":"int index = get_index();\nif (index >= 0 && static_cast<std::size_t>(index) < values.size()) {\n    use(values[static_cast<std::size_t>(index)]);\n}","failure_mode":"Incorrect bounds validation / implicit integer conversion","expected_error":"Possible out-of-bounds access or incorrect branch behavior; no required runtime exception exists for built-in indexing.","cause":"The signed operand is converted to an unsigned type during comparison.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes mathematical signed comparison semantics while relying on mixed integer types.","solution":"Reject negative values before explicit conversion and then compare compatible unsigned types, or use a consistently signed representation where appropriate.","edge_case":"The minimum representable signed integer can require care when performing arithmetic before conversion.","security_implication":"Incorrect bounds checks can become memory-safety vulnerabilities when attacker-controlled indices reach native memory operations.","performance_implication":"Explicit conversion and range checks are generally inexpensive relative to the safety gained.","test_case":"Test negative, zero, maximum valid, and first invalid indices.","test_input":"index values -1, 0, last valid index, and values.size().","expected_test_result":"Negative and out-of-range indices are rejected; valid indices are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Enable compiler warnings for sign-conversion issues and include boundary-focused tests.","verification_method":"Determine the exact static types of every operand and apply the C++ standard's usual arithmetic conversion rules before reasoning about the comparison.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer comparison behavior from source-level signedness alone; inspect the converted common type.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17 or later","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":"C++ standard language rules","execution_requirements":"C++ compiler with warning support","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"exte