{"record_id":"D36-B050","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Time Semantics","concept":"Using LocalDateTime when an absolute instant is required","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can using Java LocalDateTime for an event timestamp produce incorrect time ordering or interpretation?","answer":"LocalDateTime represents a date and time without a time-zone or UTC offset. It cannot uniquely identify an instant on the global timeline, so using it for events that cross time zones can cause ambiguous or incorrect temporal interpretation.","explanation":"A timestamp such as 2026-09-19T10:00 has no information about whether it represents UTC, UTC+5, UTC-4, or another zone. For an absolute event occurrence, Instant or an offset-aware representation is generally required. LocalDateTime is appropriate when the business meaning is deliberately zone-free or when a zone is supplied separately as part of the domain model.","principle":"Choose a Java time type according to the semantic meaning of the timestamp rather than its visual format.","objective":"Diagnose temporal bugs caused by confusing local calendar time with an absolute instant.","requirements":"The time representation must preserve every piece of temporal information required by the business invariant.","constraints":"Some scheduling and business-calendar concepts intentionally require LocalDateTime plus an associated ZoneId.","preconditions":"An event is persisted, compared, or transmitted across systems or time zones.","input":"A zone-less LocalDateTime used as if it represented an absolute event occurrence.","output":"An appropriate temporal representation such as Instant or an explicit offset/zone model.","expected_behavior":"The system preserves the intended temporal meaning across serialization, storage, comparison, and time-zone conversion.","incorrect_code":"LocalDateTime eventTime = LocalDateTime.now();\nstoreTimestamp(eventTime);","corrected_code":"Instant eventTime = Instant.now();\nstoreTimestamp(eventTime);","failure_mode":"Temporal semantic mismatch","expected_error":"Usually no runtime exception; the system can silently record or interpret an incorrect instant.","cause":"A zone-less local date-time was treated as a globally ordered timestamp.","root_cause":"The data model does not distinguish local calendar time from an absolute instant.","solution":"Use Instant for absolute event timestamps, OffsetDateTime when the offset is part of the required representation, or LocalDateTime with an explicit ZoneId when the domain is zone-aware scheduling.","edge_case":"Daylight-saving transitions can create nonexistent or repeated local times, making local-time scheduling require explicit zone rules.","security_implication":"Incorrect temporal interpretation can affect expiry, token validity, audit ordering, retention, authorization windows, and fraud controls.","performance_implication":"The primary risk is correctness rather than computational cost; repeated unnecessary conversions can also complicate processing.","test_case":"Create events around time-zone changes and daylight-saving transitions and verify ordering and round-trip conversion.","test_input":"The same local date-time interpreted in multiple zones and events surrounding a DST transition.","expected_test_result":"Absolute events retain their intended instant and local schedules follow the specified zone rules.","testing_guidance":"Test serialization, database persistence, cross-zone conversion, DST boundaries, and clock-dependent logic.","verification_method":"Document whether each timestamp represents an instant, local calendar time, offset date-time, or zoned schedule, then verify the Java type matches that semantic contract.","verification_guidance":"Review database and API schemas as well as Java types because a correct in-memory type can still be lost during serialization.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+"