{"record_id":"D36-B053","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript API Contracts","concept":"Optional property treated as definitely present","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can TypeScript code fail when an optional property is used without handling its absence?","answer":"An optional property may be undefined at runtime. Static configuration and control-flow settings can expose this possibility, but unsafe assertions or assumptions can still allow code to reach operations that require a defined value.","explanation":"An API type such as { token?: string } explicitly permits the property to be absent. Treating token as always present creates a contract mismatch between the producer and consumer.","principle":"Optionality in a type is part of the data contract and must be resolved before use.","objective":"Detect failures caused by treating optional TypeScript data as mandatory.","requirements":"Consumers must either handle absence or establish a validated invariant before accessing the value.","constraints":"The appropriate fallback or error behavior is domain-specific.","preconditions":"A value can legitimately omit a property.","input":"An object with an optional string property consumed as though the property is always defined.","output":"A narrowed value or explicit missing-value handling.","expected_behavior":"Missing values produce the documented fallback or error rather than an unexpected runtime failure.","incorrect_code":"type Config = { endpoint?: string };\n\nfunction connect(config: Config) {\n    return new URL(config.endpoint).href;\n}","corrected_code":"type Config = { endpoint?: string };\n\nfunction connect(config: Config) {\n    if (!config.endpoint) {\n        throw new Error(\"Missing endpoint\");\n    }\n    return new URL(config.endpoint).href;\n}","failure_mode":"Optional-value contract violation","expected_error":"A runtime TypeError or URL-construction