{"record_id":"D36-B054","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Resource Management","concept":"Unclosed Java resource causes resource exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can failing to close a Java file or stream eventually cause failures even when individual operations appear correct?","answer":"Resources such as files, sockets, and streams can hold operating-system or external resources. If they are not reliably closed on every path, repeated operations can exhaust descriptors, connections, or related resources.","explanation":"Garbage collection does not provide a deterministic resource-lifecycle guarantee. Exceptions and early returns make manual close logic especially error-prone. try-with-resources establishes deterministic cleanup for AutoCloseable resources.","principle":"Resource ownership should have deterministic cleanup at the narrowest appropriate lifecycle boundary.","objective":"Diagnose resource leaks caused by incomplete Java cleanup paths.","requirements":"Every acquired closeable resource must be closed even when processing fails.","constraints":"Some resources have externally managed lifecycles and should not be closed by every consumer.","preconditions":"The code acquires an AutoCloseable resource under application control.","input":"Resource acquisition followed by operations that can throw exceptions.","output":"A try-with-resources implementation with deterministic cleanup.","expected_behavior":"The resource is closed on normal completion and exceptional exit.","incorrect_code":"InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(path);\nprocess(in);","corrected_code":"try (InputStream in = Files.newInputStream(path)) {\n    process(in);\n}","failure_mode":"Resource leak","expected_error":"Possible Too many open files, connection exhaustion, or other resource-limit failures after repeated operations.","cause":"The resource is not guaranteed to be closed on every control-flow path.","root_cause":"Resource lifetime is not encoded in structured cleanup.","solution":"Use try-with-resources for resources implementing AutoCloseable when the current scope owns them.","edge_case":"Multiple resources are closed in reverse initialization order by try-with-resources; cleanup exceptions and ownership transfer should still be considered explicitly.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion can become a denial-of-service condition, and leaked resources can extend access to sensitive external systems.","performance_implication":"Leaked resources reduce available capacity and can degrade throughput before causing hard exhaustion.","test_case":"Repeat resource acquisition under both successful and exceptional processing paths and inspect resource usage.","test_input":"Large repeated workload with injected processing failures.","expected_test_result":"Resource usage returns to the expected baseline after operations complete.","testing_guidance":"Use operating-system descriptor monitoring, connection-pool metrics, or leak-detection tooling where appropriate.","verification_method":"Identify every resource acquisition and prove that ownership is either transferred explicitly or closed on all paths.","verification_guidance":"Garbage collection should not be treated as deterministic resource cleanup.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 7+","dependencies":"Java AutoCloseable and java.io/java.nio APIs","execution_requirements":"Java runtime and operating-system resource monitoring for full validation","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_AND_LIBRARY_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java Language and API documentation for try-with-resources and AutoCloseable; established resource-management principles.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B055","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Memory Management","concept":"Buffer size calculated from element count incorrectly","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can using an element count instead of byte size cause a C buffer overflow?","answer":"Memory allocation and copying APIs that operate in bytes require the total byte count, not merely the number of logical elements. Allocating or copying only the element count can under-allocate or under-copy when elements are larger than one byte.","explanation":"For an array of n objects of size sizeof(element), the required storage is n * sizeof(element). Confusing element count with byte count can produce insufficient allocation followed by writes beyond the allocated region.","principle":"Memory sizes must be expressed in the unit required by the API and derived from the actual object representation.","objective":"Diagnose buffer-sizing errors caused by confusing element counts with byte counts.","requirements":"Allocation and copying operations must use the correct byte size for the represented objects.","constraints":"The multiplication itself must also be checked for integer overflow when sizes can be attacker-controlled.","preconditions":"The program allocates storage for multiple objects larger than one byte.","input":"Element count passed directly as a byte allocation size.","output":"Allocation sized as element_count * sizeof(element), with overflow handling where necessary.","expected_behavior":"Allocated storage is sufficient for every object that the program will initialize.","incorrect_code":"int *values = malloc(count);\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {\n    values[i] = input[i];\n}","corrected_code":"int *values = malloc(count * sizeof(*values));\nif (values == NULL && count != 0) {\n    return ERROR;\n}\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {\n    values[i] = input[i];\n}","failure_mode":"Buffer overflow / under-allocation","expected_error":"Undefined behavior including memory corruption, crash, or silent data corruption.","cause":"The allocation size is expressed in elements while malloc expects bytes.","root_cause":"The implementation does not preserve the unit conversion between logical element count and byte storage size.","solution":"Use sizeof(*pointer) or sizeof(element) in the allocation calculation and validate multiplication for overflow when inputs are untrusted.","edge_case":"count * sizeof(*values) can itself overflow size_t for extremely large counts and must be checked when the count is not trusted.","security_implication":"Under-allocation followed by out-of-bounds writes can become a memory-corruption vulnerability.","performance_implication":"Correct sizing avoids corruption; overflow checks add minimal overhead compared with the cost of unsafe memory operations.","test_case":"Test zero, one, boundary, and very large element counts and verify the allocation size and writes remain within bounds.","test_input":"Arrays of multiple int elements with varying count values.","expected_test_result":"Enough storage is allocated for every element and invalid oversized requests are rejected safely.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer or equivalent memory-safety instrumentation during validation.","verification_method":"Derive required bytes mathematically from element count and sizeof(element), then inspect integer-overflow handling.","verification_guidance":"Memory-safety validation should include dynamic instrumentation rather than relying only on successful execution.","language":"C","language_version":"C11","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C99+","dependencies":"C standard library malloc","execution_requirements":"C compiler and memory-safety instrumentation for dynamic validation","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C standard allocation semantics and established secure memory-management guidance.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B056","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Borrowing","concept":"Returning a reference to data owned by a local temporary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does Rust reject a function that attempts to return a reference to a locally created value?","answer":"A reference returned from a function cannot outlive the value it refers to. A local value is dropped when the function returns, so returning a reference to it would create a dangling reference.","explanation":"Rust's borrow checker detects that the proposed reference lifetime would exceed the owner's lifetime. The correct design is generally to return ownership of the value or borrow from an input whose lifetime extends beyond the returned reference.","principle":"A reference must never outlive the value it references.","objective":"Diagnose lifetime errors caused by attempting to return references to local data.","requirements":"Returned references must be tied to data whose lifetime is guaranteed to outlive the reference.","constraints":"The correct ownership strategy depends on whether the caller needs owned data or a borrow from an existing input.","preconditions":"A function constructs a local value and attempts to return a reference to it.","input":"A locally owned String returned as a reference.","output":"An owned String or a reference tied to a valid external lifetime.","expected_behavior":"The returned value remains valid for the entire duration of its use.","incorrect_code":"fn make_name() -> &str {\n    let name = String::from(\"Nexora\");\n    &name\n}","corrected_code":"fn make_name() -> String {\n    let name = String::from(\"Nexora\");\n    name\n}","failure_mode":"Invalid reference lifetime / dangling-reference prevention","expected_error":"Compiler error indicating that borrowed data does not live long enough or cannot be returned from the function.","cause":"The returned reference points to a local variable that is dropped at function exit.","root_cause":"The requested reference lifetime exceeds the lifetime of its owner.","solution":"Return ownership or borrow from an input with a sufficiently long lifetime.","edge_case":"A reference can be returned safely when it refers to data owned by the caller or another value whose lifetime is appropriately tied to the returned reference.","security_implication":"Rust prevents this class of use-after-free through compile-time lifetime checking.","performance_implication":"Returning ownership may involve a move but does not inherently imply an expensive deep copy; Rust's ownership model can preserve efficient transfers.","test_case":"Compile the invalid function and verify that the compiler rejects the invalid lifetime relationship; compile the corrected owned-return version.","test_input":"A function returning a reference to a local String.","expected_test_result":"The invalid version fails compilation and the corrected version satisfies the borrow checker.","testing_guidance":"Treat compiler diagnostics as part of validation for ownership and lifetime defects.","verification_method":"Construct the ownership graph and determine whether the referenced owner remains alive for the complete returned-reference lifetime.","verification_guidance":"Do not bypass the error with unsafe code unless a separately proven ownership invariant exists.","language":"Rust","language_version":"Rust 1.70+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Rust stable","dependencies":"Rust compiler and standard library","execution_requirements":"Rust toolchain","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Rust Reference