{"record_id":"D36-B059","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Exception and Ownership","concept":"RAII bypassed by raw owning pointer on an exception path","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a C++ function leak memory when an exception occurs before a raw pointer is deleted?","answer":"A raw owning pointer requires manual cleanup, and an exception can transfer control out of the function before the delete operation executes. RAII types such as std::unique_ptr tie cleanup to scope exit and therefore handle normal and exceptional paths consistently.","explanation":"Manual cleanup is control-flow dependent. Any return, exception, or future modification inserted before delete can create a leak. Expressing ownership through an RAII type makes destruction automatic when the owner leaves scope.","principle":"Resource ownership should be represented by RAII objects whenever practical.","objective":"Diagnose exception-path memory leaks caused by manual ownership management.","requirements":"Owned memory must be released exactly once on every exit path.","constraints":"Some low-level APIs require raw pointers at boundaries, but ownership should remain represented by an RAII wrapper internally where possible.","preconditions":"A function owns dynamically allocated memory and can throw before cleanup.","input":"A raw owning pointer allocated before an operation that may throw.","output":"An RAII-managed owner with automatic cleanup.","expected_behavior":"Owned memory is released on normal return and exceptional exit.","incorrect_code":"void process() {\n    Item* item = new Item();\n    risky_operation();\n    delete item;\n}","corrected_code":"void process() {\n    auto item = std::make_unique<Item>();\n    risky_operation();\n}","failure_mode":"Exception-path memory leak","expected_error":"No required runtime exception beyond the original failure; leaked memory persists after stack unwinding.","cause":"Manual delete is skipped when an exception exits the scope early.","root_cause":"Ownership is represented by a raw pointer instead of an RAII owner.","solution":"Use std::unique_ptr or another appropriate RAII abstraction for exclusive ownership.","edge_case":"Ownership transfer to legacy APIs may require release(), but doing so should occur only at a clearly defined ownership-transfer boundary.","security_implication":"Repeated leaks can cause resource exhaustion and may retain sensitive data in memory longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Unreleased allocations increase memory usage and can trigger allocator or process-level degradation.","test_case":"Force risky_operation to throw repeatedly and inspect memory/resource behavior.","test_input":"An exception thrown after allocation but before normal cleanup.","expected_test_result":"The allocated object is destroyed during stack unwinding.","testing_guidance":"Use sanitizers or leak-detection tooling and test exceptional paths explicitly.","verification_method":"Review ownership at every allocation and verify that an RAII owner exists before any operation capable of transferring control out of the scope.","verification_guidance":"Do not rely on code review of only the normal execution path.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++14+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":"C++ standard library memory utilities","execution_requirements":"C++ compiler with standard library","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_AND_LIBRARY_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ standard library smart-pointer and RAII semantics; established exception-safety principles.","created_at":"2026