{"record_id":"D36-B060","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Interface Semantics","concept":"Interface containing a typed nil pointer is not equal to nil","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a Go interface value containing a nil pointer fail an if value == nil check?","answer":"An interface value contains both a dynamic type and a dynamic value. If the dynamic type is a pointer type and the dynamic value is nil, the interface itself is non-nil because it still contains a dynamic type.","explanation":"This creates a common nil-interface trap. Code can return a typed nil pointer as an interface and callers may incorrectly conclude that a non-nil interface means a usable object exists.","principle":"Nil checks on interfaces must account for the distinction between a nil interface and an interface containing a typed nil value.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect nil handling at Go interface boundaries.","requirements":"APIs returning interfaces must define whether nil means no implementation and avoid ambiguous typed-nil returns where possible.","constraints":"Reflection-based generic nil detection can be complex; API design that avoids ambiguous interface values is generally clearer.","preconditions":"A function returns an interface whose dynamic value may be a nil pointer.","input":"A typed nil pointer assigned to an interface value.","output":"A clear nil contract and implementation that avoids accidental typed-nil interfaces.","expected_behavior":"Callers can reliably determine whether a usable implementation exists.","incorrect_code":"type Service interface {\n    Run()\n}\n\ntype impl struct{}\nfunc (*impl) Run() {}\n\nfunc getService() Service {\n    var s *impl\n    return s\n}\n\nif getService() == nil {\n    handleMissing()\n}","corrected_code":"func getService() Service {\n    var s *impl\n    if s == nil {\n        return nil\n    }\n    return s\n}","failure_mode":"Typed-nil interface bug","expected_error":"No required runtime error at the nil comparison; later method calls may panic depending on the method implementation.","cause":"A nil pointer is stored in an interface, making the interface itself non-nil.","root_cause":"The API contract does not distinguish interface nilness from the dynamic value's nilness.","solution":"Return a nil interface explicitly when absence is intended, or redesign the API so absence and implementation state are unambiguous.","edge_case":"An interface can contain nil maps, slices, functions, channels, or pointers with type-specific nil semantics; interface nilness remains distinct from the contained dynamic value.","security_implication":"Incorrect absence checks can cause authorization, policy, or resource-management logic to proceed with an invalid implementation state.","performance_implication":"The primary impact is correctness; reflection-based workarounds can add complexity and runtime cost.","test_case":"Return typed nil values through interfaces and verify the API's documented absence behavior.","test_input":"A nil *impl assigned to Service and a truly nil Service interface.","expected_test_result":"The API consistently represents absence according to its contract.","testing_guidance":"Test interface values with both nil and non-nil dynamic values and inspect their dynamic types.","verification_method":"For every interface nil check, inspect both the interface value and its possible dynamic types.","verification_guidance":"A value printed as nil or a pointer being nil does not by itself prove that the containing interface equals nil.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.13+","dependencies":"Go language interfaces","execution_requirements":"Go compiler and runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B008"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Go language specification on interface values and established typed-nil behavior.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B061","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Data Semantics","concept":"Dictionary mutation during iteration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can modifying a Python dictionary while iterating over it fail at runtime?","answer":"Changing a dictionary's size during active iteration invalidates the iterator's traversal assumptions and Python raises RuntimeError for size-changing mutations.","explanation":"Dictionary iteration is not a permission to structurally modify the same dictionary. Adding or removing keys while the iterator is active can invalidate the traversal state. A common repair is to iterate over a stable snapshot when structural mutation is required.","principle":"Do not structurally mutate a mapping while iterating over its live view unless the language explicitly defines that operation as safe.","objective":"Diagnose dictionary-iteration failures caused by structural mutation.","requirements":"Iteration and mutation must use a defined safe ownership/traversal strategy.","constraints":"Updating an existing value without changing dictionary size has different semantics from adding or removing keys.","preconditions":"A dictionary is being traversed while code may add or remove keys.","input":"A dictionary loop that deletes matching keys from the same dictionary.","output":"A safe traversal over a snapshot or a separately collected set of keys to mutate.","expected_behavior":"All intended removals occur without invalidating the active iterator.","incorrect_code":"items = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\nfor key in items:\n    if items[key] < 3:\n        del items[key]","corrected_code":"items = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2, \"c\": 3}\nfor key in list(items):\n    if items[key] < 3:\n        del items[key]","failure_mode":"Concurrent structural mutation during iteration","expected_error":"RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration","cause":"The dictionary's size changes while its iterator is active.","root_cause":"The implementation mutates the live dictionary traversal source instead of a stable snapshot.","solution":"Iterate over list(mapping), tuple(mapping), or another deliberately stable collection when structural mutation is required.","edge_case":"For very large dictionaries, copying all keys increases memory usage; an explicit collection of only keys requiring removal can reduce that cost.","security_implication":"Unexpected iteration failures can interrupt cleanup, filtering, or policy enforcement operations and leave state partially processed.","performance_implication":"Snapshotting adds time and memory proportional to the snapshot size; collecting only affected keys can reduce overhead.","test_case":"Delete several keys during iteration and verify all intended keys are removed without RuntimeError.","test_input":"A dictionary containing matching and non-matching entries.","expected_test_result":"The operation completes and only the intended entries are removed.","testing_guidance":"Test empty dictionaries, single entries, all-match, no-match, and large dictionaries.","verification_method":"Identify whether the loop structurally changes the collection being traversed and verify that any mutation uses a stable traversal source.","verification_guidance":"Do not generalize this failure to every mutation; distinguish structural changes from value replacement.","language":"Python","language_version":"Python 3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":"Python built-in dict","execution_requirements":"Python runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python language reference and official dictionary-view iteration behavior.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B062","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Collections","concept":"Removing elements from a list through the wrong iteration mechanism","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can removing an element directly from a Java collection during enhanced-for iteration cause a ConcurrentModificationException?","answer":"Many Java collection iterators are fail-fast and detect structural modification outside the iterator's own mutation methods. Removing directly from the collection while its iterator is active can therefore trigger ConcurrentModificationException.","explanation":"The enhanced for loop uses an Iterator internally. Calling collection.remove while that iterator is active changes the collection independently of the iterator. Iterator.remove, removeIf, or an appropriate collection-processing strategy can preserve the required mutation semantics.","principle":"Collection mutation during iteration must use the iteration API's supported mutation mechanism or a separate traversal strategy.","objective":"Diagnose Java collection failures caused by structural mutation during iteration.","requirements":"The mutation mechanism must be compatible with the collection's iteration contract.","constraints":"Fail-fast behavior is a bug-detection aid rather than a general synchronization guarantee.","preconditions":"A mutable Java collection is traversed and structurally modified in the same operation.","input":"An enhanced-for loop that directly removes elements from its source collection.","output":"An iterator-supported or collection-level removal operation.","expected_behavior":"Matching elements are removed without invalidating the active traversal.","incorrect_code":"for (String value : values) {\n    if (value.isBlank()) {\n        values.remove(value);\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"values.removeIf(String::isBlank);","failure_mode":"Invalid structural mutation during iteration","expected_error":"java.util.ConcurrentModificationException may be thrown.","cause":"The collection is structurally modified outside the active iterator.","root_cause":"The code violates the collection traversal and mutation contract.","solution":"Use Iterator.remove when incremental iteration is required or removeIf when predicate-based bulk removal is appropriate.","edge_case":"ConcurrentModificationException does not prove that multiple threads are involved; it can arise from modification by the same thread during iteration.","security_implication":"Interrupted filtering or cleanup can leave invalid entries in memory or policy collections.","performance_implication":"removeIf can express bulk filtering efficiently, while copying or repeated removals may have different complexity characteristics depending on collection type.","test_case":"Process collections containing matching and non-matching values and verify all intended elements are removed.","test_input":"Lists containing blank and non-blank strings.","expected_test_result":"The resulting collection contains only non-blank values and no iteration failure occurs.","testing_guidance":"Test ArrayList and other collection implementations because performance and iteration characteristics differ.","verification_method":"Identify the iterator used by the traversal and verify that all structural mutations comply with its documented contract.","verification_guidance":"Do not interpret fail-fast behavior as a guarantee of thread safety.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 8+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":"Java Collections Framework","execution_requirements":"Java runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LIBRARY_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java Collections Framework Iterator and Collection documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B063","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Pointer Arithmetic","concept":"Pointer arithmetic crosses the valid object boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can pointer arithmetic in C produce undefined behavior even when the resulting address looks numerically plausible?","answer":"C pointer arithmetic is defined relative to a particular array object and its one-past-the-end position. Forming or dereferencing a pointer outside those permitted bounds can produce undefined behavior even if the underlying numeric address appears valid.","explanation":"Native pointers are not general-purpose integer coordinates. The language's pointer arithmetic and dereference rules depend on the object from which the pointer was derived. A numerically plausible address does not establish that the pointer is valid for the intended object.","principle":"Pointer validity is determined by the language object and lifetime model, not merely by numeric address plausibility.","objective":"Diagnose out-of-bounds pointer arithmetic and dereference errors.","requirements":"Every pointer computation and dereference must remain within the valid object and lifetime boundaries.","constraints":"One-past-the-end pointers may be formed for an array but must not be dereferenced.","preconditions":"The program performs pointer arithmetic over an array or allocated object.","input":"A pointer increment that exceeds the array's one-past-the-end boundary and is then dereferenced.","output":"Bounds-checked pointer access within the valid object.","expected_behavior":"Only valid elements are accessed and no out-of-bounds pointer is dereferenced.","incorrect_code":"int values[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\nint *p = values;\nfor (int i = 0; i <= 4; ++i) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", p[i]);\n}","corrected_code":"int values[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\nint *p = values;\nfor (size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", p[i]);\n}","failure_mode":"Out-of-bounds pointer dereference","expected_error":"Undefined behavior; possible crash, incorrect data, or memory corruption.","cause":"The loop accesses the element at index equal to the array length.","root_cause":"The boundary condition permits dereferencing the one-past-the-end position.","solution":"Use a strict less-than bound based on the number of valid elements.","edge_case":"A one-past-the-end pointer may be formed and compared but cannot be dereferenced.","security_implication":"Out-of-bounds native memory access can expose or corrupt adjacent memory and become exploitable depending on context.","performance_implication":"Bounds-correct iteration preserves expected performance; optimization should not be based on accepting undefined behavior.","test_case":"Exercise zero-length-equivalent cases where applicable and arrays with one, several, and boundary-sized elements.","test_input":"A four-element array with loop indices 0 through 4.","expected_test_result":"Indices 0 through 3 are accessed; index 4 is rejected.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer and compiler diagnostics during dynamic validation.","verification_method":"Derive the valid index range from the actual object extent and verify every pointer dereference against that range.","verification_guidance":"Do not use observed memory layout as evidence that an out-of-bounds access is safe.","language":"C","language_version":"C11","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C99+","dependencies":"C standard language semantics","execution_requirements":"C compiler and runtime with optional sanitizers","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C language pointer arithmetic and array semantics; established memory-safety guidance.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B064","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Move Semantics","concept":"Using a moved-from object under an invalid semantic assumption","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can code fail after moving from a C++ object even though using the moved-from object is generally allowed?","answer":"A moved-from standard-library object remains valid but its value is generally unspecified unless the type documents stronger post-move guarantees. Code that assumes the original value remains unchanged can therefore behave incorrectly.","explanation":"Move construction or assignment transfers resources when supported. The source object remains in a valid but type-dependent state. The bug occurs when application logic treats a moved-from object as if it still represented its pre-move value.","principle":"After a move, use an object only according to its documented post-move guarantees unless it has been reassigned or otherwise re-established to a known state.","objective":"Diagnose semantic bugs caused by invalid assumptions about moved-from C++ objects.","requirements":"Post-move state assumptions must be justified by the type's documented contract.","constraints":"Specific types can define stronger post-move guarantees than the general language/library expectation.","preconditions":"An object has been moved from and is subsequently used by business logic.","input":"A moved-from string assumed to retain its original contents.","output":"A design that does not depend on unspecified post-move value state.","expected_behavior":"The source object is either reassigned before value-dependent use or used only through documented valid operations.","incorrect_code":"std::string name = \"Nexora\";\nstd::string moved = std::move(name);\nif (name == \"Nexora\") {\n    use_original_name();\n}","corrected_code":"std::string name = \"Nexora\";\nstd::string moved = std::move(name);\nname = \"Nexora\";\nif (name == \"Nexora\") {\n    use_original_name();\n}","failure_mode":"Moved-from state assumption","expected_error":"Usually no exception; incorrect assumptions can produce silent logical errors.","cause":"The code assumes a particular post-move value without a documented guarantee.","root_cause":"Ownership transfer semantics were confused with preservation of the source value.","solution":"Reassign the source before requiring a known value or structure the code so the moved-from object is no longer needed for that invariant.","edge_case":"Some standard-library types document valid but unspecified states; calling operations with additional preconditions may still be invalid if those preconditions are not satisfied.","security_implication":"Incorrect post-move state assumptions can corrupt security-sensitive state machines or authorization context.","performance_implication":"Avoiding unnecessary post-move restoration can improve efficiency; restoration should occur only when the semantic contract requires reuse.","test_case":"Move objects and verify only documented post-move properties are relied upon.","test_input":"std::string and other standard-library movable objects.","expected_test_result":"Program behavior does not depend on an undocumented source value after move.","testing_guidance":"Review type-specific documentation instead of inferring behavior from one implementation's observed state.","verification_method":"Identify every use after move and compare the assumed property with the type's documented post-move guarantee.","verification_guidance":"Observed empty-string behavior on one library implementation is not proof of a universal contract.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":"C++ standard library","execution_requirements":"C++ compiler and standard library implementation","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"E