{"record_id":"D36-B066","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Error Handling","concept":"Returning nil error after a failed operation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a Go function accidentally report success after an operation has failed?","answer":"A Go function can hide an operation failure by ignoring the returned error and returning nil from the surrounding function. Callers then observe apparent success even though the requested operation did not complete.","explanation":"Go makes errors explicit return values rather than implicit exceptions. The caller is responsible for preserving the error signal when it matters. A common defect is checking an unrelated value or returning nil after a failed dependency operation.","principle":"Every meaningful error return must be propagated or deliberately transformed before the enclosing operation reports success.","objective":"Detect error-swallowing bugs in Go functions.","requirements":"Success must be reported only after required dependent operations succeed.","constraints":"Some errors are intentionally recoverable or ignorable, but that decision must be explicit.","preconditions":"A function calls a dependency that returns an error and has a success/failure contract.","input":"An ignored dependency error followed by return nil.","output":"Explicit error propagation or documented recovery.","expected_behavior":"The caller receives an error when a required operation fails.","incorrect_code":"func save() error {\n    _, err := writeRecord()\n    _ = err\n    return nil\n}","corrected_code":"func save() error {\n    _, err := writeRecord()\n    if err != nil {\n        return err\n    }\n    return nil\n}","failure_mode":"Swallowed error / false success","expected_error":"No runtime exception is required; callers incorrectly receive nil error.","cause":"The dependency's error result is discarded.","root_cause":"The enclosing function's success condition is not coupled to the dependency's result.","solution":"Check and propagate the error or explicitly recover with a documented alternative outcome.","edge_case":"Errors that are intentionally ignored should be justified by the operation's semantics and should not be confused with accidental suppression.","security_implication":"Swallowed errors in authentication, authorization, audit, persistence, or validation paths can produce fail-open behavior.","performance_implication":"False success can cause downstream retries, compensating work, or inconsistent state that increases system cost.","test_case":"Force writeRecord to return an error and verify save returns a non-nil error.","test_input":"A dependency failure during save.","expected_test_result":"save returns the dependency error and does not report success.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures into every required dependency and verify that each failure reaches the appropriate boundary.","verification_method":"Trace every returned error from dependent operations to the public operation's success condition.","verification_guidance":"Static analysis for unchecked errors can identify candidates, but semantic review is required to distinguish intentional ignores.","language":"Go","language_version":"Go 1.20+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Go 1.13+","dependencies":"Go error interface","execution_requirements":"Go compiler and test runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Go language and official error-handling documentation; established explicit-error propagation principles.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B067","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Resource Lifecycle","concept":"File handle leak from missing context-manager ownership","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can Python code leak file descriptors when exceptions occur before close is reached?","answer":"Manual file closing depends on control flow reaching the close statement. An exception or early return can bypass it, leaving the file open until implementation-dependent cleanup occurs.","explanation":"The with statement expresses deterministic ownership of a file object and closes it when the block exits, including exceptional exits. This avoids relying on garbage collection or manually maintained cleanup paths.","principle":"Use context managers for resources with deterministic acquire/release lifecycles.","objective":"Diagnose file-resource leaks caused by manual cleanup paths.","requirements":"Owned file resources must be closed on all exit paths.","constraints":"Some file-like objects have externally managed ownership and should not be closed by every consumer.","preconditions":"Code opens a file and can exit through exceptions or early returns.","input":"Manual open/read/close code with a possible exception before close.","output":"A context-managed file lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"The file descriptor is released when the managed block exits.","incorrect_code":"def read_config(path):\n    f = open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    data = parse_config(f.read())\n    f.close()\n    return data","corrected_code":"def read_config(path):\n    with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        return parse_config(f.read())","failure_mode":"File descriptor leak","expected_error":"Repeated failures can eventually produce operating-system resource exhaustion.","cause":"An exception can bypass the explicit close call.","root_cause":"Resource ownership is represented by manual control flow instead of a context manager.","solution":"Use with for file objects and other resources supporting the context-manager protocol.","edge_case":"If parse_config intentionally transfers ownership of a resource, the ownership contract must explicitly state who closes it.","security_implication":"Leaked descriptors can expose files longer than necessary and can contribute to resource exhaustion denial of service.","performance_implication":"Descriptor exhaustion increases latency and can prevent new files or connections from being opened.","test_case":"Inject a parsing exception after file acquisition and verify the file is closed.","test_input":"A configuration file whose parser raises during processing.","expected_test_result":"The exception propagates while the file resource is released.","testing_guidance":"Use repeated exception-path tests and operating-system descriptor monitoring for integration validation.","verification_method":"Identify each resource acquisition and verify a context manager or equivalent deterministic cleanup mechanism encloses all failure paths.","verification_guidance":"Do not rely on CPython reference counting as the portability contract for resource cleanup.","language":"Python","language_version":"Python 3.11+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Python 3.x","dependencies":"Python built-in context manager protocol","execution_requirements":"Python runtime and filesystem","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_AND_RUNTIME_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python official documentation for file objects and context managers.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B068","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Array Mutation","concept":"Removing array elements while incrementing the same forward index skips elements","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a JavaScript loop skip array elements when it removes the current element during forward iteration?","answer":"Removing an element shifts later elements toward lower indices while the loop then increments the index, causing the next element to move into the current index and be skipped.","explanation":"The bug is caused by modifying the collection's index positions while the traversal state assumes they remain stable. Filtering into a new array or iterating backward are common alternatives depending on ownership and mutation requirements.","principle":"Traversal state must remain consistent with collection mutation semantics.","objective":"Diagnose skipped elements caused by forward iteration combined with array deletion.","requirements":"Every matching element must be evaluated exactly as intended.","constraints":"In-place mutation may be required for memory or ownership reasons, so the repair should respect the actual lifecycle requirements.","preconditions":"The loop uses increasing indices while removing elements from the same array.","input":"An array containing adjacent elements that satisfy the removal condition.","output":"A mutation-safe traversal strategy.","expected_behavior":"Every element is evaluated without being skipped because of index shifts.","incorrect_code":"for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {\n    if (values[i] < 0) {\n        values.splice(i, 1);\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"for (let i = values.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (values[i] < 0) {\n        values.splice(i, 1);\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Skipped elements after in-place deletion","expected_error":"No runtime error; some matching elements remain unexpectedly.","cause":"splice shifts later elements left while the loop increments the index.","root_cause":"The iteration invariant assumes stable index positions despite structural mutation.","solution":"Iterate backward for safe in-place deletion or use filter when producing a new array is acceptable.","edge_case":"Removing multiple adjacent elements exposes the defect more reliably than removing isolated elements.","security_implication":"Skipped validation, filtering, or sanitization can leave prohibited data in a collection.","performance_implication":"Repeated splice operations can be costly because elements may need to be shifted; filtering can have different allocation and complexity characteristics.","test_case":"Remove all negative values from arrays containing adjacent and non-adjacent negatives.","test_input":"[-1, -2, 3, -4, -5].","expected_test_result":"No negative values remain.","testing_guidance":"Include empty arrays, all-match arrays, no-match arrays, and adjacent matches.","verification_method":"Track the index movement after each mutation and verify that every original element is either retained or removed exactly once.","verification_guidance":"Tests should include adjacent matches because isolated matches may hide the defect.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ECMAScript 2022+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"ECMAScript 2015+","dependencies":"ECMAScript Array","execution_requirements":"JavaScript runtime","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"ECMAScript Array mutation and splice semantics.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B069","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Module Boundaries","concept":"Runtime circular dependency causes partially initialized module state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a TypeScript circular module dependency produce undefined or partially initialized values at runtime?","answer":"TypeScript's static dependency graph can permit circular imports, but the emitted JavaScript module initialization order still determines when exported bindings become available. A cycle can expose partially initialized state if module code reads another module before its required initialization has completed.","explanation":"The type checker can establish that an imported symbol exists while runtime module evaluation still encounters a cycle. The failure is therefore a mismatch between static declaration availability and dynamic module initialization order.","principle":"Module dependency graphs must be reasoned about at runtime as well as at the type level.","objective":"Diagnose initialization failures caused by circular module dependencies.","requirements":"Initialization order must not require a module's runtime state before that state is initialized.","constraints":"ES module live bindings and CommonJS output have different runtime details, so diagnosis must account for the emitted module system.","preconditions":"Two or more modules import one another directly or indirectly and execute initialization logic at module load time.","input":"A circular dependency where module A reads module B's exported initialization state during module evaluation.","output":"A dependency graph without unsafe initialization cycles or a deferred initialization design.","expected_behavior":"Imported values are initialized before dependent initialization logic requires them.","incorrect_code":"// a.ts\nimport { valueB } from \"./b\";\nexport const valueA = valueB + 1;\n\n// b.ts\nimport { valueA } from \"./a\";\nexport const valueB = valueA + 1;","corrected_code":"// shared.ts\nexport const baseValue = 1;\n\n// a.ts\nimport { baseValue } from \"./shared\";\nexport const valueA = baseValue + 1;\n\n// b.ts\nimport { baseValue } from \"./shared\";\nexport const valueB = baseValue + 1;","failure_mode":"Circular initialization dependency","expected_error":"Depending on module system and access pattern, runtime initialization can throw, expose an uninitialized binding, or otherwise produce incorrect state.","cause":"Module initialization requires a value from another module whose initialization depends on the first module.","root_cause":"The dependency graph contains a cycle involving eager runtime initialization.","solution":"Break the cycle, move shared state into a lower-level module, or defer mutually dependent initialization until after module loading.","edge_case":"A circular import is not automatically incorrect; some cycles can be safe when no module reads incompletely initialized state during evaluation.","security_implication":"Partially initialized security configuration or policy state can cause fail-open or inconsistent behavior.","performance_implication":"Circular dependency workarounds that introduce repeated lazy resolution can increase complexity and runtime overhead.","test_case":"Load modules independently and through each supported module system/build target, then verify initialization state.","test_input":"A direct and indirect circular dependency under the project's emitted module format.","expected_test_result":"Modules initialize deterministically without reading unavailable runtime state.","testing_guidance":"Inspect emitted JavaScript and test the actual runtime module system rather than relying solely on TypeScript compilation.","verification_method":"Construct the module dependency graph and inspect all top-level executable initialization within cycles.","verification_guidance":"Compilation success verifies type-level consistency, not necessarily safe runtime initialization order.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"TypeScript 5.x","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":"TypeScript compiler and selected JavaScript module system","execution_requirements":"TypeScript compiler plus the actual runtime/bundler used by the application","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MODULE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D36-B033"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"TypeScript module documentation and ECMAScript module evaluation semantics; runtime module-system documentation.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B070","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Synchronization","concept":"Synchronizing on different lock objects fails to protect shared state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can Java synchronized blocks fail to prevent a race when different threads synchronize on different objects?","answer":"Synchronization only coordinates threads that use the same monitor object. If one thread locks object A while another locks object B, the critical sections do not exclude each other even if they protect the same shared state.","explanation":"The protected resource and the synchronization identity must match. Creating a new lock object for each invocation or using inconsistent lock objects provides no mutual exclusion between competing operations.","principle":"All accesses requiring the same mutual-exclusion invariant must coordinate through a common synchronization mechanism.","objective":"Diagnose ineffective Java synchronization caused by inconsistent monitor ownership.","requirements":"Every operation participating in the invariant must acquire the same lock or use an equivalent synchronization primitive.","constraints":"Read/write locks, concurrent collections, atomics, and other mechanisms can provide alternatives when their semantics match the invariant.","preconditions":"Multiple threads modify shared mutable state.","input":"Each operation synchronizes on a newly created or otherwise different lock object.","output":"A stable shared lock or appropriate concurrency primitive protecting the invariant.","expected_behavior":"Conflicting critical sections cannot execute concurrently.","incorrect_code":"void increment() {\n    Object lock = new Object();\n    synchronized (lock) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"private final Object lock = new Object();\n\nvoid increment() {\n    synchronized (lock) {\n        count++;\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Ineffective synchronization / race condition","expected_error":"Usually no exception; lost updates or inconsistent state can occur under concurrency.","cause":"Threads synchronize on different monitor objects.","root_cause":"The synchronization identity is not shared across all accesses to the protected state.","solution":"Use one stable lock associated with the protected invariant or select an appropriate concurrent primitive.","edge_case":"Synchronizing on publicly accessible or mutable objects can introduce unintended lock coupling; a private stable lock is often easier to reason about.","security_implication":"Broken synchronization around authorization, counters, quotas, or shared security state can create race-based integrity failures.","performance_implication":"A single lock can reduce parallelism; replacing it with finer-grained or lock-free mechanisms requires preserving the same correctness invariant.","test_case":"Run many concurrent increments and compare the final count with the expected number of successful operations.","test_input":"Multiple threads repeatedly invoke increment().","expected_test_result":"The final count equals the total number of intended increments.","testing_guidance":"Use high-concurrency stress tests and repeated runs; race detectors or concurrency-analysis tools can provide additional evidence.","verification_method":"Identify the shared invariant and enumerate every synchronization object used by accesses participating in that invariant.","verification_guidance":"A synchronized block is not evidence of mutual exclusion unless all competing accesses synchronize on the same monitor or equivalent primitive.","language":"Java","language_version":"Java 17+","framework":"","framework_version":"","version_range":"Java 5+","dependencies":"Java synchronization primitives","execution_requirements":"Java runtime with multithreaded execution","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java Language Specification monitor synchronization semantics and established concurrency principles.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B071","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Configuration and Build","concept":"Environment-specific configuration silently ignored due to incorrect configuration key lookup","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can an application use the wrong environment configuration even though the intended configuration file is present?","answer":"A configuration loader may request a key whose spelling, nesting, or environment-variable mapping differs from the actual configuration schema. If the loader silently falls back to a default, the application can start with incorrect behavior instead of failing fast.","explanation":"The failure is caused by a mismatch between the configuration contract and the lookup operation. The presence of the configuration file does not prove that the requested value was successfully resolved. Silent fallback converts a configuration error into an operational bug that may only appear later.","principle":"Configuration resolution should distinguish a missing required value from an intentional default and should fail explicitly when a required key cannot be resolved.","objective":"Identify configuration-key mismatches before incorrect runtime behavior occurs.","requirements":["Required configuration keys must be resolved explicitly.","Missing required configuration must be observable.","Defaults must be intentional and documented."],"constraints":["Configuration format and loader must agree on key names and nesting.","The example uses Python standard-library configuration handling only."],"preconditions":["A configuration object contains the key 'database.url'.","The application requires that value during startup."],"input":"Configuration data: {\"database\": {\"url\": \"postgresql://db/prod\"}}; lookup incorrectly requests config['db']['url'].","output":"The incorrect implementation raises a configuration lookup error instead of silently substituting an unrelated default.","expected_behavior":"A missing required configuration key should cause an explicit startup failure with enough context to diagnose the schema mismatch.","incorrect_code":"config = {\"database\": {\"url\": \"postgresql://db/prod\"}}\ndatabase_url = config.get(\"db\", {}).get(\"url\", \"sqlite:///local.db\")","corrected_code":"config = {\"database\": {\"url\": \"postgresql://db/prod\"}}\ntry:\n    database_url = config[\"database\"][\"url\"]\nexcept KeyError as exc:\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Required configuration key 'database.url' is missing\") from exc","failure_mode":"Wrong configuration is selected because a required key mismatch is converted into a silent fallback.","expected_error":"RuntimeError: Required configuration key 'database.url' is missing","cause":"The loader uses a different configuration path from the declared configuration schema.","root_cause":"Required configuration lookup and configuration schema are not governed by the same contract, while fallback behavior hides the mismatch.","solution":"Use explicit required-key access, validate configuration during startup, and reserve defaults for values that are intentionally optional.","edge_case":"A valid empty string or false-like value may be meaningful and must not be treated as equivalent to a missing key.","security_implication":"A silent fallback can redirect an application to an unintended database, endpoint, storage location, or security configuration.","performance_implication":"The direct lookup has negligible overhead; early validation can prevent costly runtime failures and repeated fallback handling.","test_case":"Provide a configuration object with the expected nested key and another with the key missing; verify successful resolution and explicit failure respectively.","test_input":"Valid: {\"database\":{\"url\":\"postgresql://db/prod\"}}; Invalid: {\"database\":{}}","expected_test_result":"The valid configuration resolves the production URL; the invalid configuration raises the required-key error.","testing_guidance":"Test required keys, optional defaults, wrong nesting, wrong spelling, empty values, and environment-specific configuration precedence.","verification_method":"Compare every required lookup path against the configuration schema and verify that missing required keys fail explicitly.","verification_guidance":"Verify both success and failure paths; do not treat application startup alone as proof that the intended configuration was loaded.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python standard library","framework_version":"3.11+","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D36-B040"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python language documentation and established configuration-validation engineering practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B072","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Serialization","concept":"JSON serialization drops undefined object properties and changes the transmitted data contract","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a JavaScript object lose fields after JSON serialization even though those fields existed before serialization?","answer":"JSON.stringify omits object properties whose values are undefined. If downstream code expects those properties to exist, serialization can silently change the data contract and cause missing-field behavior.","explanation":"JavaScript objects can contain undefined-valued properties, but JSON has no equivalent undefined value. During serialization, undefined object properties are omitted. Code that assumes object shape is preserved across serialization therefore creates a boundary mismatch.","principle":"Serialization boundaries must define how unsupported or non-representable values are encoded before data crosses the boundary.","objective":"Detect data-contract corruption caused by implicit JSON serialization semantics.","requirements":["The serialized representation must preserve all required semantic fields.","Unsupported JavaScript values must have an explicit representation when required."],"constraints":["The example uses JSON serialization semantics.","The receiving side expects a stable object contract."],"preconditions":["An object contains a required property whose value is undefined."],"input":"const payload = { name: \"Ada\", role: undefined };","output":"JSON.stringify(payload) produces '{\"name\":\"Ada\"}', omitting role.","expected_behavior":"Required fields must either contain representable values or be explicitly encoded according to the API contract.","incorrect_code":"const payload = { name: \"Ada\", role: undefined };\nconst body = JSON.stringify(payload);\n// role is silently omitted","corrected_code":"const payload = { name: \"Ada\", role: null };\nconst body = JSON.stringify(payload);\n// role is explicitly represented as null","failure_mode":"A required field disappears at the serialization boundary.","expected_error":"No JavaScript exception is necessarily raised; the observable failure is a missing serialized property.","cause":"JSON.stringify omits undefined-valued object properties.","root_cause":"The application assumes JavaScript object shape and JSON wire-format shape are equivalent without defining a representation for undefined.","solution":"Validate the payload against the wire contract and explicitly map undefined or optional values before serialization.","edge_case":"undefined inside arrays is serialized differently from undefined object properties, and functions or symbols also have JSON-specific behavior.","security_implication":"Unexpected field omission can alter authorization, policy, or audit payloads when downstream systems interpret missing fields differently from explicit null values.","performance_implication":"Pre-serialization validation adds processing cost proportional to payload size but can prevent downstream retries and failures.","test_case":"Serialize an object containing required undefined and explicit null fields and compare the resulting JSON shape.","test_input":"{name:\"Ada\", role:undefined} versus {name:\"Ada\", role:null}","expected_test_result":"The first serialization omits role; the second contains role:null.","testing_guidance":"Test undefined, null, missing properties, nested objects, arrays, and API schema validation at the serialization boundary.","verification_method":"Inspect the serialized payload and validate it against the declared wire-format schema.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual serialized representation rather than only inspecting the pre-serialization object.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","framework":"ECMAScript JSON API","framework_version":"ES2022+","version_range":"ES2015+","dependencies":["ECMAScript JSON.stringify"],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime implementing JSON.stringify"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B033"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"ECMAScript JSON serialization behavior; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B073","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Runtime Boundary","concept":"TypeScript enum or type assumption does not validate untrusted runtime input","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can TypeScript code accept an invalid runtime value despite the source type declaring a restricted set of values?","answer":"TypeScript types are erased during normal JavaScript execution. Data arriving from JSON, network requests, files, or other JavaScript code is not automatically validated against a TypeScript type.","explanation":"A compile-time type annotation constrains TypeScript source code but does not enforce the same constraint on arbitrary runtime data. Assigning an unvalidated external value through a cast or broad type can allow invalid values into logic that assumes the declared type is trustworthy.","principle":"External data must be validated at runtime before being converted into trusted domain types.","objective":"Separate compile-time type assumptions from runtime validation requirements.","requirements":["Untrusted external values must be validated before domain use.","Runtime validation must reject unsupported values."],"constraints":["The example uses TypeScript without a runtime schema library.","The input originates outside the trusted type system."],"preconditions":["An external JSON value contains an unsupported status string."],"input":"const raw: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"status\":\"destroy\"}');","output":"Runtime validation rejects the unsupported status.","expected_behavior":"Only values matching the domain contract should enter trusted application logic.","incorrect_code":"type Status = \"pending\" | \"approved\" | \"rejected\";\nconst raw: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"status\":\"destroy\"}');\nconst status = (raw as { status: Status }).status;\nif (status === \"approved\") {\n  approve();\n}","corrected_code":"type Status = \"pending\" | \"approved\" | \"rejected\";\n\nfunction isStatus(value: unknown): value is Status {\n  return value === \"pending\" || value === \"approved\" || value === \"rejected\";\n}\n\nconst raw: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"status\":\"destroy\"}');\nif (typeof raw !== \"object\" || raw === null || !(\"status\" in raw) || !isStatus(raw.status)) {\n  throw new Error(\"Invalid status\");\n}\nconst status = raw.status;","failure_mode":"Invalid external data enters logic as though it satisfied a compile-time TypeScript type.","expected_error":"Error: Invalid status","cause":"A type assertion suppresses compile-time checking without performing runtime validation.","root_cause":"The system treats a static type declaration as if it were a runtime validation mechanism.","solution":"Validate untrusted input and narrow it with a runtime type guard before domain operations.","edge_case":"A value can be structurally plausible but semantically invalid, so field-presence checks alone may be insufficient.","security_implication":"Unvalidated runtime input can bypass policy assumptions and become an injection, authorization, or state-integrity issue.","performance_implication":"Runtime validation adds proportional validation work but reduces downstream failures and unsafe processing.","test_case":"Pass supported and unsupported status values through the boundary validator.","test_input":"{\"status\":\"approved\"}; {\"status\":\"destroy\"}; {}; null","expected_test_result":"Only approved, pending, and rejected are accepted as Status values.","testing_guidance":"Test missing fields, wrong primitive types, unsupported literals, extra fields where relevant, and malformed JSON.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that every external value reaching the domain variable passed the runtime predicate.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the boundary and confirm no unchecked type assertion converts unknown input directly into a trusted type.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"TypeScript","framework_version":"5.x","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":["TypeScript compiler"],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"TypeScript type-erasure and control-flow narrowing behavior; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B074","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Equality and Hashing","concept":"Overriding equals without a compatible hashCode causes logically equal objects to occupy inconsistent hash-based collection positions","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a Java HashSet contain two objects that are equal according to equals?","answer":"Hash-based collections depend on both equals and hashCode. If equals is overridden but hashCode is not made consistent with it, equal objects can produce different hash values and be placed in different buckets.","explanation":"The Java object contract requires equal objects to have equal hash codes. HashSet and HashMap use hashCode to locate candidate entries before equality checks. Violating the contract can therefore make logically equal objects behave as distinct collection keys.","principle":"Whenever equals is overridden, hashCode must be overridden consistently so equal objects return equal hash codes.","objective":"Diagnose hash-based collection anomalies caused by an equality-contract violation.","requirements":["Equal objects must return equal hash codes.","Equality semantics must remain stable while objects are used as hash keys."],"constraints":["The example uses a mutable-free value object for conceptual clarity.","HashSet relies on equals/hashCode contracts."],"preconditions":["Two Person objects have the same logical identifier.","equals compares identifier but hashCode remains identity-based."],"input":"Person(\"A\") and Person(\"A\")","output":"A correctly implemented HashSet treats the two logically equal objects as one element.","expected_behavior":"Adding an object equal to an existing set element should not create a second logical element.","incorrect_code":"final class Person {\n    private final String id;\n    Person(String id) { this.id = id; }\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Person p && id.equals(p.id);\n    }\n    // hashCode() is not overridden\n}","corrected_code":"final class Person {\n    private final String id;\n    Person(String id) { this.id = id; }\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Person p && id.equals(p.id);\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() {\n        return id.hashCode();\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"HashSet or HashMap lookup and duplicate detection behave inconsistently with equals.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is incorrect collection behavior.","cause":"equals and hashCode implement incompatible equality semantics.","root_cause":"The object violates the Java equality contract required by hash-based collections.","solution":"Implement hashCode using the same logical fields used by equals and keep those fields stable while hashed.","edge_case":"Changing a hash-relevant field after insertion can make an object effectively unreachable in its current hash bucket.","security_implication":"Incorrect identity semantics can cause authorization, caching, deduplication, or policy checks to operate on the wrong logical entity.","performance_implication":"Contract violations can cause additional lookups or duplicate logical entries; unstable keys can also make cleanup difficult.","test_case":"Insert two logically equal Person objects into a HashSet and assert that the set size remains one.","test_input":"new Person(\"A\"), new Person(\"A\")","expected_test_result":"HashSet size equals 1 and contains recognizes either equal object.","testing_guidance":"Test equals symmetry, transitivity, consistency, null handling, hashCode equality, and hash-key immutability.","verification_method":"Check the equals/hashCode contract and test equal instances in HashSet and HashMap operations.","verification_guidance":"Do not validate only equals directly; exercise the object through actual hash-based collections.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":"Java Collections Framework","framework_version":"17+","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":["java.util.HashSet"],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_CONTRACT","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java Object equals/hashCode contract and Java Collections Framework behavior; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B075","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Integer Semantics","concept":"Signed integer overflow creates undefined behavior in C and can invalidate range checks","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can an apparently safe integer calculation make a security or bounds check unreliable in C?","answer":"Signed integer overflow in C is undefined behavior. A calculation performed before a bounds check can therefore overflow instead of producing a mathematically valid value, invalidating assumptions made by subsequent logic.","explanation":"The key issue is that the language does not define wraparound behavior for signed overflow. Optimizers may use the no-overflow assumption when transforming code, so checks based on an overflowed signed result are not reliable.","principle":"Validate operands and bounds before performing signed arithmetic that could exceed the representable range.","objective":"Identify integer-overflow root causes in arithmetic and bounds calculations.","requirements":["Arithmetic must remain within the defined range of the chosen signed type.","External sizes and offsets must be validated before arithmetic."],"constraints":["The example assumes int is a signed integer type with implementation-defined width.","Unsigned arithmetic has different language semantics and is not interchangeable with signed arithmetic."],"preconditions":["An attacker-controlled or otherwise large integer is added to a valid base offset."],"input":"int offset = INT_MAX; int length = 1;","output":"Correct code rejects the operation before signed overflow occurs.","expected_behavior":"The program must detect an out-of-range calculation without evaluating overflowing signed arithmetic.","incorrect_code":"int end = offset + length;\nif (end < offset) {\n    return -1;\n}","corrected_code":"if (length < 0 || offset > INT_MAX - length) {\n    return -1;\n}\nint end = offset + length;","failure_mode":"The arithmetic expression itself can invoke undefined behavior before the intended overflow check can reliably protect the program.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior; no portable C diagnostic or exception is guaranteed.","cause":"Signed addition exceeds the representable range.","root_cause":"The program performs potentially overflowing signed arithmetic before proving that the operands are safe.","solution":"Check operand ranges using subtraction-based bounds or another defined arithmetic strategy before performing the addition.","edge_case":"The exact integer limits depend on the implementation, so portable code should use the limits associated with the actual type.","security_implication":"Integer overflow can contribute to memory-safety vulnerabilities when sizes, offsets, allocation lengths, or bounds are derived from untrusted values.","performance_implication":"A simple precondition check has negligible cost compared with memory or I/O operations and avoids optimizer-sensitive undefined behavior.","test_case":"Test values at INT_MAX, INT_MAX-1, zero, negative lengths, and representative valid values.","test_input":"offset=INT_MAX,length=1; offset=INT_MAX-1,length=1; offset=100,length=20","expected_test_result":"Overflowing cases are rejected; valid arithmetic cases produce the expected end value.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary-value and fuzz testing around integer limits and all externally controlled size calculations.","verification_method":"Review every arithmetic operation involving untrusted or potentially large signed integers and prove that operands are bounded before evaluation.","verification_guidance":"Use compiler sanitizers and static analysis as supporting evidence, but do not rely on a single runtime test to establish absence of undefined behavior.","language":"C","language_version":"C17","framework":"C standard library","framework_version":"C17","version_range":"C11-C23","dependencies":["limits.h"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler supporting the selected language standard"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_STANDARD_RULE","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"ISO C integer arithmetic rules and established compiler/undefined-behavior analysis practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B076","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Object Semantics","concept":"Object slicing loses derived-state information when a derived object is copied into a base object by value","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a C++ function unexpectedly lose derived-class behavior or data when receiving an object by value?","answer":"Passing a derived object by value to a function expecting a base object creates a separate base object. The derived portion is sliced away, so later operations on that base value cannot access the original derived state or dynamic type.","explanation":"Object slicing occurs during value conversion from a derived object to a base object. The resulting base object is independent of the original derived object. Code that expects polymorphic identity or derived data after such a conversion therefore operates on incomplete state.","principle":"Use references, pointers, or another explicit polymorphic ownership model when preserving dynamic object identity is required.","objective":"Diagnose loss of derived state caused by unintended value semantics in polymorphic C++ code.","requirements":["The chosen parameter type must preserve the required object identity or polymorphic behavior.","Value semantics should be used only when slicing is intentional."],"constraints":["The example assumes a polymorphic base class.","The correction preserves the original object rather than copying it into a base value."],"preconditions":["A derived object contains state not represented by the base class."],"input":"Derived d; consume(d); where consume accepts Base by value.","output":"consume receives the original dynamic object through a reference instead of a sliced Base value.","expected_behavior":"Derived-specific virtual behavior and state should remain available when the API is intended to operate polymorphically.","incorrect_code":"struct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; virtual int value() const { return 1; } };\nstruct Derived : Base { int extra = 42; int value() const override { return extra; } };\nvoid consume(Base b) { std::cout << b.value(); }\nDerived d;\nconsume(d);","corrected_code":"struct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; virtual int value() const { return 1; } };\nstruct Derived : Base { int extra = 42; int value() const override { return extra; } };\nvoid consume(const Base& b) { std::cout << b.value(); }\nDerived d;\nconsume(d);","failure_mode":"Derived state and/or intended polymorphic identity is lost at a value-copy boundary.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is incorrect or unexpectedly base-only behavior.","cause":"A derived object is copied into a Base parameter by value.","root_cause":"The function interface uses value semantics where polymorphic reference semantics are required.","solution":"Pass polymorphic objects by reference or pointer when the API must preserve dynamic identity.","edge_case":"Slicing can be intentional when a base-value snapshot is explicitly desired; the bug exists when that loss of state is unintended.","security_implication":"Unexpected loss of policy or authorization state in a polymorphic object can cause incorrect enforcement if the derived state carries security decisions.","performance_implication":"Passing by const reference avoids an unnecessary base-object copy in addition to preventing slicing.","test_case":"Pass a derived object through both by-value and by-reference APIs and compare virtual dispatch and preserved state.","test_input":"Derived object with extra=42","expected_test_result":"The reference-based API invokes Derived::value and preserves derived behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test virtual dispatch, copy construction, object ownership, and APIs that accept polymorphic objects by value.","verification_method":"Inspect function signatures for value parameters of polymorphic base types and test derived instances through the interface.","verification_guidance":"Verify whether slicing is intentional; do not mechanically replace all value parameters with references.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17","framework":"C++ standard library","framework_version":"C++17","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":["iostream"],"execution_requirements":["C++17 compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ object model and polymorphism semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B077","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust UTF-8 String Handling","concept":"Indexing a Rust String by byte offset is invalid because UTF-8 characters are variable width","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can code that assumes one byte equals one character fail when processing a Rust String?","answer":"Rust String values are UTF-8 encoded and cannot be indexed directly by integer position because a character may occupy multiple bytes. Byte offsets can split a UTF-8 code point and therefore do not correspond to character boundaries.","explanation":"Rust deliberately avoids ambiguous String indexing. Operations that need textual characters must use char iteration or character-aware slicing logic, while operations that intentionally work with bytes should use byte slices and validate boundaries.","principle":"Distinguish byte-oriented processing from Unicode scalar-value or grapheme-oriented processing and use an API appropriate to the required unit.","objective":"Diagnose Unicode boundary errors caused by confusing byte positions with character positions.","requirements":["Text processing must define whether positions refer to bytes, Unicode scalar values, or grapheme clusters.","UTF-8 slicing must occur only at valid character boundaries."],"constraints":["Unicode scalar values are not identical to user-perceived grapheme clusters.","The example uses standard Rust String behavior."],"preconditions":["The input contains at least one multibyte UTF-8 character."],"input":"let text = String::from(\"café\");","output":"Character-aware iteration returns Unicode scalar values without assuming one byte per character.","expected_behavior":"Text operations must not treat UTF-8 byte offsets as character indexes.","incorrect_code":"let text = String::from(\"café\");\n// Conceptually attempts to access character 4 as if each character were one byte.\nlet byte = text.as_bytes()[3];","corrected_code":"let text = String::from(\"café\");\nlet fourth_char = text.chars().nth(3);\nassert_eq!(fourth_char, Some('é'));","failure_mode":"Byte-oriented indexing is mistaken for character-oriented indexing, producing incorrect text processing or a panic when slicing at an invalid boundary.","expected_error":"Rust does not support direct String indexing by integer; invalid byte slicing can panic at a non-character boundary.","cause":"UTF-8 characters have variable byte widths.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes a one-to-one mapping between bytes and textual characters.","solution":"Use chars for Unicode scalar-value iteration, valid byte-boundary slicing for byte-oriented operations, or a grapheme-aware library when user-visible characters are required.","edge_case":"A single user-perceived character can consist of multiple Unicode scalar values, so chars() is not sufficient for grapheme counting.","security_implication":"Incorrect Unicode boundary handling can contribute to validation inconsistencies, identifier confusion, or security-filter bypasses when different components normalize or segment text differently.","performance_implication":"Character-aware iteration can require decoding UTF-8; choose byte-level operations only when byte semantics are actually intended.","test_case":"Process ASCII, multibyte Latin text, and combining-character sequences using the intended unit of measurement.","test_input":"\"abc\", \"café\", \"e\\u{301}\"","expected_test_result":"The implementation handles valid UTF-8 without assuming fixed-width characters.","testing_guidance":"Test Unicode boundary conditions, invalid UTF-8 at byte boundaries, combining marks, and normalization-sensitive identifiers where applicable.","verification_method":"Identify the required text unit and verify that indexing/slicing APIs operate on that same unit.","verification_guidance":"Do not equate bytes, Unicode scalar values, and grapheme clusters during verification.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":"Rust standard library","framework_version":"1.75+","version_range":"Rust 1.0+","dependencies":["std::string::String"],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Rust String UTF-8 and slicing semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B078","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Map Semantics","concept":"Assignment to a nil Go map panics because reading from nil maps is permitted but writing is not","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can Go code read from a map safely but panic when it attempts to assign to the same nil map?","answer":"A nil map can be read from and reports zero values, but assigning a key-value pair to a nil map causes a runtime panic. Code that declares a map without initializing it can therefore fail only when the first write occurs.","explanation":"The distinction between nil-map reads and writes can hide initialization bugs. A zero-value map variable is nil until make or another valid map value is assigned to it. The failure often appears far from the declaration because reads do not expose the missing initialization.","principle":"Initialize a map before mutation when a nil map is not intentionally used as a read-only zero-value state.","objective":"Identify delayed runtime failure caused by writing to an uninitialized Go map.","requirements":["Maps must be initialized before writes.","Code must not rely on nil-map writes being implicitly allocated."],"constraints":["The example uses a local map variable.","Concurrent map access introduces additional synchronization requirements not covered by this record."],"preconditions":["var counts map[string]int has been declared but not initialized."],"input":"counts[\"error\"] = 1","output":"A correctly initialized map accepts the assignment.","expected_behavior":"The map must be created before its first mutation.","incorrect_code":"var counts map[string]int\ncounts[\"error\"]++","corrected_code":"counts := make(map[string]int)\ncounts[\"error\"]++","failure_mode":"The first write to a nil map triggers a runtime panic.","expected_error":"panic: assignment to entry in nil map","cause":"The map variable has a nil map value and is mutated without initialization.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes map declaration allocates the map's writable backing state.","solution":"Initialize the map with make or assign an existing non-nil map before mutation.","edge_case":"Reading, ranging over, deleting from, and taking len of a nil map have defined behavior that differs from writing.","security_implication":"If the panic occurs in request or control paths, it can become a denial-of-service condition unless recovered and handled at an appropriate boundary.","performance_implication":"make allocates the map structure; optional capacity hints can reduce growth reallocations for known workloads.","test_case":"Write to a nil map and to an initialized map, asserting that only the initialized map succeeds.","test_input":"nil map followed by counts[\"error\"]=1; make(map[string]int) followed by the same assignment","expected_test_result":"The nil-map write panics; the initialized-map write succeeds and stores value 1.","testing_guidance":"Test zero values, constructors, struct fields, lazy initialization, and all mutation paths.","verification_method":"Trace every map mutation to an initialization guarantee and test zero-value instances of containing structs.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer initialization from a successful read; explicitly inspect the map value before mutation.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.21+","framework":"Go standard library","framework_version":"1.21+","version_range":"Go 1.0+","dependencies":["Go runtime"],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Go specification and built-in map semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B079","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Concurrency Deadlock","concept":"Inconsistent lock acquisition order creates a circular wait between concurrent operations","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can two concurrent operations permanently block even though each operation acquires only the locks it needs?","answer":"If two operations acquire multiple locks in opposite orders, each can hold one lock while waiting for the other. This circular wait creates a deadlock.","explanation":"Deadlock does not require excessive locking; it can arise from a valid-looking set of individual lock operations. The root cause is a violation of a globally consistent lock-ordering rule. The problem may be intermittent because the required interleaving is timing-dependent.","principle":"When multiple locks can be acquired by the same execution context, define and enforce one global lock acquisition order.","objective":"Diagnose deadlocks caused by inconsistent lock ordering.","requirements":["All multi-lock paths must follow a consistent acquisition order.","Lock ownership and release must remain well-defined."],"constraints":["The example uses C++ mutexes for illustration.","A consistent ordering strategy must be compatible with the application's object relationships."],"preconditions":["Two mutexes exist and both operations may need both locks."],"input":"Thread A acquires m1 then m2; Thread B acquires m2 then m1.","output":"Both operations acquire locks in the same global order, eliminating the circular wait.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent operations should either complete or fail according to defined synchronization semantics rather than wait indefinitely for each other.","incorrect_code":"std::mutex m1, m2;\nvoid a() { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> x(m1); std::lock_guard<std::mutex> y(m2); }\nvoid b() { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> x(m2); std::lock_guard<std::mutex> y(m1); }","corrected_code":"std::mutex m1, m2;\nvoid a() { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> x(m1); std::lock_guard<std::mutex> y(m2); }\nvoid b() { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> x(m1); std::lock_guard<std::mutex> y(m2); }","failure_mode":"Two threads can each hold one mutex while waiting for the mutex held by the other.","expected_error":"No language exception is required; the observable symptom is a hang or blocked threads with a circular wait.","cause":"Lock acquisition order differs between concurrent code paths.","root_cause":"No global lock-ordering invariant prevents circular wait.","solution":"Establish a deterministic lock order, or use synchronization primitives such as std::lock that acquire multiple mutexes without introducing an inconsistent order.","edge_case":"A lock-order rule must account for dynamically selected objects; address-based or stable identity ordering may be required when object relationships vary.","security_implication":"Deadlocks can cause service unavailability and may become denial-of-service conditions when triggered through externally controllable workloads.","performance_implication":"Longer lock hold times increase contention; ordering fixes deadlock risk but do not by themselves optimize contention.","test_case":"Run multiple threads repeatedly through both multi-lock paths under a stress scheduler and inspect for blocked threads.","test_input":"Concurrent repeated calls to a() and b()","expected_test_result":"No circular wait occurs when both paths acquire locks in the same defined order.","testing_guidance":"Use stress tests, thread sanitizers where supported, lock-order analysis, timeouts, and deadlock detection instrumentation.","verification_method":"Build a lock acquisition graph and verify that it contains no cycle for the permitted locking order.","verification_guidance":"Static review should cover all paths, not just the two functions shown in the example.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17","framework":"C++ standard library","framework_version":"C++17","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":["mutex","thread"],"execution_requirements":["C++17 compiler with threading support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B070"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ mutex semantics and established deadlock theory based on circular wait and lock-order invariants; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B080","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Build and Dependency Compatibility","concept":"Dependency API version drift causes compilation failure or incompatible behavior after an uncoordinated upgrade","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can code that previously compiled or behaved correctly fail after a dependency upgrade?","answer":"A dependency upgrade can change public APIs, defaults, types, removed symbols, or behavioral contracts. If the application source and dependency version are not treated as a compatible pair, version drift can produce build failures or runtime regressions.","explanation":"Dependency versions are part of the implementation environment. Updating a library without checking its compatibility contract can invalidate imports, method signatures, configuration, serialization formats, or assumptions encoded by application code.","principle":"Dependency upgrades must be treated as versioned compatibility changes and validated against the application's declared dependency contract.","objective":"Diagnose failures caused by dependency-version drift.","requirements":["Dependency versions must be explicitly controlled.","Upgrade changes must be checked against release and migration information.","Build and relevant tests must run against the new dependency version."],"constraints":["The example uses a generic library API to demonstrate version drift.","Exact API changes depend on the actual dependency and version range."],"preconditions":["Application source depends on a library API that changes between versions."],"input":"Application calls a method removed or renamed in the upgraded dependency.","output":"The application either remains on a compatible version or is migrated to the new API and verified.","expected_behavior":"Dependency upgrades should produce an intentional, validated change rather than an accidental compatibility break.","incorrect_code":"// requirements.txt\nexample-lib==2.0\n\n// application code was written against example-lib 1.x\nfrom example_lib import old_api\nresult = old_api(value)","corrected_code":"// Pin or constrain a version compatible with the current application contract.\n// requirements.txt\nexample-lib>=1.8,<2.0\n\nfrom example_lib import old_api\nresult = old_api(value)","failure_mode":"Build or runtime behavior changes because the installed dependency no longer matches the application's expected API contract.","expected_error":"ImportError, AttributeError, compilation error, type mismatch, or behavior-specific failure depending on the dependency.","cause":"A dependency version changed without corresponding source migration or compatibility validation.","root_cause":"The application's dependency contract is not synchronized with the API assumptions encoded in the source.","solution":"Pin or constrain compatible versions, review migration notes, update code when intentionally upgrading, and validate the resulting dependency graph.","edge_case":"Transitive dependencies can also change even when the direct dependency declaration remains unchanged.","security_implication":"Uncontrolled dependency changes can introduce vulnerable versions or incompatible security defaults; conversely, over-pinning can prevent required security updates.","performance_implication":"A dependency upgrade may change performance characteristics even when the API remains compatible, so performance-sensitive upgrades require targeted validation.","test_case":"Resolve the dependency graph for both the known-good and upgraded versions and run compilation/import tests plus behavior-critical tests.","test_input":"Known-good dependency lock versus upgraded dependency lock","expected_test_result":"The selected dependency graph is reproducible and the application passes required compatibility tests.","testing_guidance":"Use lockfiles or equivalent reproducible dependency resolution, compile/import tests, integration tests, and migration-specific regression tests.","verification_method":"Compare declared dependency constraints with actual resolved versions and verify compatibility against the dependency's documented API contract.","verification_guidance":"Record exact versions used during validation; a passing test against an unspecified environment is insufficient evidence of version compatibility.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python packaging ecosystem","framework_version":"PEP 440-compatible tooling","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Application dependency resolver/package manager"],"execution_requirements":["Python environment with reproducible dependency resolution"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":0.97,"related_records":["D36-B033"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python packaging/version-constraint standards and established dependency-management practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B081","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Exception Handling","concept":"Broad exception handling converts unrelated programming failures into misleading fallback behavior","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can catching every exception make the original bug difficult to diagnose and cause incorrect fallback behavior?","answer":"Catching Exception around a large block can intercept errors unrelated to the expected recoverable condition. The handler may then return a fallback value or continue execution, hiding programming errors and producing misleading downstream behavior.","explanation":"Exception handling should correspond to known failure conditions. A broad handler around unrelated operations collapses distinct failure causes into one recovery path, making diagnosis harder and potentially allowing corrupted or incomplete state to continue.","principle":"Catch only exceptions that the current layer can meaningfully handle and preserve unexpected failures for diagnosis.","objective":"Identify root causes caused by over-broad exception handling.","requirements":["Expected recoverable exceptions must be identified explicitly.","Unexpected programming errors must remain observable.","Fallback behavior must preserve system invariants."],"constraints":["The example uses Python built-in exceptions.","The handler represents a boundary where a specific lookup failure is recoverable."],"preconditions":["A function performs a dictionary lookup followed by processing that may contain unrelated programming errors."],"input":"Missing key and a programming error occurring inside processing.","output":"Only the missing-key condition is converted to the intended fallback.","expected_behavior":"An absent optional key may use a documented fallback, while unrelated exceptions propagate for diagnosis.","incorrect_code":"def load_name(data):\n    try:\n        value = data[\"name\"]\n        return value.strip()\n    except Exception:\n        return \"unknown\"","corrected_code":"def load_name(data):\n    try:\n        value = data[\"name\"]\n    except KeyError:\n        return \"unknown\"\n    return value.strip()","failure_mode":"Unrelated failures are swallowed and represented as a normal fallback value.","expected_error":"Unexpected exceptions should propagate rather than being converted into 'unknown'.","cause":"Exception handling scope is broader than the failure condition the code intends to recover from.","root_cause":"The implementation treats all exceptions as equivalent to the expected recoverable condition.","solution":"Narrow the try block and catch only the documented exception type that can be handled safely.","edge_case":"Some APIs intentionally use broad exception boundaries at process or request boundaries, but such handlers should log, classify, and preserve diagnostic context rather than silently suppress failures.","security_implication":"Swallowed exceptions can hide authorization, validation, integrity, or security-control failures and may cause unsafe fallback behavior.","performance_implication":"Exception handling itself is not a substitute for normal control flow; frequent exceptions used for expected conditions can add overhead.","test_case":"Test missing key, valid string, null-like value, and an injected programming failure.","test_input":"{}, {\"name\":\"Ada\"}, {\"name\":None}","expected_test_result":"Missing key returns the documented fallback; valid input succeeds; unrelated failures are not silently converted to the fallback.","testing_guidance":"Test each expected exception separately and inject unexpected failures inside the protected operation.","verification_method":"Review every broad exception handler and identify which exception types are intentionally recoverable.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failure logs or propagated exceptions preserve the original cause when recovery is not justified.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python standard library","framework_version":"3.11+","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B040"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python exception-handling semantics and established defensive error-handling practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B082","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Async Ordering","concept":"Promise results applied without request identity checking allow stale asynchronous responses to overwrite newer state","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can an older asynchronous response overwrite a newer result in JavaScript?","answer":"Multiple requests can complete in an order different from the order in which they were started. If every response writes directly to shared state without checking whether it still represents the current request, a stale response can overwrite newer data.","explanation":"The bug is a temporal ordering problem rather than a failure of Promise execution. The application assumes completion order matches initiation order, but asynchronous operations do not guarantee that relationship.","principle":"When asynchronous results update replaceable state, associate each operation with an identity or generation and reject results that are no longer current.","objective":"Diagnose stale asynchronous responses caused by completion-order races.","requirements":["Each state-changing request must have an identifiable generation or request identity.","Only current results may update replaceable state."],"constraints":["The example uses standard JavaScript Promises.","Cancellation is an alternative mitigation but does not replace response identity when cancellation cannot guarantee suppression."],"preconditions":["Two asynchronous requests can overlap and return in different orders."],"input":"Request A starts first; request B starts later but completes first.","output":"The result from B remains current and A is ignored when it eventually completes.","expected_behavior":"Only the response associated with the current request generation updates state.","incorrect_code":"let current = \"\";\n\nfunction search(query) {\n  fetch(`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`)\n    .then(r => r.json())\n    .then(data => {\n      current = data;\n    });\n}\n\nsearch(\"old\");\nsearch(\"new\");","corrected_code":"let current = \"\";\nlet generation = 0;\n\nfunction search(query) {\n  const requestGeneration = ++generation;\n  fetch(`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`)\n    .then(r => r.json())\n    .then(data => {\n      if (requestGeneration === generation) {\n        current = data;\n      }\n    });\n}","failure_mode":"A stale asynchronous completion mutates state after a newer operation has become authoritative.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is incorrect final state.","cause":"Completion order is assumed to match request-start order.","root_cause":"Asynchronous state updates lack request identity or generation validation.","solution":"Associate asynchronous work with a request generation, cancellation token, or equivalent current-operation identity before committing its result.","edge_case":"A request can fail after a newer request succeeds; stale error handlers must also avoid replacing current state.","security_implication":"Stale authorization, policy, or identity data can become dangerous if asynchronous responses affect security-sensitive state.","performance_implication":"Identity checks are negligible compared with network latency; cancellation may additionally reduce unnecessary work and bandwidth.","test_case":"Force the older request to resolve after the newer request and verify that the older result cannot change current state.","test_input":"Request A delayed 200 ms; request B delayed 20 ms.","expected_test_result":"B becomes current and A's late result is ignored.","testing_guidance":"Use controllable test promises or mocked network delays to deterministically reverse completion order.","verification_method":"Trace every asynchronous state commit and verify that its result is associated with the current operation identity.","verification_guidance":"Test both stale success and stale failure paths.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","framework":"Fetch API","framework_version":"WHATWG Fetch","version_range":"ES2018+","dependencies":["Fetch API"],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime with Promise and fetch support or equivalent test mock"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B032"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"ECMAScript Promise semantics and Fetch API asynchronous behavior; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B083","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript Type Narrowing","concept":"Aliasing a mutable object invalidates an earlier type-narrowing assumption","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a TypeScript value that was narrowed to a safe state later violate the assumption after mutation through an alias?","answer":"TypeScript's control-flow analysis cannot guarantee that arbitrary external mutation has not changed aliased mutable state. A property narrowed earlier can therefore require revalidation before later use when another operation may have changed the object.","explanation":"Static narrowing is based on information available along a control-flow path. Mutable aliases, callbacks, asynchronous boundaries, and external code can invalidate assumptions about object properties. Treating a prior narrowing as permanently authoritative can produce runtime failures.","principle":"Do not treat a narrowing of mutable shared state as an immutable invariant across mutation boundaries.","objective":"Diagnose runtime failures caused by stale TypeScript narrowing assumptions.","requirements":["Mutable state must be revalidated after potentially invalidating mutation.","Trusted immutable snapshots should be used when a value must remain stable."],"constraints":["The exact compiler behavior depends on the syntactic control-flow path.","Runtime JavaScript remains responsible for enforcing actual invariants."],"preconditions":["A mutable object can be modified through another reference between validation and use."],"input":"A value is initially narrowed to a string, then mutated to null through an alias.","output":"The corrected implementation captures the validated value or revalidates before use.","expected_behavior":"Operations must use a value whose invariant remains valid at the point of consumption.","incorrect_code":"type State = { value: string | null };\n\nfunction use(state: State, mutate: (s: State) => void) {\n  if (state.value !== null) {\n    mutate(state);\n    console.log(state.value.toUpperCase());\n  }\n}","corrected_code":"type State = { value: string | null };\n\nfunction use(state: State, mutate: (s: State) => void) {\n  if (state.value !== null) {\n    const value = state.value;\n    mutate(state);\n    console.log(value.toUpperCase());\n  }\n}","failure_mode":"A previously validated mutable property is changed before it is consumed.","expected_error":"Potential runtime TypeError such as Cannot read properties of null when the invariant is violated.","cause":"The same mutable object can be modified through an alias after the initial check.","root_cause":"A transient type-state assumption is incorrectly treated as a durable invariant across a mutation boundary.","solution":"Capture an immutable value after validation, avoid shared mutation, or revalidate after mutation.","edge_case":"Callbacks, event handlers, worker messages, and asynchronous operations can create less obvious mutation boundaries.","security_implication":"Stale validation can allow data to pass an authorization or validation check and then be consumed under different state.","performance_implication":"Copying small immutable values is usually inexpensive; larger snapshots can have measurable memory costs and should be designed intentionally.","test_case":"Mutate the object after the initial narrowing and verify that the corrected code still operates on the validated snapshot.","test_input":"state.value=\"safe\"; mutation sets state.value=null","expected_test_result":"The corrected function uses the captured string and does not dereference the mutated null property.","testing_guidance":"Test callbacks that mutate shared objects, asynchronous boundaries, aliases, and nested mutable state.","verification_method":"Identify mutation boundaries between narrowing and use and verify that the consumed value remains valid.","verification_guidance":"Do not rely solely on successful TypeScript compilation; exercise runtime mutation paths.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"TypeScript","framework_version":"5.x","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":["TypeScript compiler"],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STATIC_ANALYSIS_LIMITATION","confidence":0.96,"related_records":["D36-B003"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"TypeScript control-flow narrowing model combined with JavaScript mutable alias semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B084","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Resource Lifecycle","concept":"JDBC resources remain open when cleanup is not guaranteed across exceptional control flow","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a Java application gradually exhaust database connections even though each query appears to close its resources?","answer":"If a ResultSet, Statement, or Connection is closed only on the normal path, an exception before the cleanup statement can leave the resource open. Repeated failures can exhaust the connection pool or database session limit.","explanation":"Resource cleanup is a control-flow guarantee, not merely a statement placed after successful work. Java try-with-resources makes cleanup part of the language-supported resource-management structure and closes resources when leaving the block.","principle":"Acquire closeable resources inside try-with-resources so cleanup occurs on both normal and exceptional exits.","objective":"Diagnose resource exhaustion caused by exception-unsafe Java resource cleanup.","requirements":["Closeable database resources must be released on every exit path.","Resource ownership must be explicit."],"constraints":["The example uses JDBC semantics.","Actual pooling behavior depends on the configured connection pool."],"preconditions":["Database operations can throw before explicit close calls execute."],"input":"A query throws before the manually written close statement.","output":"try-with-resources closes the resource automatically when control leaves the block.","expected_behavior":"Every acquired JDBC resource is closed even when query processing fails.","incorrect_code":"Connection c = dataSource.getConnection();\nPreparedStatement s = c.prepareStatement(\"SELECT name FROM users\");\nResultSet r = s.executeQuery();\nString name = r.getString(1);\nr.close();\ns.close();\nc.close();","corrected_code":"try (Connection c = dataSource.getConnection();\n     PreparedStatement s = c.prepareStatement(\"SELECT name FROM users\");\n     ResultSet r = s.executeQuery()) {\n    if (r.next()) {\n        String name = r.getString(1);\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"An exception before explicit cleanup leaves one or more JDBC resources open.","expected_error":"SQLException or another runtime failure may occur before close statements execute; repeated failures can eventually cause pool exhaustion.","cause":"Resource release is dependent on reaching sequential cleanup statements.","root_cause":"The resource lifecycle is not structurally coupled to the scope in which the resource is owned.","solution":"Use try-with-resources and define resource ownership clearly.","edge_case":"Nested resources can have dependencies; try-with-resources closes resources in reverse declaration order.","security_implication":"Leaked connections can cause availability failures and may retain transactional or privileged database context longer than intended.","performance_implication":"Connection leaks degrade throughput and can exhaust pool capacity; deterministic cleanup prevents resource accumulation.","test_case":"Force query processing to throw and verify that the connection, statement, and result set are closed.","test_input":"Mock JDBC resource that throws during result processing","expected_test_result":"All acquired resources are closed after the exception.","testing_guidance":"Use resource-tracking test doubles and stress repeated exceptional paths until no resource count accumulates.","verification_method":"Inspect ownership scopes and verify every acquired AutoCloseable resource is enclosed by try-with-resources or equivalent guaranteed cleanup.","verification_guidance":"Test exceptional paths explicitly; a successful query test does not establish cleanup correctness.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":"JDBC","framework_version":"JDBC 4.3","version_range":"Java 7+","dependencies":["java.sql","JDBC driver"],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+ and a JDBC-compatible database or test double"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_LANGUAGE_AND_API_BEHAVIOR","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B038"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java try-with-resources and JDBC resource-management contracts; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B085","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Pointer Lifetime","concept":"Returning a pointer to an automatic local object creates a dangling pointer","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why does returning the address of a local C variable produce invalid memory access after the function returns?","answer":"An automatic local object ceases to exist when its function scope ends. A pointer to that object therefore becomes indeterminate for use as an object pointer after return, and dereferencing it produces undefined behavior.","explanation":"The pointer value may appear to retain the old stack address, which can make the bug seem to work during some runs. The storage lifetime has nevertheless ended, so later access is not valid.","principle":"Never return a pointer to an automatic local object whose lifetime ends when the function returns.","objective":"Identify dangling-pointer root causes caused by returning addresses of local variables.","requirements":["Returned pointers must refer to storage whose lifetime outlives the caller's use.","Ownership and lifetime must be explicit."],"constraints":["The example uses an automatic local variable.","Static storage, dynamic storage, or caller-provided storage have different lifetime rules."],"preconditions":["A function returns the address of a local variable."],"input":"int *get_value(void)","output":"Correct implementations return caller-owned storage or explicitly allocated storage with a defined ownership contract.","expected_behavior":"The returned pointer must remain valid for the documented period of use.","incorrect_code":"int *get_value(void) {\n    int value = 42;\n    return &value;\n}","corrected_code":"int get_value(void) {\n    int value = 42;\n    return value;\n}","failure_mode":"The caller dereferences a pointer to an object whose lifetime has ended.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior; no portable runtime exception is guaranteed.","cause":"The local variable's lifetime ends when get_value returns.","root_cause":"The pointer's lifetime is assumed to extend beyond the object's storage lifetime.","solution":"Return the value directly, use caller-provided storage, or allocate storage with an explicit ownership and release contract when pointer semantics are actually required.","edge_case":"Returning a pointer to static storage avoids automatic-lifetime expiration but introduces shared state and thread-safety considerations.","security_implication":"Dangling-pointer use is a memory-safety defect that can cause crashes, corruption, information disclosure, or exploitable behavior.","performance_implication":"Returning a small scalar by value is typically simpler and avoids allocation or ownership overhead.","test_case":"Compile with address-sanitizing tooling and exercise the returned pointer after the function returns.","test_input":"Call get_value and dereference the returned pointer","expected_test_result":"The incorrect implementation is identified as invalid by lifetime analysis or runtime sanitization; the corrected value-returning implementation is safe.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer and static lifetime analysis where available, but also inspect ownership and lifetime contracts manually.","verification_method":"Trace the pointed-to object's lifetime and verify it strictly exceeds every permitted dereference.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer safety from a pointer retaining a plausible address after scope exit.","language":"C","language_version":"C17","framework":"C standard library","framework_version":"C17","version_range":"C11-C23","dependencies":["C compiler"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler with optional sanitizer support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_LIFETIME_RULE","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B005"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"ISO C object lifetime rules and established memory-safety analysis practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B086","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Temporary Lifetime","concept":"Reference or pointer escapes the lifetime of a temporary object","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a C++ reference or pointer become invalid when it refers to a temporary object beyond the expression or lifetime extension rules?","answer":"A temporary object has a limited lifetime determined by the expression and binding context. A pointer or reference returned or stored beyond that lifetime can dangle even though the type system permits the reference operation itself.","explanation":"C++ has detailed temporary-lifetime rules, and lifetime extension applies only in specific binding contexts. Code that assumes every reference prolongs the temporary's lifetime can therefore create invalid references.","principle":"Do not assume reference binding universally extends a temporary's lifetime; make ownership and lifetime explicit at API boundaries.","objective":"Diagnose dangling references caused by incorrect assumptions about C++ temporary lifetimes.","requirements":["References must not outlive their referred objects.","APIs returning references or pointers must document the lifetime relationship."],"constraints":["Temporary lifetime extension depends on the exact expression and binding form.","The example intentionally illustrates an escaping reference."],"preconditions":["A function exposes a reference whose referent is a temporary local object."],"input":"A reference returned from a function refers to a local temporary string.","output":"The corrected API returns an owning value.","expected_behavior":"The returned object remains valid for the caller's use.","incorrect_code":"const std::string& name() {\n    return std::string(\"temporary\");\n}","corrected_code":"std::string name() {\n    return std::string(\"temporary\");\n}","failure_mode":"Caller receives a reference to an object whose lifetime has already ended.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior when the dangling reference is used; no portable exception is required.","cause":"The temporary string is destroyed when the function exits.","root_cause":"The API exposes a non-owning reference whose lifetime exceeds the temporary object's lifetime.","solution":"Return an owning value when the produced object must outlive the function scope, or establish a valid owner whose lifetime exceeds the reference.","edge_case":"Some temporary objects have lifetime extension when bound to certain local references, but that extension does not generally propagate through arbitrary returns or storage.","security_implication":"Dangling references are memory-safety defects that can lead to corruption or information disclosure.","performance_implication":"Returning by value is commonly optimized through move semantics and copy elision, reducing the need for unsafe borrowed references.","test_case":"Use lifetime-focused static analysis and sanitizers while exercising the returned object after function return.","test_input":"const std::string& x = name(); use x after name returns","expected_test_result":"The corrected value-returning API provides an independent valid object.","testing_guidance":"Review reference-returning APIs, temporary expressions, ownership, and lifetime extension rules; use sanitizers for supporting evidence.","verification_method":"Trace the lifetime of the referred object through the return boundary and compare it with the caller's permitted use.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact C++ lifetime rule for the expression instead of relying on informal reference intuition.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17","framework":"C++ standard library","framework_version":"C++17","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":["string"],"execution_requirements":["C++17 compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_LIFETIME_RULE","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B064"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ temporary-object lifetime rules and standard-library value semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B087","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Shared-State Concurrency","concept":"Using non-atomic shared mutable state across threads violates Rust's ownership and synchronization requirements","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why does Rust reject ordinary shared mutable state when multiple threads need to update it concurrently?","answer":"Rust's ownership model prevents unsynchronized shared mutation across threads because ordinary mutable references cannot safely alias across concurrent execution. Shared mutable state must use synchronization such as Mutex, RwLock, or atomic types according to the data's requirements.","explanation":"The compiler rejection is a signal that the program has not established a valid concurrency ownership model. Circumventing the model with unsafe code without proving the required invariants can turn a compile-time protection into a data race or memory-safety defect.","principle":"Choose an explicit synchronization and ownership strategy before sharing mutable state between threads.","objective":"Diagnose shared-state concurrency designs that lack a valid Rust synchronization model.","requirements":["Concurrent mutation must use a synchronization primitive appropriate to the data.","Unsafe code must uphold Rust's aliasing and concurrency invariants."],"constraints":["The example uses standard-library Mutex synchronization.","Atomic operations are preferable for some simple independent counters or flags."],"preconditions":["Multiple threads need to mutate one shared counter."],"input":"Shared counter accessed by spawned threads.","output":"Arc<Mutex<i32>> provides shared ownership plus mutual exclusion.","expected_behavior":"Every mutation occurs under the synchronization guarantee required by the chosen shared-state representation.","incorrect_code":"use std::thread;\n\nlet mut count = 0;\nlet handle = thread::spawn(|| {\n    // Attempting to mutate count from another thread is not permitted safely.\n    // count += 1;\n});\nhandle.join().unwrap();","corrected_code":"use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::thread;\n\nlet count = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));\nlet worker_count = Arc::clone(&count);\nlet handle = thread::spawn(move || {\n    *worker_count.lock().unwrap() += 1;\n});\nhandle.join().unwrap();","failure_mode":"The program cannot establish safe concurrent ownership and mutation of shared state.","expected_error":"Compilation failure involving ownership, borrowing, Send, or Sync requirements depending on the attempted implementation.","cause":"Multiple execution contexts require mutable access without an established synchronization or ownership strategy.","root_cause":"The data-sharing design does not encode the required concurrency invariant in Rust's ownership model.","solution":"Use message passing, atomics, Mutex/RwLock, or another synchronization strategy selected according to the data semantics.","edge_case":"A Mutex can prevent data races but does not automatically prevent logical races, deadlocks, starvation, or incorrect transaction ordering.","security_implication":"Unsafe synchronization can cause memory corruption or inconsistent security-sensitive state; lock poisoning and failure handling must also be considered.","performance_implication":"Mutex contention can reduce throughput; atomics or sharded state may be appropriate for workloads where their semantics are sufficient.","test_case":"Spawn multiple workers that increment the same counter and verify the final value equals the number of successful increments.","test_input":"N worker threads each perform one synchronized increment.","expected_test_result":"Final counter equals N after all worker joins complete.","testing_guidance":"Use stress tests, repeated scheduling, contention testing, and race-detection tooling where available.","verification_method":"Review ownership, Send/Sync requirements, synchronization boundaries, and mutation access paths.","verification_guidance":"Prove the synchronization invariant rather than treating successful compilation as proof of logical correctness.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":"Rust standard library","framework_version":"1.75+","version_range":"Rust 1.0+","dependencies":["std::sync","std::thread"],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler with thread support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_CONCURRENCY_MODEL","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B037","D36-B079"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Rust ownership, Send/Sync, synchronization, and thread APIs; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B088","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Slice Aliasing","concept":"Appending through one alias can invalidate assumptions about another slice sharing the same backing array","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can two Go slices that initially appear to share data behave differently after append?","answer":"A slice is a descriptor over an underlying array. append may reuse the existing backing array when capacity permits or allocate a new one when capacity is exceeded. Aliased slices can therefore diverge after append, or mutations can unexpectedly affect another slice when storage is still shared.","explanation":"The bug comes from treating a slice as if it were an independent array value. The slice header is copied by value, but the backing storage may remain shared. append changes the slice descriptor and may or may not change the underlying storage.","principle":"Treat slice aliasing and capacity as part of the data-sharing contract whenever append or mutation occurs.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect assumptions about Go slice backing-array sharing.","requirements":["Code must know whether slice aliases are intentional.","Independent ownership requires copying when shared backing storage would be unsafe or surprising."],"constraints":["Exact aliasing behavior depends on available capacity at append time.","The example uses ordinary Go slices."],"preconditions":["Two slices refer to overlapping backing storage and one is appended or mutated."],"input":"base := []int{1,2,3}; alias := base[:2]","output":"A copy can be used when independent storage is required.","expected_behavior":"Mutations and append operations must follow the documented ownership and aliasing expectation.","incorrect_code":"base := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}\nalias := base[:2]\nalias = append(alias, 99)\n// alias may reuse base's backing array and overwrite base[2]","corrected_code":"base := []int{1, 2, 3, 4}\nalias := append([]int(nil), base[:2]...)\nalias = append(alias, 99)\n// alias has independent backing storage","failure_mode":"A slice mutation unexpectedly changes another slice, or append causes two previously related views to diverge.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is unexpected data mutation or divergence.","cause":"Multiple slice headers reference the same backing array and append behavior depends on capacity.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes slice values imply independent storage.","solution":"Document ownership, control capacity when aliasing is intentional, or copy the elements when independent storage is required.","edge_case":"Full-slice expressions can constrain capacity and reduce accidental append into shared storage, but they do not by themselves create independent storage.","security_implication":"Unexpected aliasing can corrupt security-sensitive buffers, configuration state, or request data shared between components.","performance_implication":"Copying creates allocation and memory-copy cost; avoiding unnecessary copies can improve performance when aliasing is intentionally controlled.","test_case":"Use slices with spare capacity and without spare capacity, then mutate or append through an alias and inspect the original.","test_input":"base with capacity greater than length; base with capacity equal to length","expected_test_result":"Tests expose whether backing storage is shared and confirm the chosen ownership contract.","testing_guidance":"Test append at capacity boundaries, overlapping subslices, mutation after append, and independent-copy behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect slice length, capacity, backing-array relationships, and ownership expectations at every aliasing boundary.","verification_guidance":"Do not reason from slice length alone; capacity affects append behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.21+","framework":"Go standard library","framework_version":"1.21+",