{"record_id":"D36-B089","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"API Contract and State Mutation","concept":"Validation performed before a state mutation can become stale when the validated state changes before commit","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a resource pass an availability check and still be unavailable when the operation commits?","answer":"A check-then-act sequence is vulnerable when another operation can modify the resource between validation and mutation. The earlier check proves only the state observed at that moment, not the state at commit time.","explanation":"The bug is a time-of-check-to-time-of-use race. A system that separately checks availability and later performs an unrestricted mutation creates a gap in which another actor can change the state.","principle":"Make validation and state transition atomic or revalidate under the same concurrency/transaction boundary that performs the commit.","objective":"Diagnose stale precondition checks in concurrent or transactional operations.","requirements":["The state transition must enforce its preconditions at commit time.","Concurrent modifications must be serialized or detected."],"constraints":["The example uses a database-style conceptual transaction.","Exact SQL syntax and isolation behavior depend on the database system."],"preconditions":["Two operations can attempt to consume the same limited resource concurrently."],"input":"Resource has quantity=1; two requests both check quantity before either commits.","output":"Only one transaction successfully consumes the resource.","expected_behavior":"The system must not allow both operations to commit based on the same stale availability observation.","incorrect_code":"if (get_quantity(resource_id) > 0) {\n    insert_allocation(resource_id, user_id);\n}","corrected_code":"UPDATE resources\nSET quantity = quantity - 1\nWHERE id = ? AND quantity > 0;\n\n-- Commit only when the affected-row count is 1.","failure_mode":"Two callers observe the same available state and both perform a state-changing operation.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is over-allocation or an invariant violation.","cause":"Validation and mutation occur as separate operations without an atomic precondition.","root_cause":"The system treats a transient observation as a guaranteed invariant across a concurrent state transition.","solution":"Enforce the condition atomically in the mutation, use an appropriate transaction/isolation strategy, or use a concurrency control mechanism that detects conflicts.","edge_case":"Retries after transaction conflicts must be designed so they do not duplicate side effects.","security_implication":"TOCTOU races can bypass authorization, quota, uniqueness, or resource-ownership checks when the validated state changes before commit.","performance_implication":"Atomic conditional updates and transactions can increase contention but avoid costly compensating work caused by invariant violations.","test_case":"Run concurrent allocation attempts against a single available resource and verify that exactly one succeeds.","test_input":"quantity=1; two concurrent allocation requests","expected_test_result":"One request commits successfully and the other observes zero affected rows or an equivalent conflict result.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic concurrency tests, transaction isolation tests, and invariant assertions around the commit boundary.","verification_method":"Identify the interval between every validation and state mutation and verify that the invariant remains protected throughout it.","verification_guidance":"Test concurrency rather than validating only sequential execution.","language":"SQL","language_version":"SQL standard concepts","framework":"Relational database","framework_version":"Database-specific","version_range":"Database-dependent","dependencies":["Relational database transaction support"],"execution_requirements":["Relational database supporting conditional atomic updates"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B009"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Established TOCTOU/concurrency-control pattern and relational conditional-update semantics; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B090","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Numeric Representation","concept":"Floating-point equality comparison fails for mathematically equivalent calculations because binary floating-point cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can two floating-point calculations that are mathematically equal compare as unequal?","answer":"Binary floating-point represents many decimal fractions approximately. Different operation sequences can therefore produce slightly different representable values, making direct equality unsuitable for many numerical comparisons.","explanation":"The failure is not necessarily arithmetic corruption. It results from finite binary representation and rounding during operations. The correct comparison strategy depends on the numerical domain, error tolerance, scale, and whether exact decimal or rational semantics are required.","principle":"Choose a numerical representation and comparison rule that matches the required precision and domain semantics.","objective":"Diagnose incorrect branching caused by exact equality checks on floating-point results.","requirements":["Numerical comparison must define an acceptable error model when approximate arithmetic is intended.","Exact financial or discrete decimal semantics should use an appropriate exact representation."],"constraints":["A single universal epsilon is not correct for every numerical scale or algorithm.","The example uses IEEE-style binary floating-point behavior."],"preconditions":["Two mathematically equivalent decimal calculations are performed using binary floating-point."],"input":"0.1 + 0.2 and 0.3","output":"The application uses an explicit tolerance for approximate comparison.","expected_behavior":"Approximate numerical equality is evaluated according to domain-specific tolerance or an exact representation is used when exactness is required.","incorrect_code":"double a = 0.1 + 0.2;\ndouble b = 0.3;\nif (a == b) {\n    System.out.println(\"equal\");\n}","corrected_code":"double a = 0.1 + 0.2;\ndouble b = 0.3;\ndouble tolerance = 1e-12;\nif (Math.abs(a - b) <= tolerance) {\n    System.out.println(\"approximately equal\");\n}","failure_mode":"A mathematically expected equality branch is not taken because floating-point representations differ.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is an unexpected false equality comparison.","cause":"Decimal fractions and operation results are represented approximately in binary floating-point.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes floating-point equality has the same semantics as mathematical real-number equality.","solution":"Use domain-appropriate approximate comparison or an exact decimal/rational representation when exact equality is required.","edge_case":"Absolute tolerance alone can fail for very large or very small magnitudes; relative or combined tolerance may be required.","security_implication":"Incorrect numeric comparisons can affect limits, quotas, financial thresholds, risk calculations, or security policy decisions.","performance_implication":"Higher-precision or arbitrary-precision arithmetic can cost more CPU and memory; tolerance comparisons are generally inexpensive.","test_case":"Compare equivalent arithmetic expressions across small, large, and near-zero values using the chosen comparison policy.","test_input":"0.1+0.2 versus 0.3; large magnitude values; values near zero","expected_test_result":"The comparison follows the documented numerical tolerance or exact representation policy.","testing_guidance":"Test scale-sensitive tolerances, accumulated error, cancellation, NaN, infinity, and domain-specific boundary conditions.","verification_method":"Document the required numerical semantics and verify that the representation and comparison method satisfy those semantics.","verification_guidance":"Do not choose an arbitrary epsilon without considering units, scale, accumulated error, and required correctness.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":"Java standard library","framework_version":"17+","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":["java.lang.Math"],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"NUMERICAL_COMPUTING_RULE","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"IEEE-style floating-point representation principles and Java floating-point behavior; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D36-B091","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Python Serialization Boundary","concept":"Deserialization accepts structurally valid but semantically invalid data because schema validation is omitted","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can Python code fail later even though JSON deserialization itself succeeds?","answer":"JSON parsing only establishes that the input is syntactically valid JSON. It does not prove that required fields, types, ranges, relationships, or domain invariants are valid. Using the resulting dictionary as trusted domain state can therefore move an invalid-input failure deeper into the application.","explanation":"Parsing and validation are separate operations. A payload such as {\"age\":\"unknown\"} can be valid JSON while violating the application's domain contract. The resulting delayed failure is often harder to diagnose because the original trust-boundary violation occurred earlier.","principle":"Treat deserialized external data as untrusted until the required structural and semantic validation has completed.","objective":"Diagnose failures caused by confusing successful parsing with successful validation.","requirements":["External data must be validated before entering trusted domain logic.","Required fields and types must be checked.","Domain constraints must be enforced at the boundary."],"constraints":["The example uses Python standard-library JSON parsing.","The correction demonstrates manual validation rather than a third-party schema framework."],"preconditions":["An external JSON payload is parsed into a Python object.","The application requires age to be an integer within a defined range."],"input":"{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"unknown\"}","output":"Validation rejects the payload before domain processing.","expected_behavior":"Only data satisfying the domain contract should enter trusted business logic.","incorrect_code":"import json\n\npayload = json.loads('{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"unknown\"}')\nage = payload[\"age\"]\nprint(age + 1)","corrected_code":"import json\n\npayload = json.loads('{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"unknown\"}')\nage = payload.get(\"age\")\nif not isinstance(age, int) or isinstance(age, bool) or not 0 <= age <= 150:\n    raise ValueError(\"age must be an integer between 0 and 150\")\nprint(age + 1)","failure_mode":"Invalid external data reaches business logic and fails at an unrelated operation.","expected_error":"ValueError: age must be an integer between 0 and 150","cause":"JSON syntax validity is mistaken for domain validity.","root_cause":"The trust boundary performs parsing without performing the validation required by the domain contract.","solution":"Separate parsing from validation and reject invalid data before domain processing.","edge_case":"Python bool is a subclass of int, so boolean values require explicit exclusion when an actual integer is required.","security_implication":"Insufficient input validation can permit malformed or attacker-controlled values to reach authorization, resource, query, or state-changing logic.","performance_implication":"Early validation adds boundary-processing cost but prevents deeper failures, retries, and corrupted state.","test_case":"Test syntactically valid JSON with valid, missing, wrong-type, boolean, negative, and excessive age values.","test_input":"{\"age\":42}, {\"age\":\"42\"}, {}, {\"age\":true}, {\"age\":-1}, {\"age\":151}","expected_test_result":"Only values satisfying the declared domain constraints are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Separate parser tests from schema/domain validation tests and include malformed JSON as a separate failure class.","verification_method":"Trace external data from deserialization to first trusted use and verify validation occurs before the trust transition.","verification_guidance":"Successful json.loads execution must not be treated as evidence that the payload is safe or semantically valid.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python standard library","framework_version":"3.11+","version_range":"Python 3.11+","dependencies":["json"],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B073"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Python JSON parsing semantics and established trust-boundary validation practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B092","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"JavaScript Resource Lifecycle","concept":"Event listeners accumulate because repeated registration is not paired with removal","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a JavaScript component execute the same event handler multiple times after repeated mounting or initialization?","answer":"Each initialization can register another listener against the same event target. If the lifecycle does not remove the previous listener, callbacks accumulate and every event triggers all registered handlers.","explanation":"The bug is a lifecycle ownership failure. Event registration creates a resource relationship that must be released when the owning component or operation is destroyed. Repeated initialization without cleanup can produce duplicate side effects and memory retention.","principle":"Every lifecycle-owned event subscription must have a deterministic cleanup path.","objective":"Diagnose duplicate event execution and listener-related memory growth caused by missing subscription cleanup.","requirements":["Subscriptions must be associated with an owner or lifecycle.","Cleanup must occur when the owner becomes inactive.","Repeated initialization must not multiply active listeners unintentionally."],"constraints":["The example uses DOM EventTarget semantics.","Framework-specific lifecycle APIs may provide automatic cleanup but do not change the underlying ownership requirement."],"preconditions":["A component initialization function may run multiple times.","Each call adds an event listener."],"input":"Repeated calls to initialize().","output":"At most one intended listener remains active for the component.","expected_behavior":"One user action should trigger one intended handler for one active component instance.","incorrect_code":"function initialize() {\n  window.addEventListener(\"resize\", handleResize);\n}\n\ninitialize();\ninitialize();","corrected_code":"let initialized = false;\n\nfunction initialize() {\n  if (initialized) return;\n  initialized = true;\n  window.addEventListener(\"resize\", handleResize);\n}\n\nfunction destroy() {\n  if (!initialized) return;\n  initialized = false;\n  window.removeEventListener(\"resize\", handleResize);\n}","failure_mode":"The same logical component receives multiple callback invocations for one event.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptoms are duplicate side effects, increasing listener count, and retained component state.","cause":"Listener registration is not paired with lifecycle cleanup or idempotent initialization.","root_cause":"The event subscription has no explicit ownership and release invariant.","solution":"Make registration idempotent and/or pair every addEventListener call with removeEventListener during teardown.","edge_case":"Anonymous functions cannot be removed unless the exact function reference is retained.","security_implication":"Stale listeners may retain sensitive objects or execute outdated authorization/state logic after a component is no longer active.","performance_implication":"Repeated listeners increase callback work per event and can retain memory through captured closures.","test_case":"Initialize and destroy the component repeatedly and assert that exactly one active listener exists during the component lifetime.","test_input":"initialize(); initialize(); destroy(); initialize();","expected_test_result":"The active listener count matches the component lifecycle and does not grow after repeated initialization.","testing_guidance":"Test mount/unmount cycles, hot reload behavior, route changes, and repeated subscriptions.","verification_method":"Track each subscription's owner and verify that its cleanup executes exactly once for each ownership lifetime.","verification_guidance":"Inspect listener registration paths rather than testing only callback output.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","framework":"DOM EventTarget","framework_version":"WHATWG DOM","version_range":"Modern browsers","dependencies":["DOM EventTarget"],"execution_requirements":["Browser or DOM-compatible runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"API_LIFECYCLE_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"WHATWG DOM EventTarget subscription/removal semantics and established lifecycle-management practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B093","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"TypeScript API Contract","concept":"API response shape is changed without updating consumers, causing runtime property access failures despite successful compilation of isolated components","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a TypeScript application compile successfully while a consumer still receives undefined data from an API?","answer":"Compile-time interfaces do not automatically enforce the actual network response. If a server changes a field name or nesting while the client retains an outdated interface, TypeScript can continue compiling because the declared type describes an assumption rather than the runtime payload.","explanation":"The failure occurs at the boundary between independently deployed systems. Static types can describe the expected contract, but without runtime validation or contract testing they cannot prove that the remote service actually returned that shape.","principle":"Treat cross-process API schemas as versioned runtime contracts, not merely TypeScript declarations.","objective":"Diagnose client/server schema drift hidden by static type declarations.","requirements":["API schemas must be versioned or otherwise contract-controlled.","Runtime responses should be validated when trust requires it.","Breaking changes require coordinated migration or compatibility handling."],"constraints":["The example uses a simplified fetch boundary.","The TypeScript interface alone does not validate runtime JSON."],"preconditions":["Server returns user_name while client expects name."],"input":"{\"user_name\":\"Ada\"}","output":"The client rejects or explicitly maps the incompatible response instead of assuming name exists.","expected_behavior":"The client must detect or correctly adapt to the actual API contract.","incorrect_code":"interface User {\n  name: string;\n}\n\nasync function loadUser(): Promise<User> {\n  const response = await fetch(\"/user\");\n  return await response.json() as User;\n}\n\nconst user = await loadUser();\nconsole.log(user.name.toUpperCase());","corrected_code":"interface User {\n  name: string;\n}\n\nfunction parseUser(value: unknown): User {\n  if (typeof value !== \"object\" || value === null || !(\"name\" in value) || typeof value.name !== \"string\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Invalid User response\");\n  }\n  return { name: value.name };\n}\n\nasync function loadUser(): Promise<User> {\n  const response = await fetch(\"/user\");\n  return parseUser(await response.json());\n}","failure_mode":"A remote response violates the assumed interface and causes undefined access or invalid downstream state.","expected_error":"Error: Invalid User response or a later TypeError if unchecked.","cause":"The client-side interface is treated as runtime evidence of the server payload.","root_cause":"The cross-process API contract is represented statically but not verified at runtime or through contract validation.","solution":"Use shared/versioned schemas, contract tests, runtime validation, or explicit compatibility adapters.","edge_case":"Backward-compatible additive fields may not require immediate consumer changes, while renamed or removed required fields usually require migration.","security_implication":"Schema drift can disable security checks or cause default paths when required identity, authorization, or policy fields disappear.","performance_implication":"Runtime validation adds response-processing cost proportional to payload size but can prevent expensive downstream failures.","test_case":"Provide responses matching the expected contract and responses using renamed, missing, null, and wrong-type fields.","test_input":"{\"name\":\"Ada\"}; {\"user_name\":\"Ada\"}; {}; {\"name\":42}","expected_test_result":"Only contract-valid responses enter trusted User state.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests between client and server and validate representative real response fixtures.","verification_method":"Compare the actual wire schema with the declared TypeScript type and verify a runtime or contract-level enforcement mechanism.","verification_guidance":"Compilation of the client alone is insufficient evidence of cross-service contract compatibility.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"Fetch API","framework_version":"WHATWG Fetch","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":["TypeScript compiler","Fetch API"],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler and JavaScript runtime with fetch or equivalent"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"API_CONTRACT_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B033","D36-B073"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"TypeScript static typing semantics and cross-service API contract engineering practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B094","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Java Initialization Order","concept":"Static initialization dependency cycle produces partially initialized or failed class state","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can Java classes fail during startup even though each static initializer appears valid independently?","answer":"Static initialization occurs as classes are initialized and can trigger initialization of other classes. A cyclic dependency can therefore expose partially initialized state or cause initialization failure when one class depends on another during class initialization.","explanation":"The individual initializers may be locally correct while the global initialization dependency graph is cyclic. Startup then becomes sensitive to initialization order and can fail before normal application logic begins.","principle":"Static initialization should have an acyclic dependency structure and avoid complex cross-class side effects.","objective":"Diagnose startup failures caused by cyclic static initialization dependencies.","requirements":["Static initialization dependencies should be explicit and preferably acyclic.","Initialization should not rely on partially initialized global state."],"constraints":["Exact initialization behavior depends on Java class initialization rules.","The example is intentionally simplified to expose the dependency cycle."],"preconditions":["Class A initialization accesses Class B static state while Class B accesses Class A state."],"input":"Two classes with mutually dependent static initialization.","output":"Initialization dependencies are refactored so required state is constructed through explicit lifecycle control.","expected_behavior":"Class initialization should not depend on partially initialized state from another class.","incorrect_code":"class A {\n    static final int VALUE = B.VALUE + 1;\n}\n\nclass B {\n    static final int VALUE = A.VALUE + 1;\n}","corrected_code":"final class Values {\n    static final int A_VALUE = 1;\n    static final int B_VALUE = A_VALUE + 1;\n\n    private Values() {}\n}","failure_mode":"Class initialization observes incomplete state or fails with an initialization-related exception.","expected_error":"ExceptionInInitializerError or a related initialization failure may occur depending on the actual dependency and failure path.","cause":"Static field initialization creates a cyclic class-initialization dependency.","root_cause":"Global initialization order is implicitly determined by cross-class static side effects rather than an explicit construction dependency graph.","solution":"Remove cyclic static dependencies, centralize immutable constants, or move complex construction into an explicit lifecycle controlled by the application.","edge_case":"Lazy holders can defer initialization but do not eliminate an underlying cyclic dependency if initialization paths still recurse.","security_implication":"Initialization failures can prevent security controls from loading, while partially initialized policy state can create incorrect defaults.","performance_implication":"Complex static initialization can increase startup latency and make startup failures harder to isolate.","test_case":"Load each involved class independently and together while exercising the complete initialization dependency graph.","test_input":"Class loading of A and B from a fresh JVM.","expected_test_result":"The corrected design initializes deterministically without cyclic dependency failures.","testing_guidance":"Use isolated JVM tests where necessary and inspect class-initialization traces for complex startup systems.","verification_method":"Construct a directed graph of static initialization dependencies and identify cycles.","verification_guidance":"Verify initialization ordering as a system property rather than inspecting each static initializer independently.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":"Java runtime","framework_version":"17+","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":["JVM class initialization"],"execution_requirements":["JDK 17+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_INITIALIZATION_RULE","confidence":0.96,"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Java class initialization semantics and established initialization-dependency analysis; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B095","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C Resource Ownership","concept":"Ownership transfer ambiguity causes double release or resource leaks in C APIs","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a C program either leak or double-free a resource when ownership is passed between functions?","answer":"C does not encode ownership transfer in the type system. If the caller and callee both assume they own the same allocation, the resource may be released twice; if neither assumes ownership after transfer, it may leak.","explanation":"The defect is an ownership-contract failure. Memory, file descriptors, handles, and other resources require an explicit owner and a defined transfer point. Without that contract, callers and callees can make incompatible cleanup decisions.","principle":"Every resource must have one clearly defined owner at each lifecycle stage, with explicit transfer and release rules.","objective":"Diagnose C resource-management bugs caused by ambiguous ownership transfer.","requirements":["Ownership must be documented or encoded by API conventions.","Exactly one component should release an owned resource.","Transfer operations must define whether the caller retains or loses ownership."],"constraints":["The example uses malloc/free for clarity.","The same reasoning applies to other manually managed C resources."],"preconditions":["A function accepts an allocated buffer and may assume ownership.","Caller also frees the buffer without a documented transfer contract."],"input":"Caller allocates buffer, passes it to consume(), and both caller and callee free it.","output":"The ownership contract is explicit and only the owner releases the buffer.","expected_behavior":"The buffer is freed exactly once after the final owner finishes using it.","incorrect_code":"void consume(char *buffer) {\n    printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n    free(buffer);\n}\n\nchar *buffer = malloc(32);\nconsume(buffer);\nfree(buffer);","corrected_code":"void consume(char *buffer) {\n    printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n    free(buffer); // consume() takes ownership\n}\n\nchar *buffer = malloc(32);\nconsume(buffer);\nbuffer = NULL; // caller no longer owns the allocation","failure_mode":"The same allocation is released by both caller and callee, or ownership is lost and the allocation leaks.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior on double free; a leak if no component releases the allocation.","cause":"Caller and callee have incompatible assumptions about resource ownership.","root_cause":"The API does not establish a single authoritative ownership contract.","solution":"Document ownership transfer explicitly, use naming conventions, wrapper APIs, or static-analysis annotations where available, and clear transferred pointers in the caller when appropriate.","edge_case":"Setting a pointer to NULL after transfer prevents accidental reuse of that pointer but does not itself establish ownership correctness.","security_implication":"Double-free and use-after-free defects are memory-safety vulnerabilities that can lead to corruption or code execution.","performance_implication":"Ownership clarity prevents leaks and repeated allocation pressure; defensive copying may add cost but can simplify ownership at selected boundaries.","test_case":"Exercise the API under ownership-transfer and non-transfer contracts and run memory sanitization tools.","test_input":"Allocated buffer passed to consume(); caller attempts post-call release.","expected_test_result":"Exactly one release occurs according to the documented contract.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer, leak detection, static analysis, and ownership-focused code review.","verification_method":"For every allocation, trace creation, ownership transfer, use, and release through all control-flow paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify both success and failure paths, including early returns and partial initialization.","lan