{"record_id":"D36-B096","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"C++ Exception and Lock Lifecycle","concept":"Manual lock management leaks synchronization ownership when an exception exits before unlock","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can one exception permanently block other C++ threads even though a function explicitly unlocks its mutex?","answer":"If unlock is performed manually after operations that can throw, an exception can bypass the unlock statement and leave the mutex locked. RAII lock guards tie lock ownership to scope so destruction releases the mutex during both normal and exceptional exit.","explanation":"The bug is a resource-lifetime problem applied to synchronization. A mutex is a resource whose release must be guaranteed. Manual unlock statements are control-flow fragile when exceptions can interrupt execution.","principle":"Use RAII synchronization guards such as std::lock_guard or std::unique_lock instead of manually pairing lock and unlock across exception-prone code.","objective":"Diagnose mutex leaks caused by exception-unsafe manual synchronization management.","requirements":["Every acquired mutex must be released on all control-flow paths.","Synchronization ownership must be scoped.","Exception paths must be included in the release guarantee."],"constraints":["The example uses standard C++ mutexes.","The correction assumes the critical section does not require deferred locking behavior."],"preconditions":["A function manually locks a mutex and performs an operation that may throw before unlock.","Another thread later needs the same mutex."],"input":"Mutex lock followed by throwing operation.","output":"RAII guard releases the mutex when the scope exits.","expected_behavior":"Exceptions must not leave the mutex permanently locked.","incorrect_code":"std::mutex mutex;\n\nvoid update() {\n    mutex.lock();\n    perform_operation_that_may_throw();\n    mutex.unlock();\n}","corrected_code":"std::mutex mutex;\n\nvoid update() {\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);\n    perform_operation_that_may_throw();\n}","failure_mode":"An exception exits the function before manual unlock, leaving other threads blocked indefinitely.","expected_error":"The original operation may throw; the synchronization failure manifests as blocked threads rather than a required exception.","cause":"Unlock is placed after an exception-prone operation.","root_cause":"Mutex lifetime is managed manually rather than being bound to lexical scope.","solution":"Use RAII lock guards and choose the appropriate standard synchronization wrapper for the required locking semantics.","edge_case":"unique_lock is appropriate when deferred locking, manual unlock/relock, or condition-variable integration is required.","security_implication":"A locked security-critical mutex can cause denial of service and prevent policy or audit operations from progressing.","performance_implication":"RAII lock guards have minimal abstraction overhead and prevent prolonged contention caused by leaked locks.","test_case":"Force an exception inside the critical section and then attempt to acquire the same mutex from another thread.","test_input":"perform_operation_that_may_throw() throws after mutex acquisition.","expected_test_result":"The second operation can acquire the mutex after the first operation exits exceptionally.","testing_guidance":"Test exceptional exits, early returns, nested locks, and contention under repeated failure conditions.","verification_method":"Inspect synchronization ownership and verify every lock acquisition is paired with scope-guaranteed release.","verification_guidance":"A normal-path test does not establish exception safety of lock management.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17","framework":"C++ standard library","framework_version":"C++17","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":["mutex"],"execution_requirements":["C++17 compiler with threading support"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"EXCEPTION_SAFETY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B079"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"C++ RAII and mutex semantics plus established exception-safety practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B097","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Rust Error-State Integrity","concept":"Partial state mutation before a fallible operation leaves inconsistent state when the operation fails","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a Rust operation leave an object internally inconsistent even though Rust prevents memory-safety violations?","answer":"Rust guarantees memory safety but does not automatically guarantee application-level transactional invariants. If code mutates one part of a state structure and then a fallible operation fails before the remaining mutation occurs, the object can remain logically inconsistent.","explanation":"Ownership and borrowing prevent many classes of memory bugs, but business and state invariants remain the programmer's responsibility. Multi-step mutations should either be prepared before commit, performed transactionally, or rolled back explicitly when failure is possible.","principle":"Separate fallible preparation from state commit so partial failure cannot leave a broken logical invariant.","objective":"Diagnose logical state corruption caused by non-atomic multi-step mutation in safe Rust.","requirements":["State invariants must hold after every externally observable operation.","Fallible work should complete before committing multi-field state changes when practical."],"constraints":["Rust memory safety does not imply application-level transactional consistency.","The example uses a simple in-memory state object."],"preconditions":["An operation changes one field and then performs a fallible operation before changing a dependent field."],"input":"Inventory state with quantity and audit record that must remain consistent.","output":"The corrected implementation prepares the fallible result before committing state.","expected_behavior":"Either the complete logical state transition succeeds or the original state remains valid.","incorrect_code":"struct Account {\n    balance: i64,\n    audit: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nfn debit(account: &mut Account, amount: i64) -> Result<(), String> {\n    account.balance -= amount;\n    let event = create_audit_event()?;\n    account.audit.push(event);\n    Ok(())\n}","corrected_code":"struct Account {\n    balance: i64,\n    audit: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nfn debit(account: &mut Account, amount: i64) -> Result<(), String> {\n    let event = create_audit_event()?;\n    account.balance -= amount;\n    account.audit.push(event);\n    Ok(())\n}","failure_mode":"A fallible operation fails after one part of the logical state has already been changed.","expected_error":"The fallible operation returns Err; the incorrect implementation has already modified balance.","cause":"State mutation occurs before all failure-prone preparation has completed.","root_cause":"The implementation lacks an atomic commit boundary for the logical state invariant.","solution":"Perform fallible preparation first, then commit the related state changes together; use a transaction or explicit rollback when preparation cannot be separated.","edge_case":"If the final commit itself can fail, a stronger transactional abstraction or rollback strategy is required.","security_implication":"Partial security, billing, quota, or authorization state can produce inconsistent policy enforcement and audit records.","performance_implication":"Preparing data before mutation can temporarily increase memory usage but avoids expensive recovery and inconsistency handling.","test_case":"Force audit-event creation to fail and verify that the account balance remains unchanged.","test_input":"debit(account, amount) with create_audit_event() returning Err","expected_test_result":"The operation returns Err and the original account state remains unchanged.","testing_guidance":"Test every fallible step independently and assert state invariants after each failure path.","verification_method":"Identify the logical invariant and prove that no externally visible mutation occurs before all required fallible prerequisites succeed.","verification_guidance":"Compilation and borrow-checking are not sufficient evidence of transactional correctness.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":"Rust standard library","framework_version":"1.75+","version_range":"Rust 1.0+","dependencies":["Rust standard library"],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"STATE_INTEGRITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B037","D36-B065"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Rust Result/error semantics and established transactional state-management practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B098","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Go Interface and Error Semantics","concept":"Returning a typed nil pointer inside a non-nil interface causes nil checks to pass incorrectly","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can an error or interface value in Go compare non-nil even though it contains a nil pointer?","answer":"An interface value contains both a dynamic type and a dynamic value. If a typed nil pointer is stored in an interface, the interface itself can be non-nil because it still contains the dynamic type.","explanation":"This is a representation-level distinction between a nil pointer and a nil interface. Code that assumes interface != nil means the underlying pointer is non-nil can enter an invalid path or report a false error.","principle":"Do not use interface nil checks as a substitute for a clearly defined error/value contract involving typed nil pointers.","objective":"Diagnose false non-nil results caused by typed nil interface values.","requirements":["Error and interface-returning APIs must define nil semantics explicitly.","Implementations should avoid returning typed nil values through interfaces when nil interface semantics are expected."],"constraints":["The example focuses on pointer-backed interfaces and error-like values.","Reflection can inspect dynamic values but should not replace a sound API contract."],"preconditions":["A function returns a typed nil pointer as an interface value."],"input":"var p *MyError = nil; return p","output":"The corrected implementation returns a literal nil interface when no error exists.","expected_behavior":"A no-error result should compare equal to nil under the API's documented contract.","incorrect_code":"type MyError struct{}\nfunc (e *MyError) Error() string { return \"failure\" }\n\nfunc operation() error {\n    var err *MyError = nil\n    return err\n}","corrected_code":"type MyError struct{}\nfunc (e *MyError) Error() string { return \"failure\" }\n\nfunc operation() error {\n    return nil\n}","failure_mode":"The caller sees a non-nil error interface even though the underlying pointer is nil.","expected_error":"No panic is required; the observable symptom is an unexpected non-nil error result.","cause":"A typed nil pointer is boxed into a non-nil interface value.","root_cause":"The API's nil contract is violated by returning a typed nil through an interface.","solution":"Return literal nil for no-error cases and define interface-returning contracts carefully.","edge_case":"If the dynamic type carries meaningful nil semantics, callers may need an explicit API-specific check rather than a generic nil comparison.","security_implication":"False error states can trigger incorrect recovery, deny legitimate operations, or bypass intended success-path controls.","performance_implication":"Avoiding unnecessary interface/reflection handling is generally cheaper and simpler than inspecting dynamic values after the fact.","test_case":"Call operation and assert that the returned error compares equal to nil when no failure occurred.","test_input":"operation() with typed nil pointer path","expected_test_result":"Corrected operation returns a nil error interface.","testing_guidance":"Test typed nil pointers, nil interfaces, non-nil concrete errors, wrapped errors, and interface implementations.","verification_method":"Inspect the dynamic type/value pair returned through the interface and verify the documented nil contract.","verification_guidance":"Do not test only the concrete pointer; test the value after interface conversion.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.21+","framework":"Go standard library","framework_version":"1.21+","version_range":"Go 1.0+","dependencies":["Go interfaces"],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_INTERFACE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"r