{"record_id":"D36-B099","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Distributed Operation Semantics","concept":"Retrying a non-idempotent operation without an idempotency mechanism duplicates the side effect","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can a network retry create two resources or charge twice even when the first request may already have succeeded?","answer":"A client may not know whether a timed-out request was processed by the server. Retrying a non-idempotent operation without a stable idempotency key can cause the server to perform the same side effect again.","explanation":"A timeout creates uncertainty about the outcome, not proof of failure. Network retries therefore require an operation identity that lets the server recognize duplicate attempts and return the original result or otherwise enforce exactly-once effect semantics appropriate to the application.","principle":"Retries of non-idempotent operations require idempotency keys, deduplication, transactional state, or another mechanism that makes repeated attempts safe.","objective":"Diagnose duplicate side effects caused by unsafe retry logic across unreliable network boundaries.","requirements":["Retryable operations must define duplicate-attempt semantics.","A stable operation identity must survive retries.","Server-side deduplication or transactional protection must be enforced where required."],"constraints":["Exactly-once network delivery is not assumed.","The example is conceptual HTTP-style behavior."],"preconditions":["A request may reach the server even when the client does not receive the response.","The client retries after a timeout."],"input":"POST /payments with amount=100; first request commits but response is lost; client retries.","output":"The second attempt is recognized as the same operation and does not create a second side effect.","expected_behavior":"A retry must not duplicate a logically single operation.","incorrect_code":"async function charge(amount) {\n  try {\n    return await post(\"/charge\", { amount });\n  } catch (error) {\n    return await post(\"/charge\", { amount });\n  }\n}","corrected_code":"async function charge(amount, operationId) {\n  return await post(\"/charge\", {\n    amount,\n    idempotencyKey: operationId\n  });\n}","failure_mode":"A timeout followed by retry causes duplicate server-side effects.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the observable symptom is duplicate resource creation, duplicate charge, or another repeated side effect.","cause":"The retry does not identify whether it represents the same logical operation as the first attempt.","root_cause":"The system treats uncertain network outcome as definite operation failure and retries a non-idempotent action without duplicate-effect protection.","solution":"Use stable idempotency keys with server-side deduplication or a transactional operation record.","edge_case":"The idempotency record itself must have defined retention, scope, parameter consistency, and failure semantics.","security_implication":"Duplicate financial, provisioning, authorization, or account operations can cause serious integrity and abuse consequences.","performance_implication":"Idempotency storage adds lookup/write overhead but can prevent expensive duplicate work and compensation operations.","test_case":"Make the server commit the first request but suppress its response, then retry with the same operation identifier.","test_input":"Two requests with identical idempotency key and equivalent parameters","expected_test_result":"Only one logical side effect occurs and repeated attempts return the established operation result or documented equivalent.","testing_guidance":"Test timeouts before response, connection resets after commit, client retries, concurrent duplicate requests, mismatched parameters, and expired keys.","verification_method":"Trace the operation identity from client initiation through server-side commit and verify duplicate attempts map to one logical operation.","verification_guidance":"A successful single-request test does not establish retry safety.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","framework":"HTTP API","framework_version":"HTTP semantics are protocol/API dependent","version_range":"API-dependent","dependencies":["HTTP client/server"],"execution_requirements":["HTTP-compatible client and server test environment"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B009","D36-B089"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Distributed-systems retry semantics and established idempotency-key API design practice; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D36-B100","dataset_id":"D36","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Bug → Root Cause","subtopic":"Cross-Language Verification","concept":"A function can satisfy its local type and syntax checks while violating the higher-level invariant required by its caller","knowledge_type":"BUG_ROOT_CAUSE","question":"Why can code compile, pass local tests, and still be wrong for the system that consumes it?","answer":"Compilation and local tests establish only selected properties of an implementation. A function can satisfy its local type contract while violating a caller's semantic invariant, state-transition requirement, protocol expectation, or error-handling contract.","explanation":"Root-cause diagnosis must operate across abstraction boundaries. Local correctness does not imply system-level correctness when the caller and callee disagree about meaning, ownership, units, lifecycle, error semantics, or state transitions.","principle":"Verify implementation behavior against the full caller-callee contract and system invariant, not only local syntax, types, and isolated tests.","objective":"Identify defects that survive local compilation and testing because the violated requirement exists at a higher abstraction boundary.","requirements":["Caller and callee contracts must be explicit.","Tests must include boundary and integration behavior where semantics cross components.","Verification must address the requirement actually being claimed."],"constraints":["The example uses Go for a concise API contract illustration.","The same diagnostic principle applies across programming languages."],"preconditions":["A function returns a value whose local type is correct but whose semantic unit differs from caller expectations."],"input":"Function returns milliseconds while caller interprets result as seconds.","output":"The API contract explicitly states the unit and the implementation converts or preserves it consistently.","expected_behavior":"Caller and callee must agree on units and semantic meaning, not merely on the primitive type.","incorrect_code":"func timeout() int64 {\n    return 5000 // milliseconds\n}\n\nfunc waitForTimeout() {\n    seconds := timeout()\n    time.Sleep(time.Duration(seconds) * time.Second)\n}","corrected_code":"func timeout() time.Duration {\n    return 5 * time.Second\n}\n\nfunc waitForTimeout() {\n    time.Sleep(timeout())\n}","failure_mode":"The function returns a locally valid integer but the caller interprets its semantic unit incorrectly.","expected_error":"No compile-time error is required; the observable symptom is an incorrect timeout duration.","cause":"The API communicates a primitive type without encoding or documenting its semantic unit.","root_cause":"Verification stops at local type correctness and does not verify the cross-component semantic contract.","solution":"Use semantic types where practical, explicit units, contract tests, integration tests, and requirement-level verification.","edge_case":"Even strong types cannot encode every domain invariant; integration and requirement verification remain necessary.","security_implication":"Unit or semantic mismatches in timeouts, quotas, expiration, rate limits, or security windows can weaken controls or create denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implication":"A unit mismatch can cause excessive waits or premature timeouts, directly affecting latency and resource utilization.","test_case":"Verify timeout behavior against a known duration and assert both API output semantics and observable wait behavior.","test_input":"timeout expected to represent 5 seconds","expected_test_result":"The corrected implementation waits for the documented five-second duration rather than treating 5000 as seconds.","testing_guidance":"Include contract, integration, boundary, unit-conversion, and requirement-level tests; encode units in types where practical.","verification_method":"Trace the requirement from caller expectation through API contract to implementation and observable behavior.","verification_guidance":"Classify compilation, unit-test success, integration correctness, and requirement satisfaction as separate verification claims.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.21+","framework":"Go standard library","framework_version":"1.21+","version_range":"Go 1.0+","dependencies":["time"],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"CROSS_BOUNDARY_VERIFICATION_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B089","D36-B009"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":"Go time.Duration semantics and established contract/integration verification principles; example is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D37-B001","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Python State Isolation","concept":"Replace mutable default argument state with per-call state","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should shared mutable state created by a Python mutable default argument be fixed?","answer":"Replace the mutable default with a sentinel such as None and allocate a new object inside the function for each call. This establishes per-invocation ownership instead of sharing one mutable object across calls.","explanation":"The root cause is that Python evaluates default argument objects once when the function is defined, not once per invocation. A mutable default therefore becomes shared state. The fix moves mutable-state creation into the invocation path.","principle":"Mutable per-call state must be created per invocation unless intentional shared state is explicitly designed and documented.","objective":"Convert accidental shared default state into isolated invocation state.","requirements":["Each invocation must receive an independent mutable container.","Existing caller behavior for omitted arguments should remain compatible.","The fix must not introduce another hidden shared mutable object."],"constraints":["The example uses a list default.","Intentional shared state should not be removed blindly if it is part of the API design."],"preconditions":["A function uses a mutable default argument.","Repeated calls must not observe mutations from previous calls."],"input":"def collect(item, items=[]):\n    items.append(item)\n    return items","output":"Each call without an explicit items argument receives a fresh list.","expected_behavior":"collect(\"a\") and collect(\"b\") produce independent lists rather than sharing state.","incorrect_code":"def collect(item, items=[]):\n    items.append(item)\n    return items","corrected_code":"def collect(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items","failure_mode":"State from an earlier invocation appears in a later invocation.","expected_error":"No exception is required; the failure is incorrect persistent state across calls.","cause":"The default list object is created once and reused.","root_cause":"Mutable invocation state is stored in a function default object with definition-time lifetime.","solution":"Use None as the default and allocate the mutable object inside the function, or use an explicit immutable sentinel when None is a valid domain value.","edge_case":"If None is itself a valid caller value, use a unique sentinel object instead of None.","security_implication":"Shared state can cause cross-request data contamination in long-lived services if request-specific data is accidentally retained.","performance_implication":"Creating a small per-call container adds allocation overhead but prevents unbounded accidental state retention.","test_case":"Call the function twice without supplying items and assert that the returned lists are independent.","test_input":"collect(\"a\"); collect(\"b\")","expected_test_result":"First result is [\"a\"] and second result is [\"b\"].","testing_guidance":"Also test explicit caller-provided lists to ensure intentional state sharing through the parameter still works.","verification_method":"Inspect object identity across independent calls and verify mutations in one result do not affect another.","verification_guidance":"Verify both omitted-argument and explicitly supplied mutable-container paths.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python standard library","framework_version":"3.11+","version_range":"Python 3.8+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Python 3.11+"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B001"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D36-B001"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["D36-B001"],"provenance":"Python function-default evaluation semantics and established mutable-default remediation pattern; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D37-B002","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"JavaScript Asynchronous Error Handling","concept":"Move asynchronous failure handling into the Promise or async control-flow boundary","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a JavaScript asynchronous operation be fixed when a surrounding synchronous try/catch does not catch its rejection?","answer":"Use await inside an async try/catch or attach an explicit rejection handler with catch(). A synchronous try/catch only catches errors thrown during its synchronous execution and does not automatically handle a Promise rejection created later.","explanation":"The correct fix aligns error handling with the asynchronous control-flow mechanism. The caller must await the Promise within the try block or explicitly handle its rejection.","principle":"Error handling must be attached to the control-flow boundary that actually carries the failure.","objective":"Repair Promise rejection handling without changing the intended asynchronous operation.","requirements":["Rejected Promises must have a defined handling path.","The caller must preserve the intended success value and failure semantics.","Unhandled rejections must not be silently discarded."],"constraints":["The example assumes the caller can be made async.","A synchronous API boundary may require explicit Promise rejection handling instead."],"preconditions":["An asynchronous function returns a rejected Promise.","The caller currently wraps invocation in a synchronous try/catch without await."],"input":"An async operation that rejects.","output":"The rejection is converted into the caller's intended error-handling path.","expected_behavior":"The failure is handled by the same logical operation that initiated the asynchronous work.","incorrect_code":"try {\n    fetchData();\n} catch (error) {\n    handleError(error);\n}","corrected_code":"async function run() {\n    try {\n        await fetchData();\n    } catch (error) {\n        handleError(error);\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"Promise rejection escapes the surrounding synchronous catch block.","expected_error":"The rejection remains unhandled or reaches a higher-level Promise rejection handler.","cause":"The Promise is created but not awaited or explicitly rejected-handled inside the try boundary.","root_cause":"Synchronous and asynchronous error propagation mechanisms are treated as equivalent.","solution":"Await the Promise inside an async try/catch or attach a catch handler at the appropriate boundary.","edge_case":"If work is intentionally fire-and-forget, explicitly handle rejection at the detached Promise boundary rather than pretending the caller owns the failure.","security_implication":"Unhandled failures in authentication, authorization, transaction, or cleanup paths can leave operations incomplete while appearing successful to callers.","performance_implication":"Awaiting preserves asynchronous execution but can change sequencing; unnecessary serialization should be avoided when independent operations can remain concurrent.","test_case":"Make fetchData reject and assert that handleError receives the rejection.","test_input":"fetchData() returns Promise.reject(new Error(\"failure\"))","expected_test_result":"handleError is invoked and the rejection is not unhandled.","testing_guidance":"Test both rejection and success paths, including concurrent Promise execution where applicable.","verification_method":"Trace the Promise from creation to settlement and identify the exact rejection handler or await boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every intentionally detached Promise has its own explicit failure handling.","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","framework":"ECMAScript Promise","framework_version":"ES2022+","version_range":"ES2017+","dependencies":["Promise"],"execution_requirements":["JavaScript runtime supporting async/await"],"execution_status"