{"record_id":"D37-B003","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"TypeScript Nullability","concept":"Establish an explicit nullable-state guard before dereferencing a possibly absent value","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a TypeScript nullable-value failure be fixed without simply suppressing the compiler warning?","answer":"Narrow the value through an explicit runtime guard or establish a validated invariant before dereferencing it. The non-null assertion operator should not be used merely to silence the compiler when absence is genuinely possible.","explanation":"The fix should make the application's nullability assumption executable. If absence is valid, handle it. If absence is invalid after a boundary check, validate the invariant and then use the narrowed value.","principle":"Turn nullable assumptions into explicit control-flow or validated invariants instead of suppressing type-system evidence.","objective":"Repair nullable dereferences while preserving correct behavior for absent values.","requirements":["Possible absence must be represented and handled.","The program must not dereference an unvalidated nullable value.","The fix must preserve intended behavior for valid and absent states."],"constraints":["The example uses strict TypeScript null checking.","The correct handling of absence depends on the domain contract."],"preconditions":["A function receives a string or null.","The code requires a string for uppercase conversion."],"input":"string | null","output":"A string is processed only after null has been handled.","expected_behavior":"Null input follows an explicit defined path instead of causing a runtime failure.","incorrect_code":"function normalize(name: string | null): string {\n    return name!.trim().toUpperCase();\n}","corrected_code":"function normalize(name: string | null): string {\n    if (name === null) {\n        return \"\";\n    }\n    return name.trim().toUpperCase();\n}","failure_mode":"A null runtime value reaches a dereference despite the code claiming it is non-null.","expected_error":"TypeError when the runtime value is null and a property or method is accessed.","cause":"Non-null assertion suppresses static checking without establishing a runtime invariant.","root_cause":"The implementation treats a possibility in the input contract as impossible without validating that assumption.","solution":"Handle null explicitly or validate the invariant at the boundary and then rely on type narrowing.","edge_case":"If empty string and null have different business meanings, the correction must preserve that distinction rather than mapping both to the same output.","security_implication":"Unchecked optional values in authentication, authorization, configuration, or identity paths can cause unsafe fallback behavior.","performance_implication":"A simple null check has negligible cost compared with the reliability benefit; repeated deep validation should be centralized at boundaries where practical.","test_case":"Test valid string, null, empty string, and whitespace-only input.","test_input":"\"Ada\"; null; \"\"; \"   \"","expected_test_result":"Each input follows the documented domain behavior without runtime dereference failure.","testing_guidance":"Enable strictNullChecks and include runtime tests because compile-time narrowing does not validate external data.","verification_method":"Identify the exact point where the nullable value becomes guaranteed non-null and verify the guarantee is established by control flow or validated input.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat a non-null assertion as evidence that the value cannot be null.","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","framework":"TypeScript compiler","framework_version":"5.x","version_range":"TypeScript 4.x+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["TypeScript compiler with strictNullChecks enabled"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","v