{"record_id":"D37-B004","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Java Contract Validation","concept":"Validate required producer-consumer contracts at the boundary before dereferencing dependent data","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a Java nullability failure caused by an invalid producer-consumer contract be fixed?","answer":"Make the contract explicit and validate it at the boundary. If null is invalid, reject it immediately; if null is valid, model and handle that state explicitly rather than allowing a later NullPointerException.","explanation":"The fix moves failure closer to the violated contract. This makes the root cause observable at the producer-consumer boundary instead of producing a secondary failure in unrelated code.","principle":"Fail at the boundary where an invalid contract is detected rather than allowing invalid state to propagate.","objective":"Convert delayed NullPointerException failures into explicit contract enforcement.","requirements":["Required values must be checked before entering code that assumes them.","The contract must state whether null is valid.","Failure behavior must be deterministic and diagnosable."],"constraints":["The example uses standard Java without a dependency injection or validation framework.","A domain where null is valid requires a different modeling strategy."],"preconditions":["A service method requires a non-null identifier.","The caller can accidentally supply null."],"input":"null identifier","output":"An explicit contract exception is raised before business processing.","expected_behavior":"Invalid input is rejected at the boundary.","incorrect_code":"void loadUser(String id) {\n    User user = repository.find(id);\n    System.out.println(user.getName());\n}","corrected_code":"void loadUser(String id) {\n    if (id == null || id.isBlank()) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"id must be non-empty\");\n    }\n    User user = repository.find(id);\n    if (user == null) {\n        throw new IllegalStateException(\"repository returned no user for valid id\");\n    }\n    System.out.println(user.getName());\n}","failure_mode":"Invalid producer output or caller input propagates until an unrelated dereference fails.","expected_error":"IllegalArgumentException or IllegalStateException at the violated contract boundary.","cause":"The implementation assumes a non-null contract without enforcing it.","root_cause":"Producer-consumer semantics are implicit and invalid states are allowed to propagate.","solution":"Define the nullability contract and validate it at the boundary; model legitimate absence explicitly when required.","edge_case":"If absence is expected, Optional or an explicit result type may be more appropriate than throwing for every missing value.","security_implication":"Null or missing identity/configuration values can trigger unsafe defaults or bypass checks if callers interpret absence incorrectly.","performance_implication":"Early validation is cheap and avoids deeper work performed on invalid requests.","test_case":"Test null, blank, valid, and repository-missing cases separately.","test_input":"null; \"\"; \"u123\" with existing user; \"u404\" with no user","expected_test_result":"Each contract state produces its documented outcome without an accidental NullPointerException.","testing_guidance":"Test both caller-side and repository-side contract violations because they represent different root causes.","verification_method":"Trace the producer output contract and consumer preconditions and verify that each boundary enforces its assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that the resulting exception identifies the violated contract rather than an unrelated downstream operation.","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","framework":"Java standard library","framework_version":"17+","version_range":"Java 8+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["JD