{"record_id":"D37-B005","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"C Memory Lifetime","concept":"Make allocation ownership explicit and pair release with the owning lifecycle","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a C use-after-free root cause be fixed?","answer":"Define ownership explicitly and ensure the resource remains alive for every operation that can access it. Prefer moving the use before release, transferring ownership deliberately, or redesigning the API so borrowed and owned lifetimes are unambiguous.","explanation":"A use-after-free cannot be reliably fixed by adding arbitrary allocations or nulling pointers. The lifetime relationship between the allocation and every consumer must be corrected.","principle":"A pointer may be dereferenced only while the object it designates is alive and owned according to the API contract.","objective":"Repair memory lifetime violations by correcting ownership and lifetime ordering.","requirements":["The object must outlive every use.","Ownership transfer must be explicit.","Released memory must never be accessed afterward."],"constraints":["The example uses heap allocation for clarity.","A real system may use a longer-lived owner or a copied value instead."],"preconditions":["A pointer is retained after its allocation has been released."],"input":"Heap object released before a later consumer uses its pointer.","output":"The consumer accesses the object only while its lifetime is active.","expected_behavior":"No pointer remains usable after the corresponding object lifetime ends.","incorrect_code":"char *buffer = malloc(32);\nstrcpy(buffer, \"hello\");\nfree(buffer);\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);","corrected_code":"char *buffer = malloc(32);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    return 1;\n}\nstrcpy(buffer, \"hello\");\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\nfree(buffer);","failure_mode":"A dangling pointer is dereferenced after the allocation has ended.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior; sanitizers may report heap-use-after-free.","cause":"The allocation is released before the final use.","root_cause":"Object lifetime does not cover the complete period during which a pointer is treated as valid.","solution":"Move release after the final use or redesign ownership so the object remains alive for all required consumers.","edge_case":"Setting the pointer to NULL after free can prevent some accidental reuse but does not repair aliases held elsewhere.","security_implication":"Use-after-free is a memory-safety vulnerability that can enable corruption or code execution depending on allocator and program state.","performance_implication":"Extending object lifetime can increase memory usage, so ownership should be correct rather than simply making everything long-lived.","test_case":"Run the program under AddressSanitizer and exercise all paths that consume the buffer.","test_input":"Normal allocation/use/free path plus early-return and error paths","expected_test_result":"No use-after-free or leak is reported and all valid consumers execute before release.","testing_guidance":"Use AddressSanitizer and leak detection where available, plus ownership-focused static analysis and code review.","verification_method":"Build an allocation lifetime trace from allocation through every alias/use to release and confirm no use occurs after release.","verification_guidance":"Check aliases and callbacks, not only the original pointer variable.","language":"C","language_version":"C17","framework":"C standard library","framework_version":"C17","version_range":"C11-C23","dependencies":["stdlib.h","string.h"],"execution_requirements":["C compiler; sanitizer support recommended"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"MEMORY_SAFETY_PATTERN","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B005"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D36-B005"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["D36-B005"],"provenance":"ISO C object lifetime semantics and established memory-safety remediation practice; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D37-B006","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"C++ Container Invalidation","concept":"Replace invalidated iterator use with a lifetime-valid access pattern","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a C++ vector iterator invalidation bug be fixed?","answer":"Avoid retaining iterators across operations that may invalidate them. Reacquire the iterator after the mutating operation or structure the algorithm so mutation and iteration do not overlap in an invalid way.","explanation":"The correct fix depends on the required algorithm. If erase is necessary during iteration, use the iterator returned by erase where the container API guarantees that pattern, rather than continuing to use an invalidated iterator.","principle":"Never use an iterator after an operation that invalidates it unless the program reacquires a valid iterator or uses an API-defined replacement.","objective":"Repair container iterator lifetime violations without disabling required mutation.","requirements":["Iterator validity rules must be respected for the specific container and operation.","Mutation during traversal must use a defined safe iteration pattern.","The corrected algorithm must preserve intended element processing."],"constraints":["The example uses std::vector.","Different C++ containers have different invalidation rules."],"preconditions":["A vector element is removed while iterating.","The implementation must continue scanning the remaining elements."],"input":"Vector containing values to remove.","output":"All matching elements are removed without accessing invalid iterators.","expected_behavior":"Iteration continues using a valid iterator after erase.","incorrect_code":"std::vector<int> values{1, 2, 3, 4};\nfor (auto it = values.begin(); it != values.end(); ++it) {\n    if (*it % 2 == 0) {\n        values.erase(it);\n    }\n}","corrected_code":"std::vector<int> values{1, 2, 3, 4};\nfor (auto it = values.begin(); it != values.end(); ) {\n    if (*it % 2 == 0) {\n        it = values.erase(it);\n    } else {\n        ++it;\n    }\n}","failure_mode":"The loop increments or dereferences an iterator invalidated by vector mutation.","expected_error":"Undefined behavior may occur; debug iterator implementations may diagnose invalid iterator use.","cause":"erase invalidates the iterator being used and the loop continues as if it remained valid.","root_cause":"The iteration algorithm does not account for container-specific iterator invalidation semantics.","solution":"Use the iterator returned by erase or choose an algorithm/container whose invalidation rules match the required mutation pattern.","edge_case":"For different containers such as list or deque, invalidation behavior differs; fixes must be container-specific.","security_implication":"Undefined behavior can produce memory corruption and potentially security vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Repeated vector erasure can be O(n²) due to element movement; a filter-and-rebuild strategy may be preferable for large vectors.","test_case":"Remove all even values from vectors of empty, singleton, already-filtered, and mixed inputs.","test_input":"{}, {2}, {1,3}, {1,2,3,4,5,6}","expected_test_result":"Only odd values remain and no invalid iterator is used.","testing_guidance":"Use debug iterators and sanitizers where supported, and include performance tests for large containers.","verification_method":"Check the invalidation rule for the exact container operation and verify every iterator use occurs within its validity interval.","verification_guidance":"Do not generalize iterator invalidation behavior from one STL container to another.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17","framework":"C++ standard library","framework_version":"C++17","version_range":"C++11+","dependencies":["vector"],"execution_requirements":["C++17 compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILL