{"record_id":"D37-B007","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Rust Ownership Transfer","concept":"Restructure ownership so a value is not accessed after a move","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should Rust moved-value failures be fixed without cloning everything indiscriminately?","answer":"Decide whether the subsequent operation needs ownership or only a borrow. If only read access is required, borrow before moving or change the consuming function to accept a reference. Clone only when independent ownership is actually required.","explanation":"A move error is often a design signal about ownership requirements. The best fix is to express the true ownership relationship rather than blindly cloning the value to satisfy the compiler.","principle":"Choose ownership, borrowing, or cloning according to the actual lifetime and ownership requirements of each operation.","objective":"Repair moved-value errors while preserving Rust's ownership guarantees and avoiding unnecessary copies.","requirements":["Each use must obey the value's ownership state.","Borrowing should be preferred when ownership transfer is not required.","Cloning should be used only when independent ownership is semantically necessary."],"constraints":["The example uses a String.","Changing a function signature may affect callers and API compatibility."],"preconditions":["A String is passed by value to a function and then used again.","The called function only needs read access."],"input":"Owned String passed to a read-only operation.","output":"The String remains owned by the caller after a borrowed operation.","expected_behavior":"The value can be reused because ownership was not transferred.","incorrect_code":"fn print_name(name: String) {\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}\n\nfn run() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ada\");\n    print_name(name);\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","corrected_code":"fn print_name(name: &str) {\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}\n\nfn run() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ada\");\n    print_name(&name);\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","failure_mode":"A value is used after ownership has been moved to another function.","expected_error":"Rust compiler error E0382: borrow of moved value.","cause":"The function signature consumes ownership even though the operation only requires read access.","root_cause":"The API ownership contract is stronger than the operation's actual requirement.","solution":"Accept a borrow when the callee does not need ownership; retain ownership transfer only when semantically required.","edge_case":"If the callee must retain the value beyond the caller's lifetime or independently mutate/own it, borrowing is insufficient and ownership transfer or cloning may be correct.","security_implication":"Ownership-safe correction prevents lifetime bugs; unnecessary cloning can still increase memory pressure for sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Borrowing avoids allocation and copying, while cloning can add O(n) time and memory cost for String-sized data.","test_case":"Compile and run the corrected function with repeated reads after the borrowed call.","test_input":"name = \"Ada\"","expected_test_result":"The value is printed by both operations without ownership errors.","testing_guidance":"Prefer compile-time ownership tests and benchmark clone-heavy alternatives when deciding between borrowing and cloning.","verification_method":"Determine whether each callee requires ownership, mutable access, or shared read access, then compare that requirement with the function signature.","verification_guidance":"Treat compiler move errors as design evidence rather than simply suppressing them with clones.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","framework":"Rust standard library","framework_version":"1.75+","version_range":"Rust 1.0+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Rust compiler"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OWNERSHIP_TYPE_SYSTEM","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B007"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D36-B007"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["D36-B007"],"provenance":"Rust ownership and borrowing semantics; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D37-B008","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Go Interface Nil Semantics","concept":"Return a literal nil interface instead of a typed nil pointer when the API contract means no error","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a Go typed-nil error result be fixed?","answer":"When an operation succeeds, return the literal nil error interface rather than a typed nil pointer assigned to the error interface. This preserves the expected nil semantics for callers.","explanation":"A typed nil pointer stored in an interface contains a dynamic type, so the interface itself can be non-nil. The fix is primarily an API-return-value correction, not a caller-side nil-check workaround.","principle":"The representation returned by an interface-based API must match the documented nil semantics.","objective":"Repair false non-nil error results caused by typed nil interface values.","requirements":["Successful operations must return a nil error interface.","Typed error values should be returned only when an actual error exists.","Callers should be able to use the standard if err != nil contract."],"constraints":["The example uses an error interface.","There are legitimate cases for pointer-backed custom error types when a real error exists."],"preconditions":["A function returns an error interface.","The no-error path currently returns a typed nil pointer."],"input":"Typed nil *MyError converted to error.","output":"Literal nil error on success.","expected_behavior":"if err != nil is false on a successful operation.","incorrect_code":"type MyError struct{}\nfunc (e *MyError) Error() string { return \"failure\" }\n\nfunc operation() error {\n    var err *MyError\n    return err\n}","corrected_code":"type MyError struct{}\nfunc (e *MyError) Error() string { return \"failure\" }\n\nfunc operation() error {\n    return nil\n}","failure_mode":"Caller incorrectly enters the error path after a successful operation.","expected_error":"No exception; the observable defect is a non-nil error interface representing a nil pointer.","cause":"A typed nil pointer is converted into an interface containing a dynamic type.","root_cause":"The function violates the caller-facing nil error contract.","solution":"Return literal nil when no error exists and reserve typed error values for actual failures.","edge_case":"Wrapped errors and errors.As/errors.Is require separate contract tests because their semantics are intentionally different from direct nil comparison.","security_implication":"False failures can trigger incorrect denial, retry, rollback, or recovery behavior in security-sensitive operations.","performance_implication":"Returning nil directly avoids unnecessary interface wrapping and downstream inspection.","test_case":"Assert that a successful operation returns nil using the idiomatic Go error check.","test_input":"err := operation()","expected_test_result":"err == nil evaluates true.","testing_guidance":"Include tests for real typed errors, wrapped errors, nil interfaces, and typed nil pointers.","verification_method":"Inspect the dynamic type and value of the returned interface and verify the no-error path returns an untyped nil interface value.","verification_guidance":"Test the API through its declared interface type, not only through the concrete pointer type.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.21+","framework":"Go standard library","framework_version":"1.21+","version_range":"Go 1.0+","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":["Go compiler/runtime"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"LANGUAGE_INTERFACE_SEMANTICS","confidence":0.99,"related_records":["D36-B008"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D36-B008"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["D36-B008"],"provenance":"Go interface a