{"record_id":"D37-B009","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Database Transaction Integrity","concept":"Move related database mutations into one transaction with explicit rollback semantics","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should a partial database-state failure be fixed when multiple related writes must succeed together?","answer":"Group the logically atomic mutations inside one database transaction and commit only after all required operations succeed. On failure, roll back the transaction so the database does not expose an intermediate state.","explanation":"The root cause is a mismatch between the logical operation and the persistence boundary. If two writes represent one business transition, independently committing them permits partial success. A transaction aligns persistence atomicity with the business invariant.","principle":"The transaction boundary should encompass all persistent mutations that must succeed or fail as one logical operation.","objective":"Repair partial database state caused by independently committed related writes.","requirements":["All mutually dependent writes must share the same transaction.","Failure before commit must prevent partial persistent state.","Commit must occur only after required operations succeed."],"constraints":["The example uses Python DB-API-style pseudocode semantics.","Actual transaction behavior depends on the database driver and database engine configuration."],"preconditions":["Two writes must represent one atomic business operation.","The second write can fail after the first succeeds."],"input":"Create order and corresponding audit record.","output":"Either both records are committed or neither is committed.","expected_behavior":"The database never exposes a committed order without its required audit record when the invariant requires both.","incorrect_code":"order_id = create_order()\ncreate_audit_record(order_id)\ncommit_order()","corrected_code":"with connection:\n    order_id = create_order(connection)\n    create_audit_record(connection, order_id)\n    # Connection commits only after the block completes successfully.","failure_mode":"The first persistent mutation survives after a later required mutation fails.","expected_error":"Database-specific exception from the failed operation; transaction must not leave the invariant partially committed.","cause":"Related writes use separate commit boundaries.","root_cause":"The persistence transaction boundary does not match the business atomicity boundary.","solution":"Place all required mutations inside one transaction and define rollback behavior for every failure path.","edge_case":"External side effects such as email or message delivery cannot automatically participate in a local database transaction and may require an outbox or compensation design.","security_implication":"Partial authorization, billing, entitlement, or audit state can create integrity and compliance failures.","performance_implication":"Transactions can increase lock duration and contention; transaction scope should be large enough for atomicity but small enough to avoid unnecessary blocking.","test_case":"Force the second write to fail and inspect the database after the operation.","test_input":"First write succeeds; second write raises an exception.","expected_test_result":"Neither required mutation is committed.","testing_guidance":"Test commit, rollback, deadlock, constraint violation, connection loss, and retry behavior according to the database system.","verification_method":"Map the business invariant to the transaction boundary and verify all required writes participate in the same atomic commit.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer transaction atomicity merely because individual SQL statements succeed.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","framework":"Python DB-API style","framework_version":"Driver-dependent","version_range":"Database/driver dependent","dependencies":["Database connection supporting transactions"],"execution_requirements":["Transactional database and compatible Python driver"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"TRANSACTIONAL_INTEGRITY_PATTERN","confidence":0.98,"related_records":["D36-B009"],"depends_on":[],"requires":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D36-B009"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":["D36-B009"],"provenance":"Database transaction atomicity principles and Python DB-API transaction conventions; code is illustrative and not executed.","created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D37-B010","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Network Failure Boundaries","concept":"Add explicit connection, read, and overall operation timeouts to prevent unbounded waiting","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","question":"How should an unbounded network wait be fixed?","answer":"Configure explicit timeouts appropriate to the network operation and define what happens when each timeout expires. The fix should distinguish connection establishment, response/read, and overall operation deadlines where the client API supports them.","explanation":"A network operation without a deadline can consume threads, tasks, connections, or other resources indefinitely when the remote system becomes unavailable. A timeout converts an unbounded failure into a bounded, observable state that the caller can retry, fail, or compensate for.","principle":"Every remote operation must have a bounded waiting policy unless indefinite waiting is an explicit and justified requirement.","objective":"Convert indefinite network waiting into controlled timeout-aware failure behavior.","requirements":["Remote operations must have explicit deadlines or timeout policies.","Timeout failures must be distinguishable from successful completion.","Cleanup and retry behavior must be defined after timeout."],"constraints":["Exact timeout APIs vary by language and HTTP client.","Timeout values must be chosen from service-level requirements rather than arbitrary constants."],"preconditions":["A client performs an HTTP request without a configured timeout.","The remote endpoint may become unavailable or stop responding."],"input":"HTTP request to an unresponsive endpoint.","output":"The request terminates after the configured deadline and enters explicit failure handling.","expected_behavior":"The client does not wait indefinitely for the remote system.","incorrect_code":"import requests\n\nresponse = requests.get(\"https:/