{"record_id":"D37-B029","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"JavaScript inherited-property confusion","concept":"Own-property and prototype-aware validation","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should JavaScript code be fixed when it assumes that a property found through ordinary lookup is an own property of the object?","answer":"Use an explicit own-property check when ownership matters, and define whether inherited properties are valid before processing the value.","explanation":"JavaScript property lookup can traverse the prototype chain. Code that interprets property existence as own-property ownership can therefore process inherited values unexpectedly, particularly when objects originate outside trusted code.","cause":"The implementation conflated successful property lookup with ownership by the immediate object.","solution":"Use an ownership-aware property check such as Object.hasOwn when supported by the target environment, or an equivalent prototype-safe check, before relying on own-property semantics.","alternatives":["Use Object.keys or Object.entries when enumeration of own enumerable properties is the actual requirement.","Normalize untrusted objects into controlled data structures before processing.","Explicitly allow inherited properties when prototype-chain lookup is part of the intended contract."],"tradeoffs":"Own-property validation makes the contract explicit but adds a branch or normalization step. Enumeration-based approaches may impose different key and enumerability semantics.","regression_impact":"Changing from inherited lookup to own-property lookup can exclude values previously accepted from prototypes; callers and tests must reflect the intended contract.","verification_method":"Test own properties, inherited properties, absent properties, null-prototype objects, and objects with unusual prototype chains.","when_not_to_use":"Do not require own-property semantics