{"record_id":"D37-B030","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Java resource cleanup failure","concept":"Deterministic resource ownership and cleanup","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should Java code be fixed when a resource may remain open because cleanup is not guaranteed on every control-flow path?","answer":"Use try-with-resources for AutoCloseable resources so cleanup is tied to lexical scope and occurs on normal completion and exceptional exits.","explanation":"Resources such as streams, files, sockets, and database-related handles require deterministic cleanup. Manual cleanup distributed across return and exception paths is error-prone; try-with-resources expresses ownership and cleanup directly in the control flow.","cause":"Resource acquisition and release were separated such that exceptional or early-return paths could bypass cleanup.","solution":"Acquire AutoCloseable resources in a try-with-resources statement and handle operational exceptions separately from deterministic cleanup.","alternatives":["Use a framework-managed lifecycle when the framework explicitly owns the resource.","Use carefully structured finally cleanup when try-with-resources cannot represent the resource contract.","Encapsulate resource ownership in a dedicated abstraction with a clearly defined close lifecycle."],"tradeoffs":"Try-with-resources can change exception structure because close failures are managed as part of the construct; however, it makes cleanup behavior explicit and reduces leak-prone control-flow paths.","regression_impact":"Adding deterministic close behavior can expose code that previously depended on resources remaining open after a method returned; lifecycle-dependent callers must be reviewed.","verification_method":"Test normal completion, each relevant exception path, early return, multiple resources, and close-time failure. Verify resources are closed exactly according to the ownership contract.","when_not_to_use":"Do not close a resource in a layer that does not own it merely because that layer can access it; ownership must determine cleanup responsibility.","version_constraints":"Try-with-resources requires Java 7 or later; AutoCloseable and framework-specific resource behavior depend on the target JDK/library version.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B030"],"derived_from":["D36-B030"],"provenance":{"source_type":"LANGUAGE_SPECIFICATION_AND_STANDARD_LIBRARY","source_reference":"Java try-with-resources and AutoCloseable semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B031","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Iterator or generator exhaustion","concept":"Explicit iterator consumption semantics","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should Python code be fixed when a generator or one-shot iterator is consumed before a later operation expects to iterate over its values again?","answer":"Treat one-shot iterators as consumable resources and either materialize the values when repeated traversal is required or recreate the iterator from a reusable source.","explanation":"Generators and many iterators maintain traversal state. Once consumed, subsequent iteration may produce no values. The repair must match the required lifecycle rather than assuming iterator reuse behaves like iterating a reusable collection.","cause":"The implementation assumed that an iterator could be traversed repeatedly without resetting or recreating its state.","solution":"Materialize the sequence when its size is acceptable and repeated traversal is required, or retain the underlying reusable source and create a fresh iterator for each traversal.","alternatives":["Use a list or tuple when bounded materialization is appropriate.","Create a new generator from the original source for each independent traversal.","Redesign the API to make one-shot consumption explicit."],"tradeoffs":"Materialization increases memory usage and may delay processing; recreating an iterator may repeat I/O or computation. Streaming preserves memory efficiency but requires explicit consumption semantics.","regression_impact":"Materializing data can change timing, memory usage, and side-effect behavior. Recreating an iterator can repeat external operations if the source is not purely in-memory.","verification_method":"Iterate once and twice, test empty and partially consumed iterators, verify expected results after repeated calls, and test large inputs where materialization may be impractical.","when_not_to_use":"Do not materialize an unbounded or very large stream solely to make it reusable; preserve streaming semantics when repeated traversal is not actually required.","version_constraints":"Python iterator and generator semantics are core language behavior across supported Python 3.x versions.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B031"],"derived_from":["D36-B031"],"provenance":{"source_type":"LANGUAGE_SEMANTICS","source_reference":"Python iterator and generator protocol semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B032","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Asynchronous stale-state update","concept":"Fresh-state derivation across asynchronous boundaries","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should JavaScript asynchronous code be fixed when a delayed callback updates state using a stale value captured before the asynchronous operation?","answer":"Derive the update from the current authoritative state at the point of application, or use the framework's functional/state-transition mechanism that guarantees updates are based on the latest state.","explanation":"An asynchronous callback can execute after other state transitions have occurred. A value captured when the callback was created may therefore no longer represent current state. The repair must make state ownership and update ordering explicit.","cause":"The asynchronous operation captured a state snapshot and later applied it as though no intervening state transition could occur.","solution":"Use an atomic or functional state update where available, re-read authoritative state at the appropriate boundary, or attach a version/sequence condition when stale results must be rejected.","alternatives":["Use functional state updates in state-management frameworks that provide them.","Associate requests with sequence identifiers and ignore results older than the current state version.","Cancel obsolete asynchronous operations when cancellation is supported and semantically appropriate."],"tradeoffs":"Fresh-state reads or functional updates improve correctness but can require architectural changes. Version checks add metadata and logic; cancellation may not stop already-completed external work.","regression_impact":"Changing update ordering can expose previously hidden race conditions and may alter UI or event timing.","verification_method":"Create overlapping asynchronous operations with deliberately different completion orders and verif