{"record_id":"D37-B054","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"C++ moved-from object misuse","concept":"Post-move object contract preservation","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should C++ code be fixed when it assumes that a moved-from object retains its previous value or semantic state?","answer":"Treat moved-from objects according to their documented post-move contract, typically as valid but with an unspecified value for standard library types, and avoid relying on the pre-move state unless explicitly guaranteed.","explanation":"A move operation transfers resources or state but does not generally preserve the original object's value. The moved-from object remains subject to its type's validity contract, which may permit limited operations but does not guarantee its former semantic contents.","cause":"The code treated a moved-from object as though move construction or move assignment had copied its original state unchanged.","solution":"Consume or replace the moved-from object, explicitly reinitialize it before semantic reuse, or rely only on operations guaranteed by the type's documented post-move contract.","alternatives":["Assign a known value before reuse when the object remains part of the workflow.","Redesign the flow so the moved-from object is not semantically needed afterward.","Define stronger post-move guarantees for user-defined types when the API benefits from them."],"tradeoffs":"Reinitialization may add work; stronger user-defined post-move guarantees constrain implementation freedom but can simplify callers.","regression_impact":"Changing reuse behavior can affect object identity, resource ownership, performance, and state-dependent logic.","verification_method":"Test operations performed after move, including destruction, assignment, permitted member functions, and semantic reuse after explicit reinitialization.","when_not_to_use":"Do not infer a universal post-move val