{"record_id":"D37-B067","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Go concurrent map access","concept":"Synchronization ownership for shared maps","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should Go code be fixed when multiple goroutines access a map concurrently and at least one access mutates it?","answer":"Protect the map with a synchronization mechanism appropriate to the access pattern, or redesign ownership so only one goroutine mutates the map and others communicate through messages.","explanation":"Ordinary Go maps are not a general-purpose synchronization primitive for concurrent conflicting access. The repair must establish a clear ownership or synchronization protocol.","cause":"Multiple goroutines performed conflicting map operations without a valid synchronization mechanism.","solution":"Use a mutex/RWMutex around the relevant invariant, confine ownership to one goroutine, or use sync.Map only when its workload and semantics are appropriate.","alternatives":["Use a mutex when compound operations involve several map accesses.","Use a single owner goroutine and channels for serialized state transitions.","Use sync.Map for workloads that match its documented concurrency characteristics."],"tradeoffs":"Mutexes can create contention; owner-goroutine designs change control flow; sync.Map has different performance and API tradeoffs than a typed ordinary map protected by a mutex.","regression_impact":"Synchronization can alter latency and scheduling. Moving ownership to one goroutine can change shutdown and backpressure behavior.","verification_method":"Run concurrent read/write stress tests, exercise shutdown and cancellation, verify compound invariants, and use the race detector where available.","when_not_to_use":"Do not select sync.Map solely because it sounds more concurrent; use it only when its documented workload characteristics fit the access pattern.","version_constraints":"Map behavior and synchronization APIs depend on the target Go version and runtime/library contract.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B048"],"derived_from":["D36-B048"],"provenance":{"source_type":"LANGUAGE_AND_STANDARD_LIBRARY","source_reference":"Go map concurrency rules, sync.Mutex/RWMutex, sync.Map, and race-detector guidance","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B068","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Timeout without cancellation propagation","concept":"End-to-end deadline and cancellation ownership","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a timeout be fixed when the caller stops waiting but the underlying operation continues consuming resources?","answer":"Propagate cancellation or deadlines through every layer that owns or waits on the underlying work, and ensure the operation observes and terminates according to the defined cancellation contract.","explanation":"A caller-side timeout only limits the caller's wait unless the underlying operation receives and honors cancellation. Otherwise abandoned work can continue consuming threads, sockets, memory, database connections, or downstream capacity.","cause":"Timeout semantics were implemented at the waiting boundary without propagating cancellation to the work being abandoned.","solution":"Pass an explicit deadline or cancellation signal through the full call chain and make each cancellable operation release resources when cancellation occurs.","alternatives":["Use a deadline context propagated through service boundaries.","Use cooperative cancellation tokens for asynchronous work.","Use bounded worker queues and explicit task ownership when cancellation cannot interrupt the underlying operation immediately."],"tradeoffs":"Cancellation propagation adds API plumbing and requires every layer to define cancellation semantics. Forced termination may not be safe for operations with external side effects.","regression_impact":"Earlier cancellation can interrupt operations that callers previously assumed would finish, requiring idempotency and partial-state handling.","verification_method":"Trigger timeout while work is active and verify the underlying task receives cancellation, releases resources, and cannot continue producing unintended side effects.","when_not_to_use":"Do not forcibly cancel non-interruptible external side effects without first defining their transactional or idempotency behavior.","version_constraints":"Cancellation mechanisms differ across Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, Java, Go, Rust, C++, and framework-specific async runtimes.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B049"],"derived_from":["D36-B049"],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_AND_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Deadline propagation, cooperative cancellation, and resource-lifecycle semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B069","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Distributed partial failure across side effects","concept":"Idempotent state-machine recovery","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a distributed workflow be fixed when one side effect succeeds and a later side effect fails, leaving the system partially completed?","answer":"Model the workflow as explicit durable state transitions, make repeatable operations idempotent where possible, persist progress, and provide reconciliation or compensating actions for incomplete states.","explanation":"A process-spanning transaction usually cannot atomically roll back arbitrary external side effects. Recovery therefore requires explicit knowledge of which effects occurred and what can safely be retried or compensated.","cause":"Multiple external side effects were treated as though they formed one atomic transaction without a durable recovery protocol.","solution":"Persist workflow state, assign stable operation identifiers, make retries idempotent, distinguish completed from pending states, and reconcile uncertain outcomes.","alternatives":["Use a transactional outbox when database state must reliably produce external events.","Use compensating actions when completed effects can be safely reversed.","Use a saga-style state machine for long-running multi-step workflows."],"tradeoffs":"Durable state and idempotency increase implementation complexity and storage requirements. Compensation may not perfectly restore the original state.","regression_impact":"Recovery-aware workflows can expose previously hidden partial states and may change retry behavior, ordering, and external API traffic.","verification_method":"Inject failures after every side-effect boundary, including ambiguous timeouts, restart the workflow, replay operations, and verify convergence to a valid terminal state.","when_not_to_use":"Do not claim atomic rollback across independent external systems unless their actual transaction protocol provides that guarantee.","version_constraints":"Implementation depends on the databases, messaging systems, APIs, and orchestration framework involved.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B050"],"derived_from":["D36-B050"],"provenance":{"source_type":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Distributed transaction, idempotency, saga, outbox, and reconciliation patterns","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B070","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Concurrency fix selection and invariant preservation","concept":"Repairing races by invariant rather than symptom","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a concurrency bug be fixed when several possible synchronization mechanisms appear to remove the observed race?","answer":"Define the shared invariant and required ordering first, then select the smallest synchronization or ownership mechanism that provably preserves that invariant under all relevant interleavings.","explanation":"A race symptom can disappear after adding a lock while the underlying multi-step invariant remains vulnerable elsewhere. Correct repair requires reasoning about the complete state transition rather than merely suppressing one observed interleaving.","cause":"The original implementation lacked an explicit concurrency invariant and selected synchronization based on the observed symptom instead of the complete state transition.","solution":"Specify the invariant, identify all conflicting accesses, establish the required happens-before or ownership relation, and verify the complete transition under concurrent execution.","alternatives":["Use a single lock for a tightly coupled invariant.","Use atomics when the invariant is genuinely expressible through atomic operations.","Redesign ownership to eliminate shared mutable state."],"tradeoffs":"Broader locking simplifies reasoning but can reduce throughput; finer-grained synchronization can improve concurrency while increasing proof and deadlock complexity; ownership redesign may require architectural changes.","regression_impact":"Concurrency repairs can alter timing, fairness, throughput, deadlock characteristics, and externally visible ordering.","verification_method":"Perform invariant-based stress testing across controlled interleavings, use race/deadlock analysis where available, and test cancellation, timeout, restart, and failure paths.","when_not_to_use":"Do not accept a repair merely because one reproducer stops failing; the fix must establish the required invariant across the full concurrency surface.","version_constraints":"The precise memory-order and synchronization model depends on the target language/runtime and concurrency primitives.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B046","D36-B047","D36-B048","D36-B070"],"derived_from":["D36-B046","D36-B047","D36-B048","D36-B070"],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_REASONING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Invariant-based concurrency design, synchronization, ownership, and happens-before reasoning","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B071","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Retrying non-idempotent side effects","concept":"Idempotency-aware retry repair","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a retry mechanism be fixed when repeating a failed operation can duplicate an external side effect?","answer":"Classify the operation's idempotency properties before retrying, then use an idempotency key, deduplication mechanism, transactional boundary, or non-retry strategy appropriate to the side effect.","explanation":"A timeout does not prove that the remote operation failed; the server may have completed it before the response was lost. Blindly retrying a non-idempotent operation can therefore create duplicate effects.","cause":"Retry policy treated every failure or timeout as proof that no external side effect occurred.","solution":"Assign a stable operation identity and make the receiving operation safely deduplicate repeated requests, or avoid automatic retry when the side effect cannot be made idempotent.","alternatives":["Use a server-supported idempotency key.","Persist an operation record before issuing the external request and reconcile ambiguous outcomes.","Retry only operations whose API contract guarantees safe repetition."],"tradeoffs":"Idempotency records require storage and lifecycle management. Reconciliation increases complexity but handles ambiguous network outcomes more safely.","regression_impact":"Introducing deduplication can change behavior for callers that previously relied on repeated submissions producing repeated effects.","verification_method":"Test success, explicit failure, timeout after remote completion, timeout before remote receipt, duplicate requests, client restart, and idempotency-key reuse.","when_not_to_use":"Do not automatically retry a non-idempotent side effect merely because the transport returned a timeout or connection error.","version_constraints":"Exact idempotency guarantees depend on the target API, database, messaging system, and protocol.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B020","D36-B050"],"derived_from":["D36-B020","D36-B050"],"provenance":{"source_type":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Idempotency, retry, ambiguous outcome, and distributed side-effect semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B072","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Database transaction isolation mismatch","concept":"Isolation level aligned with invariant","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a database concurrency bug be fixed when the selected transaction isolation level does not protect the application's required invariant?","answer":"Define the invariant and concurrency anomaly that must be prevented, then use an isolation level, locking strategy, constraint, or atomic database operation that actually enforces that requirement.","explanation":"A transaction boundary alone does not guarantee every business invariant. Different isolation levels permit different anomalies, and application-level read-modify-write sequences may still race unless the database operation or isolation protocol prevents the relevant interleaving.","cause":"The implementation assumed that transaction atomicity automatically provided the required concurrency semantics.","solution":"Identify the required invariant, choose the appropriate database mechanism, and move critical checks and mutations into an atomic or correctly isolated operation where possible.","alternatives":["Use an appropriate unique or check constraint.","Use row-level locking when the invariant requires serialized access.","Use optimistic concurrency with a version predicate and explicit conflict handling."],"tradeoffs":"Stronger isolation and locking can reduce concurrency and increase contention. Optimistic concurrency can improve throughput but requires retry or conflict handling.","regression_impact":"Changing isolation or locking can alter latency, deadlock frequency, throughput, and transaction failure behavior.","verification_method":"Run concurrent transactions designed to reproduce the target anomaly and verify the invariant under the actual production database engine and isolation configuration.","when_not_to_use":"Do not increase isolation globally when only one narrowly defined invariant requires stronger protection.","version_constraints":"Transaction and isolation behavior is database-engine and configuration specific; validate against the exact supported database/version.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B009"],"derived_from":["D36-B009"],"provenance":{"source_type":"DATABASE_STANDARDS_AND_DOCUMENTATION","source_reference":"SQL transaction isolation, locking, constraints, and concurrency-control semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B073","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Resource exhaustion caused by unbounded concurrency","concept":"Bounded concurrency and backpressure","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a system be fixed when concurrent work grows without a bound and eventually exhausts memory, threads, connections, or downstream capacity?","answer":"Introduce explicit concurrency limits, bounded queues, admission control, backpressure, and appropriate cancellation so workload growth cannot create unlimited resource consumption.","explanation":"Concurrency is not free. Creating work faster than the system or its dependencies can process it can transform temporary load into resource exhaustion and cascading failure.","cause":"The implementation accepted work without enforcing a bound on active tasks or queued work.","solution":"Define resource budgets, bound active concurrency and queue depth, reject or delay excess work, and propagate backpressure to producers.","alternatives":["Use a bounded worker pool.","Apply semaphore-based concurrency limits.","Use rate limiting or admission control at the system boundary."],"tradeoffs":"Bounding concurrency can increase individual request latency or reject work during overload, but it protects system stability and downstream dependencies.","regression_impact":"Previously accepted bursts may receive throttling, queue-full, or overload responses. Capacity and timeout assumptions may need adjustment.","verification_method":"Generate load above service capacity and verify bounded memory, connection, and worker counts; confirm overload behavior is deterministic and recoverable.","when_not_to_use":"Do not choose an arbitrary concurrency limit without relating it to CPU, memory, connection, downstream, and latency constraints.","version_constraints":"Concurrency primitives and queue semantics depend on the target language/runtime/framework.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B019","D36-B038","D36-B049"],"derived_from":["D36-B019","D36-B038","D36-B049"],"provenance":{"source_type":"RELIABILITY_AND_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Bounded concurrency, backpressure, resource budgeting, and overload-control principles","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B074","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Cache invalidation and stale state","concept":"Explicit cache consistency contract","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should stale-cache behavior be fixed when cached data remains valid-looking after the authoritative state has changed?","answer":"Define the cache consistency requirement and enforce it with an explicit invalidation, versioning, expiration, write-through, or revalidation strategy appropriate to the data's correctness requirements.","explanation":"A cache is a second representation of state. Without a defined freshness or invalidation contract, consumers can observe obsolete values even though the authoritative source is correct.","cause":"The implementation introduced caching without defining when cached state becomes invalid relative to the source of truth.","solution":"Choose and document a consistency model, associate cached entries with appropriate versions or expiry, and invalidate or revalidate them when authoritative state changes.","alternatives":["Use versioned cache keys.","Invalidate affected keys after successful authoritative writes.","Use bounded TTL with explicit acceptance of staleness.","Bypass cache for operations requiring authoritative reads."],"tradeoffs":"Aggressive invalidation increases coordination cost; short TTL increases backend load; versioned keys can increase storage until old entries expire.","regression_impact":"More frequent cache misses can increase latency and database/network load. Stronger freshness can reduce throughput.","verification_method":"Update authoritative state and test reads immediately, after invalidation, after TTL expiry, after process restart, and under concurrent updates.","when_not_to_use":"Do not add a cache to correctness-critical state without first specifying an acceptable staleness bound or consistency model.","version_constraints":"Caching semantics depend on the cache implementation, storage layer, framework, and deployment topology.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B032","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B032","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SYSTEM_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Cache consistency, invalidation, versioning, and stale-data control","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B075","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Configuration drift between environments","concept":"Single-source configuration validation","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should an environment-specific failure be fixed when configuration differs silently between development, testing, and production?","answer":"Define a versioned configuration contract, validate required settings at startup or deployment, make environment differences explicit, and fail clearly when mandatory configuration is invalid or missing.","explanation":"Silent defaults and undocumented environment differences can cause software to behave differently despite identical application code. Configuration should therefore be treated as a typed, validated input with an explicit contract.","cause":"Configuration values, defaults, formats, or dependencies differed between environments without an enforced contract.","solution":"Centralize configuration schema, validate it before serving traffic, classify required versus optional settings, and record non-secret configuration provenance/version information.","alternatives":["Use typed configuration objects with validation.","Generate environment-specific configuration from a common schema.","Use deployment-time validation before application startup."],"tradeoffs":"Strict validation can prevent startup when configuration is incomplete, which is preferable to serving incorrect behavior but may affect deployment workflows.","regression_impact":"Previously tolerated missing or malformed settings may become deployment failures and require configuration cleanup.","verification_method":"Test missing values, malformed values, conflicting settings, environment-specific overrides, secret references, and version migration scenarios.","when_not_to_use":"Do not silently substitute operationally significant defaults merely to keep the application running.","version_constraints":"Configuration behavior depends on the application framework, deployment platform, environment-variable conventions, and configuration library.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"RELIABILITY_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Configuration validation, environment parity, and fail-fast deployment practices","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B076","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Schema or API contract evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible contract migration","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a software component be fixed when a changed API or data schema breaks consumers that still depend on the previous contract?","answer":"Version or evolve the contract compatibly, provide an explicit migration path, validate compatibility at boundaries, and remove deprecated behavior only after dependent consumers have migrated.","explanation":"Changing a producer contract without coordinating consumers creates failures even when the producer's new implementation is internally correct. Compatibility must be treated as an interface-level invariant.","cause":"The implementation changed field names, types, semantics, requiredness, or protocol behavior without preserving or versioning the consumer contract.","solution":"Introduce a compatibility layer or versioned contract, migrate consumers incrementally, and verify both old and new contract paths during the transition.","alternatives":["Additive schema evolution when semantics permit it.","Use an adapter translating legacy requests into the current internal representation.","Run dual-read or dual-write migration phases where consistency requirements allow them."],"tradeoffs":"Compatibility layers increase temporary complexity and maintenance burden. Dual operation can increase storage, processing, and reconciliation requirements.","regression_impact":"Consumer behavior may diverge if old and new representations have subtly different semantics; migration must verify semantic equivalence where required.","verification_method":"Test old and new clients against the producer, validate serialization/deserialization, verify defaults and required fields, and test rollback during migration.","when_not_to_use":"Do not preserve an obsolete contract indefinitely when its semantics are unsafe or incompatible with required correctness; use an explicit breaking-version migration instead.","version_constraints":"Contract evolution rules depend on the protocol, schema format, API style, and compatibility guarantees of the target system.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B033","D36-B069"],"derived_from":["D36-B033","D36-B069"],"provenance":{"source_type":"API_AND_SCHEMA_EVOLUTION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Backward compatibility, semantic versioning, schema evolution, and migration patterns","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B077","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Security fix for untrusted input reaching a sensitive sink","concept":"Trust-boundary validation and context-specific encoding","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should code be fixed when untrusted input reaches a security-sensitive sink without appropriate validation or context-specific handling?","answer":"Validate input against the intended domain constraints and apply the security control appropriate to the destination context, such as parameterization for database queries or context-specific output encoding.","explanation":"Generic sanitization is not a universal security control. The required defense depends on the sink and the interpreter that consumes the data. Validation should constrain business input while sink-specific controls prevent interpretation as unintended syntax or commands.","cause":"The implementation treated externally controlled data as trusted or applied a control unrelated to the sink's interpretation rules.","solution":"Identify the trust boundary and sink, constrain accepted input, use parameterized APIs or safe structured interfaces where available, and encode output according to its destination context.","alternatives":["Use prepared statements for SQL data values.","Use structured APIs instead of constructing interpreter commands as strings.","Use allowlists for constrained identifiers when the protocol permits only known values."],"tradeoffs":"Strict validation can reject previously accepted inputs. Parameterization or structured APIs may require interface changes but substantially reduce interpretation ambiguity.","regression_impact":"Previously accepted malformed or adversarial inputs may fail earlier. Encoding can change output representation and must be tested for functional correctness.","verification_method":"Test valid, malformed, boundary, and adversarial inputs at the actual trust boundary and verify that data cannot become unintended executable syntax at the sink.","when_not_to_use":"Do not rely on client-side validation alone or on generic escaping without considering the destination interpreter and context.","version_constraints":"Exact controls depend on the target database, template engine, shell, browser, serialization format, framework, and runtime version.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B040","D36-B066"],"derived_from":["D36-B040","D36-B066"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURITY_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Trust boundaries, input validation, parameterization, output encoding, and secure API design","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B078","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Performance regression from repeated expensive computation","concept":"Evidence-driven computation reuse","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should code be fixed when profiling shows that the same expensive computation is repeatedly performed for identical inputs?","answer":"First verify the repeated work with profiling, then introduce an appropriate reuse strategy such as memoization, caching, batching, or precomputation while preserving invalidation and memory constraints.","explanation":"Optimization should follow measured evidence rather than assumed hotspots. Reusing deterministic results can reduce repeated CPU or I/O work, but cached results must remain valid under the application's state and consistency requirements.","cause":"The implementation recomputed an expensive deterministic result despite repeated equivalent inputs and no requirement for fresh computation.","solution":"Measure the workload, identify a stable cache key and validity contract, then reuse results within a bounded memory and freshness policy.","alternatives":["Memoize pure function results.","Batch equivalent requests.","Precompute stable derived data during initialization or scheduled jobs.","Cache at a lower architectural layer when multiple callers share the same expensive operation."],"tradeoffs":"Caching consumes memory and can create stale-data bugs. Batching can increase latency for individual requests while improving throughput.","regression_impact":"Optimization can change memory usage, latency distribution, freshness, and failure behavior when cached computations depend on external state.","verification_method":"Profile before and after, compare relevant latency/throughput/resource measurements, test cache hits and misses, invalidation, memory bounds, and correctness under changing inputs.","when_not_to_use":"Do not introduce caching solely because an operation appears expensive without evidence that repeated computation materially affects the target workload.","version_constraints":"Performance behavior depends on runtime, workload, hardware, data size, and implementation; benchmark results are workload-specific.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B067","D36-B068"],"derived_from":["D36-B067","D36-B068"],"provenance":{"source_type":"PERFORMANCE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Profiling-guided optimization, memoization, caching, and workload-specific performance analysis","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B079","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Deadlock introduced by lock-order inversion","concept":"Global lock-order invariant","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a deadlock be fixed when two execution paths acquire multiple locks in inconsistent orders?","answer":"Establish a global lock-order invariant and ensure every path acquires overlapping locks according to that order, or redesign ownership so multiple locks do not need to be held simultaneously.","explanation":"If one path acquires A then B while another acquires B then A, concurrent execution can produce circular waiting. A consistent acquisition order prevents that specific cycle when all paths obey it.","cause":"Multiple locks protected related state but lacked a consistent global acquisition order.","solution":"Define and document lock ordering, acquire locks in that order everywhere, minimize simultaneous lock scope, and consider ownership redesign for highly coupled state.","alternatives":["Use a single higher-level lock when the invariant is tightly coupled.","Use lock-order checking tooling where available.","Redesign operations to release one lock before acquiring another when correctness permits."],"tradeoffs":"A global order reduces deadlock risk but can constrain architecture and increase lock hold time. A single lock simplifies reasoning but can reduce concurrency.","regression_impact":"Changing lock order can alter timing and contention and may expose hidden dependencies between components.","verification_method":"Construct conflicting interleavings, run concurrency stress tests, inspect all multi-lock acquisition paths, and use deadlock detection or thread analysis where available.","when_not_to_use":"Do not impose an arbitrary lock order without considering reentrant calls, callbacks, external code, and locks acquired indirectly.","version_constraints":"Deadlock behavior depends on the language memory model, synchronization primitives, runtime, and framework.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B046","D36-B070"],"derived_from":["D36-B046","D36-B070"],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Lock-ordering, deadlock prevention, and synchronization-scope principles","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B080","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Repair verification and regression containment","concept":"Evidence-backed fix acceptance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a root-cause fix be accepted when the original symptom disappears but correctness has not yet been established?","answer":"Accept the repair only after verifying the original failure condition, the intended postcondition, relevant edge cases, regression behavior, and any security or performance invariants affected by the change.","explanation":"A disappearing symptom is evidence that one observed execution path changed, not proof that the root cause has been eliminated or that the replacement behavior is correct. Fix acceptance requires evidence aligned with the original invariant and affected system boundaries.","cause":"The repair was evaluated primarily by symptom disappearance rather than by explicit root-cause, invariant, and regression verification.","solution":"Map the fix to the original root cause, define acceptance properties, reproduce the original failure before the fix when possible, verify the corrected behavior, and run targeted regression tests.","alternatives":["Use a regression test derived directly from the original failure.","Add property or invariant checks for the repaired behavior.","Use static analysis, race detection, type checking, or formal reasoning when appropriate to the failure class."],"tradeoffs":"Broader verification increases engineering time but reduces the risk of accepting a superficially successful repair. Excessive unrelated testing can dilute diagnostic value.","regression_impact":"A fix can resolve the original defect while introducing new failures in neighboring behavior, performance, concurrency, or security boundaries.","verification_method":"Require evidence for symptom elimination, root-cause correction, intended postcondition, edge cases, regression suite, and applicable security/performance constraints; record unverified claims explicitly.","when_not_to_use":"Do not mark a root-cause repair as fully verified solely because code compiles, a single test passes, or the original reproducer no longer fails.","version_constraints":"Verification tooling and available static/dynamic analyses depend on the language, runtime, framework, and deployment environment.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B001","D36-B010","D36-B070"],"derived_from":["D36-B001","D36-B010","D36-B070"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Regression testing, invariant verification, root-cause validation, and evidence-based defect closure","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B081","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Resource ownership across API boundaries","concept":"Explicit ownership transfer and lifecycle contracts","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a resource-lifetime bug be fixed when the caller and callee disagree about who owns cleanup of a resource?","answer":"Define ownership explicitly at the API boundary and ensure exactly one component is responsible for releasing the resource, unless the contract explicitly supports shared ownership.","explanation":"Ambiguous ownership can cause leaks, double release, premature closure, or use-after-release. The repair must make acquisition, transfer, borrowing, and destruction responsibilities explicit.","cause":"The API exposed a resource without specifying whether ownership was transferred, borrowed, shared, or retained by the provider.","solution":"Document and encode ownership semantics in the API, transfer ownership exactly once, or use a scoped/resource-management abstraction that makes lifetime boundaries explicit.","alternatives":["Use RAII or deterministic scope-based ownership where supported.","Use reference counting only when shared ownership is genuinely required.","Return a managed handle whose disposal semantics are explicit."],"tradeoffs":"Explicit ownership can require API redesign. Shared ownership simplifies some call sites but increases lifecycle complexity and can delay resource release.","regression_impact":"Ownership changes can alter object lifetimes, cleanup timing, memory usage, and compatibility with existing callers.","verification_method":"Test normal completion, exceptions, early returns, repeated ownership transfer, premature disposal, and double-disposal scenarios.","when_not_to_use":"Do not introduce shared ownership merely to avoid deciding which component should own a resource.","version_constraints":"Ownership mechanisms depend on the target language/runtime and resource type.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B005","D36-B035","D36-B067"],"derived_from":["D36-B005","D36-B035","D36-B067"],"provenance":{"source_type":"RESOURCE_MANAGEMENT_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Resource ownership, lifetime, deterministic cleanup, and API contract design","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B082","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Partial initialization and invalid object state","concept":"Atomic object construction and valid-state invariants","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should an object be fixed when it can become externally visible before all required initialization has completed?","answer":"Construct the object completely before publication, validate required invariants during construction, and avoid exposing partially initialized state to consumers.","explanation":"If consumers can observe an object between initialization steps, they may invoke methods against missing dependencies or inconsistent fields. Construction should establish the minimum valid state before publication.","cause":"Initialization was spread across multiple steps while the object was accessible to other code or threads before completion.","solution":"Use constructors or factories that establish required invariants before returning the object, and publish the object only after successful initialization.","alternatives":["Use a builder that cannot produce the final object until required fields are supplied.","Use an explicit lifecycle state machine when staged initialization is unavoidable.","Use dependency injection to provide mandatory dependencies at construction time."],"tradeoffs":"Atomic construction can make initialization code larger and may require refactoring legacy APIs. Explicit lifecycle states add state-management complexity.","regression_impact":"Previously tolerated incomplete objects may now fail at construction time, moving failures closer to their actual cause.","verification_method":"Attempt construction with missing dependencies, initialization failures, concurrent publication, repeated initialization, and invalid configuration; verify no externally observable invalid state exists.","when_not_to_use":"Do not add arbitrary lifecycle states when the object can instead be made valid through ordinary construction.","version_constraints":"Exact publication and initialization semantics depend on the target language memory model and concurrency model.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B036","D36-B069","D36-B070"],"derived_from":["D36-B036","D36-B069","D36-B070"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_DESIGN_AND_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Object invariants, safe publication, initialization safety, and lifecycle design","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B083","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"File-system race between validation and use","concept":"Atomic or authority-preserving filesystem operations","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a filesystem race be fixed when code checks a path before using it and another actor can change the filesystem between the check and the operation?","answer":"Avoid security- or correctness-critical check-then-use sequences; instead use an operation that performs the required action atomically or binds the operation to an already validated resource handle.","explanation":"A path check establishes only a temporary observation. The filesystem can change before the subsequent open, rename, delete, or execute operation, invalidating the assumption.","cause":"The implementation treated a separate preliminary filesystem check as a durable guarantee about the later operation.","solution":"Use atomic filesystem operations where available, open the resource and validate the resulting handle where appropriate, or use platform-specific APIs that preserve the intended security boundary.","alternatives":["Use exclusive creation flags for create-if-absent requirements.","Use descriptor/handle-based operations when the platform supports them.","Perform authorization and validation as close as possible to the actual privileged operation."],"tradeoffs":"Atomic APIs can be platform-specific. Handle-based designs may require lower-level APIs and careful cleanup.","regression_impact":"Removing pre-checks can change error handling because the authoritative operation becomes the source of truth for existence, permissions, and conflicts.","verification_method":"Run concurrent filesystem modifications during the operation and verify the resulting action follows the intended authorization and atomicity contract.","when_not_to_use":"Do not treat os.path.exists-style checks or equivalent existence tests as a security guarantee for a subsequent privileged operation.","version_constraints":"Exact atomicity and handle semantics depend on the operating system and filesystem API.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B039","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B039","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"OPERATING_SYSTEM_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"TOCTOU avoidance, atomic filesystem operations, and handle-based resource access","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B084","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Unicode and text-boundary assumptions","concept":"Explicit text encoding and boundary semantics","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a text-processing bug be fixed when code assumes characters, bytes, indexes, or display units are interchangeable?","answer":"Define the required text unit explicitly and use APIs appropriate to that unit, distinguishing bytes, Unicode code points, grapheme clusters, and encoded storage representations.","explanation":"Text length and indexing are not universally equivalent across languages and runtimes. A repair must identify whether the requirement concerns encoded bytes, Unicode scalar values/code points, user-perceived characters, or display/storage units.","cause":"The implementation used an indexing or length operation whose unit differed from the application's actual requirement.","solution":"Keep text decoded at the semantic layer, encode only at explicit boundaries, and choose Unicode-aware operations for the required semantic unit.","alternatives":["Use byte-oriented APIs for protocol/storage byte limits.","Use grapheme-aware libraries when user-perceived character boundaries matter.","Validate encoded length separately from logical text length when both constraints apply."],"tradeoffs":"Unicode-aware processing can require additional computation and specialized libraries. Byte limits may still require careful truncation to avoid invalid encodings.","regression_impact":"Correct Unicode handling can change lengths, truncation behavior, sorting, validation, and UI display for multilingual input.","verification_method":"Test ASCII, combining marks, emoji sequences, non-Latin scripts, invalid byte sequences, normalization-sensitive data, and exact byte/code-point/grapheme boundaries.","when_not_to_use":"Do not equate language-level string length with user-perceived character count unless the language/runtime explicitly defines the required semantic unit.","version_constraints":"Unicode behavior depends on the language, runtime, Unicode version, normalization libraries, and platform APIs.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B039"],"derived_from":["D36-B039"],"provenance":{"source_type":"UNICODE_AND_TEXT_PROCESSING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Unicode encoding, code-point, grapheme-cluster, and byte-boundary semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B085","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Serialization and deserialization trust boundary","concept":"Schema validation before object use","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should code be fixed when deserialized data is treated as trusted application objects without validating its structure or allowed values?","answer":"Validate the serialized input against an explicit schema and security policy before converting it into trusted application state or invoking behavior based on it.","explanation":"Deserialization establishes a data boundary, not correctness. External or persisted data can be malformed, stale, version-incompatible, or intentionally crafted to violate assumptions.","cause":"The implementation assumed serialized representation implied valid domain state.","solution":"Parse into a constrained representation, validate required fields and types, enforce allowed values and size limits, then construct trusted domain objects only after validation succeeds.","alternatives":["Use schema-driven validation before domain construction.","Use typed constructors that reject invalid state.","Introduce explicit migration for older serialized versions instead of accepting incompatible data implicitly."],"tradeoffs":"Validation adds CPU and code complexity but prevents invalid state from spreading through the application.","regression_impact":"Previously accepted malformed or legacy data may fail loading and require migration or recovery paths.","verification_method":"Test missing fields, wrong types, unknown fields, oversized values, invalid enum values, corrupt payloads, old schema versions, and adversarial input.","when_not_to_use":"Do not deserialize untrusted data directly into executable or privileged application state without an explicit trust and validation boundary.","version_constraints":"Serialization semantics and safe-deserialization mechanisms depend on the format, library, language, and runtime version.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B014","D36-B033","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B014","D36-B033","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SECURE_DESERIALIZATION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Schema validation, trust boundaries, serialization contracts, and secure deserialization","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B086","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Cancellation-unsafe cleanup","concept":"Cancellation-aware resource and state transitions","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should asynchronous code be fixed when cancellation can interrupt an operation between state updates and cleanup steps?","answer":"Design cancellation boundaries explicitly, make cleanup guaranteed, and ensure externally visible state cannot remain inconsistent when cancellation occurs at each interruptible point.","explanation":"Cancellation is a control-flow event, not merely an error. If cancellation interrupts a multi-step operation after one side effect but before cleanup or state recording, the system can enter a state that later retries misinterpret.","cause":"The implementation assumed asynchronous work would either complete normally or fail before any externally visible partial state was produced.","solution":"Define cancellation-safe state transitions, use guaranteed cleanup mechanisms, record durable progress where required, and make repeated recovery idempotent.","alternatives":["Shield or defer cancellation only around small critical cleanup sections when the runtime supports it.","Use explicit state-machine transitions for long-running operations.","Persist operation status before and after externally visible side effects."],"tradeoffs":"Deferring cancellation can increase shutdown latency; durable state adds I/O and complexity; state machines increase explicit design overhead.","regression_impact":"Cancellation may become more deterministic but can change shutdown timing and partial-operation recovery behavior.","verification_method":"Inject cancellation at every meaningful await/interruption point and verify resource release, durable state, retry behavior, and final consistency.","when_not_to_use":"Do not suppress cancellation across long-running work merely to simplify cleanup.","version_constraints":"Cancellation semantics vary substantially across Python asyncio, JavaScript promises/AbortSignal, Java concurrency, Rust async runtimes, and Go contexts.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B037","D36-B049"],"derived_from":["D36-B037","D36-B049"],"provenance":{"source_type":"ASYNC_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Cancellation safety, cleanup guarantees, and state-machine recovery","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B087","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Integer conversion and boundary mismatch","concept":"Range-preserving numeric conversion","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should code be fixed when a value is converted between integer types or numeric representations and the destination cannot represent the original range?","answer":"Validate the source range before conversion, use a representation capable of expressing the required domain, or define explicit overflow behavior instead of relying on implicit narrowing.","explanation":"Numeric conversions can truncate, wrap, saturate, or otherwise change values depending on the language and conversion operation. The repair must preserve the application's numeric invariant.","cause":"The implementation assumed that a source numeric value could always be represented by the destination type.","solution":"Establish range checks before narrowing, choose a sufficiently wide type, or use an explicit checked/conversion API with defined failure behavior.","alternatives":["Use checked arithmetic or conversion APIs where available.","Reject values outside the supported domain at the trust boundary.","Use arbitrary-precision integers when the domain genuinely exceeds fixed-width limits."],"tradeoffs":"Range checking adds branches; wider or arbitrary-precision representations can increase memory or computation costs.","regression_impact":"Previously wrapped or truncated values may now produce explicit errors, changing downstream control flow but restoring domain correctness.","verification_method":"Test minimum, maximum, just-outside-range, negative, zero, large, and repeated conversion values under the target platform.","when_not_to_use":"Do not silently clamp or wrap values unless that behavior is explicitly part of the domain contract.","version_constraints":"Conversion and overflow behavior varies by language, type, compiler, and operation; verify the exact target semantics.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B024","D36-B063"],"derived_from":["D36-B024","D36-B063"],"provenance":{"source_type":"LANGUAGE_NUMERIC_SEMANTICS","source_reference":"Integer ranges, narrowing conversions, checked arithmetic, and overflow semantics","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B088","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Hidden dependency on global mutable state","concept":"Dependency isolation and explicit state ownership","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should code be fixed when behavior depends on mutable global state that is difficult to test, reason about, or isolate between requests?","answer":"Move mutable state behind an explicit owner or dependency boundary, inject required state into the component, and isolate per-request or per-operation state from process-wide configuration.","explanation":"Implicit global state creates hidden coupling, test-order dependence, race risks, and accidental cross-request contamination. Making state ownership explicit improves reasoning and lifecycle control.","cause":"Functions or components read or mutate process-global state without an explicit ownership or lifecycle contract.","solution":"Encapsulate state in a dedicated component, inject it where needed, and distinguish immutable configuration from mutable operational state.","alternatives":["Use immutable configuration objects for read-only settings.","Use request-scoped or task-scoped context for transient state.","Use a synchronized shared store only when process-wide shared state is genuinely required."],"tradeoffs":"Dependency injection can increase parameter or object-construction complexity. Isolating state can increase allocations or require explicit persistence.","regression_impact":"Refactoring global state can change initialization order, caching behavior, test assumptions, and process-wide coordination.","verification_method":"Run tests in different orders, execute concurrent requests, reset state between operations, and verify one operation cannot unexpectedly modify another operation's state.","when_not_to_use":"Do not eliminate legitimate immutable process-wide configuration merely because it is globally accessible.","version_constraints":"State-isolation mechanisms depend on the language runtime, framework lifecycle, threading model, and deployment architecture.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B011","D36-B028","D36-B032","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B011","D36-B028","D36-B032","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"State ownership, dependency injection, lifecycle isolation, and concurrency design","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B089","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Fallback behavior masking operational failure","concept":"Fail-closed fallback classification","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should fallback logic be fixed when an unavailable dependency is silently replaced with a degraded result that looks successful?","answer":"Classify the fallback explicitly, preserve failure provenance, expose degraded state to the appropriate caller, and use fail-closed behavior when continuing would violate correctness or security requirements.","explanation":"Fallbacks can improve availability but can also convert dependency failure into false success. The correct design distinguishes acceptable degraded operation from a state where the requested guarantee cannot be provided.","cause":"Fallback behavior substituted a default or cached result without communicating that the primary operation had failed.","solution":"Define fallback eligibility and guarantees, attach degraded-state metadata, preserve the original failure, and reject the operation when required guarantees cannot be maintained.","alternatives":["Return stale-but-explicitly-marked data when the contract permits it.","Use a circuit breaker to prevent repeated dependency pressure.","Require an explicit caller policy for degraded operation."],"tradeoffs":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability; fail-open or degraded behavior can preserve availability while weakening guarantees.","regression_impact":"Callers may begin seeing explicit degraded or failure states instead of apparently successful results and must handle those states correctly.","verification_method":"Simulate dependency timeout, unavailable service, malformed response, stale cache, and recovery; verify each fallback path preserves correct outcome semantics.","when_not_to_use":"Do not silently substitute a default value when the missing dependency is required to establish correctness, authorization, safety, or security properties.","version_constraints":"Fallback behavior is application and dependency specific; define the contract for each operational dependency.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B040","D36-B066"],"derived_from":["D36-B040","D36-B066"],"provenance":{"source_type":"RELIABILITY_AND_SECURITY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Fail-closed design, degraded-mode semantics, circuit breakers, and failure provenance","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B090","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Fix completeness across implementation, tests, and verification","concept":"Root-cause repair closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required before a root-cause fix is considered complete rather than merely patched?","answer":"A complete fix should connect the identified root cause to the changed implementation, demonstrate the intended behavior through targeted tests, cover relevant edge and failure cases, and document any remaining unverified assumptions or limitations.","explanation":"A patch is not equivalent to a verified repair. High-quality defect closure requires traceability from failure to cause to change to evidence and should explicitly distinguish executed evidence from illustrative reasoning.","cause":"The defect workflow ended after code modification without establishing a traceable verification chain.","solution":"Create a repair record linking root cause, change, regression test, expected behavior, observed result, verification method, affected versions, and residual uncertainty.","alternatives":["Use a formal proof or static analysis result for properties amenable to formal verification.","Use regression plus property-based testing for complex input spaces.","Use production telemetry or controlled replay for failures that cannot be fully reproduced locally."],"tradeoffs":"Comprehensive closure requires more evidence and engineering time, but it reduces recurrence and makes future automated reasoning more reliable.","regression_impact":"Additional tests may reveal unrelated latent defects or invalidate assumptions previously hidden by the original bug.","verification_method":"Check traceability, targeted regression coverage, edge/failure coverage, execution truth, security/performance implications, version applicability, and unresolved assumptions before marking the repair verified.","when_not_to_use":"Do not mark a fix as verified when the only evidence is compilation, static syntax validity, a single passing example, or disappearance of one observed symptom.","version_constraints":"Verification depth depends on the target language, runtime, framework, deployment environment, and available tooling.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D37-B080","D36-B001","D36-B050"],"derived_from":["D37-B080","D36-B001","D36-B050"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Defect traceability, regression testing, evidence-based verification, and residual-risk documentation","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B091","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Ambiguous ownership after asynchronous handoff","concept":"Explicit task ownership and completion observation","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should asynchronous work be fixed when the code starts a task but no component clearly owns its completion, failure, or cancellation?","answer":"Assign explicit ownership to the task lifecycle, retain an observable completion handle, propagate cancellation, and define how failures are surfaced to the component responsible for the operation.","explanation":"A background task without lifecycle ownership can outlive its request, lose exceptions, continue after shutdown, or leave partial state. The repair must establish who starts, supervises, cancels, and observes the task.","cause":"Task creation was treated as completion of the operation without a defined supervisor or lifecycle owner.","solution":"Use structured task ownership, await or supervise spawned work, retain a handle when detached execution is intentional, and define cancellation and failure propagation.","alternatives":["Use structured concurrency where supported.","Use a durable job queue for work intentionally longer-lived than the request lifecycle.","Use explicit task registries with shutdown and failure handling when detached execution is required."],"tradeoffs":"Supervision adds lifecycle coordination. Durable queues add persistence and operational complexity but provide stronger restart semantics.","regression_impact":"Previously detached work may now be cancelled when its owner exits, changing background processing behavior.","verification_method":"Test successful completion, task failure, owner cancellation, process shutdown, repeated task creation, and partial side effects.","when_not_to_use":"Do not detach work from its owner merely to avoid waiting for completion when the result is required for correctness.","version_constraints":"Task ownership and cancellation semantics depend on the target async runtime/framework.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B012","D36-B019","D36-B037"],"derived_from":["D36-B012","D36-B019","D36-B037"],"provenance":{"source_type":"ASYNC_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Task ownership, structured concurrency, cancellation, and asynchronous lifecycle management","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B092","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Ordering assumption in asynchronous workflows","concept":"Explicit sequencing and dependency synchronization","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should asynchronous code be fixed when it assumes that concurrently scheduled operations will complete in the order they were started?","answer":"Represent the required dependency explicitly by awaiting the prerequisite, chaining operations, using a synchronization primitive, or redesigning the workflow so completion order does not affect correctness.","explanation":"Task creation order does not generally establish completion order. If operation B depends on the state produced by operation A, that dependency must be represented in the execution model.","cause":"The implementation relied on incidental scheduling order rather than an explicit happens-before relationship.","solution":"Encode the dependency through await/chaining, synchronization, sequence numbers, or an explicit state machine.","alternatives":["Run operations sequentially when ordering is mandatory and concurrency provides no required benefit.","Use queues when ordered processing is the primary invariant.","Use versioned state updates when stale completions must be rejected."],"tradeoffs":"Explicit sequencing can reduce concurrency. Versioned or queued designs preserve more concurrency but increase state-management complexity.","regression_impact":"Correct ordering can expose callers that previously depended on accidental race behavior.","verification_method":"Introduce artificial delays in every operation and verify correctness under all completion orders, including cancellation and failure of prerequisites.","when_not_to_use":"Do not serialize independent operations solely because they happen to be started together.","version_constraints":"Exact scheduling and synchronization semantics depend on the target language/runtime/framework.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B032","D36-B037"],"derived_from":["D36-B032","D36-B037"],"provenance":{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_REASONING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Happens-before, asynchronous sequencing, task scheduling, and state-ordering principles","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

