{"record_id":"D37-B095","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Migration failure and rollback safety","concept":"Forward-compatible migration with recoverable state","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a deployment migration be fixed when failure during the migration can leave application and data schemas incompatible?","answer":"Design migrations as explicit, staged transitions with compatibility between old and new versions, backup or recovery procedures where required, and a tested rollback or forward-repair strategy.","explanation":"A migration is itself a distributed state transition between application versions, schemas, stored data, and possibly external consumers. A partial migration can make neither the old nor new application fully valid.","cause":"The migration assumed atomic deployment across components that can actually change independently or fail partway through.","solution":"Separate incompatible changes into compatibility-preserving stages, migrate data explicitly, deploy consumers/producers in a safe order, and verify recovery from interruption.","alternatives":["Use expand-and-contract schema migration.","Maintain dual-read or dual-write temporarily where justified.","Use a forward-only migration with tested repair procedures when rollback is unsafe."],"tradeoffs":"Staged migrations increase temporary schema and code complexity. Rollback support can be difficult when irreversible data transformations have occurred.","regression_impact":"During migration, multiple schema/application versions may coexist and must be tested together.","verification_method":"Interrupt migration at each step, restart from partial state, test old/new application compatibility, validate data invariants, and exercise recovery procedures.","when_not_to_use":"Do not assume a database rollback automatically restores external side effects or irreversible data transformations.","version_constraints":"Migration behavior depends on the database engine, schema tooling, deployment topology, and application compatibility contract.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D37-B076","D36-B009"],"derived_from":["D37-B076","D36-B009"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_MIGRATION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Expand-contract migration, compatibility staging, rollback, and forward-recovery practices","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B096","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Dependency version incompatibility","concept":"Version-constrained dependency resolution","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a software failure be fixed when a dependency update changes behavior or API compatibility?","answer":"Identify the exact dependency and runtime versions involved, constrain compatible ranges, migrate code to the supported contract, and test the dependency combination actually deployed.","explanation":"Dependency compatibility is a versioned contract. A package that worked under one version can fail after an update because APIs, defaults, semantics, transitive dependencies, or runtime requirements changed.","cause":"The application relied on behavior that was removed, changed, deprecated, or incompatible with the selected dependency version.","solution":"Pin or constrain supported versions appropriately, inspect release and compatibility documentation, migrate to the new contract when upgrading, and test the complete dependency graph.","alternatives":["Use a lockfile for reproducible resolution.","Maintain a compatibility matrix for supported runtime/dependency combinations.","Temporarily constrain the dependency while a migration is prepared."],"tradeoffs":"Strict pinning improves reproducibility but can delay security or feature updates. Broad ranges increase upgrade flexibility but require stronger compatibility testing.","regression_impact":"Dependency upgrades can change transitive behavior, performance, serialization, defaults, and security properties.","verification_method":"Reproduce with the exact old and new dependency graphs, run compatibility tests, inspect resolved versions, and test installation from a clean environment.","when_not_to_use":"Do not claim compatibility merely because the package installs successfully; installation success does not establish behavioral compatibility.","version_constraints":"Version compatibility must be recorded against exact language/runtime/framework/library versions where behavior materially differs.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B033","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B033","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"DEPENDENCY_MANAGEMENT_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Dependency versioning, compatibility constraints, lockfiles, and migration practices","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B097","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Cross-platform behavior divergence","concept":"Platform-neutral abstraction with explicit capability handling","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a bug be fixed when code works on one operating system or architecture but fails because it assumes platform-specific behavior?","answer":"Move platform-specific behavior behind an explicit abstraction, use portable APIs where they satisfy the requirement, and detect or declare platform capabilities when behavior genuinely differs.","explanation":"Filesystem paths, line endings, permissions, process behavior, clocks, networking, case sensitivity, and native dependencies can differ across environments. Portability requires identifying the actual platform assumption rather than patching one observed machine.","cause":"The implementation encoded a platform-specific assumption without declaring or isolating it.","solution":"Use standard cross-platform APIs, isolate unavoidable platform-specific code, and test on every supported platform or explicitly constrain the supported environment.","alternatives":["Use capability detection rather than hard-coded platform names when behavior is feature-dependent.","Provide platform-specific adapters behind a common interface.","Declare unsupported platforms explicitly when portability is not a requirement."],"tradeoffs":"Portable abstractions can hide platform-specific capabilities; multi-platform testing increases CI and maintenance cost.","regression_impact":"Replacing platform-specific behavior may alter performance, permissions, path handling, process control, or filesystem semantics.","verification_method":"Run the affected behavior on each supported OS/architecture, including path, encoding, permissions, process, and case-sensitivity edge cases as relevant.","when_not_to_use":"Do not introduce unnecessary portability abstractions when the product explicitly supports one platform and the constraint is intentional.","version_constraints":"Platform behavior depends on operating system, architecture, runtime, filesystem, compiler, and library versions.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B039","D36-B040"],"derived_from":["D36-B039","D36-B040"],"provenance":{"source_type":"PORTABILITY_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Cross-platform APIs, platform capability detection, filesystem and runtime portability","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B098","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Invariant violation after recovery","concept":"Post-recovery state reconciliation","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a recovery path be fixed when the system resumes operation but internal state may no longer agree with external reality?","answer":"Reconcile local state with the authoritative source after recovery, explicitly classify uncertain operations, and rebuild or repair derived state before declaring the system healthy.","explanation":"Restart, timeout, crash, or dependency recovery can leave local state stale relative to external systems. Continuing from the last in-memory assumption can reproduce the original inconsistency.","cause":"Recovery restored process execution without restoring the consistency relationship between local state, durable state, and external systems.","solution":"Perform reconciliation, verify durable checkpoints, re-read authoritative state where necessary, mark uncertain operations, and only transition to healthy operation after required invariants are restored.","alternatives":["Replay durable events when event history is authoritative.","Rebuild derived indexes or caches from the source of truth.","Use reconciliation jobs for externally owned state."],"tradeoffs":"Reconciliation adds startup or recovery latency and external reads. Rebuilding derived state can consume significant resources.","regression_impact":"Recovery may take longer and may expose previously hidden inconsistent records requiring explicit remediation.","verification_method":"Inject crashes and dependency failures at each state boundary, restart, compare local and authoritative state, and verify convergence before accepting new dependent operations.","when_not_to_use":"Do not mark a recovered process healthy merely because its process is running and its basic health endpoint responds.","version_constraints":"Recovery semantics depend on the persistence model, external systems, event history, and application lifecycle.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B009","D36-B050","D37-B069"],"derived_from":["D36-B009","D36-B050","D37-B069"],"provenance":{"source_type":"RESILIENCE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"State reconciliation, recovery, durable checkpoints, replay, and convergence","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B099","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"Repair regression from changing observable behavior","concept":"Contract-preserving repair","knowledge_type":"SOLUTION","question":"How should a bug fix be designed when correcting the root cause could unintentionally change behavior relied upon by existing consumers?","answer":"Separate the defective behavior from the intended public contract, preserve documented compatibility where possible, and introduce an explicit versioned or migration path when the correction necessarily changes observable behavior.","explanation":"Not every existing behavior is a valid contract, but consumers may still depend on it. A repair should identify whether the observed behavior is specified, accidental, unsafe, or undocumented before changing it.","cause":"The repair treated all existing behavior as either correct or irrelevant without analyzing the actual interface contract and dependent consumers.","solution":"Classify the behavior against documentation and tests, preserve legitimate contract semantics, correct undefined or unsafe behavior deliberately, and provide migration guidance for unavoidable changes.","alternatives":["Introduce a compatibility mode during migration.","Version the API when the semantic change is intentionally breaking.","Add deprecation warnings before removing a supported behavior."],"tradeoffs":"Compatibility increases maintenance cost; breaking changes simplify the implementation but require coordinated consumer migration.","regression_impact":"Correcting a latent defect can legitimately change outputs, timing, errors, ordering, or accepted inputs; regression tests should distinguish intended correction from unintended breakage.","verification_method":"Compare documented contract, existing tests, representative consumers, security requirements, and corrected behavior; test both compatibility and intended new semantics.","when_not_to_use":"Do not preserve behavior that violates an explicit security, safety, correctness, or documented contract merely because an existing consumer happens to depend on it.","version_constraints":"Compatibility decisions depend on API version, language/runtime behavior, framework contracts, and documented support policy.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D37-B076","D37-B090"],"derived_from":["D37-B076","D37-B090"],"provenance":{"source_type":"API_CONTRACT_AND_MIGRATION_PRINCIPLE","source_reference":"Contract preservation, compatibility analysis, deprecation, and breaking-change migration","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}

{"record_id":"D37-B100","dataset_id":"D37","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Root Cause → Fix","subtopic":"End-to-end root-cause repair closure","concept":"Evidence-backed repair lifecycle","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What complete process should be used to convert an identified root cause into a trustworthy production fix?","answer":"Trace the failure to its root cause, define the required invariant and intended postcondition, select a repair with explicit trade-offs, implement it within the applicable version and dependency constraints, test normal and failure paths, verify security and performance implications where relevant, and record residual uncertainty and provenance.","explanation":"A world-class repair is a traceable evidence chain rather than a code edit. The complete chain should distinguish diagnosis from solution, solution from execution evidence, and passing tests from broader correctness claims.","cause":"Defect repair was treated as an isolated code modification rather than a lifecycle connecting diagnosis, implementation, testing, verification, deployment constraints, and learning.","solution":"Use a structured repair lifecycle: failure evidence → root cause → invariant → candidate fixes → selected implementation → regression/edge/security/performance tests → verification evidence → residual risk → versioned knowledge update.","alternatives":["For safety-critical properties, add formal verification where the property is amenable to proof.","For concurrency defects, combine deterministic reproducers with stress/race/deadlock analysis.","For distributed failures, combine replay with durable state inspection and reconciliation testing."],"tradeoffs":"Complete repair closure requires more evidence collection and engineering effort but produces reusable knowledge for future diagnosis and reduces recurrence risk.","regression_impact":"The repair process may identify adjacent defects, contract incompatibilities, performance regressions, or previously undocumented assumptions that require additional changes.","verification_method":"Require traceability to the originating D36 failure, valid candidate-fix reasoning, implementation evidence, targeted and regression tests, edge/failure coverage, security/performance assessment where applicable, exact execution status, provenance, version applicability, deduplication, and explicit residual uncertainty before closure.","when_not_to_use":"Do not classify a repair as verified merely because the code compiles, a sample succeeds, a test passes, or the original symptom disappears.","version_constraints":"All claims must be evaluated against the actual language, runtime, framework, library, operating system, and dependency versions for which the repair is intended.","dependencies":[],"related_records":["D36-B001","D36-B009","D36-B020","D36-B050","D37-B080","D37-B090"],"derived_from":["D36-B001","D36-B009","D36-B020","D36-B050","D37-B080","D37-B090"],"provenance":{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_ENGINEERING_AND_VERIFICATION_SYNTHESIS","source_reference":"Root-cause analysis, repair engineering, testing, verification, reliability, security, performance, and knowledge lifecycle principles","evidence_level":"HIGH"},"confidence":"HIGH","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE"}


{"record_id":"D38-B001","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Failure Classification","concept":"System-under-test failure versus test-execution failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should an engineer determine whether a failing test indicates a defect in the system under test or a failure in the test itself?","answer":"First establish whether the test executed the intended setup, input, assertion, and dependencies correctly. A failure is attributable to the system under test only when the observed behavior violates a valid expected behavior and the test infrastructure is not the cause. If setup, fixture construction, test data, mocks, assertions, environment, runner, or dependencies invalidate the test, the failure should be classified as a test-execution or test-validity failure instead.","explanation":"A failing test is evidence of a discrepancy, not automatically evidence of a production defect. Diagnosis must separate the behavior being tested from the machinery used to test it.","failure_classes":["SYSTEM_UNDER_TEST_FAILURE","TEST_EXECUTION_FAILURE","TEST_VALIDITY_FAILURE","ENVIRONMENT_FAILURE","DEPENDENCY_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["test input","preconditions","expected behavior","observed behavior","stack trace or failure output","fixture/setup result","dependency state","runtime/environment information"],"candidate_causes":["defect in system under test","incorrect test expectation","broken fixture","invalid test data","mock or stub mismatch","test runner failure","environment drift","dependency incompatibility"],"diagnosis_rule":"Do not modify production code until evidence establishes that the failing behavior originates in the system under test.","repair_strategy":"Repair the failing layer identified by evidence; if the test is invalid, correct the test and preserve evidence showing why the original failure was not a product defect.","verification_method":"Reproduce the original failure, isolate the failing layer, correct the identified cause, rerun the focused test, then run the relevant regression suite.","security_implications":"Misclassifying a security-test failure can either leave a vulnerability unresolved or cause unnecessary security-sensitive code changes; preserve security-test evidence and verify the actual boundary behavior.","performance_implications":"Do not optimize production behavior to compensate for a test harness timing or resource problem without measuring the system separately.","version_constraints":"Diagnosis should record runtime, framework, test-runner, dependency, and relevant platform versions because the failure may be version-specific.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Failure attribution requires distinguishing the system under test from test infrastructure and oracle validity."}],"related_records":["D37-B100"],"relationships":[{"type":"FOUNDATION_FOR","record_id":"D38-B002"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B005"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B002","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Reconstructing the exact failed test state","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"What evidence should be reconstructed before diagnosing a nontrivial test failure?","answer":"Reconstruct the exact test invocation, inputs, preconditions, expected result, observed result, state transitions, exceptions, fixture state, mocks, environment, configuration, dependency versions, timing, and external resources involved in the failure. Diagnosis should use this reconstructed evidence to eliminate competing explanations rather than relying on the final assertion message alone.","explanation":"A final assertion or stack trace often identifies only where the discrepancy became visible. The causal condition may have occurred earlier during setup, state mutation, dependency interaction, or asynchronous execution.","evidence_required":["exact test identifier","test command and relevant options","input values","pre-test state","fixture state","expected result","actual result","exception and stack","logs/traces","configuration","dependency versions","runtime/platform","external resource state"],"candidate_causes":["incorrect initial state","unexpected state transition","invalid input assumption","fixture contamination","dependency behavior","configuration drift","timing issue","external resource failure"],"diagnosis_rule":"Trace backward from the observed failure to the earliest evidence-supported divergence between expected and actual state.","repair_strategy":"Correct the earliest causal divergence rather than only changing the final assertion or symptom-producing code.","verification_method":"Capture the same evidence before and after the repair and confirm that the original causal divergence is eliminated without invalidating the test oracle.","security_implications":"Evidence collection must avoid exposing secrets, credentials, tokens, or sensitive test data in logs or artifacts.","performance_implications":"Diagnostic instrumentation should be targeted enough to preserve useful evidence without materially changing timing-sensitive behavior.","version_constraints":"Record all versions that can influence execution so evidence can be compared across environments.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TEST_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"Reliable diagnosis depends on reconstructing observable execution state and separating evidence from assumptions."}],"related_records":["D37-B094"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B001"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B010"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B003","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Intermittent Failure","concept":"Diagnosing nondeterministic test failures","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should intermittent test failures be diagnosed instead of being dismissed as flaky?","answer":"Determine which execution dimension changes between passing and failing runs, such as scheduling, concurrency, execution order, randomness, time, resource availability, external dependency behavior, or leaked state. Reproduce under controlled variation, capture the differing conditions, and identify the smallest condition set that predicts failure. A test should not be labeled merely flaky when a reproducible nondeterministic cause can be isolated.","explanation":"Intermittency is an observation about repeatability, not a root cause. The diagnostic task is to identify uncontrolled state or scheduling that changes the outcome.","failure_classes":["RACE_CONDITION","ORDER_DEPENDENCY","TIME_DEPENDENCY","RESOURCE_CONTENTION","RANDOMNESS","EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCY","STATE_LEAKAGE"],"evidence_required":["pass/fail execution history","random seeds where applicable","test ordering","concurrency configuration","timing information","resource measurements","external dependency responses","persistent or shared state"],"candidate_causes":["race condition","shared mutable state","uncontrolled randomness","time dependency","resource contention","network variability","test-order contamination","unfinished asynchronous work"],"diagnosis_rule":"Vary one relevant nondeterministic factor at a time where practical and correlate the factor with failure occurrence.","repair_strategy":"Remove or explicitly control the nondeterministic dependency, synchronize required operations, isolate mutable state, or inject deterministic test controls.","verification_method":"Run repeated controlled executions under both the previously failing and previously passing conditions and confirm stable behavior across the relevant regression set.","security_implications":"Do not weaken security assertions or isolation merely to make an intermittent security test pass.","performance_implications":"Concurrency and resource-sensitive tests should distinguish genuine performance/resource defects from harness-induced contention.","version_constraints":"Record scheduler/runtime/test-runner/dependency versions when concurrency or timing behavior may differ by version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Intermittent outcomes require controlled analysis of nondeterministic execution factors."}],"related_records":["D36-B046","D36-B047","D36-B048"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXPANDS","record_id":"D38-B004"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B009"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B004","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Order Dependence","concept":"Detecting leaked state between tests","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How can an order-dependent test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Run the failing test alone, after the suspected predecessor, and in reversed or randomized order where supported. Compare shared mutable state, database state, environment variables, caches, mocks, temporary files, global registries, open resources, and unfinished asynchronous work. If the failure appears only after another test changes persistent or process-level state, investigate lifecycle isolation and cleanup before changing the system under test.","explanation":"Order dependence commonly indicates that a test leaves state behind or relies on state created by another test. The diagnosis should identify the leaked state and the lifecycle boundary that failed to contain it.","failure_classes":["TEST_STATE_LEAK","ORDER_DEPENDENCY","INCOMPLETE_TEARDOWN","SHARED_RESOURCE_CONTAMINATION","ASYNC_LIFECYCLE_LEAK"],"evidence_required":["isolated test result","ordered-suite result","reversed-order result","shared-state inspection","setup/teardown behavior","resource ownership","database state","temporary artifacts"],"candidate_causes":["mutable global state","incomplete teardown","database residue","environment variable leakage","cache contamination","mock leakage","temporary file residue","unfinished tasks"],"diagnosis_rule":"Treat a failure that changes with test order as evidence requiring lifecycle-isolation investigation until the dependency is explained.","repair_strategy":"Restore test isolation by controlling setup state, deterministic teardown, resource ownership, transaction cleanup, mock lifecycle, and asynchronous task completion.","verification_method":"Run the affected test independently and in multiple relevant suite positions, including repeated suite execution, and confirm that outcome no longer depends on predecessor tests.","security_implications":"Leaked authentication state, credentials, permissions, or security configuration can produce false security results; isolate security-sensitive test state.","performance_implications":"Unreleased resources may cause later tests to fail through exhaustion, so resource lifecycle should be inspected before treating later failures as independent.","version_constraints":"Test-runner ordering and parallelism behavior can vary by version and configuration; preserve those details.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TEST_ISOLATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Tests should not depend unintentionally on state produced by other tests."}],"related_records":["D36-B019"],"relationships":[{"type":"DIAGNOSES","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"PRECEDES","record_id":"D38-B008"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B005","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Test Oracle","concept":"Invalid expected result","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure caused by an incorrect expected result be diagnosed?","answer":"Validate the expected result independently against the authoritative requirement, API contract, specification, invariant, mathematical property, or explicitly documented behavior. If the implementation satisfies the authoritative behavior but the test expects something else, the failure is an invalid test oracle rather than evidence of an implementation defect. The test should be corrected only after the authoritative behavior is established.","explanation":"Assertions are evidence only when their expected values are themselves valid. An incorrect oracle can systematically report correct implementations as failures.","failure_classes":["INVALID_ORACLE","OUTDATED_EXPECTATION","MISREAD_REQUIREMENT","CONTRACT_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["authoritative requirement","API or protocol contract","documented behavior","invariant or property","actual implementation behavior","test expectation","version context"],"candidate_causes":["outdated requirement interpretation","incorrect expected value","changed API contract","version-specific behavior","misunderstood invariant","test copied from obsolete behavior"],"diagnosis_rule":"Never infer implementation incorrectness solely from an assertion mismatch; first establish that the expected value is authoritative for the tested version and context.","repair_strategy":"Update the test oracle when authoritative evidence proves it incorrect; update the implementation only when authoritative behavior requires a different result.","verification_method":"Verify the corrected oracle against the authoritative contract and execute the test against the intended software version and configuration.","security_implications":"An incorrect security oracle can either hide a vulnerability or falsely report one; security expectations require explicit authoritative criteria.","performance_implications":"Performance tests require validated thresholds and measurement methodology; arbitrary thresholds should not be treated as correctness or performance evidence.","version_constraints":"The expected result must be tied to the software/API version when behavior legitimately changes between versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"A test oracle must define expected behavior from a valid specification, property, invariant, or authoritative reference."}],"related_records":["D37-B099"],"relationships":[{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B001"},{"type":"VALIDATES","record_id":"D38-B002"}]}
