portability requirement in the test.","verification_method":"Reproduce using the identified environmental difference, apply the targeted correction, and rerun on both the failing and known-good environments.","security_implications":"Do not weaken permissions or isolation merely to reproduce a test; diagnose permission-dependent behavior using controlled least-privilege environments.","performance_implications":"Resource limits and hardware differences can alter timing and capacity; distinguish functional failure from resource-sensitive behavior.","version_constraints":"Environment manifests must include relevant language/runtime/framework/library versions and platform information.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TEST_ENVIRONMENT_PRINCIPLE","source":"Reproducible diagnosis requires controlling environmental variables that can affect test behavior."}],"related_records":["D37-B075","D37-B097"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B007"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B002"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B007","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Dependency Behavior","concept":"Dependency-induced test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure caused by a dependency change be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the dependency graph and runtime behavior between a known-good version and the failing version. Inspect direct and transitive dependency changes, API or contract changes, default configuration changes, serialization behavior, error behavior, and supported runtime versions. Reproduce the failure with controlled dependency versions before attributing it to the system under test.","explanation":"A dependency can change observable behavior without any application source change. Diagnosis must establish whether the application violated a stable dependency contract or whether the dependency changed a behavior on which the test relies.","failure_classes":["API_INCOMPATIBILITY","TRANSITIVE_DEPENDENCY_CHANGE","BEHAVIOR_CHANGE","ABI_OR_RUNTIME_INCOMPATIBILITY","DEPENDENCY_CONFIGURATION_CHANGE"],"evidence_required":["dependency lockfile or manifest","known-good dependency versions","failing dependency versions","dependency graph","API/contract documentation","stack trace","runtime version","reproduction results"],"candidate_causes":["breaking API change","transitive upgrade","changed default","removed behavior","runtime incompatibility","dependency bug","incorrect application assumption"],"diagnosis_rule":"Reproduce with controlled dependency versions and compare the first behavior change before modifying application code.","repair_strategy":"Pin or constrain incompatible versions when appropriate, migrate to the supported contract when required, or isolate the dependency behind a stable application boundary.","verification_method":"Test against the intended dependency range and lock state, including regression coverage for the behavior that originally failed.","security_implications":"Dependency downgrades or version pinning can preserve vulnerable versions; security constraints must be evaluated alongside compatibility.","performance_implications":"Dependency upgrades may alter latency, memory, connection pooling, or algorithmic behavior; do not attribute such changes solely to application code without measurement.","version_constraints":"Record exact dependency versions and supported ranges; dependency behavior is inherently version-sensitive.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DEPENDENCY_MANAGEMENT_PRINCIPLE","source":"Dependency graphs and version constraints are part of the executable behavior of software systems."}],"related_records":["D37-B096"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXPANDS","record_id":"D38-B006"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B096"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B008","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Diagnosing resource exhaustion during repeated tests","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should repeated tests that eventually fail with resource exhaustion be diagnosed?","answer":"Measure resource consumption across repeated executions and identify whether memory, file descriptors, sockets, database connections, threads, processes, tasks, temporary files, or caches grow without returning to an expected baseline. Correlate resource growth with test lifecycle boundaries and ownership to determine whether the leak is in the system under test, test harness, fixture, or external service.","explanation":"A late-suite failure may be caused by accumulated resource consumption rather than the test that finally reports the error. Diagnosis requires observing resource lifecycle over time.","failure_classes":["MEMORY_EXHAUSTION","FILE_DESCRIPTOR_EXHAUSTION","CONNECTION_POOL_EXHAUSTION","THREAD_EXHAUSTION","PROCESS_EXHAUSTION","TEMPORARY_STORAGE_EXHAUSTION"],"evidence_required":["resource measurements over repetitions","resource limits","before/after lifecycle measurements","fixture teardown behavior","open-resource ownership","failure threshold","process/task counts","connection counts"],"candidate_causes":["resource leak","missing cleanup","unbounded cache","connection leak","unfinished task","fixture accumulation","external-service quota exhaustion"],"diagnosis_rule":"Measure resource state before and after repeated test lifecycles and identify the owner of resources that fail to return to the expected state.","repair_strategy":"Repair lifecycle ownership and cleanup, bound caches or pools where appropriate, terminate abandoned work, or isolate tests from finite external resources.","verification_method":"Repeat the workload over sufficient cycles to reproduce the original exhaustion and verify that resource usage stabilizes within defined limits.","security_implications":"Resource exhaustion can become a denial-of-service condition; security tests should distinguish application limits from test-environment limits.","performance_implications":"Resource usage should be measured rather than inferred; peak usage, steady-state usage, and growth rate are distinct properties.","version_constraints":"Runtime, operating system, database driver, test runner, and dependency versions can change resource behavior and limits.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Finite resources require explicit lifecycle ownership and release."}],"related_records":["D36-B019","D36-B038","D37-B073"],"relationships":[{"type":"DIAGNOSES","record_id":"D38-B004"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B010"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B009","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Time Dependence","concept":"Diagnosing clock, timezone, and timing-sensitive failures","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure involving dates, time, deadlines, or elapsed durations be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the failure depends on wall-clock time, timezone, calendar rules, timestamp precision, clock adjustments, or elapsed duration. Record the effective clock and timezone, distinguish absolute timestamps from durations, and reproduce with controlled time where the test permits injection. For elapsed-time behavior, reason from a monotonic clock rather than assuming wall-clock timestamps are suitable for duration measurement.","explanation":"Time-sensitive failures can arise from incorrect assumptions about timezone, daylight-saving transitions, precision, clock adjustments, or the distinction between calendar time and elapsed time.","failure_classes":["CLOCK_DEPENDENCY","TIMEZONE_MISMATCH","DATE_BOUNDARY","DST_BEHAVIOR","TIMESTAMP_PRECISION","ELAPSED_TIME_ERROR"],"evidence_required":["effective timestamp","timezone","locale","clock source","timestamp precision","expected boundary","actual boundary","elapsed duration","runtime/platform information"],"candidate_causes":["implicit local timezone","DST transition","precision truncation","clock adjustment","incorrect date arithmetic","wall-clock use for duration","race around time boundary"],"diagnosis_rule":"Make the temporal assumptions explicit and determine whether the failure is caused by calendar semantics, timestamp representation, or elapsed-time measurement.","repair_strategy":"Inject a controlled clock for deterministic tests, use explicit timezone rules, preserve required precision, and use monotonic time for elapsed-duration logic.","verification_method":"Test ordinary dates plus relevant boundary cases such as timezone transitions, date changes, precision boundaries, and controlled elapsed-time scenarios.","security_implications":"Time-based authorization, token expiration, replay protection, and certificate validation require careful temporal semantics; test clocks must not bypass the security behavior being validated.","performance_implications":"Timing tests should distinguish functional timeout semantics from machine-load variability and should avoid arbitrary thresholds unsupported by measurement.","version_constraints":"Timezone databases, runtime date/time APIs, and platform behavior can change; record relevant versions when results depend on them.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DATE_TIME_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Deterministic temporal testing requires explicit control of time sources and temporal assumptions."}],"related_records":["D36-B049"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B010"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B010","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Minimal Reproduction","concept":"Failure-preserving reduction","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a complex failing test be reduced to a minimal reproducible failure?","answer":"Reduce the failing case while repeatedly verifying that the failure remains: remove irrelevant input data, optional dependencies, setup steps, state, concurrency, configuration, and execution paths one factor at a time or through controlled reduction. Preserve the smallest condition set that still produces the same failure signature. Then validate the eventual repair against both the minimal reproducer and the original full scenario.","explanation":"A minimal reproducer narrows the causal search space and makes competing explanations easier to eliminate. Reduction must preserve the failure rather than merely producing a smaller but different problem.","failure_classes":["COMPLEX_FAILURE","MULTI_FACTOR_FAILURE","NON_MINIMAL_REPRODUCTION"],"evidence_required":["original failure","reduction steps","failure signature at each reduction stage","remaining preconditions","minimal input/state","environment and dependency constraints"],"candidate_causes":["irrelevant setup complexity","multiple interacting conditions","hidden state dependency","concurrency interaction","external dependency interaction","configuration interaction"],"diagnosis_rule":"Every reduction step must be checked against the original failure criterion; if the failure disappears, the removed condition may be causally relevant and must be restored or investigated.","repair_strategy":"Use the minimal reproducer to identify the causal mechanism, then verify the correction against the minimal case and the original scenario.","verification_method":"Demonstrate reproducibility of the minimal failure, verify the targeted correction, then rerun the original test and relevant regression suite.","security_implications":"Reduction must not remove the security boundary, attacker-controlled condition, or authorization state that makes the security failure meaningful.","performance_implications":"Reduction can remove the load or concurrency condition responsible for a performance failure; preserve the relevant workload characteristics when diagnosing performance-specific issues.","version_constraints":"Record the versions and environment required for the minimal reproducer because reduction does not eliminate version dependencies.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DEBUGGING_AND_TEST_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"Reducing a failure while preserving its observable signature narrows the causal search space."}],"related_records":["D38-B002","D38-B003","D38-B006"],"relationships":[{"type":"BUILDS_ON","record_id":"D38-B002"},{"type":"BUILDS_ON","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B100"}]}

{"record_id":"D38-B011","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B011","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Fixture Validity","concept":"Invalid or stale fixture data","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a failure caused by invalid fixture data be diagnosed?","answer":"Validate that the fixture represents the contract, schema, invariants, and lifecycle state required by the test. Compare the fixture with authoritative test data requirements and determine whether the failure disappears when the fixture is corrected. Do not attribute fixture-induced failures to the system under test.","explanation":"Fixtures are part of the test's executable preconditions. A stale, malformed, incomplete, or semantically invalid fixture can create failures that resemble product defects.","failure_classes":["INVALID_FIXTURE","STALE_FIXTURE","MALFORMED_FIXTURE","INCOMPLETE_FIXTURE","FIXTURE_SCHEMA_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["fixture definition","fixture version or generation source","required schema","required invariants","actual fixture state","failure output","known-valid fixture comparison"],"candidate_causes":["schema drift","stale fixture","missing required field","invalid relationship","incorrect lifecycle state","fixture transformation defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Validate fixture correctness independently before changing the implementation under test.","repair_strategy":"Correct or regenerate the fixture from the authoritative contract and add fixture validation where repeated invalid data is possible.","verification_method":"Validate the fixture independently, rerun the focused test, then execute relevant tests using representative and boundary fixture states.","security_implications":"Fixtures containing authorization, identity, or security state must preserve the intended security semantics; sanitization must not accidentally remove the condition under test.","performance_implications":"Oversized fixtures can create artificial resource failures; distinguish fixture-scale effects from product scalability defects.","version_constraints":"Fixture schemas and generators must be aligned with the software version being tested.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SOFTWARE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Test fixtures are executable test inputs and must satisfy the preconditions and contracts assumed by the test."}],"related_records":["D38-B002","D38-B005"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B002"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B001"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B012","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B011","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Fixture Lifecycle","concept":"Fixture scope and lifecycle mismatch","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure caused by an incorrectly scoped fixture be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the fixture lifetime with the state lifetime required by the test. Determine whether a shared fixture is retaining mutable state across tests or whether a fixture is being recreated too often and losing required state. Inspect setup, teardown, caching, dependency injection scope, transaction boundaries, and ownership of mutable resources.","explanation":"Fixture scope determines which tests share state and resources. Incorrect scope can produce order dependence, contamination, unexpected resets, or resource exhaustion.","failure_classes":["FIXTURE_SCOPE_ERROR","SHARED_STATE_LEAK","PREMATURE_FIXTURE_DISPOSAL","RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_ERROR"],"evidence_required":["fixture scope configuration","setup/teardown events","state before and after test","resource ownership","test ordering","isolated versus suite execution"],"candidate_causes":["session-wide mutable fixture","missing teardown","incorrect dependency-injection scope","premature cleanup","shared connection or cache","stateful fixture reuse"],"diagnosis_rule":"Identify the intended state lifetime first, then compare it with the actual fixture lifetime and ownership model.","repair_strategy":"Align fixture scope with required isolation and resource lifetime; explicitly reset mutable state when sharing is intentional.","verification_method":"Run tests individually and as a suite, inspect state transitions, and verify that fixture reuse neither leaks state nor destroys required state.","security_implications":"Shared security fixtures can leak identities, permissions, credentials, or authorization state between tests.","performance_implications":"Overly narrow fixture scope can cause expensive setup repeatedly; optimize only after correctness and isolation requirements are established.","version_constraints":"Fixture lifecycle semantics may depend on the test framework and runner version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_FIXTURE_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Fixture scope should match intended state isolation and resource lifetime."}],"related_records":["D38-B004","D38-B008"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B004"},{"type":"PRECEDES","record_id":"D38-B013"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B013","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B011","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Mocking","concept":"Mock behavior inconsistent with real dependency contract","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a failure caused by an unrealistic mock be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the mock's interface, return values, exceptions, ordering, side effects, timing assumptions, and state transitions with the real dependency contract. If the mock permits behavior that the real dependency cannot provide, or rejects behavior that the contract permits, the test may be testing the mock rather than the system's real interaction.","explanation":"Mocks can become alternate implementations of dependencies. Contract drift between a mock and its real dependency can create false failures and false confidence.","failure_classes":["MOCK_CONTRACT_DRIFT","UNREALISTIC_MOCK","MISSING_SIDE_EFFECT","INCORRECT_EXCEPTION_MODEL","INTERACTION_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["dependency contract","mock configuration","real dependency behavior where safely observable","interaction trace","return and exception behavior","version information"],"candidate_causes":["stale mock","incomplete mock behavior","wrong exception","incorrect call ordering","missing side effect","incorrect serialization"],"diagnosis_rule":"Treat mock behavior as suspect when the failing expectation depends on behavior not guaranteed by the dependency contract.","repair_strategy":"Align the mock with the authoritative contract, replace brittle mocking with a contract/integration test where appropriate, and avoid encoding undocumented behavior as a mock expectation.","verification_method":"Validate the mock against the contract and execute both focused unit tests and an appropriate real-boundary or contract test.","security_implications":"Mocks must not disable authentication, authorization, validation, or security-boundary behavior that the test is intended to exercise.","performance_implications":"Replacing all mocks with live dependencies may introduce unnecessary test cost; use the narrowest test level that validates the relevant contract.","version_constraints":"Mock behavior must correspond to the dependency version under test.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_DOUBLE_CONTRACT_PRINCIPLE","source":"Test doubles are useful only when their behavior represents the contract relevant to the test."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D37-B099"],"relationships":[{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B014"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B020"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B014","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B011","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Stub and Fake Behavior","concept":"Stub or fake implementation divergence","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a failure involving a stub or fake dependency implementation be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the stub or fake implements the dependency behavior needed by the test and whether that behavior remains consistent with the supported contract. Compare state transitions, validation, error handling, serialization, persistence semantics, and boundary conditions where they affect the tested behavior.","explanation":"Unlike a simple mock, a stub or fake may contain substantial behavior. Incorrect behavior in that substitute can become the actual source of the test failure.","failure_classes":["FAKE_IMPLEMENTATION_ERROR","STUB_BEHAVIOR_DRIFT","SIMULATION_MISMATCH","STATE_MODEL_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["fake/stub implementation","dependency contract","test scenario","expected interaction","observed substitute behavior","real dependency comparison when appropriate"],"candidate_causes":["incomplete fake","incorrect state model","missing validation","different error semantics","different persistence behavior","obsolete implementation"],"diagnosis_rule":"Inspect the substitute itself when the failure depends on behavior implemented by the stub or fake rather than merely an interaction expectation.","repair_strategy":"Correct the substitute only to the extent required by the supported contract; prefer contract tests to continuously detect divergence.","verification_method":"Run the failing test against the corrected substitute and validate the relevant behavior against the real dependency or authoritative contract.","security_implications":"A fake that omits security checks can create false confidence; security properties must be tested at an authentic boundary where necessary.","performance_implications":"A fake may have radically different performance characteristics; do not use fake timing as evidence about the real dependency.","version_constraints":"Substitute behavior must track the dependency contract applicable to the tested version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_DOUBLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Behavioral substitutes can introduce failures or false confidence when they diverge from the dependency contract."}],"related_records":["D38-B013"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B013"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B015"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B015","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B011","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Test Double Selection","concept":"Failure caused by inappropriate test-double boundary","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should an engineer diagnose a failure caused by testing through the wrong substitute boundary?","answer":"Identify the behavior that actually needs verification and determine whether the selected mock, stub, fake, simulator, or real dependency can provide the required evidence. If the failure depends on an interaction or behavior that the substitute intentionally does not model, the test may be placed at the wrong boundary rather than exposing a product defect.","explanation":"A test double is a deliberate abstraction. Failures can result when a test expects guarantees that only an integration or system boundary can establish.","failure_classes":["WRONG_TEST_BOUNDARY","OVER_MOCKING","UNDER_SPECIFIED_SUBSTITUTE","INTEGRATION_BEHAVIOR_MISSING"],"evidence_required":["test objective","boundary under test","substitute capabilities","dependency contract","expected evidence","failure trace"],"candidate_causes":["over-mocking","missing integration behavior","test asserting implementation detail","substitute unable to represent required state","incorrect test level"],"diagnosis_rule":"Choose the test boundary based on the behavior that must be evidenced, not on convenience alone.","repair_strategy":"Move the assertion to the appropriate test level or redesign the test double so that it represents the supported contract without encoding irrelevant implementation details.","verification_method":"Confirm that the revised test demonstrates the intended requirement and remains stable under legitimate implementation changes.","security_implications":"Security-boundary behavior often requires testing the actual boundary; excessive mocking can bypass the control being evaluated.","performance_implications":"Use unit-level substitutes for fast logic verification and targeted integration tests for expensive boundary behavior rather than making every test end-to-end.","version_constraints":"The appropriate boundary may change when dependency APIs or framework integration mechanisms change.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Test level should correspond to the behavior and boundary for which evidence is required."}],"related_records":["D38-B013","D38-B014"],"relationships":[{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D38-B013"},{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D38-B014"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B016","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B012","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Integration Boundary","concept":"Failure at an integration boundary","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure at an integration boundary be localized?","answer":"Partition the interaction into request construction, transport, authentication, serialization, remote processing, response transport, response decoding, and application-side interpretation. Capture evidence at each boundary and identify the first layer whose observed behavior differs from the contract.","explanation":"Integration failures often surface at one endpoint while the actual cause occurs in another layer. Boundary decomposition prevents premature attribution to either side.","failure_classes":["REQUEST_CONSTRUCTION_FAILURE","TRANSPORT_FAILURE","AUTHENTICATION_FAILURE","SERIALIZATION_FAILURE","REMOTE_PROCESSING_FAILURE","RESPONSE_DECODING_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["outbound request representation","transport result","authentication outcome","serialized payload","remote response","response decoding result","contract/version information"],"candidate_causes":["invalid request","network failure","credential mismatch","schema mismatch","remote service defect","encoding mismatch","client interpretation error"],"diagnosis_rule":"Locate the earliest contract violation or transport failure in the interaction chain.","repair_strategy":"Repair the layer responsible for the earliest evidence-supported divergence and preserve boundary diagnostics for regression testing.","verification_method":"Replay the isolated interaction, validate both sides of the contract, then rerun the full integration scenario.","security_implications":"Capture boundary evidence without logging credentials, tokens, secrets, or sensitive payloads unnecessarily.","performance_implications":"Measure latency by boundary to distinguish client, network, remote-service, and decoding costs.","version_constraints":"Record both client and service versions plus protocol/schema versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"INTEGRATION_TEST_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"Boundary decomposition localizes failures by identifying the earliest observable contract divergence."}],"related_records":["D38-B002","D37-B099"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B002"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B017"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B017","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B012","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Protocol Contract","concept":"Request/response contract mismatch","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a contract mismatch between test client and service be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the actual request and response structures with the authoritative contract for the exact protocol and version. Inspect required fields, types, encoding, status codes, headers, ordering requirements where applicable, error representations, and compatibility rules. Determine whether the producer, consumer, or test expectation violates the contract.","explanation":"A test can fail even when both components are individually functional if they disagree about the contract connecting them.","failure_classes":["SCHEMA_MISMATCH","API_CONTRACT_MISMATCH","PROTOCOL_VERSION_MISMATCH","ERROR_CONTRACT_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["contract specification","actual request","actual response","client version","service version","schema/version identifiers","test expectation"],"candidate_causes":["client using obsolete contract","service changed contract","incorrect test expectation","serialization difference","version negotiation failure"],"diagnosis_rule":"Use the authoritative contract to classify the first incompatible field, type, status, encoding, or protocol assumption.","repair_strategy":"Restore contract compatibility, update the consumer or producer according to the supported migration path, and update contract tests when the contract intentionally changes.","verification_method":"Validate both valid and invalid contract cases, including compatibility and error behavior relevant to the changed contract.","security_implications":"Contract validation must cover authorization and input-validation semantics; a syntactically valid request can still violate security requirements.","performance_implications":"Large payloads, compression, pagination, and serialization choices can alter performance while remaining contract-compatible; diagnose separately.","version_constraints":"Contract version must be explicitly recorded because compatibility is version-dependent.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CONTRACT_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Integration compatibility is determined against an authoritative producer-consumer contract."}],"related_records":["D38-B016","D38-B018"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B016"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D38-B018"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B018","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B012","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Contract Test Diagnosis","concept":"Producer versus consumer contract violation","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a contract test determine whether the producer or consumer is responsible for the failure?","answer":"Identify the contract version and evaluate the producer response and consumer expectation independently against it. If the producer violates a required contract element, the producer is implicated; if the producer conforms and the consumer rejects or misinterprets the valid response, the consumer is implicated. If both follow different valid contract versions, the compatibility configuration must be investigated.","explanation":"Contract tests are most useful when they establish which side violates a shared agreement rather than simply reporting that the interaction failed.","failure_classes":["PRODUCER_CONTRACT_VIOLATION","CONSUMER_CONTRACT_VIOLATION","VERSION_COMPATIBILITY_FAILURE","AMBIGUOUS_CONTRACT_VERSION"],"evidence_required":["contract artifact","producer output","consumer expectation","contract version","compatibility policy","test configuration"],"candidate_causes":["producer regression","consumer regression","version skew","incorrect contract artifact","unsupported compatibility combination"],"diagnosis_rule":"Evaluate each participant independently against the same authoritative contract before assigning responsibility.","repair_strategy":"Correct the violating participant or align supported versions; do not silently weaken the contract assertion to accommodate an unexplained mismatch.","verification_method":"Run producer-side and consumer-side contract validation independently and then execute the combined interaction.","security_implications":"Contract validation should include security-relevant fields and error semantics where they form part of the security boundary.","performance_implications":"Contract compliance does not prove acceptable performance; latency and payload-size behavior require separate tests.","version_constraints":"Contract and compatibility matrix versions are mandatory diagnostic evidence.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CONTRACT_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Shared contracts permit independent attribution of producer and consumer compatibility failures."}],"related_records":["D38-B017"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D38-B017"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B019","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B012","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Database Test State","concept":"Unexpected database state caused by transaction or isolation behavior","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a database-backed test failure caused by unexpected transaction state be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine the transaction boundary, isolation level, commit or rollback behavior, connection ownership, fixture setup, cleanup order, and visibility rules for the test. Compare the expected database state with the state visible to the test connection and determine whether uncommitted, leaked, concurrent, or rolled-back state explains the failure.","explanation":"Database tests can fail because the data exists in one transaction but is invisible to another, because cleanup did not occur, or because concurrent transactions observe different states.","failure_classes":["TRANSACTION_BOUNDARY_ERROR","ISOLATION_MISMATCH","UNCOMMITTED_STATE","ROLLBACK_MISMATCH","DATABASE_STATE_LEAK"],"evidence_required":["transaction lifecycle","connection identity","isolation level","SQL statements or operations","commit/rollback events","visible database state","fixture cleanup"],"candidate_causes":["missing commit","unexpected rollback","wrong isolation level","connection reuse","fixture leakage","concurrent transaction"],"diagnosis_rule":"Trace database state together with transaction and connection ownership instead of inspecting final rows alone.","repair_strategy":"Correct transaction boundaries, isolation assumptions, cleanup, or connection lifecycle according to the behavior the test is intended to verify.","verification_method":"Reproduce with explicit transaction tracing, verify expected visibility and cleanup, then execute isolation and regression scenarios.","security_implications":"Database tests must preserve authorization boundaries and avoid accidentally using elevated credentials to hide access-control failures.","performance_implications":"Connection pooling and transaction duration can influence test behavior and should be measured when failures occur only under load or repetition.","version_constraints":"Database engine, driver, ORM, and isolation semantics may vary by version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DATABASE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Database test diagnosis requires observing state together with transaction, connection, and isolation semantics."}],"related_records":["D36-B009","D38-B004","D38-B008"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B004"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B016"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B020","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B012","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Database Cleanup","concept":"Database contamination across tests","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should cross-test database contamination be distinguished from a product defect?","answer":"Compare database state before and after each test and verify that the test's cleanup mechanism restores the required baseline. Run the affected test against a clean database and after suspected predecessor tests. If results depend on residual rows, schema state, sequences, caches, or transactions from earlier tests, the test environment is contaminated.","explanation":"Database residue can cause both false positives and false negatives. Isolation must be demonstrated rather than assumed from the presence of teardown code.","failure_classes":["DATABASE_CONTAMINATION","INCOMPLETE_CLEANUP","SCHEMA_RESIDUE","SEQUENCE_STATE_LEAK","CACHE_DATABASE_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["clean baseline state","post-test state","cleanup operations","test ordering","database metadata","transaction state","cache state"],"candidate_causes":["missing cleanup","rollback failure","persistent test database","shared schema","sequence assumptions","database cache residue"],"diagnosis_rule":"Require reproducible evidence that the failure depends on prior database state before attributing it to application logic.","repair_strategy":"Use explicit isolation, deterministic cleanup, transaction rollback, isolated schemas/databases, or state-aware fixtures as appropriate to the test objective.","verification_method":"Run the test from a clean state, after repeated execution, and in relevant suite orders; verify stable results and clean postconditions.","security_implications":"Shared databases can leak test identities or authorization state; security tests should use controlled isolation.","performance_implications":"Full database recreation may be expensive; choose the smallest isolation mechanism that preserves correctness and evidence quality.","version_constraints":"Database engine and migration versions must match the tested schema state.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DATABASE_TEST_ISOLATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Independent database tests require controlled initial and final state."}],"related_records":["D38-B004","D38-B019"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B004"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B019"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B021","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B013","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Asynchronous Timeout","concept":"Timeout caused by unfinished asynchronous work","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should an asynchronous test timeout be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the awaited operation was started, whether completion is possible, whether cancellation propagates, whether a task is blocked on another task, and whether the test is awaiting the correct lifecycle event. Capture task states, dependency waits, cancellation state, and timeout boundaries before increasing the timeout.","explanation":"A timeout is an observable deadline violation, not a diagnosis. Increasing the timeout can conceal a deadlock, missing completion signal, blocked dependency, or leaked task.","failure_classes":["ASYNC_TIMEOUT","DEADLOCK","MISSING_COMPLETION_SIGNAL","CANCELLATION_FAILURE","UNFINISHED_TASK"],"evidence_required":["timeout duration","task state","await chain","cancellation state","dependency state","scheduler/runtime information","logs/traces"],"candidate_causes":["deadlock","missing await","blocked dependency","task never completes","cancellation not propagated","incorrect completion condition"],"diagnosis_rule":"Identify why completion cannot occur within the intended lifecycle before changing timeout thresholds.","repair_strategy":"Correct task ownership, completion signaling, cancellation propagation, synchronization, or dependency handling rather than merely extending the timeout.","verification_method":"Reproduce the timeout, observe task lifecycle, verify completion/cancellation behavior, then execute repeated and cancellation-focused regression tests.","security_implications":"Timeout handling around authentication, authorization, or security-sensitive operations must not leave partially completed security state.","performance_implications":"Distinguish legitimate slow execution from unbounded waiting by measuring operation latency and wait causes.","version_constraints":"Async runtime and framework versions can affect scheduling and cancellation semantics.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"ASYNCHRONOUS_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Timeout diagnosis requires examining task lifecycle, completion, waiting, and cancellation rather than only changing the deadline."}],"related_records":["D36-B049","D37-B091","D37-B092"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B008"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B022","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B013","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Test cancellation not reaching underlying operation","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure caused by ineffective cancellation be diagnosed?","answer":"Trace cancellation from the test timeout or cancellation request through every layer to the underlying operation. Determine whether the operation observes cancellation, whether nested tasks propagate it, whether blocking calls prevent it, and whether cleanup waits for cancellation to complete.","explanation":"A test may report cancellation while the underlying work continues. That can create leaked tasks, resource consumption, later failures, and misleading test outcomes.","failure_classes":["CANCELLATION_PROPAGATION_FAILURE","ORPHANED_TASK","BLOCKING_OPERATION","CLEANUP_AFTER_TIMEOUT_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["cancellation event","task lifecycle","underlying operation state","cleanup events","resource ownership","post-timeout activity"],"candidate_causes":["ignored cancellation","blocking I/O","detached task","swallowed cancellation","missing cancellation token propagation","cleanup race"],"diagnosis_rule":"A cancellation test is not successful merely because the caller stops waiting; underlying work must reach the intended terminal state.","repair_strategy":"Propagate cancellation explicitly, make blocking operations cancellable where supported, and ensure ownership includes cleanup of abandoned work.","verification_method":"Trigger cancellation under controlled conditions and verify that underlying work terminates, resources are released, and no post-cancellation side effects occur unexpectedly.","security_implications":"Cancelled security-sensitive operations must not leave partially applied authorization or credential state.","performance_implications":"Orphaned work can cause cumulative resource and throughput degradation; measure post-cancellation resource activity.","version_constraints":"Cancellation semantics depend on the runtime, concurrency library, and framework version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"ASYNC_CANCELLATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Cancellation requires propagation to owned asynchronous work and defined cleanup semantics."}],"related_records":["D38-B021","D37-B091"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D38-B021"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B023","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B013","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"External Service Failure","concept":"Unavailable or degraded external dependency","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure caused by an unavailable external service be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the test requires a live external service and whether that service was reachable, authenticated, healthy, and compatible at execution time. Separate transport failure, service rejection, service error, timeout, rate limiting, and contract mismatch from failures in the system under test.","explanation":"External dependencies introduce failure modes outside the test process. Diagnosis must establish whether the observed failure is an expected dependency failure, a product handling failure, or an invalid test environment.","failure_classes":["SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE","NETWORK_FAILURE","RATE_LIMIT","REMOTE_TIMEOUT","REMOTE_ERROR","AUTHENTICATION_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["endpoint","connectivity result","request metadata","response status","service health evidence","authentication result","timeout information","dependency version"],"candidate_causes":["service outage","network partition","rate limit","credential expiration","remote contract change","client defect","test environment restriction"],"diagnosis_rule":"Classify the external failure independently before deciding whether the system under test handled it incorrectly.","repair_strategy":"Restore dependency availability when environmental, or repair error handling when the product violates the required failure-handling contract.","verification_method":"Test both healthy and controlled failure responses, including timeout, unavailable, authentication, and rate-limit conditions where relevant.","security_implications":"Do not expose credentials or sensitive remote responses in diagnostic artifacts.","performance_implications":"Remote latency and rate limiting must be distinguished from local performance defects.","version_constraints":"Record service API version and client dependency version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"INTEGRATION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"External dependencies introduce independent availability and contract failure modes that must be classified separately."}],"related_records":["D38-B016","D38-B017"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B016"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B024"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B024","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B013","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"External Service Resilience","concept":"Incorrect product response to dependency failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should an external-service outage be distinguished from a failure in the product's resilience behavior?","answer":"First establish that the external failure was real and reproducible. Then compare the system's observed behavior with the documented resilience contract, such as timeout handling, bounded retry, fallback, circuit behavior, error propagation, or state preservation. A dependency outage is not itself a product defect; failure to handle the outage according to an applicable requirement may be.","explanation":"The dependency and the product have separate responsibilities. Diagnosis must preserve that distinction.","failure_classes":["DEPENDENCY_FAILURE","RESILIENCE_BEHAVIOR_FAILURE","RETRY_POLICY_FAILURE","FALLBACK_FAILURE","STATE_PRESERVATION_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["controlled dependency failure","product resilience requirement","retry/timeout trace","fallback behavior","resulting state","side effects"],"candidate_causes":["missing timeout","unbounded retry","incorrect fallback","partial state mutation","incorrect error mapping","dependency outage"],"diagnosis_rule":"Compare observed product behavior under a controlled dependency failure with the explicit resilience requirement rather than treating dependency failure alone as evidence of product failure.","repair_strategy":"Correct only the resilience behavior that violates the applicable contract and preserve the dependency-failure scenario as a regression test.","verification_method":"Inject controlled dependency failures and verify bounded retries, correct terminal behavior, state integrity, and recovery semantics.","security_implications":"Fallbacks must not bypass authentication, authorization, validation, or integrity controls.","performance_implications":"Retries and fallbacks can amplify load; measure retry volume, latency, and resource impact under dependency degradation.","version_constraints":"Dependency and resilience-policy versions should be recorded because behavior may change across releases.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"RESILIENCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Failure of a dependency and failure to handle that dependency failure are distinct diagnostic conditions."}],"related_records":["D38-B023","D37-B093"],"relationships":[{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B023"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B093"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B025","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B013","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Network Test Failure","concept":"Network-induced test flakiness","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a flaky network test be diagnosed?","answer":"Separate application behavior from transport variability by recording connection establishment, DNS, routing, TLS, request transmission, response timing, retries, packet or connection failures where observable, and service-side responses. Repeat under controlled network conditions and determine whether the test lacks appropriate isolation or deterministic failure injection.","explanation":"Network variability can create intermittent failures unrelated to the application's correctness. Conversely, excessive reliance on retries can hide real application defects.","failure_classes":["NETWORK_VARIABILITY","DNS_FAILURE","CONNECTION_FAILURE","TLS_FAILURE","TRANSPORT_TIMEOUT","REMOTE_LATENCY"],"evidence_required":["network error","DNS result","connection timing","TLS outcome","request/response timing","retry history","service response","environment network state"],"candidate_causes":["network instability","DNS issue","TLS mismatch","service latency","missing timeout","test dependency on live network","application protocol defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Establish the transport-level cause and determine whether the test is intended to validate network behavior or application behavior independent of network variability.","repair_strategy":"Use deterministic network simulation for application tests, retain targeted real-network tests for integration coverage, and correct genuine timeout or protocol handling defects.","verification_method":"Run deterministic injected failures plus representative live-network tests and compare failure classification.","security_implications":"TLS and certificate failures must not be bypassed merely to stabilize tests.","performance_implications":"Record latency distributions rather than single timings when diagnosing network-sensitive performance tests.","version_constraints":"TLS libraries, DNS behavior, OS networking, and protocol versions can affect results.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"NETWORK_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Network-dependent tests must distinguish transport variability from application-level behavior."}],"related_records":["D38-B023","D38-B003"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B023"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B003"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B026","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B014","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Property-Based Testing","concept":"Shrunken counterexample diagnosis","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a property-based test failure involving a generated counterexample be diagnosed?","answer":"Preserve the generated seed and minimized counterexample, confirm that the property itself is valid, then determine which invariant is violated and whether the minimized input retains the original causal condition. Inspect generator assumptions as well as the implementation because invalid generators or invalid properties can produce misleading failures.","explanation":"Property-based testing separates three possible failure sources: implementation behavior, property/oracle definition, and input generator validity.","failure_classes":["PROPERTY_VIOLATION","INVALID_PROPERTY","INVALID_GENERATOR","SHRINKING_ARTIFACT"],"evidence_required":["property definition","generation seed","original input","shrunk input","failure trace","generator constraints","invariant"],"candidate_causes":["real invariant violation","incorrect property","invalid generated input","over-aggressive shrinking","undefined input domain"],"diagnosis_rule":"Validate the property and generated input domain before treating the counterexample as proof of an implementation defect.","repair_strategy":"Correct the violated implementation invariant, property, or generator according to authoritative requirements.","verification_method":"Replay the original seed, replay the minimized counterexample, add a focused regression case, and rerun the property suite.","security_implications":"Security properties require explicit domain constraints; generators must cover attacker-relevant invalid and boundary inputs without assuming trusted data.","performance_implications":"Large generated cases can expose complexity defects; preserve size characteristics when diagnosing performance-related properties.","version_constraints":"Record property-testing library, runtime, seed, and relevant dependency versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"PROPERTY_BASED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Counterexamples must be interpreted in the context of the property definition and generated input domain."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B010"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B005"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B010"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B027","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B014","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Fuzz Testing","concept":"Fuzz failure triage","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a fuzzing failure be triaged before being classified as a product defect?","answer":"Preserve the exact input and execution conditions, determine whether the failure is a crash, assertion violation, timeout, resource exhaustion, sanitizer finding, or incorrect output, then minimize the input while preserving the same failure signature. Confirm that the harness and oracle are valid before attributing the result to the target.","explanation":"Fuzzing can expose failures in the target, harness, oracle, environment, or resource limits. Classification is necessary before remediation.","failure_classes":["CRASH","ASSERTION_FAILURE","MEMORY_SAFETY_FAILURE","TIMEOUT","RESOURCE_EXHAUSTION","OUTPUT_PROPERTY_FAILURE","HARNESS_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["exact fuzz input","seed","harness configuration","failure signature","sanitizer output if applicable","resource limits","target version","environment"],"candidate_causes":["target defect","invalid harness assumption","oracle defect","resource limit","undefined input domain","tooling failure"],"diagnosis_rule":"Preserve and replay the exact input before reducing or changing the harness.","repair_strategy":"Repair the target when the failure is target-originated; correct the harness or oracle when evidence establishes that the test machinery is responsible.","verification_method":"Replay exact input, minimize while preserving failure, add regression coverage, and rerun under the same and relevant supported environments.","security_implications":"Fuzz failures involving memory safety, parsing, authentication, authorization, or resource exhaustion require security-sensitive handling and complete evidence preservation.","performance_implications":"Timeout and resource findings require controlled resource thresholds and distinction between pathological input and infrastructure noise.","version_constraints":"Record fuzzing engine, target build, compiler/runtime, sanitizer, and dependency versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"FUZZ_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Fuzz failures require deterministic replay, classification, and preservation of the triggering input."}],"related_records":["D38-B026","D38-B010"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B026"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B010"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B028","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B014","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Mutation Testing","concept":"Mutation testing signal interpretation","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a surviving mutation be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the mutation should have been detected by the existing tests, whether the mutated behavior is reachable, whether the test oracle distinguishes the original and mutated behavior, and whether the mutation represents a meaningful fault. A surviving mutation is evidence of a potential test-suite blind spot, not automatically evidence that the implementation is defective.","explanation":"Mutation testing evaluates test sensitivity by introducing controlled changes. Diagnosis must distinguish unreachable or equivalent mutations from meaningful undetected behavior.","failure_classes":["SURVIVING_MUTATION","EQUIVALENT_MUTATION","UNREACHABLE_MUTATION","ORACLE_INSENSITIVITY"],"evidence_required":["mutation description","mutant reachability","test execution result","expected behavior","equivalence analysis","coverage/path evidence"],"candidate_causes":["missing assertion","uncovered path","weak oracle","equivalent mutation","unreachable code","test design gap"],"diagnosis_rule":"Classify the mutation before adding a test; only meaningful reachable mutations that should violate a requirement indicate a test-sensitivity gap.","repair_strategy":"Strengthen tests where the mutation exposes missing behavioral evidence; document or exclude demonstrably equivalent or unreachable mutations with justification.","verification_method":"Re-run the mutation and confirm that the intended test now kills the meaningful mutant without introducing brittle assertions.","security_implications":"Security-relevant surviving mutations can indicate missing tests around validation or authorization and deserve explicit classification.","performance_implications":"Mutation execution cost should not be confused with application performance; use mutation selectively to evaluate test sensitivity.","version_constraints":"Mutation tooling and language instrumentation may differ by compiler, runtime, and tool version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"MUTATION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Mutation testing measures whether tests detect meaningful changes in program behavior."}],"related_records":["D38-B005"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B005"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B029"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B029","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B014","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Coverage Interpretation","concept":"Coverage gap versus behavioral gap","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure or missing behavior be interpreted when code coverage is high?","answer":"Do not use high structural coverage as proof that behavior is adequately tested. Determine whether the relevant branches, states, error paths, contracts, security properties, and expected outcomes are actually distinguished by assertions. A coverage gap concerns execution; a behavioral gap concerns insufficient evidence about correctness.","explanation":"A line or branch can execute without the test detecting incorrect behavior. Diagnosis should focus on observable requirements and meaningful oracles rather than coverage percentage alone.","failure_classes":["BEHAVIORAL_COVERAGE_GAP","WEAK_ORACLE","UNASSERTED_PATH","STRUCTURAL_COVERAGE_FALSE_CONFIDENCE"],"evidence_required":["coverage data","requirements","assertions","reachable states","failure modes","test outcomes"],"candidate_causes":["weak assertions","missing negative tests","unverified state transition","uncovered error semantics","incorrect oracle"],"diagnosis_rule":"Evaluate whether tests can fail when the relevant requirement is violated, not merely whether the code executes.","repair_strategy":"Add tests that distinguish required correct and incorrect behaviors and use coverage only as supporting evidence for reachability.","verification_method":"Introduce representative fault or mutation conditions and confirm that the relevant tests detect them.","security_implications":"Security tests must establish security properties explicitly; execution of security code is not proof that controls work.","performance_implications":"Coverage does not measure performance behavior; load and resource tests require separate evidence.","version_constraints":"Coverage tooling and instrumentation can differ across languages and build configurations.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_COVERAGE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Structural coverage indicates execution, not by itself behavioral correctness or oracle strength."}],"related_records":["D38-B028","D38-B005"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B028"},{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D38-B005"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B030","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B014","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"False Test Signals","concept":"False positive test result","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test that passes while the behavior is actually incorrect be diagnosed?","answer":"Identify whether the test oracle is too weak, the assertion is disconnected from the requirement, the test does not exercise the intended path, failures are swallowed, or the test environment masks the defect. Reconstruct the requirement-to-observation chain and determine whether an incorrect implementation could still produce a passing result.","explanation":"A passing test proves only that its implemented checks passed under the observed conditions. It does not prove that the requirement was fully exercised or verified.","failure_classes":["FALSE_POSITIVE","WEAK_ASSERTION","UNREACHABLE_ASSERTION","SWALLOWED_FAILURE","ORACLE_GAP"],"evidence_required":["requirement","test objective","execution path","assertions","failure propagation","mock behavior","test output"],"candidate_causes":["weak assertion","wrong assertion target","exception swallowed","mock hiding behavior","unexecuted branch","incorrect expected condition"],"diagnosis_rule":"Construct a counterexample: determine whether a known-invalid behavior could still satisfy the test.","repair_strategy":"Strengthen the test oracle, execution path, failure propagation, or boundary setup so that relevant invalid behavior is detectable.","verification_method":"Use targeted fault injection, mutation, negative cases, or independently derived expected results to demonstrate that the test detects representative incorrect behavior.","security_implications":"False-positive security tests are especially dangerous because they can create unjustified assurance; security assertions should be explicit and boundary-aware.","performance_implications":"A passing performance test with an ineffective workload or measurement can create false confidence; validate workload and measurement validity.","version_constraints":"False positives can arise after framework or dependency changes alter what the test actually exercises.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_ORACLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"A passing test establishes only the behavior actually exercised and asserted by that test."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B029"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONTRASTS_WITH","record_id":"D38-B031"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B029"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B031","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B015","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"False Negative Signals","concept":"False negative test result","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test that fails even though the implementation satisfies the requirement be diagnosed?","answer":"Validate the expected result, test input, environment, dependency state, timing, fixture, and assertion semantics independently. A false negative occurs when the test reports failure despite the system satisfying the authoritative requirement under the intended conditions.","explanation":"False failures waste diagnostic effort and can cause engineers to introduce unnecessary regressions if the test itself is changed without evidence.","failure_classes":["FALSE_NEGATIVE","INVALID_ORACLE","ENVIRONMENT_ARTIFACT","FIXTURE_ERROR","TIMING_ARTIFACT","DEPENDENCY_ARTIFACT"],"evidence_required":["authoritative requirement","test input","actual implementation behavior","expected value","environment","fixture state","dependency versions","assertion behavior"],"candidate_causes":["wrong expected value","stale fixture","environment mismatch","dependency drift","timing sensitivity","assertion defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Independently establish the required behavior before changing implementation code in response to an isolated failing test.","repair_strategy":"Correct the test artifact when evidence proves it invalid; preserve the requirement and add regression evidence preventing recurrence.","verification_method":"Validate the corrected test against authoritative behavior and run it across intended environments and relevant regression scenarios.","security_implications":"Do not suppress failing security tests merely because the implementation appears correct; validate the security requirement independently.","performance_implications":"Performance assertions can be false negatives when thresholds ignore workload variance or measurement noise; validate methodology before optimization.","version_constraints":"Expected behavior must correspond to the tested software and dependency versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_ORACLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"A failing test can be invalid when its oracle, inputs, environment, or execution conditions do not represent the intended requirement."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B030"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONTRASTS_WITH","record_id":"D38-B030"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B005"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B032","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B015","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Assertion Granularity","concept":"Failure caused by overly broad assertion","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should an overly broad assertion that obscures the real test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the assertion compares an entire object, document, collection, or snapshot when only specific contractual properties matter. Decompose the expected behavior into meaningful invariants and identify which invariant actually differs. Broad comparison should not be treated as evidence that every differing field is defective.","explanation":"Large assertions can combine independent differences and produce noisy failures, making causal diagnosis harder and encouraging unnecessary fixes.","failure_classes":["OVER_BROAD_ASSERTION","NOISY_ASSERTION","UNRELATED_DIFFERENCE","ASSERTION_AMBIGUITY"],"evidence_required":["assertion definition","actual value","expected value","relevant requirement","field-level differences","test objective"],"candidate_causes":["incidental metadata","unordered fields","timestamps","environment-specific values","irrelevant representation differences","multiple independent defects"],"diagnosis_rule":"Map each asserted difference to an explicit requirement or invariant before deciding that it represents a defect.","repair_strategy":"Assert stable contractual properties explicitly and isolate independent invariants where that improves diagnostic precision.","verification_method":"Rerun the test and confirm that failures identify the relevant violated invariant while still detecting meaningful regressions.","security_implications":"Do not omit security-critical fields merely because broad assertions are noisy; preserve explicit checks for security properties.","performance_implications":"Large deep comparisons can add test cost; optimize only after ensuring that reduced assertions retain required behavioral coverage.","version_constraints":"Serialization and representation differences may legitimately vary across versions; assert version-stable contracts where appropriate.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_ASSERTION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Assertions should provide precise evidence for the behavior they claim to verify."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B030"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D38-B005"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B033","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B015","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Snapshot Testing","concept":"Snapshot or golden-file mismatch","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a snapshot or golden-file test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the changed snapshot content with the semantic contract represented by the snapshot. Classify differences as intended behavior changes, real regressions, nondeterministic output, environment-dependent representation, or obsolete snapshot data. Never update the snapshot solely because the test failed without establishing why the output changed.","explanation":"Snapshot tests detect output changes but do not inherently determine whether a change is correct. Diagnosis requires understanding the semantic significance of the difference.","failure_classes":["SNAPSHOT_MISMATCH","GOLDEN_FILE_DRIFT","NONDETERMINISTIC_OUTPUT","INTENDED_OUTPUT_CHANGE","REGRESSION"],"evidence_required":["old snapshot","new output","requirement","change history","normalization rules","environment/version information"],"candidate_causes":["legitimate behavior change","regression","timestamp/randomness","platform-specific formatting","dependency output change","stale golden file"],"diagnosis_rule":"Classify the semantic cause of the diff before accepting or rejecting the new snapshot.","repair_strategy":"Update the snapshot only when authoritative behavior intentionally changed; otherwise correct the implementation or normalize legitimate nondeterministic representation.","verification_method":"Review the semantic diff, rerun the focused test, and execute related regression tests against representative environments.","security_implications":"Snapshots may contain secrets, tokens, personal data, or sensitive configuration; sanitize test artifacts without removing security-relevant semantics.","performance_implications":"Large snapshots can obscure performance-relevant changes and increase test cost; keep snapshots focused on meaningful output.","version_constraints":"Dependency and formatter versions can legitimately change serialized or textual output.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SNAPSHOT_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Snapshot differences require semantic review before being accepted as new expected output."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B032"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B005"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B032"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B034","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B015","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Serialization Failure","concept":"Serialization/deserialization mismatch","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a serialization or deserialization test failure be localized?","answer":"Separate data construction, serialization, transport representation, schema validation, deserialization, type conversion, default handling, and semantic interpretation. Compare the actual encoded representation with the contract and identify the first stage where information is lost, rejected, transformed incorrectly, or interpreted differently.","explanation":"Serialization failures can appear as type errors or incorrect final values even though the actual defect is an earlier representation mismatch.","failure_classes":["SERIALIZATION_MISMATCH","DESERIALIZATION_FAILURE","SCHEMA_MISMATCH","TYPE_CONVERSION_ERROR","DATA_LOSS"],"evidence_required":["source object","encoded representation","schema","decoder behavior","decoded object","version information","expected semantic value"],"candidate_causes":["field omission","type mismatch","encoding mismatch","default-value difference","schema evolution","precision loss","unknown-field handling"],"diagnosis_rule":"Identify the earliest representation boundary where the actual value diverges from the contract.","repair_strategy":"Correct the violating encoder, decoder, schema, or compatibility rule according to the supported data contract.","verification_method":"Test round-trip behavior, backward/forward compatibility where required, malformed inputs, omitted fields, and boundary values.","security_implications":"Deserialization boundaries must be tested for unsafe object construction, injection, type confusion, and resource amplification where applicable.","performance_implications":"Large payloads and repeated serialization can expose allocation or CPU issues; separate correctness failures from performance observations.","version_constraints":"Serializer, schema, runtime, and protocol versions must be recorded because representation compatibility is version-dependent.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"DATA_SERIALIZATION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Serialization diagnosis requires identifying the first representation boundary that violates the data contract."}],"related_records":["D38-B017"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B017"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D38-B035"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B035","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B015","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Backward or forward compatibility failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure caused by schema evolution be diagnosed?","answer":"Identify the producer and consumer schema versions and determine the supported compatibility direction. Compare required, optional, removed, renamed, type-changed, and newly introduced fields against the compatibility contract. Reproduce the failing version pair independently before modifying either side.","explanation":"A schema change can be valid for one compatibility direction and invalid for another. Diagnosis must use the explicit compatibility policy rather than assuming all version changes are interchangeable.","failure_classes":["BACKWARD_COMPATIBILITY_FAILURE","FORWARD_COMPATIBILITY_FAILURE","SCHEMA_VERSION_SKEW","FIELD_EVOLUTION_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["old schema","new schema","producer version","consumer version","compatibility policy","encoded payload","failure output"],"candidate_causes":["removed required field","type change","renamed field","new required field","unknown-field rejection","default mismatch"],"diagnosis_rule":"Evaluate the exact producer-consumer version pair against the documented compatibility direction before assigning a defect.","repair_strategy":"Use a compatible schema evolution strategy, version negotiation, migration, or coordinated deployment according to the supported compatibility model.","verification_method":"Test all supported version pairs and representative old/new payloads, including malformed and boundary cases.","security_implications":"Compatibility mechanisms must not permit unsafe type coercion, validation bypass, or acceptance of data outside the security contract.","performance_implications":"Compatibility layers may add parsing or transformation cost; measure separately from correctness diagnosis.","version_constraints":"Exact schema and producer/consumer versions are mandatory diagnostic context.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SCHEMA_EVOLUTION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Compatibility is directional and depends on explicit producer-consumer version contracts."}],"related_records":["D38-B017","D38-B034"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B034"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B017"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B036","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B016","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Parallel Test Execution","concept":"Failure caused by unsafe test parallelism","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a failure that appears only during parallel test execution be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare serial and parallel execution and identify shared resources, mutable process state, ports, files, databases, caches, environment variables, external services, and task schedulers that are not isolated. Determine whether tests have an explicit concurrency-safe ownership model or merely happen to pass serially.","explanation":"Parallel execution exposes hidden coupling that serial execution can conceal. The diagnosis must identify the shared resource or ordering assumption responsible for the failure.","failure_classes":["PARALLEL_STATE_COLLISION","RESOURCE_CONTENTION","RACE_IN_TEST_HARNESS","SHARED_PORT_CONFLICT","DATABASE_COLLISION"],"evidence_required":["serial result","parallel result","worker configuration","resource identifiers","shared state","test ordering","timing trace"],"candidate_causes":["shared temp path","fixed port","shared database rows","global state","unsafe fixture","resource limit","race in harness"],"diagnosis_rule":"Find the resource or state whose ownership is shared without safe isolation before changing production code.","repair_strategy":"Isolate resources, assign unique ownership, synchronize intentional sharing, or mark tests non-parallel only when isolation is impractical and the restriction is justified.","verification_method":"Repeat under multiple worker counts and execution orders and verify deterministic results without resource collisions.","security_implications":"Parallel tests must not share credentials, identities, authorization state, or sensitive files without explicit isolation.","performance_implications":"Parallelism can increase throughput but also contention; measure resource saturation before interpreting timing failures.","version_constraints":"Test runner parallelism semantics and concurrency primitives can change across versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"PARALLEL_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Parallel tests require explicit isolation or synchronization for shared state and finite resources."}],"related_records":["D38-B004","D38-B008"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B004"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B037","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B016","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Race in Test Harness","concept":"Race condition inside test infrastructure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a failure caused by a race in the test harness be distinguished from a race in the product?","answer":"Inspect concurrent operations performed by fixtures, runners, hooks, reporters, mocks, temporary-resource managers, and the system under test. Reproduce with controlled scheduling or increased repetition and determine which shared state is accessed without required synchronization. The race location, not merely the failing assertion, determines the responsible layer.","explanation":"Test infrastructure is executable concurrent software and can contain races independently of the system being tested.","failure_classes":["HARNESS_RACE","PRODUCT_RACE","RESOURCE_RACE","UNSAFE_FIXTURE_CONCURRENCY"],"evidence_required":["concurrency trace","shared resource","worker identities","fixture operations","product operations","reproduction frequency"],"candidate_causes":["unsafe runner state","shared fixture","mock race","resource allocator race","product synchronization defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Trace ownership and synchronization at both the harness and product layers before attributing a race to the product.","repair_strategy":"Correct synchronization or isolation in the layer proven responsible and retain a regression scenario that reproduces the race conditions.","verification_method":"Repeat under controlled concurrency and scheduling variation, then execute both serial and parallel regression tests.","security_implications":"Race diagnosis must include security-sensitive shared state where concurrent authorization or identity handling is involved.","performance_implications":"Synchronization fixes can alter contention and latency; measure after correctness is established.","version_constraints":"Scheduler and runtime behavior may vary across versions, so preserve the execution environment.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CONCURRENT_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"The test harness and the system under test are both concurrent software and must be separately considered during race diagnosis."}],"related_records":["D38-B036","D36-B046"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D38-B036"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D36-B046"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B038","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B016","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Deadlock","concept":"Deadlock or cyclic wait in test execution","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a hanging test caused by deadlock be diagnosed?","answer":"Capture the wait graph or equivalent synchronization state and identify resources or tasks waiting cyclically. Distinguish deadlock from slow execution, external blocking, starvation, or an unfinished asynchronous operation. The diagnostic objective is to establish a cycle or permanent wait condition supported by execution evidence.","explanation":"A test timeout alone cannot prove deadlock. Deadlock diagnosis requires evidence about ownership, waiting relationships, and absence of progress.","failure_classes":["DEADLOCK","CYCLIC_WAIT","LOCK_ORDERING_FAILURE","RESOURCE_WAIT_CYCLE"],"evidence_required":["thread/task states","lock ownership","wait relationships","resource state","timeout timeline","scheduler state"],"candidate_causes":["inconsistent lock order","nested locks","mutual waiting","resource cycle","synchronization protocol failure"],"diagnosis_rule":"Construct the relevant wait-for relationship and determine whether a cycle prevents progress.","repair_strategy":"Break the cycle through consistent ordering, reduced lock scope, redesigned ownership, or nonblocking synchronization as appropriate.","verification_method":"Reproduce the hang, capture synchronization state, apply the correction, and run repeated concurrent tests under varied scheduling.","security_implications":"Security-sensitive locks must not be removed merely to avoid deadlock; preserve required atomicity and authorization invariants.","performance_implications":"Lock contention without deadlock can still cause severe latency; distinguish high contention from permanent cycles.","version_constraints":"Runtime scheduling and synchronization implementation details can vary by version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"Deadlock is established by evidence of a permanent cyclic wait rather than by timeout alone."}],"related_records":["D38-B021","D38-B037"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B021"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B039"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B039","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B016","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Starvation","concept":"Starvation or scheduling-induced test timeout","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should starvation be distinguished from deadlock in a hanging test?","answer":"Determine whether the waiting operation can theoretically progress and whether another task repeatedly receives the resource or scheduling opportunity. Unlike deadlock, starvation may have no wait cycle; progress is possible but indefinitely or excessively delayed because access is unfair or continuously preempted.","explanation":"Both starvation and deadlock can appear as timeouts, but their corrective strategies differ.","failure_classes":["STARVATION","UNFAIR_SCHEDULING","RESOURCE_MONOPOLIZATION","PRIORITY_INVERSION"],"evidence_required":["scheduler trace","resource ownership history","wait duration","task progress","priority information","resource acquisition frequency"],"candidate_causes":["unfair lock","resource monopolization","priority inversion","busy worker","scheduler configuration"],"diagnosis_rule":"Look for evidence of possible but indefinitely delayed progress rather than a cyclic wait before classifying the failure as starvation.","repair_strategy":"Correct fairness, resource ownership, scheduling priority, or work partitioning as supported by the concurrency contract.","verification_method":"Repeat under varied scheduling and worker configurations and measure progress and wait distributions.","security_implications":"Priority or fairness fixes must not bypass security-critical synchronization.","performance_implications":"Starvation can appear as tail-latency degradation even when aggregate throughput is acceptable; measure per-task progress.","version_constraints":"Scheduler behavior can vary by operating system, runtime, and test-runner version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CONCURRENCY_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"Starvation involves delayed progress without necessarily having a cyclic wait condition."}],"related_records":["D38-B038"],"relationships":[{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B038"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B040","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B016","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Deadlock and Cleanup","concept":"Deadlock during test teardown","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test that hangs during teardown be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine which cleanup operation is waiting, which resource or task it owns, and whether teardown is attempting to join, close, flush, or release work that depends on another cleanup action. Compare teardown order with ownership dependencies and identify whether cleanup itself creates a wait cycle.","explanation":"Teardown is part of test execution and can deadlock independently of the test body. A failure reported as a timeout may therefore originate after assertions have completed.","failure_classes":["TEARDOWN_DEADLOCK","CLEANUP_ORDER_ERROR","RESOURCE_RELEASE_CYCLE","ASYNC_TEARDOWN_HANG"],"evidence_required":["teardown timeline","resource ownership","task states","cleanup ordering","join/close operations","timeout location"],"candidate_causes":["cleanup dependency cycle","unfinished background task","lock held during cleanup","blocking close","incorrect fixture dependency order"],"diagnosis_rule":"Treat teardown as an executable lifecycle phase and diagnose its wait graph independently from the test body.","repair_strategy":"Align cleanup ordering with resource dependencies, ensure owned work reaches termination, and avoid blocking cleanup on resources whose release depends on the current cleanup action.","verification_method":"Run teardown-focused repetitions, forced-failure paths, cancellation paths, and normal success paths to confirm deterministic cleanup.","security_implications":"Cleanup must revoke or release sensitive resources without creating races that expose residual credentials or authorization state.","performance_implications":"Cleanup latency can accumulate across large suites; measure it separately from test-body execution.","version_constraints":"Fixture and runner teardown semantics may vary by framework version.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Setup and teardown are executable phases whose failures must be diagnosed independently from test assertions."}],"related_records":["D38-B012","D38-B038"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B012"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B038"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B041","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B017","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Build Failure","concept":"Build or compilation failure versus test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a build or compilation failure be separated from a test failure?","answer":"Classify the failure according to whether test execution began. If compilation, linking, packaging, dependency resolution, or test discovery prevents the test from executing, the failure is an execution-pipeline failure rather than evidence from the test oracle. Preserve compiler or build evidence and diagnose the earliest failed build stage.","explanation":"A test suite can report zero executed tests or a generic failure even though the actual problem occurred before test execution.","failure_classes":["COMPILATION_FAILURE","LINK_FAILURE","DEPENDENCY_RESOLUTION_FAILURE","TEST_DISCOVERY_FAILURE","PACKAGING_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["build command","compiler output","linker output","dependency resolution output","test discovery output","execution count"],"candidate_causes":["syntax error","type error","missing dependency","incompatible compiler","link failure","discovery configuration error"],"diagnosis_rule":"Verify that the target test actually executed before interpreting a reported test failure as behavioral evidence.","repair_strategy":"Repair the earliest failed build or discovery stage, then rerun the intended tests rather than modifying application behavior based on non-execution.","verification_method":"Confirm successful build/discovery and then execute the affected test independently and within the intended suite.","security_implications":"Do not bypass compiler or static security checks merely to execute tests.","performance_implications":"Build cache or incremental compilation problems can affect pipeline timing but should not be confused with runtime test performance.","version_constraints":"Compiler, build tool, language version, dependency versions, and platform must be captured.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_PIPELINE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Behavioral test evidence exists only when the intended test actually executes."}],"related_records":["D38-B001"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B001"},{"type":"PRECEDES","record_id":"D38-B042"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B042","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B017","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Test Discovery","concept":"Missing or incorrect test discovery","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a missing-test-discovery failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the intended test was discovered, selected, loaded, and executed. Inspect naming rules, directory inclusion, tags, filters, configuration, test collection errors, module loading, and runner selection. A green suite with zero or unexpectedly few tests can represent a discovery failure rather than successful validation.","explanation":"Test runners can silently exclude tests through filters or configuration, creating false confidence rather than explicit failures.","failure_classes":["TEST_NOT_DISCOVERED","FILTER_EXCLUSION","COLLECTION_FAILURE","MODULE_LOAD_FAILURE","CONFIGURATION_ERROR"],"evidence_required":["expected test count","actual discovered count","runner configuration","filters/tags","collection logs","test identifiers"],"candidate_causes":["incorrect naming","path exclusion","tag filter","module import failure","configuration drift","runner mismatch"],"diagnosis_rule":"Compare expected and actual test discovery and treat unexplained test-count reduction as a diagnostic event.","repair_strategy":"Correct discovery configuration or test metadata and add pipeline checks where feasible to detect unexpected discovery changes.","verification_method":"Verify exact discovery count, execute representative tests directly, and confirm suite-level discovery remains stable.","security_implications":"Security suites must fail visibly when required tests are excluded; silent filtering can create dangerous false assurance.","performance_implications":"Unexpectedly missing tests can make pipelines appear faster; test-count and execution-time monitoring can expose this anomaly.","version_constraints":"Discovery rules can change with test-runner/framework versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_DISCOVERY_PRINCIPLE","source":"A test suite's validity depends on executing the tests intended by its configuration."}],"related_records":["D38-B041","D38-B030"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B041"},{"type":"CONTRASTS_WITH","record_id":"D38-B030"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B043","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B017","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Runner Configuration","concept":"Test-runner configuration drift","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a failure caused by test-runner configuration drift be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the current runner configuration with the known-good configuration, including filters, retries, parallelism, timeouts, environment variables, plugins, working directory, discovery paths, and reporting behavior. Determine which changed configuration value alters execution before modifying the test or product.","explanation":"The runner is part of the test environment. Configuration drift can change which tests execute, how they execute, and how failures are reported.","failure_classes":["RUNNER_CONFIGURATION_DRIFT","FILTER_DRIFT","TIMEOUT_CONFIGURATION","PARALLELISM_CONFIGURATION","PLUGIN_CONFIGURATION"],"evidence_required":["current runner configuration","known-good configuration","runner version","test selection","execution logs","plugin list"],"candidate_causes":["changed timeout","new filter","different worker count","plugin update","environment variable","working-directory change"],"diagnosis_rule":"Treat runner configuration as versioned executable input and compare it before altering test assertions or application code.","repair_strategy":"Restore or intentionally update the runner configuration and encode critical settings in version-controlled configuration.","verification_method":"Run with known-good and current configurations and verify intended test discovery, execution, and reporting behavior.","security_implications":"Runner plugins and environment variables can affect credentials and permissions; configuration changes must be reviewed for security impact.","performance_implications":"Parallelism, retries, and timeouts directly affect suite cost and apparent performance.","version_constraints":"Runner and plugin versions must be recorded alongside configuration.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_INFRASTRUCTURE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Test-runner configuration is part of the effective test execution environment."}],"related_records":["D38-B042","D38-B044"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B042"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B044","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B017","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Retry Behavior","concept":"Retries masking deterministic failures","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure be diagnosed when retries cause the suite to pass intermittently?","answer":"Record every attempt and determine whether retries change the execution conditions or merely hide the same failure. Identify the underlying failure signature and establish whether it is nondeterministic, environment-dependent, or deterministic. A retry-induced pass does not prove the original failure was harmless.","explanation":"Retries can reduce pipeline interruption while simultaneously concealing defects, flaky tests, or environmental instability.","failure_classes":["RETRY_MASKED_FAILURE","FLAKINESS","DETERMINISTIC_RETRY_FAILURE","ENVIRONMENTAL_TRANSIENCE"],"evidence_required":["all attempt results","failure signatures","retry policy","execution conditions","timing","environment state"],"candidate_causes":["true transient dependency failure","race","resource contention","deterministic defect","test contamination"],"diagnosis_rule":"Analyze the first failure independently before accepting a later retry pass as evidence of recovery.","repair_strategy":"Fix the underlying deterministic cause or isolate and characterize legitimate transient behavior; use bounded retries only where their semantics are justified.","verification_method":"Run without retries for diagnosis, reproduce the first failure, then validate retry behavior separately under controlled transient-failure conditions.","security_implications":"Retries must not repeat sensitive operations without idempotency and authorization safeguards.","performance_implications":"Retries can amplify load and latency; measure retry amplification under failure conditions.","version_constraints":"Retry defaults and runner behavior can change across versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEST_RETRY_PRINCIPLE","source":"A later passing retry does not invalidate evidence from the initial failure."}],"related_records":["D38-B003","D37-B093"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B003"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D37-B093"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B045","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B017","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Build Artifacts","concept":"Stale build or test artifact","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure caused by stale compiled or generated artifacts be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the executed artifact was produced from the current source, dependency state, generated files, and configuration. Compare clean and incremental builds, inspect artifact timestamps or hashes where reliable, and identify stale generated or cached outputs that can preserve obsolete behavior.","explanation":"Build caches and generated artifacts can make the executed program differ from the source tree engineers believe they are testing.","failure_classes":["STALE_ARTIFACT","CACHE_STALENESS","GENERATED_CODE_DRIFT","INCREMENTAL_BUILD_ERROR"],"evidence_required":["source revision","artifact identity","build configuration","cache state","generated-file state","clean-build result","runtime artifact version"],"candidate_causes":["stale object file","incorrect cache key","generated source not regenerated","wrong binary","incremental build defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Establish artifact provenance before diagnosing runtime behavior from source code alone.","repair_strategy":"Invalidate or correct the affected build artifact path and ensure artifact identity is reproducibly tied to source and configuration.","verification_method":"Perform a clean reproducible build, compare artifact identity, rerun the failing test, and then verify incremental builds if they are supported.","security_implications":"Stale artifacts can preserve vulnerable code after a source-level fix; deployment and test artifacts require provenance.","performance_implications":"Clean builds may change compilation but do not establish runtime performance; separate build and runtime measurements.","version_constraints":"Compiler, build system, generated-code tool, and cache versions can affect artifact generation.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"BUILD_REPRODUCIBILITY_PRINCIPLE","source":"Runtime test evidence depends on knowing which artifact was actually executed."}],"related_records":["D38-B041","D38-B043"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B041"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B046","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B018","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Platform-Specific Behavior","concept":"Operating-system-specific test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure that occurs only on one operating system be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare platform-specific filesystem, process, signal, path, permission, networking, environment, timing, and system-call behavior. Determine whether the tested requirement promises portability or explicitly permits platform-specific behavior. Reproduce the difference on the affected platform before modifying cross-platform code.","explanation":"Platform-specific behavior can be legitimate or defective depending on the portability contract. Diagnosis requires distinguishing environmental semantics from accidental assumptions.","failure_classes":["OS_BEHAVIOR_DIFFERENCE","FILESYSTEM_SEMANTICS","PROCESS_BEHAVIOR","PERMISSION_DIFFERENCE","PATH_SEMANTICS"],"evidence_required":["OS/version","architecture","runtime","filesystem behavior","permissions","environment","system interaction","expected portability contract"],"candidate_causes":["path assumption","case sensitivity","permission model","signal behavior","newline convention","process semantics","OS API difference"],"diagnosis_rule":"Compare the failing platform behavior against the explicit portability requirement before classifying it as a defect.","repair_strategy":"Use platform-neutral APIs where portability is required or isolate documented platform-specific behavior behind explicit boundaries.","verification_method":"Run representative tests on affected and supported platforms and include the diagnosed boundary condition in regression coverage.","security_implications":"Permission and process differences can create security vulnerabilities; do not normalize behavior by weakening platform security controls.","performance_implications":"Platform-specific I/O and process behavior can change performance independently of correctness.","version_constraints":"OS, architecture, runtime, and relevant system-library versions are diagnostic inputs.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"CROSS_PLATFORM_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Portability diagnosis requires comparing actual platform semantics with the intended portability contract."}],"related_records":["D38-B006","D37-B097"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B006"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D37-B097"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B047","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B018","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Architecture-Specific Behavior","concept":"CPU or architecture-specific failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should an architecture-specific test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare architecture, word size, alignment requirements, instruction availability, endianness where relevant, atomic behavior, compiler target, native dependencies, and runtime assumptions. Determine whether the failure is caused by unsupported assumptions or by a legitimate architecture-specific contract.","explanation":"Architecture differences can expose undefined assumptions, native-library incompatibilities, alignment issues, or incorrect binary representations.","failure_classes":["ARCHITECTURE_DIFFERENCE","NATIVE_BINARY_INCOMPATIBILITY","ALIGNMENT_FAILURE","WORD_SIZE_ASSUMPTION","ENDIANNESS_ASSUMPTION"],"evidence_required":["CPU architecture","compiler target","runtime","native dependencies","binary representation","build flags","failure trace"],"candidate_causes":["pointer-size assumption","alignment assumption","native library mismatch","instruction availability","binary format assumption"],"diagnosis_rule":"Identify the architecture-dependent assumption and determine whether the supported platform contract permits it.","repair_strategy":"Remove invalid architecture assumptions, use portable abstractions, or explicitly constrain supported architectures with evidence.","verification_method":"Build and test on the affected architecture and a known-good architecture, including relevant native integration tests.","security_implications":"Architecture-specific memory or binary-handling defects can have security consequences and require targeted validation.","performance_implications":"Architecture-specific optimizations should be evaluated independently from functional correctness.","version_constraints":"Compiler, runtime, ABI, and native dependency versions must be recorded.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"PORTABLE_SYSTEMS_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Architecture-dependent assumptions must be tested against the supported ABI and runtime contract."}],"related_records":["D38-B046"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B046"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B048","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B018","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Locale and Encoding","concept":"Locale or encoding-dependent test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test failure caused by locale or text encoding be diagnosed?","answer":"Record locale, language, character encoding, normalization assumptions, collation rules, line-ending behavior, and input/output encoding at each relevant boundary. Determine whether the test expects locale-independent behavior or intentionally tests locale-sensitive behavior.","explanation":"Text can change through encoding, normalization, collation, formatting, and locale-sensitive APIs. Diagnosis requires identifying the exact representation or semantic boundary that differs.","failure_classes":["ENCODING_MISMATCH","LOCALE_DEPENDENCY","NORMALIZATION_DIFFERENCE","COLLATION_DIFFERENCE","LINE_ENDING_DIFFERENCE"],"evidence_required":["input bytes","decoded text","encoding","locale","normalization form","expected representation","actual representation"],"candidate_causes":["implicit default encoding","locale-sensitive formatting","Unicode normalization","platform line endings","collation differences"],"diagnosis_rule":"Compare byte-level and semantic text representations separately to identify where the expected and actual behavior diverge.","repair_strategy":"Use explicit encoding and locale semantics where required, normalize only when the domain requires it, and avoid destructive normalization that changes meaning.","verification_method":"Test representative Unicode, locale, normalization, and line-ending cases across supported environments.","security_implications":"Encoding confusion can enable validation bypass or injection; security-sensitive parsing must use explicit and consistent encoding rules.","performance_implications":"Normalization and collation can have nontrivial cost; measure separately from correctness.","version_constraints":"Runtime, Unicode data, locale libraries, and operating-system versions can affect behavior.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"TEXT_PROCESSING_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Text behavior depends on explicit encoding, locale, normalization, and representation semantics."}],"related_records":["D38-B006","D38-B034"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B006"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D38-B034"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B049","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B018","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Filesystem Behavior","concept":"Filesystem-dependent test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a filesystem-dependent test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Inspect path construction, path normalization, case sensitivity, permissions, symlink behavior, atomicity assumptions, file locking, temporary-directory selection, filesystem type, and cleanup state. Determine which filesystem property differs between environments and whether the application or test contract requires portability.","explanation":"Filesystem semantics vary across operating systems and filesystem implementations. Tests that assume one behavior can fail elsewhere or become nondeterministic.","failure_classes":["FILESYSTEM_SEMANTICS","PATH_ERROR","PERMISSION_FAILURE","CASE_SENSITIVITY","LOCKING_DIFFERENCE","SYMLINK_DIFFERENCE"],"evidence_required":["absolute path","filesystem type","permissions","directory state","path normalization","file metadata","OS/environment"],"candidate_causes":["case sensitivity","path separator assumption","permission difference","file lock","symlink behavior","temporary directory collision"],"diagnosis_rule":"Identify the filesystem semantic responsible for the failure before changing application logic.","repair_strategy":"Use platform-aware filesystem APIs, explicit temporary-resource ownership, and documented portability assumptions.","verification_method":"Run against supported filesystem/platform combinations and verify creation, access, locking, cleanup, and boundary path behavior.","security_implications":"Filesystem tests must preserve permission boundaries and avoid broadening access merely to make tests pass.","performance_implications":"Filesystem latency and caching can affect performance tests; functional filesystem failures should be diagnosed separately.","version_constraints":"OS, filesystem, runtime, and filesystem-library versions may affect behavior.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"FILESYSTEM_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Portable filesystem behavior requires explicit handling of platform and filesystem semantics."}],"related_records":["D38-B039","D38-B046"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B046"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B039"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B050","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B018","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Permission Failures","concept":"Permission-dependent test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure involving permissions be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine the identity, effective permissions, resource ownership, security policy, and operation being attempted. Establish whether the test expects access to succeed or fail and whether the observed result matches the intended authorization contract. Avoid changing permissions globally before identifying the required access boundary.","explanation":"Permission failures may indicate correct security enforcement, incorrect test setup, or an application authorization defect. The expected authorization outcome must be established first.","failure_classes":["EXPECTED_DENIAL","UNEXPECTED_DENIAL","PRIVILEGE_ESCALATION","RESOURCE_PERMISSION_MISMATCH"],"evidence_required":["effective identity","resource owner","permissions","authorization policy","requested operation","expected outcome","actual error"],"candidate_causes":["incorrect fixture identity","missing permission","wrong resource owner","application authorization defect","environment policy"],"diagnosis_rule":"Classify the expected authorization result before treating an access denial as a test failure.","repair_strategy":"Correct test identity/setup for intended cases or repair authorization behavior when the application violates its documented access policy.","verification_method":"Test both allowed and denied cases under controlled identities and verify least-privilege behavior.","security_implications":"Never resolve a permission-test failure by granting broad privileges without evidence; authorization behavior is part of the security requirement.","performance_implications":"Permission checks can affect latency but should not be bypassed for performance tests unless the test explicitly isolates non-security behavior.","version_constraints":"OS, container, runtime, security framework, and policy versions may affect effective permissions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"AUTHORIZATION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Permission outcomes must be evaluated against the intended authorization policy and identity context."}],"related_records":["D38-B006","D38-B049"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B049"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B001"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B051","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B019","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Security Test Failure","concept":"Security test failure classification","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a security test failure be diagnosed without weakening the security assertion?","answer":"Identify the security property being tested, the trust boundary, attacker capability, expected security invariant, observed behavior, and evidence source. Determine whether the failure represents a genuine control violation, invalid test setup, environment limitation, or incorrect security oracle. Security failures should not be suppressed merely because the normal functional path succeeds.","explanation":"Security tests frequently operate under intentionally hostile or restricted conditions. Their diagnosis requires preserving the adversarial condition while determining which security invariant failed.","failure_classes":["AUTHORIZATION_FAILURE","AUTHENTICATION_FAILURE","INPUT_VALIDATION_FAILURE","SECRECY_FAILURE","INTEGRITY_FAILURE","SECURITY_ORACLE_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["security requirement","trust boundary","test identity","attacker capability","input","expected security property","observed result","security control evidence"],"candidate_causes":["control defect","incorrect identity setup","invalid threat assumption","weak oracle","environment restriction","configuration defect"],"diagnosis_rule":"Validate the security property and adversarial preconditions before changing the security-sensitive implementation.","repair_strategy":"Correct the control when evidence shows a requirement violation; correct the test only when its security premise or oracle is invalid.","verification_method":"Reproduce the security condition, verify the control independently, add regression coverage, and confirm legitimate functionality remains intact.","security_implications":"Security-test diagnosis itself must preserve confidentiality, integrity, authorization, and evidence handling requirements.","performance_implications":"Security controls should not be removed to improve test speed; measure their cost separately if performance is relevant.","version_constraints":"Security libraries, runtime, configuration, and protocol versions must be recorded.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SECURITY_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Security tests must evaluate explicit security properties under defined threat and trust-boundary conditions."}],"related_records":["D38-B005","D38-B050"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B050"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B005"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B052","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B019","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Authentication Tests","concept":"Authentication test failure versus authorization test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should authentication and authorization failures be distinguished in security tests?","answer":"Determine whether the system failed to establish identity or established identity but made an incorrect permission decision. Inspect credentials or identity assertions, authentication outcome, authenticated principal, authorization policy, requested resource, and decision evidence separately.","explanation":"Conflating authentication and authorization can lead to incorrect fixes, such as changing permission policy to solve an identity-establishment problem.","failure_classes":["AUTHENTICATION_FAILURE","AUTHORIZATION_FAILURE","IDENTITY_MAPPING_FAILURE","SESSION_STATE_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["authentication request","authentication result","principal identity","authorization policy","resource","decision result","session state"],"candidate_causes":["invalid credentials","identity mapping defect","expired session","missing permission","incorrect policy evaluation"],"diagnosis_rule":"Establish the authenticated principal before diagnosing the authorization decision.","repair_strategy":"Repair identity establishment, identity mapping, session handling, or authorization policy according to the first evidence-supported violation.","verification_method":"Test valid/invalid authentication separately from allowed/denied authorization and verify that the layers fail independently as intended.","security_implications":"Security tests must verify both correct denial and correct allowance without weakening the trust boundary.","performance_implications":"Authentication and authorization latency should be measured separately when diagnosing performance-sensitive security tests.","version_constraints":"Identity protocols, security libraries, and policy engines can change semantics across versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","source":"Authentication establishes identity while authorization evaluates permissions for an established identity."}],"related_records":["D38-B051"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B051"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B053","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B019","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Input Validation","concept":"Security validation failure at a trust boundary","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a security test failure involving input validation be localized?","answer":"Identify the trust boundary, accepted input contract, validation stage, canonicalization steps, parser behavior, and downstream interpretation. Determine whether malicious or malformed input was rejected, transformed safely, or reached a sensitive operation in an unsafe representation.","explanation":"Security validation can fail at parsing, normalization, validation, encoding, or downstream interpretation. Diagnosis should locate the boundary where untrusted data became trusted or was incorrectly transformed.","failure_classes":["INPUT_VALIDATION_FAILURE","CANONICALIZATION_ERROR","PARSER_MISMATCH","ENCODING_CONFUSION","INJECTION_PATH"],"evidence_required":["raw input","decoded/canonicalized input","validation result","trust boundary","downstream operation","expected rejection or safe handling"],"candidate_causes":["incomplete validation","canonicalization mismatch","parser differential","unsafe decoding","validation bypass"],"diagnosis_rule":"Trace untrusted data from ingress through validation to sensitive use and identify the first point where the required security invariant is violated.","repair_strategy":"Correct validation or canonicalization at the appropriate trust boundary and add regression tests for equivalent and adversarial representations.","verification_method":"Test valid, invalid, encoded, canonical-equivalent, boundary, and adversarial inputs while preserving the intended downstream behavior.","security_implications":"Do not weaken validation or rely on downstream rejection when the documented trust boundary requires explicit input controls.","performance_implications":"Validation overhead should be measured separately; performance optimization must preserve complete security checks.","version_constraints":"Parser, encoding, runtime, and security-library versions can affect interpretation.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SECURE_INPUT_VALIDATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Untrusted input must be interpreted consistently and validated at the appropriate trust boundary."}],"related_records":["D38-B051","D38-B034"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B051"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B034"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B054","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B019","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Secrets in Test Artifacts","concept":"Security failure caused by test artifact exposure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a test failure involving leaked secrets or sensitive test artifacts be diagnosed?","answer":"Determine whether the test output, logs, snapshots, traces, fixtures, dumps, or reports contain credentials, tokens, personal data, cryptographic material, or other sensitive values. Establish whether exposure occurred because of instrumentation, assertion output, fixture content, error handling, or artifact storage rather than treating the leak as ordinary test noise.","explanation":"Diagnostic artifacts can create a security failure independently of the application behavior being tested.","failure_classes":["SECRET_EXPOSURE","SENSITIVE_LOGGING","ARTIFACT_LEAK","REPORTING_EXPOSURE"],"evidence_required":["artifact content","secret classification","artifact destination","logging configuration","test fixture source","access permissions"],"candidate_causes":["verbose failure output","unsanitized fixture","snapshot capture","debug logging","report upload","trace export"],"diagnosis_rule":"Treat sensitive data exposure in test artifacts as a separate security event and preserve enough evidence to determine the exposure path without proliferating the secret.","repair_strategy":"Remove or redact sensitive values at the appropriate artifact boundary, replace real secrets with controlled test credentials, and restrict artifact access.","verification_method":"Repeat the failing test and inspect all generated artifacts to confirm that sensitive values are absent or appropriately protected.","security_implications":"Never reproduce a secret by copying it into additional diagnostic artifacts; use redacted evidence and controlled credentials.","performance_implications":"Redaction and secure artifact handling can add overhead but should not be removed from security-sensitive test pipelines without evidence.","version_constraints":"Logging, reporting, CI, and test framework versions may alter artifact generation behavior.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SECURE_TEST_ARTIFACT_PRINCIPLE","source":"Test diagnostics must preserve useful evidence while preventing unnecessary exposure of sensitive data."}],"related_records":["D38-B051"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B051"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B055","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B019","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Security test failure caused by configuration drift","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should security-test failures caused by configuration drift be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the effective security configuration with the configuration required by the test and deployment contract. Inspect authentication modes, authorization policies, cryptographic settings, certificate material, security headers, network restrictions, feature flags, and environment-specific overrides. Establish whether the configuration is intentionally different or accidentally drifted.","explanation":"Security behavior can change materially through configuration without source-code changes. Tests must diagnose effective configuration rather than source defaults alone.","failure_classes":["SECURITY_CONFIGURATION_DRIFT","POLICY_DRIFT","CRYPTO_CONFIGURATION_MISMATCH","FEATURE_FLAG_SECURITY_DRIFT"],"evidence_required":["effective configuration","expected security policy","environment overrides","feature flags","identity configuration","crypto settings","test result"],"candidate_causes":["environment override","stale secret/config","policy mismatch","feature flag","certificate mismatch","insecure default"],"diagnosis_rule":"Compare effective runtime configuration against the authoritative security requirement before modifying application logic.","repair_strategy":"Correct configuration drift or update the test only when the documented security policy intentionally changed.","verification_method":"Validate effective configuration and execute both positive and negative security scenarios after correction.","security_implications":"Security configuration must follow least privilege and secure defaults; tests should fail visibly when required controls are disabled.","performance_implications":"Cryptographic and policy controls can affect performance, but performance changes must not justify weakening required security settings.","version_constraints":"Security configuration schemas and defaults can change across framework, library, and deployment versions.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"SECURITY_CONFIGURATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"Effective security behavior depends on runtime configuration as well as source code."}],"related_records":["D38-B051","D38-B006"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B006"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B051"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B056","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B020","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Performance Test Failure","concept":"Functional failure versus performance-threshold failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should a performance-test failure be distinguished from a functional correctness failure?","answer":"Determine whether the observed result violates functional correctness, a quantitative performance requirement, or both. Validate workload, measurement method, warm-up, environment, threshold provenance, and statistical interpretation before changing implementation code.","explanation":"A test can produce correct results but fail a latency or throughput threshold, or it can produce incorrect results under load. These are distinct diagnostic dimensions.","failure_classes":["LATENCY_THRESHOLD_FAILURE","THROUGHPUT_FAILURE","RESOURCE_LIMIT_FAILURE","FUNCTIONAL_FAILURE_UNDER_LOAD"],"evidence_required":["workload definition","measurement method","latency/throughput observations","threshold source","environment","resource utilization","correctness result"],"candidate_causes":["performance regression","invalid threshold","environment noise","resource contention","functional defect exposed under load"],"diagnosis_rule":"Classify correctness and performance independently and validate the measurement methodology before optimization.","repair_strategy":"Correct the performance bottleneck when the requirement and measurements support it; correct the functional defect when correctness is violated.","verification_method":"Repeat under controlled workload and environment, collect sufficient measurements, and rerun functional regression tests.","security_implications":"Load tests must preserve authentication, authorization, rate limits, and other security controls when those are part of the production path.","performance_implications":"Performance claims require measured evidence; never invent benchmarks or infer quantitative improvement without measurement.","version_constraints":"Runtime, compiler, database, dependency, hardware, and workload versions/configuration must be recorded.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Performance failures require validated workloads, measurements, thresholds, and controlled environments."}],"related_records":["D38-B008","D38-B030"],"relationships":[{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B008"},{"type":"DISTINGUISHES_FROM","record_id":"D38-B030"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B057","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B020","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Load Test Failure","concept":"Load-induced failure versus test infrastructure saturation","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should a load-test failure be diagnosed when the test runner itself may be saturated?","answer":"Measure resource utilization and request generation capacity on both the system under test and the load generator. Establish whether the generator can produce the intended workload and whether its CPU, memory, network, connections, or scheduling capacity becomes the bottleneck before the target reaches its intended load.","explanation":"A saturated load generator can produce apparent target failures or under-deliver the intended workload, invalidating conclusions about system capacity.","failure_classes":["LOAD_GENERATOR_SATURATION","TARGET_SATURATION","NETWORK_GENERATOR_LIMIT","WORKLOAD_GENERATION_FAILURE"],"evidence_required":["intended workload","actual generated workload","generator resource metrics","target resource metrics","network capacity","request rate","error rate"],"candidate_causes":["generator CPU limit","generator connection limit","target saturation","network bottleneck","incorrect workload model"],"diagnosis_rule":"Prove that the intended workload was actually delivered before attributing observed degradation to the target system.","repair_strategy":"Scale or redesign the load generator, correct workload generation, or address the target bottleneck according to measured evidence.","verification_method":"Repeat with validated workload generation and independent resource measurements on generator and target.","security_implications":"Load testing must respect authorization and rate-control boundaries; do not disable production security controls solely to increase load.","performance_implications":"All quantitative findings must be tied to measured workload and resource evidence; no unmeasured capacity claims.","version_constraints":"Load tool, runtime, network stack, target version, and hardware configuration must be recorded.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"LOAD_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Load-test conclusions require evidence that the load generator delivered the intended workload without becoming the bottleneck."}],"related_records":["D38-B056","D38-B008"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B056"},{"type":"SPECIALIZES","record_id":"D38-B008"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B058","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B020","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Regression Failure","concept":"Selecting the relevant regression scope after a failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"How should the relevant regression scope be determined after diagnosing a test failure?","answer":"Map the diagnosed behavior to affected requirements, components, interfaces, state transitions, dependencies, security boundaries, and previously related tests. Select regression tests based on causal and dependency relationships rather than rerunning only the failing test or indiscriminately running every test without understanding coverage.","explanation":"The purpose of regression selection is to detect consequences of the diagnosed change while preserving efficient and meaningful evidence.","failure_classes":["REGRESSION_SCOPE_ERROR","INSUFFICIENT_REGRESSION","UNRELATED_REGRESSION_NOISE"],"evidence_required":["diagnosed failure mechanism","affected component","dependency graph","related requirements","existing tests","changed interfaces","historical regressions"],"candidate_causes":["narrow regression selection","hidden dependency","shared state","contract impact","security boundary impact"],"diagnosis_rule":"Choose regression scope from the causal dependency surface of the diagnosed behavior.","repair_strategy":"Add or select regression tests that directly exercise the repaired behavior and its meaningful dependent surfaces.","verification_method":"Run the focused regression, impacted integration/contract tests, and broader suites justified by the dependency surface.","security_implications":"Any change affecting authentication, authorization, input validation, secrets, or trust boundaries requires corresponding security regression coverage.","performance_implications":"Changes to hot paths, concurrency, I/O, or resource lifecycle may require targeted performance regression tests in addition to functional tests.","version_constraints":"Regression selection should reflect the supported versions and compatibility matrix affected by the change.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"REGRESSION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","source":"Regression scope should be derived from the behavior and dependency surface affected by a change."}],"related_records":["D37-B099","D37-B100"],"relationships":[{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B100"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D37-B099"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B059","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B020","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Fix Verification","concept":"Distinguishing symptom disappearance from verified repair","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an engineer determine whether a repaired test failure is actually fixed rather than merely hidden?","answer":"Reproduce the original failure, establish its causal condition, apply the targeted repair, and verify that the causal condition no longer produces the failure. Then execute relevant regression, negative, boundary, and environment-specific tests. A passing rerun alone is insufficient when the test could be bypassed, weakened, or made nonrepresentative by the repair.","explanation":"Repair verification must demonstrate that the original failure mechanism was addressed while preserving required behavior and test validity.","failure_classes":["SYMPTOM_ONLY_REPAIR","WEAKENED_TEST","UNVERIFIED_REPAIR","REGRESSION_INTRODUCTION"],"evidence_required":["original failure evidence","root diagnostic condition","repair change","post-repair behavior","regression results","test-oracle validity"],"candidate_causes":["assertion removal","condition bypass","test disabling","environment change","unrelated state change","genuine causal repair"],"diagnosis_rule":"Verify the causal mechanism and the requirement, not merely disappearance of the original failure message.","repair_strategy":"Prefer targeted repairs that eliminate the evidence-supported cause while preserving the original test's intent and oracle.","verification_method":"Replay original failure, verify causal correction, execute focused regression, negative/boundary cases, and relevant broader tests.","security_implications":"A security failure is not repaired if the security assertion is weakened or the attack path is simply disabled.","performance_implications":"A performance failure is not repaired by reducing workload below the requirement; verify against the original workload and measurement method.","version_constraints":"Verification must use the intended supported version and configuration because a fix proven elsewhere may not apply to the target environment.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"REPAIR_VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","source":"A valid repair must eliminate the diagnosed cause while preserving the intended test requirement."}],"related_records":["D38-B010","D37-B100"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D38-B010"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B100"}]}
{"record_id":"D38-B060","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"D38-S1","batch_id":"B020","topic":"Test Failure → Diagnosis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Evidence Closure","concept":"Closing the evidence chain for a test failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","question":"What constitutes a complete evidence chain for a diagnosed test failure?","answer":"A complete diagnosis connects the original failure to the exact test execution, expected behavior, observed divergence, eliminated competing causes, evidence-supported causal mechanism, targeted correction or test correction, focused verification, regression evidence, and known limitations. Each conclusion should be distinguishable as observed evidence, inference, or unresolved uncertainty.","explanation":"Diagnosis quality depends not only on finding a plausible cause but on preserving traceability from observation to conclusion and verification.","failure_classes":["INCOMPLETE_DIAGNOSIS","UNSUPPORTED_ROOT_CAUSE","INSUFFICIENT_VERIFICATION","PROVENANCE_GAP"],"evidence_required":["original failure","test context","expected behavior","observed behavior","candidate causes","elimination evidence","diagnostic conclusion","repair/test correction","verification evidence","limitations"],"candidate_causes":["premature attribution","missing environment evidence","weak oracle","unreproduced hypothesis","incomplete regression"],"diagnosis_rule":"Do not mark a test failure as fully diagnosed when the causal claim cannot be connected to observable evidence and a reproducible verification result.","repair_strategy":"Collect missing evidence, distinguish hypotheses from established facts, and preserve the diagnostic chain for future regression and retrieval.","verification_method":"Audit the record for evidence-to-conclusion traceability and confirm that the final verification addresses the diagnosed mechanism and original requirement.","security_implications":"Security diagnostic records must preserve enough evidence for audit while minimizing sensitive-data exposure.","performance_implications":"Performance conclusions require measured evidence and workload context; inferred bottlenecks must remain explicitly labeled as inference.","version_constraints":"All version-sensitive evidence must identify the environment in which the diagnosis was established.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":[{"source_type":"EVIDENCE_DRIVEN_DIAGNOSIS_PRINCIPLE","source":"A reliable diagnosis requires traceable evidence from failure observation through causal explanation and verification."}],"related_records":["D38-B002","D38-B059","D37-B100"],"relationships":[{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D38-B002"},{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D38-B059"},{"type":"FEEDS","record_id":"D37-B100"}]}

