ication scope and inspect persisted key state.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis establishes key-scope mismatch; request generation or server policy changes belong to repair.","test_method":"Repeat identical and distinct requests across controlled scope boundaries.","verification_method":"Confirm duplicate handling changes according to the documented key scope.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B146","D38-B333"],"relationships":["diagnoses:idempotency-failure","related_to:request-deduplication"]} {"record_id":"D38-B493","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Webhook Signature Timestamp","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","title":"Diagnosing webhook signature failures caused by timestamp skew","objective":"Determine whether a webhook verification test fails because the signed timestamp falls outside the accepted freshness window rather than because the signature secret is incorrect.","failure_context":"A webhook signature includes a timestamp and the verifier rejects otherwise valid signatures after clock or fixture-time differences.","failure_signal":"Signature verification fails only when the timestamp is sufficiently old, future-dated, or interpreted under a different clock.","observable_symptom":"Fresh fixtures pass while replay-style or delayed fixtures fail unexpectedly.","expected_behavior":"Signature verification should enforce the documented timestamp freshness policy.","diagnostic_evidence":["Capture signed timestamp","Record verifier clock","Recompute expected signing input","Compare within and outside freshness window"],"candidate_causes":["Clock skew","Fixture timestamp reuse","Incorrect timestamp units","Expired signature"],"root_cause":"The test's signed timestamp falls outside the verifier's accepted freshness window.","diagnostic_metho