c_method":"Inspect the complete chain and validity properties rather than relying on a generic client error string.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis classifies certificate validation failure; certificate or trust-store remediation belongs to repair.","test_method":"Use controlled certificates that separately exercise expiry, trust, and hostname conditions.","verification_method":"Confirm the exact validation rule responsible for each rejection.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B328","D38-B287"],"relationships":["diagnoses:tls-validation-failure","related_to:certificate-trust"]} {"record_id":"D38-B495","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Monotonic Versus Wall Clock","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","title":"Diagnosing timeout tests that use the wrong clock source","objective":"Determine whether a timeout assertion fails because elapsed-duration measurement uses a wall clock subject to adjustment rather than a monotonic clock.","failure_context":"A test measures elapsed time across clock changes or under an environment where wall-clock adjustments occur.","failure_signal":"Measured duration changes unexpectedly after system time adjustment while real elapsed time remains stable.","observable_symptom":"Timeouts trigger early, late, or inconsistently when the system clock changes.","expected_behavior":"Elapsed-duration measurement should use a clock appropriate to the timing contract, normally monotonic for durations.","diagnostic_evidence":["Identify clock source","Record clock values at start/end","Compare monotonic and wall-clock measurements","Inject controlled clock changes"],"candidate_causes":["Wall-clock adjustment","NTP correction","Timezone change","Clock mocking"],"root_cause":"The timing calculation uses a clock whose value can change independently of elapsed duration.","diagnostic_method":"Trace the exact clock source used for timeout calculation and compare behavior under controlled clock shifts.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis establishes clock-source mismatch; timing implementation changes belong to repair.","test_method":"Run elapsed-time tests with controlled wall-clock changes while monitoring monotonic time.","verification_method":"Confirm that only the wall-clock-based calculation changes when wall time is adjusted.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B136","D38-B449"],"relationships":["diagnoses:timing-source-failure","related_to:monotonic-clock"]} {"record_id":"D38-B496","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Language Runtime Semantic Drift","knowledge_type":"VERSION_AWARE","title":"Diagnosing test failures caused by runtime semantic changes","objective":"Determine whether a test failure is caused by a changed language or runtime behavior rather than an application regression.","failure_context":"The same source and test produce different results under different supported runtime versions.","failure_signal":"Failure correlates with runtime version and can be reproduced without changing application inputs.","observable_symptom":"Exception types, ordering, coercion, standard-library behavior, or diagnostics differ between runtimes.","expected_behavior":"The expected result must correspond to the runtime version declared by the project contract.","diagnostic_evidence":["Record runtime version","Compare official behavior for affected feature","Run across supported versions","Inspect dependency compatibility"],"candidate_causes":["Runtime behavior change","Deprecated behavior removal","Bug fix in runtime","Version-specific standard library change"],"root_cause":"The test expectation or implementation interacts with behavior that differs across runtime versions.","diagnostic_method":"Bisect runtime versions while keeping source, dependencies, and inputs controlled.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis establishes version-specific behavior; compatibility or migration work belongs to repair.","test_method":"Execute the same test matrix across declared runtime versions.","verification_method":"Confirm that the failure tracks a specific runtime behavior change and does not result from unrelated environment drift.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B141","D38-B273"],"relationships":["diagnoses:version-specific-failure","related_to:runtime-compatibility"]} {"record_id":"D38-B497","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Compiler Diagnostic Versus Runtime Failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","title":"Distinguishing compile-time diagnostic changes from runtime regressions","objective":"Determine whether a failing test reflects a compilation or static-checking failure before program execution rather than a runtime behavioral defect.","failure_context":"A build or test reports an error before the target program reaches its runtime assertions.","failure_signal":"No runtime evidence exists for the failing case.","observable_symptom":"The test appears to fail on expected output, but the executable was not successfully built or loaded.","expected_behavior":"Diagnosis should identify the earliest failed execution phase.","diagnostic_evidence":["Inspect build output","Confirm executable creation","Confirm test process startup","Inspect compiler and runtime logs"],"candidate_causes":["Syntax error","Type-checking failure","Compiler incompatibility","Linking failure"],"root_cause":"The test case never reached the runtime behavior being asserted.","diagnostic_method":"Establish phase ordering: source processing, compilation, linking, process startup, then test execution.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis classifies pre-runtime failure; source/build remediation belongs to another phase.","test_method":"Verify compilation and executable startup independently before running the behavioral assertion.","verification_method":"Confirm whether runtime evidence exists; if not, classify the failure at the earliest failed phase.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B117","D38-B119"],"relationships":["diagnoses:execution-phase-failure","related_to:build-versus-runtime"]} {"record_id":"D38-B498","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Generated Test Artifact Drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","title":"Diagnosing generated-test artifact drift","objective":"Determine whether a failure originates from generated test code or fixtures that no longer correspond to their source templates or inputs.","failure_context":"Tests execute generated artifacts that may have been created by an earlier template or generation configuration.","failure_signal":"Regenerating artifacts changes the failure without changing application source.","observable_symptom":"Generated assertions, schemas, fixtures, or helper code disagree with current source definitions.","expected_behavior":"Generated test artifacts should correspond to the declared generator inputs and version.","diagnostic_evidence":["Record generator version","Compare generated artifact timestamp/hash","Regenerate from clean inputs","Diff generated output"],"candidate_causes":["Stale generated files","Template change","Generator version mismatch","Partial regeneration"],"root_cause":"The executed test artifact does not correspond to the current authoritative generation inputs.","diagnostic_method":"Reproduce from a clean generation step and compare artifact identity and behavior with the failing artifact.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis establishes generated-artifact drift; regeneration workflow changes belong to repair.","test_method":"Delete generated artifacts, regenerate deterministically, and execute the affected tests.","verification_method":"Confirm that the failure changes when artifact provenance changes and identify the stale generation source.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B296","D38-B273"],"relationships":["diagnoses:generated-artifact-drift","related_to:artifact-provenance"]} {"record_id":"D38-B499","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Test Configuration Precedence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","title":"Diagnosing hidden test configuration precedence conflicts","objective":"Determine which configuration source actually controls a failing test when multiple configuration layers define the same setting.","failure_context":"Project, environment, command-line, fixture, and runtime configuration values overlap.","failure_signal":"Changing one configuration source has no effect because a higher-precedence source overrides it.","observable_symptom":"The test appears to ignore a documented setting or behaves differently between invocation methods.","expected_behavior":"The effective configuration should be derived from the actual precedence order rather than an assumed source.","diagnostic_evidence":["Record all configuration sources","Compute effective value","Inspect runner arguments","Compare environment and fixture overrides"],"candidate_causes":["Environment override","CLI precedence","Fixture mutation","Nested configuration merge"],"root_cause":"The failing test uses an effective configuration value supplied by a higher-precedence source than the investigator assumed.","diagnostic_method":"Reconstruct the complete configuration chain and record the winning value for each relevant setting.","resolution_boundary":"Diagnosis identifies effective configuration precedence; configuration cleanup belongs to repair.","test_method":"Change one source at a time while recording the effective runtime value.","verification_method":"Confirm the observed behavior changes only when the highest-precedence controlling source is changed.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B142","D38-B349"],"relationships":["diagnoses:configuration-failure","related_to:configuration-precedence"]} {"record_id":"D38-B500","dataset_id":"D38","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Failure Diagnosis","subtopic":"Evidence Freshness and Causal Closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","title":"Diagnosing with stale evidence after environment or code change","objective":"Ensure that a test-failure diagnosis is based on evidence from the same relevant code, configuration, dependency, and environment state as the failure being explained.","failure_context":"Investigators modify code, dependencies, configuration, or environment before collecting or reusing diagnostic evidence.","failure_signal":"The original failure cannot be reproduced under the state represented by the collected evidence.","observable_symptom":"A previously plausible root cause no longer explains current behavior or the evidence comes from a different artifact/environment.","expected_behavior":"Diagnostic conclusions should be tied to an identifiable failure state and should distinguish original evidence from post-change observations.","diagnostic_evidence":["Record source revision","Record dependency/environment versions","Preserve original logs and traces","Compare pre-change and post-change reproduction"],"candidate_causes":["Code changed before diagnosis","Dependency updated","Environment rebuilt","Test data replaced"],"root_cause":"The diagnostic evidence is temporally or state-wise disconnected from the failure it is claimed to explain.","diagnostic_method":"Establish a failure-state identity containing relevant code, configuration, dependency, data, and environment references before evaluating causal claims.","resolution_boundary":"This record governs diagnostic evidence validity; repair and regression validation remain separate activities.","test_method":"Reproduce the failure from the preserved failure-state identity and compare it with the current environment.","verification_method":"Accept the diagnosis only when the claimed cause explains reproduced evidence from the identified failure state and remains distinguishable from later changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D38-B149","D38-B294","D38-B297","D38-B350"],"relationships":["diagnoses:evidence-integrity","related_to:causal-closure","governs:diagnostic-confidence"]}

{"record_id":"D39-B001","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Measurement Before Optimization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","title":"Establishing a measured performance baseline before optimization","objective":"Prevent optimization decisions based solely on subjective slowness or unmeasured assumptions.","performance_problem":"Application behavior is reported as slow, but the responsible operation and magnitude of the problem are unknown.","measurement_method":"Define the workload, measure latency or throughput under controlled conditions, identify relevant resource metrics, and preserve the baseline with environment and version metadata.","optimization_strategy":"Optimize only after a measurable bottleneck or constraint has been identified.","tradeoffs":["Measurement introduces diagnostic overhead","A synthetic workload may not represent production behavior","Averages can hide tail latency"],"failure_mode":"Changing implementation without establishing a baseline makes improvement or regression difficult to determine.","edge_cases":["Highly variable workloads","Warm versus cold execution","Low-frequency tail latency","Shared-resource contention"],"verification_method":"Repeat the same workload after optimization and compare equivalent metrics under equivalent conditions.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":[],"relationships":["establishes:performance-baseline","precedes:optimization-verification"]} {"record_id":"D39-B002","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Profiling-Guided Optimization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","title":"Using profiling evidence to select an optimization target","objective":"Prioritize optimization according to observed execution cost rather than code size or intuition.","performance_problem":"A workload has unacceptable runtime, but multiple functions and operations could plausibly contribute.","measurement_method":"Profile representative executions and identify functions, call paths, allocation sites, or waits that account for significant measured cost.","optimization_strategy":"Target the highest-impact bottleneck that is within the optimization scope and whose behavior is sufficiently stable to justify intervention.","tradeoffs":["Profiling can add overhead","Sampling and instrumentation provide different evidence","Hot code is not necessarily the only source of end-to-end latency"],"failure_mode":"Optimizing an unimportant function leaves the dominant bottleneck unchanged.","edge_cases":["Short-lived processes","Highly concurrent workloads","Input-dependent hotspots","Profiler-distorted timing"],"verification_method":"Re-profile the same workload and verify that the targeted cost decreased without unacceptable regression elsewhere.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B001"],"relationships":["uses:performance-baseline","requires:profiling-evidence"]} {"record_id":"D39-B003","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Amdahl-Limited Optimization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","title":"Recognizing limits on optimization from non-optimized work","objective":"Estimate whether improving one component can materially improve total runtime when other components remain unchanged.","performance_problem":"A substantial optimization is proposed for one portion of a workload while other portions still consume significant execution time.","measurement_method":"Measure the fraction of total execution attributable to the candidate component.","optimization_strategy":"Use the measured contribution to determine whether the candidate optimization can produce meaningful end-to-end improvement.","tradeoffs":["Optimizing a small fraction may have limited total effect","Workload proportions can change after optimization","Measurement boundaries affect interpretation"],"failure_mode":"A locally large speedup is mistaken for a large end-to-end speedup.","edge_cases":["Parallel workloads","I/O-bound systems","Changing workload composition"],"verification_method":"Measure end-to-end performance before and after the local optimization rather than extrapolating solely from local speedup.","performance_claim_type":"ESTIMATED_UNTIL_MEASURED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B001","D39-B002"],"relationships":["constrains:optimization-impact","related_to:bottleneck-analysis"]} {"record_id":"D39-B004","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Algorithmic Complexity","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","title":"Replacing repeated linear search with indexed lookup","objective":"Reduce repeated search cost when the workload repeatedly looks up values by a stable key.","performance_problem":"A collection is scanned linearly for each lookup, producing approximately O(n) search work per lookup.","measurement_method":"Measure lookup count, collection size, and cumulative lookup time.","optimization_strategy":"Build an appropriate hash-based index once and perform subsequent key lookups through the index when memory and key uniqueness requirements permit.","tradeoffs":["Additional memory consumption","Index construction cost","Mutation requires index maintenance","Hash-table behavior depends on implementation"],"incorrect_implementation":"for key in keys:\n    for item in items:\n        if item.key == key:\n            consume(item)","corrected_implementation":"index = {item.key: item for item in items}\nfor key in keys:\n    item = index.get(key)\n    if item is not None:\n        consume(item)","failure_mode":"A lookup optimization can produce incorrect results when duplicate keys have meaningful semantics or when the source collection changes without rebuilding the index.","testing_method":"Compare optimized and reference implementations across empty collections, missing keys, duplicate keys, and large inputs.","verification_method":"Verify output equivalence against the reference implementation and measure lookup performance on representative workloads.","performance_claim_type":"ESTIMATED_UNTIL_MEASURED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":[],"relationships":["optimizes:repeated-linear-search","uses:hash-indexing"]} {"record_id":"D39-B005","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Quadratic-to-Linear Transformation","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","title":"Removing repeated pairwise membership checks","objective":"Reduce avoidable quadratic work caused by repeated membership scans.","performance_problem":"An algorithm repeatedly searches one collection while iterating another, causing O(n*m) work where a set-based membership structure can represent the required predicate.","measurement_method":"Measure input sizes and cumulative membership-search time.","optimization_strategy":"Materialize membership keys into a set when the predicate requires equality-based membership and memory cost is acceptable.","tradeoffs":["Additional memory","Set construction cost","Different ordering semantics","Hashability requirements"],"incorrect_implementation":"matches = []\nfor value in values:\n    for allowed in allowed_values:\n        if value == allowed:\n            matches.append(value)\n            break","corrected_implementation":"allowed = set(allowed_values)\nmatches = [value for value in values if value in allowed]","failure_mode":"The transformation can change behavior if equality semantics, duplicate handling, or ordering requirements differ from the original algorithm.","testing_method":"Compare duplicate-heavy, empty, missing, and large-input cases against the reference implementation.","verification_method":"Verify semantic equivalence before measuring runtime improvement.","performance_claim_type":"ESTIMATED_UNTIL_MEASURED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B004"],"relationships":["optimizes:repeated-membership-search","related_to:complexity-reduction"]} {"record_id":"D39-B006","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"Database N+1 Queries","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","title":"Replacing N+1 database queries with set-based retrieval","objective":"Reduce database round trips caused by issuing one dependent query per retrieved entity.","performance_problem":"A parent query retrieves N entities and application code performs an additional database query for each entity.","measurement_method":"Count database round trips and measure total query and application latency.","optimization_strategy":"Retrieve required related data using a set-based query, join, batch query, or explicit prefetch strategy appropriate to the data relationship.","tradeoffs":["Potentially larger result sets","More complex query planning","Memory usage can increase","Join semantics must preserve required behavior"],"failure_mode":"Naive eager loading can over-fetch data or multiply rows and introduce new performance problems.","edge_cases":["Large relationship cardinality","Pagination","Optional relatio