{"record_id":"D39-B101","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"algorithmic complexity","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","title":"Quadratic pair comparison to sort-and-scan","objective":"Reduce repeated pairwise comparison when the problem permits ordering-based detection.","performance_problem":"Comparing every pair in an input of size n creates O(n²) comparisons even when the required relationship can be detected after sorting.","requirements":["Preserve the intended duplicate-or-adjacent relationship semantics.","Do not assume the input is already sorted.","Account for sorting cost in the performance analysis."],"incorrect_implementation":"def has_duplicate(values):\n    for i in range(len(values)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(values)):\n            if values[i] == values[j]:\n                return True\n    return False","corrected_implementation":"def has_duplicate(values):\n    ordered = sorted(values)\n    return any(a == b for a, b in zip(ordered, ordered[1:]))","optimization_strategy":"Replace exhaustive pairwise comparison with sorting followed by a single adjacent scan.","complexity_before":"O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space beyond the input representation.","complexity_after":"O(n log n) time for comparison-based sorting plus O(n) scan; auxiliary space depends on the sorting implementation and input representation.","tradeoffs":["Sorting changes element order in the working representation.","The optimization is not appropriate when the original ordering must be preserved and cannot be copied.","Hash-based membership may provide expected O(n) behavior for compatible hashable values, but has different memory and semantic tradeoffs."],"edge_cases":["Empty input.","Single-element input.","Values whose equality semantics are nontrivial.","Inputs containing incomparable heterogeneous values may fail during sorting."],"testing_method":"Test empty, singleton, duplicate-containing, duplicate-free, repeated, and large representative inputs; compare results against the original implementation on equivalent valid inputs.","verification_method":"Verify semantic equivalence across representative and boundary inputs and confirm complexity analysis against the actual algorithm used by the runtime.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B043","D39-B047"],"relationships":[{"type":"OPTIMIZES","target":"D39-B004"},{"type":"VERIFIES_SEMANTIC_EQUIVALENCE","target":"D39-B047"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B102","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"dynamic programming","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","title":"Repeated recursive subproblems to memoized computation","objective":"Avoid recomputing identical subproblems in recursive algorithms when overlapping subproblems are present.","performance_problem":"A recursive formulation may evaluate the same subproblem many times, producing exponential or otherwise unnecessarily high runtime.","requirements":["Preserve recurrence semantics.","Use a stable key representing each subproblem state.","Bound memoization memory according to the state space."],"incorrect_implementation":"def fib(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)","corrected_implementation":"def fib(n, memo=None):\n    if memo is None:\n        memo = {}\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    if n not in memo:\n        memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo)\n    return memo[n]","optimization_strategy":"Memoize results for previously evaluated subproblem states.","complexity_before":"Naive Fibonacci recursion has exponential time growth in n.","complexity_after":"O(n) time and O(n) memoization space for the shown recurrence.","tradeoffs":["Memoization consumes memory.","An iterative formulation can avoid recursive call-stack growth and may have lower overhead.","Memoization is useful only when subproblems overlap and their results are reusable."],"edge_cases":["Negative inputs if the API does not define them.","Very large n causing recursion-depth or integer-size considerations.","Mutable or incomplete memoization keys in more complex state spaces."],"testing_method":"Compare memoized and reference implementations for small valid n, boundary values, and representative larger values.","verification_method":"Inspect that each logical subproblem is keyed once and verify recurrence-equivalent outputs.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHMIC_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B064","D39-B069"],"relationships":[{"type":"APPLIES_WORK_AVOIDANCE","target":"D39-B064"},{"type":"REUSES_INTERMEDIATE_RESULTS","target":"D39-B069"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B103","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"graph traversal","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","title":"Repeated graph traversal to shared traversal state","objective":"Prevent redundant graph exploration when multiple queries traverse overlapping reachable nodes.","performance_problem":"Running an independent traversal for every query can repeatedly visit the same graph regions.","requirements":["Preserve reachability semantics.","Track visited state correctly for the intended query scope.","Do not share visited state across independent queries when that changes semantics."],"optimization_strategy":"Reuse precomputed reachability or traversal-derived structures when query semantics permit, rather than repeatedly traversing the same graph.","tradeoffs":["Precomputation consumes memory and startup time.","Dynamic graph mutations can invalidate derive