ent first requests.","verification_method":"Verify readiness criteria accurately represent required initialized state and that failed warm-up does not falsely signal readiness.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance_type":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_PERFORMANCE_KNOWLEDGE","related_records":["D39-B001","D39-B122","D39-B049"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","target":"D39-B001"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","target":"D39-B122"},{"type":"REQUIRES","target":"D39-B049"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B199","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","subtopic":"startup initialization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","title":"Eager initialization of unused components to lazy initialization","objective":"Reduce startup time and initial resource consumption by delaying initialization of components that are not always needed.","performance_problem":"Initializing every optional subsystem at startup can increase startup latency and memory use even when many components are never used.","requirements":["Lazy initialization must be thread-safe.","First-use latency must be acceptable or prewarming must be available.","Initialization failure semantics must be explicit."],"optimization_strategy":"Initialize optional components on first use or through controlled background warm-up when startup cost is material.","tradeoffs":["First-use latency increases.","Concurrency during first initialization requires coordination.","Failures move from startup to request-time unless handled explicitly."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent first use.","Initialization failure and retry.","Component dependencies.","Shutdown during initialization.","Readiness semantics."],"testing_method":"Measure startup time, first-use latency, steady-state performance, memory, and concurrent initialization behavior.","verification_method":"Verify exactly-once or s