{"record_id":"D39-B201","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Query Optimization","subtopic":"Partition Pruning","objective":"Reduce database work by eliminating partitions that cannot contain rows relevant to a query.","requirements":["Query predicates must expose partition-key constraints in a form usable by the optimizer","Partitioning must match the access pattern"],"constraints":["Pruning effectiveness depends on database engine and query-plan rules","Incorrect partitioning can increase planning or maintenance overhead"],"implementation_pattern":"Apply selective predicates on partition keys and verify the execution plan shows only relevant partitions being accessed.","failure_mode":"A query scans many or all partitions despite returning a small subset of data.","root_cause":"The predicate is absent, non-sargable, type-mismatched, wrapped in an optimizer-unfriendly expression, or does not constrain the partition key sufficiently.","optimization":"Rewrite predicates to preserve partition-key visibility and align partition boundaries with common filtering dimensions.","verification_method":"Inspect the actual execution plan and confirm excluded partitions are not scanned; compare representative workloads before and after the change.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database engine optimizer documentation","Database partitioning documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B046","D39-B049"],"relationships":["OPTIMIZES_QUERY_WORK","REQUIRES_PLAN_VALIDATION"]} {"record_id":"D39-B202","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Query Optimization","subtopic":"Covering Index","objective":"Reduce table or heap access when a query can be satisfied directly from an index.","requirements":["The index must contain all columns required by the query or support engine-specific included-column semantics","The index must remain selective and maintainable"],"constraints":["Larger indexes increase storage and write-maintenance cost","The optimizer may still choose another access path"],"implementation_pattern":"Add only the required projected and predicate columns to an appropriate index, then validate the resulting execution plan.","failure_mode":"A query performs additional row lookups after finding matching index entries.","root_cause":"Required output columns are absent from the chosen index, causing additional table or heap access.","optimization":"Use a covering index where workload frequency and lookup cost justify additional index maintenance.","verification_method":"Compare actual plans, logical reads or equivalent engine-specific I/O metrics, latency, and write overhead under representative workloads.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database indexing documentation","Database query optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B008","D39-B062"],"relationships":["REDUCES_RANDOM_IO","TRADES_READ_COST_FOR_WRITE_COST"]} {"record_id":"D39-B203","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Query Optimization","subtopic":"Aggregation Pushdown","objective":"Reduce data transferred and processed by performing aggregations as close as practical to the data source.","requirements":["The pushed aggregation must preserve query semantics","The storage or database layer must support the required aggregation"],"constraints":["Pushdown depends on optimizer and query-engine capabilities","Some expressions cannot be safely pushed down"],"implementation_pattern":"Structure queries and data-access operations so filters and safe aggregations execute at the database or storage layer rather than after full result materialization.","failure_mode":"The application retrieves a large intermediate result and performs an aggregation locally.","root_cause":"Aggregation is expressed or implemented above the data-access boundary despite being executable by the underlying data engine.","optimization":"Push filtering and semantically safe aggregation into the data source.","verification_method":"Inspect the execution plan and measure rows transferred, application CPU, memory consumption, and end-to-end latency.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database optimizer documentation","Relational query processing literature"],"related_records":["D39-B008","D39-B037","D39-B061"],"relationships":["REDUCES_DATA_MOVEMENT","REDUCES_APPLICATION_WORK"]} {"record_id":"D39-B204","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Query Optimization","subtopic":"Materialized View Selection","objective":"Reduce repeated expensive query computation by reusing precomputed relational results.","requirements":["The workload must contain sufficiently repeated or expensive query patterns","Refresh semantics must satisfy application freshness requirements"],"constraints":["Materialized data consumes storage","Refresh work introduces maintenance cost and potential staleness"],"implementation_pattern":"Create a materialized representation for a stable expensive query pattern when measured workload cost justifies refresh and storage overhead.","failure_mode":"The same complex joins or aggregations are recomputed frequently.","root_cause":"No reusable precomputed representation exists for a repeatedly requested result.","optimization":"Introduce a materialized view or equivalent derived structure with an explicit refresh policy.","verification_method":"Measure query latency and resource consumption together with refresh cost and freshness behavior.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database materialized-view documentation","Database performance literature"],"related_records":["D39-B011","D39-B046","D39-B048"],"relationships":["TRADES_REFRESH_COST_FOR_READ_COST","REQUIRES_FRESHNESS_POLICY"]} {"record_id":"D39-B205","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Query Optimization","subtopic":"Prepared Statement Reuse","objective":"Avoid repeated statement preparation overhead when executing structurally identical parameterized queries.","requirements":["The database driver and server must support reusable prepared statements or equivalent mechanisms","Parameterization must preserve query semantics and security"],"constraints":["Caching too many statements can consume server or client resources","Benefits depend on database and driver behavior"],"implementation_pattern":"Reuse parameterized prepared statements when repeated execution of the same statement shape is common.","failure_mode":"High-frequency database operations repeatedly incur statement parsing or preparation work.","root_cause":"Statement lifecycle is recreated for each execution despite stable query structure.","optimization":"Reuse prepared statements according to driver- and database-supported lifecycle rules.","verification_method":"Measure preparation frequency, database CPU, request latency, and resource consumption before and after reuse.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database driver documentation","Database prepared-statement documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B057","D39-B040","D39-B049"],"relationships":["REDUCES_REPEATED_PREPARATION","VERSION_AND_DRIVER_DEPENDENT"]} {"record_id":"D39-B206","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Execution","subtopic":"Plan Cache Reuse","objective":"Reduce repeated query-planning overhead for stable parameterized workloads.","requirements":["The database engine must provide plan reuse or equivalent optimization","Query shape must be stable enough for reuse"],"constraints":["Plan reuse can be harmful when parameter distributions vary significantly","Cache eviction and invalidation behavior is engine-specific"],"implementation_pattern":"Use parameterized, structurally stable statements where the database can safely reuse execution plans.","failure_mode":"Frequent replanning consumes CPU and increases latency.","root_cause":"Statements differ unnecessarily or plans are repeatedly invalidated or unsuitable for reuse.","optimization":"Stabilize statement shapes and inspect plan-cache behavior before changing query structure.","verification_method":"Measure planning time, execution time, plan reuse, and workload variance using engine-specific diagnostics.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database query-planner documentation","Database plan-cache documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B049","D39-B062","D39-B205"],"relationships":["REDUCES_PLANNING_OVERHEAD","CAN_CONFLICT_WITH_PARAMETER_SENSITIVITY"]} {"record_id":"D39-B207","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Statistics","subtopic":"Statistics Freshness","objective":"Improve optimizer plan selection by keeping cardinality statistics representative of current data.","requirements":["The database engine must use statistics for planning","Statistics maintenance must reflect workload characteristics"],"constraints":["Refreshing statistics consumes resources","Freshness requirements vary with data mutation rate"],"implementation_pattern":"Monitor statistics staleness and refresh or configure automatic maintenance when significant data distribution changes occur.","failure_mode":"The optimizer chooses an inefficient execution plan after substantial data distribution changes.","root_cause":"Cardinality or distribution estimates no longer represent current data.","optimization":"Maintain statistics according to data-change characteristics and validate resulting plans.","verification_method":"Compare estimated versus actual cardinalities and inspect plan changes after statistics maintenance.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database statistics documentation","Database optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B201","D39-B206"],"relationships":["IMPROVES_PLAN_SELECTION","MAINTENANCE_HAS_RUNTIME_COST"]} {"record_id":"D39-B208","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Transactions","subtopic":"Transaction Log Overhead","objective":"Reduce unnecessary transaction logging work while preserving durability and correctness requirements.","requirements":["Durability semantics must remain within explicit application requirements","The database must support an appropriate transaction strategy"],"constraints":["Reducing durability guarantees can change correctness guarantees","Larger transactions may increase lock duration and recovery work"],"implementation_pattern":"Separate logically independent work, remove unnecessary writes, and select transaction boundaries based on measured logging and contention costs.","failure_mode":"Write-heavy workloads spend substantial resources generating and flushing transaction-log records.","root_cause":"Excessive write volume, overly granular commits, or unnecessary transactional operations increase logging overhead.","optimization":"Reduce redundant writes and choose transaction boundaries that balance logging, locking, durability, and recovery cost.","verification_method":"Measure log volume, flush behavior, transaction latency, lock duration, and recovery characteristics.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database transaction and logging documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B058","D39-B060","D39-B062"],"relationships":["TRADES_DURABILITY_AND_BATCHING","REQUIRES_CORRECTNESS_VALIDATION"]} {"record_id":"D39-B209","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Maintenance","subtopic":"Vacuum or Garbage-Collection Maintenance","objective":"Prevent accumulated obsolete database storage from degrading read/write efficiency or increasing maintenance work.","requirements":["The selected database engine requires background or explicit storage cleanup","Maintenance policy must match mutation workload"],"constraints":["Maintenance itself consumes I/O and CPU","Aggressive maintenance can interfere with foreground workload"],"implementation_pattern":"Tune or schedule database cleanup according to engine-specific storage-reclamation and workload behavior rather than disabling maintenance blindly.","failure_mode":"Long-running workloads accumulate obsolete storage versions or metadata and experience increasing resource costs.","root_cause":"Storage cleanup falls behind data mutation or is configured without regard to workload characteristics.","optimization":"Align cleanup frequency and resource limits with observed mutation rate and acceptable foreground overhead.","verification_method":"Measure storage growth, cleanup lag, foreground latency, I/O, and maintenance duration.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database storage-maintenance documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B016","D39-B208","D39-B250"],"relationships":["REQUIRES_WORKLOAD_AWARE_MAINTENANCE","TRADES_BACKGROUND_COST_FOR_STORAGE_HEALTH"]} {"record_id":"D39-B210","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Durability","subtopic":"Checkpoint and WAL Tuning","objective":"Control checkpoint or write-ahead-log pressure without weakening required durability guarantees.","requirements":["The database engine must expose relevant checkpoint or WAL configuration","Durability requirements must be explicit"],"constraints":["Aggressive checkpointing can increase write pressure","Deferred checkpointing can increase recovery work or storage requirements","Exact controls are engine-specific"],"implementation_pattern":"Tune checkpoint and WAL parameters only after identifying whether the workload is limited by write amplification, flush pressure, checkpoint bursts, or recovery constraints.","failure_mode":"Periodic checkpoint activity causes latency spikes or sustained write pressure.","root_cause":"Checkpoint behavior is mismatched with workload write rate and storage characteristics.","optimization":"Adjust engine-supported checkpoint/WAL policies based on measured workload and durability constraints.","verification_method":"Measure checkpoint duration, write latency, WAL growth, I/O utilization, and recovery characteristics.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database WAL documentation","Database checkpoint documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B208","D39-B209","D39-B249"],"relationships":["REQUIRES_DURABILITY_PRESERVATION","REDUCES_WRITE_PRESSURE_WHEN_VALIDATED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B211","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Filesystem Performance","subtopic":"Directory Traversal Optimization","objective":"Reduce filesystem overhead when scanning large directory trees.","requirements":["Traversal must preserve required file-selection semantics","Filesystem metadata requirements must be explicit"],"constraints":["Directory layout and filesystem implementation affect traversal cost","Skipping metadata can change behavior"],"implementation_pattern":"Prune irrelevant directories early, avoid repeated traversal, and request only metadata required by the operation.","failure_mode":"Large recursive scans consume excessive time and filesystem metadata operations.","root_cause":"The traversal visits irrelevant paths or repeatedly retrieves metadata for the same entries.","optimization":"Use early path pruning, efficient directory iterators, and controlled metadata access.","verification_method":"Measure files visited, metadata operations, elapsed time, and I/O activity on representative directory structures.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Operating-system filesystem documentation","Language standard-library filesystem documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B036","D39-B064","D39-B070"],"relationships":["REDUCES_METADATA_WORK","PRESERVES_SELECTION_SEMANTICS"]} {"record_id":"D39-B212","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Filesystem I/O","subtopic":"Memory Mapping Versus Read","objective":"Select between memory mapping and explicit reads based on access pattern and working-set behavior.","requirements":["The operating system must support the selected mechanism","Access pattern and lifetime must justify the choice"],"constraints":["Memory mapping does not guarantee physical memory residency","Large mappings can increase address-space and page-fault effects","Explicit reads may be preferable for sequential or small access"],"implementation_pattern":"Use memory mapping for suitable random-access or shared-file workloads only after measuring page-fault and cache behavior against explicit buffered reads.","failure_mode":"Replacing reads with memory mapping increases page faults or latency.","root_cause":"The chosen ac