ilter for high-volume negative lookups.","verification_method":"Measure negative-query rate, filter false-positive rate, backing-store requests, memory, and end-to-end latency.","performance_claim_type":"ESTIMATED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Probabilistic data-structure literature","Bloom filter research"],"related_records":["D39-B005","D39-B064","D39-B243"],"relationships":["REDUCES_NEGATIVE_LOOKUP_COST","FALSE_POSITIVES_ARE_EXPECTED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B245","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Planning","subtopic":"Cardinality Estimation Error","objective":"Identify and correct query-plan regressions caused by inaccurate row-count estimates.","requirements":["Actual execution statistics or equivalent evidence must be available","The database engine must expose estimated and observed cardinality information"],"constraints":["Cardinality errors can originate from correlated columns, skew, stale statistics, or complex predicates","Correcting one estimate may alter other plans"],"implementation_pattern":"Compare estimated and actual row counts at expensive plan nodes and identify the earliest significant estimation divergence.","failure_mode":"The optimizer selects an inefficient join order or access method.","root_cause":"Estimated cardinality differs materially from actual data distribution at a plan decision point.","optimization":"Improve statistics, predicates, schema information, or query structure according to the identified estimation cause.","verification_method":"Compare estimated-versus-actual cardinalities and validate plan stability across representative parameter distributions.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database optimizer documentation","Query optimization literature"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B207","D39-B206"],"relationships":["DIAGNOSES_PLAN_REGRESSION","REQUIRES_ACTUAL_PLAN_EVIDENCE"]} {"record_id":"D39-B246","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Storage Performance","subtopic":"Read-Ahead Strategy","objective":"Improve sequential or predictable access by allowing storage layers to prefetch data likely to be consumed next.","requirements":["Access pattern must exhibit predictable locality","Storage and operating-system layers must support read-ahead or equivalent behavior"],"constraints":["Excessive prefetching wastes I/O bandwidth and memory","Random workloads may receive little benefit"],"implementation_pattern":"Use or tune read-ahead only when measurements show sequential or predictable access and the prefetch volume matches the working set.","failure_mode":"Sequential access underutilizes available storage bandwidth or performs many small reads.","root_cause":"The system fetches data too conservatively for a predictable access pattern.","optimization":"Use suitable buffering or read-ahead and validate that prefetched data is actually consumed.","verification_method":"Measure I/O request size, cache hit behavior, bandwidth, memory usage, and latency.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Operating-system storage documentation","Filesystem documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B039","D39-B212","D39-B216"],"relationships":["ACCESS_PATTERN_DEPENDENT","PREFETCH_WASTE_MUST_BE_MEASURED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B247","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Storage Performance","subtopic":"Storage Queue Depth","objective":"Match outstanding storage operations to device and workload capacity without creating excessive latency.","requirements":["Storage device characteristics must be known or measurable","Workload must expose controllable I/O concurrency"],"constraints":["Higher queue depth can improve throughput but increase latency","Optimal depth varies by device and access pattern"],"implementation_pattern":"Tune outstanding I/O concurrency using measured latency-throughput curves rather than maximizing queue depth.","failure_mode":"Storage throughput is poor at low concurrency or latency becomes excessive at high concurrency.","root_cause":"Outstanding I/O does not match the storage device's effective parallelism.","optimization":"Select a queue depth that satisfies the workload's latency and throughput objectives.","verification_method":"Measure IOPS or throughput, latency percentiles, queue occupancy, and device utilization across queue-depth settings.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Storage-device documentation","Operating-system I/O documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B023","D39-B039","D39-B246"],"relationships":["LATENCY_THROUGHPUT_TRADEOFF","HARDWARE_DEPENDENT"]} {"record_id":"D39-B248","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"CPU Environment","subtopic":"CPU Frequency Scaling","objective":"Avoid misinterpreting CPU frequency variation as an application-level optimization or regression.","requirements":["Performance environment must record CPU frequency behavior when relevant","Comparisons must use sufficiently stable workloads"],"constraints":["Modern systems dynamically adjust frequency based on load, temperature, and power policy","Frequency control can be restricted by operating-system or platform policy"],"implementation_pattern":"Record CPU frequency and power-policy context during performance experiments when CPU-bound results are sensitive to frequency scaling.","failure_mode":"Identical application builds show materially different runtime across benchmark runs.","root_cause":"CPU frequency, power state, or thermal conditions changed between measurements.","optimization":"Control or record relevant CPU power and frequency conditions and repeat measurements sufficiently to distinguish application changes from environment effects.","verification_method":"Correlate runtime measurements with CPU frequency, utilization, temperature, and power-state evidence.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Operating-system power-management documentation","Processor performance documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B049","D39-B219","D39-B249"],"relationships":["ENVIRONMENTAL_CONFUNDER","HARDWARE_DEPENDENT"]} {"record_id":"D39-B249","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Thermal Performance","subtopic":"Thermal Throttling","objective":"Detect performance degradation caused by thermal constraints rather than application changes.","requirements":["Temperature and frequency telemetry must be available or measurable","The workload must be sufficiently long to expose thermal effects"],"constraints":["Thermal behavior depends on hardware, cooling, ambient conditions, and power policy","Short tests may not expose throttling"],"implementation_pattern":"Include thermal state and sustained frequency behavior in long-running performance experiments when hardware is thermally constrained.","failure_mode":"Throughput declines during sustained execution despite unchanged software and workload.","root_cause":"Thermal limits cause the processor or device to reduce operating frequency or power.","optimization":"Improve cooling or workload scheduling only when evidence confirms thermal throttling; do not attribute the effect to code without environmental evidence.","verification_method":"Correlate performance over time with temperature, frequency, and power telemetry.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"M