by unnecessary database sorting.","requirements":["Required result ordering must be explicitly identified","An equivalent ordered access path must exist or be safely introduced"],"constraints":["Removing a required sort changes semantics","An index that provides ordering can increase write and storage cost"],"implementation_pattern":"Determine whether an existing index or naturally ordered access path can satisfy the required ordering without a separate sort operation.","failure_mode":"A large intermediate result is fully sorted before being returned.","root_cause":"The query requires ordering that is not supported by the selected access path.","optimization":"Use an appropriate ordered index or query structure when measured sort cost justifies the maintenance overhead.","verification_method":"Inspect the actual plan for sort operations and compare CPU, memory, temporary I/O, and latency before and after the change.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database indexing documentation","Database query optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B043","D39-B066"],"relationships":["REDUCES_SORT_WORK","TRADES_READ_COST_FOR_WRITE_COST"]} {"record_id":"D39-B253","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Predicate Pushdown","objective":"Reduce intermediate data volume by evaluating selective predicates as close to the data source as semantics permit.","requirements":["Predicate movement must preserve query semantics","The execution engine must support predicate pushdown or equivalent optimization"],"constraints":["Outer joins, nondeterministic expressions, aggregation, and user-defined functions can restrict safe pushdown","Optimizer behavior is engine-specific"],"implementation_pattern":"Place selective source-level predicates at the earliest semantically valid boundary and verify the actual plan performs filtering before expensive joins, transfers, or materialization.","failure_mode":"Large intermediate datasets are produced before filtering occurs.","root_cause":"Filtering is applied at a higher layer or cannot be recognized by the optimizer.","optimization":"Push safe predicates toward the scan or storage boundary.","verification_method":"Compare rows scanned, rows transferred, plan operators, CPU, memory, and end-to-end latency.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database optimizer documentation","Query processing literature"],"related_records":["D39-B008","D39-B061","D39-B203"],"relationships":["REDUCES_INTERMEDIATE_DATA","SEMANTIC_PRESERVATION_REQUIRED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B254","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Window Function Cost","objective":"Reduce unnecessary sorting, partitioning, and repeated processing caused by expensive window-function workloads.","requirements":["Window semantics must remain unchanged","Required partitioning and ordering must be understood"],"constraints":["Window functions can require substantial sorting or buffering","Replacing a window operation with another method may alter semantics"],"implementation_pattern":"Inspect window-function plan nodes and reduce unnecessary input rows, align indexes with required partition/order patterns where beneficial, or eliminate redundant window computations.","failure_mode":"A query spends substantial resources sorting or buffering rows for window calculations.","root_cause":"Large unfiltered input, incompatible ordering, or repeated window expressions increases processing cost.","optimization":"Reduce input volume and redundant computation before considering specialized indexing or query rewrites.","verification_method":"Measure rows entering the window operation, sort cost, memory usage, temporary I/O, and latency.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database window-function documentation","Database query optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B203","D39-B252","D39-B053"],"relationships":["QUERY_SHAPE_DEPENDENT","SEMANTIC_EQUIVALENCE_REQUIRED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B255","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Recursive Query Expansion","objective":"Control performance costs of recursive queries by limiting unnecessary expansion and repeated traversal.","requirements":["Termination conditions must be explicit","Required reachable-result semantics must be preserved"],"constraints":["Graph depth and branching factor can cause rapid growth","Recursive query behavior differs across database engines"],"implementation_pattern":"Apply safe predicates and explicit termination conditions early, avoid revisiting equivalent states where the engine supports deduplication, and validate recursive expansion size.","failure_mode":"Recursive query execution grows dramatically with graph depth or branching.","root_cause":"Broad recursive expansion traverses irrelevant nodes or repeatedly explores equivalent paths.","optimization":"Constrain recursion using selective predicates, depth limits, cycle handling, and workload-appropriate query structure.","verification_method":"Measure rows generated per recursion level, execution time, memory, temporary storage, and termination behavior.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database recursive-query documentation","Graph query processing literature"],"related_records":["D39-B064","D39-B203","D39-B245"],"relationships":["EXPANSION_BOUND_REQUIRED","GRAPH_SHAPE_DEPENDENT"]} {"record_id":"D39-B256","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Join Input Reduction","objective":"Reduce join cost by shrinking inputs before expensive relational operations.","requirements":["Pre-join filtering must preserve semantics","Selective predicates must be available before the join"],"constraints":["Predicate movement across outer joins or nullable expressions can change results","Optimization depends on actual data selectivity"],"implementation_pattern":"Apply semantically safe selective filters before joining large relations rather than filtering the joined result afterward.","failure_mode":"Large relations are joined and only then reduced to a small final result.","root_cause":"Filtering occurs after the expensive relational expansion.","optimization":"Reduce join inputs before the join when semantics and optimizer behavior permit.","verification_method":"Compare input cardinalities, join work, memory, I/O, and execution latency using actual plans.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Relational algebra literature","Database optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B253","D39-B061","D39-B251"],"relationships":["REDUCES_JOIN_WORK","OUTER_JOIN_SEMANTICS_MUST_BE_PRESERVED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B257","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Temporary Table Avoidance","objective":"Avoid unnecessary intermediate persistence when a computation can safely remain within a query plan or streaming pipeline.","requirements":["Intermediate materialization must not be required for correctness or transaction semantics","Equivalent execution must preserve visibility and ordering requirements"],"constraints":["Materialization can intentionally improve reuse or isolate expensive computation","Removing it may increase repeated work"],"implementation_pattern":"Determine whether an intermediate table exists only because of implementation convenience and compare it with a directly composable query or pipeline.","failure_mode":"Large intermediate data is written and reread despite being consumed only once.","root_cause":"The pipeline materializes transient data without a measured reuse or isolation benefit.","optimization":"Eliminate unnecessary materialization when the direct execution plan has lower measured total cost.","verification_method":"Measure temporary storage I/O, memory, CPU, execution time, and repeated computation after removing materialization.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_REQUIRED","validation_status":"METHODOLOGY_RULE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database query-processing documentation","Data-processing architecture literature"],"related_records":["D39-B017","D39-B203","D39-B256"],"relationships":["TRADES_MATERIALIZATION_FOR_RECOMPUTATION","WORKLOAD_DEPENDENT"]} {"record_id":"D39-B258","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Distinct Operation Cost","objective":"Reduce unnecessary duplicate-elimination work in queries.","requirements":["Duplicate semantics must be understood","Removing DISTINCT must not change externally required results"],"constraints":["DISTINCT may be semantically required","Indexes or uniqueness constraints may make duplicate elimination cheaper"],"implementation_pattern":"Determine whether upstream relational operations already guarantee uniqueness before retaining an explicit duplicate-elimination step.","failure_mode":"A large result is sorted or hashed solely to remove duplicates that cannot exist.","root_cause":"Duplicate elimination is requested without recognizing an existing uniqueness guarantee.","optimization":"Remove redundant duplicate elimination or exploit an existing uniqueness property when semantically safe.","verification_method":"Prove the uniqueness condition and inspect the execution plan for removed or reduced sort/hash work.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Relational algebra literature","Database query optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B067","D39-B252","D39-B253"],"relationships":["REQUIRES_SEMANTIC_PROOF","REDUCES_SORT_OR_HASH_WORK"]} {"record_id":"D39-B259","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Join Key Data Type Alignment","objective":"Avoid conversion and inefficient access caused by incompatible join-key data types.","requirements":["Equivalent logical key domains must be identified","Schema changes must preserve compatibility and data correctness"],"constraints":["Changing stored types can require migration","Implicit conversion behavior varies by database engine"],"implementation_pattern":"Align join-key data types and avoid runtime conversion on indexed join columns when schema control permits.","failure_mode":"An indexed join performs scans or expensive conversions despite an apparently suitable index.","root_cause":"Join operands use incompatible types, causing implicit conversion or preventing efficient index use.","optimization":"Use compatible types at schema and query boundaries or perform explicit, appropriately placed conversions outside the indexed access path.","verification_method":"Inspect actual plans, conversion operators, index usage, CPU, and latency before and after alignment.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database data-type and indexing documentation","Database optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B245","D39-B253"],"relationships":["SCHEMA_DEPENDENT","INDEX_USAGE_SENSITIVE"]} {"record_id":"D39-B260","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Expression Sargability","objective":"Preserve efficient index or partition access by expressing predicates in optimizer-friendly forms.","requirements":["The rewritten predicate must be semantically equivalent","The target database must support the resulting access pattern"],"constraints":["Not every expression has an equivalent sargable rewrite","Function behavior around nulls, collation, time zones, or precision must be preserved"],"implementation_pattern":"Avoid wrapping indexed columns in functions when a semantically equivalent transformation can place the expression on the parameter side.","failure_mode":"An indexed column is scanned despite a selective predicate.","root_cause":"A function, calculation, cast, or transformation prevents the optimizer from using the index efficiently.","optimization":"Rewrite the predicate into an index-compatible form when correctness permits.","verification_method":"Compare actual access paths, rows examined, CPU, and latency and test boundary values for semantic equivalence.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Database indexing documentation","Database optimizer documentation"],"related_records":["D39-B007","D39-B201","D39-B259"],"relationships":["PRESERVES_INDEX_ACCESS","SEMANTIC_EQUIVALENCE_REQUIRED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B261","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Database Performance","subtopic":"Result Cache Scope","objective":"Reuse expensive query results only within a cache scope whose consistency and invalidation semantics are safe.","requirements":["Result identity and freshness requirements must be defined","Cache scope must not expose data across unauthorized contexts"],"constraints":["Broader scope can improve reuse but increase staleness or isolation risk","User-specific results cannot be safely shared without complete context identity"],"implementation_pattern":"Define result-cache keys from all semantically relevant inputs and select the narrowest scope that provides meaningful reuse.","failure_mode":"A result cache has low hit rate or returns stale or incorrectly shared results.","root_cause":"Cache scope or key omits relevant query context, tenant, authorization, or freshness information.","optimization":"Correct cache identity and scope before increasing cache capacity.","verification_method":"Test cache-key completeness, authorization isolation, invalidation behavior, hit rate, and latency.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED_PATTERN","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_basis":["Caching-system documentation","Distributed-systems caching literature"],"related_records":["D39-B011","D39-B012","D39-B241"],"relationships":["PERFORMANCE_AND_ISOLATION_CONSTRAINT","CACHE_IDENTITY_REQUIRED"]} {"record_id":"D39-B262","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","topic":"Caching","subtopic":"Negative Cache Invalidation","objective":"Reduce repeated expensive lookups for absent resources while preventing stale absence decisions after resource creation.","requirements":["Absence results must be cacheable under explicit freshness rules","Creation or restoration events must invalidate relevant negative entries"],"constraints":["Negative entries can become incorrect when resources appear","Long TTLs improve hit rate but extend stale absence"],"implementation_pattern":"Cache confirmed absence only for a bounded period and invalidate the negative result when the underlying resource can become available.","failure_mode":"Repeated misses overload a backend, or newly created resources remain invisible until a long negative-cache TTL expires.","root_cause":"No negative caching exists