s and protocols","TLS or transformation layers can prevent direct zero-copy","Reduced CPU cost does not guarantee lower latency for every workload"],"failure_mode":"Large file transfers consume excessive CPU because data is repeatedly copied through user space.","diagnosis":"Profile CPU consumption and copy-related system activity during representative transfers.","optimization":"Use a supported zero-copy transfer path when measured profiling shows copy overhead is material.","verification_method":"Compare throughput, CPU utilization, memory bandwidth, and correctness across representative file sizes and network conditions.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_OR_UNKNOWN","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Operating-system and runtime API documentation.","related_records":["D39-B213","D39-B037"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B213"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B037"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B315","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Filesystem Overlay Copy-Up Overhead","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","objective":"Identify performance costs caused by copy-up behavior in layered or overlay filesystems.","principle":"A write to a lower-layer file can require copying data into an upper layer, adding I/O and storage overhead.","requirements":["Deployment must actually use an overlay or layered filesystem","Copy-up behavior must be observable or documented for the target environment"],"constraints":["Behavior differs by filesystem and container runtime","Changing storage layout can affect durability and deployment semantics"],"failure_mode":"Write-heavy workloads perform unexpectedly poorly inside layered storage despite acceptable native-filesystem performance.","diagnosis":"Compare write latency and I/O activity between overlay and appropriate native storage while observing copy-up events where supported.","optimization":"Place write-intensive or mutable data on storage de