oth aggregate and stratified results and confirm class-specific changes against predefined thresholds.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Performance measurement methodology and workload-specific evidence.","related_records":["D39-B296","D39-B350"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B296"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B350"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B395","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Performance Evidence Retention","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","objective":"Preserve sufficient benchmark, profile, environment, and workload evidence to reproduce and audit optimization decisions.","requirements":["Measurement artifacts must have stable identifiers","Environment and workload metadata must be retained","Baseline and optimized results must remain linked"],"constraints":["Raw profiling artifacts can be large","Retention policies may limit historical depth","Sensitive workload data may require sanitization"],"failure_mode":"A claimed optimization cannot later be reproduced or independently assessed because its evidence was discarded.","diagnosis":"Check whether baseline, optimized measurements, environment identity, workload definition, and analysis artifacts are recoverable.","optimization":"Retain compact but sufficient evidence packages with provenance and integrity metadata.","verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct the optimization decision from retained artifacts and identify missing evidence.","performance_claim_type":"VERIFIED_PROCESS","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Engineering evidence-management methodology.","related_records":["D39-B049","D39-B250"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B049"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B250"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B396","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Optimization Rollout Canarying","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","objective":"Limit risk from performance optimizations by evaluating them on a controlled subset of representative traffic before broad rollout.","requirements":["Traffic segmentation must be available","Baseline and optimized cohorts must be comparable","Rollback mechanism must exist"],"constraints":["Small cohorts may have insufficient statistical power","Traffic differences can confound comparison","Some optimizations affect shared infrastructure and cannot be isolated cleanly"],"failure_mode":"An optimization that passes synthetic tests causes production latency or resource regressions after full deployment.","diagnosis":"Compare matched baseline and optimized cohorts across latency, throughput, errors, and resource consumption.","optimization":"Use staged rollout with explicit acceptance and rollback criteria when production behavior cannot be fully represented in preproduction.","verification_method":"Evaluate predefined metrics across the canary cohort and require evidence before expansion.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Progressive-delivery methodology and production performance measurements.","related_records":["D39-B048","D39-B350"],"relationships":[{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B048"},{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B350"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B397","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Optimization Rollback Trigger","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","objective":"Define measurable conditions under which a performance optimization must be reverted because its observed costs outweigh its benefits.","requirements":["Predefined performance and correctness criteria","Monitoring capable of detecting violations","Rollback path must be tested"],"constraints":["Thresholds must account for normal variance","Some regressions emerge only under specific workload classes","Rollback itself can temporarily affect performance"],"failure_mode":"A performance optimization remains deployed despite measurable degradation because rollback criteria were undefined.","diagnosis":"Compare observed metrics with predefined acceptance thresholds and affected workload classes.","optimization":"Encode explicit rollback triggers for material latency, throughput, resource, error, or correctness regressions.","verification_method":"Exercise rollback procedures and verify that the triggering condition and recovery behavior are observable and repeatable.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Performance governance methodology and controlled rollout evidence.","related_records":["D39-B048","D39-B396"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B048"},{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B396"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B398","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Optimization Interaction Effects","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","objective":"Detect cases where individually beneficial optimizations interact and produce a different combined performance result.","requirements":["Optimizations must have identifiable changes","Combined workload measurement must be possible","Correctness equivalence must remain established"],"constraints":["Interaction space grows rapidly with many changes","One optimization can remove the benefit of another","Shared caches, plans, scheduling, and resource limits create nonlinear effects"],"failure_mode":"Several individually validated optimizations produce a combined regression when deployed together.","diagnosis":"Measure each change independently and in relevant combinations, then inspect altered resource bottlenecks.","optimization":"Retain combinations supported by end-to-end evidence and remove or reorder changes whose interaction causes unacceptable behavior.","verification_method":"Run factorial or staged experiments appropriate to the number of interacting changes and compare end-to-end metrics.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Experimental performance methodology and controlled workload evidence.","related_records":["D39-B046","D39-B391"],"relationships":[{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B046"},{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B391"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B399","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Optimization Complexity Budget","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","objective":"Prevent performance optimizations from introducing disproportionate implementation and maintenance complexity relative to measured benefit.","requirements":["Measured performance benefit must exist","Operational and maintenance cost must be assessed","Correctness and security impact must be reviewed"],"constraints":["Small gains can matter at very high scale","Complexity costs are difficult to quantify precisely","Future workload changes can invalidate current gains"],"failure_mode":"A micro-optimization substantially increases code complexity while producing negligible or unstable performance improvement.","diagnosis":"Compare measured benefit with added code paths, testing burden, operational complexity, and future maintenance risk.","optimization":"Prefer simpler changes when measured performance benefit is immaterial; retain complexity only when the evidence justifies it.","verification_method":"Record before/after performance evidence and review the added complexity against explicit acceptance criteria.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED_AND_EVALUATIVE","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Engineering optimization governance; benefit and complexity must be established from project evidence.","related_records":["D39-B046","D39-B048"],"relationships":[{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B046"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B048"}]} {"record_id":"D39-B400","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"End-to-End Performance Closure With Operational Evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","objective":"Close an optimization cycle only after demonstrating that the change improves the intended end-to-end metric without introducing unacceptable correctness, security, reliability, or resource regressions.","requirements":["Original performance problem must be recorded","Optimization must have explicit hypothesis","Representative measurements before and after","Correctness and relevant safety checks","Operational acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["Improvement in one component may not improve end-to-end behavior","Production workload can differ from benchmark workload","Some long-term effects require continued monitoring"],"procedure":["State the original bottleneck and evidence","Apply the optimization","Validate correctness and safety","Measure component and end-to-end performance","Compare against predefined acceptance criteria","Deploy progressively where appropriate","Monitor post-deployment behavior","Record outcome and unresolved limitations"],"verification_method":"Require linked baseline, implementation, test, measurement, rollout, and post-deployment evidence before marking the optimization closed.","performance_claim_type":"MEASURED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"Evidence-driven performance-engineering lifecycle and project-specific validation artifacts.","related_records":["D39-B050","D39-B350","D39-B396","D39-B397"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B050"},{"type":"DEEPENS","target":"D39-B350"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B396"},{"type":"RELATED_TO","target":"D39-B397"}]}

{"record_id":"D39-B401","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B81","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Adaptive concurrency limit optimization","problem":"A service permits an unnecessarily high number of concurrent requests, causing queue growth, contention, downstream saturation, and tail-latency degradation.","objective":"Determine and enforce a concurrency limit based on measured workload behavior rather than an arbitrary static value.","principle":"Concurrency should be bounded near the operating region where additional parallelism no longer improves useful throughput and instead increases queueing or contention.","optimization_strategy":"Measure throughput, utilization, queue depth, error rate, and latency while varying concurrency; select a limit that satisfies the service objectives with headroom.","constraints":["The limit must not be selected from a single short benchmark.","Downstream capacity must be considered.","Latency percentiles and error rates must be evaluated together with throughput."],"expected_benefit":"Reduced saturation-driven latency and resource contention while preserving useful throughput.","verification_method":"Compare controlled workloads across multiple concurrency limits using representative traffic and verify that the selected limit remains within defined latency, throughput, and error budgets.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D39-B019","D39-B353","D39-B376","D39-B398"]} {"record_id":"D39-B402","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B81","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Adaptive rate limiting for overload prevention","problem":"Incoming request rate can exceed sustainable service capacity, producing growing queues, timeouts, retries, and cascading resource consumption.","objective":"Control admitted work so offered load remains within measured sustainable capacity.","principle":"Admission rate should be constrained using observed capacity and service objectives rather than allowing unlimited accumulation of work.","optimization_strategy":"Use measured service capacity, queue state, latency, and error signals to adapt admission limits; reject, defer, or shed excess work according to explicit policy.","constraints":["Rejected work must have an explicit client-visible outcome.","Rate limits must not hide a genuine capacity requirement.","Control behavior must be tested under sudden traffic increases and recovery."],"expected_benefit":"Lower overload amplification and more predictable latency under demand spikes.","verification_method":"Inject controlled overload and verify bounded queue growth, bounded resource utilization, predictable rejection behavior, and recovery after demand falls.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D39-B023","D39-B353","D39-B356","D39-B373"]} {"record_id":"D39-B403","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B81","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Work-conserving admission optimization","problem":"A system can leave capacity idle because admission or scheduling rules unnecessarily restrict work even when resources are available.","objective":"Use available capacity without violating concurrency, fairness, dependency, or latency constraints.","principle":"Capacity should remain usable when safe work is available, but optimization must distinguish idle capacity from intentionally reserved headroom.","optimization_strategy":"Measure resource utilization, runnable work, queue state, and service-level outcomes; relax unnecessary admission restrictions while retaining explicit safety limits.","constraints":["Reserved capacity must not be mistaken for wasted capacity.","Fairness constraints may intentionally prevent maximum instantaneous utilization.","The change must not increase tail latency beyond accepted limits."],"expected_benefit":"Higher useful utilization without uncontrolled queue growth.","verification_method":"Compare equivalent workloads before and after the admission change and correlate utilization with throughput, queueing, latency, and error metrics.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D39-B359","D39-B375","D39-B400"]} {"record_id":"D39-B404","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B81","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Workload-aware resource partitioning","problem":"Heterogeneous workloads compete for the same resources even though their CPU, memory, I/O, and latency characteristics differ substantially.","objective":"Prevent one workload class from degrading the performance of unrelated workload classes.","principle":"Resource allocation should reflect workload characteristics and service objectives when shared-resource contention is measurable.","optimization_strategy":"Classify workloads by measured resource behavior and isolate or proportion resources according to demand and priority requirements.","constraints":["Partitioning introduces resource fragmentation.","Workload classification must be based on observable behavior.","Unused reservations should not permanently consume scarce capacity."],"expected_benefit":"More predictable performance and reduced cross-workload interference.","verification_method":"Run mixed workloads and compare per-class latency, throughput, utilization, queueing, and resource consumption before and after partitioning.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D39-B357","D39-B360","D39-B398"]} {"record_id":"D39-B405","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B81","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Latency budget decomposition","problem":"An end-to-end latency target exists, but optimization effort cannot identify which internal stages consume the available budget.","objective":"Allocate measurable latency contributions across processing stages and dependencies.","principle":"End-to-end latency should be decomposed into observable components before optimizing individual stages.","optimization_strategy":"Instrument stage boundaries and dependency calls, calculate contribution distributions, and prioritize components that materially consume the latency budget.","constraints":["Instrumentation overhead must be accounted for.","Parallel operations cannot always be summed directly.","Percentile decomposition requires careful interpretation."],"expected_benefit":"More targeted optimization and reduced effort on components with negligible end-to-end impact.","verification_method":"Compare stage-level timing distributions with end-to-end latency distributions and verify that changes to a targeted stage produce the expected system-level effect.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance_type":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D39-B002","D39-B024","D39-B355"]} {"record_id":"D39-B406","dataset_id":"D39","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Problem → Optimization","batch":"B82","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","concept":"Critical-path latency optimization","problem":"Several operations execute concurrently, but optimization focuses on expensive operations that do not materially affect completion time.","objective":"Optimize operations that lie on the effective completion critical path.","principle":"For parallel workloads, re