{"record_id":"D40-B001","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B001","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Vulnerability identification","concept":"Security remediation begins by establishing that a security-relevant weakness exists and identifying its affected asset or component.","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a software security problem be converted into a remediation task?","answer":"Identify the weakness, affected component, exposure boundary, preconditions, evidence, impact, root cause, remediation option, verification criteria, and regression protection.","explanation":"A remediation decision should be evidence-driven rather than based only on a vulnerability label or scanner finding.","principle":"Remediation quality depends on correctly connecting the observed security condition to its technical root cause and a verifiable corrective action.","requirements":["Identify affected component","Preserve evidence","Determine exposure and preconditions","Define remediation and verification criteria"],"constraints":["Do not infer exploitability solely from a vulnerability identifier","Do not claim remediation without verification"],"expected_behavior":"A security finding becomes a traceable remediation chain.","security_implication":"Incorrect diagnosis can result in ineffective remediation or unnecessary changes.","testing_guidance":"Test the vulnerable condition before remediation and the intended secure behavior after remediation.","verification_method":"Trace finding → evidence → root cause → remediation → security test → regression result.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["MITRE CWE root-cause and weakness model","OWASP ASVS security verification model"],"related_records":["D40-B002","D40-B003","D40-B005"],"relationships":{"extends":["D40-B002"],"requires":["D40-B003"],"verifies":["D40-B005"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B002","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B001","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Attack surface","concept":"Attack-surface mapping","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should be mapped before selecting remediation?","answer":"Map externally reachable interfaces, trust boundaries, data flows, privileged operations, dependencies, and security-sensitive resources relevant to the finding.","explanation":"A vulnerability may have different remediation requirements depending on where the affected component sits in the attack surface.","requirements":["Identify entry points","Identify trust boundaries","Identify sensitive resources","Identify affected dependencies"],"expected_behavior":"The remediation analysis identifies the security boundary affected by the problem.","security_implication":"Missing an exposed interface or trust boundary can leave the underlying weakness reachable after remediation.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary-specific security tests for each affected entry point.","verification_method":"Compare documented attack-surface elements with deployed interfaces and security-test coverage.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP ASVS","OWASP Developer Guide ASVS"],"related_records":["D40-B001","D40-B003","D40-B010"],"relationships":{"depends_on":["D40-B001"],"requires":["D40-B003"],"related_to":["D40-B010"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B003","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B001","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Preconditions","concept":"Security precondition analysis","knowledge_type":"ANALYSIS","question":"Why must remediation reasoning identify preconditions?","answer":"Because a weakness may only become security-relevant when specific inputs, privileges, configuration states, network reachability, or environmental conditions exist.","explanation":"Separating the weakness from its activation conditions prevents both overstatement and underestimation of risk.","requirements":["Identify required attacker-controlled inputs where applicable","Identify required privileges","Identify configuration prerequisites","Identify environmental prerequisites"],"expected_behavior":"The record distinguishes the existence of a weakness from the conditions required for security impact.","security_implication":"Ignoring preconditions can cause remediation to target the wrong boundary or incorrectly prioritize the finding.","testing_guidance":"Construct authorized security tests that establish or invalidate each relevant precondition.","verification_method":"For each claimed precondition, identify observable evidence demonstrating whether it exists.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["MITRE CWE","OWASP ASVS"],"related_records":["D40-B001","D40-B004"],"relationships":{"requires":["D40-B001"],"refines":["D40-B004"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B004","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B001","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Evidence","concept":"Evidence-backed security diagnosis","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should support a remediation decision?","answer":"Use reproducible observations such as source-code location, configuration state, logs, security-test results, dependency metadata, or controlled reproduction evidence.","explanation":"A scanner result or theoretical possibility is not equivalent to demonstrated impact or root cause.","requirements":["Record evidence source","Record observation","Record affected component","Preserve reproducibility information where possible"],"expected_behavior":"Another engineer can inspect the evidence and understand why remediation was selected.","security_implication":"Weak evidence can produce false remediation and leave the actual security weakness unresolved.","verification_method":"Check that each major diagnosis claim maps to explicit evidence.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["MITRE CWE","OWASP ASVS"],"related_records":["D40-B003","D40-B005"],"relationships":{"requires":["D40-B003"],"verifies":["D40-B005"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B005","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B001","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Remediation chain","concept":"Security remediation traceability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the canonical D40 remediation chain?","answer":"Vulnerability → Attack Surface → Preconditions → Evidence → Impact → Root Cause → Remediation → Verification → Regression.","explanation":"The chain provides a deterministic structure for turning security findings into verified corrective actions.","expected_behavior":"Every important remediation record can be traced from an observed security problem to evidence of corrected behavior.","security_implication":"Breaking traceability makes remediation difficult to validate and regressions easier to introduce.","verification_method":"Audit every stage and mark unavailable evidence as UNKNOWN rather than inventing it.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D40 authoritative scope specification","OWASP ASVS","MITRE CWE"],"related_records":["D40-B001","D40-B002","D40-B003","D40-B004"],"relationships":{"generalizes":["D40-B001","D40-B002","D40-B003","D40-B004"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B006","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B002","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Input validation","concept":"Missing server-side input validation","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","objective":"Reject malformed or unexpected input at a trusted service boundary.","requirements":["Validate type and permitted range before security-sensitive processing"],"constraints":["Validation must occur on the trusted server side"],"correct_example":"Use explicit type/range validation before processing.","source_code":"def process_age(age):\n    if not isinstance(age, int) or not 0 <= age <= 120:\n        raise ValueError('invalid age')\n    return age","invalid_example":"def process_age(age):\n    return int(age)","incorrect_code":"def process_age(age):\n    return int(age)","corrected_code":"def process_age(age):\n    if not isinstance(age, int) or not 0 <= age <= 120:\n        raise ValueError('invalid age')\n    return age","failure_mode":"Malformed or unexpected input reaches application logic without enforcing the intended contract.","root_cause":"The trusted service boundary lacks validation of input type and allowed range.","testing_method":"Unit and negative tests.","test_input":"None, -1, 121, '30', 30","expected_test_result":"Only integer values within the defined range are accepted.","verification_method":"Execute validation tests and inspect the service boundary for server-side enforcement.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","security_implication":"Boundary validation reduces exposure caused by malformed input but must not be treated as a complete security control.","performance_implication":"Validation adds small processing cost; avoid unnecessary repeated validation after the trusted boundary.","provenance":["OWASP ASVS V5 validation requirements"],"related_records":["D40-B007","D40-B008"],"relationships":{"corrects":["D40-B006"],"tested_by":["D40-B007"],"verifies":["D40-B008"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B007","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B002","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Input validation testing","concept":"Negative security tests for validation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","objective":"Verify that invalid input is rejected by the trusted boundary.","test_case":"Call process_age with None, negative, over-range, and wrong-type values.","test_input":"[None,-1,121,\"30\"]","expected_test_result":"Each invalid value raises the defined validation error.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed values separately so failure evidence identifies which validation rule is missing.","verification_method":"Execute the negative test suite and confirm rejection occurs before security-sensitive processing.","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP ASVS","D40-B006"],"related_records":["D40-B006","D40-B008"],"relationships":{"tests":["D40-B006"],"verifies":["D40-B008"]}}
{"record_id":"D40-B008","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B002","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Validation verification","concept":"Verification of security boundary enforcement","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should server-side validation remediation be verified?","answer":"Verify both rejection of invalid inputs and acceptance of valid inputs at the trusted boundary, then confirm the validated value is the only value used downstream.","explanation":"Testing only rejection cases does not prove valid behavior or downstream use of the validated representation.","verification_method":"Review implementation and execute positive and negative tests; inspect the data path from boundary validation to security-sensitive operation.","validation_status":"STRUCTURALLY_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP ASVS"],"related_records":["D40-B006","D40-B007"],"relationships":{"verifies":["D40-B006","D40-B007"]}}
