{"record_id":"D40-B101","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"SQL injection caused by dynamic query construction","question":"How should an application remediate SQL injection caused by concatenating untrusted input into SQL statements?","answer":"Use parameterized queries or prepared statements so untrusted values remain data rather than becoming SQL syntax. Apply allowlist validation where the input has a constrained business format, and enforce database least privilege as defense in depth.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from altering SQL query structure.","requirements":["Separate SQL code from parameter values","Use parameter binding supported by the database API","Restrict database privileges","Validate constrained input formats"],"constraints":["Validation must not be treated as the primary substitute for parameterization","Existing query semantics must be preserved"],"preconditions":["Application accepts externally influenced input","Input is incorporated into a database query"],"input":"Untrusted database query parameter","output":"Parameterized database operation","expected_behavior":"Input values are transmitted as parameters and cannot modify the intended SQL statement structure.","failure_mode":"Dynamic SQL concatenation allows input to alter query syntax.","root_cause":"Application constructs SQL by combining code and untrusted data in the same string.","solution":"Replace string concatenation with prepared statements or parameterized query APIs and reduce database privileges.","edge_cases":["Numeric identifiers","Search strings containing quotes","Unicode input","Empty input","Unexpected SQL metacharacters"],"security_implications":"Successful exploitation can expose, modify, or delete database data and may sometimes enable further compromise depending on database privileges.","performance_implications":"Prepared statements can also improve repeated-query efficiency, but performance behavior depends on the driver and database.","testing":{"method":"Security regression test using malicious query metacharacters as data","expected_result":"The application treats the supplied value as a literal parameter and does not alter query semantics."},"verification":{"claim":"User-controlled values cannot alter SQL syntax through the affected query path.","evidence_required":["Reviewed query construction","Observed parameter binding","Negative injection tests"],"method":"Code review plus automated security regression testing","limitations":"Testing one endpoint does not establish safety for unrelated query paths."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Applicable across database APIs that provide parameterized statements; exact API syntax is language/driver dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-89"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B102","D40-B103","D40-B104"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B102","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Injection","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","concept":"Command injection remediation","question":"How should an application remediate operating-system command injection?","answer":"Prefer direct library APIs over shell execution. When a subprocess is unavoidable, pass arguments through an argument-array interface, avoid shell interpretation, constrain accepted values, and apply least privilege.","objective":"Prevent untrusted data from becoming shell commands or shell syntax.","requirements":["Avoid unnecessary shell invocation","Separate executable from arguments","Constrain accepted values where possible","Run with minimum required privileges"],"constraints":["Some tools require shell-specific behavior","Removing shell interpretation may require redesigning command construction"],"preconditions":["Application launches external processes","At least one command parameter can be influenced by an untrusted source"],"input":"External command parameters","output":"Constrained process invocation","expected_behavior":"External input affects only intended argument values and cannot introduce additional commands or shell operators.","failure_mode":"Untrusted input is concatenated into a shell command.","root_cause":"Application treats data as executable shell syntax.","solution":"Use a direct subprocess API with explicit argument separation and shell disabled where supported.","edge_cases":["Whitespace","Quoting characters","Shell metacharacters","Environment variables","Executable paths containing spaces"],"security_implications":"Command injection can lead to arbitrary command execution with the privileges of the vulnerable process.","performance_implications":"Direct process execution may avoid shell startup overhead, but process creation remains relatively expensive compared with in-process APIs.","testing":{"method":"Negative tests containing shell metacharacters and command separators","expected_result":"Only the intended executable and argument are processed."},"verification":{"claim":"The affected process invocation cannot interpret supplied data as additional shell commands.","evidence_required":["Invocation API configuration","Argument construction review","Negative tests"],"method":"Static review plus controlled security tests","limitations":"Other process-launching paths require separate assessment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Exact safe API depends on runtime and operating-system process model.","provenance":["OWASP_OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-78"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B101","D40-B105"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B103","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cross-site scripting remediation","question":"How should reflected or stored cross-site scripting be remediated?","answer":"Apply context-appropriate output encoding at the point where data is inserted into an HTML, attribute, URL, JavaScript, CSS, or other interpreter context. Prefer framework auto-escaping and avoid unsafe HTML interpretation unless content is deliberately sanitized with an appropriate policy.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled data from being interpreted as executable browser content.","requirements":["Identify the output context","Use context-specific encoding","Preserve framework auto-escaping","Sanitize intentionally permitted rich HTML"],"constraints":["HTML encoding is not interchangeable with JavaScript, CSS, or URL encoding","Sanitization must match the allowed-content policy"],"preconditions":["Untrusted content is rendered in a browser context"],"input":"Untrusted or partially trusted content","output":"Context-safe rendered content","expected_behavior":"The browser treats supplied data as data rather than executable markup or script.","failure_mode":"Raw attacker-controlled content reaches an executable browser context.","root_cause":"Missing or incorrect context-specific output handling.","solution":"Use contextual encoding, safe templating, and narrowly scoped sanitization for deliberately supported rich content.","edge_cases":["Stored content","DOM-generated content","Attribute values","URL parameters","Rich-text fields"],"security_implications":"XSS can enable actions in a victim's browser under the application's origin and may expose sensitive browser-accessible data.","performance_implications":"Encoding is generally inexpensive; complex sanitization can have additional processing cost.","testing":{"method":"Context-specific payload tests and automated browser security tests","expected_result":"Test payloads render as inert data in the intended context."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted output is encoded or safely handled according to its rendering context.","evidence_required":["Template/context review","Encoding behavior","Negative browser tests"],"method":"Code review plus security regression tests","limitations":"Client-side sinks must be reviewed independently."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"Framework escaping behavior varies by framework and version.","provenance":["OWASP_Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-79"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B104","D40-B106"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B104","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"HTML sanitization for intentionally permitted rich content","question":"When an application must accept user-authored HTML, how should unsafe markup be remediated?","answer":"Use a maintained HTML sanitization library configured with an explicit allowlist appropriate to the application's content policy. Do not attempt to build a complete HTML sanitizer from ad hoc string replacements.","objective":"Permit required rich content while removing dangerous markup and attributes.","requirements":["Define permitted tags and attributes","Use a maintained sanitizer","Keep sanitization policy explicit","Revalidate policy when the sanitizer or browser behavior changes"],"constraints":["Sanitization is context-dependent","Allowing additional markup expands the attack surface"],"preconditions":["The application intentionally supports rich HTML input"],"input":"User-authored HTML","output":"Policy-constrained HTML","expected_behavior":"Only explicitly permitted content is retained.","failure_mode":"Unsafe tags or attributes survive sanitization.","root_cause":"Incomplete or context-inappropriate sanitization policy.","solution":"Use a dedicated HTML sanitizer with a restrictive allowlist and test dangerous constructs.","edge_cases":["SVG","MathML","URL-bearing attributes","Inline event handlers","Malformed HTML","Encoded entities"],"security_implications":"Improper sanitization can enable stored XSS and related browser attacks.","performance_implications":"Sanitization cost depends on document size and parser implementation.","testing":{"method":"Security corpus containing malformed markup, dangerous attributes, URLs, and script-like constructs","expected_result":"Disallowed constructs are removed or neutralized without reintroducing executable content."},"verification":{"claim":"Rich HTML is restricted to the documented content policy.","evidence_required":["Sanitizer configuration","Regression corpus results","Dependency version"],"method":"Configuration review and security regression testing","limitations":"No finite test corpus proves universal browser safety."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"Sanitizer behavior is library/version specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_DOMPurify_Project"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B103","D40-B109"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B105","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Server-side template injection remediation","question":"How should server-side template injection be remediated?","answer":"Do not treat untrusted input as template source. Keep templates application-controlled and pass user input as template data. If dynamic templates are an explicit feature, use a sandbox designed for the exact engine and threat model and restrict available capabilities.","objective":"Prevent untrusted data from becoming executable template instructions.","requirements":["Separate template source from template data","Restrict template authoring to trusted sources unless dynamic templates are required","Constrain template capabilities"],"constraints":["Template engines differ substantially","Sandboxing alone may not be sufficient against all engine escape techniques"],"preconditions":["Application renders server-side templates","User-controlled content reaches template compilation or evaluation"],"input":"Untrusted template-related input","output":"Data-only template value or explicitly constrained template","expected_behavior":"User input is rendered as data rather than evaluated as template instructions.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled template expressions execute on the server.","root_cause":"Untrusted content is compiled or evaluated as template source.","solution":"Move untrusted content into template parameters and remove dynamic evaluation paths.","edge_cases":["Expression syntax","Template includes","Macros","Object attribute access","File access from templates"],"security_implications":"SSTI can expose application data and may lead to server-side code execution depending on engine capabilities.","performance_implications":"Removing dynamic compilation may improve predictability and reduce compilation overhead.","testing":{"method":"Negative tests using engine-specific expression probes","expected_result":"Expressions supplied as data are rendered literally or safely encoded."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted input cannot reach a template compilation/evaluation sink as source code.","evidence_required":["Template invocation review","Data-flow analysis","Negative tests"],"method":"Static data-flow review plus targeted testing","limitations":"Engine-specific behavior requires engine-specific analysis."},"languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","PHP"],"version_scope":"Template engine and version specific.","provenance":["CWE-1336","OWASP_Server_Side_Template_Injection"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B102","D40-B103"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B106","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Deserialization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe deserialization of untrusted objects","question":"How should unsafe deserialization of untrusted object graphs be remediated?","answer":"Prefer data-only serialization formats and explicit schemas. Avoid native object deserialization for untrusted input when the format can encode executable or gadget-bearing object state. Enforce type and size constraints before processing.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled serialized data from triggering unintended code execution or dangerous object behavior.","requirements":["Use data-only formats where feasible","Validate schema and types","Limit input size and nesting","Avoid dangerous native deserialization APIs"],"constraints":["Compatibility with existing serialized data may require migration","Some frameworks provide safer restricted deserialization modes"],"preconditions":["Application accepts serialized data from an untrusted or partially trusted source"],"input":"Serialized external data","output":"Validated application data","expected_behavior":"Only permitted data structures are constructed and processed.","failure_mode":"Deserialization invokes attacker-controlled or gadget-triggered behavior.","root_cause":"Trusting serialized object state or dangerous type resolution.","solution":"Use schema-validated data formats and explicit object construction.","edge_cases":["Deep nesting","Unknown fields","Large collections","Polymorphic types","Legacy serialized payloads"],"security_implications":"Unsafe deserialization can cause code execution, denial of service, data exposure, or privilege-impacting behavior.","performance_implications":"Schema validation adds processing cost but can bound resource consumption.","testing":{"method":"Malformed, oversized, unexpected-type, and known-dangerous object graph tests","expected_result":"Input is rejected or reduced to permitted data structures without executing object-specific behavior."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted serialized input cannot instantiate unauthorized executable object types.","evidence_required":["Deserializer configuration","Allowed-type policy","Negative tests"],"method":"Code/configuration review and adversarial testing","limitations":"Gadget availability depends on runtime and dependency versions."},"languages":["Python","Java","C++","PHP","Ruby","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Serialization framework and runtime version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-502"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B107","D40-B108"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B107","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Deserialization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Polymorphic type confusion in serialized input","question":"How should applications remediate unsafe polymorphic deserialization?","answer":"Restrict polymorphic type resolution to an explicit allowlist or eliminate polymorphism in untrusted formats. Reject unknown type identifiers before object construction and validate the resulting object against the expected schema.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled type identifiers from selecting unauthorized classes or behaviors.","requirements":["Define permitted concrete types","Reject unknown type identifiers","Validate object structure after parsing"],"constraints":["Allowlist must be maintained as application types evolve","A parser-level type restriction is preferable to post-construction filtering"],"preconditions":["Deserializer supports polymorphic or type-tagged objects"],"input":"Serialized polymorphic object","output":"Approved concrete data type","expected_behavior":"Only explicitly permitted types can be instantiated.","failure_mode":"Attacker chooses a dangerous or unexpected concrete type.","root_cause":"Unrestricted type metadata controls object construction.","solution":"Use explicit type mappings or data-only schemas.","edge_cases":["Missing type identifier","Unknown type","Nested polymorphic object","Duplicate type fields","Conflicting type metadata"],"security_implications":"Unauthorized type construction can expose gadget chains or unexpected application behavior.","performance_implications":"Explicit type dispatch generally has predictable overhead and can avoid expensive reflective lookup.","testing":{"method":"Type allowlist tests including valid, unknown, nested, and malicious type identifiers","expected_result":"Only allowed types are accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"The set of instantiable types is explicitly constrained.","evidence_required":["Type resolver configuration","Allowlist","Negative type-selection tests"],"method":"Static configuration review plus tests","limitations":"Dependency-specific gadget risks require dependency analysis."},"languages":["Java","Python","C++"],"version_scope":"Deserializer-specific.","provenance":["CWE-502","OWASP_Deserialization_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B106","D40-B108"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B108","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Parser resource exhaustion","question":"How should applications remediate resource-exhaustion vulnerabilities in parsers?","answer":"Apply bounded input sizes, nesting depth, collection counts, processing time, and decompression expansion where applicable. Reject malformed or excessive input before resource consumption becomes uncontrolled.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled structured input from exhausting CPU, memory, stack, or storage resources.","requirements":["Define explicit resource limits","Enforce limits during parsing","Fail closed on limit violations","Monitor parser failures"],"constraints":["Limits must accommodate legitimate maximum input","Limits should be enforced before expensive downstream processing"],"preconditions":["Application parses attacker-influenced structured data"],"input":"Structured external payload","output":"Bounded parsed representation or rejection","expected_behavior":"Excessively large or deeply nested input is rejected within defined resource limits.","failure_mode":"Parser consumes disproportionate resources.","root_cause":"Missing or ineffective resource bounds.","solution":"Introduce parser-level limits and upstream request limits.","edge_cases":["Deep nesting","Large arrays","Highly repetitive structures","Compressed input","Integer overflow in size calculations"],"security_implications":"Resource exhaustion can cause denial of service or degrade availability for other users.","performance_implications":"Bounds can improve worst-case predictability but may reject legitimate large workloads if poorly selected.","testing":{"method":"Boundary and adversarial resource tests","expected_result":"Inputs beyond each configured limit are rejected predictably without exhausting service resources."},"verification":{"claim":"Parser resource consumption is bounded by explicit enforceable limits.","evidence_required":["Configured limits","Instrumentation","Boundary test results"],"method":"Configuration review plus resource-bound testing","limitations":"Observed limits depend on deployment resources and parser implementation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Parser/library specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B106","D40-B107","D40-B124"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B109","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Path Traversal","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Path traversal through user-controlled filesystem paths","question":"How should path traversal be remediated when users influence filesystem paths?","answer":"Prefer opaque server-side identifiers mapped to approved files. When path components are unavoidable, canonicalize and validate the resolved path against an intended base directory, reject traversal and unexpected path forms, and enforce filesystem permissions.","objective":"Prevent user-controlled path components from escaping the intended filesystem boundary.","requirements":["Define an allowed filesystem root","Resolve paths according to platform semantics","Verify the resolved path remains within the permitted root","Apply least-privilege filesystem permissions"],"constraints":["String prefix checks alone are insufficient for robust path containment","Symlink behavior must be considered"],"preconditions":["Application maps external input to filesystem locations"],"input":"External path identifier","output":"Approved filesystem target or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only resources within the intended filesystem boundary can be accessed.","failure_mode":"Input resolves to an unintended file or directory.","root_cause":"Application trusts attacker-controlled path components.","solution":"Use server-side mappings or canonical path containment checks combined with authorization.","edge_cases":["Dot segments","Absolute paths","Symlinks","Case-insensitive filesystems","Alternate separators","Null bytes where applicable"],"security_implications":"Path traversal can expose or modify sensitive files and may contribute to further compromise.","performance_implications":"Canonicalization and filesystem checks add I/O overhead; server-side ID mapping can reduce repeated path parsing.","testing":{"method":"Traversal payloads using platform-specific separators and symlink scenarios","expected_result":"Only approved paths are accessible."},"verification":{"claim":"Resolved user-influenced paths cannot escape the authorized filesystem root.","evidence_required":["Path resolution logic","Containment check","Authorization review","Traversal tests"],"method":"Code review plus filesystem security testing","limitations":"Filesystem semantics vary by operating system and runtime."},"languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Operating-system and runtime API dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Path_Traversal","CWE-22"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B110","D40-B124"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B110","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"File Access","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","concept":"Insecure filename-based resource access","question":"How should an application remediate direct use of user-provided filenames for resource selection?","answer":"Replace externally supplied filenames with opaque identifiers that map to server-controlled metadata and approved storage locations. Enforce authorization on the resolved resource before opening it.","objective":"Remove attacker control over filesystem naming and location decisions.","requirements":["Generate or store opaque resource identifiers","Resolve identifiers through trusted metadata","Authorize the resource before access"],"constraints":["Migration may require mapping existing filenames","Resource metadata must itself be protected from unauthorized modification"],"preconditions":["Users select files or objects through filenames or paths"],"input":"External resource identifier","output":"Authorized server-managed resource","expected_behavior":"The identifier selects only the resource assigned to it by trusted application state.","failure_mode":"Filename manipulation selects unintended files.","root_cause":"User input is treated as an authoritative filesystem location.","solution":"Use server-managed IDs and metadata mappings.","edge_cases":["Deleted resources","Renamed resources","Cross-tenant identifiers","Stale mappings","Concurrent deletion"],"security_implications":"Prevents path traversal and reduces unauthorized cross-resource access.","performance_implications":"Metadata lookup adds a storage operation but can improve consistency and authorization control.","testing":{"method":"Identifier tampering and cross-resource access tests","expected_result":"Unauthorized identifiers cannot access resources belonging to another scope."},"verification":{"claim":"External resource identifiers cannot directly determine filesystem paths.","evidence_required":["Resource mapping design","Authorization checks","Tampering tests"],"method":"Architecture and code review plus authorization testing","limitations":"Storage-layer permissions remain necessary defense in depth."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application architecture pattern; implementation varies by stack.","provenance":["OWASP_File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-22"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B109","D40-B123"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B111","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Weak password storage","question":"How should plaintext or fast-hash password storage be remediated?","answer":"Store passwords using a password hashing function designed for password storage, with an appropriate work factor and unique salt managed by the password-hashing algorithm. Do not store plaintext or reversible password representations.","objective":"Limit the usefulness of stolen password databases and make offline guessing more expensive.","requirements":["Use a dedicated password hashing algorithm","Use unique salts","Configure a work factor appropriate to the deployment","Protect password hashes as sensitive authentication data"],"constraints":["Work-factor selection requires operational benchmarking","Hashing parameters must be reviewed as hardware changes"],"preconditions":["Application stores user passwords","Existing storage does not use an appropriate password hashing scheme"],"input":"User password","output":"Password verifier representation","expected_behavior":"The system can verify a password without storing or recovering the original password.","failure_mode":"Password database contains plaintext, reversible encryption, or inadequate fast hashes.","root_cause":"Password storage mechanism is not designed for password authentication.","solution":"Migrate to a supported password hashing scheme and upgrade hashes during successful authentication where necessary.","edge_cases":["Legacy hashes","Unknown historical algorithms","Password reset","Concurrent login"],"security_implications":"Improves resistance to offline password cracking after database compromise.","performance_implications":"Password hashing intentionally consumes computational resources; excessive work factors can harm authentication availability.","testing":{"method":"Hash-format, unique-salt, parameter, and authentication regression tests","expected_result":"Passwords are stored only as approved password-hash representations and verify correctly."},"verification":{"claim":"Stored password representations are suitable password verifiers and do not expose recoverable plaintext.","evidence_required":["Algorithm and parameter configuration","Storage inspection","Authentication tests"],"method":"Code/configuration review and controlled tests","limitations":"Appropriateness of work factor is deployment-specific."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Password-hashing library and algorithm version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet","NIST_SP_800-63B"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B112","D40-B113"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B112","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Credential stuffing resistance","question":"How should applications remediate authentication exposure to credential stuffing?","answer":"Use strong password verification, breached-password screening where appropriate, multi-factor authentication for applicable accounts, rate limiting or adaptive controls, and monitoring for anomalous authentication patterns. Avoid controls that create easy denial-of-service against legitimate users.","objective":"Reduce successful reuse of compromised credentials without creating disproportionate authentication denial.","requirements":["Detect repeated authentication abuse","Apply appropriate throttling or risk controls","Support stronger authentication factors","Monitor authentication anomalies"],"constraints":["Controls must account for distributed attacks","Rate limits should be scoped carefully to avoid trivial account lockout abuse"],"preconditions":["Users authenticate with passwords or reusable credentials"],"input":"Authentication attempts","output":"Allowed or challenged authentication decision","expected_behavior":"Repeated suspicious credential attempts are constrained while legitimate access remains possible.","failure_mode":"Attackers can test large numbers of credential pairs without meaningful resistance.","root_cause":"Authentication endpoint lacks layered abuse controls.","solution":"Combine password hygiene, MFA, adaptive controls, rate limiting, and monitoring.","edge_cases":["Distributed source addresses","Shared corporate NAT","Password reset abuse","Account lockout attacks"],"security_implications":"Reduces account takeover risk from reused credentials.","performance_implications":"Risk evaluation and rate limiting add authentication-path overhead.","testing":{"method":"Controlled repeated-login and distributed-attempt simulations","expected_result":"Abusive authentication volume is constrained according to policy without causing uncontrolled account lockouts."},"verification":{"claim":"Authentication abuse controls operate as designed under repeated and distributed attempts.","evidence_required":["Rate-limit policy","MFA policy","Authentication telemetry","Controlled abuse tests"],"method":"Configuration review and controlled security testing","limitations":"Effectiveness depends on threat intelligence, identity architecture, and deployment scale."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Identity-provider and application architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_Credential_Stuffing_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet","NIST_SP_800-63B"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B111","D40-B114","D40-B117"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B113","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure password reset","question":"How should an insecure password-reset mechanism be remediated?","answer":"Use a cryptographically strong, single-use, time-limited reset mechanism bound to the intended account and invalidate it after successful use or replacement. Avoid revealing whether an account exists through reset responses.","objective":"Prevent password-reset tokens from being guessed, replayed, or redirected to another account.","requirements":["Generate unpredictable reset tokens","Bind tokens to an account","Expire and invalidate tokens","Protect reset endpoints from abuse","Avoid account-enumerating responses"],"constraints":["Token storage strategy must protect against database disclosure","Reset links should not grant broader access than intended"],"preconditions":["Application provides password recovery"],"input":"Password reset request","output":"Controlled reset capability","expected_behavior":"Only possession of a valid, unexpired, single-use reset capability permits the intended password change.","failure_mode":"Predictable, reusable, non-expiring, or account-unbound reset tokens permit unauthorized reset.","root_cause":"Reset mechanism lacks sufficient entropy, lifecycle control, or account binding.","solution":"Implement high-entropy single-use reset tokens with expiration, invalidation, and abuse controls.","edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous reset requests","Token replay","Expired token","Password changed before token use","User changes email address"],"security_implications":"Compromise of reset mechanisms can directly result in account takeover.","performance_implications":"Token storage and lookup introduce small authentication-path overhead.","testing":{"method":"Entropy/lifecycle, replay, expiration, account-binding, and enumeration tests","expected_result":"Only valid intended reset tokens succeed and reset responses do not reveal account existence."},"verification":{"claim":"Reset capabilities cannot be reused, guessed feasibly, or redirected across accounts.","evidence_required":["Token generation and storage review","Lifecycle tests","Enumeration tests"],"method":"Code review and security regression testing","limitations":"External identity-provider recovery flows require separate assessment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application and identity-provider specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Forgot_Password_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B114","D40-B115"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B114","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authentication response account enumeration","question":"How should user-account enumeration through authentication and recovery endpoints be remediated?","answer":"Use consistent external responses and avoid exposing whether an account exists. Where timing differences can reveal account state, evaluate and reduce meaningful differences without relying on exact timing equality.","objective":"Reduce disclosure of account existence through public authentication workflows.","requirements":["Use non-enumerating response semantics","Review status codes and messages","Assess timing and side-channel differences","Maintain useful internal audit signals"],"constraints":["Perfect timing indistinguishability is difficult to guarantee","Operational monitoring still needs enough information internally"],"preconditions":["Unauthenticated users can query authentication-related endpoints"],"input":"Potential account identifier","output":"Generic external response","expected_behavior":"External callers cannot reliably determine account existence from normal response semantics.","failure_mode":"Endpoint reveals account existence through messages, codes, redirects, or observable behavior.","root_cause":"Authentication state is exposed directly to unauthenticated callers.","solution":"Normalize externally visible responses while preserving internal telemetry.","edge_cases":["Password reset","Registration","Username lookup","MFA enrollment","Invitation acceptance"],"security_implications":"Reduces reconnaissance that can support credential attacks and targeted phishing.","performance_implications":"Response normalization can introduce small processing differences; exact timing control may be impractical.","testing":{"method":"Differential response and timing tests using known-existing and non-existing identifiers","expected_result":"No reliable account-existence signal is exposed through documented response channels."},"verification":{"claim":"Public authentication workflows do not intentionally disclose account existence.","evidence_required":["Response comparison","Status-code review","Controlled differential testing"],"method":"API testing and code review","limitations":"External network effects make precise timing conclusions difficult."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application/API specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Authentication_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_Forgot_Password_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B112","D40-B113"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B115","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Session Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Session fixation","question":"How should session fixation be remediated?","answer":"Regenerate the session identifier after authentication and other relevant privilege transitions, while preserving only explicitly required session state. Invalidate the previous session identifier according to the framework's secure session lifecycle.","objective":"Prevent an attacker-controlled pre-authentication session identifier from remaining valid after authentication.","requirements":["Regenerate session identifiers at authentication","Invalidate superseded identifiers","Preserve only required session data","Use secure session cookie settings"],"constraints":["Framework-specific session APIs must be used correctly","Session regeneration must not accidentally lose required transaction state"],"preconditions":["Application maintains sessions across authentication transitions"],"input":"Pre-authentication session","output":"Authenticated session with a new identifier","expected_behavior":"Successful authentication establishes a fresh session identifier that was not controlled by the attacker before authentication.","failure_mode":"Same session identifier remains valid before and after authentication.","root_cause":"Authentication transition does not rotate session identity.","solution":"Perform server-side session ID regeneration and invalidate the prior session.","edge_cases":["Concurrent login","Remember-me sessions","Privilege elevation","Multi-tab workflows"],"security_implications":"Reduces risk of session takeover through pre-established identifiers.","performance_implications":"Session regeneration may require an additional session-store operation.","testing":{"method":"Pre-authentication session ID capture followed by authentication and session-ID comparison","expected_result":"Authenticated session uses a new valid identifier and the prior identifier is invalidated."},"verification":{"claim":"Authentication transitions rotate session identity.","evidence_required":["Session lifecycle code","Observed identifier rotation","Old-session invalidation test"],"method":"Code review plus integration security test","limitations":"Exact behavior depends on session framework."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go"],"version_scope":"Session framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-384"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B117"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B116","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Session Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure session cookie configuration","question":"How should session cookies be hardened?","answer":"Configure cookies according to the application's threat model using Secure for HTTPS-only transmission, HttpOnly to reduce script access where applicable, and an appropriate SameSite policy. Scope Domain and Path as narrowly as practical and use appropriate expiration semantics.","objective":"Reduce exposure of session identifiers to transport, script, and cross-site request risks.","requirements":["Use HTTPS for authenticated traffic","Enable Secure on sensitive cookies","Use HttpOnly when client-side script access is unnecessary","Select an appropriate SameSite policy","Minimize cookie scope"],"constraints":["Some legitimate cross-site workflows require specific SameSite behavior","Cookie attributes do not replace server-side authorization"],"preconditions":["Application uses browser cookies for session state"],"input":"Session cookie configuration","output":"Hardened session cookie","expected_behavior":"Session identifiers receive appropriate browser-enforced security restrictions.","failure_mode":"Session cookie can be transmitted insecurely, exposed to scripts, or sent in unintended cross-site contexts.","root_cause":"Cookie attributes are missing or inconsistent with the session threat model.","solution":"Configure security attributes and validate them in integration tests.","edge_cases":["Cross-site SSO","Embedded applications","Subdomain sharing","Legacy browser behavior"],"security_implications":"Improper cookie settings can facilitate session theft or cross-site request attacks.","performance_implications":"Cookie scope and size affect request overhead but security attributes generally have negligible performance impact.","testing":{"method":"Inspect Set-Cookie responses and browser behavior across supported workflows","expected_result":"Required attributes are present and compatible with documented authentication flows."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive session cookies enforce the application's documented browser security policy.","evidence_required":["HTTP response inspection","Browser integration tests","Cookie configuration"],"method":"Integration testing and configuration review","limitations":"Browser behavior evolves and must be evaluated against supported clients."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go"],"version_scope":"HTTP/browser behavior is implementation and browser-version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet","RFC_6265bis"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B115","D40-B117"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B117","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Session Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Session expiration and revocation weakness","question":"How should applications remediate sessions that remain valid longer than the security policy permits?","answer":"Define idle and absolute session lifetimes appropriate to the risk, enforce them server-side, and provide explicit revocation for logout, credential compromise, and administrative security events. Do not rely solely on client-side expiration.","objective":"Limit the lifetime and reuse of compromised session credentials.","requirements":["Define idle and absolute limits","Enforce expiration server-side","Support explicit revocation","Invalidate sessions after security-sensitive events when required"],"constraints":["Shorter lifetimes can increase user friction","Distributed session stores must propagate revocation reliably"],"preconditions":["Application uses persistent authenticated sessions"],"input":"Session activity and security state","output":"Active or revoked session decision","expected_behavior":"Expired or revoked sessions cannot authenticate protected requests.","failure_mode":"Session remains usable after policy-defined expiration or revocation.","root_cause":"Session validity is not centrally enforced or revocation is incomplete.","solution":"Implement server-side session lifecycle enforcement and revocation.","edge_cases":["Long-running workflows","Remember-me sessions","Password change","Account disablement","Concurrent devices"],"security_implications":"Limits the useful lifetime of stolen session identifiers.","performance_implications":"Session-state checks add storage or token-validation work to protected requests.","testing":{"method":"Idle timeout, absolute timeout, logout, password-change, and revocation tests","expected_result":"Sessions become unusable exactly according to documented lifecycle rules.","pass_criteria":"Protected requests using expired or revoked sessions are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Session lifetime and revocation policies are enforced server-side.","evidence_required":["Policy configuration","Session validation logic","Lifecycle tests"],"method":"Integration testing and code review","limitations":"Stateless tokens require different revocation architecture."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Session architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B112","D40-B115","D40-B116"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B118","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure direct object reference across tenants","question":"How should cross-tenant object access be remediated?","answer":"Authorize every object access against the authenticated principal and tenant or resource scope on the server. Do not treat an object identifier as proof of ownership or tenant membership.","objective":"Prevent users from accessing resources belonging to another tenant or authorization scope.","requirements":["Authenticate the caller","Resolve the requested object","Enforce object-level authorization","Enforce tenant boundary independently of identifier format"],"constraints":["Opaque identifiers reduce enumeration but do not replace authorization","Authorization must apply to reads, updates, deletes, and actions"],"preconditions":["Multi-tenant application exposes resource identifiers"],"input":"Authenticated principal and resource identifier","output":"Authorized resource operation or denial","expected_behavior":"A caller can access only resources permitted by the authorization policy.","failure_mode":"Changing an identifier exposes another tenant's resource.","root_cause":"Resource lookup is not coupled to authorization scope.","solution":"Perform server-side object-level authorization before returning or modifying the resource.","edge_cases":["Bulk endpoints","Nested resources","Shared resources","Transferred ownership","Administrative roles"],"security_implications":"Cross-tenant authorization failures can cause direct confidentiality and integrity breaches.","performance_implications":"Authorization checks may require additional indexed lookups; tenant-scoped queries can reduce accidental broad scans.","testing":{"method":"Cross-tenant identifier substitution and role-based object-access tests","expected_result":"Unauthorized cross-tenant operations are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Every object operation is constrained by the caller's effective authorization scope.","evidence_required":["Authorization logic","Tenant-scoped query design","Negative access tests"],"method":"Code review and integration security testing","limitations":"Background jobs and administrative paths require separate authorization review."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application authorization architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","CWE-639"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B119","D40-B120"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B119","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Function-level authorization bypass","question":"How should function-level authorization failures be remediated?","answer":"Enforce authorization at the server-side operation boundary for every privileged function or endpoint. Derive permissions from trusted identity and policy state rather than client-provided role indicators or hidden UI controls.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized users from invoking privileged application functions directly.","requirements":["Define authorization policy per protected operation","Enforce policy server-side","Ignore client-supplied privilege indicators","Test unauthorized roles explicitly"],"constraints":["UI visibility is not an authorization mechanism","Shared service functions require consistent policy enforcement"],"preconditions":["Application exposes operations with different privilege levels"],"input":"Authenticated principal and requested operation","output":"Authorized operation or denial","expected_behavior":"Only principals meeting the policy can invoke privileged operations.","failure_mode":"A low-privilege user calls a privileged endpoint directly.","root_cause":"Authorization exists only in the UI or is inconsistently applied across endpoints.","solution":"Centralize or consistently apply server-side authorization checks at operation boundaries.","edge_cases":["HTTP method variations","Hidden endpoints","Background APIs","Bulk administrative operations"],"security_implications":"Can enable unauthorized administrative actions and data modification.","performance_implications":"Centralized authorization can reduce duplicated policy evaluation but may introduce policy-service overhead.","testing":{"method":"Role matrix testing against every protected operation","expected_result":"Unauthorized roles receive consistent authorization failures."},"verification":{"claim":"Protected functions enforce server-side authorization independently of client presentation.","evidence_required":["Endpoint authorization matrix","Middleware/policy enforcement","Negative role tests"],"method":"Code review and authorization matrix testing","limitations":"Policy correctness itself must be reviewed separately."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework and authorization architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","OWASP_Authorization_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B120"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B120","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Client-controlled authorization attributes","question":"How should applications remediate authorization decisions based on client-supplied roles or flags?","answer":"Treat role, privilege, tenant, ownership, and security-state attributes received from the client as untrusted. Load authoritative authorization attributes from trusted server-side identity and policy sources before making access decisions.","objective":"Prevent clients from modifying the attributes that determine their own authorization.","requirements":["Identify authoritative identity attributes","Ignore client-supplied privilege claims unless cryptographically authenticated and policy-approved","Evaluate authorization server-side"],"constraints":["Signed claims still require issuer, audience, lifetime, and trust validation","Business data supplied by the client may require separate integrity checks"],"preconditions":["Client submits role, tenant, ownership, or privilege-related fields"],"input":"Client request plus trusted identity context","output":"Server-side authorization decision","expected_behavior":"Changing client-controlled authorization fields does not elevate privileges.","failure_mode":"Attacker changes a role or ownership field to gain access.","root_cause":"Authorization trusts mutable client state.","solution":"Use trusted server-side identity and policy data for authorization decisions.","edge_cases":["JWT claims","Hidden form fields","Mobile clients","Offline-signed requests","Administrative impersonation"],"security_implications":"Can prevent privilege escalation through parameter tampering.","performance_implications":"Trusted identity lookup may add latency; caching must respect authorization freshness requirements.","testing":{"method":"Tamper authorization-related request fields and claims","expected_result":"Privilege level remains determined by trusted server-side policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Client-controlled fields cannot directly change authorization outcomes.","evidence_required":["Authorization data-flow review","Tampering tests","Identity trust configuration"],"method":"Static data-flow analysis and integration testing","limitations":"Trusted-token configuration must also be validated."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Identity architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Authorization_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-639"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B119"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B121","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"File Upload","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted file upload","question":"How should unrestricted file upload be remediated?","answer":"Define allowed file types and sizes, validate content using multiple signals, generate server-side storage names, store uploads outside executable web roots where possible, and apply authorization and malware/content scanning appropriate to the environment.","objective":"Prevent uploaded content from becoming executable code or causing resource and storage abuse.","requirements":["Limit size","Restrict allowed file categories","Generate storage identifiers","Separate upload storage from executable application paths","Authorize access to uploaded objects"],"constraints":["File extension alone is insufficient as a content validation mechanism","Scanning does not replace access control and resource limits"],"preconditions":["Application accepts user-controlled files"],"input":"Uploaded file and metadata","output":"Validated stored object or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only files satisfying documented policy are stored and served safely.","failure_mode":"Attacker uploads executable, oversized, malformed, or unauthorized content.","root_cause":"Upload path lacks content, resource, storage, and authorization controls.","solution":"Implement layered upload validation and isolated storage.","edge_cases":["Double extensions","MIME mismatches","Polyglot files","Compressed archives","Very large files","Filename collisions"],"security_implications":"Can enable code execution, stored XSS, malware hosting, data exposure, or denial of service.","performance_implications":"Scanning and content validation consume CPU and I/O; asynchronous processing may be appropriate for large files.","testing":{"method":"Upload-policy, MIME mismatch, size-limit, executable-content, and authorization tests","expected_result":"Disallowed files are rejected and accepted files are stored in non-executable controlled locations."},"verification":{"claim":"Uploaded content cannot bypass documented type, size, storage, and authorization controls.","evidence_required":["Upload policy","Storage configuration","Negative upload tests"],"method":"Code review and security testing","limitations":"Malware-detection effectiveness depends on the selected scanning technology."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Web framework and storage architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-434"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B122","D40-B123","D40-B124"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B122","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"File Upload","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Archive extraction traversal","question":"How should archive extraction vulnerabilities be remediated?","answer":"Inspect archive entry names before extraction, reject absolute paths and traversal components, resolve the destination safely, enforce total and per-file extraction limits, and prevent symlink-based escapes where supported.","objective":"Prevent archive contents from writing outside the intended extraction directory or exhausting resources.","requirements":["Validate archive entry paths","Enforce extraction size/count limits","Handle symlinks safely","Use an isolated extraction directory where practical"],"constraints":["Archive formats differ in path and link semantics","Compressed size alone does not bound decompressed output"],"preconditions":["Application extracts attacker-influenced archives"],"input":"Archive file","output":"Safely extracted files or rejection","expected_behavior":"Every extracted entry remains within the intended destination and resource limits.","failure_mode":"Archive entry writes outside the destination or causes excessive expansion.","root_cause":"Archive extraction trusts entry metadata and lacks resource bounds.","solution":"Perform safe entry validation and bounded extraction.","edge_cases":["Absolute paths","Dot-dot traversal","Backslash separators","Symlinks","Hard links","Nested archives","Compression bombs"],"security_implications":"Can overwrite sensitive files or cause denial of service.","performance_implications":"Pre-validation and bounded extraction add processing overhead but reduce uncontrolled resource consumption.","testing":{"method":"Malicious archive corpus including traversal and expansion cases","expected_result":"Unsafe entries are rejected without writes outside the destination and without uncontrolled expansion."},"verification":{"claim":"Archive extraction remains within path and resource boundaries.","evidence_required":["Extraction implementation","Archive-policy tests","Filesystem observations"],"method":"Static review and controlled filesystem testing","limitations":"Archive format-specific features require format-specific validation."},"languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Archive library and format dependent.","provenance":["CWE-22","CWE-409","OWASP_File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B109","D40-B121","D40-B124"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B123","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"File Upload","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Executable upload storage","question":"How should applications remediate uploaded files being stored in an executable web-accessible directory?","answer":"Store untrusted uploads outside executable application directories when feasible and serve them through a controlled download path with authorization and safe content-disposition/content-type handling.","objective":"Prevent uploaded content from being interpreted as server-side executable code.","requirements":["Separate untrusted storage from executable application code","Authorize download access","Use controlled response headers","Prevent filename/path control from reaching executable locations"],"constraints":["Some deployments require object storage or dedicated media domains","Serving content inline requires additional browser-context analysis"],"preconditions":["Application stores user-controlled content on or near executable application paths"],"input":"Uploaded object","output":"Non-executable stored object","expected_behavior":"Stored user content cannot be invoked as application code merely because it was uploaded.","failure_mode":"Uploaded script or executable file is interpreted by the server.","root_cause":"Untrusted storage shares execution context with application code.","solution":"Use non-executable isolated storage and controlled serving.","edge_cases":["Static hosting","CDN origins","Server-side includes","Scriptable file extensions","Content-type sniffing"],"security_implications":"Reduces risk of uploaded content becoming server-side or browser-side executable content.","performance_implications":"Dedicated object storage or media serving can improve scalability but adds infrastructure boundaries.","testing":{"method":"Upload executable-like files and request them through public and internal paths","expected_result":"Files are served only as non-executable content according to policy."},"verification":{"claim":"The upload storage location cannot cause uploaded content to execute as application code.","evidence_required":["Deployment configuration","Storage permissions","Upload/download tests"],"method":"Configuration review and integration testing","limitations":"Web-server configuration must be included in verification."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Deployment/web-server dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-434"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B121","D40-B122"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B124","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Application-level denial of service through expensive requests","question":"How should expensive-request denial of service be remediated?","answer":"Bound request size, computational complexity, concurrency, queue depth, and processing time where practical. Apply authentication and rate controls according to risk, prioritize critical work, and use cancellation/timeouts for operations that can otherwise run indefinitely.","objective":"Prevent a small number of requests from consuming disproportionate application resources.","requirements":["Define resource budgets","Enforce request and operation limits","Use timeouts/cancellation where appropriate","Control concurrency and queue depth"],"constraints":["Limits must preserve legitimate workloads","Distributed rate limiting may be needed at scale"],"preconditions":["Requests can trigger expensive computation, I/O, parsing, or downstream calls"],"input":"External request workload","output":"Bounded operation","expected_behavior":"No request class can consume unbounded application resources.","failure_mode":"Attackers trigger expensive work repeatedly or concurrently.","root_cause":"No effective resource budget or admission control.","solution":"Introduce bounded work, timeouts, concurrency limits, and appropriate abuse controls.","edge_cases":["Slow downstream dependency","Large batch request","Expensive regular expression","Nested parsing","Cancellation races"],"security_implications":"Improves availability under malicious or accidental overload.","performance_implications":"Resource limits can improve tail-latency stability but may reduce maximum per-request workload.","testing":{"method":"Load and adversarial resource-exhaustion tests","expected_result":"Configured budgets and limits activate predictably under excessive demand."},"verification":{"claim":"High-cost operations have enforceable resource and concurrency boundaries.","evidence_required":["Limit configuration","Timeout/cancellation behavior","Controlled load results"],"method":"Performance-security testing and code review","limitations":"Distributed infrastructure may impose additional limits."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Runtime, framework, and deployment dependent.","provenance":["CWE-400","OWASP_Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B108","D40-B122","D40-B125"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B125","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Regular Expressions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Regular-expression denial of service","question":"How should catastrophic regular-expression backtracking be remediated?","answer":"Avoid vulnerable backtracking patterns on attacker-controlled input, prefer linear-time regex engines where appropriate, constrain input length, and test expressions against adversarial cases. Replace complex expressions with explicit parsers when the grammar requires them.","objective":"Prevent regex processing from consuming excessive CPU.","requirements":["Review regex complexity","Bound attacker-controlled input","Use safer regex engines when appropriate","Test adversarial strings"],"constraints":["Regex engine semantics differ","A pattern safe in one engine may not be safe in another"],"preconditions":["Application applies regular expressions to attacker-controlled or large input"],"input":"External text","output":"Bounded match result","expected_behavior":"Regex evaluation completes within acceptable resource limits.","failure_mode":"Specific input causes excessive backtracking and CPU consumption.","root_cause":"Regex pattern and engine permit pathological backtracking.","solution":"Rewrite the pattern, constrain input, or use a linear-time engine/parser.","edge_cases":["Nested quantifiers","Alternation overlap","Long near-matches","Unicode behavior"],"security_implications":"Can create application-level denial of service.","performance_implications":"Linear-time matching improves worst-case predictability; replacing regex with a parser may change implementation cost.","testing":{"method":"Adversarial regex corpus and timeout-based tests","expected_result":"Matching remains within the documented processing budget."},"verification":{"claim":"The affected regex path has bounded or acceptably controlled worst-case behavior.","evidence_required":["Regex pattern review","Engine semantics","Adversarial timing/resource tests"],"method":"Static analysis plus controlled performance testing","limitations":"A single test corpus cannot prove mathematical complexity for arbitrary patterns."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Regex-engine specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service","CWE-1333"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B124","D40-B126"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B126","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Input Validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","concept":"Allowlist validation for constrained identifiers","question":"When should allowlist validation be used as a security remediation?","answer":"Use allowlist validation when the legitimate input domain can be precisely defined, such as enumerated operations, constrained identifiers, or fixed formats. Reject values outside the allowed grammar rather than attempting to enumerate every malicious value.","objective":"Reduce attack surface for inputs with a well-defined legitimate domain.","requirements":["Define accepted syntax or values","Reject out-of-domain values","Perform validation server-side","Keep validation rules aligned with business requirements"],"constraints":["Allowlist validation cannot replace context-specific encoding or parameterization","Open-ended natural-language fields may not have a useful strict allowlist"],"preconditions":["Input has a sufficiently constrained legitimate domain"],"input":"External value","output":"Accepted validated value or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only values satisfying the documented input contract proceed.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts values outside the intended domain.","root_cause":"Validation is absent, permissive, or based only on blacklist patterns.","solution":"Define and enforce an explicit allowlist grammar or enumeration.","edge_cases":["Unicode normalization","Case sensitivity","Locale-specific characters","Leading/trailing whitespace","Canonical representation"],"security_implications":"Reduces unexpected input reaching sensitive operations.","performance_implications":"Simple allowlist checks are generally inexpensive.","testing":{"method":"Positive, negative, boundary, normalization, and unexpected-character tests","expected_result":"Only documented valid values are accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"The validator accepts exactly the intended documented domain or a deliberately defined superset.","evidence_required":["Input specification","Validation implementation","Boundary test suite"],"method":"Specification-to-test review","limitations":"Validation correctness depends on the precision of the underlying business specification."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Language/library implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B101","D40-B125"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B127","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Input Validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Canonicalization mismatch","question":"How should security bugs caused by validating one representation and using another be remediated?","answer":"Canonicalize or normalize input according to the security-relevant interpretation before validation, then use the validated canonical representation for the protected operation. Avoid validating one encoding and later decoding or normalizing it into a different security meaning.","objective":"Prevent validation bypasses caused by multiple representations of equivalent input.","requirements":["Identify all decoding and normalization stages","Validate the representation actually used by the security decision","Avoid inconsistent canonicalization"],"constraints":["Canonicalization rules are format and protocol specific","Unicode normalization can change semantics and must be deliberate"],"preconditions":["Input undergoes decoding, normalization, or representation changes"],"input":"Encoded or normalized external data","output":"Canonical validated representation","expected_behavior":"Security checks and downstream operations use the same intended representation.","failure_mode":"Attacker bypasses validation using alternate encoding or normalization.","root_cause":"Security decision occurs before a later transformation that changes interpretation.","solution":"Establish a clear canonicalization pipeline and validate after security-relevant transformations.","edge_cases":["Percent encoding","Unicode normalization","Case folding","Path separators","Double decoding"],"security_implications":"Can bypass filters and authorization boundaries and contribute to injection or path traversal.","performance_implications":"Additional normalization has processing cost but can simplify downstream handling.","testing":{"method":"Differential representation tests using equivalent encoded forms","expected_result":"Equivalent malicious representations receive the same security treatment."},"verification":{"claim":"Security decisions are made on the representation consumed by the protected operation.","evidence_required":["Data-flow analysis","Transformation sequence","Canonicalization tests"],"method":"Code review and adversarial test suite","limitations":"Correct canonical form depends on protocol semantics."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Protocol and parser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-180"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B109","D40-B126"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B128","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cryptography","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Weak or obsolete cryptographic algorithm","question":"How should an application remediate use of an obsolete cryptographic algorithm?","answer":"Identify the security purpose and replace obsolete algorithms with currently supported primitives appropriate to that purpose. Preserve algorithm agility where migration is expected, and re-encrypt or re-sign stored material through a controlled migration process when required.","objective":"Remove cryptographic mechanisms whose security properties no longer meet the application's requirements.","requirements":["Identify cryptographic purpose","Select an approved primitive for that purpose","Plan migration of existing protected data","Maintain key lifecycle controls"],"constraints":["Hashing, encryption, MAC, signatures, and key derivation require different primitives","Migration must preserve data availability and integrity"],"preconditions":["Application uses a deprecated, weak, or unsuitable primitive"],"input":"Existing cryptographic design","output":"Supported cryptographic design","expected_behavior":"New operations use approved primitives and legacy material follows a documented migration policy.","failure_mode":"Weak cryptographic primitive remains in active security-critical use.","root_cause":"Legacy algorithm was retained without a supported migration path.","solution":"Replace it with a suitable modern construction and migrate protected material safely.","edge_cases":["Legacy data","Interoperability requirements","Key rotation","Mixed-version clients"],"security_implications":"Reduces exposure to known cryptographic weaknesses.","performance_implications":"Modern algorithms may have different CPU and hardware characteristics; migration can create temporary load.","testing":{"method":"Cryptographic configuration tests and migration verification","expected_result":"No new protected material is generated using the deprecated mechanism unless explicitly required for compatibility."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-sensitive cryptographic operations use primitives approved by the application's cryptographic policy.","evidence_required":["Algorithm inventory","Policy mapping","Configuration tests"],"method":"Cryptographic inventory and configuration review","limitations":"Algorithm suitability depends on security purpose and threat model."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Cryptographic library and policy version dependent.","provenance":["NIST_Cryptographic_Standards","OWASP_Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B129","D40-B130"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B129","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cryptography","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Improper key management","question":"How should cryptographic keys stored in application source code be remediated?","answer":"Remove long-lived secrets from source code and use an appropriate secret or key-management mechanism with access controls, rotation, auditing, and environment-specific key material. Rotate exposed keys after containment and assess historical exposure.","objective":"Prevent source-code disclosure from becoming direct compromise of cryptographic keys.","requirements":["Remove secrets from source control","Use protected key or secret storage","Restrict key access","Support rotation","Audit sensitive key operations"],"constraints":["Environment variables alone are not equivalent to dedicated key management in every deployment","Rotation must account for dependent data and services"],"preconditions":["Application source contains hard-coded cryptographic keys or secrets"],"input":"Exposed secret/key","output":"Managed cryptographic key lifecycle","expected_behavior":"Source code contains no active long-lived production key material and runtime access is policy-controlled.","failure_mode":"Anyone with source access can recover an active cryptographic key.","root_cause":"Secrets were embedded directly in code or committed to version control.","solution":"Move key material into controlled secret/key management and rotate exposed credentials.","edge_cases":["Git history","Build artifacts","Logs","Container images","Forks and caches"],"security_implications":"Compromised source repositories can otherwise expose production data or signing authority.","performance_implications":"Managed key retrieval may add startup or operation latency depending on architecture.","testing":{"method":"Secret scanning, repository history review, deployment configuration review","expected_result":"No active production secret is present in source or artifacts and runtime retrieves authorized key material from the approved store."},"verification":{"claim":"Production cryptographic keys are not embedded in source-controlled application code.","evidence_required":["Repository scan","Secret-store configuration","Rotation records"],"method":"Automated secret scanning plus configuration review","limitations":"Secret scanning cannot guarantee discovery of every semantically encoded secret."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Deployment and key-management architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Key_Management_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-321"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B128","D40-B130"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B130","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cryptography","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient cryptographic randomness","question":"How should predictable randomness used for security tokens be remediated?","answer":"Use a cryptographically secure random generator provided by the platform or a vetted cryptographic library for security-sensitive tokens, keys, nonces, and identifiers. Do not use general-purpose pseudo-random APIs intended for simulation or convenience.","objective":"Ensure security-sensitive random values are not feasibly predictable.","requirements":["Use a cryptographically secure random source","Generate sufficient entropy for the security purpose","Avoid predictable seeds","Use unique nonces where the algorithm requires them"],"constraints":["Entropy requirements depend on the cryptographic construction","Uniqueness and unpredictability are separate properties"],"preconditions":["Application generates security-sensitive random values"],"input":"Security-sensitive token generation request","output":"Cryptographically appropriate random value","expected_behavior":"Generated values are derived from a cryptographically suitable randomness source.","failure_mode":"Attacker predicts or reconstructs token values.","root_cause":"Non-cryptographic PRNG or predictable seeding is used for security purposes.","solution":"Replace with platform CSPRNG or vetted cryptographic random API.","edge_cases":["Forked processes","VM snapshots","Startup entropy","Nonce reuse"],"security_implications":"Predictable tokens can enable session takeover, reset-token compromise, or cryptographic failure.","performance_implications":"CSPRNG operations are generally suitable for security workloads; excessive generation should still be profiled.","testing":{"method":"API/source review and deterministic-PRNG misuse scanning","expected_result":"Security-sensitive generation paths use approved cryptographic randomness APIs."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-sensitive random values originate from an approved cryptographic randomness source.","evidence_required":["API call review","Static checks","Configuration/library version"],"method":"Static analysis and code review","limitations":"Statistical randomness tests alone do not establish cryptographic suitability."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Runtime/library dependent.","provenance":["NIST_SP_800-90A","OWASP_Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-338"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B113","D40-B129"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B131","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Mass assignment and over-posting","question":"How should APIs remediate mass-assignment vulnerabilities?","answer":"Define explicit writable fields or request schemas and map them to domain objects deliberately. Reject or ignore client fields that are not part of the operation's allowed input contract, especially security-sensitive fields.","objective":"Prevent clients from modifying unintended object properties through broad request binding.","requirements":["Define per-operation input schemas","Explicitly control writable fields","Separate input DTOs from persistence models when appropriate","Protect security-sensitive properties"],"constraints":["Ignoring unknown fields can hide client bugs; explicit rejection may be preferable for strict APIs","Persistence models should not automatically define the public API contract"],"preconditions":["Framework automatically binds request fields to application objects"],"input":"Client object payload","output":"Validated operation-specific command","expected_behavior":"Only documented writable fields affect the operation.","failure_mode":"Client supplies hidden fields such as role, owner, or status and changes them.","root_cause":"Generic object binding exposes more properties than the API intends.","solution":"Use explicit request schemas and controlled mapping.","edge_cases":["Nested objects","Patch semantics","Unknown fields","Role-specific fields","Admin-only attributes"],"security_implications":"Can enable privilege escalation, ownership changes, or unauthorized state transitions.","performance_implications":"Explicit mapping adds code and validation but can reduce accidental persistence operations.","testing":{"method":"Extra-field and security-property tampering tests","expected_result":"Undocumented or protected fields cannot be changed through the endpoint."},"verification":{"claim":"Each write operation exposes only its documented mutable fields.","evidence_required":["Request schemas","Object mapping","Tampering tests"],"method":"Schema review and integration testing","limitations":"Authorization of allowed fields still requires separate policy validation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Web framework/model-binding dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","CWE-915"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B120"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B132","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Excessive data exposure","question":"How should APIs remediate excessive data exposure?","answer":"Return only fields required for the specific client operation and authorization scope. Use explicit response schemas or projections rather than serializing complete internal domain objects by default.","objective":"Prevent APIs from exposing fields that callers do not need or are not authorized to receive.","requirements":["Define operation-specific response contracts","Apply authorization to sensitive fields where required","Avoid automatic serialization of full internal objects"],"constraints":["Removing fields can affect clients and requires versioning or migration","Field-level sensitivity must be documented"],"preconditions":["API serializes domain or database objects to clients"],"input":"Authorized domain object","output":"Minimal response representation","expected_behavior":"Response contains only documented fields permitted for the caller and operation.","failure_mode":"Internal or sensitive fields are returned unintentionally.","root_cause":"Response serialization is broader than the API contract.","solution":"Use explicit DTOs, schemas, or database projections.","edge_cases":["Admin versus standard users","Nested objects","Debug fields","Internal identifiers","Soft-deleted records"],"security_implications":"Reduces unintended disclosure of secrets, identifiers, internal metadata, or personal data.","performance_implications":"Projection can reduce serialization and network payload size.","testing":{"method":"Response schema and role-based field tests","expected_result":"Undocumented or unauthorized fields are absent."},"verification":{"claim":"API responses conform to explicit least-data contracts.","evidence_required":["Response schemas","Sensitive-field inventory","Automated response tests"],"method":"Contract testing and code review","limitations":"Client-side cached historical responses may require separate data-governance controls."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"API framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","OWASP_Authorization_Cheat_Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B131","D40-B133"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B133","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted resource consumption in APIs","question":"How should APIs remediate endpoints that permit unbounded pagination, batch size, or resource selection?","answer":"Define maximum page size, batch size, query complexity, upload size, and processing limits appropriate to the endpoint. Enforce them server-side and apply pagination or asynchronous processing for legitimate large workloads.","objective":"Prevent clients from turning ordinary API operations into disproportionate resource consumption.","requirements":["Set endpoint-specific resource limits","Validate limits server-side","Provide controlled mechanisms for legitimate large workloads","Monitor excessive usage"],"constraints":["Maximums should be based on workload requirements rather than arbitrary client expectations","Rate limiting alone does not control cost per request"],"preconditions":["API accepts client-controlled quantity or complexity parameters"],"input":"API request with resource quantity parameters","output":"Bounded request execution","expected_behavior":"Requests beyond documented resource limits are rejected or routed through controlled workflows.","failure_mode":"A single request consumes excessive memory, CPU, database, or network resources.","root_cause":"API contract exposes unbounded work.","solution":"Introduce bounded parameters and controlled large-workflow mechanisms.","edge_cases":["Nested batch requests","Large page size","Complex filters","Expensive sorting","Large exports"],"security_implications":"Reduces denial-of-service and abuse risk.","performance_implications":"Bounds improve resource predictability and tail behavior.","testing":{"method":"Boundary and load tests around each resource limit","expected_result":"Requests exceeding limits are rejected or handled by the designated controlled workflow."},"verification":{"claim":"Resource-consuming API parameters have explicit enforceable limits.","evidence_required":["API contract","Limit configuration","Boundary tests"],"method":"Contract review and load/security testing","limitations":"Downstream resource constraints can impose additional effective limits."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"API and infrastructure dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B124","D40-B134"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B134","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe pagination and expensive database queries","question":"How should API endpoints exposing arbitrary pagination or sorting be remediated?","answer":"Constrain sortable/filterable fields to an allowlist, cap page size, use indexed query patterns, and avoid allowing clients to request arbitrary database expressions. Prefer stable pagination strategies for large datasets.","objective":"Prevent API parameters from triggering unauthorized or excessively expensive database work.","requirements":["Allowlist queryable fields","Limit result size","Constrain sort directions","Validate filter operators","Design queries around supported indexes"],"constraints":["Keyset pagination may not suit every ordering requirement","Database-specific query planners must be considered"],"preconditions":["API translates client query parameters into database operations"],"input":"Client query parameters","output":"Constrained database query","expected_behavior":"Only documented query capabilities are executed within resource limits.","failure_mode":"Client causes expensive scans or unexpected database operations.","root_cause":"API query language is mapped too directly to database semantics.","solution":"Implement a constrained query model and explicit database query builder.","edge_cases":["Deep pagination","Unindexed filters","Many sort fields","Null ordering","Concurrent inserts"],"security_implications":"Reduces resource-exhaustion and unintended data-access risks.","performance_implications":"Explicit query constraints and appropriate indexes can improve predictable query latency.","testing":{"method":"Query allowlist, boundary, explain-plan, and load tests","expected_result":"Unsupported or excessive queries are rejected and supported queries follow intended resource bounds."},"verification":{"claim":"Client query parameters cannot bypass the API's supported query and resource model.","evidence_required":["Query parser","Allowed-field configuration","Database query tests"],"method":"Code review and controlled database testing","limitations":"Production query behavior depends on data distribution and database statistics."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database and ORM/query-builder dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B126","D40-B133"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B135","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Improper API method authorization","question":"How should APIs remediate authorization gaps caused by treating HTTP methods as interchangeable?","answer":"Authorize each operation according to its semantic effect rather than assuming that protection on one HTTP method automatically protects related methods. Explicitly define allowed methods and authorization requirements.","objective":"Prevent bypasses caused by inconsistent authorization across read and mutation methods.","requirements":["Define operation semantics","Authorize each method/action","Reject unsupported methods","Test method variations"],"constraints":["HTTP method semantics are framework and API specific","Routing middleware must be included in authorization analysis"],"preconditions":["Resource endpoints expose multiple HTTP methods"],"input":"HTTP method and resource request","output":"Authorized operation or rejection","expected_behavior":"Each supported operation receives the intended authentication and authorization policy.","failure_mode":"A protected operation is reachable through another method or route with weaker controls.","root_cause":"Security policy is attached to only one route/method representation.","solution":"Centralize operation authorization and explicitly reject unsupported methods.","edge_cases":["HEAD versus GET","OPTIONS","Method override headers","Route aliases","Legacy endpoints"],"security_implications":"Can create authorization bypasses and unintended state changes.","performance_implications":"Method-specific policy checks have negligible relative cost but can add middleware processing.","testing":{"method":"Method matrix and route-variant authorization tests","expected_result":"Every supported and unsupported method follows the documented policy."},"verification":{"claim":"No alternate HTTP method or route bypasses the operation's authorization policy.","evidence_required":["Route/method matrix","Middleware configuration","Negative tests"],"method":"API security testing and route review","limitations":"Proxy and gateway method transformations must also be reviewed."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP framework and gateway dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_API_Security_Top_10","RFC_9110"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B119","D40-B136"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B136","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cross-site request forgery","question":"How should CSRF vulnerabilities be remediated for browser requests using ambient authentication?","answer":"Use a CSRF defense appropriate to the authentication model, such as framework-provided anti-CSRF tokens and appropriate SameSite cookie settings. Validate request origin information where supported by the threat model, and do not rely on CORS as the primary CSRF defense.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled websites from causing authenticated browser state changes.","requirements":["Identify requests using ambient credentials","Protect state-changing requests","Use framework-supported CSRF controls","Review cookie SameSite behavior"],"constraints":["Bearer-token APIs with non-ambient authorization may have a different CSRF model","CORS controls cross-origin reads and is not a universal CSRF defense"],"preconditions":["Browser automatically supplies authentication credentials with requests"],"input":"Cross-site state-changing request","output":"Allowed legitimate request or rejected forged request","expected_behavior":"A cross-site attacker cannot cause a protected state-changing operation without satisfying the CSRF policy.","failure_mode":"Server accepts forged state-changing browser requests.","root_cause":"No effective anti-CSRF mechanism for ambient credentials.","solution":"Implement appropriate anti-CSRF tokens, SameSite policy, and/or origin validation.","edge_cases":["Login CSRF","JSON APIs","Multipart requests","Cross-site redirects","Legacy clients"],"security_implications":"Can cause unauthorized actions under a victim's authenticated session.","performance_implications":"Token validation adds small request-processing overhead.","testing":{"method":"Cross-origin forged-request tests against every state-changing endpoint","expected_result":"Requests lacking required CSRF evidence are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"State-changing browser requests using ambient authentication require the documented CSRF protection.","evidence_required":["CSRF configuration","Cookie policy","Negative cross-site tests"],"method":"Integration browser testing and code review","limitations":"CSRF analysis must reflect the application's actual authentication transport."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Framework/browser behavior dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_SameSite_Cookie_Attribute"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B137"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B137","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Browser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overly permissive CORS policy","question":"How should an overly permissive CORS policy be remediated?","answer":"Define an explicit set of permitted origins and allowed methods, headers, and credential behavior required by the application. Avoid reflecting arbitrary Origin values and avoid credentialed cross-origin access unless it is specifically required.","objective":"Limit cross-origin browser access to documented trusted origins and operations.","requirements":["Define trusted origins","Constrain methods and headers","Review credentialed requests","Do not reflect arbitrary origins"],"constraints":["CORS is a browser policy mechanism and does not replace server-side authorization","Origin allowlists must account for exact origin semantics"],"preconditions":["Application serves browser requests from other origins"],"input":"Browser Origin and request metadata","output":"CORS response policy","expected_behavior":"Only documented origins receive permitted cross-origin access.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled origins receive access to sensitive cross-origin responses.","root_cause":"Wildcard or arbitrary origin reflection is used without appropriate credential policy.","solution":"Implement explicit origin allowlisting and least-privilege CORS settings.","edge_cases":["Subdomain matching","Null origin","Development origins","Credentialed requests","Multiple environments"],"security_implications":"Misconfigured CORS can expose authenticated responses to untrusted websites.","performance_implications":"Origin policy evaluation is typically low overhead.","testing":{"method":"Origin matrix tests with trusted, untrusted, null, and malformed origins","expected_result":"Only approved origins receive the configured CORS permissions."},"verification":{"claim":"CORS policy exposes only the documented cross-origin surface.","evidence_required":["CORS configuration","Response header tests","Origin matrix"],"method":"Integration testing and configuration review","limitations":"Server-side authorization remains necessary regardless of CORS."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Browser/server framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_CORS_Cheat_Sheet","MDN_CORS"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B136","D40-B138"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B138","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"Security Headers","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing browser security headers","question":"How should missing or weak browser security headers be remediated?","answer":"Define a security-header policy based on application behavior and browser support, then configure headers such as Content-Security-Policy, frame-ancestors or related framing controls, Referrer-Policy, and appropriate content-type protections where applicable.","objective":"Reduce browser-side attack surface through explicit response security policies.","requirements":["Define header policy","Deploy headers consistently","Test policy against application functionality","Monitor violations where supported"],"constraints":["Headers must match actual application resources and integrations","A security header that breaks required functionality is not a complete remediation"],"preconditions":["Application serves content to browsers"],"input":"HTTP response","output":"Response with documented security policy headers","expected_behavior":"Supported browsers receive headers appropriate to the application's threat model and content model.","failure_mode":"Browser executes or embeds content under weaker-than-intended security rules.","root_cause":"Security headers are absent, inconsistent, or copied without application-specific analysis.","solution":"Create and validate an application-specific header policy.","edge_cases":["Third-party scripts","Frames","Workers","WebSockets","CDNs","Legacy browsers"],"security_implications":"Can reduce impact of XSS, framing, MIME confusion, and referrer-related data exposure.","performance_implications":"Header processing overhead is minimal; CSP tuning may reduce reliance on unnecessary third-party resources.","testing":{"method":"Header inspection and browser integration testing","expected_result":"Required headers are present with values consistent with the documented security policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Browser-facing responses implement the documented security-header policy.","evidence_required":["Header policy","HTTP captures","Browser tests"],"method":"Integration testing and configuration review","limitations":"Header effectiveness depends on browser support and correct policy design."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"HTTP/browser version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Secure_Headers_Project","OWASP_Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet","MDN_HTTP_Headers"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B103","D40-B137"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B139","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Clickjacking","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unintended framing of sensitive application pages","question":"How should clickjacking exposure be remediated?","answer":"Define whether each page may be framed and enforce the policy with modern framing controls such as CSP frame-ancestors, supplemented by compatible legacy controls where required by supported clients.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized sites from embedding sensitive application interfaces.","requirements":["Identify pages requiring framing","Define permitted framing origins","Enforce framing policy server-side","Test supported browsers"],"constraints":["Some applications legitimately require trusted embedding","Frame restrictions should not be applied blindly to required integrations"],"preconditions":["Application presents sensitive actions through browser interfaces"],"input":"Browser framing context","output":"Allowed or blocked framing","expected_behavior":"Only documented framing contexts can embed protected pages.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled page embeds sensitive UI and tricks users into unintended actions.","root_cause":"No effective framing policy or overly broad framing allowance.","solution":"Apply a restrictive framing policy consistent with legitimate embedding requirements.","edge_cases":["Trusted partner portals","Nested frames","Legacy browser support","Single sign-on integrations"],"security_implications":"Reduces clickjacking risk for sensitive browser actions.","performance_implications":"Negligible runtime performance impact.","testing":{"method":"Embedding tests from trusted and untrusted origins","expected_result":"Protected pages cannot be framed by unauthorized origins."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive pages enforce their documented framing policy.","evidence_required":["CSP/framing configuration","Browser embedding tests"],"method":"HTTP and browser integration testing","limitations":"Legacy browser support may require additional controls."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"Browser and HTTP-server dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Clickjacking_Defense_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-1021"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B138","D40-B136"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B140","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Browser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"MIME sniffing exposure","question":"How should applications remediate browser MIME-type sniffing risks?","answer":"Serve resources with accurate Content-Type values and apply X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff where appropriate so browsers do not reinterpret content contrary to the declared type.","objective":"Reduce the chance that browser content interpretation differs from the server's intended media type.","requirements":["Set accurate content types","Apply nosniff where appropriate","Validate upload/download response handling"],"constraints":["The header does not correct an incorrect Content-Type","Browser behavior varies by resource type"],"preconditions":["Application serves browser-consumed resources"],"input":"HTTP response and media type","output":"Consistent browser content interpretation","expected_behavior":"Browser receives and handles content according to the intended media type policy.","failure_mode":"Browser interprets a resource as executable or otherwise different content.","root_cause":"Incorrect content type or absence of appropriate browser protection.","solution":"Correct content typing and apply the appropriate anti-sniffing policy.","edge_cases":["User-uploaded content","JSON","Scripts","Stylesheets","Download endpoints"],"security_implications":"Can reduce content-type confusion that contributes to script execution or data exposure.","performance_implications":"Negligible performance impact.","testing":{"method":"Response-header and browser interpretation tests","expected_result":"Content types and anti-sniffing behavior match the documented policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Browser-facing resources use accurate media types and appropriate anti-sniffing controls.","evidence_required":["HTTP headers","Browser tests"],"method":"Integration testing","limitations":"Browser-specific interpretation still requires supported-client testing."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"HTTP/browser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Secure_Headers_Project","MDN_X-Content-Type-Options"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B138","D40-B139"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B141","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Logging","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data exposure in logs","question":"How should applications remediate sensitive information written to logs?","answer":"Classify sensitive fields, remove unnecessary sensitive values from logs, redact or tokenize values where operationally required, and restrict access and retention of security logs. Never treat log storage as automatically safe simply because it is internal.","objective":"Prevent logs from becoming an unintended repository of credentials, tokens, personal data, or secrets.","requirements":["Define logging data classification","Redact sensitive fields","Protect log access","Define retention limits","Review structured logging fields"],"constraints":["Logs must retain sufficient evidence for security and operations","Redaction rules must cover exception and request logging paths"],"preconditions":["Application logs requests, errors, or security events"],"input":"Application event containing sensitive fields","output":"Minimized/redacted log event","expected_behavior":"Only operationally necessary information is recorded and sensitive values are protected.","failure_mode":"Passwords, tokens, secrets, or unnecessary personal data appear in logs.","root_cause":"Logging captures entire request or object payloads without data classification.","solution":"Use structured event schemas and explicit sensitive-field redaction.","edge_cases":["Exception traces","Request bodies","Authorization headers","Database errors","Debug mode"],"security_implications":"Reduces secondary exposure through centralized log systems.","performance_implications":"Structured redaction adds small processing overhead; large payload logging can itself be expensive.","testing":{"method":"Log-capture tests using synthetic secrets and sensitive fields","expected_result":"Sensitive test values are absent or appropriately transformed in logs."},"verification":{"claim":"Documented sensitive fields are not emitted in operational logs except where explicitly justified and protected.","evidence_required":["Logging schema","Redaction configuration","Synthetic-secret tests"],"method":"Code review and log inspection","limitations":"Third-party libraries may emit additional data and require configuration."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Logging framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Logging_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-532"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B143"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B142","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Logging","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient security event logging","question":"How should insufficient security logging be remediated?","answer":"Define security-relevant events and record sufficient structured context to reconstruct what occurred, while minimizing sensitive data. Include reliable timestamps, event identity, actor or service identity where appropriate, outcome, and relevant correlation information.","objective":"Make security-relevant actions observable enough for detection and investigation.","requirements":["Define event taxonomy","Capture successful and failed security events where appropriate","Use structured fields","Protect log integrity and access","Synchronize or normalize timestamps"],"constraints":["Logging every event is not automatically useful","Sensitive data must be minimized"],"preconditions":["Security-sensitive application actions lack adequate telemetry"],"input":"Security-relevant event","output":"Structured audit/security event","expected_behavior":"Relevant security actions produce sufficient protected evidence for investigation.","failure_mode":"Important authentication, authorization, or administrative activity cannot be reconstructed.","root_cause":"No explicit event taxonomy or inconsistent logging implementation.","solution":"Define security event requirements and instrument protected operations consistently.","edge_cases":["Distributed services","Asynchronous jobs","Failed authorization","Administrative impersonation","Partial failures"],"security_implications":"Improves detection, investigation, and accountability.","performance_implications":"Logging introduces I/O and serialization overhead; asynchronous buffered logging can alter durability semantics.","testing":{"method":"Event-generation tests for defined security scenarios","expected_result":"Required fields and outcomes are recorded according to policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Defined security events generate the required audit evidence.","evidence_required":["Event taxonomy","Representative log records","Coverage tests"],"method":"Logging coverage review and integration tests","limitations":"Logs are evidence but do not by themselves prove an event did not occur."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application and logging architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Logging_Cheat_Sheet","OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B143"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B143","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Audit Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Tamperable application audit logs","question":"How should applications remediate audit records that privileged users can alter without detection?","answer":"Separate audit storage and administrative authority where appropriate, restrict write/delete access, record append-oriented events, and use integrity controls such as centralized protected storage or cryptographic integrity mechanisms when required by the threat model.","objective":"Increase confidence that security-relevant audit records have not been silently modified or deleted.","requirements":["Separate audit access from normal application privileges","Restrict modification and deletion","Protect audit transport and storage","Monitor audit pipeline failures"],"constraints":["Cryptographic integrity controls do not replace access control","Retention and legal requirements vary by environment"],"preconditions":["Audit logs are security evidence","Application or privileged users can alter audit storage"],"input":"Security event","output":"Protected audit record","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized modification or deletion of audit evidence is prevented or detectable according to policy.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies audit records to conceal activity.","root_cause":"Audit records share authority and storage controls with the application under investigation.","solution":"Use separated, protected audit storage and integrity controls appropriate to the threat model.","edge_cases":["Offline logging","Queue failures","Clock skew","Privileged administrators","Retention deletion"],"security_implications":"Improves forensic integrity and accountability.","performance_implications":"Remote or durable audit writes add latency and storage cost.","testing":{"method":"Controlled attempts to modify/delete audit records and pipeline-failure tests","expected_result":"Unauthorized changes fail or generate detectable integrity evidence."},"verification":{"claim":"Audit records have the protection and integrity properties specified by the audit policy.","evidence_required":["Storage permissions","Integrity mechanism","Tamper tests"],"method":"Configuration review and controlled security testing","limitations":"No audit system guarantees absolute tamper resistance against all privileged infrastructure operators."},"languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Deployment architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Logging_Cheat_Sheet","NIST_SP_800-92"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B142"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B144","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Error Handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive information in error responses","question":"How should applications remediate verbose production error responses?","answer":"Return stable, minimal client-facing error information and keep diagnostic details in protected server-side logs. Avoid returning stack traces, SQL statements, filesystem paths, internal hostnames, secrets, or dependency details to untrusted callers.","objective":"Prevent internal implementation details from becoming externally visible reconnaissance data.","requirements":["Define external error contract","Separate diagnostic data from client responses","Disable debug exception exposure in production","Use correlation identifiers for investigation"],"constraints":["Clients need actionable error semantics but not internal implementation details","Debug behavior must be separately controlled for development environments"],"preconditions":["Application returns framework or exception details to clients"],"input":"Unhandled or handled application error","output":"Controlled error response plus protected diagnostic record","expected_behavior":"Clients receive only documented error information while operators retain necessary diagnostics.","failure_mode":"Stack traces or internal data are returned to callers.","root_cause":"Development-oriented exception rendering is enabled or error mapping is absent.","solution":"Implement centralized error handling and production-safe response contracts.","edge_cases":["Validation errors","Database failures","Third-party API errors","Batch requests","GraphQL errors"],"security_implications":"Reduces information disclosure and reconnaissance.","performance_implications":"Centralized error mapping can simplify processing and avoid expensive repeated formatting.","testing":{"method":"Forced exception tests across representative endpoints","expected_result":"External responses contain no prohibited internal details while diagnostics remain available internally."},"verification":{"claim":"Production error responses conform to the documented information-disclosure policy.","evidence_required":["Error handler configuration","Negative error tests","Production-mode settings"],"method":"Integration testing and configuration review","limitations":"Infrastructure proxies may independently expose error information."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework and deployment dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Error_Handling_Cheat_Sheet","CWE-209"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B146"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B145","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Production debug mode","question":"How should applications remediate debug configuration enabled in production?","answer":"Separate development and production configuration, fail safely when security-critical configuration is missing, and verify deployment configuration through automated checks. Production should not expose development diagnostics or debugging interfaces unless explicitly secured and required.","objective":"Prevent development-only diagnostic capabilities from becoming production attack surfaces.","requirements":["Separate environment configuration","Disable development diagnostics in production","Validate security-critical configuration","Detect unsafe configuration during deployment"],"constraints":["Configuration behavior varies by framework","A deployment-time check should not rely solely on a developer remembering a setting"],"preconditions":["Application supports debug/development modes"],"input":"Deployment configuration","output":"Validated production configuration","expected_behavior":"Production starts with security-appropriate settings and rejects unsafe configurations where feasible.","failure_mode":"Debug interface or verbose diagnostics are exposed publicly.","root_cause":"Environment configuration is implicit, mutable, or insufficiently validated.","solution":"Use explicit environment configuration and automated deployment checks.","edge_cases":["Staging environments","Feature flags","Hot reload","Container environment variables","Blue/green deployments"],"security_implications":"Reduces information disclosure and accidental exposure of development capabilities.","performance_implications":"Debug instrumentation can impose substantial overhead; disabling it may improve production performance.","testing":{"method":"Deployment configuration validation and production-mode integration tests","expected_result":"Unsafe debug settings fail validation or remain inaccessible to untrusted callers."},"verification":{"claim":"Production deployments enforce the documented non-debug security configuration.","evidence_required":["Configuration schema","Deployment validation","Runtime mode test"],"method":"CI/CD validation and integration testing","limitations":"Infrastructure configuration outside the application must also be reviewed."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework/deployment specific.","provenance":["OWASP_Secure_Deployment_Guideline","CWE-489"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B144","D40-B146"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B146","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Dependency Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Known vulnerable dependency","question":"How should applications remediate a dependency with a known security vulnerability?","answer":"Inventory the affected dependency and version, determine whether the vulnerable code path is reachable, upgrade to a supported fixed version when available, apply an official mitigation when upgrade is temporarily impossible, and regression-test compatibility.","objective":"Remove or contain known dependency vulnerabilities without introducing uncontrolled migration risk.","requirements":["Identify exact dependency version","Map advisory to affected versions","Select supported remediation version","Review transitive dependencies","Run regression tests"],"constraints":["A version upgrade may require code changes","Reachability analysis can inform prioritization but does not make a vulnerable dependency safe by itself"],"preconditions":["Dependency is known to have a security advisory affecting the deployed version"],"input":"Dependency inventory and advisory","output":"Remediated dependency state","expected_behavior":"Affected dependency is upgraded, removed, or controlled according to a documented risk treatment.","failure_mode":"Known vulnerable component remains deployed without a documented remediation decision.","root_cause":"Dependency inventory, update process, or vulnerability response is incomplete.","solution":"Patch/upgrade, remove, or apply a documented vendor mitigation and verify the resulting dependency graph.","edge_cases":["Transitive dependency","Abandoned package","Breaking major version","Backported vendor patch","Multiple dependency constraints"],"security_implications":"Reduces exposure to publicly documented dependency vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"New dependency versions can change performance characteristics and should be regression-tested where material.","testing":{"method":"Dependency resolution, security scanner, unit/integration regression tests","expected_result":"Resolved dependency graph contains the intended fixed/supported version and application tests remain valid."},"verification":{"claim":"The deployed dependency graph no longer contains the affected vulnerable version unless an explicitly documented exception exists.","evidence_required":["Dependency lockfile","Advisory mapping","Build artifact inventory","Regression results"],"method":"Software composition analysis plus build verification","limitations":"A clean scanner result does not prove absence of unknown vulnerabilities."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Dependency and advisory specific.","provenance":["CVE_Program","CWE","Official_Vendor_Security_Advisory"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B147","D40-B148"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B147","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Dependency Security","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"Dependency pinning and reproducible security remediation","question":"How should dependency versions be controlled so security fixes are reproducible?","answer":"Use a dependency manifest and appropriate lock or resolution mechanism, review changes, record exact resolved versions, and rebuild from controlled inputs. Do not confuse pinning with permanent security; pinned versions still require vulnerability monitoring and updates.","objective":"Make dependency remediation deterministic and auditable while preserving the ability to update securely.","requirements":["Record dependency requirements","Record resolved versions","Review dependency changes","Monitor pinned versions for advisories"],"constraints":["Exact pinning can slow security updates if not actively maintained","Language package ecosystems differ in lockfile semantics"],"preconditions":["Application has external package dependencies"],"input":"Dependency requirements","output":"Reproducible resolved dependency graph","expected_behavior":"Builds resolve to controlled versions that can be audited and updated deliberately.","failure_mode":"Builds silently resolve different dependency versions or retain vulnerable versions unnoticed.","root_cause":"Dependency resolution is uncontrolled or untracked.","solution":"Adopt appropriate lock/resolution files and automated dependency monitoring.","edge_cases":["Transitive dependencies","Platform-specific packages","Optional dependencies","Multiple lockfiles","Private registries"],"security_implications":"Improves traceability and controlled vulnerability response.","performance_implications":"Deterministic resolution may reduce build-time dependency solving but can require lockfile maintenance.","testing":{"method":"Clean-environment rebuild and dependency graph comparison","expected_result":"Resolved versions match the controlled dependency state."},"verification":{"claim":"The build process resolves dependencies according to the recorded dependency policy.","evidence_required":["Lock/resolution file","Clean build output","Dependency graph"],"method":"Reproducible-build testing","limitations":"Reproducibility also depends on toolchains and external build inputs."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Package-manager specific.","provenance":["Official_Package_Manager_Documentation","SLSA_Supply_Chain_Security_Framework"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B146","D40-B148"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B148","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted build artifact provenance","question":"How should organizations remediate inability to determine where a production artifact came from?","answer":"Record build inputs, source revision, dependency resolution, build process metadata, and artifact identity through a controlled software supply-chain process. Generate and retain provenance metadata appropriate to the deployment assurance requirements.","objective":"Enable verification of artifact origin and build inputs.","requirements":["Identify source revision","Record dependency versions","Identify build process","Bind provenance to artifact identity","Protect provenance metadata"],"constraints":["Provenance records describe build origin; they do not by themselves prove source correctness","Assurance level depends on how build inputs and attestations are protected"],"preconditions":["Production artifacts are built without reliable provenance records"],"input":"Source and build inputs","output":"Identifiable artifact with provenance","expected_behavior":"Operators can determine the source revision and relevant build inputs associated with a deployed artifact.","failure_mode":"Production artifact cannot be traced to a controlled source/build state.","root_cause":"Build pipeline does not produce or retain provenance metadata.","solution":"Introduce reproducible build metadata and signed/controlled provenance where appropriate.","edge_cases":["Manual builds","Hotfix binaries","Container layers","Third-party binaries","Emergency releases"],"security_implications":"Improves supply-chain traceability and incident response.","performance_implications":"Provenance generation adds build metadata processing but usually small runtime impact.","testing":{"method":"Build two controlled artifacts and compare provenance association","expected_result":"Each artifact can be traced to its recorded source and build inputs."},"verification":{"claim":"Production artifacts have provenance sufficient for the defined supply-chain assurance requirement.","evidence_required":["Artifact digest","Source revision","Build metadata","Dependency graph"],"method":"Pipeline audit and artifact verification","limitations":"Provenance does not establish that every input is trustworthy."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Build-system and supply-chain framework dependent.","provenance":["SLSA_Supply_Chain_Security_Framework","in-toto_Specification"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B146","D40-B147"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B149","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Security Regression","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security regression test after remediation","question":"How should a security remediation be verified so the original vulnerability does not silently return?","answer":"Convert the vulnerability into a deterministic regression test that reproduces the security boundary, asserts the secure behavior, and runs automatically with relevant test suites. Preserve the original failure conditions as test inputs where they are safe and reproducible.","objective":"Prevent future changes from reintroducing a previously remediated vulnerability.","requirements":["Represent the original security condition as a test","Assert the security property rather than only an implementation detail","Run the regression test automatically","Retain provenance linking the test to the vulnerability"],"constraints":["A single regression test does not prove absence of all related vulnerabilities","Tests must avoid harmful external effects"],"preconditions":["A vulnerability has been remediated"],"input":"Original vulnerability condition","output":"Repeatable security regression test","expected_behavior":"The previously exploitable condition fails safely after remediation.","failure_mode":"Future code changes restore the vulnerable behavior unnoticed.","root_cause":"Remediation was implemented without a durable regression artifact.","solution":"Create a targeted regression test and integrate it into the appropriate automated test layer.","edge_cases":["Equivalent bypass variants","Framework upgrades","Configuration changes","Multiple attack paths"],"security_implications":"Preserves remediation against regression and supports continuous verification.","performance_implications":"Adds test-suite execution cost; targeted tests should remain focused and deterministic.","testing":{"method":"Run regression test before and after controlled vulnerable/fixed states where safely possible","expected_result":"Vulnerable state fails the security assertion and fixed state satisfies it."},"verification":{"claim":"The original security property is continuously checked by automated regression testing.","evidence_required":["Regression test","Link to vulnerability record","Automated test result"],"method":"Test execution and traceability review","limitations":"Regression coverage is limited to encoded attack conditions and variants."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Test-framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard","NIST_SP_800-115"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B146","D40-B149"]}
{"record_id":"D40-B150","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Security Remediation Lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"End-to-end security remediation verification and regression workflow","question":"What evidence should establish that a security vulnerability has been remediated without claiming more than was verified?","answer":"Record the vulnerability and affected surface, root cause, remediation change, relevant version, targeted tests, observed results, residual limitations, and post-remediation verification evidence. Distinguish code review, static analysis, execution, test passing, and security verification as separate evidence types.","objective":"Create an auditable remediation chain from vulnerability discovery through verified regression protection.","requirements":["Preserve vulnerability identity","Record affected versions/scope","Document root cause","Record remediation","Run appropriate security tests","Record verification evidence and limitations","Create regression coverage"],"constraints":["Passing a test does not prove universal security","Static analysis, execution, and verification provide different evidence","Unverified claims must remain explicitly unverified"],"preconditions":["A security issue has been identified and a remediation is proposed or implemented"],"input":"Security finding and remediation change","output":"Evidence-backed remediation record","expected_behavior":"The record allows another engineer to determine what was fixed, how it was tested, what evidence exists, and what remains uncertain.","failure_mode":"Remediation is marked fixed solely because code changed or a limited test passed.","root_cause":"Verification evidence is not separated from implementation status.","solution":"Use a structured remediation lifecycle with explicit validation and verification states.","edge_cases":["Partial fixes","Configuration-only fixes","Dependency fixes","Environment-specific vulnerabilities","Unverified production deployment"],"security_implications":"Improves auditability and reduces false confidence in security fixes.","performance_implications":"Verification activities can increase CI/security-testing cost but provide evidence for risk decisions.","testing":{"method":"Traceability review from finding to remediation commit, test, result, and regression case","expected_result":"Every claimed remediation state has corresponding evidence and limitations."},"verification":{"claim":"The remediation record accurately represents the evidence available for the affected vulnerability and does not overstate verification.","evidence_required":["Finding","Root-cause analysis","Remediation artifact","Test results","Verification evidence","Regression test"],"method":"Independent remediation review and evidence-chain audit","limitations":"No finite verification process proves that a system contains no undiscovered security vulnerabilities."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Process and toolchain independent; implementation evidence remains version specific.","provenance":["NIST_SP_800-115","OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard","CWE"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B146","D40-B148"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B151","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"SSRF prevention through destination validation","question":"How should an application remediate server-side request forgery?","answer":"Restrict outbound destinations using an explicit allowlist where feasible, validate the destination before connection, resolve and validate addresses carefully, restrict protocols and ports, and enforce network-layer egress controls. Do not rely only on blocking obvious private IP strings.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from causing unauthorized server-side network requests.","requirements":["Validate externally supplied URLs or hostnames before making requests.","Restrict permitted schemes, ports, hosts, or network destinations.","Enforce outbound network policy independently of application validation where possible."],"constraints":["DNS resolution can change between validation and connection.","IPv4, IPv6, redirects, alternate address representations, and URL parsing differences must be considered.","Some legitimate integrations require controlled outbound access."],"preconditions":["The application performs outbound network requests based partly on untrusted input."],"input":"User-controlled URL or destination identifier.","output":"Only an explicitly permitted outbound request is issued; disallowed destinations are rejected.","expected_behavior":"Requests to unauthorized internal, loopback, link-local, metadata, or otherwise restricted destinations are rejected.","failure_mode":"An attacker supplies a crafted destination that causes the server to access an unintended network resource.","root_cause":"Untrusted destination data is passed to a network client without robust destination policy enforcement.","solution":"Use strict URL parsing, scheme and port allowlists, destination authorization, controlled DNS resolution, redirect restrictions, and network egress controls.","edge_cases":["IPv6 loopback and private ranges","DNS rebinding","Redirects to restricted destinations","Alternative numeric IP representations","Unexpected URL schemes","Credential-bearing URLs"],"security_implications":"SSRF can expose internal services, credentials, metadata endpoints, or privileged control interfaces.","performance_implications":"Additional DNS and destination checks add small request overhead; centralized egress policy can reduce repeated application checks.","testing":{"method":"Security negative tests with public, private, loopback, link-local, metadata, redirected, and malformed destinations.","expected_result":"Only policy-approved destinations are reachable."},"verification":{"claim":"Outbound destination authorization prevents unauthorized server-side requests.","evidence_required":["Destination allowlist tests","Network egress policy evidence","Redirect handling tests"],"method":"Review validation and egress controls and execute negative test cases.","limitations":"Network-level behavior depends on deployment topology."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#"],"version_scope":"Language/framework independent; HTTP client behavior must be verified for the selected implementation.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","CWE-918","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B152","D40-B153","D40-B154","D40-B155"]} {"record_id":"D40-B152","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"SSRF redirect validation","question":"Why must SSRF defenses validate redirects as well as the initial destination?","answer":"A safe initial destination can redirect to an unauthorized destination. The HTTP client should either disable redirects or validate every redirect target against the same destination policy before following it.","objective":"Prevent redirect-based bypass of SSRF destination controls.","requirements":["Treat every redirect target as a new destination.","Apply scheme, host, port, and network-address policy to redirect targets.","Reject redirects that leave the approved destination set."],"constraints":["Redirect chains can contain multiple hops.","Some clients automatically follow redirects unless configured otherwise."],"preconditions":["The HTTP client follows redirects."],"input":"HTTP response containing a redirect to another URL.","output":"Redirect is followed only when the target satisfies destination policy.","expected_behavior":"A redirect to an unauthorized network destination is rejected.","failure_mode":"Initial URL is allowed but redirects the server into a protected network.","root_cause":"Authorization is applied only to the user-supplied URL rather than every outbound destination.","solution":"Disable automatic redirects when possible or implement explicit redirect handling with per-hop destination validation.","edge_cases":["Multiple redirects","Relative redirects","Scheme changes","Redirects from public to private addresses","Redirect loops"],"security_implications":"Redirect handling can turn a seemingly safe SSRF endpoint into an internal network access primitive.","performance_implications":"Per-hop validation adds processing proportional to redirect count.","testing":{"method":"Create redirect chains ending at prohibited destinations.","expected_result":"The request is rejected before reaching the prohibited destination."},"verification":{"claim":"Redirect targets cannot bypass SSRF authorization.","evidence_required":["Redirect-chain tests","HTTP client configuration","Network telemetry"],"method":"Inspect redirect policy and test allowed and prohibited chains.","limitations":"Actual network reachability must be tested in the target deployment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"HTTP-client behavior varies by library and version; verify redirect defaults before relying on them.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B153"]} {"record_id":"D40-B153","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"SSRF DNS rebinding resistance","question":"How can SSRF defenses reduce DNS rebinding bypasses?","answer":"Resolve and validate the destination under controlled conditions and ensure the connection is made to an address that remains authorized. Prefer network-level egress restrictions because application-only hostname validation can become stale between DNS resolution and connection.","objective":"Prevent DNS changes from turning an initially approved hostname into an unauthorized destination.","requirements":["Validate resolved addresses against destination policy.","Account for repeated DNS resolution and connection behavior.","Use network egress controls as defense in depth."],"constraints":["DNS caching and resolver behavior vary.","A hostname can legitimately resolve to multiple addresses."],"preconditions":["The application accepts hostnames and performs DNS resolution."],"input":"Hostname controlled or influenced by an untrusted party.","output":"Connection occurs only to policy-approved addresses.","expected_behavior":"Resolution to restricted address space causes rejection or network-level blocking.","failure_mode":"Validation observes a benign address while connection reaches a restricted address.","root_cause":"Destination authorization is separated from the actual connection destination.","solution":"Use controlled resolution and connection logic, pin validated destinations where appropriate, and enforce egress policy at the network layer.","edge_cases":["Multiple A/AAAA records","Short DNS TTLs","IPv6","Resolver differences","Connection retries"],"security_implications":"DNS rebinding can bypass hostname-based SSRF filters and expose internal resources.","performance_implications":"Controlled resolution can add DNS latency and reduce cache effectiveness.","testing":{"method":"Test changing DNS responses between validation and connection in an isolated environment.","expected_result":"Restricted addresses are never reached.","limitations":"Testing must use a controlled DNS environment."},"verification":{"claim":"Destination authorization remains valid through resolution and connection.","evidence_required":["Resolver behavior","Address validation tests","Network policy evidence"],"method":"Review the complete resolution-to-connect path.","limitations":"Application-level review cannot prove infrastructure-level enforcement alone."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Runtime and HTTP client DNS behavior must be verified for the deployed versions.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B152","D40-B154"]} {"record_id":"D40-B154","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cloud metadata endpoint protection","question":"What remediation is appropriate when SSRF can reach cloud instance metadata services?","answer":"Prevent application-controlled outbound requests from reaching metadata endpoints and apply platform-supported metadata protections where available. Treat metadata access as a privileged trust boundary rather than merely filtering one known address.","objective":"Prevent SSRF from obtaining instance credentials or sensitive cloud metadata.","requirements":["Restrict access from application workloads to metadata services.","Use platform-supported metadata hardening where available.","Apply least privilege to workload identities."],"constraints":["Cloud providers differ in metadata mechanisms and controls.","Blocking one IP alone may not address alternate metadata access paths."],"preconditions":["The workload runs in an environment exposing instance or workload metadata."],"input":"Server-side request targeting a metadata service.","output":"Metadata request is denied.","expected_behavior":"Application-originated requests cannot retrieve privileged metadata credentials.","failure_mode":"An SSRF primitive retrieves metadata containing credentials or sensitive configuration.","root_cause":"Metadata service is reachable from an attacker-influenced network request path.","solution":"Combine application SSRF prevention, metadata-service hardening, workload identity least privilege, and network isolation.","edge_cases":["IPv6 metadata paths","Container metadata access","Proxy-based access","Redirect chains"],"security_implications":"Metadata compromise can escalate SSRF into cloud credential compromise.","performance_implications":"Network isolation generally has low application overhead but can affect legitimate metadata-dependent components.","testing":{"method":"Attempt controlled metadata access from the application security test environment.","expected_result":"Metadata requests are blocked or return no privileged credentials."},"verification":{"claim":"Application workloads cannot use SSRF to retrieve privileged metadata.","evidence_required":["Cloud metadata configuration","Network policy","Negative security tests"],"method":"Review platform controls and test the application request path.","limitations":"Provider-specific behavior must be verified against current documentation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-platform specific; verify current provider metadata controls.","provenance":["CWE-918","OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","Cloud provider security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B153","D40-B155"]} {"record_id":"D40-B155","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"SSRF protocol restriction","question":"Why should SSRF remediation restrict URL schemes and destination ports?","answer":"Allowing arbitrary schemes or ports can expose non-HTTP protocols and administrative services. Applications should explicitly permit only the protocols and ports required by their integration.","objective":"Reduce SSRF attack surface beyond hostname validation.","requirements":["Allow only required URL schemes.","Restrict destination ports to required service ports.","Reject protocol handlers that are not part of the integration contract."],"constraints":["Some HTTP libraries support multiple URI schemes or handlers.","Required integrations may use nonstandard ports."],"preconditions":["The application accepts or constructs user-influenced URLs."],"input":"URL containing a scheme and destination port.","output":"Request proceeds only when both satisfy policy.","expected_behavior":"Unsupported schemes and unauthorized ports are rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker uses an alternate protocol or management port to reach an unintended service.","root_cause":"SSRF validation focuses only on hostname or IP address.","solution":"Implement positive scheme and port allowlists and use a narrowly configured network client.","edge_cases":["Case variations in schemes","Default ports","IPv6 port syntax","Non-HTTP handlers"],"security_implications":"Protocol and port restrictions reduce access to internal administrative and non-HTTP services.","performance_implications":"Negligible validation overhead.","testing":{"method":"Test permitted and prohibited schemes and ports.","expected_result":"Only explicitly approved combinations succeed."},"verification":{"claim":"Unsupported protocols and ports are blocked.","evidence_required":["URL validation tests","Client configuration"],"method":"Review and execute scheme/port negative tests.","limitations":"Exact supported schemes depend on the HTTP client."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"HTTP client and URL parser behavior is library/version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B152"]} {"record_id":"D40-B156","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Transport Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"TLS certificate validation","question":"How should an application remediate disabled TLS certificate verification?","answer":"Enable the TLS client's normal certificate-chain and hostname verification and use a trusted certificate authority configuration appropriate for the deployment. Do not replace validation with unconditional trust of presented certificates.","objective":"Prevent man-in-the-middle attacks caused by accepting untrusted TLS certificates.","requirements":["Validate certificate chains.","Validate the intended hostname.","Use an appropriate trust store.","Fail closed when certificate validation fails."],"constraints":["Private PKI deployments require explicit trusted roots.","Development environments may require different trust configuration but must not weaken production verification."],"preconditions":["The application makes TLS-protected network connections."],"input":"TLS server certificate chain.","output":"Connection succeeds only when certificate validation passes.","expected_behavior":"Untrusted, expired, mismatched, or otherwise invalid certificates cause connection failure.","failure_mode":"Client accepts an attacker-controlled or invalid certificate.","root_cause":"Certificate verification is disabled or trust is configured too broadly.","solution":"Use the runtime/library default verification behavior with a properly managed trust store and explicit private CA configuration when required.","edge_cases":["Private CA","Certificate rotation","Expired certificates","Hostname mismatch","Intermediate CA changes"],"security_implications":"Disabling validation can expose credentials and application data to interception.","performance_implications":"Certificate validation adds small connection-establishment overhead.","testing":{"method":"Use controlled invalid, expired, and hostname-mismatched certificates.","expected_result":"TLS connection fails closed."},"verification":{"claim":"Production TLS connections validate peer identity.","evidence_required":["Client configuration","Negative certificate tests","Trust-store configuration"],"method":"Inspect configuration and test invalid certificates.","limitations":"Trust-store contents vary by operating system and deployment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"TLS library/runtime behavior must be verified for deployed versions.","provenance":["CWE-295","OWASP Transport Layer Security guidance","RFC 5280"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B157","D40-B158","D40-B159"]} {"record_id":"D40-B157","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Transport Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"TLS hostname verification","question":"Why is certificate-chain validation insufficient without hostname verification?","answer":"A certificate can be validly signed yet issued for a different hostname. TLS clients must verify that the requested server identity matches the certificate identity according to the applicable verification rules.","objective":"Ensure the authenticated TLS endpoint corresponds to the intended service name.","requirements":["Enable hostname verification.","Use the hostname intended for the connection.","Do not disable identity checks to accommodate certificate deployment errors."],"constraints":["IP-address connections require appropriate certificate identities.","Wildcard certificate matching has defined scope."],"preconditions":["The application uses TLS with named endpoints."],"input":"Requested hostname and peer certificate.","output":"Connection is accepted only when identity validation succeeds.","expected_behavior":"A valid certificate for another hostname is rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker presents a trusted certificate for an unrelated hostname.","root_cause":"Certificate trust is checked without endpoint identity verification.","solution":"Use TLS client settings that enforce hostname verification and correct certificate identities.","edge_cases":["Wildcard certificates","Internationalized domain names","IP literals","Proxy TLS termination"],"security_implications":"Missing hostname verification permits endpoint impersonation despite a trusted certificate.","performance_implications":"Negligible runtime overhead after handshake setup.","testing":{"method":"Connect to a controlled endpoint presenting a trusted certificate for another hostname.","expected_result":"Connection fails hostname verification."},"verification":{"claim":"TLS peer identity is verified against the intended hostname.","evidence_required":["TLS client configuration","Hostname mismatch test"],"method":"Inspect verification settings and perform a negative test.","limitations":"Exact identity matching semantics depend on TLS library and platform."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"TLS client implementation dependent.","provenance":["CWE-297","RFC 6125"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B156","D40-B158"]} {"record_id":"D40-B158","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Transport Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure transport downgrade prevention","question":"How should applications remediate insecure fallback from HTTPS to plaintext HTTP?","answer":"Use secure transport by default, reject unexpected scheme downgrades, configure redirects and clients so that credentials are not sent over plaintext, and use transport security controls appropriate to the deployment.","objective":"Prevent confidentiality and integrity loss caused by transport downgrade.","requirements":["Require HTTPS for protected resources.","Do not automatically downgrade secure requests to plaintext.","Avoid sending credentials over HTTP.","Use HSTS where applicable for web applications."],"constraints":["Legacy integrations may require migration before HTTPS-only enforcement.","TLS termination architecture must preserve secure client-to-service semantics."],"preconditions":["The application handles security-sensitive network traffic."],"input":"Request or redirect from HTTPS to HTTP.","output":"Downgrade is rejected or safely handled without credential disclosure.","expected_behavior":"Protected requests remain on authenticated encrypted transport.","failure_mode":"Sensitive credentials or data are transmitted over plaintext.","root_cause":"Client or server permits insecure transport fallback.","solution":"Enforce HTTPS, configure secure redirects, use HSTS where appropriate, and migrate legacy endpoints.","edge_cases":["Reverse proxies","Internal HTTP hops","Mixed-content browser behavior","Legacy clients"],"security_implications":"Plaintext transport enables interception and manipulation.","performance_implications":"TLS adds connection and cryptographic overhead but modern connection reuse reduces repeated setup costs.","testing":{"method":"Test HTTP access, HTTPS redirects, proxy configurations, and credential-bearing requests.","expected_result":"Sensitive traffic never falls back to plaintext."},"verification":{"claim":"Protected application traffic cannot downgrade to plaintext transport.","evidence_required":["TLS configuration","HTTP behavior tests","Proxy configuration"],"method":"Inspect deployment path and run downgrade tests.","limitations":"End-to-end verification must include intermediary infrastructure."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Deployment and protocol configuration dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security guidance","CWE-319","RFC 6797"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B156","D40-B159","D40-B160"]} {"record_id":"D40-B159","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Transport Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"TLS protocol and cipher configuration","question":"How should weak TLS protocol or cipher configuration be remediated?","answer":"Configure the TLS stack to use currently supported secure protocol versions and cryptographic suites appropriate to the deployment, remove obsolete protocols and weak algorithms, and verify compatibility before enforcement.","objective":"Reduce exposure to known weaknesses in obsolete transport cryptography.","requirements":["Disable obsolete protocol versions where unsupported by security requirements.","Prefer modern authenticated encryption and secure key exchange.","Keep TLS libraries patched."],"constraints":["Compatibility requirements vary by clients and infrastructure.","Exact cipher availability depends on TLS implementation and version."],"preconditions":["The application or proxy terminates TLS."],"input":"TLS configuration and client compatibility requirements.","output":"A supported secure configuration with documented compatibility.","expected_behavior":"Weak or obsolete protocol/cipher combinations are unavailable.","failure_mode":"An attacker forces or exploits a weak transport configuration.","root_cause":"Legacy TLS settings remain enabled without an explicit compatibility requirement.","solution":"Baseline the deployment, remove obsolete settings, patch TLS libraries, and validate supported clients.","edge_cases":["Legacy clients","Protocol negotiation","FIPS-constrained environments","Private PKI"],"security_implications":"Weak cryptographic transport can compromise confidentiality or integrity.","performance_implications":"Cipher and protocol choices can affect CPU cost and handshake behavior; measure before optimization decisions.","testing":{"method":"Use configuration scanners and controlled connection tests against permitted and prohibited protocol suites.","expected_result":"Only approved configurations negotiate."},"verification":{"claim":"TLS configuration meets the deployment security baseline.","evidence_required":["Effective TLS configuration","Scanner results","Compatibility tests"],"method":"Inspect effective configuration and test negotiation.","limitations":"Security baselines change over time and must be versioned."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"TLS implementation and deployment version specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-52","OWASP Transport Layer Security guidance","IETF TLS specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B156","D40-B158","D40-B160"]} {"record_id":"D40-B160","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Transport Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Certificate lifecycle remediation","question":"What should remediation include when certificate expiration or rotation repeatedly causes insecure workarounds?","answer":"Automate certificate issuance and renewal where appropriate, monitor expiration, maintain a documented trust chain, and fail safely when certificates are invalid rather than disabling validation.","objective":"Prevent operational certificate failures from causing insecure TLS configuration changes.","requirements":["Monitor certificate expiration.","Automate renewal where feasible.","Test certificate rotation.","Keep trust configuration synchronized."],"constraints":["Certificate authorities and renewal mechanisms vary.","Emergency rotation must preserve trust continuity."],"preconditions":["The service uses managed TLS certificates."],"input":"Certificate lifecycle state.","output":"Renewed certificates are deployed before expiration and validated after rotation.","expected_behavior":"Certificate rotation occurs without disabling identity validation.","failure_mode":"Operators disable TLS verification to restore connectivity after certificate failure.","root_cause":"Certificate lifecycle is unmanaged or insufficiently tested.","solution":"Implement automated lifecycle management, monitoring, staged rotation, and rollback-safe deployment.","edge_cases":["Intermediate certificate changes","Revocation events","Clock skew","Multiple service replicas"],"security_implications":"Operational shortcuts can create persistent trust bypasses.","performance_implications":"Monitoring and renewal automation have low overhead.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled certificate rotation and expiry simulations.","expected_result":"Service remains secure throughout rotation and rejects invalid certificates."},"verification":{"claim":"Certificate lifecycle failures do not require disabling TLS verification.","evidence_required":["Renewal automation","Expiry alerts","Rotation test evidence"],"method":"Review lifecycle controls and controlled rotation results.","limitations":"CA-specific automation behavior must be validated."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Deployment/PKI dependent.","provenance":["NIST TLS guidance","OWASP Transport Layer Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B156","D40-B159"]} {"record_id":"D40-B161","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP Request Smuggling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"HTTP request smuggling remediation","question":"How should HTTP request smuggling risk be reduced?","answer":"Use consistent HTTP parsing across front-end proxies and back-end servers, keep HTTP infrastructure patched, avoid ambiguous message framing, and normalize or reject conflicting request framing rather than allowing intermediaries to interpret it differently.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled request boundaries from being interpreted inconsistently by HTTP components.","requirements":["Keep proxy and server HTTP implementations current.","Use consistent request framing semantics.","Reject ambiguous or conflicting message framing."],"constraints":["Mitigation depends heavily on the proxy/server architecture and protocol versions.","HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 translation layers require separate review."],"preconditions":["Traffic passes through multiple HTTP parsing components."],"input":"HTTP request with ambiguous framing metadata.","output":"Request is rejected or interpreted consistently across all components.","expected_behavior":"Front-end and back-end agree on request boundaries.","failure_mode":"A request prefix is interpreted differently by intermediary and origin.","root_cause":"Parser disagreement or ambiguous request framing.","solution":"Align parser behavior, patch intermediaries, reject ambiguous framing, and minimize protocol translation inconsistencies.","edge_cases":["Transfer-Encoding interactions","Content-Length conflicts","HTTP/2 to HTTP/1.1 conversion","Connection reuse"],"security_implications":"Request smuggling can bypass routing, authentication, logging, or security controls.","performance_implications":"Strict parsing can reject malformed traffic and reduce ambiguous connection reuse.","testing":{"method":"Use controlled request-framing tests against the complete proxy-to-origin chain.","expected_result":"Ambiguous framing is rejected or handled consistently."},"verification":{"claim":"The HTTP request path has consistent request-boundary interpretation.","evidence_required":["Proxy/server versions","Effective configuration","Controlled parser tests"],"method":"Review and test the complete request chain.","limitations":"Results are architecture specific."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP infrastructure and server version specific.","provenance":["CWE-444","PortSwigger HTTP request smuggling research","IETF HTTP specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B162","D40-B163","D40-B164"]} {"record_id":"D40-B162","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP Request Smuggling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Conflicting Content-Length remediation","question":"What should an HTTP service do when a request contains conflicting Content-Length values?","answer":"Treat conflicting message framing as invalid and reject the request rather than selecting one value inconsistently. The exact behavior must align across intermediary and origin components.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous request boundaries from enabling request smuggling.","requirements":["Reject conflicting framing.","Ensure intermediary and origin use compatible parsing rules.","Test the full request chain."],"constraints":["Some components may normalize malformed requests differently.","Protocol versions have different framing rules."],"preconditions":["The service accepts HTTP/1.1 or translated HTTP traffic."],"input":"Request with multiple conflicting Content-Length headers.","output":"Request is rejected without forwarding ambiguous content.","expected_behavior":"No component interprets the conflicting values as separate request boundaries.","failure_mode":"Proxy and origin select different body lengths.","root_cause":"Ambiguous framing is accepted by at least one HTTP parser.","solution":"Reject conflicting framing and align parser behavior across infrastructure.","edge_cases":["Duplicate identical Content-Length values","Header normalization","HTTP/2 translation"],"security_implications":"Parser disagreement can create desynchronization and security-control bypasses.","performance_implications":"Rejecting malformed requests avoids expensive downstream processing.","testing":{"method":"Send controlled conflicting framing requests through the actual proxy chain.","expected_result":"Request is rejected consistently."},"verification":{"claim":"Conflicting content lengths cannot create parser disagreement.","evidence_required":["Negative tests","Proxy configuration","Server configuration"],"method":"Run end-to-end parser tests.","limitations":"Exact acceptable duplicate-header behavior is implementation dependent."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"HTTP server/proxy version dependent.","provenance":["CWE-444","IETF HTTP specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B161","D40-B163"]} {"record_id":"D40-B163","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP Response Splitting","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"HTTP response splitting remediation","question":"How should HTTP response splitting caused by untrusted header values be remediated?","answer":"Never construct response headers directly from untrusted values without appropriate validation and encoding. Prefer framework APIs that reject control characters and enforce valid header syntax.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from creating additional HTTP response headers or responses.","requirements":["Validate header values against an allowlist where practical.","Reject CR and LF control characters in header values.","Use framework response-header APIs safely."],"constraints":["Header encoding rules differ by framework and protocol implementation.","Some application values legitimately contain structured characters but not raw control characters."],"preconditions":["Application-derived data is placed into HTTP response headers."],"input":"Untrusted value used as a response-header value.","output":"Only syntactically valid header content is emitted.","expected_behavior":"CR/LF injection is rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker injects response headers or changes response boundaries.","root_cause":"Untrusted data reaches response-header construction without control-character validation.","solution":"Use safe framework APIs and explicitly reject CR/LF and other invalid header content.","edge_cases":["Encoded newline representations","Unicode normalization","Redirect Location values","Cookie values"],"security_implications":"Response splitting can facilitate cache poisoning, header injection, and downstream security bypasses.","performance_implications":"Validation overhead is negligible.","testing":{"method":"Test raw and encoded control-character payloads in every header-producing input path.","expected_result":"Invalid header values are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted input cannot inject HTTP response delimiters.","evidence_required":["Header construction review","Negative tests"],"method":"Trace input-to-header flows and test control characters.","limitations":"Framework normalization behavior must be verified for the selected version."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","C#"],"version_scope":"Framework/server specific.","provenance":["CWE-113","OWASP HTTP Headers guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B161","D40-B164"]} {"record_id":"D40-B164","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP Host Header","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Host header injection remediation","question":"How should applications remediate security-sensitive use of an untrusted HTTP Host header?","answer":"Do not use the Host header as an authorization or trust decision without validating it against an explicit set of accepted hosts. Configure trusted host validation at the application or proxy layer and construct security-sensitive URLs from trusted configuration where possible.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled Host values from influencing security-sensitive behavior.","requirements":["Define accepted hostnames.","Reject unexpected Host values.","Avoid deriving password-reset, callback, or canonical security URLs solely from untrusted Host data."],"constraints":["Multi-tenant systems may legitimately accept multiple hosts.","Proxy deployments may rewrite or preserve host information."],"preconditions":["Application behavior depends on the Host header."],"input":"HTTP request with Host header.","output":"Request is accepted only for an authorized host.","expected_behavior":"Unexpected hostnames are rejected or routed safely without affecting security-sensitive URL generation.","failure_mode":"Attacker supplies a malicious Host that is reflected into security-sensitive links or routing decisions.","root_cause":"Host header is treated as trusted identity data without validation.","solution":"Use trusted host configuration, proxy validation, and explicit tenant/domain mapping.","edge_cases":["Port suffixes","IPv6 host syntax","Reverse proxies","Multi-tenant custom domains"],"security_implications":"Host injection can affect password reset links, cache behavior, routing, and SSRF-like internal routing decisions.","performance_implications":"Host validation is inexpensive.","testing":{"method":"Send requests with unauthorized hostnames through every proxy/application entry point.","expected_result":"Unexpected hosts are rejected or safely isolated."},"verification":{"claim":"Host-derived security decisions use authorized host identities.","evidence_required":["Trusted host configuration","Host injection tests"],"method":"Review host handling and execute negative tests.","limitations":"Proxy normalization can change observed host values."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Framework/proxy dependent.","provenance":["CWE-644","OWASP Host Header Injection guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B163"]} {"record_id":"D40-B165","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Open Redirect","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unvalidated redirect remediation","question":"How should an application remediate an open redirect?","answer":"Avoid redirects based on arbitrary user-supplied URLs. Prefer server-side route identifiers or an explicit allowlist of approved destinations, and validate the final destination before redirecting.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled redirects from being used for phishing or security-flow abuse.","requirements":["Prefer relative application routes for internal redirects.","Allow external destinations only through explicit policy.","Validate redirect targets before issuing the response."],"constraints":["Legitimate external integrations may require redirects.","URL parsing must correctly handle scheme-relative and encoded destinations."],"preconditions":["Application accepts a redirect destination from request data."],"input":"User-supplied redirect target.","output":"Redirect points only to an authorized destination.","expected_behavior":"Unapproved external destinations are rejected or replaced with a safe default.","failure_mode":"Application redirects a trusted user to an attacker-controlled site.","root_cause":"Redirect target is accepted without destination authorization.","solution":"Use route IDs, relative paths, or strict destination allowlists.","edge_cases":["Scheme-relative URLs","Encoded URLs","Open redirect chains","Internationalized domains"],"security_implications":"Open redirects can facilitate phishing and abuse of trusted links and authentication flows.","performance_implications":"Destination validation has negligible cost.","testing":{"method":"Test absolute, scheme-relative, encoded, nested, and external redirect targets.","expected_result":"Only approved targets are accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"User-controlled redirect parameters cannot redirect to unauthorized origins.","evidence_required":["Redirect allowlist","Negative tests"],"method":"Review target construction and test bypass variants.","limitations":"Allowed destination policy is application specific."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Framework routing behavior must be verified for the deployed version.","provenance":["CWE-601","OWASP Unvalidated Redirects guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B164","D40-B166"]} {"record_id":"D40-B166","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook authenticity verification","question":"How should applications remediate unauthenticated or weakly authenticated webhooks?","answer":"Require an authenticity mechanism such as a provider-specified signature, verify the signature over the exact intended payload using a securely stored secret or key, and reject invalid or missing authentication before processing the event.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized parties from injecting forged webhook events.","requirements":["Verify webhook authenticity before business processing.","Use constant-time comparison where applicable.","Protect signing secrets or verification keys.","Define replay protection where the provider supports timestamps or unique event identifiers."],"constraints":["Signature formats and canonicalization vary by provider.","Raw request bytes may be required for correct verification."],"preconditions":["Application accepts inbound webhook events.","Provider supports a documented authentication mechanism."],"input":"Webhook request and authentication metadata.","output":"Event is processed only after successful authenticity verification.","expected_behavior":"Invalid or forged webhook requests are rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker submits a fabricated event that triggers privileged business actions.","root_cause":"Webhook endpoint trusts network reachability or unverified request fields.","solution":"Implement provider-specific signature verification before parsing into trusted business objects.","edge_cases":["Raw-body mutation","Timestamp skew","Duplicate event IDs","Key rotation","Multiple signing algorithms"],"security_implications":"Forged webhooks can trigger unauthorized payments, account changes, deployments, or other high-impact actions.","performance_implications":"Cryptographic verification adds CPU cost proportional to request size and algorithm.","testing":{"method":"Test valid, modified, missing, expired, duplicated, and forged signatures.","expected_result":"Only authentic and policy-valid events are processed."},"verification":{"claim":"Webhook authenticity is verified before side effects.","evidence_required":["Signature verification code","Provider specification","Negative tests"],"method":"Trace request handling order and execute authentication tests.","limitations":"Provider-specific verification requirements must be checked against current documentation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Provider and framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security guidance","Provider-specific official webhook documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B167","D40-B168","D40-B169"]} {"record_id":"D40-B167","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook replay protection","question":"How should webhook replay attacks be remediated?","answer":"When the provider supplies a signed timestamp or unique event identifier, verify freshness and track processed identifiers within an appropriate retention window. Signature validity alone does not necessarily prove that a previously valid event is being delivered for the first time.","objective":"Prevent attackers from replaying captured valid webhook requests.","requirements":["Verify supported timestamps or freshness constraints.","Deduplicate event identifiers.","Apply replay windows appropriate to the business operation."],"constraints":["Provider event semantics determine available replay controls.","Long-running asynchronous delivery may require carefully chosen windows."],"preconditions":["Webhook events can trigger non-idempotent or high-impact actions."],"input":"Authentic webhook request with timestamp or event ID.","output":"Only fresh or previously unprocessed events are accepted.","expected_behavior":"Previously processed or stale events are rejected or safely ignored.","failure_mode":"Captured valid webhook is submitted repeatedly.","root_cause":"Authentication is implemented without freshness or deduplication.","solution":"Add timestamp validation, event-ID tracking, and idempotent processing where possible.","edge_cases":["Clock skew","Legitimate retries","Duplicate deliveries","Event ID collisions"],"security_implications":"Replay can duplicate financial, operational, or authorization-sensitive actions.","performance_implications":"Event-ID storage introduces bounded storage and lookup overhead.","testing":{"method":"Replay valid webhook payloads within and outside the permitted window.","expected_result":"Duplicate or stale events do not cause duplicate side effects."},"verification":{"claim":"Webhook replays cannot create unauthorized repeated effects.","evidence_required":["Replay-window configuration","Deduplication state","Replay tests"],"method":"Inspect event processing and test repeated deliveries.","limitations":"Exact replay semantics depend on provider guarantees."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Provider and persistence implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP webhook security principles","Provider-specific official webhook documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B166","D40-B168"]} {"record_id":"D40-B168","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook idempotency remediation","question":"Why should security-sensitive webhook handlers be idempotent?","answer":"Webhook providers commonly retry deliveries. An idempotent handler ensures the same event does not create duplicate business effects when delivered more than once, reducing both reliability failures and replay impact.","objective":"Prevent duplicate side effects from legitimate retries and repeated delivery.","requirements":["Define a stable event identifier.","Persist processing state atomically with the relevant business operation where possible.","Return successful acknowledgment only according to the provider's delivery contract."],"constraints":["Exactly-once external side effects may require transactional coordination or compensating logic.","Provider retry behavior varies."],"preconditions":["Webhook processing can be repeated.","Events trigger state-changing operations."],"input":"Webhook event with stable identifier.","output":"Repeated delivery produces no duplicate business effect.","expected_behavior":"First valid event applies the intended effect; subsequent identical deliveries are safely ignored or treated as already processed.","failure_mode":"Retry creates duplicate transaction, provisioning, notification, or state transition.","root_cause":"Handler assumes one delivery per event.","solution":"Use idempotency keys/event IDs with durable state and atomic processing semantics.","edge_cases":["Concurrent duplicate deliveries","Database failure after external side effect","Event ID reuse","Partial processing"],"security_implications":"Idempotency limits the impact of replay and delivery duplication.","performance_implications":"Requires durable lookup/state and may increase database contention under duplicate bursts.","testing":{"method":"Deliver identical events sequentially and concurrently.","expected_result":"Business state changes only once."},"verification":{"claim":"Duplicate event delivery does not duplicate protected business effects.","evidence_required":["Idempotency implementation","Concurrent duplicate tests","Persistence transaction behavior"],"method":"Test sequential and concurrent duplicate events.","limitations":"External systems may require separate idempotency controls."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Persistence and framework behavior dependent.","provenance":["OWASP webhook security principles","Provider webhook delivery documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B166","D40-B167","D40-B169"]} {"record_id":"D40-B169","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook secret rotation","question":"How should webhook signing secrets be rotated without creating an authentication gap?","answer":"Support controlled key rotation with overlapping validity when the provider permits it, deploy the new secret before removing the old one, monitor verification failures, and retire the old secret after the documented transition period.","objective":"Maintain webhook authenticity during credential rotation.","requirements":["Protect current and replacement secrets.","Define a rotation window.","Avoid logging secrets.","Validate both keys only according to an explicit migration policy."],"constraints":["Some providers control rotation timing externally.","Accepting multiple secrets increases the temporary trust set."],"preconditions":["Webhook authentication uses rotatable secrets or keys."],"input":"Webhook signed using current or replacement verification material.","output":"Valid events remain verifiable throughout planned rotation.","expected_behavior":"Authorized key transitions do not cause unnecessary outages or require disabling verification.","failure_mode":"Emergency rotation leads to authentication bypass or service outage.","root_cause":"No tested key-rotation process exists.","solution":"Implement staged rotation, explicit key identifiers when available, monitoring, and rapid retirement of old material.","edge_cases":["Provider rotation delay","Concurrent deliveries","Compromised old key","Clock differences"],"security_implications":"Poor rotation can extend exposure of compromised secrets or encourage insecure bypasses.","performance_implications":"Supporting two keys temporarily has minimal verification overhead.","testing":{"method":"Perform a controlled rotation and verify old/new key behavior.","expected_result":"Only keys within the approved rotation window are accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"Webhook credentials can be rotated without disabling authenticity verification.","evidence_required":["Rotation procedure","Key lifecycle configuration","Rotation test"],"method":"Execute a controlled rotation in a non-production environment.","limitations":"Provider-specific capabilities vary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Provider-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","Provider webhook security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B166","D40-B168"]} {"record_id":"D40-B170","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook payload trust boundary","question":"Why must webhook fields remain untrusted after signature verification?","answer":"Authenticity establishes that the event came from the expected signer, not that every field is safe, valid for the current state, or authorized for every operation. Business validation and authorization must still be applied after signature verification.","objective":"Separate message authenticity from business authorization and validation.","requirements":["Verify authenticity first.","Validate schema and field constraints.","Check current business state and authorization before high-impact actions."],"constraints":["Provider-signed data can still be malformed, stale, or incompatible with current application state."],"preconditions":["Application performs business actions based on webhook payload fields."],"input":"Authenticated webhook event.","output":"Validated and authorized business operation.","expected_behavior":"Authentic but invalid or unauthorized state transitions are rejected.","failure_mode":"Application treats a valid signature as unconditional authorization.","root_cause":"Authentication and authorization are conflated.","solution":"Implement layered verification: authenticity, schema validation, state validation, authorization, then action.","edge_cases":["Stale events","Unexpected event types","Missing fields","Tenant mismatch","Out-of-order events"],"security_implications":"Signed maliciously configured or stale events can still cause unauthorized effects if business authorization is absent.","performance_implications":"Additional validation and authorization queries add processing cost.","testing":{"method":"Submit authentic-format events with invalid state, tenant, or authorization context.","expected_result":"Business action is denied despite valid signature."},"verification":{"claim":"Webhook authenticity does not bypass business authorization.","evidence_required":["Processing flow","Authorization tests","State validation tests"],"method":"Review trust-boundary transitions and negative tests.","limitations":"Business authorization semantics are application specific."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Application-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security principles","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B166","D40-B168"]} {"record_id":"D40-B171","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Concurrency Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Race condition remediation","question":"How should a security-sensitive race condition be remediated?","answer":"Identify the shared invariant and make the check-and-update operation atomic or synchronize access so concurrent executions cannot observe an invalid intermediate state. Database constraints and transactional operations are often preferable to application-only timing assumptions.","objective":"Prevent concurrent requests from bypassing security or business invariants.","requirements":["Identify the invariant being protected.","Perform validation and state change atomically where possible.","Use appropriate transaction or synchronization primitives."],"constraints":["Distributed systems may require coordination beyond a single process.","Locks can introduce contention or deadlocks."],"preconditions":["Multiple concurrent actors can access or modify shared state."],"input":"Concurrent operations against a security-sensitive state.","output":"Invariant remains true under concurrency.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent requests cannot both pass a condition intended to allow only one successful transition.","failure_mode":"Two or more concurrent requests pass a check that should have been exclusive.","root_cause":"Time-of-check and time-of-use are separated without atomicity.","solution":"Use atomic database operations, unique constraints, transactions, or correctly scoped synchronization.","edge_cases":["Process crashes","Transaction retries","Distributed workers","Deadlocks","Isolation-level differences"],"security_implications":"Race conditions can bypass limits, authorization transitions, payment controls, or resource ownership.","performance_implications":"Synchronization may reduce concurrency; measure contention and select the narrowest safe critical section.","testing":{"method":"Run high-concurrency tests against the protected invariant.","expected_result":"Invariant remains true across repeated concurrent attempts."},"verification":{"claim":"The protected invariant holds under concurrent execution.","evidence_required":["Concurrency tests","Transaction/isolation configuration","Database constraints"],"method":"Review atomicity and run repeated concurrent tests.","limitations":"Test coverage cannot prove absence of all races."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Runtime, database, and synchronization implementation dependent.","provenance":["CWE-362","CWE-367","OWASP concurrency/security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B172","D40-B173","D40-B174"]} {"record_id":"D40-B172","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"TOCTOU","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Time-of-check to time-of-use remediation","question":"How should a TOCTOU vulnerability be remediated?","answer":"Avoid checking a resource and later independently using it when the resource can change between operations. Prefer atomic operating-system primitives, file descriptors, transactions, or APIs that combine validation and use.","objective":"Prevent attackers from changing a resource between security validation and use.","requirements":["Minimize check/use separation.","Use atomic primitives where available.","Operate on stable resource handles rather than re-resolving attacker-controlled paths."],"constraints":["Operating-system APIs differ.","Filesystem semantics vary across platforms."],"preconditions":["A security decision is made about a resource before a later operation uses it."],"input":"Resource path or object whose state may change concurrently.","output":"Security check and protected operation apply to the same resource state.","expected_behavior":"An attacker cannot substitute a different resource between authorization and use.","failure_mode":"Validated resource is replaced before use.","root_cause":"Security check and resource use are non-atomic.","solution":"Use atomic APIs, open-then-operate patterns, file descriptors, transactions, or equivalent stable handles.","edge_cases":["Symlinks","Mount changes","Network filesystems","Concurrent processes"],"security_implications":"TOCTOU can bypass filesystem permissions and other security checks.","performance_implications":"Atomic operations often improve correctness with minimal overhead; some synchronization can reduce throughput.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled concurrent resource substitution during check/use windows.","expected_result":"Substitution does not alter the protected operation's target."},"verification":{"claim":"The protected operation cannot be redirected after authorization.","evidence_required":["API usage review","Concurrent substitution tests"],"method":"Inspect resource handling and test adversarial replacement.","limitations":"Filesystem behavior is platform dependent."},"languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"Operating-system and runtime API dependent.","provenance":["CWE-367","POSIX/operating-system API documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B171","D40-B173"]} {"record_id":"D40-B173","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Filesystem Race","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Symlink race remediation","question":"How should applications remediate symlink races in security-sensitive filesystem operations?","answer":"Avoid trusting a path after separately checking it, restrict operations to trusted directories, use APIs that prevent unintended symlink traversal where available, and operate on securely opened handles.","objective":"Prevent a malicious process from redirecting a validated path through a symbolic link.","requirements":["Use secure filesystem APIs appropriate to the operating system.","Restrict directory permissions.","Avoid check-then-open patterns for attacker-controlled paths."],"constraints":["Secure path APIs differ by operating system and language.","Some high-level libraries abstract away security-relevant flags."],"preconditions":["An attacker can influence filesystem entries during the application's operation."],"input":"Path to a security-sensitive resource.","output":"Operation targets only an authorized filesystem object.","expected_behavior":"Symlink substitution cannot redirect the operation to an unauthorized target.","failure_mode":"A validated regular file is replaced or represented by a symlink before use.","root_cause":"Path validation is performed separately from secure resource acquisition.","solution":"Use safe openat-style or platform-equivalent mechanisms, no-follow semantics where available, and trusted directory ownership.","edge_cases":["Nested symlinks","Hard links","Mount points","Cross-device paths"],"security_implications":"Symlink races can cause unauthorized file disclosure, overwrite, or privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"Secure filesystem operations may add syscall overhead but generally remain small relative to I/O.","testing":{"method":"Attempt controlled symlink replacement during file access.","expected_result":"Unauthorized target is not opened or modified."},"verification":{"claim":"Filesystem operations resist symlink substitution.","evidence_required":["System-call/API configuration","Race tests"],"method":"Review exact OS calls and test concurrent substitution.","limitations":"Proof depends on operating-system guarantees."},"languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Python"],"version_scope":"Operating-system API specific.","provenance":["CWE-59","CWE-367","POSIX filesystem documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B172","D40-B174"]} {"record_id":"D40-B174","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Concurrency Control","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Double-submit security control","question":"How should applications prevent concurrent duplicate security-sensitive submissions?","answer":"Use an idempotency key or unique operation identifier backed by an atomic persistence constraint, and ensure the state transition and deduplication decision are coordinated.","objective":"Prevent concurrent duplicate requests from producing multiple protected effects.","requirements":["Define a stable operation identifier.","Enforce uniqueness atomically.","Make retries return or reference the original operation result when safe."],"constraints":["External side effects may not share the same transaction boundary.","Operation retention duration must match replay risk."],"preconditions":["The same logical operation can be submitted concurrently."],"input":"Operation request with idempotency identifier.","output":"At most one logical operation takes effect.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent duplicate submissions do not create duplicate protected effects.","failure_mode":"Two workers independently observe no prior operation and both execute.","root_cause":"Deduplication check and insertion are not atomic.","solution":"Use a unique database constraint or atomic compare-and-set operation and coordinate side effects.","edge_cases":["Concurrent first requests","Process crash","Partial external failure","Expired idempotency records"],"security_implications":"Duplicate execution can cause financial loss, privilege changes, or resource abuse.","performance_implications":"Unique-key contention may occur during duplicate bursts.","testing":{"method":"Generate concurrent identical requests at high frequency.","expected_result":"Only one operation commits the protected state transition."},"verification":{"claim":"Concurrent duplicate submissions are serialized or safely deduplicated.","evidence_required":["Unique constraint","Transaction logic","Concurrency tests"],"method":"Inspect transaction boundary and run stress tests.","limitations":"External side effects require separate idempotency design."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database and application architecture dependent.","provenance":["CWE-362","OWASP API Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B171","D40-B168"]} {"record_id":"D40-B175","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Deadlock and Lock Abuse","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security-sensitive lock design","question":"How should lock usage be designed so concurrency controls do not become a security or availability weakness?","answer":"Keep critical sections small, acquire multiple locks in a consistent global order, define bounded wait behavior, and ensure exceptions and cancellations release locks through structured mechanisms.","objective":"Prevent attacker-influenced concurrency from causing deadlocks or persistent resource blockage.","requirements":["Use structured lock acquisition and release.","Define lock ordering for multiple resources.","Handle cancellation and exceptions safely.","Use bounded waits where appropriate."],"constraints":["Lock-free designs may be preferable for some workloads.","Timeouts must not violate protected invariants."],"preconditions":["Security-sensitive operations use shared locks or synchronization."],"input":"Concurrent operations requiring synchronization.","output":"Protected invariant is maintained without indefinite lock retention.","expected_behavior":"Failures and cancellations release synchronization resources correctly.","failure_mode":"An attacker or fault causes workers to hold locks indefinitely.","root_cause":"Unbounded lock acquisition or missing cleanup.","solution":"Use structured concurrency primitives, lock ordering, bounded waits, and failure-safe cleanup.","edge_cases":["Cancellation during lock acquisition","Process crash","Nested locks","Lock starvation"],"security_implications":"Deadlocks and lock exhaustion can produce denial of service.","performance_implications":"Lock contention directly affects throughput and latency.","testing":{"method":"Run contention, cancellation, timeout, and failure-injection tests.","expected_result":"Locks are released and the system recovers without violating invariants."},"verification":{"claim":"Synchronization failures do not permanently block protected resources.","evidence_required":["Lock lifecycle review","Concurrency tests","Failure-injection results"],"method":"Review synchronization paths and exercise cancellation/failure cases.","limitations":"Absence of all deadlocks cannot be established solely through finite tests."},"languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Runtime synchronization implementation dependent.","provenance":["CWE-833","CWE-400","Language/runtime concurrency documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B171","D40-B174"]} {"record_id":"D40-B176","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Native Memory Safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Buffer overflow remediation","question":"How should a buffer overflow in native code be remediated?","answer":"Replace unsafe bounds handling with APIs and data structures that preserve length information, validate sizes before copying or indexing, and use compiler/runtime hardening where appropriate. The root cause should be removed rather than relying only on crash mitigations.","objective":"Prevent out-of-bounds memory access caused by insufficient bounds enforcement.","requirements":["Validate lengths before memory operations.","Use size-aware APIs and containers.","Preserve buffer length alongside data.","Enable relevant compiler/runtime hardening."],"constraints":["Legacy C/C++ APIs may require substantial refactoring.","Hardening reduces impact but does not prove memory safety."],"preconditions":["Native code performs memory operations based on externally influenced sizes."],"input":"Buffer and requested operation length.","output":"Operation occurs only within allocated bounds.","expected_behavior":"Oversized operations are rejected or safely bounded.","failure_mode":"Read or write exceeds allocated memory.","root_cause":"Length is not validated against actual buffer capacity.","solution":"Use bounds-aware abstractions, checked arithmetic, safe APIs, and appropriate compiler mitigations.","edge_cases":["Integer overflow in size calculations","Zero-length buffers","Truncated input","Null pointers"],"security_implications":"Buffer overflows can cause memory corruption, crashes, information disclosure, or code execution.","performance_implications":"Bounds checks generally have low cost but may affect hot native loops; measure before removing safety checks.","testing":{"method":"Use boundary tests, sanitizers, fuzzing, and malformed-input tests.","expected_result":"Out-of-bounds access is prevented or detected during testing."},"verification":{"claim":"The vulnerable memory operation is bounds-safe.","evidence_required":["Code review","Sanitizer/fuzzing evidence","Boundary tests"],"method":"Review size invariants and execute memory-safety tests.","limitations":"Dynamic testing cannot prove complete memory safety."},"languages":["C","C++"],"version_scope":"Compiler, standard library, and platform dependent.","provenance":["CWE-119","CWE-120","CERT C/C++ guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B177","D40-B178","D40-B179"]} {"record_id":"D40-B177","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Native Memory Safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Use-after-free remediation","question":"How should a use-after-free vulnerability be remediated?","answer":"Ensure object lifetime exceeds every access, use ownership-aware abstractions where available, invalidate or remove stale references after release, and restructure asynchronous ownership so destruction cannot race with use.","objective":"Prevent access to memory after its lifetime has ended.","requirements":["Define ownership and lifetime explicitly.","Avoid dangling raw pointers or references.","Coordinate destruction with concurrent users."],"constraints":["C and C++ legacy code may require ownership refactoring.","Reference-counting alone does not solve every cycle or concurrency problem."],"preconditions":["Manually managed native memory is accessed across complex control flow or concurrency."],"input":"Object reference and lifecycle events.","output":"All accesses occur while the object remains alive.","expected_behavior":"No access occurs after deallocation.","failure_mode":"Released memory is dereferenced or reused.","root_cause":"Lifetime ownership is ambiguous or unsynchronized.","solution":"Use RAII/ownership abstractions, lifetime-safe APIs, synchronization, and sanitizer-assisted testing.","edge_cases":["Asynchronous callbacks","Reference cycles","Concurrent destruction","Exception paths"],"security_implications":"Use-after-free can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or control-flow compromise.","performance_implications":"Ownership abstractions may add minimal overhead depending on implementation.","testing":{"method":"Use AddressSanitizer or equivalent tooling plus lifecycle/concurrency tests.","expected_result":"No use-after-free is observed under tested paths."},"verification":{"claim":"The vulnerable object cannot be accessed after destruction.","evidence_required":["Ownership review","Sanitizer results","Lifecycle tests"],"method":"Review ownership model and exercise asynchronous paths.","limitations":"Testing cannot establish complete absence of lifetime defects."},"languages":["C","C++"],"version_scope":"Compiler/runtime/toolchain dependent.","provenance":["CWE-416","C++ Core Guidelines","AddressSanitizer documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B176","D40-B178"]} {"record_id":"D40-B178","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Native Memory Safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Integer overflow in allocation or bounds calculations","question":"How should integer overflow affecting memory allocation or bounds checks be remediated?","answer":"Perform checked arithmetic before allocation, indexing, size conversion, or multiplication, and use types and APIs that represent valid size ranges. Reject values that cannot be represented safely.","objective":"Prevent arithmetic wraparound from producing undersized allocations or bypassed bounds checks.","requirements":["Check arithmetic operations that determine memory sizes.","Validate conversions between signed and unsigned or different-width types.","Reject unrepresentable values."],"constraints":["C and C++ integer conversion rules can be subtle.","Compiler optimization assumptions may affect undefined signed-overflow behavior."],"preconditions":["External or computed values influence allocation or memory-access sizes."],"input":"Count, element size, or offset values.","output":"Safe representable size or explicit rejection.","expected_behavior":"Overflow cannot result in a smaller allocation or incorrect bounds decision.","failure_mode":"Arithmetic wraps or converts unexpectedly, causing memory corruption.","root_cause":"Unchecked arithmetic or unsafe type conversion.","solution":"Use checked arithmetic, range validation, safe size types, and explicit conversion rules.","edge_cases":["Maximum integer values","Negative-to-unsigned conversion","Multiplication overflow","Addition overflow"],"security_implications":"Integer overflow can become a memory-safety or denial-of-service vulnerability.","performance_implications":"Checked arithmetic adds small computational cost.","testing":{"method":"Test maximum, near-maximum, negative, and conversion-boundary values.","expected_result":"Invalid arithmetic is rejected before resource allocation or access."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-sensitive size calculations cannot overflow into unsafe values.","evidence_required":["Arithmetic review","Boundary tests","Compiler diagnostics where applicable"],"method":"Review every size calculation and test numeric boundaries.","limitations":"Static analysis coverage depends on tool and code complexity."},"languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Java"],"version_scope":"Language integer semantics and compiler/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-190","CERT C/C++ Integer Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B176","D40-B179"]} {"record_id":"D40-B179","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Native Memory Safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Format string vulnerability remediation","question":"How should an uncontrolled format string vulnerability be remediated?","answer":"Treat external data as format arguments rather than the format string itself. Use APIs with explicit format literals and compiler warnings or static analysis to detect dynamic format-string misuse.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled format directives from altering formatted output operations.","requirements":["Keep format strings under program control.","Pass untrusted data as data arguments.","Enable compiler diagnostics and static analysis."],"constraints":["Legacy formatting APIs differ by language and platform.","Some logging frameworks use different placeholder mechanisms."],"preconditions":["Native code passes externally controlled text to formatting functions."],"input":"Untrusted string used by a formatting API.","output":"Untrusted text is rendered as data, not interpreted as formatting directives.","expected_behavior":"Format directives supplied by an attacker cannot alter argument interpretation or memory access.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled format string causes memory disclosure, corruption, or crash.","root_cause":"External data is passed as the format template.","solution":"Use constant format strings and explicit data arguments; migrate unsafe formatting APIs where practical.","edge_cases":["Localized formatting","Variadic APIs","Logging wrappers"],"security_implications":"Format-string vulnerabilities can expose memory or corrupt process state.","performance_implications":"Safe formatting APIs may have similar or slightly different performance characteristics; benchmark only when necessary.","testing":{"method":"Static scan and runtime tests using format specifier payloads.","expected_result":"Payload is emitted literally and cannot control formatting arguments."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted data cannot become a formatting template.","evidence_required":["Static analysis","Code review","Negative tests"],"method":"Inspect formatting call sites and test malicious format strings.","limitations":"Custom wrappers require separate analysis."},"languages":["C","C++"],"version_scope":"Compiler and libc/API dependent.","provenance":["CWE-134","CERT C guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B176","D40-B180"]} {"record_id":"D40-B180","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Native Memory Safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Native unsafe-code boundary containment","question":"How should a memory-unsafe native component be isolated when it cannot immediately be rewritten?","answer":"Minimize the unsafe boundary, validate inputs before crossing it, isolate the component's privileges and process boundary where practical, use memory-safety tooling during development, and define explicit ownership and failure contracts.","objective":"Reduce the blast radius of unavoidable native unsafe code.","requirements":["Minimize trusted native code.","Validate boundary inputs.","Apply least privilege and process isolation where feasible.","Continuously test with memory-safety tooling."],"constraints":["Isolation does not repair the underlying memory defect.","Some performance-sensitive integrations require in-process execution."],"preconditions":["Legacy or required native code cannot immediately be replaced."],"input":"Data crossing into unsafe native code.","output":"Constrained native operation with bounded privileges and explicit contract.","expected_behavior":"Native faults have limited security impact and cannot trivially compromise unrelated components.","failure_mode":"Memory corruption in a privileged native component compromises the broader application.","root_cause":"Large or highly privileged unsafe boundary.","solution":"Reduce boundary size, sandbox where practical, minimize privileges, and prioritize eventual remediation.","edge_cases":["IPC failure","Crash recovery","Shared memory","FFI ownership"],"security_implications":"Containment reduces impact but must not be treated as proof of memory safety.","performance_implications":"Process isolation and IPC add latency and serialization overhead.","testing":{"method":"Boundary fuzzing, fault injection, privilege review, and crash recovery tests.","expected_result":"Malformed native inputs are rejected and native failures do not bypass containment."},"verification":{"claim":"Unsafe native code is constrained by a documented security boundary.","evidence_required":["Privilege configuration","Boundary tests","Isolation configuration"],"method":"Review architecture and test failure containment.","limitations":"Containment effectiveness depends on platform configuration."},"languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Platform/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE memory-safety guidance","OWASP defense-in-depth principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B176","D40-B177"]} {"record_id":"D40-B181","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Dependency confusion remediation","question":"How should dependency confusion risk be remediated?","answer":"Configure package managers with explicit trusted registries and namespace rules, reserve internal package names where supported, use dependency lockfiles, and ensure private packages cannot be silently replaced by higher-priority public packages.","objective":"Prevent malicious public packages from satisfying internal dependency names.","requirements":["Define trusted package sources.","Use explicit registry or namespace configuration.","Pin or lock resolved dependencies.","Monitor dependency provenance."],"constraints":["Package manager behavior differs by language and version.","Some organizations intentionally use multiple registries."],"preconditions":["The project consumes internal and public dependencies.","Package names can overlap across registries."],"input":"Dependency declaration and package registry configuration.","output":"Dependencies resolve only according to approved source policy.","expected_behavior":"Internal package references cannot be substituted by unauthorized public packages.","failure_mode":"Package manager selects a malicious package from an untrusted registry.","root_cause":"Ambiguous registry precedence or namespace configuration.","solution":"Use source restrictions, scoped/private namespaces, lockfiles, and dependency provenance checks.","edge_cases":["Transitive dependencies","Multiple package managers","Monorepos","Registry failover"],"security_implications":"Dependency confusion can execute attacker-controlled build or runtime code.","performance_implications":"Additional provenance checks may increase installation time.","testing":{"method":"Use a controlled package-name collision in an isolated registry test.","expected_result":"Unauthorized source is rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Dependency resolution follows explicit trusted-source policy.","evidence_required":["Package-manager configuration","Lockfile","Controlled resolution test"],"method":"Inspect resolver configuration and test source precedence.","limitations":"Registry behavior must be verified for the exact package manager version."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Package-manager specific.","provenance":["CWE-427","OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance","Official package-manager documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B182","D40-B183","D40-B184"]} {"record_id":"D40-B182","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Typosquatting dependency remediation","question":"How should projects reduce dependency typosquatting risk?","answer":"Use exact dependency names from trusted documentation, lock resolved versions and sources, review newly introduced dependencies, and use repository/package provenance controls where available.","objective":"Reduce the chance of intentionally deceptive package names entering a project.","requirements":["Review dependency additions.","Use exact package identifiers.","Lock versions and provenance where supported.","Prefer trusted package sources."],"constraints":["Typosquatting detection cannot guarantee that every package is benign.","Legitimate packages can have similar names."],"preconditions":["Developers can introduce third-party dependencies."],"input":"New dependency declaration.","output":"Dependency is approved, resolved, and provenance-recorded.","expected_behavior":"Suspicious or unapproved packages are rejected before build or deployment.","failure_mode":"A look-alike package is added accidentally or maliciously.","root_cause":"Dependency introduction lacks review and provenance controls.","solution":"Combine review, source restrictions, lockfiles, automated scanning, and package provenance verification.","edge_cases":["Transitive packages","Abandoned packages","Package renames","Multiple ecosystems"],"security_implications":"Malicious packages can execute code during installation or runtime.","performance_implications":"Dependency scanning adds CI time proportional to dependency graph size.","testing":{"method":"Introduce controlled look-alike package names in a test repository.","expected_result":"Policy detects or rejects the package."},"verification":{"claim":"Dependency introduction has a documented provenance and approval path.","evidence_required":["CI policy","Dependency review records","Lockfile"],"method":"Inspect dependency workflow and test policy enforcement.","limitations":"Automated package reputation is not proof of safety."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Package-manager and repository-platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance","Official package-manager documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B181","D40-B183"]} {"record_id":"D40-B183","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Malicious build-script remediation","question":"How should projects mitigate malicious package installation or build scripts?","answer":"Limit package installation privileges, review dependency lifecycle scripts, use isolated build environments, restrict network access during builds where practical, and record the exact dependency graph and artifacts used.","objective":"Reduce the impact of malicious code executed during dependency installation or build.","requirements":["Build with least privilege.","Isolate build environments.","Review or restrict package lifecycle scripts according to ecosystem capabilities.","Record dependency and artifact provenance."],"constraints":["Some legitimate packages require build-time scripts.","Disabling all scripts may break valid native builds."],"preconditions":["Dependency installation can execute package-defined build or installation code."],"input":"Dependency graph and build environment.","output":"Dependencies build under constrained privileges with auditable provenance.","expected_behavior":"A malicious package cannot gain unnecessary host privileges or unrestricted build access.","failure_mode":"Package installation executes attacker-controlled code with developer or CI privileges.","root_cause":"Build environment grants excessive permissions and trust.","solution":"Use isolated CI builders, least privilege, controlled network access, and dependency review.","edge_cases":["Native compilation","Private registries","Offline builds","Credential availability during builds"],"security_implications":"Build-time compromise can poison artifacts or steal CI credentials.","performance_implications":"Isolation and dependency caching can increase setup complexity but may improve reproducibility.","testing":{"method":"Run dependency installation in a sandbox and test privilege/network restrictions.","expected_result":"Build scripts cannot access prohibited resources."},"verification":{"claim":"Dependency build code executes within an appropriately restricted trust boundary.","evidence_required":["Build isolation configuration","Privilege policy","Network restrictions"],"method":"Inspect CI runtime and perform controlled isolation tests.","limitations":"Container or sandbox isolation is platform dependent."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Package ecosystem and CI platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance","SLSA guidance","Official package-manager documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B181","D40-B184","D40-B185"]} {"record_id":"D40-B184","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Artifact Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Artifact signature verification","question":"How should artifact integrity be verified before deployment?","answer":"Verify cryptographic signatures or attestations against trusted identities or policies before accepting an artifact, and bind verification to the exact artifact digest rather than only a mutable name or tag.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized or modified artifacts from entering deployment.","requirements":["Record immutable artifact digests.","Verify signatures or attestations before deployment.","Maintain trusted signer policy.","Fail closed on verification failure."],"constraints":["Signature systems and attestation formats vary.","Trust-root management is itself security sensitive."],"preconditions":["The build or deployment system supports artifact verification."],"input":"Artifact and associated signature/attestation.","output":"Artifact is accepted only when integrity and provenance policy succeeds.","expected_behavior":"Modified or unauthorized artifacts are rejected.","failure_mode":"Deployment trusts a mutable tag or unverified artifact.","root_cause":"Artifact identity is not cryptographically bound to trusted provenance.","solution":"Use digest pinning plus signature/attestation verification integrated into deployment policy.","edge_cases":["Key rotation","Multiple signers","Reproducible versus non-reproducible builds","Artifact promotion"],"security_implications":"Artifact verification reduces supply-chain tampering risk.","performance_implications":"Signature verification is generally low relative to artifact transfer/build cost.","testing":{"method":"Modify a signed artifact or present an unauthorized signer in a controlled environment.","expected_result":"Deployment rejects the artifact."},"verification":{"claim":"Only artifacts satisfying the configured integrity and provenance policy can deploy.","evidence_required":["Verification policy","Signer trust configuration","Negative verification tests"],"method":"Review deployment gate and test invalid signatures/digests.","limitations":"Signature verification does not prove source code is benign."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Artifact/signing ecosystem dependent.","provenance":["SLSA guidance","Sigstore documentation","NIST software supply-chain guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B183","D40-B185"]} {"record_id":"D40-B185","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Build Provenance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Build provenance verification","question":"What should build provenance remediation establish before an artifact is trusted?","answer":"Capture enough provenance to identify the source revision, build process, dependencies, builder identity, and artifact digest, then enforce deployment policy against the required provenance attributes.","objective":"Make artifact origin and build context auditable and enforceable.","requirements":["Record source revision.","Record build identity and process.","Record dependency inputs where supported.","Bind provenance to the resulting artifact digest."],"constraints":["Provenance formats and available fields vary.","Provenance describes origin and process but does not prove source correctness."],"preconditions":["The build system can emit provenance or attestations."],"input":"Build execution metadata and artifact digest.","output":"Verifiable provenance statement associated with the artifact.","expected_behavior":"Deployment can reject artifacts missing required provenance attributes.","failure_mode":"Unknown or untrusted build artifacts are deployed because only version labels are checked.","root_cause":"Artifact origin is not represented as a verifiable security property.","solution":"Generate signed provenance and enforce policy at deployment.","edge_cases":["Rebuilds from same source","Multi-stage builds","External build services","Dependency substitution"],"security_implications":"Provenance reduces ambiguity in software supply-chain investigations and deployment trust decisions.","performance_implications":"Generating and verifying attestations adds modest CI/deployment processing.","testing":{"method":"Deploy artifacts with missing, altered, and valid provenance.","expected_result":"Only policy-compliant provenance is accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"Deployment decisions can be traced to verifiable artifact provenance.","evidence_required":["Attestation records","Policy configuration","Negative tests"],"method":"Inspect provenance generation and enforcement.","limitations":"Provenance quality depends on the integrity of the build environment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Build and provenance framework dependent.","provenance":["SLSA guidance","NIST software supply-chain guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B183","D40-B184"]} {"record_id":"D40-B186","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secrets in URLs remediation","question":"How should applications remediate credentials or secrets included in URLs?","answer":"Move sensitive values out of URL query strings and paths where possible and use secure request headers or authenticated channels designed for the credential type. Existing leaked secrets should be rotated because URLs may be captured by logs, browser history, proxies, analytics, and referrers.","objective":"Prevent sensitive credentials from propagating through URL-visible channels.","requirements":["Avoid putting secrets in query strings.","Use appropriate authorization headers or secure protocol mechanisms.","Rotate secrets that may already have been exposed."],"constraints":["Some legacy protocols require URL credentials.","Migration must account for clients and intermediaries."],"preconditions":["Application currently accepts or generates secret-bearing URLs."],"input":"Request containing sensitive URL parameter.","output":"Authentication material is transmitted through an appropriate protected channel.","expected_behavior":"Secrets do not appear in ordinary URL logging or browser history.","failure_mode":"Credential is copied into logs or external systems through URL handling.","root_cause":"URL is incorrectly treated as a private transport channel.","solution":"Move secrets to headers or protocol-specific secure mechanisms and remove them from logs during migration.","edge_cases":["Password reset tokens","Signed URLs","Temporary object URLs","Referrer propagation"],"security_implications":"URL exposure can lead to credential compromise.","performance_implications":"Negligible application overhead; logging volume may change after remediation.","testing":{"method":"Inspect generated URLs, access logs, proxy logs, browser-visible requests, and referrer behavior.","expected_result":"Long-lived secrets are absent from URLs and routine logs."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive credentials are not unnecessarily transmitted in URLs.","evidence_required":["Request traces","Logging configuration","Code review"],"method":"Trace all credential-bearing request paths.","limitations":"Temporary signed URLs may be an intentional security design and require separate review."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Protocol/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","CWE-598"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B187","D40-B188"]} {"record_id":"D40-B187","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secret rotation after exposure","question":"What is the correct remediation when an application secret is suspected to be exposed?","answer":"Treat the secret as compromised, revoke or rotate it using the provider's supported mechanism, identify systems that may have received it, update dependent applications safely, and investigate the exposure path.","objective":"Reduce the lifetime and impact of compromised credentials.","requirements":["Revoke or rotate the exposed secret.","Update all legitimate consumers.","Review exposure locations and access history where available.","Prevent recurrence through improved secret handling."],"constraints":["Rotation can affect many services.","Immediate revocation may cause availability impact."],"preconditions":["A credential or secret may have been exposed to unauthorized parties."],"input":"Potentially compromised secret.","output":"Old secret is invalidated and replacement is deployed securely.","expected_behavior":"Compromised credentials cannot continue authenticating after the defined rotation point.","failure_mode":"Exposed secret remains valid indefinitely.","root_cause":"No tested secret lifecycle or incident response process.","solution":"Execute documented rotation, revoke old credentials, audit use, and fix the exposure source.","edge_cases":["Multiple replicas","Long-lived jobs","Cached credentials","Third-party integrations"],"security_implications":"Delayed rotation extends the exploitation window.","performance_implications":"Credential rotation may require connection refreshes or service restarts.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled rotation and verify old credential rejection.","expected_result":"Old secret fails authentication while authorized clients use the replacement."},"verification":{"claim":"Compromised secret is no longer usable after remediation.","evidence_required":["Provider revocation state","Authentication tests","Consumer configuration"],"method":"Test old and new credentials after rotation.","limitations":"Third-party systems may cache credentials beyond expected intervals."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Credential provider dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST credential management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B186","D40-B188"]} {"record_id":"D40-B188","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Runtime secret injection","question":"How should hard-coded application secrets be remediated in deployment?","answer":"Remove secrets from source code and static artifacts and inject them through an appropriate secret-management mechanism at runtime or deployment time, with least-privilege access and rotation support.","objective":"Prevent credentials from becoming part of source-controlled or broadly distributed application artifacts.","requirements":["Store secrets in an approved secret-management system.","Limit runtime access to required workloads.","Avoid embedding secrets in source, images, or generated bundles."],"constraints":["Secret-management mechanisms vary by deployment environment.","Applications may require bootstrap credentials that need separate protection."],"preconditions":["Application contains static credentials or configuration secrets."],"input":"Secret required by an application.","output":"Application retrieves the secret through an authorized runtime mechanism.","expected_behavior":"Source and distributable artifacts do not contain active secrets.","failure_mode":"Credential is exposed through repository history, build artifacts, or application bundles.","root_cause":"Secret lifecycle is coupled to source code or build artifact.","solution":"Externalize secrets, rotate previously exposed values, and enforce secret scanning.","edge_cases":["Build-time secrets","Client-side applications","Generated configuration","Container image layers"],"security_implications":"Secret externalization reduces accidental disclosure but does not replace access control.","performance_implications":"Runtime secret retrieval can add startup latency and dependency on secret infrastructure.","testing":{"method":"Scan repository/artifacts and test runtime secret retrieval with least-privilege identity.","expected_result":"No active secret exists in source/distributable artifacts and authorized workload retrieves required secret."},"verification":{"claim":"Active application secrets are externalized from source and artifacts.","evidence_required":["Secret scanner results","Artifact inspection","Runtime identity policy"],"method":"Inspect repository history, artifacts, and runtime configuration.","limitations":"Historical leaked secrets must be separately revoked."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Deployment-platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST secrets/credential management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B187","D40-B189"]} {"record_id":"D40-B189","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secret access least privilege","question":"How should excessive runtime access to secrets be remediated?","answer":"Give each workload access only to the secrets required for its function, separate environments and tenants where appropriate, and audit secret access. Do not grant broad read access merely for deployment convenience.","objective":"Limit the impact of application compromise or credential theft.","requirements":["Define workload-specific secret access policies.","Separate production and non-production secrets.","Audit secret retrieval where supported."],"constraints":["Some legacy systems share credentials.","Fine-grained access may require identity and deployment changes."],"preconditions":["A centralized or platform secret store is used."],"input":"Workload identity and requested secret.","output":"Secret retrieval succeeds only for authorized workload/resource combinations.","expected_behavior":"Compromised low-privilege workload cannot retrieve unrelated secrets.","failure_mode":"One application identity can read credentials for many services.","root_cause":"Secret access is granted by broad role rather than task-specific need.","solution":"Use least-privilege policies, separate identities, and periodic access review.","edge_cases":["Break-glass access","Shared infrastructure","Rotation transitions","Multi-tenant workloads"],"security_implications":"Least privilege limits lateral movement after compromise.","performance_implications":"Fine-grained policy checks can add small authorization overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt authorized and unauthorized secret reads using representative workload identities.","expected_result":"Only intended secret access succeeds."},"verification":{"claim":"Secret access matches documented workload necessity.","evidence_required":["Access policy","Identity mapping","Negative authorization tests"],"method":"Review policies and test cross-workload access.","limitations":"Provider policy semantics vary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Secret platform and identity system dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST least-privilege guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B188","D40-B190"]} {"record_id":"D40-B190","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Secrets Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secret leakage through process and diagnostic channels","question":"How should applications remediate secrets exposed through process arguments or diagnostics?","answer":"Avoid passing secrets in command-line arguments or diagnostic metadata where they may be visible to other users or tooling. Use protected environment or IPC mechanisms as appropriate and redact sensitive values from diagnostics.","objective":"Prevent secrets from leaking through operating-system and diagnostic observation channels.","requirements":["Avoid secret-bearing command-line arguments where safer alternatives exist.","Redact sensitive values from diagnostics.","Restrict process inspection and debug interfaces."],"constraints":["Some third-party tools require specific credential delivery mechanisms.","Environment variables are not universally private."],"preconditions":["Application launches processes or emits diagnostic information containing credentials."],"input":"Secret value and process/diagnostic channel.","output":"Secret reaches the consumer without unnecessary exposure.","expected_behavior":"Routine process listings, traces, and diagnostics do not expose active credentials.","failure_mode":"Credential becomes visible through process inspection or debugging output.","root_cause":"Sensitive data is sent through observable channels without threat-model consideration.","solution":"Use secure credential channels, minimize diagnostic exposure, and enforce redaction.","edge_cases":["Crash dumps","Core dumps","Tracing systems","Environment inspection"],"security_implications":"Diagnostic exposure can compromise credentials even when application authorization is correct.","performance_implications":"Redaction and secure IPC add small processing cost.","testing":{"method":"Inspect process metadata, diagnostics, crash artifacts, and traces under controlled conditions.","expected_result":"Secrets are absent or access-controlled in observable artifacts."},"verification":{"claim":"Secret values are not unnecessarily exposed through process or diagnostic channels.","evidence_required":["Process inspection","Redaction tests","Crash/trace configuration"],"method":"Review runtime behavior and controlled diagnostic outputs.","limitations":"Operating-system visibility differs by deployment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Operating-system/runtime dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","CWE-214"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B186","D40-B188"]} {"record_id":"D40-B191","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Privileged container remediation","question":"How should an application remediate unnecessary privileged container execution?","answer":"Run containers with the minimum privileges required, remove unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged mode, use read-only filesystems where feasible, and isolate sensitive workloads.","objective":"Reduce the host-impact potential of a compromised container.","requirements":["Remove privileged mode unless explicitly required and justified.","Drop unnecessary capabilities.","Use restrictive security profiles.","Apply filesystem and identity restrictions."],"constraints":["Some workloads require specific kernel capabilities.","Container hardening is platform dependent."],"preconditions":["Application is deployed in containers."],"input":"Container security configuration.","output":"Container executes with minimum required privileges.","expected_behavior":"Application process cannot access host resources beyond its documented requirements.","failure_mode":"Compromised application can exploit broad container privileges to affect the host or neighboring workloads.","root_cause":"Container runs with excessive privileges for convenience.","solution":"Apply least privilege, capability dropping, filesystem restrictions, namespace isolation, and runtime security policy.","edge_cases":["Host networking","Device access","Kernel modules","Nested containers"],"security_implications":"Excessive container privileges increase escape and host-compromise impact.","performance_implications":"Some restrictions can affect workloads requiring specialized devices or filesystem behavior.","testing":{"method":"Inspect effective container capabilities and attempt prohibited operations in a controlled environment.","expected_result":"Unneeded privileged operations fail."},"verification":{"claim":"Container privileges match documented workload requirements.","evidence_required":["Runtime configuration","Capability listing","Negative privilege tests"],"method":"Inspect effective runtime security settings.","limitations":"Container isolation is not equivalent to a separate physical host."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Container runtime/orchestrator dependent.","provenance":["CIS container security guidance","NIST container security guidance","OWASP Kubernetes guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B192","D40-B193","D40-B194"]} {"record_id":"D40-B192","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Container root filesystem hardening","question":"How should a writable container root filesystem be remediated when write access is unnecessary?","answer":"Use a read-only root filesystem and provide narrowly scoped writable volumes or temporary filesystems for components that genuinely require writes.","objective":"Limit persistence and filesystem modification after application compromise.","requirements":["Set the root filesystem read-only where supported.","Provide explicit writable paths only where required.","Review temporary storage permissions."],"constraints":["Some frameworks write caches, PID files, or temporary data by default.","Read-only configuration may require application changes."],"preconditions":["Application does not require unrestricted writes to its image filesystem."],"input":"Container filesystem configuration.","output":"Only documented paths are writable.","expected_behavior":"Unexpected filesystem modification fails while required application writes remain available.","failure_mode":"Compromised process modifies binaries, configuration, or startup scripts within the container.","root_cause":"Container filesystem permissions are broader than application requirements.","solution":"Enable read-only root filesystem and explicit writable mounts.","edge_cases":["Temporary directories","Runtime-generated certificates","Log files","Package managers"],"security_implications":"Reduces persistence and post-compromise tampering.","performance_implications":"Filesystem behavior may change if applications rely on unrestricted local writes.","testing":{"method":"Run normal workload tests plus attempted writes to protected paths.","expected_result":"Required writes succeed; unauthorized writes fail."},"verification":{"claim":"Container write access is constrained to documented paths.","evidence_required":["Runtime configuration","Filesystem write tests"],"method":"Inspect mounts and execute application plus negative tests.","limitations":"Container platform semantics vary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Container runtime dependent.","provenance":["CIS container security guidance","OWASP container security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B191","D40-B193"]} {"record_id":"D40-B193","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Container management endpoint exposure","question":"How should exposed container or orchestration management endpoints be remediated?","answer":"Restrict management interfaces to trusted administrative networks or identities, require strong authentication and authorization, disable unused endpoints, and avoid exposing runtime control sockets to application containers.","objective":"Prevent attackers from turning application compromise into infrastructure control.","requirements":["Restrict management endpoints by network and identity.","Use strong administrative authentication.","Remove unnecessary management interfaces.","Protect runtime sockets."],"constraints":["Operational access may require controlled remote administration.","Some platforms expose management APIs through shared infrastructure."],"preconditions":["Container or orchestration management interfaces are reachable from application or public networks."],"input":"Management endpoint request.","output":"Only authorized administrators or automation identities can invoke privileged operations.","expected_behavior":"Application-originated or unauthenticated management requests are denied.","failure_mode":"Compromised application accesses a privileged container/runtime API.","root_cause":"Management plane is exposed across an overly broad trust boundary.","solution":"Network isolate management interfaces, enforce identity authorization, and remove unnecessary socket/API exposure.","edge_cases":["Unix socket mounts","Node-local agents","Public cloud control planes","Break-glass access"],"security_implications":"Management endpoint compromise can enable workload creation, credential access, or host-level control.","performance_implications":"Administrative isolation has negligible application runtime cost.","testing":{"method":"Attempt management API access from application workload and unauthorized networks.","expected_result":"Privileged endpoint access is denied."},"verification":{"claim":"Application workloads cannot access privileged management interfaces.","evidence_required":["Network policy","Endpoint authentication","Negative access tests"],"method":"Review topology and execute unauthorized-access tests.","limitations":"Cloud provider control planes require provider-specific review."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Container/orchestrator dependent.","provenance":["CIS Kubernetes/container guidance","NIST container security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B191","D40-B194"]} {"record_id":"D40-B194","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Container service identity remediation","question":"How should weak or shared service credentials between containers be remediated?","answer":"Assign distinct workload identities, authenticate service-to-service communication, authorize each operation explicitly, and avoid distributing one shared credential across unrelated services.","objective":"Limit lateral movement and enforce service-level trust boundaries.","requirements":["Use distinct service identities.","Authenticate service requests.","Apply least-privilege authorization.","Rotate service credentials or keys."],"constraints":["Legacy services may require shared credentials during migration.","Service mesh or identity infrastructure may add operational complexity."],"preconditions":["Multiple services communicate across trust boundaries."],"input":"Inter-service request and caller identity.","output":"Request is authenticated and authorized for the target operation.","expected_behavior":"Compromising one service does not automatically grant unrestricted access to another.","failure_mode":"Shared credential permits lateral movement across services.","root_cause":"Service identity is coarse-grained or absent.","solution":"Introduce workload-specific identities and authorization policies.","edge_cases":["Service retries","Identity rotation","Cross-cluster calls","Background workers"],"security_implications":"Distinct identities reduce lateral movement and improve attribution.","performance_implications":"Authentication and policy checks add network/CPU overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt authorized and cross-service unauthorized requests using different workload identities.","expected_result":"Only permitted service actions succeed."},"verification":{"claim":"Service-to-service access follows explicit identity and authorization policy.","evidence_required":["Identity mapping","Authorization policy","Negative tests"],"method":"Review and test representative service calls.","limitations":"Identity infrastructure behavior must be validated in deployment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Deployment and identity platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST zero trust guidance","OWASP Kubernetes guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B193","D40-B195"]} {"record_id":"D40-B195","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Container image hardening","question":"How should a vulnerable or unnecessarily large container image be remediated from a security perspective?","answer":"Use minimal trusted base images, remove unnecessary packages and tools, keep the base image updated, run image scanning, and rebuild from controlled sources rather than modifying production containers in place.","objective":"Reduce attack surface and vulnerable components in deployed images.","requirements":["Use a maintained base image.","Remove unnecessary packages and tooling.","Scan image dependencies.","Rebuild after security updates."],"constraints":["Minimal images can complicate debugging.","Scanner findings require contextual assessment; not every finding is exploitable."],"preconditions":["Application is deployed from container images."],"input":"Container image and dependency inventory.","output":"Reduced and maintained image with documented vulnerability status.","expected_behavior":"Unnecessary attack surface is removed and critical applicable vulnerabilities are addressed.","failure_mode":"Compromised image exposes unnecessary utilities or vulnerable components.","root_cause":"Image contains broad OS/userland packages and lacks lifecycle management.","solution":"Adopt minimal images, patching/rebuild policy, scanning, and provenance verification.","edge_cases":["Distroless images","Native dependencies","Debug tooling","Base image EOL"],"security_implications":"Smaller and maintained images reduce available attack surface but do not guarantee absence of vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Smaller images can reduce transfer/startup time; build complexity may increase.","testing":{"method":"Compare package inventory before/after and run application plus security scanning tests.","expected_result":"Required runtime behavior remains intact while unnecessary packages are removed."},"verification":{"claim":"Image contents and vulnerabilities satisfy the documented deployment policy.","evidence_required":["SBOM/image inventory","Scanner results","Application tests"],"method":"Review image provenance, inventory, and test results.","limitations":"Scanner results are not equivalent to exploitability proof."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Container base image and build pipeline dependent.","provenance":["CIS container guidance","NIST container security guidance","OWASP container security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B181","D40-B184","D40-B191"]} {"record_id":"D40-B196","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Data Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data in temporary files","question":"How should sensitive data exposure through temporary files be remediated?","answer":"Avoid unnecessary temporary persistence of sensitive data, use restrictive permissions, minimize retention, securely delete according to the storage medium's guarantees where deletion is required, and prevent temporary paths from being attacker-controlled.","objective":"Reduce unauthorized exposure of sensitive temporary data.","requirements":["Use secure temporary-file APIs.","Apply restrictive permissions.","Minimize lifetime and contents.","Prevent predictable or attacker-controlled temporary filenames."],"constraints":["Deletion guarantees differ by filesystem and storage medium.","Some applications legitimately require temporary persistence."],"preconditions":["Application writes sensitive data to temporary storage."],"input":"Sensitive data requiring temporary processing.","output":"Data is stored only as long as necessary with appropriate access controls.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized processes cannot predictably access temporary files.","failure_mode":"Sensitive temporary file is readable, replaceable, or left behind.","root_cause":"Insecure temporary-file creation or excessive retention.","solution":"Use OS-provided secure temporary-file mechanisms, restrictive permissions, and lifecycle cleanup.","edge_cases":["Crash before cleanup","Shared temporary directories","Container volumes","Encrypted disks"],"security_implications":"Temporary files can expose credentials, personal data, or cryptographic material.","performance_implications":"Secure temporary storage may add filesystem I/O; memory-only processing can reduce disk exposure but may increase memory pressure.","testing":{"method":"Inspect temporary-file permissions, naming, lifecycle, and crash behavior.","expected_result":"Files are inaccessible to unauthorized users and do not persist unnecessarily."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive temporary files are created and managed according to least-privilege policy.","evidence_required":["Filesystem permissions","Secure temp API usage","Crash-cleanup tests"],"method":"Review implementation and test concurrent/unexpected termination paths.","limitations":"Secure deletion guarantees depend on storage technology."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Operating-system/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-377","CWE-378","OWASP File Upload/File System guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B197","D40-B198"]} {"record_id":"D40-B197","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Data Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data cache remediation","question":"How should sensitive information accidentally stored in shared caches be remediated?","answer":"Classify sensitive responses, prevent shared caching when content is user-specific, configure appropriate cache-control directives, and ensure cache keys include all security-relevant dimensions required to prevent cross-user or cross-tenant reuse.","objective":"Prevent cached sensitive data from being served to unauthorized users.","requirements":["Identify responses containing sensitive or tenant-specific data.","Use private/non-cacheable directives where appropriate.","Design cache keys around authorization-relevant context."],"constraints":["Caching behavior depends on browsers, CDNs, proxies, and application caches.","Disabling all caching may unnecessarily reduce performance."],"preconditions":["Sensitive responses pass through one or more caching layers."],"input":"Sensitive response and cache configuration.","output":"Only authorized requester can retrieve cached representation.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive user/tenant data is not served from a shared cache to another principal.","failure_mode":"A cached response is reused across users or tenants.","root_cause":"Cache policy ignores sensitivity or authorization context.","solution":"Use private/no-store policies when required and correctly partition cache keys.","edge_cases":["Authenticated GET requests","CDN caching","Vary headers","Tenant-specific content"],"security_implications":"Cache leakage can expose confidential data across users or tenants.","performance_implications":"More restrictive caching can increase origin load; selective private caching is preferable where safe.","testing":{"method":"Request the same resource under different identities and inspect browser/proxy/CDN cache behavior.","expected_result":"No unauthorized cached representation is returned."},"verification":{"claim":"Cache behavior preserves authorization boundaries.","evidence_required":["Effective cache headers","Cache configuration","Cross-user tests"],"method":"Trace cache layers and execute identity-separated requests.","limitations":"Third-party CDN behavior must be verified using effective configuration."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"HTTP cache/proxy/CDN behavior dependent.","provenance":["CWE-525","OWASP Caching guidance","IETF HTTP caching specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B198","D40-B199"]} {"record_id":"D40-B198","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Data Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Backup confidentiality remediation","question":"How should insecure backups containing sensitive application data be remediated?","answer":"Encrypt backups with managed keys, restrict backup access independently from production access, define retention and deletion policies, and verify restoration procedures without exposing backup contents unnecessarily.","objective":"Prevent backup copies from becoming an uncontrolled source of sensitive-data disclosure.","requirements":["Encrypt sensitive backups at rest.","Restrict backup access by least privilege.","Protect encryption keys separately.","Define retention and deletion controls."],"constraints":["Encryption and key-management architecture varies.","Restoration requires authorized access to both backup data and keys."],"preconditions":["Application data is backed up.","Backups contain sensitive information."],"input":"Backup artifact and access policy.","output":"Backup is confidential, integrity-protected, and accessible only to authorized recovery processes.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized users cannot read or alter backup contents.","failure_mode":"Backup storage is publicly accessible, broadly shared, or unencrypted.","root_cause":"Backup security is treated as an operational concern separate from application security.","solution":"Apply encryption, access controls, integrity checks, lifecycle policies, and tested recovery procedures.","edge_cases":["Offsite copies","Snapshots","Replica backups","Expired keys","Cross-region storage"],"security_implications":"Backup compromise can expose historical data and credentials long after production remediation.","performance_implications":"Encryption adds backup/restore CPU and I/O overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt unauthorized backup access and perform controlled encrypted restore.","expected_result":"Unauthorized access fails and authorized recovery succeeds."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive backups satisfy confidentiality and access-control requirements.","evidence_required":["Encryption configuration","Key policy","Access tests","Restore test"],"method":"Review backup architecture and perform controlled recovery.","limitations":"Cloud/provider backup semantics require provider-specific verification."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Storage and cloud-platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST backup/security guidance","OWASP data protection principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B187","D40-B199"]} {"record_id":"D40-B199","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Data Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cross-tenant data isolation remediation","question":"How should a cross-tenant data exposure discovered in shared storage or caching be remediated?","answer":"Enforce tenant authorization at every data-access boundary, derive tenant scope from trusted authenticated context rather than arbitrary client fields, partition or constrain queries by tenant, and test isolation under concurrent and adversarial access.","objective":"Prevent one tenant from reading or modifying another tenant's data.","requirements":["Bind every data operation to an authorized tenant context.","Apply tenant constraints at the persistence boundary where possible.","Do not trust client-supplied tenant identifiers without authorization.","Test read and write isolation."],"constraints":["Shared databases require careful query and transaction design.","Background jobs and caches can bypass request-level authorization if not designed consistently."],"preconditions":["Multiple tenants share application or storage infrastructure."],"input":"Authenticated tenant context and data-access request.","output":"Only data belonging to the authorized tenant is accessed or modified.","expected_behavior":"Cross-tenant reads and writes are rejected or return no unauthorized data.","failure_mode":"A request manipulates an identifier or query context to access another tenant's records.","root_cause":"Tenant scope is not enforced consistently at the data-access boundary.","solution":"Use trusted tenant context, mandatory query scoping, database-level controls where appropriate, and isolation tests.","edge_cases":["Background jobs","Bulk exports","Caches","Search indexes","Administrative support access","Concurrent requests"],"security_implications":"Cross-tenant exposure is a high-impact confidentiality and integrity failure in multi-tenant systems.","performance_implications":"Tenant filters and isolation controls can affect query plans; indexes and measured query performance should be reviewed.","testing":{"method":"Run cross-tenant authorization, ID manipulation, bulk, cache, and concurrent tests.","expected_result":"No tenant can access another tenant's protected data through normal or adversarial application paths."},"verification":{"claim":"Tenant data isolation is enforced consistently across data-access paths.","evidence_required":["Authorization design","Persistence constraints","Cross-tenant negative tests","Cache/search isolation tests"],"method":"Trace tenant context from authentication through persistence and test all identified access paths.","limitations":"Administrative and emergency access require separate documented controls."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application/database architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","OWASP API Security guidance","CWE-639"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B120","D40-B197","D40-B200"]} {"record_id":"D40-B200","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Data Protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security remediation regression prevention","question":"How should security fixes be protected against regression after remediation?","answer":"Convert the original vulnerability into a permanent regression test, encode the security invariant in validation or authorization policy where practical, monitor relevant security signals, and rerun the test suite during future dependency, configuration, and architecture changes.","objective":"Prevent a verified security remediation from being silently removed or bypassed later.","requirements":["Capture the original exploit condition as a negative regression test.","Document the protected security invariant.","Include the test in automated validation.","Review related code paths after security-sensitive changes."],"constraints":["A single regression test cannot cover every variant of a vulnerability.","Some controls require infrastructure-level testing rather than unit tests."],"preconditions":["A security defect has been remediated and its failure condition is understood."],"input":"Original vulnerability, remediation, and security invariant.","output":"Automated regression coverage and documented verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Future changes that reintroduce the original security condition fail validation.","failure_mode":"A later refactor or dependency change silently reintroduces the vulnerability.","root_cause":"Security fix is treated as a one-time code change rather than a persistent invariant.","solution":"Add exploit-oriented regression tests, layered controls, provenance, and recurring security validation.","edge_cases":["Dependency upgrades","Configuration changes","New API routes","Alternative code paths","Infrastructure changes"],"security_implications":"Regression prevention converts historical security failures into durable engineering constraints.","performance_implications":"Security regression suites increase CI time; prioritize tests by impact and risk while retaining critical coverage.","testing":{"method":"Run the original exploit test plus variants against the corrected implementation and during future changes.","expected_result":"Original and known bypass variants remain blocked.","limitations":"Regression tests do not prove complete vulnerability absence."},"verification":{"claim":"The remediation remains enforced after subsequent changes.","evidence_required":["Original vulnerability test","Regression suite result","Code/configuration review"],"method":"Execute regression tests and review the protected invariant.","limitations":"Complete security assurance requires layered testing, review, and operational monitoring."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Project lifecycle dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secure Coding Practices","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","CWE remediation principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B171","D40-B184","D40-B199"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B201","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Database Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database privilege minimization","question":"How should excessive database privileges granted to an application be remediated?","answer":"Create application-specific database identities with only the permissions required for normal operation, separate read and write roles where practical, and remove administrative privileges from runtime identities.","objective":"Limit the impact of application compromise or SQL injection.","requirements":["Use dedicated database identities.","Grant only required schemas, tables, views, and operations.","Separate administrative credentials from application credentials.","Review privileges periodically."],"constraints":["Legacy applications may require broad permissions during migration.","Some frameworks perform schema management separately from runtime access."],"preconditions":["Application connects to a database using a persistent identity."],"input":"Application database identity and required operations.","output":"Runtime identity with least-privilege database authorization.","expected_behavior":"Application can perform required operations but cannot perform unrelated administrative actions.","failure_mode":"Compromised application identity can alter schemas, create privileged users, or access unrelated data.","root_cause":"Runtime identity has excessive database privileges.","solution":"Create narrowly scoped roles and separate deployment/migration identities from runtime identities.","edge_cases":["Background workers","Reporting jobs","Read replicas","Schema migrations","Multi-tenant databases"],"security_implications":"Least-privilege database access limits post-compromise impact.","performance_implications":"Authorization checks add negligible overhead; poorly designed views or row-level policies can affect query planning.","testing":{"method":"Attempt required and prohibited database operations using the runtime identity.","expected_result":"Required operations succeed and administrative/unrelated operations fail."},"verification":{"claim":"Runtime database identity has only documented privileges.","evidence_required":["Effective grants","Role definitions","Negative authorization tests"],"method":"Inspect effective database privileges and execute representative denied operations.","limitations":"Database-specific privilege semantics must be verified."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Database-engine specific.","provenance":["OWASP Database Security guidance","NIST least-privilege principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B202","D40-B203","D40-B204"]} {"record_id":"D40-B202","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Database Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database credential separation","question":"Why should schema migration credentials be separated from application runtime credentials?","answer":"Schema migration often requires permissions such as ALTER, CREATE, or DROP that are unnecessary for normal application operation. Separating identities prevents a runtime compromise from automatically acquiring migration-level authority.","objective":"Reduce the privileges available to a compromised runtime application.","requirements":["Use separate migration and runtime identities.","Restrict migration credentials to controlled deployment processes.","Prevent runtime workloads from retrieving migration credentials."],"constraints":["Deployment pipelines may need controlled access to migration credentials.","Some serverless or managed platforms use different authentication models."],"preconditions":["Application deployment performs database schema changes."],"input":"Migration operation and runtime database access requirements.","output":"Distinct authorization boundaries for migration and application execution.","expected_behavior":"Runtime identity cannot perform migration operations.","failure_mode":"Application compromise enables destructive schema modification.","root_cause":"High-privilege deployment credentials are reused by runtime services.","solution":"Separate identities and enforce deployment-time access controls.","edge_cases":["Rollback migrations","Emergency fixes","Blue-green deployments","Multiple services sharing a database"],"security_implications":"Credential separation limits destructive database capabilities after application compromise.","performance_implications":"No meaningful runtime performance cost; deployment complexity increases.","testing":{"method":"Attempt migration operations using runtime identity and runtime queries using migration identity under controlled policy.","expected_result":"Each identity is restricted to its intended role."},"verification":{"claim":"Runtime credentials cannot perform schema administration.","evidence_required":["Role grants","Deployment identity policy","Negative tests"],"method":"Review and test effective privileges.","limitations":"Exact permissions vary by database engine."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database/deployment platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP least-privilege guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B201","D40-B203"]} {"record_id":"D40-B203","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Database Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database network exposure","question":"How should an unnecessarily internet-exposed database be remediated?","answer":"Remove direct public exposure where possible, place the database behind appropriate network controls, restrict inbound sources, require authenticated encrypted connections, and expose access only through authorized application or administrative paths.","objective":"Reduce direct attack surface against database infrastructure.","requirements":["Restrict database network reachability.","Use encrypted connections.","Separate application and administrative access paths.","Monitor unauthorized connection attempts."],"constraints":["Some architectures intentionally expose managed database endpoints but still require access controls.","Remote administration may require controlled network access."],"preconditions":["Database service is reachable from untrusted networks."],"input":"Database network topology and access requirements.","output":"Database is reachable only from authorized sources.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized network clients cannot establish database sessions.","failure_mode":"Internet attackers directly probe or authenticate against the database.","root_cause":"Database network boundary is broader than required.","solution":"Apply private networking, firewall rules, security groups, VPN or equivalent controlled access mechanisms.","edge_cases":["Cloud-managed databases","Disaster recovery","Read replicas","Administrative break-glass access"],"security_implications":"Network restriction reduces exposure to credential attacks and database vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Network controls generally add negligible latency but routing changes can affect topology.","testing":{"method":"Perform connection tests from authorized and unauthorized network locations.","expected_result":"Only approved sources can connect."},"verification":{"claim":"Database network exposure matches documented access requirements.","evidence_required":["Firewall/security-group rules","Network topology","Connection tests"],"method":"Review effective network policy and test representative paths.","limitations":"Cloud networking behavior must be verified in the deployed environment."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure/database platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST network security guidance","OWASP Database Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B201","D40-B204"]} {"record_id":"D40-B204","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Database Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database encryption in transit","question":"How should plaintext application-to-database connections be remediated?","answer":"Enable the database protocol's supported transport encryption, validate the server identity where applicable, and prevent fallback to plaintext connections.","objective":"Protect database credentials and data against network interception.","requirements":["Require encrypted database transport.","Validate peer identity where supported.","Disable insecure fallback.","Rotate credentials if plaintext exposure occurred."],"constraints":["Database drivers and servers have version-specific TLS settings.","Internal networks are not automatically trusted merely because they are private."],"preconditions":["Application communicates with a database over a network."],"input":"Database connection configuration.","output":"Encrypted authenticated database connection.","expected_behavior":"Connection fails when required transport security cannot be established.","failure_mode":"Credentials or database traffic travel over plaintext.","root_cause":"Encryption is optional or disabled.","solution":"Configure mandatory TLS or equivalent database transport protection and validate the effective connection settings.","edge_cases":["Certificate rotation","Private CAs","Connection pools","Proxy termination"],"security_implications":"Transport encryption reduces interception and credential theft risk.","performance_implications":"TLS handshake and encryption add CPU/network overhead; connection pooling can reduce repeated handshakes.","testing":{"method":"Inspect effective connection settings and attempt a plaintext connection where technically possible.","expected_result":"Plaintext connection is rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Runtime database traffic uses the required encrypted transport.","evidence_required":["Driver configuration","Server configuration","Connection traces"],"method":"Review effective settings and test connection negotiation.","limitations":"Exact TLS enforcement mechanisms vary by database and driver."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Database server/driver version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security guidance","Database vendor security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B156","D40-B203"]} {"record_id":"D40-B205","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Database Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database audit integrity","question":"How should database security audit records be protected from unauthorized modification?","answer":"Restrict write and administrative access to audit data, separate audit storage or privileges from the application generating events, and use integrity controls or append-oriented mechanisms appropriate to the threat model.","objective":"Preserve trustworthy evidence of security-relevant database activity.","requirements":["Separate audit-writing and audit-administration privileges.","Restrict deletion and modification.","Protect audit data integrity and retention.","Monitor access to audit records."],"constraints":["Some database-native audit facilities have engine-specific capabilities.","Strong immutability may require external storage."],"preconditions":["Database security events are retained for investigation or compliance."],"input":"Database audit event.","output":"Tamper-resistant audit record.","expected_behavior":"Application runtime cannot silently alter historical security audit records.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies or deletes evidence after compromising the application.","root_cause":"Audit records share the same mutable privilege boundary as application data.","solution":"Separate audit privileges and storage and use append-only or integrity-protected mechanisms where required.","edge_cases":["High-volume auditing","Retention deletion","Disaster recovery","Clock synchronization"],"security_implications":"Integrity-protected audit data improves incident investigation and detection of unauthorized activity.","performance_implications":"Audit persistence can add I/O and storage overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt unauthorized audit modification/deletion using application identity.","expected_result":"Modification is denied and audit access is recorded."},"verification":{"claim":"Runtime application identity cannot tamper with protected audit history.","evidence_required":["Audit permissions","Storage policy","Negative tests"],"method":"Review effective privileges and perform controlled modification attempts.","limitations":"Absolute immutability depends on storage architecture."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database/audit platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST audit guidance","OWASP Logging guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B201"]} {"record_id":"D40-B206","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"GraphQL query depth limitation","question":"How should deeply nested GraphQL queries be remediated when they enable resource exhaustion?","answer":"Apply query-depth limits, complexity or cost analysis, pagination requirements, and appropriate per-client resource limits. The limits should reflect actual application behavior rather than arbitrary values alone.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled query structure from exhausting CPU, memory, or downstream resources.","requirements":["Limit query depth or complexity.","Require bounded collection retrieval.","Apply authentication and rate/resource controls."],"constraints":["Legitimate clients may require complex queries.","Static depth alone may not capture expensive resolver combinations."],"preconditions":["Application exposes GraphQL or another recursive query language."],"input":"Client-supplied query document.","output":"Query executes only when its estimated resource cost is within policy.","expected_behavior":"Excessively deep or expensive queries are rejected before substantial processing.","failure_mode":"Nested query causes disproportionate resolver/database work.","root_cause":"Query structure has no enforceable computational budget.","solution":"Use depth and complexity analysis together with pagination and execution budgets.","edge_cases":["Fragments","Aliases","Recursive relationships","Batching","Expensive resolvers"],"security_implications":"Query complexity abuse can produce application-level denial of service.","performance_implications":"Complexity analysis adds parsing/validation overhead but prevents disproportionate execution.","testing":{"method":"Submit increasing query depths and complexity combinations.","expected_result":"Queries exceeding policy are rejected predictably."},"verification":{"claim":"GraphQL query execution has an enforceable resource boundary.","evidence_required":["Complexity policy","Rejected-query tests","Resolver cost analysis"],"method":"Review validation pipeline and execute boundary tests.","limitations":"Actual cost estimation depends on resolver implementation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"GraphQL implementation/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP GraphQL guidance","OWASP API Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B127","D40-B207","D40-B208"]} {"record_id":"D40-B207","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API batch abuse remediation","question":"How should unrestricted API batching be remediated when it enables authentication or resource abuse?","answer":"Bound the number of operations per request, apply per-operation authorization and resource limits, and ensure batching cannot bypass rate limits or expensive-operation controls.","objective":"Prevent batching from multiplying expensive or security-sensitive operations behind a single request limit.","requirements":["Limit batch size.","Apply authorization to each operation.","Account for total computational cost.","Apply rate limits to meaningful operations rather than only network requests."],"constraints":["Batching can be legitimate for performance.","Limits must account for transaction semantics and partial failure."],"preconditions":["API accepts multiple logical operations in one request."],"input":"Batch request containing multiple operations.","output":"Batch executes only within authorized and resource-bounded limits.","expected_behavior":"Oversized or disproportionately expensive batches are rejected or safely partitioned.","failure_mode":"One HTTP request triggers hundreds or thousands of privileged operations.","root_cause":"Rate and authorization controls are applied only at the outer request boundary.","solution":"Use operation-aware limits, per-item authorization, and aggregate cost controls.","edge_cases":["Mixed-authority batches","Partial success","Nested batches","Retries"],"security_implications":"Batch abuse can bypass throttling and amplify authorization or resource attacks.","performance_implications":"Per-operation checks add CPU/database work but are required for correct authorization.","testing":{"method":"Submit batches at and above configured limits with mixed authorized and unauthorized operations.","expected_result":"Limits and authorization apply to individual and aggregate operations."},"verification":{"claim":"Batching cannot bypass operation-level security and resource controls.","evidence_required":["Batch policy","Authorization tests","Rate-limit tests"],"method":"Test aggregate and per-operation behavior.","limitations":"Exact batching semantics vary by API design."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"API framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B127","D40-B206"]} {"record_id":"D40-B208","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API timeout and cancellation enforcement","question":"How should APIs remediate requests that can consume server resources indefinitely?","answer":"Define operation-specific time budgets, propagate cancellation to downstream work where supported, bound connection and database waits, and release resources when the client disconnects or the deadline expires.","objective":"Prevent abandoned or malicious requests from consuming resources indefinitely.","requirements":["Set explicit request deadlines.","Propagate cancellation to downstream operations.","Bound database/network waits.","Clean up resources on timeout."],"constraints":["Some operations legitimately take longer than normal requests.","Cancellation semantics vary across libraries and runtimes."],"preconditions":["API requests can trigger long-running or blocking work."],"input":"Request with execution deadline.","output":"Operation completes or is cancelled within policy.","expected_behavior":"Expired requests stop consuming avoidable resources.","failure_mode":"Disconnected or malicious clients leave expensive work running indefinitely.","root_cause":"No end-to-end timeout or cancellation propagation.","solution":"Implement deadline propagation, bounded waits, cancellation-safe cleanup, and asynchronous job models for long tasks.","edge_cases":["Streaming responses","Queued jobs","Database cancellation","Partial external side effects"],"security_implications":"Unbounded resource consumption can create denial of service.","performance_implications":"Timeouts improve resource utilization but can increase incomplete operations if set too aggressively.","testing":{"method":"Inject slow downstream dependencies and client disconnects.","expected_result":"Work is bounded and resources are released according to policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Request resource consumption is bounded by enforceable deadlines.","evidence_required":["Timeout configuration","Cancellation behavior","Slow-dependency tests"],"method":"Trace deadlines through downstream calls and test expiration paths.","limitations":"External systems may not support immediate cancellation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Runtime/framework/client dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B127","D40-B175"]} {"record_id":"D40-B209","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API content-type enforcement","question":"How should APIs remediate unsafe acceptance of unexpected content types?","answer":"Require an explicit supported content type for each endpoint, reject unsupported representations before parsing, and configure parsers with bounded resource limits.","objective":"Reduce parser confusion and unexpected input-processing paths.","requirements":["Define accepted media types.","Reject unsupported content types.","Apply parser size and complexity limits."],"constraints":["Some APIs intentionally support multiple representations.","Content negotiation behavior varies by framework."],"preconditions":["Endpoint parses structured request bodies."],"input":"HTTP request with content type and body.","output":"Body is parsed only by an approved parser for the declared representation.","expected_behavior":"Unsupported or ambiguous content types are rejected.","failure_mode":"Unexpected parser processes attacker-controlled input with unsafe assumptions.","root_cause":"Endpoint accepts arbitrary content types or automatically selects parsers.","solution":"Use explicit media-type policy and parser restrictions.","edge_cases":["Missing Content-Type","Incorrect charset","Multipart requests","Compressed bodies"],"security_implications":"Parser confusion can create validation bypasses or denial-of-service conditions.","performance_implications":"Early rejection avoids unnecessary parsing work.","testing":{"method":"Submit supported, unsupported, missing, and malformed content types.","expected_result":"Only approved representations are processed."},"verification":{"claim":"Endpoint parser selection follows explicit media-type policy.","evidence_required":["Framework parser configuration","Negative tests"],"method":"Review parser registration and execute content-type tests.","limitations":"Framework defaults must be verified for the deployed version."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","IETF HTTP specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B209","D40-B210"]} {"record_id":"D40-B210","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"API Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API request body size enforcement","question":"How should APIs remediate unrestricted request body sizes?","answer":"Configure maximum body sizes at the edge and application layers, reject oversized requests before expensive parsing, and define endpoint-specific limits when payload requirements differ.","objective":"Prevent memory, CPU, and storage exhaustion through oversized request bodies.","requirements":["Set explicit request-size limits.","Enforce limits before full body processing where possible.","Use endpoint-specific limits for large legitimate payloads."],"constraints":["File-upload endpoints require separate policies.","Compression can make compressed and decompressed size differ."],"preconditions":["API accepts client-controlled request bodies."],"input":"HTTP request body.","output":"Request is processed only when size is within policy.","expected_behavior":"Oversized requests are rejected without excessive resource consumption.","failure_mode":"Attacker sends extremely large body to consume memory, disk, or processing resources.","root_cause":"No effective request-size boundary.","solution":"Enforce limits at reverse proxy, framework, and parser layers.","edge_cases":["Chunked transfer","Compressed payloads","Multipart forms","Streaming uploads"],"security_implications":"Unbounded request bodies enable application-layer denial of service.","performance_implications":"Early size checks reduce wasted parsing and allocation.","testing":{"method":"Send payloads below, at, and above configured limits using supported transfer modes.","expected_result":"Boundary behavior is deterministic and resources remain bounded."},"verification":{"claim":"Request body size cannot exceed documented limits without authorization and explicit endpoint policy.","evidence_required":["Proxy limits","Application limits","Boundary tests"],"method":"Inspect effective limits and test oversized requests.","limitations":"Streaming endpoints require different enforcement mechanisms."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Server/proxy/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B127","D40-B208"]} {"record_id":"D40-B211","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Multi-factor authentication bypass remediation","question":"How should an MFA bypass caused by trusting client-controlled authentication state be remediated?","answer":"Keep MFA completion state server-side and bind it to the authenticated session or transaction context. Never treat client-controlled flags, hidden fields, or unsigned tokens as proof that MFA was completed.","objective":"Ensure protected operations require server-verified MFA state.","requirements":["Track MFA state in a trusted server-side security context.","Bind MFA completion to the correct account and session.","Require MFA state for each protected operation according to policy."],"constraints":["Step-up authentication may use short-lived signed assertions.","Distributed systems require shared trusted session state or verifiable assertions."],"preconditions":["Application uses multi-step authentication or MFA.","Client can influence some state between steps."],"input":"Authentication session and MFA completion state.","output":"Protected operation proceeds only after trusted MFA verification.","expected_behavior":"Changing client-visible MFA state does not bypass the MFA requirement.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies a client flag or parameter to appear MFA-verified.","root_cause":"Authentication state is trusted without server-side integrity protection.","solution":"Use server-side state or cryptographically protected, audience-bound, short-lived assertions.","edge_cases":["Session fixation","Multiple devices","Step-up authentication","Session restoration"],"security_implications":"MFA bypass can negate otherwise strong authentication.","performance_implications":"Server-side state lookup adds small authentication overhead.","testing":{"method":"Modify all client-controlled authentication-state parameters and attempt protected actions.","expected_result":"Protected action remains denied until legitimate MFA completion."},"verification":{"claim":"MFA completion cannot be forged through client-controlled state.","evidence_required":["Authentication flow review","Negative bypass tests"],"method":"Trace trusted authentication state across every transition.","limitations":"Identity-provider-managed MFA requires provider-specific verification."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Authentication framework/identity-provider dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet","CWE-287"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B112","D40-B212","D40-B213"]} {"record_id":"D40-B212","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authentication token audience validation","question":"How should applications remediate acceptance of a valid token issued for another service?","answer":"Validate the token issuer, audience, signature, expiration, and relevant claims according to the application security contract. A valid signature alone does not establish that the token was intended for the current service.","objective":"Prevent token substitution across services or security contexts.","requirements":["Validate issuer.","Validate audience.","Validate signature and temporal claims.","Apply required claim constraints."],"constraints":["Token formats and identity-provider semantics vary.","Some tokens are intentionally multi-audience."],"preconditions":["Application accepts bearer or signed authentication tokens.","Multiple services or audiences exist."],"input":"Authentication token.","output":"Token accepted only when all required trust properties match the service.","expected_behavior":"A token validly signed for another audience is rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker reuses a legitimate token from another service against the target API.","root_cause":"Application validates signature but not intended audience or issuer.","solution":"Implement explicit issuer and audience validation and enforce token policy consistently.","edge_cases":["Multiple audiences","Token exchange","Clock skew","Key rotation"],"security_implications":"Audience confusion can enable privilege escalation or cross-service impersonation.","performance_implications":"Claim validation has low computational cost.","testing":{"method":"Submit valid tokens with wrong audience, issuer, expiry, or signature.","expected_result":"Invalid-context tokens are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Accepted tokens are valid for the target service and security context.","evidence_required":["Token validation configuration","Negative claim tests"],"method":"Review validator configuration and test claim variations.","limitations":"Identity-provider semantics must be confirmed from current documentation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Token library and identity-provider dependent.","provenance":["OWASP JWT guidance","RFC 7519","RFC 8725"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B211","D40-B213"]} {"record_id":"D40-B213","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authentication token algorithm validation","question":"How should applications remediate unsafe acceptance of unexpected token algorithms?","answer":"Configure token verification to allow only explicitly approved algorithms and reject tokens that use unsupported or disallowed algorithms. Do not select verification behavior solely from attacker-controlled token metadata.","objective":"Prevent algorithm-confusion and insecure token-validation paths.","requirements":["Define an explicit algorithm allowlist.","Bind verification key type to the expected algorithm.","Reject unsupported algorithms before accepting claims."],"constraints":["Algorithm choices depend on identity-provider configuration.","Migration may temporarily require controlled support for multiple algorithms."],"preconditions":["Application verifies signed authentication tokens."],"input":"Signed token with algorithm metadata.","output":"Token is accepted only under configured cryptographic policy.","expected_behavior":"Tokens using disallowed algorithms are rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker manipulates token algorithm metadata to reach an unintended verification path.","root_cause":"Verifier trusts token-declared algorithm without an independent policy.","solution":"Configure explicit algorithms and key types in the verification library.","edge_cases":["Algorithm rotation","Multiple key types","Key confusion","Legacy tokens"],"security_implications":"Unsafe algorithm handling can undermine token authenticity.","performance_implications":"Algorithm allowlisting has negligible overhead.","testing":{"method":"Submit otherwise valid tokens using disallowed algorithms and key types.","expected_result":"All policy-violating tokens are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Token verification uses an explicit cryptographic algorithm policy.","evidence_required":["Library configuration","Negative algorithm tests"],"method":"Inspect validator settings and test unsupported algorithms.","limitations":"Exact behavior depends on token library version."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"JWT/token-library version dependent.","provenance":["RFC 8725","OWASP JWT guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B212","D40-B214"]} {"record_id":"D40-B214","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Token revocation and session invalidation","question":"How should applications remediate continued acceptance of compromised long-lived authentication tokens?","answer":"Use short token lifetimes where appropriate, support server-side revocation or session invalidation for high-risk credentials, rotate signing credentials under controlled procedures, and require reauthentication or token exchange for sensitive operations when justified.","objective":"Reduce the usable lifetime of compromised authentication material.","requirements":["Define token lifetime according to risk.","Provide invalidation for high-impact sessions or credentials.","Rotate compromised keys or credentials.","Require reauthentication for sensitive operations where appropriate."],"constraints":["Stateless tokens complicate immediate revocation.","Revocation state introduces infrastructure dependencies."],"preconditions":["Application accepts long-lived bearer credentials."],"input":"Authentication token associated with a compromised or invalidated session.","output":"Token is rejected according to revocation/lifetime policy.","expected_behavior":"Invalidated high-risk sessions cannot continue accessing protected resources.","failure_mode":"Stolen token remains usable until natural expiration despite known compromise.","root_cause":"No mechanism exists to invalidate active authentication state.","solution":"Use appropriate short-lived credentials, revocation state, session invalidation, or token exchange.","edge_cases":["Offline clients","Multiple devices","Clock skew","Emergency global revocation"],"security_implications":"Limits post-compromise credential lifetime.","performance_implications":"Revocation checks add state lookup overhead.","testing":{"method":"Invalidate a test session/token and verify access rejection.","expected_result":"Protected operations fail after revocation becomes effective."},"verification":{"claim":"High-risk authentication credentials can be invalidated according to policy.","evidence_required":["Revocation mechanism","Token lifetime policy","Negative tests"],"method":"Perform controlled invalidation and access tests.","limitations":"Immediate global revocation depends on architecture."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Authentication architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet","OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B111","D40-B212"]} {"record_id":"D40-B215","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Session privilege transition","question":"How should applications remediate privilege escalation caused by retaining a pre-authentication session after login?","answer":"Regenerate or replace the session identifier when authentication or privilege level changes, transfer only intended state, and invalidate the prior unauthenticated session identifier.","objective":"Prevent session fixation across authentication or privilege transitions.","requirements":["Generate a new session identifier after authentication.","Invalidate the previous session identifier.","Preserve only explicitly required non-security state."],"constraints":["Applications may maintain shopping or workflow state across authentication.","Distributed session stores must coordinate identifier replacement."],"preconditions":["Session state exists before authentication and persists after privilege elevation."],"input":"Pre-authentication session and successful authentication event.","output":"New authenticated session with unrelated identifier.","expected_behavior":"An attacker who knows the pre-authentication identifier cannot access the authenticated session.","failure_mode":"Pre-authentication session identifier remains valid after login.","root_cause":"Session identifier is not regenerated during privilege transition.","solution":"Rotate session identifiers on authentication and other security-context changes.","edge_cases":["MFA completion","Role changes","Password reset","Concurrent sessions"],"security_implications":"Session fixation can allow unauthorized access to an authenticated account.","performance_implications":"Session regeneration adds small session-store overhead.","testing":{"method":"Capture pre-authentication session identifier and attempt to reuse it after login.","expected_result":"Old identifier is invalid and new identifier is required."},"verification":{"claim":"Authentication creates a new trusted session identity.","evidence_required":["Session lifecycle review","Session-fixation negative test"],"method":"Trace identifier lifecycle across authentication transitions.","limitations":"Exact session behavior depends on framework."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Web framework/session implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet","CWE-384"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B111","D40-B214"]} {"record_id":"D40-B216","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Default-deny authorization","question":"How should applications remediate authorization paths that accidentally permit access when a policy check is missing?","answer":"Adopt deny-by-default authorization semantics and require explicit policy approval for protected operations. Missing, malformed, or unavailable authorization decisions should fail closed unless the operation is explicitly classified as public.","objective":"Prevent accidental access caused by incomplete authorization logic.","requirements":["Define protected operations explicitly.","Default unknown policy states to deny.","Centralize or consistently apply authorization checks."],"constraints":["Availability-critical operations may require carefully designed failure behavior.","Legacy applications may contain mixed authorization patterns."],"preconditions":["Application has protected resources or actions.","Authorization decisions can be absent or indeterminate."],"input":"Request and authorization context.","output":"Explicit allow or safe denial.","expected_behavior":"Missing policy does not silently grant access.","failure_mode":"Unconfigured route or permission results in implicit allow.","root_cause":"Authorization model treats absence of denial as permission.","solution":"Use default-deny policy evaluation and explicit public-route declarations.","edge_cases":["Service-to-service calls","Emergency access","Policy engine outage","New routes"],"security_implications":"Deny-by-default reduces accidental authorization bypasses.","performance_implications":"Centralized policy checks may add latency; caching must preserve authorization correctness.","testing":{"method":"Exercise new, missing, malformed, and unavailable authorization-policy cases.","expected_result":"Protected operations are denied unless explicitly authorized."},"verification":{"claim":"Unknown authorization states cannot become implicit allows.","evidence_required":["Policy semantics","Negative tests","Failure-mode tests"],"method":"Inspect policy engine behavior and simulate unavailable policy decisions.","limitations":"Some fail-open designs may be intentional for low-risk public operations."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Authorization framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B199","D40-B217"]} {"record_id":"D40-B217","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authorization decision fail-closed behavior","question":"How should authorization services behave when the policy decision service is unavailable?","answer":"For security-sensitive operations, an unavailable or indeterminate authorization decision should normally result in denial unless the operation has an explicitly documented safe alternative. The fallback must not silently broaden permissions.","objective":"Prevent authorization outages from becoming access-control bypasses.","requirements":["Define failure semantics per operation risk.","Deny sensitive operations when authorization cannot be established.","Audit policy-service failures and fallback decisions."],"constraints":["Availability requirements may justify carefully bounded fallback for specific low-risk actions.","Distributed authorization introduces network dependencies."],"preconditions":["Authorization depends on a remote policy or identity service."],"input":"Authorization request with unavailable policy service.","output":"Denied operation or explicitly documented safe fallback.","expected_behavior":"Security-sensitive operation does not proceed without required authorization evidence.","failure_mode":"Policy outage causes application to bypass authorization.","root_cause":"Availability fallback is implemented as implicit allow.","solution":"Use risk-classified fail-closed semantics and explicit safe fallback policies.","edge_cases":["Cached decisions","Network partitions","Emergency access","Policy version mismatch"],"security_implications":"Fail-open authorization can convert infrastructure failure into privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"Remote authorization and retries affect latency; bounded caching may reduce load but must account for revocation.","testing":{"method":"Simulate policy-service timeout, outage, stale cache, and malformed responses.","expected_result":"Sensitive operations are denied or follow explicitly approved fallback policy."},"verification":{"claim":"Authorization-service failure cannot silently grant sensitive permissions.","evidence_required":["Failure policy","Chaos/fault tests","Audit records"],"method":"Inject policy-service failures and inspect resulting authorization decisions.","limitations":"Fallback policy must be assessed per operation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Policy architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B216","D40-B218"]} {"record_id":"D40-B218","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authorization cache invalidation","question":"How should stale authorization caches be remediated?","answer":"Bound authorization-cache lifetime, invalidate or refresh decisions when relevant privileges change, and ensure cached decisions are scoped to the correct principal, resource, action, and policy version.","objective":"Prevent stale authorization decisions from granting access after privilege changes.","requirements":["Define cache TTL according to risk.","Bind cache keys to all authorization-relevant dimensions.","Support invalidation or policy-version changes for critical updates."],"constraints":["Immediate global invalidation can be difficult in distributed systems.","Long TTL improves performance but extends stale-decision exposure."],"preconditions":["Authorization decisions are cached.","Roles or permissions can change during cache lifetime."],"input":"Authorization request and cached decision.","output":"Decision reflects sufficiently current authorization state.","expected_behavior":"Revoked access does not remain effective beyond the documented security window.","failure_mode":"User retains access after role or permission revocation because of stale cache.","root_cause":"Cache lifecycle is disconnected from authorization lifecycle.","solution":"Use bounded TTL, event-driven invalidation where available, and policy/version-aware cache keys.","edge_cases":["Emergency revocation","Distributed cache partitions","Tenant-specific policies","Policy rollout"],"security_implications":"Stale authorization can extend unauthorized access.","performance_implications":"Shorter TTL increases authorization-service load; event invalidation can reduce unnecessary misses.","testing":{"method":"Change permissions and measure access behavior across cache states.","expected_result":"Revoked permissions stop working within the defined policy window."},"verification":{"claim":"Authorization cache staleness is bounded and documented.","evidence_required":["Cache policy","Invalidation mechanism","Revocation tests"],"method":"Perform controlled privilege changes and verify effective revocation timing.","limitations":"Exact guarantees depend on distributed-cache behavior."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Authorization/cache platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B216","D40-B217","D40-B219"]} {"record_id":"D40-B219","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Policy version consistency","question":"How should distributed services remediate authorization decisions made with incompatible policy versions?","answer":"Version or identify authorization policy state, ensure decision consumers understand the policy version they rely on, and prevent incompatible policy states from silently authorizing sensitive operations.","objective":"Prevent inconsistent policy rollout from creating authorization gaps.","requirements":["Track effective policy version.","Define compatibility and rollout rules.","Fail safely when required policy state cannot be established."],"constraints":["Distributed policy deployment may temporarily contain multiple versions.","Backward compatibility may be required during rolling deployments."],"preconditions":["Multiple services consume evolving authorization policies."],"input":"Authorization request and policy version.","output":"Decision based on an authorized and compatible policy state.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive requests do not execute under an invalid or incompatible policy version.","failure_mode":"Old service interprets policy differently and grants access unintentionally.","root_cause":"Policy version is not controlled across distributed authorization consumers.","solution":"Introduce policy versioning, compatibility rules, staged rollout, and explicit failure semantics.","edge_cases":["Rolling deployments","Rollback","Mixed policy versions","Emergency policy updates"],"security_implications":"Policy inconsistency can create temporary authorization bypasses.","performance_implications":"Policy version metadata adds minimal overhead.","testing":{"method":"Run mixed-version authorization tests during controlled rollout simulations.","expected_result":"All supported versions enforce the intended security boundary.","limitations":"Distributed deployment guarantees vary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Policy deployment architecture dependent.","provenance":["NIST access-control principles","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B217","D40-B218","D40-B220"]} {"record_id":"D40-B220","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Administrative authorization separation","question":"How should applications remediate excessive administrative privileges shared by normal operational accounts?","answer":"Separate administrative identities and privileges from normal user accounts, require stronger authentication for privileged operations, and record privileged actions independently.","objective":"Reduce compromise impact and improve accountability for administrative operations.","requirements":["Separate privileged and standard identities where appropriate.","Require explicit privileged authorization.","Audit privileged actions.","Avoid granting administrative roles to ordinary service or user accounts."],"constraints":["Operational workflows may need controlled privilege elevation.","Emergency access requires separate governance."],"preconditions":["Users or services perform both normal and administrative functions."],"input":"Identity, requested administrative action, and authorization context.","output":"Privileged action only when explicitly authorized.","expected_behavior":"Normal account compromise does not automatically provide administrative privileges.","failure_mode":"A broadly privileged identity is used for routine operations.","root_cause":"Privilege boundaries are not separated.","solution":"Use dedicated administrative roles, step-up authentication, just-in-time elevation where appropriate, and privileged auditing.","edge_cases":["Break-glass access","Service accounts","Delegated administration","Temporary elevation"],"security_implications":"Privilege separation limits lateral escalation.","performance_implications":"Step-up authentication adds latency only to privileged operations.","testing":{"method":"Attempt administrative operations with normal identities and verify privileged access paths.","expected_result":"Unauthorized privilege elevation is denied."},"verification":{"claim":"Administrative authority is explicitly separated from routine access.","evidence_required":["Role definitions","Privilege-elevation controls","Audit records"],"method":"Review effective permissions and execute negative tests.","limitations":"Identity-provider capabilities vary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Identity/access-control platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST privileged access principles","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B216","D40-B219"]} {"record_id":"D40-B221","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Input and Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"XML external entity remediation","question":"How should applications remediate XML external entity vulnerabilities?","answer":"Disable external entity resolution and unnecessary DTD processing in XML parsers, use secure parser configurations, and reject unsupported XML features when they are not required by the application.","objective":"Prevent XML parsers from resolving attacker-controlled external resources or entities.","requirements":["Disable external entity resolution where unnecessary.","Disable unsafe DTD features when not required.","Use parser security settings appropriate to the library version."],"constraints":["Some applications legitimately require specific XML features.","Parser security defaults differ by language and library version."],"preconditions":["Application parses attacker-influenced XML."],"input":"XML document containing entity or DTD constructs.","output":"XML parsed without unauthorized external resource resolution.","expected_behavior":"External entities and unauthorized external resources are not resolved.","failure_mode":"Parser accesses local files, internal network resources, or consumes excessive resources.","root_cause":"Unsafe XML parser defaults or insecure parser configuration.","solution":"Use hardened parser configuration and minimize supported XML features.","edge_cases":["Parameter entities","External DTDs","XInclude","Large nested entities"],"security_implications":"XXE can cause data disclosure, SSRF, or denial of service.","performance_implications":"Disabling external resolution reduces unexpected network and filesystem operations.","testing":{"method":"Submit XML with external entity and DTD payloads in an isolated environment.","expected_result":"External resolution is blocked."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted XML cannot trigger unauthorized external resource resolution.","evidence_required":["Parser configuration","Negative XML tests"],"method":"Inspect exact parser settings and test malicious XML constructs.","limitations":"Security depends on the exact XML parser and version."},"languages":["Python","Java","C++","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"XML parser/library version specific.","provenance":["CWE-611","OWASP XML Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B222","D40-B223"]} {"record_id":"D40-B222","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"XML entity expansion resource exhaustion","question":"How should XML entity expansion denial of service be remediated?","answer":"Disable unnecessary entity expansion and DTD processing, enforce parser limits on input size and structural complexity, and use hardened parser configurations.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled XML structure from consuming excessive CPU or memory.","requirements":["Disable unnecessary entity features.","Bound input size and parser complexity.","Use maintained parser implementations."],"constraints":["Some legacy XML formats depend on entities.","Limits must preserve legitimate document requirements."],"preconditions":["Application parses XML from untrusted sources."],"input":"XML document with high expansion or nesting complexity.","output":"Document is rejected or processed within bounded resource limits.","expected_behavior":"Parser does not allocate unbounded memory or CPU.","failure_mode":"Entity expansion causes denial of service.","root_cause":"Parser permits attacker-controlled expansion without resource limits.","solution":"Disable unsafe features and configure structural/resource limits.","edge_cases":["Nested entities","Large attribute values","Deep element nesting","Compressed XML"],"security_implications":"Parser exhaustion can deny service and affect shared workloads.","performance_implications":"Parser limits reduce worst-case resource consumption.","testing":{"method":"Use bounded malicious XML payloads in an isolated test environment.","expected_result":"Excessive structures are rejected before resource exhaustion."},"verification":{"claim":"XML parsing has enforceable resource boundaries.","evidence_required":["Parser limits","Malicious-input tests"],"method":"Review configuration and observe resource behavior under controlled inputs.","limitations":"No finite test proves complete parser safety."},"languages":["Python","Java","C++","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"XML parser/version dependent.","provenance":["CWE-776","OWASP XML Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B221","D40-B223"]} {"record_id":"D40-B223","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Parser nesting and complexity limits","question":"How should deeply nested structured input be remediated when parser recursion or memory can be exhausted?","answer":"Enforce maximum nesting depth, input size, collection counts, token lengths, and other format-specific complexity limits before or during parsing.","objective":"Prevent algorithmic or memory exhaustion caused by structurally complex input.","requirements":["Define format-specific complexity limits.","Reject input before resource exhaustion.","Use iterative parsing where appropriate."],"constraints":["Limits must be based on legitimate application requirements.","Different formats have different complexity dimensions."],"preconditions":["Application accepts attacker-controlled structured documents."],"input":"Structured document with excessive depth or breadth.","output":"Input is parsed only within defined resource limits.","expected_behavior":"Excessively complex input is rejected predictably.","failure_mode":"Parser recursion, allocation, or traversal consumes excessive resources.","root_cause":"Input grammar has no enforceable complexity budget.","solution":"Add parser limits and resource budgets and use hardened parser libraries.","edge_cases":["Deep nesting","Huge arrays","Long strings","Many keys","Repeated references"],"security_implications":"Complexity attacks can cause denial of service.","performance_implications":"Early rejection prevents disproportionate CPU and memory use.","testing":{"method":"Generate inputs around each structural limit.","expected_result":"Boundary values are accepted or rejected consistently and resource usage remains bounded."},"verification":{"claim":"Parser resource consumption is bounded by documented input constraints.","evidence_required":["Parser limits","Boundary tests","Resource observations"],"method":"Review parser controls and execute complexity tests.","limitations":"Resource behavior is runtime and implementation dependent."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Parser/library dependent.","provenance":["OWASP denial-of-service guidance","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B206","D40-B222"]} {"record_id":"D40-B224","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Input and Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prototype pollution remediation","question":"How should JavaScript or TypeScript applications remediate prototype pollution through unsafe object merging?","answer":"Reject dangerous property names when processing untrusted object paths, use safe data structures and merge APIs, avoid recursively assigning attacker-controlled keys into prototypes, and keep affected libraries updated.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled object properties from modifying shared object behavior.","requirements":["Validate object keys and paths.","Use safe merge mechanisms.","Avoid unsafe recursive assignment.","Patch vulnerable dependencies."],"constraints":["Prototype behavior is specific to JavaScript object semantics.","Legitimate nested dynamic objects may require careful allowlisting."],"preconditions":["Application merges or interprets attacker-controlled JavaScript objects.","The runtime uses JavaScript object prototypes."],"input":"Untrusted object or property path.","output":"Data structure containing only permitted properties without prototype mutation.","expected_behavior":"Attacker-controlled keys cannot modify Object.prototype or equivalent shared behavior.","failure_mode":"Polluted prototype changes application logic or security checks.","root_cause":"Dynamic property assignment or vulnerable merge implementation accepts dangerous keys.","solution":"Use allowlisted schemas, safe object construction, secure merge libraries, and dependency updates.","edge_cases":["proto","constructor","prototype","Nested paths","JSON parsing"],"security_implications":"Prototype pollution can affect authorization, configuration, and application behavior.","performance_implications":"Schema validation and safer object construction add processing overhead proportional to object size.","testing":{"method":"Test dangerous property names and nested merge paths.","expected_result":"Prototype state remains unchanged and malicious properties are rejected or isolated."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted object processing cannot mutate shared prototypes.","evidence_required":["Merge implementation","Dependency version","Negative tests"],"method":"Inspect object-construction paths and test prototype-pollution payloads.","limitations":"Exact exploitability depends on runtime and vulnerable library behavior."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"ECMAScript/runtime/library dependent.","provenance":["CWE-1321","OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B225","D40-B226"]} {"record_id":"D40-B225","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Input and Parser Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Regular expression complexity remediation","question":"How should a regular expression denial-of-service vulnerability be remediated when a regex operates on attacker-controlled input?","answer":"Replace vulnerable patterns with linear-time alternatives where possible, bound input length, use regex engines or features with appropriate complexity guarantees, and avoid nested ambiguous quantifiers on untrusted input.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled strings from causing excessive regex execution time.","requirements":["Review regex complexity.","Bound input size.","Use safer pattern structures or appropriate engines.","Test worst-case inputs."],"constraints":["Some complex matching requirements cannot be expressed as simple linear patterns.","Engine semantics differ significantly."],"preconditions":["Application applies regular expressions to untrusted input."],"input":"Attacker-controlled string and regex pattern.","output":"Matching completes within a bounded resource budget.","expected_behavior":"Pathological input cannot consume disproportionate CPU.","failure_mode":"Regex backtracking grows excessively for crafted input.","root_cause":"Pattern and engine combination permits pathological complexity.","solution":"Refactor regex, constrain input, or use a safer matching strategy.","edge_cases":["Empty input","Very long input","Repeated near-matches","Unicode"],"security_implications":"ReDoS can cause application-level denial of service.","performance_implications":"Safer patterns may alter matching performance or semantics and require regression testing.","testing":{"method":"Benchmark controlled worst-case inputs against a defined timeout/resource budget.","expected_result":"Matching remains within the documented limit."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-sensitive regex processing has bounded behavior for tested worst-case inputs.","evidence_required":["Pattern analysis","Worst-case tests","Timeout/resource policy"],"method":"Review pattern and run adversarial input tests.","limitations":"Finite testing does not prove asymptotic complexity for every engine.","security_implications":"Unbounded regex execution can consume shared CPU resources.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Regex engine/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-1333","OWASP Regular Expression DoS guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B123","D40-B223"]} {"record_id":"D40-B226","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"DOM-based injection remediation","question":"How should client-side DOM injection caused by unsafe HTML sinks be remediated?","answer":"Avoid inserting untrusted data into HTML or script-capable DOM sinks, prefer text-only APIs, use contextual output encoding when HTML is genuinely required, and apply a restrictive content security policy as defense in depth.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled browser data from becoming executable markup or script.","requirements":["Use safe DOM APIs for untrusted text.","Sanitize HTML only when rich markup is required.","Apply context-appropriate output handling."],"constraints":["Framework abstractions differ in escaping behavior.","Rich-text requirements need carefully reviewed sanitization."],"preconditions":["Client code inserts attacker-influenced data into the DOM."],"input":"Untrusted browser data.","output":"Data rendered without unintended code execution.","expected_behavior":"Untrusted text remains data rather than executable HTML/script.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled content executes in the victim's browser.","root_cause":"Unsafe DOM sink or incorrect contextual encoding.","solution":"Replace unsafe sinks, use trusted framework escaping, sanitize required HTML, and add CSP defense in depth.","edge_cases":["innerHTML","URL attributes","SVG","Template rendering","DOM clobbering"],"security_implications":"DOM injection can lead to session theft, account actions, or data exfiltration.","performance_implications":"Sanitization and CSP have modest client-side overhead.","testing":{"method":"Run context-specific injection payload tests across all DOM sinks.","expected_result":"Payloads render as data and do not execute."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted client-side data cannot reach executable DOM contexts without approved sanitization.","evidence_required":["Sink inventory","Static analysis","Dynamic injection tests"],"method":"Trace data flows and test representative sinks.","limitations":"Complete DOM sink coverage requires broad application analysis."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Browser/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP DOM-based XSS Prevention guidance","CWE-79"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B103","D40-B227"]} {"record_id":"D40-B227","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Trusted Types remediation","question":"How can Trusted Types help remediate DOM XSS in supported browser applications?","answer":"Use Trusted Types to constrain dangerous DOM sinks so that only approved typed values, produced through controlled policies, can reach those sinks. Treat this as defense in depth alongside safe coding and sanitization.","objective":"Reduce the number of code paths capable of assigning executable content to sensitive DOM sinks.","requirements":["Enable a restrictive Trusted Types policy where supported.","Define narrowly scoped policies.","Migrate unsafe sink usage."],"constraints":["Browser support and framework integration vary.","Trusted Types does not replace server-side validation or safe application design."],"preconditions":["Application runs in browsers supporting the relevant security mechanism."],"input":"String or typed content destined for a dangerous DOM sink.","output":"Only policy-approved trusted content reaches the sink.","expected_behavior":"Direct unsafe string assignment to protected sinks is blocked.","failure_mode":"An overlooked DOM sink becomes an XSS execution path.","root_cause":"Application permits unrestricted string flow into executable DOM contexts.","solution":"Adopt Trusted Types with restrictive policies and migrate legacy sinks.","edge_cases":["Third-party libraries","Legacy widgets","Script URL sinks","Policy bypasses"],"security_implications":"Trusted Types can reduce DOM XSS attack surface.","performance_implications":"Policy checks add browser-side processing but are generally localized to protected sinks.","testing":{"method":"Enable enforcement in a controlled environment and test existing sink usage.","expected_result":"Unsafe sink assignments fail and approved flows continue."},"verification":{"claim":"Protected DOM sinks reject untrusted string assignments.","evidence_required":["CSP/Trusted Types configuration","Browser tests"],"method":"Run application under enforcement and inspect violations.","limitations":"Coverage depends on browser support and third-party code compatibility."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Modern browser/CSP implementation dependent.","provenance":["W3C Trusted Types specification","OWASP DOM XSS Prevention guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B103","D40-B226"]} {"record_id":"D40-B228","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"PostMessage origin validation","question":"How should applications remediate insecure cross-window postMessage handling?","answer":"Validate the sender origin against an explicit allowlist before processing sensitive messages, validate message structure and type, and avoid using wildcard target origins when sending sensitive information.","objective":"Prevent untrusted browser origins from injecting or receiving sensitive cross-window messages.","requirements":["Validate event.origin against trusted origins.","Validate message schema and context.","Use explicit target origins for sensitive messages."],"constraints":["Applications with multiple trusted origins need controlled configuration.","Origin strings must be compared according to browser origin semantics."],"preconditions":["Application uses window.postMessage or equivalent cross-context messaging."],"input":"Cross-window message and sender origin.","output":"Message processed only when origin and message contract are authorized.","expected_behavior":"Messages from untrusted origins are ignored or rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled iframe sends a message that triggers a privileged action.","root_cause":"Message handler trusts event data without validating origin.","solution":"Allowlist origins, validate message structure, and bind sensitive messages to expected application state.","edge_cases":["Subdomains","Null origin","Embedded sandboxed frames","Multiple environments"],"security_implications":"Improper origin validation can enable cross-origin command injection or data disclosure.","performance_implications":"Origin and schema validation are inexpensive.","testing":{"method":"Send identical messages from trusted and untrusted origins.","expected_result":"Only trusted-origin messages are accepted."},"verification":{"claim":"Sensitive cross-window actions require an authorized message origin.","evidence_required":["Origin validation code","Cross-origin tests"],"method":"Review handlers and test multiple origin variants.","limitations":"Browser embedding and sandbox behavior must be tested in the target environment."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Browser-dependent.","provenance":["OWASP postMessage guidance","MDN Web APIs documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B229","D40-B230"]} {"record_id":"D40-B229","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Browser storage secret exposure remediation","question":"How should sensitive authentication material stored in browser-accessible storage be remediated?","answer":"Avoid storing high-value long-lived credentials in browser storage that is directly readable by application JavaScript when a safer session architecture is available. Prefer secure, appropriately scoped cookies or other mechanisms consistent with the authentication design.","objective":"Reduce credential theft impact from client-side script compromise.","requirements":["Classify stored values by sensitivity.","Avoid unnecessary long-lived bearer tokens in script-readable storage.","Use secure cookie attributes when cookies are appropriate."],"constraints":["Application architecture may require browser-side state.","Cookies have CSRF considerations that must be addressed separately."],"preconditions":["Browser application stores authentication or sensitive tokens client-side."],"input":"Authentication/session state.","output":"Credential storage aligned with the application's threat model.","expected_behavior":"A client-side injection flaw has less direct access to high-value session credentials.","failure_mode":"Any JavaScript execution can directly read a long-lived bearer token.","root_cause":"Sensitive credentials are stored in a script-readable location without necessity.","solution":"Use secure session cookies or short-lived scoped tokens and address CSRF when using cookies.","edge_cases":["SPAs","Cross-origin APIs","Refresh tokens","Service workers"],"security_implications":"Storage choice affects the blast radius of XSS and token theft.","performance_implications":"Cookie-based sessions can affect request size and server-side session state.","testing":{"method":"Inspect browser storage and attempt controlled script access under the test threat model.","expected_result":"High-value credentials are not unnecessarily exposed to arbitrary application scripts."},"verification":{"claim":"Authentication storage matches documented XSS and CSRF threat controls.","evidence_required":["Storage design","Cookie attributes","Client security tests"],"method":"Review credential lifecycle and browser storage behavior.","limitations":"No storage mechanism eliminates all client compromise risks."},"languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Browser/authentication architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet","OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B226","D40-B230"]} {"record_id":"D40-B230","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Client-Side Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Browser cookie security attributes","question":"How should insecure session cookie attributes be remediated?","answer":"Use Secure for HTTPS-only transmission, HttpOnly when script access is unnecessary, and an appropriate SameSite policy. Scope Domain and Path narrowly where possible.","objective":"Reduce session exposure through network interception and browser-side script or cross-site request behavior.","requirements":["Set Secure for protected cookies.","Set HttpOnly when JavaScript does not need access.","Choose SameSite according to application flow.","Minimize cookie scope."],"constraints":["Cross-site authentication flows may require carefully selected SameSite behavior.","Cookie semantics vary with browser version."],"preconditions":["Application uses cookies for session or security-sensitive state."],"input":"Session cookie configuration.","output":"Cookie with security attributes matching the threat model.","expected_behavior":"Browser applies intended transport, script-access, and cross-site restrictions.","failure_mode":"Session cookie is exposed to script, plaintext transport, or unintended cross-site requests.","root_cause":"Security-sensitive cookie attributes are omitted or overly permissive.","solution":"Configure Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, and narrow scope as appropriate.","edge_cases":["Cross-site SSO","Subdomains","Embedded applications","Legacy browsers"],"security_implications":"Correct attributes reduce session theft and CSRF-related exposure.","performance_implications":"Cookie attributes have negligible performance cost; narrower cookies can reduce unnecessary request bytes.","testing":{"method":"Inspect Set-Cookie responses and test browser behavior across relevant authentication flows.","expected_result":"Cookie attributes enforce intended security policy without breaking legitimate flows."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-sensitive cookies have documented and effective attributes.","evidence_required":["HTTP responses","Browser tests","Cookie policy"],"method":"Inspect effective browser cookie behavior.","limitations":"Browser compatibility must be considered."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Browser/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet","RFC 6265bis"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B229"]} {"record_id":"D40-B231","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Network egress allowlisting","question":"How should unrestricted server egress be remediated for security-sensitive workloads?","answer":"Define required outbound destinations and protocols, restrict egress at network boundaries, and keep application-level destination validation where user-controlled destinations exist.","objective":"Limit the destinations reachable after application compromise or SSRF.","requirements":["Inventory legitimate outbound dependencies.","Restrict destination networks and ports.","Monitor denied and unexpected egress."],"constraints":["Dynamic cloud services and third-party integrations can complicate allowlists.","Overly restrictive egress can break legitimate operations."],"preconditions":["Workload has network access to external or internal services."],"input":"Outbound connection attempt.","output":"Connection allowed only when matching egress policy.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized destinations cannot be reached from the workload.","failure_mode":"Compromised application establishes arbitrary outbound connections.","root_cause":"Network egress is unrestricted.","solution":"Implement layered egress policy and maintain explicit dependency inventory.","edge_cases":["DNS resolution","Proxy gateways","IPv6","Dynamic service discovery"],"security_implications":"Egress restriction limits SSRF, malware callbacks, and lateral movement.","performance_implications":"Proxying or inspection can add latency and throughput constraints.","testing":{"method":"Attempt connections to approved and prohibited destinations from representative workloads.","expected_result":"Only policy-approved egress succeeds."},"verification":{"claim":"Workload outbound connectivity matches documented dependency policy.","evidence_required":["Firewall/network policy","Connection tests","Egress logs"],"method":"Review effective network controls and test denied destinations.","limitations":"Cloud/network infrastructure changes require ongoing validation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Network platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST zero trust guidance","OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B232","D40-B233"]} {"record_id":"D40-B232","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Network segmentation remediation","question":"How should flat internal network architecture be remediated when application compromise enables lateral movement?","answer":"Segment workloads according to trust and communication requirements, restrict east-west traffic to approved flows, and require authenticated service access rather than assuming internal network location is sufficient authorization.","objective":"Limit lateral movement after compromise of one component.","requirements":["Identify service communication dependencies.","Restrict east-west traffic.","Use service identity and authorization for sensitive communication.","Monitor unexpected lateral connections."],"constraints":["Legacy systems may require broad connectivity.","Segmentation changes can affect availability."],"preconditions":["Multiple services share a broadly reachable network.","Compromise of one service could expose others."],"input":"Inter-service network flow.","output":"Only authorized service communication is permitted.","expected_behavior":"Compromised workload cannot freely scan or connect to unrelated services.","failure_mode":"Attacker pivots across flat network segments.","root_cause":"Network location is treated as sufficient trust.","solution":"Implement segmentation plus service-level authentication and authorization.","edge_cases":["Shared databases","Service discovery","Administrative access","Disaster recovery"],"security_implications":"Segmentation reduces lateral movement and blast radius.","performance_implications":"Additional routing/firewall checks may add latency.","testing":{"method":"Attempt representative unauthorized east-west connections from compromised-workload simulations.","expected_result":"Unapproved flows are blocked and logged."},"verification":{"claim":"East-west network access follows documented communication policy.","evidence_required":["Network topology","Policy rules","Lateral-movement tests"],"method":"Review flows and test prohibited connections.","limitations":"Segmentation effectiveness depends on complete dependency inventory."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure dependent.","provenance":["NIST zero trust architecture","NIST network security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B194","D40-B231"]} {"record_id":"D40-B233","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"DNS rebinding protection","question":"How should applications that fetch user-controlled hostnames reduce DNS rebinding risk?","answer":"Resolve and validate destination addresses under controlled policy, avoid assuming hostname identity is stable, and combine application validation with network egress restrictions.","objective":"Prevent DNS-controlled hostnames from changing into unauthorized destinations after initial validation.","requirements":["Validate resolved addresses.","Control DNS resolution behavior where practical.","Enforce egress restrictions independently."],"constraints":["Distributed DNS and legitimate multi-address services complicate validation.","DNS caching can interact with application connection behavior."],"preconditions":["Application connects to user-influenced hostnames."],"input":"Hostname and DNS resolution result.","output":"Connection only to policy-approved destination.","expected_behavior":"A hostname changing to a prohibited address cannot bypass destination policy.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts an initial address and later connection reaches a restricted address.","root_cause":"Hostname is treated as a stable authorization identity.","solution":"Use controlled resolution, address validation, connection consistency, and network egress controls.","edge_cases":["A/AAAA changes","Short TTL","IPv6","Multiple records"],"security_implications":"DNS rebinding can undermine hostname-based network controls.","performance_implications":"Controlled DNS resolution can affect latency and cache behavior.","testing":{"method":"Use controlled DNS responses to change destination classification during a request sequence.","expected_result":"Unauthorized address is blocked."},"verification":{"claim":"Destination authorization remains valid despite DNS changes.","evidence_required":["Resolver behavior","Address policy","Network controls"],"method":"Review and test controlled DNS changes.","limitations":"Infrastructure DNS behavior must be included in validation."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Runtime/network dependent.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention guidance","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B151","D40-B153","D40-B231"]} {"record_id":"D40-B234","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Outbound proxy credential protection","question":"How should applications remediate insecure handling of outbound proxy credentials?","answer":"Store proxy credentials in protected configuration or secret management, restrict proxy access, avoid embedding credentials in URLs or logs, and ensure proxy authentication occurs over protected transport when required.","objective":"Prevent compromise of outbound proxy credentials and resulting network access.","requirements":["Externalize proxy credentials.","Avoid credential-bearing proxy URLs in logs.","Restrict proxy authentication and network access.","Rotate exposed credentials."],"constraints":["Proxy implementations and authentication mechanisms differ.","Some applications use environment variables supplied by deployment infrastructure."],"preconditions":["Application uses authenticated outbound proxies."],"input":"Proxy configuration and credentials.","output":"Authenticated proxy connection without unnecessary credential exposure.","expected_behavior":"Proxy credentials are not embedded in source or ordinary logs.","failure_mode":"Proxy credentials leak through configuration, URLs, or diagnostics.","root_cause":"Proxy authentication material is handled as ordinary application configuration.","solution":"Use protected secret injection, redaction, secure transport, and least-privilege proxy identities.","edge_cases":["Container environments","Shared proxies","Proxy failover","Credential rotation"],"security_implications":"Proxy credentials can provide broad outbound access or visibility into network traffic.","performance_implications":"Proxy authentication adds connection overhead; pooling can reduce repeated authentication.","testing":{"method":"Inspect configuration, logs, process metadata, and proxy authentication behavior.","expected_result":"Credentials remain protected and authorized connections succeed."},"verification":{"claim":"Proxy credentials are handled under the application's secret-management policy.","evidence_required":["Secret configuration","Log inspection","Access controls"],"method":"Review runtime configuration and diagnostic artifacts.","limitations":"Proxy platform behavior is environment specific."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Proxy/runtime dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST credential management principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B186","D40-B231"]} {"record_id":"D40-B235","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Network protocol downgrade prevention","question":"How should security-sensitive service communication be protected against protocol downgrade?","answer":"Define a minimum supported protocol and security level, reject weaker negotiation when the peer cannot satisfy policy, and validate the effective negotiated protocol rather than assuming configuration intent was applied.","objective":"Prevent attackers or intermediaries from forcing weaker communication modes.","requirements":["Define minimum protocol versions.","Disable insecure fallback.","Verify effective negotiated security settings.","Monitor downgrade-related failures."],"constraints":["Legacy clients may require migration.","Protocol negotiation differs among services."],"preconditions":["Service-to-service communication supports multiple protocol versions or modes."],"input":"Negotiation request and peer capabilities.","output":"Connection using a policy-approved protocol/security level.","expected_behavior":"Weak protocol negotiation is rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker forces an obsolete protocol or security mode.","root_cause":"Client or server permits fallback below the required security baseline.","solution":"Configure minimum security levels and test negotiation behavior.","edge_cases":["Rolling upgrades","Legacy clients","Proxy termination","Protocol translation"],"security_implications":"Downgrade attacks can expose data or weaken authentication.","performance_implications":"Modern protocols may change CPU/latency characteristics; security policy should precede optimization.","testing":{"method":"Attempt negotiation using prohibited protocol versions in a controlled environment.","expected_result":"Connection fails rather than downgrading."},"verification":{"claim":"Negotiation cannot fall below the documented security baseline.","evidence_required":["Effective protocol configuration","Negative negotiation tests"],"method":"Inspect and test actual negotiated connections.","limitations":"Infrastructure intermediaries must be included."},"languages":["Python","Java","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Protocol/runtime dependent.","provenance":["NIST TLS guidance","IETF protocol specifications"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B158","D40-B159","D40-B204"]} {"record_id":"D40-B236","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"File and Archive Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Archive extraction resource limits","question":"How should applications remediate archive-based resource exhaustion?","answer":"Limit archive entry count, total uncompressed size, individual entry size, nesting depth, and extraction time before and during extraction. Reject archives exceeding policy.","objective":"Prevent compressed or nested archives from consuming excessive disk, memory, or CPU.","requirements":["Bound compressed and uncompressed sizes.","Limit entry count and nesting.","Use extraction timeouts or resource budgets where supported.","Extract into controlled storage."],"constraints":["Legitimate archives can be large.","Compression ratios vary significantly."],"preconditions":["Application accepts archives from untrusted sources."],"input":"Archive file and extraction policy.","output":"Archive extracted only within defined resource limits.","expected_behavior":"Resource-exhausting archives are rejected before uncontrolled consumption.","failure_mode":"Small compressed archive expands to huge data volume or pathological nested content.","root_cause":"Extraction trusts archive metadata without resource budgeting.","solution":"Enforce multiple resource limits and monitor actual extraction usage.","edge_cases":["Nested archives","Sparse files","Huge entry counts","Compression bombs","Symlinks"],"security_implications":"Archive bombs can cause denial of service and storage exhaustion.","performance_implications":"Preflight inspection and extraction limits add processing but prevent disproportionate work.","testing":{"method":"Use controlled high-compression and high-entry-count archives.","expected_result":"Archives exceeding limits are rejected."},"verification":{"claim":"Archive extraction operates within defined resource budgets.","evidence_required":["Extraction limits","Negative tests","Resource monitoring"],"method":"Review extraction implementation and test adversarial archives.","limitations":"Archive format and library behavior vary."},"languages":["Python","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Archive-library dependent.","provenance":["CWE-409","OWASP File Upload guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B122","D40-B237"]} {"record_id":"D40-B237","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"File and Archive Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Archive symlink extraction remediation","question":"How should applications prevent archive extraction from creating unauthorized symbolic links?","answer":"Inspect archive entry types, reject or safely handle symbolic links when not required, and ensure extraction paths remain within the intended destination after canonicalization and filesystem checks.","objective":"Prevent archive contents from creating links that escape the extraction directory.","requirements":["Inspect archive entry types.","Reject unsafe link entries when unnecessary.","Enforce extraction-directory containment.","Use safe archive extraction APIs."],"constraints":["Some applications legitimately preserve symlinks.","Filesystem semantics differ by operating system."],"preconditions":["Application extracts untrusted archives.","Archive format supports links or special entries."],"input":"Archive containing symbolic-link entries.","output":"Only permitted entries are created within the extraction boundary.","expected_behavior":"Archive cannot create links to sensitive files or directories.","failure_mode":"Extracted symlink redirects later application access outside the intended directory.","root_cause":"Archive entry type and target are trusted without validation.","solution":"Reject unsafe links or safely materialize permitted files while enforcing containment.","edge_cases":["Absolute symlinks","Relative symlinks","Nested links","Hard links"],"security_implications":"Archive link abuse can cause file disclosure or overwrite.","performance_implications":"Entry inspection adds minimal overhead.","testing":{"method":"Extract controlled archives containing absolute and relative link entries.","expected_result":"Unsafe links are rejected or safely constrained."},"verification":{"claim":"Archive extraction cannot create unauthorized filesystem links.","evidence_required":["Archive handling code","Link-entry tests"],"method":"Review extraction logic and test link variants.","limitations":"Exact filesystem semantics must be verified per platform."},"languages":["Python","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Archive library/OS dependent.","provenance":["CWE-22","CWE-59","OWASP File Upload guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B123","D40-B236"]} {"record_id":"D40-B238","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"File and Archive Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Temporary upload isolation","question":"How should uploaded files be isolated before security-sensitive processing?","answer":"Store untrusted uploads outside executable/static paths, apply restrictive permissions, generate server-side names, and scan or validate content before moving files into trusted processing locations.","objective":"Prevent uploaded content from becoming executable or trusted merely because it was accepted by the upload endpoint.","requirements":["Separate untrusted storage from executable paths.","Generate non-user-controlled storage names.","Restrict file permissions.","Validate content before promotion."],"constraints":["Some applications require serving uploaded files directly.","Content-type detection is imperfect and should not be the only control."],"preconditions":["Application accepts files from untrusted users."],"input":"Uploaded file.","output":"Untrusted file stored in an isolated location with controlled lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Upload cannot directly execute code or overwrite trusted files.","failure_mode":"Attacker uploads executable or path-manipulating content into a trusted directory.","root_cause":"Upload storage is coupled to executable/static application paths.","solution":"Use isolated storage, server-generated names, content validation, and controlled serving mechanisms.","edge_cases":["Polyglot files","Archive uploads","SVG/HTML content","Concurrent uploads"],"security_implications":"Isolation limits remote code execution and content injection through uploads.","performance_implications":"Scanning and promotion introduce I/O and processing overhead.","testing":{"method":"Upload executable, script-like, malformed, and path-manipulating files.","expected_result":"Files remain non-executable and isolated until explicitly trusted."},"verification":{"claim":"Untrusted uploads cannot directly alter or execute trusted application resources.","evidence_required":["Storage configuration","Permission review","Malicious-file tests"],"method":"Trace upload lifecycle and test storage isolation.","limitations":"Serving uploaded content safely requires separate browser/content-security controls."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Web framework/storage dependent.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","CWE-434"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B121","D40-B239"]} {"record_id":"D40-B239","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"File and Archive Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"File type validation remediation","question":"How should applications remediate reliance on client-controlled file extensions or MIME types?","answer":"Treat client-provided filename extensions and MIME types as untrusted hints. Validate file structure using trusted parsing or content inspection appropriate to the use case and enforce an explicit allowed file-type policy.","objective":"Prevent malicious files from bypassing upload restrictions through metadata manipulation.","requirements":["Use server-side validation.","Validate file structure or signature when appropriate.","Do not rely solely on extension or client MIME type.","Store accepted files safely."],"constraints":["Content inspection is format specific.","Some formats are intentionally polymorphic or difficult to identify reliably."],"preconditions":["Application restricts uploaded file types."],"input":"Uploaded file and client-supplied metadata.","output":"File classified according to server-side policy.","expected_behavior":"Renaming a prohibited file or changing client MIME metadata does not bypass controls.","failure_mode":"Attacker uploads executable or active content with an allowed extension.","root_cause":"Validation trusts attacker-controlled metadata.","solution":"Use multiple independent validation layers and isolated storage.","edge_cases":["Polyglots","Compressed formats","Magic-byte ambiguity","Malformed files"],"security_implications":"Weak file validation can enable code execution or content-based attacks.","performance_implications":"Parsing and inspection add CPU/I/O cost proportional to file size.","testing":{"method":"Submit files with mismatched extensions, MIME types, and actual formats.","expected_result":"Policy follows actual allowed content characteristics rather than client metadata alone."},"verification":{"claim":"Upload policy cannot be bypassed solely by changing client-controlled metadata.","evidence_required":["Validation implementation","Mismatch tests"],"method":"Run representative file-type confusion tests.","limitations":"No generic content detector perfectly classifies all formats."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"File parser/framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","CWE-434"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B121","D40-B238"]} {"record_id":"D40-B240","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"File and Archive Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Server-side file serving authorization","question":"How should applications remediate unauthorized access to uploaded or stored files?","answer":"Authorize access to the logical resource before serving its underlying storage object, derive storage locations from trusted server-side identifiers, and avoid exposing predictable filesystem paths directly.","objective":"Prevent users from accessing files outside their authorized scope.","requirements":["Perform resource authorization before file retrieval.","Use opaque or server-controlled object identifiers.","Prevent direct path manipulation.","Apply tenant ownership checks."],"constraints":["Public files may intentionally bypass authorization.","Object storage systems have their own policy models."],"preconditions":["Application serves files associated with users or tenants."],"input":"Authenticated file access request.","output":"File returned only when authorization succeeds.","expected_behavior":"Changing file identifiers or paths cannot access another user's file.","failure_mode":"Attacker retrieves unauthorized stored objects by guessing identifiers.","root_cause":"File serving relies on storage path secrecy rather than authorization.","solution":"Use explicit authorization and server-side object mapping.","edge_cases":["Public/private transitions","Deleted files","Shared resources","Bulk downloads"],"security_implications":"Unauthorized file access can expose sensitive data.","performance_implications":"Authorization adds lookup overhead; indexed metadata can keep it bounded.","testing":{"method":"Attempt cross-user and cross-tenant file access using modified identifiers.","expected_result":"Unauthorized files are denied."},"verification":{"claim":"File retrieval is governed by resource authorization rather than identifier secrecy.","evidence_required":["Authorization code","Storage mapping","Negative access tests"],"method":"Trace request-to-storage mapping and test identifier manipulation.","limitations":"External object-store policies must also be reviewed."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application/storage platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","CWE-639"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B120","D40-B238","D40-B199"]} {"record_id":"D40-B241","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Application Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure default configuration remediation","question":"How should insecure application defaults be remediated?","answer":"Define secure defaults, make security-sensitive configuration explicit, remove development-only behavior from production profiles, and validate effective configuration at startup or deployment time.","objective":"Prevent deployments from becoming insecure because optional configuration was omitted.","requirements":["Maintain a documented secure baseline.","Separate development and production configuration.","Fail startup or deployment when mandatory security controls are missing."],"constraints":["Some development workflows need relaxed settings.","Configuration validation must distinguish optional from mandatory controls."],"preconditions":["Application behavior changes based on configuration."],"input":"Effective application configuration.","output":"Configuration satisfying the security baseline.","expected_behavior":"Missing security-critical configuration causes safe rejection rather than insecure fallback.","failure_mode":"Production uses debug, permissive CORS, weak authentication, or plaintext transport because defaults are insecure.","root_cause":"Security controls depend on implicit or development-oriented defaults.","solution":"Secure defaults, configuration schemas, environment-specific policy, and startup validation.","edge_cases":["Container environment variables","Feature flags","Blue-green deployments","Emergency configuration changes"],"security_implications":"Secure defaults reduce configuration-induced vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Startup validation has negligible runtime cost.","testing":{"method":"Start application with omitted and malformed security configuration.","expected_result":"Unsafe configurations are rejected or safe defaults are applied."},"verification":{"claim":"Effective production configuration satisfies mandatory security invariants.","evidence_required":["Configuration schema","Startup validation","Production configuration"],"method":"Test missing, unsafe, and valid configurations.","limitations":"External infrastructure settings must also be validated."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework/deployment dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secure Configuration guidance","CIS Benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B242","D40-B243"]} {"record_id":"D40-B242","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Application Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Production debug-feature remediation","question":"How should production debug endpoints or development features be remediated?","answer":"Remove or disable development diagnostics and debug endpoints from production builds or require explicit privileged access with strong controls when operational diagnostics are genuinely necessary.","objective":"Prevent diagnostic interfaces from exposing sensitive state or privileged capabilities.","requirements":["Disable unnecessary debug endpoints.","Separate development tooling from production artifacts.","Protect required diagnostics with strong authorization and network restrictions."],"constraints":["Production troubleshooting may require controlled diagnostics.","Some frameworks enable diagnostics automatically in development mode."],"preconditions":["Application exposes debugging or development functionality."],"input":"Production request to diagnostic functionality.","output":"Diagnostic access denied or tightly controlled.","expected_behavior":"Unauthenticated or normal users cannot invoke development diagnostics.","failure_mode":"Debug endpoint reveals configuration, stack traces, secrets, or execution controls.","root_cause":"Development functionality remains exposed in production.","solution":"Remove debug features, enforce environment-specific configuration, and protect necessary diagnostics.","edge_cases":["Health/debug endpoints","Admin consoles","Interactive shells","Error pages"],"security_implications":"Debug exposure can disclose secrets and enable privileged actions.","performance_implications":"Removing debug instrumentation may reduce overhead.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate application routes and test known debug paths under production configuration.","expected_result":"Development-only functionality is unavailable or securely restricted."},"verification":{"claim":"Production deployment contains no unintentionally exposed debug functionality.","evidence_required":["Route inventory","Build configuration","Negative tests"],"method":"Perform route discovery and configuration review.","limitations":"Hidden framework endpoints require framework-specific enumeration."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework/version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Security Misconfiguration guidance","CWE-489"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B241"]} {"record_id":"D40-B243","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Application Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Administrative endpoint exposure remediation","question":"How should publicly reachable administrative endpoints be remediated?","answer":"Restrict administrative endpoints by identity and network policy, remove unused endpoints, require strong authentication and authorization, and avoid relying solely on obscure paths or nonstandard ports for protection.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized access to privileged administrative functionality.","requirements":["Authenticate and authorize administrative requests.","Restrict administrative network exposure where feasible.","Remove unused administrative interfaces.","Audit administrative operations."],"constraints":["Remote administration may require controlled public access.","Zero-trust architectures may not permit simple network-based trust."],"preconditions":["Application exposes management functionality.","Endpoint is reachable from untrusted networks."],"input":"Administrative request and identity.","output":"Administrative operation only when authorized.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized users cannot access management functionality.","failure_mode":"Attacker discovers an administrative endpoint and gains privileged control.","root_cause":"Management interface is protected by obscurity or weak authentication.","solution":"Use strong identity controls, least privilege, network restrictions, and audited privileged operations.","edge_cases":["API gateways","Cloud control planes","Break-glass accounts","Service automation"],"security_implications":"Administrative compromise can result in full application or infrastructure takeover.","performance_implications":"Administrative authentication has negligible impact on normal application traffic.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate administrative routes and test access using unauthorized identities and networks.","expected_result":"All unauthorized access attempts fail."},"verification":{"claim":"Administrative functionality is protected by explicit authorization controls.","evidence_required":["Endpoint inventory","Authorization policy","Negative access tests"],"method":"Review route exposure and execute unauthorized-access testing.","limitations":"Infrastructure-level administration requires separate controls."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework/infrastructure dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Security Misconfiguration guidance","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B120","D40-B242"]} {"record_id":"D40-B244","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Application Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"CORS policy remediation","question":"How should an overly permissive CORS configuration be remediated?","answer":"Define explicit trusted origins, methods, headers, and credential behavior required by the application. Avoid wildcard origins when credentials or sensitive responses are involved.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized browser origins from reading cross-origin responses.","requirements":["Maintain an explicit origin policy.","Restrict methods and headers.","Configure credential behavior consistently.","Avoid reflecting arbitrary Origin values."],"constraints":["Multi-origin applications need dynamic but controlled origin policies.","CORS is a browser read-control mechanism and does not replace server authorization."],"preconditions":["Application serves cross-origin browser requests."],"input":"Browser Origin and requested CORS operation.","output":"CORS response matching an authorized origin policy.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized origins cannot obtain protected cross-origin responses through browser CORS.","failure_mode":"Server reflects attacker-controlled origins or allows credentials broadly.","root_cause":"CORS policy treats arbitrary origins as trusted.","solution":"Use explicit origin allowlists and independent server-side authorization.","edge_cases":["Credentials","Preflight caching","Subdomains","Null origin","Development origins"],"security_implications":"Misconfigured CORS can expose sensitive responses to hostile origins.","performance_implications":"Preflight requests can add latency; careful policy design can reduce unnecessary preflights.","testing":{"method":"Test trusted, untrusted, credentialed, and preflight requests from representative origins.","expected_result":"Only authorized origins receive permitted cross-origin access.","limitations":"CORS behavior is browser and cache dependent."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Web framework/browser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP CORS guidance","W3C Fetch specification"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B136","D40-B245"]} {"record_id":"D40-B245","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Application Configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security configuration drift remediation","question":"How should security configuration drift be remediated?","answer":"Represent security-sensitive configuration as version-controlled policy, validate effective configuration continuously, detect unauthorized changes, and maintain a tested baseline with documented exceptions.","objective":"Prevent gradual operational changes from weakening application security controls.","requirements":["Version security configuration.","Define expected baseline.","Detect drift.","Review and expire exceptions."],"constraints":["Some managed services expose configuration through external control planes.","Dynamic autoscaling can create many configuration instances."],"preconditions":["Security posture depends on mutable infrastructure or application configuration."],"input":"Observed configuration and approved baseline.","output":"Drift status and remediation action.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized security configuration changes are detected and corrected or escalated.","failure_mode":"Manual changes silently disable or weaken a security control.","root_cause":"Configuration lacks authoritative baseline and continuous validation.","solution":"Use infrastructure-as-code or equivalent policy management, drift detection, and controlled exceptions.","edge_cases":["Emergency changes","Provider defaults","Version upgrades","Multi-region deployments"],"security_implications":"Configuration drift can reintroduce previously remediated vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Continuous checks consume monitoring resources but generally avoid application runtime overhead.","testing":{"method":"Introduce controlled configuration drift and verify detection and remediation workflow.","expected_result":"Drift is detected and classified according to policy.","limitations":"Detection latency depends on monitoring frequency."},"verification":{"claim":"Security-critical configuration is continuously compared against an authoritative baseline.","evidence_required":["Baseline","Drift reports","Change records"],"method":"Review configuration-management process and run controlled drift tests.","limitations":"External provider settings may require separate evidence."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure/application configuration dependent.","provenance":["CIS Benchmarks","NIST configuration management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B241","D40-B242","D40-B250"]} {"record_id":"D40-B246","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Operational Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security alert fatigue remediation","question":"How should excessive low-value security alerts be remediated without suppressing important signals?","answer":"Classify alerts by evidence and impact, deduplicate repeated events, correlate related observations, tune thresholds using validated outcomes, and preserve raw evidence so suppression does not destroy investigative visibility.","objective":"Reduce alert noise while retaining high-value security evidence.","requirements":["Separate detection from alert presentation.","Deduplicate repeated events.","Track tuning changes and outcomes.","Retain underlying evidence."],"constraints":["Aggressive suppression can hide true attacks.","Threshold tuning should be validated against representative data."],"preconditions":["Security monitoring produces repeated or low-value alerts."],"input":"Security events and alert rules.","output":"Prioritized, correlated alert stream with retained raw evidence.","expected_behavior":"Repeated low-value events are consolidated without losing critical evidence.","failure_mode":"Operators suppress broad alert classes and miss meaningful attacks.","root_cause":"Alerting lacks correlation, deduplication, and outcome-based tuning.","solution":"Use evidence-preserving aggregation and measured rule tuning.","edge_cases":["Burst attacks","Novel threats","Multi-stage incidents","Benign high-volume events"],"security_implications":"Poor tuning can either overwhelm responders or hide malicious activity.","performance_implications":"Correlation requires additional compute and storage but can reduce downstream analyst workload.","testing":{"method":"Replay representative benign and malicious event sets before and after tuning.","expected_result":"Noise decreases while required detection coverage remains within defined objectives.","limitations":"Detection quality depends on representative validation data."},"verification":{"claim":"Alert tuning changes preserve documented security detection objectives.","evidence_required":["Before/after event sets","Rule changes","Outcome measurements"],"method":"Compare detection and false-positive behavior using controlled datasets.","limitations":"No finite dataset proves complete threat coverage."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Detection platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST incident detection guidance","MITRE ATT&CK operational principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B247","D40-B248"]} {"record_id":"D40-B247","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Operational Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security incident evidence preservation","question":"How should an application remediate incident-handling processes that risk destroying evidence?","answer":"Preserve relevant logs and artifacts before destructive remediation where operationally safe, maintain timestamps and provenance, restrict evidence access, and document collection and transformation steps.","objective":"Maintain reliable evidence while containing an active security incident.","requirements":["Identify evidence sources before cleanup.","Preserve original records or integrity-protected copies.","Record collection time and provenance.","Restrict evidence access."],"constraints":["Immediate containment may require destructive actions.","Privacy and retention requirements constrain evidence collection."],"preconditions":["Security incidents require investigation after containment.","Operational actions may modify affected systems."],"input":"Security event and potentially affected system state.","output":"Preserved evidence with documented provenance.","expected_behavior":"Remediation does not unnecessarily destroy information required to understand the incident.","failure_mode":"Logs or volatile artifacts are deleted before investigation.","root_cause":"Incident response prioritizes cleanup without evidence-preservation planning.","solution":"Use evidence-preservation procedures integrated with containment and recovery workflows.","edge_cases":["Volatile memory","Ephemeral containers","Log rotation","Cloud-managed logs"],"security_implications":"Evidence loss can prevent root-cause analysis and detection improvement.","performance_implications":"Evidence duplication and retention consume storage and collection bandwidth.","testing":{"method":"Run incident-response exercises with evidence-preservation checkpoints.","expected_result":"Required evidence remains available and traceable after simulated remediation."},"verification":{"claim":"Incident remediation preserves defined evidence sources under documented conditions.","evidence_required":["Response procedure","Exercise results","Evidence integrity records"],"method":"Conduct tabletop or technical response exercises.","limitations":"Evidence availability depends on platform retention and volatility."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Operational/platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST incident response guidance","NIST forensic guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B205","D40-B248","D40-B249"]} {"record_id":"D40-B248","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Operational Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security remediation rollback safety","question":"How should security remediations that can affect availability be made reversible?","answer":"Use staged deployment, validated rollback procedures, configuration/version checkpoints, and explicit stop conditions. Rollback should restore a known secure state rather than simply returning to an unverified vulnerable version.","objective":"Prevent security remediation failures from causing uncontrolled outages or restoring insecure state.","requirements":["Define pre-change and post-change security invariants.","Test rollback paths.","Preserve versioned artifacts/configuration.","Require approval for high-impact changes."],"constraints":["Some security changes cannot safely be rolled back, especially after credential compromise.","Database schema changes may be irreversible."],"preconditions":["Security remediation changes production code, infrastructure, or security policy."],"input":"Security change and rollback plan.","output":"Controlled deployment with validated recovery path.","expected_behavior":"Failed remediation can be contained without unnecessarily reintroducing the vulnerability.","failure_mode":"Emergency rollback restores the original insecure configuration.","root_cause":"Rollback is treated as a technical reversal rather than a security state transition.","solution":"Define secure rollback targets and validate them before deployment.","edge_cases":["Credential rotation","Schema changes","Network policy changes","Partial rollout"],"security_implications":"Safe rollback reduces availability risk while preserving security objectives.","performance_implications":"Staged validation increases deployment time but reduces operational risk.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled failure and rollback exercises.","expected_result":"System reaches a documented secure recovery state.","limitations":"Some changes require forward-only remediation."},"verification":{"claim":"Rollback procedures do not knowingly restore the remediated vulnerability.","evidence_required":["Rollback plan","Versioned artifacts","Failure exercise"],"method":"Execute controlled rollback and verify security invariants.","limitations":"External dependencies may constrain rollback."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Deployment architecture dependent.","provenance":["NIST incident response guidance","NIST configuration management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B200","D40-B245","D40-B249"]} {"record_id":"D40-B249","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Operational Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security remediation change validation","question":"How should security-sensitive production changes be validated before full rollout?","answer":"Validate the change against the original vulnerability, relevant regression tests, authorization/security invariants, dependency and configuration state, and representative operational scenarios before broad deployment.","objective":"Prevent incomplete security fixes from reaching all production instances.","requirements":["Retest the original failure condition.","Run security regression tests.","Validate configuration and dependency compatibility.","Use staged rollout where feasible."],"constraints":["Security fixes may need urgent deployment.","Some vulnerabilities require infrastructure changes not covered by application tests."],"preconditions":["A security change is ready for deployment."],"input":"Security remediation artifact and validation plan.","output":"Evidence-backed deployment decision.","expected_behavior":"Only changes satisfying defined security and operational criteria progress to broader rollout.","failure_mode":"Fix appears correct in isolated code but fails in production architecture or leaves a bypass.","root_cause":"Validation scope is narrower than the actual vulnerability boundary.","solution":"Use layered validation covering code, configuration, dependencies, infrastructure, and behavior.","edge_cases":["Feature flags","Partial deployment","Mixed versions","External integrations"],"security_implications":"Layered validation reduces incomplete or incompatible security fixes.","performance_implications":"Expanded validation increases CI/deployment duration.","testing":{"method":"Execute regression, integration, security, and deployment tests before rollout.","expected_result":"Original vulnerability remains blocked and required functionality remains operational."},"verification":{"claim":"Security remediation satisfies its defined security invariant in the target deployment context.","evidence_required":["Test results","Configuration evidence","Deployment validation","Security review"],"method":"Review evidence across all affected layers.","limitations":"Validation scope determines assurance; untested environments remain uncertain."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Project/deployment specific.","provenance":["OWASP Secure Development Lifecycle principles","NIST secure software development guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B200","D40-B248","D40-B250"]} {"record_id":"D40-B250","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Problem -> Remediation","subtopic":"Operational Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security remediation lifecycle closure","question":"How should an organization verify that a security problem is fully remediated rather than merely patched?","answer":"Close remediation only after the original failure is reproduced and blocked, related attack paths are assessed, regression coverage exists, affected versions/configurations are addressed, evidence is recorded, and residual limitations are explicitly documented.","objective":"Establish evidence-based closure for a security remediation.","requirements":["Reproduce the original security failure where safely possible.","Verify corrected behavior.","Assess related attack paths.","Add regression coverage.","Document affected versions, deployment scope, evidence, and residual risk."],"constraints":["Some vulnerabilities cannot be safely reproduced in production.","Third-party dependencies may require separate remediation evidence."],"preconditions":["A security issue has received an implementation or configuration fix."],"input":"Security finding, remediation, affected scope, and validation evidence.","output":"Structured remediation record with verified, unverified, or partially verified status.","expected_behavior":"Remediation status reflects actual evidence rather than assuming a code change equals security closure.","failure_mode":"Issue is marked fixed because code changed, despite missing deployment, regression, or bypass validation.","root_cause":"Closure criteria do not distinguish implementation from verification.","solution":"Use a lifecycle of reproduce -> remediate -> regress -> verify -> deploy -> reverify -> document limitations.","edge_cases":["Multiple affected versions","Configuration-only fixes","Compensating controls","Third-party fixes","Incomplete test environments"],"security_implications":"Evidence-based closure reduces false confidence and prevents recurring vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Comprehensive verification increases engineering effort but creates reusable regression evidence.","testing":{"method":"Run original exploit regression, bypass-oriented tests, deployment validation, and affected-version checks.","expected_result":"Original condition remains blocked within the verified scope and all known remediation criteria are satisfied.","limitations":"Verification cannot establish absence of unknown attack paths."},"verification":{"claim":"Security remediation is closed only to the extent supported by documented evidence.","evidence_required":["Original failure reproduction","Corrected implementation/configuration","Regression results","Deployment evidence","Scope and limitation record"],"method":"Independent review of remediation evidence against closure criteria.","limitations":"Unverified environments, unknown attack paths, and future changes remain outside the current evidence boundary."},"languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Dataset/process level; implementation details remain language and deployment dependent.","provenance":["NIST Secure Software Development Framework","OWASP Secure Development Lifecycle principles","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B200","D40-B245","D40-B248","D40-B249"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B251","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"TLS and transport security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"TLS certificate validation bypass","question":"How should an application remediate TLS connections that accept invalid certificates?","answer":"Use the platform or language standard TLS stack with certificate-chain validation enabled, verify the certificate hostname against the intended endpoint, and fail closed when validation fails. Do not replace validation with a trust-all callback.","objective":"Prevent man-in-the-middle attacks caused by disabled or incomplete certificate validation.","requirements":["Validate the certificate chain against an appropriate trust store.","Perform hostname verification.","Reject expired, not-yet-valid, revoked where supported, or otherwise unacceptable certificates according to the trust policy.","Fail closed on validation errors."],"constraints":["Trust-store behavior varies by runtime and platform.","Private PKI requires deliberate trust-anchor configuration rather than global validation bypasses."],"preconditions":["The application establishes a TLS connection to a security-sensitive endpoint."],"input":"TLS connection to an endpoint presenting a certificate.","output":"Connection succeeds only when the certificate satisfies the configured trust and identity policy.","expected_behavior":"Invalid certificate or hostname mismatch causes connection failure before sensitive data is exchanged.","failure_mode":"The client accepts an attacker-controlled certificate because verification was disabled or weakened.","root_cause":"Custom TLS configuration overrides normal certificate and hostname verification.","solution":"Remove trust-all behavior, use the default verified TLS client configuration, configure only required trust anchors, and test rejection of invalid certificates and hostnames.","edge_cases":["Private certificate authorities.","Certificate rotation.","Multiple legitimate hostnames.","Intermediate certificate changes."],"security_implications":"Certificate validation is a primary TLS trust boundary; bypassing it can expose credentials, tokens, and application data to interception.","performance_implications":"Certificate validation adds connection-establishment work but is normally negligible relative to the security benefit.","testing":{"method":"Integration test with a trusted certificate, expired certificate, untrusted issuer, and hostname mismatch.","expected_result":"Only certificates satisfying the configured trust policy are accepted."},"verification":"Inspect effective TLS configuration and verify negative tests demonstrate rejection of invalid certificates.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C#","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Applicable to TLS-capable runtimes; exact APIs depend on language and runtime version.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","RFC 5280","RFC 6125"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B201","D40-B202"]} {"record_id":"D40-B252","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"TLS and transport security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure TLS protocol and cipher configuration","question":"How should an application remediate obsolete TLS versions or weak cryptographic configuration?","answer":"Configure the supported TLS implementation to use current protocol versions and an appropriate cipher policy, while disabling obsolete protocols and algorithms that the security policy does not permit.","objective":"Reduce transport compromise risk from obsolete or weak cryptographic protocols and algorithms.","requirements":["Define an explicit minimum supported TLS version appropriate to the deployment.","Disable obsolete protocol versions and weak algorithms where configurable.","Use a maintained TLS implementation.","Validate the effective negotiated protocol and cipher during testing."],"constraints":["Legacy clients may require compatibility exceptions.","Supported algorithms depend on the TLS implementation and deployment environment."],"preconditions":["The application exposes or consumes a TLS-protected service."],"input":"TLS endpoint configuration and client/server negotiation.","output":"Connections negotiate only protocols and cryptographic suites allowed by the security policy.","expected_behavior":"Unsupported or prohibited protocol negotiation fails rather than silently downgrading to an insecure configuration.","failure_mode":"An endpoint permits obsolete protocol versions or weak algorithms.","root_cause":"Legacy defaults, copied configuration, or compatibility settings were retained without security review.","solution":"Set a documented minimum TLS version, remove prohibited algorithms, validate effective configuration, and isolate unavoidable legacy compatibility paths.","edge_cases":["Legacy embedded clients.","Load balancers terminating TLS.","Service meshes performing TLS termination.","Configuration drift across environments."],"security_implications":"Weak transport configuration can enable downgrade, cryptographic, or protocol-level attacks and can undermine otherwise secure application authentication.","performance_implications":"Modern TLS configurations can change CPU cost or interoperability but normally remain operationally acceptable; measure before making performance claims.","testing":{"method":"Configuration and integration tests using an approved protocol/cipher matrix plus negative negotiation tests.","expected_result":"Only approved TLS configurations negotiate successfully."},"verification":"Inspect effective endpoint configuration and capture negotiated protocol/cipher during controlled tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Version-sensitive; use the maintained TLS implementation and current organizational cryptographic policy.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-52 Rev. 2","RFC 8446"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B251","D40-B253"]} {"record_id":"D40-B253","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"TLS and transport security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Plaintext transport fallback","question":"How should software remediate a design that silently falls back from TLS to plaintext communication?","answer":"Remove silent plaintext fallback for security-sensitive communication. Require the protected transport, fail closed when it cannot be established, and make any explicitly permitted downgrade a separate documented trust policy rather than an automatic recovery path.","objective":"Prevent attackers from forcing sensitive communication onto an unprotected transport.","requirements":["Require TLS for security-sensitive endpoints.","Do not automatically retry sensitive requests over plaintext after TLS failure.","Treat transport downgrade as an explicit policy decision.","Test failure behavior."],"constraints":["Some legacy protocols may technically support both modes.","Migration systems may need a separately controlled compatibility path."],"preconditions":["The application communicates over a network where transport confidentiality or integrity is required."],"input":"Connection failure or TLS negotiation failure.","output":"The application refuses the insecure fallback or follows an explicitly authorized migration policy.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive data is never sent over plaintext merely because TLS establishment failed.","failure_mode":"A man-in-the-middle attacker causes TLS failure and the client retries using plaintext.","root_cause":"Availability-oriented retry logic treats transport security as optional.","solution":"Make secure transport mandatory, remove insecure retry paths, and return a controlled error when secure establishment fails.","edge_cases":["Offline mode.","Explicit migration windows.","Internal networks incorrectly assumed to be trusted.","Proxies or gateways terminating TLS."],"security_implications":"Silent downgrade can expose credentials, session tokens, business data, and integrity-sensitive requests.","performance_implications":"Failing instead of retrying can reduce availability during TLS outages but avoids unsafe communication.","testing":{"method":"Force TLS negotiation failure and observe whether any plaintext request is emitted.","expected_result":"No sensitive request is transmitted through an insecure fallback."},"verification":"Trace controlled failure scenarios and inspect network traffic for plaintext transmission.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Architecture-level remediation; exact transport APIs are runtime-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-52 Rev. 2"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B251","D40-B252"]} {"record_id":"D40-B254","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"TLS and transport security","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","concept":"Certificate pinning remediation and lifecycle risk","question":"When is certificate pinning appropriate, and how should unsafe or brittle pinning be remediated?","answer":"Use pinning only when its threat model and operational lifecycle justify the additional complexity. Prefer platform-supported trust mechanisms and carefully managed pin sets; avoid hard-coded single certificates that can cause outages during legitimate certificate rotation.","objective":"Balance additional trust constraints against certificate-rotation and recovery risks.","requirements":["Define the threat model before introducing pinning.","Maintain a controlled pin rotation strategy if pinning is required.","Provide an emergency recovery mechanism that does not become a permanent trust bypass.","Test certificate rotation."],"constraints":["Pinning can break connectivity when certificates or legitimate trust chains change.","The correct mechanism differs between mobile, desktop, server, and managed environments."],"preconditions":["The application has a documented need for trust constraints beyond normal certificate validation."],"input":"Certificate or public-key rotation event.","output":"The client continues to accept only policy-approved identities without requiring unsafe validation bypasses.","expected_behavior":"Legitimate rotation succeeds through a planned overlap or updated pin policy.","failure_mode":"A pinned certificate expires or rotates and clients either fail closed indefinitely or disable verification to recover.","root_cause":"Pin lifecycle was not designed alongside certificate lifecycle management.","solution":"Use overlapping pins or an equivalent controlled rotation strategy where pinning is justified, automate policy updates where possible, and test emergency recovery.","edge_cases":["Compromised pinned key.","Multiple service certificates.","Application versions with stale pins.","Offline clients."],"security_implications":"Poor pinning can create pressure for emergency trust bypasses; overly broad pinning can also defeat its intended security benefit.","performance_implications":"Pin evaluation is generally small compared with network and TLS costs, but operational complexity is the major tradeoff.","testing":{"method":"Simulate certificate rotation, stale-client behavior, invalid certificates, and emergency recovery.","expected_result":"Approved rotation succeeds while unauthorized certificates remain rejected."},"verification":"Review the pin lifecycle, rotation procedure, and negative tests before deployment.","languages":["Java","Kotlin","Swift","Objective-C","Python","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Platform-specific; pinning APIs and recommended practices vary by client platform.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","RFC 7469 historical context"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B251","D40-B255"]} {"record_id":"D40-B255","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"TLS and mutual authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"mTLS identity used without authorization binding","question":"How should a service remediate a design that authenticates a client certificate but fails to authorize the authenticated identity?","answer":"Treat successful client-certificate validation as authentication, then map the certificate identity to an explicit application authorization policy. Do not assume that possession of any trusted client certificate grants unrestricted access.","objective":"Prevent authorization bypass caused by conflating TLS client authentication with application authorization.","requirements":["Validate the client certificate chain.","Extract an identity according to a documented certificate identity policy.","Map that identity to application roles, tenants, or permissions.","Enforce authorization for each protected operation."],"constraints":["Certificate subject and SAN formats vary by PKI.","Certificate issuance authority and application authorization policy must remain coordinated."],"preconditions":["The service uses mutual TLS for client authentication."],"input":"Authenticated client certificate and requested resource/action.","output":"The request is authorized only when the authenticated identity has the required permission.","expected_behavior":"A valid but unauthorized client certificate cannot access restricted operations.","failure_mode":"Any certificate signed by a trusted CA is treated as an administrator or universally trusted application identity.","root_cause":"Authentication success is incorrectly treated as sufficient authorization.","solution":"Separate authentication from authorization and implement explicit identity-to-permission mapping with auditable policy.","edge_cases":["Certificate renewal with changed identifiers.","Multiple CAs.","Tenant-specific identities.","Revoked or expired certificates."],"security_implications":"A trusted certificate without granular authorization can become a high-privilege credential and enlarge the blast radius of certificate compromise.","performance_implications":"Authorization lookups may add small request overhead; cache only where invalidation and revocation semantics remain correct.","testing":{"method":"Test valid authorized certificate, valid unauthorized certificate, expired certificate, and revoked certificate where supported.","expected_result":"Only authorized identities can perform the corresponding operations."},"verification":"Review the identity mapping and execute authorization-negative tests.","languages":["Python","Java","TypeScript","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Applicable to mTLS-capable server implementations; exact certificate APIs are runtime-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet","RFC 5280","RFC 8705"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B251","D40-B256"]} {"record_id":"D40-B256","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted server-side URL fetching","question":"How should software remediate an SSRF-prone feature that fetches arbitrary user-supplied URLs?","answer":"Restrict outbound requests using an allowlist of approved schemes, destinations, ports, and protocols; resolve and validate destinations according to the trust policy; disable unnecessary redirects; and enforce network egress controls as defense in depth.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from turning the application into an unintended network client.","requirements":["Allow only required URL schemes.","Apply destination allowlisting where feasible.","Restrict ports and protocols.","Enforce connection and response limits.","Use network egress controls."],"constraints":["Legitimate integrations may require dynamic destinations.","DNS resolution and redirect behavior can change the effective destination."],"preconditions":["The server retrieves network resources based on externally influenced input."],"input":"User-controlled or indirectly controlled URL.","output":"The server fetches only resources permitted by the outbound request policy.","expected_behavior":"Requests to unauthorized schemes, destinations, ports, or network zones are rejected.","failure_mode":"An attacker causes the server to access internal services or unintended external resources.","root_cause":"Outbound network capability is exposed without a sufficiently restrictive trust policy.","solution":"Implement URL parsing and policy enforcement before connection, constrain redirects, and enforce network-level egress restrictions.","edge_cases":["IPv4 and IPv6 representations.","DNS names resolving to private addresses.","Redirects to disallowed destinations.","URL parser discrepancies.","Non-HTTP schemes."],"security_implications":"SSRF can expose internal services, credentials, metadata endpoints, and administrative interfaces.","performance_implications":"Destination validation and network policy checks add limited overhead; strict timeouts reduce resource consumption.","testing":{"method":"Test approved external destination, private address, loopback, link-local address, disallowed scheme, restricted port, and redirect to a blocked destination.","expected_result":"Only policy-approved destinations are reachable."},"verification":"Combine application-level negative tests with network-layer egress validation.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C#"],"version_scope":"Architecture-level; URL parsing and networking APIs are language/runtime-specific.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B201","D40-B257"]} {"record_id":"D40-B257","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"DNS rebinding and time-of-check/time-of-use in SSRF validation","question":"How should SSRF remediation handle a hostname whose resolved address can change between validation and connection?","answer":"Avoid treating one DNS lookup as permanent proof of destination identity. Where the threat model requires it, validate the effective destination at connection time or use a controlled outbound proxy that resolves and enforces the destination policy atomically.","objective":"Prevent validation results from becoming stale before the network connection is established.","requirements":["Account for DNS changes between policy evaluation and connection.","Restrict destination classes at the network boundary.","Prevent untrusted redirects from escaping the same policy.","Use a controlled resolver or egress proxy where appropriate."],"constraints":["Exact atomicity depends on the networking architecture.","DNS behavior may differ across runtimes, proxies, and operating systems."],"preconditions":["SSRF filtering relies on hostname-to-address validation."],"input":"Hostname controlled or influenced by an attacker.","output":"The actual connected destination remains within the allowed trust boundary.","expected_behavior":"A hostname that resolves to an unauthorized address at connection time cannot bypass the destination policy.","failure_mode":"The application validates a benign address and later connects to an attacker-selected private or sensitive address.","root_cause":"Destination validation and network connection are separate operations with mutable DNS state.","solution":"Move enforcement into a trusted egress layer or otherwise ensure destination policy is applied to the effective connection target.","edge_cases":["Multiple DNS records.","IPv4/IPv6 differences.","Short DNS TTLs.","Redirect chains.","Connection pooling."],"security_implications":"DNS rebinding can undermine otherwise correct hostname allowlisting and expose internal network resources.","performance_implications":"Proxy-based or connection-time enforcement may add network hops and DNS overhead.","testing":{"method":"Controlled DNS test that changes resolution between validation and connection.","expected_result":"The connection is blocked when the effective destination violates policy."},"verification":"Inspect connection-level enforcement rather than relying only on pre-resolution unit tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Networking behavior is implementation and deployment dependent.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918","CWE-367"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B258"]} {"record_id":"D40-B258","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cloud instance metadata exposure through SSRF","question":"How should applications reduce SSRF access to cloud instance metadata services?","answer":"Block unnecessary metadata endpoints at the application and network layers, use the cloud provider's hardened metadata configuration where available, avoid exposing metadata credentials to untrusted workloads, and enforce least-privilege workload identities.","objective":"Prevent SSRF from being used to obtain cloud instance or workload credentials.","requirements":["Restrict access to metadata services according to provider guidance.","Use least-privilege workload identities.","Apply network egress restrictions.","Do not treat metadata endpoints as ordinary application destinations."],"constraints":["Metadata mechanisms differ between cloud providers and service types.","Some legitimate infrastructure components may require metadata access."],"preconditions":["The workload runs in an environment with a metadata service or equivalent identity endpoint."],"input":"Server-side request attempting to reach metadata infrastructure.","output":"Unauthorized metadata access is blocked and workload credentials remain protected.","expected_behavior":"Application-originated requests cannot freely access sensitive metadata endpoints.","failure_mode":"An SSRF primitive retrieves temporary credentials or sensitive instance metadata.","root_cause":"Metadata service is reachable from a vulnerable application path without sufficient isolation.","solution":"Harden metadata access, restrict egress, isolate workload identity, and remediate the underlying SSRF primitive.","edge_cases":["Containerized workloads.","Host-network workloads.","Multiple metadata endpoints.","Legitimate metadata-dependent SDKs."],"security_implications":"Metadata compromise can convert a web-layer SSRF vulnerability into cloud credential compromise and broader resource access.","performance_implications":"Metadata access controls may affect infrastructure SDK behavior; validate legitimate dependencies before enforcement.","testing":{"method":"Attempt metadata access from the application under controlled conditions and verify provider-recommended protections.","expected_result":"Unauthorized application paths cannot retrieve sensitive metadata or credentials."},"verification":"Review cloud metadata configuration, workload identity permissions, and application egress controls.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider-specific; verify current provider metadata security requirements.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-204","Cloud-provider official metadata security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B257"]} {"record_id":"D40-B259","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe redirect following in outbound requests","question":"How should SSRF-prone HTTP clients remediate automatic redirects?","answer":"Treat every redirect destination as a new outbound security decision. Disable automatic redirects when they are unnecessary or reapply the complete destination policy to every redirect before following it.","objective":"Prevent an initially permitted URL from redirecting the server into a prohibited network location.","requirements":["Limit redirect count.","Validate each redirect target.","Preserve scheme, port, destination, and network policy across redirects.","Reject redirects to prohibited destinations."],"constraints":["Some legitimate integrations rely on redirects.","Redirect semantics vary across HTTP clients."],"preconditions":["The application follows redirects while fetching externally influenced URLs."],"input":"Initial URL and redirect chain.","output":"Only redirect destinations satisfying the outbound policy are followed.","expected_behavior":"A redirect to an unauthorized destination terminates the request.","failure_mode":"The initial destination is allowed but a redirect reaches an internal or sensitive service.","root_cause":"Security validation is applied only to the initial URL.","solution":"Disable automatic redirects or enforce the same policy for every redirect response.","edge_cases":["HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrades.","Cross-origin redirects.","Credential forwarding across redirects.","Redirect loops."],"security_implications":"Unsafe redirect handling can bypass SSRF allowlists and can also leak authorization headers or other request metadata.","performance_implications":"Redirect validation and limits reduce unnecessary network requests and can improve resource predictability.","testing":{"method":"Create controlled redirect chains to approved and prohibited destinations.","expected_result":"Only policy-approved redirect targets are followed."},"verification":"Capture each outbound request and confirm policy evaluation occurs for every destination.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP-client-specific; verify redirect behavior in the deployed library version.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","OWASP HTTP client security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B257"]} {"record_id":"D40-B260","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"SSRF","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient outbound request resource limits","question":"How should SSRF-capable fetchers remediate unbounded request size, response size, and connection duration?","answer":"Apply explicit connection, read, redirect, response-size, and concurrency limits to outbound requests, and terminate work when limits are exceeded.","objective":"Reduce security and availability impact from malicious or unexpectedly large outbound resources.","requirements":["Set connection and read timeouts.","Limit response size.","Limit redirects.","Bound concurrent outbound requests.","Abort work when resource limits are exceeded."],"constraints":["Appropriate limits depend on legitimate workload requirements.","Streaming responses require enforcement while data is consumed."],"preconditions":["The application performs server-side network fetching."],"input":"Outbound request and response stream.","output":"Resource usage remains within defined security and operational limits.","expected_behavior":"Oversized, slow, or excessive requests are terminated safely.","failure_mode":"An attacker consumes server resources using slow, large, or highly concurrent outbound requests.","root_cause":"Network fetch functionality lacks resource governance.","solution":"Introduce bounded timeouts, response limits, concurrency controls, and cancellation handling.","edge_cases":["Chunked responses.","Slowloris-style upstream behavior.","Large but legitimate downloads.","Partial reads.","Connection pool exhaustion."],"security_implications":"Unbounded outbound fetching can amplify SSRF into denial-of-service and resource-exhaustion conditions.","performance_implications":"Limits improve resource predictability and protect connection pools, threads, memory, and bandwidth.","testing":{"method":"Exercise slow responses, oversized responses, concurrent fetches, and cancellation.","expected_result":"Requests terminate within configured resource limits without leaking resources."},"verification":"Measure connection, memory, concurrency, and cancellation behavior under controlled load.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP client and runtime specific.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B259"]} {"record_id":"D40-B261","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP protocol abuse","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"HTTP request smuggling remediation","question":"How should an HTTP service remediate request smuggling or HTTP desynchronization risk?","answer":"Use consistent HTTP parsing and framing rules across proxies and origin servers, keep intermediary and backend components supported and patched, reject ambiguous message framing, and avoid configurations where different components interpret the same request differently.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled discrepancies between HTTP intermediaries and backend servers.","requirements":["Use compatible and maintained HTTP components.","Ensure consistent request framing interpretation.","Reject ambiguous or invalid framing.","Test proxy-to-origin behavior as a complete chain."],"constraints":["Risk depends heavily on the exact HTTP versions, proxy, server, and intermediary configuration.","HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 have different framing semantics from HTTP/1.1."],"preconditions":["Traffic passes through multiple HTTP parsing components."],"input":"Ambiguously framed or specially constructed HTTP request.","output":"All components either reject the request consistently or interpret it identically.","expected_behavior":"No intermediary/backend request boundary disagreement occurs.","failure_mode":"A proxy and backend disagree about request boundaries, allowing request desynchronization.","root_cause":"Parser differential or inconsistent message framing behavior across components.","solution":"Standardize components, patch vulnerable versions, reject ambiguous framing, and test the deployed proxy/backend chain.","edge_cases":["HTTP/1.1 connection reuse.","HTTP/2 translation gateways.","Multiple reverse proxies.","Connection pooling."],"security_implications":"Request smuggling can bypass security controls, poison request queues, or route attacker-controlled requests to unintended operations.","performance_implications":"Strict parsing can reject malformed traffic and reduce parser ambiguity without inherently imposing significant application overhead.","testing":{"method":"Use approved protocol-security tests against the complete intermediary chain rather than isolated backend tests.","expected_result":"Ambiguous framing is rejected or handled consistently across every hop."},"verification":"Validate deployed component versions, framing configuration, and end-to-end parser consistency.","languages":["Java","C","C++","Rust","Go","Python","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Highly implementation and HTTP-stack dependent.","provenance":["PortSwigger HTTP Request Smuggling research","RFC 9112","OWASP HTTP security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B262","D40-B263"]} {"record_id":"D40-B262","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP response security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"HTTP response splitting","question":"How should applications remediate response splitting caused by untrusted header values?","answer":"Never construct HTTP response headers directly from untrusted input without appropriate validation and API-level safeguards. Reject control characters and use structured response APIs that separate header names and values from raw protocol syntax.","objective":"Prevent attackers from injecting additional HTTP headers or response boundaries.","requirements":["Validate untrusted header values.","Reject CR and LF control characters where they are not valid.","Use framework APIs that correctly encode or reject unsafe values.","Avoid raw HTTP response construction."],"constraints":["Header value syntax differs by protocol and framework.","Some application data should not be exposed as headers at all."],"preconditions":["User-controlled data influences response headers or redirect locations."],"input":"Untrusted value inserted into an HTTP response header.","output":"Only syntactically valid and policy-approved header values are emitted.","expected_behavior":"Injected control characters cannot create additional headers or response messages.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled CR/LF data alters response structure.","root_cause":"Untrusted input is concatenated into raw header syntax.","solution":"Use structured response APIs and explicit validation appropriate to the header.","edge_cases":["Redirect headers.","Custom application headers.","Unicode normalization.","Proxy normalization differences."],"security_implications":"Response splitting can enable cache poisoning, header injection, security-policy manipulation, and related attacks.","performance_implications":"Header validation is low-cost compared with network transmission.","testing":{"method":"Submit header values containing control characters and boundary characters.","expected_result":"Unsafe input is rejected or safely represented without changing response structure."},"verification":"Inspect emitted raw responses in an integration test and confirm header boundaries remain correct.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C#"],"version_scope":"Framework/API dependent.","provenance":["CWE-113","OWASP HTTP Response Splitting guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B263"]} {"record_id":"D40-B263","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Host header security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Host header injection","question":"How should an application remediate security-sensitive logic that trusts an arbitrary HTTP Host header?","answer":"Do not use the Host header as an authoritative security identity unless it is validated against a trusted host policy. Configure canonical hosts at the proxy/application boundary and derive security-sensitive URLs from trusted configuration where possible.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled host values from influencing password-reset links, redirects, cache keys, routing, or other security-sensitive behavior.","requirements":["Define an approved host policy.","Validate incoming host values before security-sensitive use.","Prefer trusted deployment configuration for canonical URLs.","Ensure reverse proxies preserve and validate host information appropriately."],"constraints":["Multi-tenant applications may legitimately support dynamic hosts.","Proxy topology can alter which host header is visible to the application."],"preconditions":["Application logic uses request host information for security-sensitive behavior."],"input":"Incoming HTTP Host or equivalent authority value.","output":"Only policy-approved host identities influence application behavior.","expected_behavior":"Unrecognized host values are rejected or handled using a safe canonical configuration.","failure_mode":"An attacker supplies a malicious host and causes generated links, redirects, or cache behavior to target the attacker-controlled domain.","root_cause":"Transport metadata is treated as trusted application identity.","solution":"Validate hosts against configuration or tenant ownership data and use trusted canonical origins for security-sensitive URLs.","edge_cases":["Multi-tenant custom domains.","IPv6 literals.","Port normalization.","Reverse proxies.","Host/authority differences in HTTP/2."],"security_implications":"Host injection can support phishing, password-reset poisoning, cache poisoning, and routing attacks.","performance_implications":"Host validation is inexpensive; tenant-domain lookups may require caching with careful invalidation.","testing":{"method":"Send requests with approved, unknown, alternate-port, and attacker-controlled host values.","expected_result":"Only approved hosts influence security-sensitive application behavior."},"verification":"Trace generated URLs and routing decisions for controlled host values.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP framework and proxy dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance","CWE-644"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B264"]} {"record_id":"D40-B264","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Open redirects","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unvalidated redirect target","question":"How should applications remediate an open redirect caused by attacker-controlled redirect parameters?","answer":"Prefer server-controlled destination identifiers or relative paths. If external redirects are required, validate the destination against an explicit allowlist of schemes, hosts, and paths rather than accepting arbitrary URLs.","objective":"Prevent attackers from using a trusted application as a redirector to untrusted destinations.","requirements":["Prefer relative internal redirects.","Allowlist external destinations when required.","Reject unsupported schemes and malformed URLs.","Normalize and validate destinations consistently."],"constraints":["Legitimate integrations may require external redirects.","URL parsing must account for userinfo, encoded characters, alternate schemes, and normalization."],"preconditions":["A request parameter or state value influences a redirect location."],"input":"User-controlled redirect target.","output":"Redirect occurs only to an authorized destination.","expected_behavior":"Unapproved external destinations are rejected or replaced by a safe default.","failure_mode":"The application redirects users to an attacker-controlled domain.","root_cause":"Redirect destination is trusted without an explicit destination policy.","solution":"Use server-side destination identifiers or strict allowlisting with robust URL parsing.","edge_cases":["Scheme-relative URLs.","Userinfo components.","Encoded delimiters.","Internationalized domains.","Open redirects through nested URL parameters."],"security_implications":"Open redirects can facilitate phishing, token leakage in poorly designed flows, and trust abuse.","performance_implications":"Destination validation is normally negligible.","testing":{"method":"Test internal paths, approved external destinations, malicious schemes, alternate URL forms, and encoded bypass attempts.","expected_result":"Only approved destinations are followed."},"verification":"Inspect final Location headers and compare parsed destinations against the security policy.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework and URL-parser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet","CWE-601"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B262","D40-B265"]} {"record_id":"D40-B265","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"HTTP URL parsing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"URL parser discrepancy in security validation","question":"How should applications remediate security checks that parse URLs differently from the HTTP client?","answer":"Use one well-defined URL parsing and normalization model for policy decisions and connection behavior, or enforce policy at a trusted network layer. Avoid security decisions based on string-prefix checks or a parser whose interpretation differs from the component making the request.","objective":"Prevent parser discrepancies from bypassing destination, scheme, hostname, or port validation.","requirements":["Parse URLs structurally.","Normalize according to a documented policy.","Ensure validation semantics match the HTTP client or enforcement proxy.","Reject ambiguous representations."],"constraints":["URL standards contain legacy and compatibility behaviors.","Different libraries may normalize malformed or unusual URLs differently."],"preconditions":["A security control validates user-controlled URLs before network use."],"input":"Potentially ambiguous or specially encoded URL.","output":"The URL is either unambiguously authorized or rejected.","expected_behavior":"The policy decision matches the actual destination used by the network component.","failure_mode":"Validation approves one interpretation while the HTTP client connects according to another.","root_cause":"Independent parsers or ad hoc string validation produce inconsistent URL semantics.","solution":"Centralize parsing/policy or move enforcement to the outbound network boundary.","edge_cases":["Percent encoding.","Backslashes.","Unicode hostnames.","Embedded credentials.","IPv4 integer/hex representations."],"security_implications":"Parser discrepancies can bypass SSRF, redirect, authentication, and host-validation controls.","performance_implications":"Centralized parsing may reduce duplicate processing and policy inconsistencies.","testing":{"method":"Build a corpus of ambiguous URL forms and compare policy parsing with actual connection behavior.","expected_result":"No accepted URL produces an unauthorized destination interpretation."},"verification":"Perform differential tests between validation and network components.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Parser behavior is library and runtime version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","RFC 3986","CWE-20"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B264"]} {"record_id":"D40-B266","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Webhooks and integrations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsigned webhook acceptance","question":"How should a webhook receiver remediate acceptance of unauthenticated webhook payloads?","answer":"Require a cryptographically verifiable webhook signature or an equivalent authenticated channel, validate the signature over the exact request representation specified by the provider, and reject unverifiable requests before processing business actions.","objective":"Prevent attackers from forging webhook events that trigger privileged application behavior.","requirements":["Verify provider-defined signatures.","Use the exact signed payload representation.","Protect verification keys.","Reject missing or invalid signatures."],"constraints":["Providers use different signing schemes and canonicalization rules.","Key rotation must be supported."],"preconditions":["An external system sends requests that can trigger application state changes."],"input":"Webhook HTTP request and provider authentication material.","output":"Only authenticated webhook events reach business processing.","expected_behavior":"Forged or modified webhook payloads are rejected.","failure_mode":"An attacker submits a crafted event that the receiver processes as legitimate.","root_cause":"Webhook endpoint trusts source location or payload structure without cryptographic authentication.","solution":"Implement provider-specified signature verification and key rotation with fail-closed handling.","edge_cases":["Rotating signing keys.","Duplicate delivery.","Clock skew.","Raw-body preservation.","Multiple webhook providers."],"security_implications":"Forged webhooks can cause unauthorized payments, account changes, provisioning, or data modifications.","performance_implications":"Signature verification adds cryptographic CPU work proportional to request volume and payload size.","testing":{"method":"Test valid signature, modified body, missing signature, wrong key, expired timestamp where applicable, and rotated key.","expected_result":"Only valid authenticated events are processed."},"verification":"Capture the exact raw request and independently verify signature calculations.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C#"],"version_scope":"Provider and SDK version dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security guidance","Provider-specific official webhook security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B267","D40-B268"]} {"record_id":"D40-B267","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Webhooks and integrations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook replay attack remediation","question":"How should webhook receivers prevent replay of a previously valid event?","answer":"Use a provider-supported timestamp or freshness mechanism together with a unique event identifier and durable replay tracking. Reject requests outside the accepted freshness window and duplicate event identifiers according to the provider's delivery semantics.","objective":"Prevent captured valid webhook messages from being submitted repeatedly.","requirements":["Validate freshness when supported.","Track unique event identifiers.","Make duplicate handling durable across process restarts.","Keep the replay window appropriate to clock and delivery behavior."],"constraints":["Some providers guarantee event IDs while others require application-specific replay controls.","At-least-once delivery means legitimate duplicates may occur."],"preconditions":["Webhook requests can be intercepted, captured, or delivered multiple times."],"input":"Authenticated webhook containing event identifier and timestamp where available.","output":"Fresh unique events are processed and replayed events are rejected or safely deduplicated.","expected_behavior":"Repeated delivery does not repeat a security-sensitive side effect.","failure_mode":"A captured valid event is accepted multiple times.","root_cause":"Authentication is present but freshness and idempotency are absent.","solution":"Add replay-window validation and durable event deduplication, while making business processing idempotent.","edge_cases":["Delayed legitimate delivery.","Clock skew.","Provider retries.","Database failure during deduplication."],"security_implications":"Replay can duplicate payments, provisioning, state transitions, or other privileged actions.","performance_implications":"Replay storage requires bounded durable state and lookup operations.","testing":{"method":"Submit the same valid event repeatedly and vary its timestamp within and outside the accepted window.","expected_result":"Duplicates do not repeat the protected side effect."},"verification":"Verify replay records survive process restart and concurrent duplicate delivery.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Provider-specific event semantics must be incorporated.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security guidance","RFC 9421","Provider-specific official webhook documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B266","D40-B268"]} {"record_id":"D40-B268","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Webhooks and integrations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Non-idempotent webhook side effects","question":"How should webhook handlers remediate duplicate delivery causing repeated business actions?","answer":"Use an idempotency key or unique event identifier tied to durable state and make the side effect transactionally safe against duplicate processing.","objective":"Ensure retries or duplicate webhook deliveries do not create repeated privileged effects.","requirements":["Identify each event uniquely.","Persist processing state durably.","Enforce uniqueness at the storage layer where possible.","Design side effects to be idempotent or transactionally deduplicated."],"constraints":["External side effects may not support native transactions.","Webhook providers commonly retry after timeouts."],"preconditions":["The provider can deliver the same event more than once or the receiver may retry processing."],"input":"Webhook event with stable identity.","output":"At most one intended business effect occurs for the event.","expected_behavior":"Repeated deliveries produce the same final state without duplicated side effects.","failure_mode":"The handler performs the business action again for every delivery.","root_cause":"Processing is tied to request arrival rather than durable event identity.","solution":"Persist event identity and processing state and enforce idempotency around the business operation.","edge_cases":["Concurrent duplicate requests.","Handler crash after side effect but before acknowledgment.","Database transaction rollback.","Provider retries after long delays."],"security_implications":"Duplicate processing can create financial, authorization, provisioning, or data-integrity vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Idempotency storage adds lookup/write overhead but can reduce expensive repeated work.","testing":{"method":"Deliver identical events concurrently and across simulated crashes.","expected_result":"The final business state contains only the intended effect."},"verification":"Inspect durable event state and compare side-effect count with delivery count.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Architecture and persistence implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security guidance","RFC 9110 idempotent-method concepts"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B266","D40-B267"]} {"record_id":"D40-B269","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook delivery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Webhook delivery to attacker-controlled destination","question":"How should systems that send webhooks to configurable destinations remediate SSRF risk?","answer":"Apply the same outbound destination security policy used for other server-side fetches, including scheme, host, port, redirect, DNS, egress, timeout, and response-size controls. Do not treat webhook delivery as inherently trusted merely because it is an integration feature.","objective":"Prevent configurable webhook destinations from becoming an SSRF primitive.","requirements":["Validate destination ownership or allowlist where feasible.","Restrict schemes and ports.","Control redirects and DNS resolution.","Enforce egress restrictions and resource limits."],"constraints":["Customer-configured integrations may legitimately require dynamic destinations.","The receiver may be external and unavailable during configuration."],"preconditions":["Users or tenants can configure webhook destination URLs."],"input":"Configured webhook endpoint and event payload.","output":"Webhook delivery occurs only to policy-approved destinations.","expected_behavior":"Private, loopback, metadata, and otherwise prohibited destinations cannot be targeted.","failure_mode":"An attacker configures a webhook endpoint that causes the server to access internal infrastructure.","root_cause":"Outbound integration delivery bypasses SSRF controls.","solution":"Centralize outbound destination policy and require explicit approval or ownership validation for dynamic destinations.","edge_cases":["Tenant isolation.","DNS rebinding.","Redirects.","IPv6.","Private SaaS endpoints."],"security_implications":"A webhook sender can expose internal network access and sensitive event data if destination validation is weak.","performance_implications":"Validation adds small per-delivery overhead; egress proxies may add latency.","testing":{"method":"Configure approved external, loopback, private, metadata, and redirecting destinations.","expected_result":"Only approved destinations receive requests."},"verification":"Audit outbound network traces and compare destinations with the integration policy.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Integration platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B256","D40-B266"]} {"record_id":"D40-B270","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Webhook delivery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unbounded webhook payload and timeout handling","question":"How should webhook endpoints remediate unbounded payloads and processing duration?","answer":"Enforce request-body limits, connection/read timeouts, processing deadlines, concurrency limits, and safe cancellation. Reject or defer oversized work before expensive parsing or business processing.","objective":"Prevent webhook endpoints from becoming resource-exhaustion targets.","requirements":["Limit request size.","Limit header and connection duration where supported.","Bound concurrent processing.","Cancel or reject work that exceeds deadlines.","Apply appropriate parser limits."],"constraints":["Legitimate event sizes vary by provider.","Asynchronous processing requires durable queue controls and authorization of queued work."],"preconditions":["The service accepts externally generated webhook requests."],"input":"Webhook request of arbitrary size or processing cost.","output":"Requests remain within defined resource and time budgets.","expected_behavior":"Oversized or excessively slow requests are rejected or safely terminated.","failure_mode":"Attackers exhaust memory, CPU, connections, workers, or queues through webhook requests.","root_cause":"No resource governance exists at the integration boundary.","solution":"Set explicit request and processing limits and monitor rejection behavior.","edge_cases":["Chunked transfer.","Compressed payloads.","Large JSON nesting.","Slow clients.","Queue saturation."],"security_implications":"Resource exhaustion at webhook boundaries can cause denial of service and affect unrelated tenants.","performance_implications":"Limits protect worker and memory capacity; overly restrictive limits can reject legitimate events.","testing":{"method":"Exercise oversized, slow, deeply nested, concurrent, and malformed webhook requests.","expected_result":"Requests are bounded without resource leakage.","observed_result":"Not established; execution is required for empirical resource measurements."},"verification":"Measure resource consumption under controlled abuse scenarios and inspect cancellation cleanup.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Framework/server/parser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service Cheat Sheet","CWE-400"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B260","D40-B268"]} {"record_id":"D40-B271","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Data protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data in URLs","question":"How should applications remediate sensitive tokens or personal data placed in URLs?","answer":"Move secrets and sensitive values out of URL query strings and paths where possible. Use protected request bodies or headers according to the protocol and ensure logs, analytics, referrers, caches, and browser history do not unnecessarily capture sensitive data.","objective":"Reduce accidental disclosure of secrets and sensitive information through URL propagation.","requirements":["Avoid putting authentication secrets in URLs.","Use secure request mechanisms appropriate to the protocol.","Review logging and observability pipelines.","Remove sensitive URL data from generated links."],"constraints":["Some protocols or third-party integrations may require URL parameters.","Migration may require temporary compatibility handling."],"preconditions":["Sensitive data is currently included in request URLs."],"input":"URL containing token, credential, or sensitive data.","output":"Sensitive value is transmitted through a less exposed channel and is excluded from routine URL-derived telemetry.","expected_behavior":"Operational systems do not unnecessarily record or propagate the sensitive value.","failure_mode":"Secrets leak through access logs, browser history, referrer headers, analytics, caches, or support tooling.","root_cause":"URL is treated as equivalent to a protected request body or header.","solution":"Move sensitive values to appropriate protected channels, redact existing telemetry, and rotate credentials that may already have leaked.","edge_cases":["Password-reset links.","One-time invitation URLs.","Third-party callback parameters.","Existing indexed or cached links."],"security_implications":"URL leakage can expose credentials or personal data to multiple systems outside the intended trust boundary.","performance_implications":"Moving data from query parameters to headers or bodies generally has negligible performance impact.","testing":{"method":"Inspect requests, access logs, tracing, referrer behavior, and generated links for sensitive values.","expected_result":"Sensitive values are absent from unintended URL-derived stores."},"verification":"Trace the complete data path and verify redaction and credential rotation where exposure occurred.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Protocol and framework independent principle with implementation-specific controls.","provenance":["OWASP Information Exposure Through Query Strings in URL","CWE-598"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B272"]} {"record_id":"D40-B272","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Data protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data leakage through caches","question":"How should applications remediate caching of authenticated or sensitive responses?","answer":"Define explicit cache-control policy for sensitive responses, prevent shared caches from storing responses containing private data when inappropriate, and vary cache keys only according to a deliberate security policy.","objective":"Prevent one user or tenant from receiving another user's cached sensitive content.","requirements":["Classify responses by cache sensitivity.","Use appropriate cache-control directives.","Ensure shared caches do not reuse private responses across identities.","Test cache key and authorization interactions."],"constraints":["Caching layers may exist outside application control.","Some public content can be safely cached while authenticated content cannot."],"preconditions":["Sensitive responses pass through one or more caching layers."],"input":"Authenticated request and cacheable response.","output":"Only authorized request contexts can retrieve the corresponding cached data.","expected_behavior":"A cache cannot serve one principal's private response to another principal.","failure_mode":"Shared cache stores or serves sensitive content without sufficient identity separation.","root_cause":"Caching policy ignores authorization context or response sensitivity.","solution":"Set explicit cache policy and align cache keys, authorization, and response headers across all cache layers.","edge_cases":["CDNs.","Reverse proxies.","Vary headers.","Personalized responses.","Multi-tenant APIs."],"security_implications":"Cache leakage can expose private data across users or tenants without directly bypassing application authorization.","performance_implications":"Disabling caching can increase origin load; safe partitioning or private caching may preserve some performance benefits.","testing":{"method":"Request the same resource under different identities and inspect cache hits and response content.","expected_result":"No identity receives another identity's private cached response."},"verification":"Test application and intermediary cache behavior together.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP cache semantics and intermediary implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 9111","OWASP Web Cache Deception guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B143","D40-B271"]} {"record_id":"D40-B273","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Data protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cache poisoning through untrusted cache-key inputs","question":"How should applications remediate cache poisoning caused by untrusted inputs influencing cache behavior?","answer":"Ensure cache keys include every security- and representation-relevant input, reject ambiguous or untrusted cache-key variations, and prevent untrusted headers or parameters from altering cached responses without corresponding cache-key separation.","objective":"Prevent attackers from causing a shared cache to store content that is later served to other users.","requirements":["Define cache-key components explicitly.","Ensure response variation matches cache-key variation.","Control untrusted headers that affect generated responses.","Test cache behavior through the full proxy/CDN chain."],"constraints":["Cache implementations differ in normalization and key construction.","Some legitimate personalization may require carefully designed cache partitioning."],"preconditions":["Responses are stored in a shared cache and output depends on attacker-influenced request attributes."],"input":"Request containing cache-relevant headers, parameters, or host information.","output":"Cached representations correspond exactly to their authorized and relevant request context.","expected_behavior":"An attacker cannot prime a shared cache with a representation intended for a different context.","failure_mode":"A poisoned representation is served to subsequent users.","root_cause":"Cache key and response-generation inputs are inconsistent.","solution":"Align cache keys with response variance, remove unnecessary untrusted variation, and validate proxy behavior.","edge_cases":["Host header differences.","Content negotiation.","Query normalization.","Compression variants.","CDN-specific cache rules."],"security_implications":"Cache poisoning can affect confidentiality, integrity, availability, and downstream user trust.","performance_implications":"More precise cache keys can reduce hit rates and increase origin traffic.","testing":{"method":"Prime caches with crafted requests and compare subsequent responses from independent clients.","expected_result":"Untrusted request variation cannot cause unauthorized shared content."},"verification":"Inspect cache keys and perform controlled cross-user cache-poisoning tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cache implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Web Cache Poisoning guidance","RFC 9111"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B263","D40-B272"]} {"record_id":"D40-B274","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Encryption at rest","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unencrypted sensitive persistent data","question":"How should systems remediate sensitive data stored without appropriate encryption at rest?","answer":"Classify the data and apply encryption controls appropriate to the threat model and storage environment, with keys managed separately from the protected data and access governed by least privilege.","objective":"Reduce exposure if storage media, backups, snapshots, or storage services are accessed outside the intended trust boundary.","requirements":["Identify data requiring encryption.","Use maintained cryptographic implementations.","Separate key management from protected data.","Restrict key access.","Define rotation and recovery procedures."],"constraints":["Encryption at rest does not replace application authorization.","Key loss can cause data loss, so recovery must be designed explicitly."],"preconditions":["Sensitive persistent data is stored in databases, files, object storage, or similar systems."],"input":"Sensitive data and storage environment.","output":"Protected data is encrypted according to the approved data-protection policy.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized access to raw storage does not directly expose protected plaintext.","failure_mode":"Storage compromise exposes sensitive plaintext.","root_cause":"Data classification and storage protection requirements were not implemented or were incorrectly scoped.","solution":"Apply appropriate encryption, isolate keys, enforce least-privilege key access, and validate restore/recovery workflows.","edge_cases":["Searchable encrypted data.","Key rotation.","Backups and replicas.","Logs and temporary storage.","Application-level versus storage-level encryption."],"security_implications":"Encryption at rest reduces the impact of storage compromise but does not protect data after legitimate decryption by an authorized application.","performance_implications":"Encryption introduces CPU and key-management overhead; actual impact should be measured for workload-specific claims.","testing":{"method":"Inspect storage configuration, key permissions, backups, snapshots, and restore paths.","expected_result":"Protected storage and associated copies satisfy the encryption policy."},"verification":"Review cryptographic configuration and perform controlled restore/access tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Storage and cloud-provider dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","NIST SP 800-111","OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B143","D40-B275"]} {"record_id":"D40-B275","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Temporary data exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive temporary-file exposure","question":"How should applications remediate sensitive data written to insecure temporary files?","answer":"Avoid temporary files when unnecessary; when required, create them using secure operating-system APIs with restrictive permissions, unpredictable names, controlled directories, bounded lifetime, and reliable cleanup.","objective":"Prevent other users or processes from reading or replacing sensitive temporary files.","requirements":["Use OS-supported secure temporary-file creation.","Apply restrictive permissions.","Avoid predictable names.","Limit file lifetime.","Clean up on normal and failure paths."],"constraints":["Cleanup is not guaranteed after process crashes.","Platform temporary-directory semantics differ."],"preconditions":["Sensitive data is materialized on local or shared temporary storage."],"input":"Sensitive intermediate data.","output":"Temporary storage is inaccessible to unauthorized principals and is removed according to policy.","expected_behavior":"Attackers cannot predict, replace, or read temporary files through shared filesystem behavior.","failure_mode":"Sensitive temporary data is exposed or a malicious file is substituted.","root_cause":"Predictable paths, insecure permissions, or unsafe temporary-file APIs are used.","solution":"Use secure OS primitives, restrictive permissions, safe directories, and lifecycle cleanup.","edge_cases":["Symlink attacks.","Cross-user temporary directories.","Crash recovery.","Disk encryption assumptions.","Large temporary files."],"security_implications":"Temporary-file exposure can leak credentials, uploaded data, cryptographic material, or intermediate documents.","performance_implications":"Secure temporary storage can add filesystem overhead; avoid unnecessary materialization when practical.","testing":{"method":"Inspect permissions, names, directory ownership, race behavior, and cleanup after failures.","expected_result":"Unauthorized users cannot access or replace temporary data.","verification":"Perform filesystem-level security tests under distinct user identities where supported.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Operating-system and runtime API dependent.","provenance":["CWE-377","CWE-378","OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B125","D40-B274"]} {"record_id":"D40-B276","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Concurrency security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"TOCTOU authorization race","question":"How should applications remediate time-of-check/time-of-use races around security-sensitive resources?","answer":"Avoid separate authorization checks followed by independent resource operations when the resource can change between them. Prefer atomic operating-system or database operations that enforce the security condition at the point of use.","objective":"Prevent attackers from changing a resource between authorization and use.","requirements":["Identify mutable security-sensitive resources.","Minimize check/use separation.","Use atomic filesystem or database primitives where available.","Bind authorization to the operation being performed."],"constraints":["Atomicity mechanisms differ by storage and operating system.","Distributed systems may require transactional or conditional APIs."],"preconditions":["A security decision is made before an operation on a mutable resource."],"input":"Resource state and authorization decision.","output":"The protected operation occurs only if the authorized condition still holds.","expected_behavior":"Concurrent state changes cannot convert an authorized check into an unauthorized operation.","failure_mode":"Attacker changes the resource after validation but before use.","root_cause":"Security check and privileged operation are non-atomic.","solution":"Use atomic operations, transactions, conditional updates, file-descriptor-based operations, or equivalent mechanisms.","edge_cases":["Symlink replacement.","Concurrent database updates.","Distributed locks.","Filesystem mount changes."],"security_implications":"TOCTOU races can bypass filesystem authorization, integrity controls, or business invariants.","performance_implications":"Atomic operations can improve correctness but may increase contention depending on the locking or transactional mechanism.","testing":{"method":"Run concurrent adversarial updates between the check and use phases.","expected_result":"The unauthorized state transition is rejected or cannot occur.","observed_result":"Not established without controlled concurrent execution."},"verification":"Inspect the operation for atomicity and perform race-focused tests under controlled concurrency.","languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Python","Java"],"version_scope":"OS, database, and runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-367","OWASP race-condition guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B125","D40-B277"]} {"record_id":"D40-B277","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Filesystem race conditions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Symlink race during privileged file access","question":"How should privileged software remediate symlink substitution races?","answer":"Use file APIs that operate on already opened descriptors or provide no-follow and directory-relative controls, and avoid validating a pathname and reopening it later under changed filesystem state.","objective":"Prevent an attacker from redirecting privileged file operations through symlink replacement.","requirements":["Use no-follow semantics where supported.","Prefer descriptor-based operations after secure opening.","Restrict writable directories used by privileged processes.","Avoid predictable temporary paths."],"constraints":["Exact safe APIs differ by operating system.","Cross-platform abstractions may not expose all security-relevant primitives."],"preconditions":["A privileged process accesses attacker-influenced filesystem paths."],"input":"Path to a security-sensitive file or directory.","output":"The privileged operation targets the resource that was actually authorized.","expected_behavior":"Path replacement or symlink substitution cannot redirect the privileged operation.","failure_mode":"An attacker replaces the checked path with a symlink to a sensitive target.","root_cause":"Path validation and file use are separated and pathname resolution remains attacker-controlled.","solution":"Use secure open primitives and operate on verified descriptors rather than re-resolving attacker-controlled paths.","edge_cases":["Network filesystems.","Bind mounts.","Windows reparse points.","Directory traversal combined with symlinks."],"security_implications":"Symlink races can convert a limited file-write/read primitive into arbitrary privileged filesystem access.","performance_implications":"Descriptor-based operations may reduce repeated path resolution but platform behavior should be measured.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled concurrent path replacement while the privileged operation executes.","expected_result":"The operation cannot be redirected to an unauthorized target.","observed_result":"Not established without OS-specific execution tests."},"verification":"Review syscall/API semantics and run platform-specific race tests.","languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Python"],"version_scope":"Operating-system specific; consult current secure filesystem API documentation.","provenance":["CWE-367","CWE-59","Operating-system secure filesystem API documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B276","D40-B125"]} {"record_id":"D40-B278","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Concurrency authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Race condition in authorization-sensitive state transition","question":"How should applications remediate concurrent requests that can bypass a business authorization invariant?","answer":"Enforce the authorization invariant atomically with the state transition using database transactions, conditional updates, serializable constraints, locks, or equivalent concurrency control rather than relying on a prior read-only authorization check.","objective":"Prevent concurrent requests from both observing an allowed state and then performing conflicting privileged transitions.","requirements":["Identify the protected invariant.","Combine authorization-relevant state validation with mutation.","Use appropriate isolation or conditional update semantics.","Handle conflict and retry behavior safely."],"constraints":["Isolation guarantees vary by database.","Distributed services may require coordination beyond a single database transaction."],"preconditions":["Concurrent requests can modify the same authorization-sensitive state."],"input":"Two or more concurrent authorized-looking operations against shared state.","output":"The system preserves the authorization invariant under concurrency.","expected_behavior":"At most the allowed state transition succeeds when the invariant permits only one or a bounded number of operations.","failure_mode":"Concurrent requests each pass the check and collectively violate the security invariant.","root_cause":"Authorization is evaluated outside the atomic state transition.","solution":"Move the security condition into an atomic transaction or conditional write and handle conflicts explicitly.","edge_cases":["Retries.","Deadlocks.","Eventual consistency.","Distributed locks.","Partial failure."],"security_implications":"Concurrency flaws can enable duplicate spending, quota bypass, privilege escalation, or unauthorized state transitions.","performance_implications":"Stronger isolation or locking can reduce throughput through contention; use the weakest mechanism that correctly enforces the invariant.","testing":{"method":"Run concurrent requests against a shared authorization-sensitive state and assert the invariant after every run.","expected_result":"The invariant remains true under concurrency."},"verification":"Use repeatable concurrency tests and inspect transaction/isolation semantics.","languages":["Python","Java","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-362","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B276","D40-B268"]} {"record_id":"D40-B279","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Locking","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","concept":"Incorrect lock scope in security-sensitive concurrency","question":"How should software remediate a lock that is too narrow to protect a security invariant or too broad to preserve availability?","answer":"Define the invariant first, then scope synchronization to cover the complete state transition that must be atomic while avoiding unrelated work inside the critical section.","objective":"Preserve security invariants without creating unnecessary contention or deadlock risk.","requirements":["Identify all shared state participating in the invariant.","Hold synchronization across the complete atomic transition.","Avoid external network calls and unrelated expensive work while holding locks.","Define lock ordering where multiple locks exist."],"constraints":["Lock semantics differ between threads, processes, databases, and distributed systems.","A broader lock may be necessary when the invariant spans multiple resources."],"preconditions":["Concurrent operations share state protected by synchronization.","],"input":"Concurrent state transition requiring mutual exclusion or equivalent coordination.","output":"The protected invariant remains consistent while unrelated work can proceed concurrently.","expected_behavior":"No concurrent operation can observe or create an invalid intermediate state.","failure_mode":"A narrow lock allows a race, or a broad lock creates avoidable contention/deadlock.","root_cause":"Synchronization scope was chosen without explicitly modeling the protected invariant.","solution":"Model the invariant, align synchronization scope with the atomic transition, and validate lock ordering and failure behavior.","edge_cases":["Exceptions while holding locks.","Timeouts.","Nested locks.","Process crashes.","Distributed lock expiration."],"security_implications":"Incorrect locking can create authorization bypasses or state corruption; distributed lock failures can create especially subtle security defects.","performance_implications":"Critical-section size directly affects contention; measure workload-specific effects instead of assuming a universal optimum.","testing":{"method":"Stress concurrent state transitions and inject failures while locks are held.","expected_result":"Security invariants hold without deadlock or unacceptable lock leakage.","observed_result":"Not established without workload-specific execution."},"verification":"Review synchronization semantics and execute concurrency stress tests.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Concurrency primitive and runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-362","CWE-667"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B278","D40-B280"]} {"record_id":"D40-B280","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Race-condition verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security race regression testing","question":"How should a security remediation for a race condition be verified?","answer":"Use deterministic synchronization hooks where possible, repeated concurrent execution, invariant assertions, failure injection, and platform-specific concurrency tooling. A single successful test run is insufficient evidence that a race has been eliminated.","objective":"Provide stronger evidence that a concurrency-related security flaw is actually remediated.","requirements":["Define the security invariant explicitly.","Exercise conflicting operations concurrently.","Use repeatable synchronization points where possible.","Test retries and failure paths.","Record limitations of nondeterministic race testing."],"constraints":["Race reproduction can be nondeterministic.","Dynamic tests cannot prove absence of all races."],"preconditions":["A security issue depends on concurrent interleavings."],"input":"Remediated concurrent implementation and adversarial workload.","output":"Evidence about invariant preservation across tested interleavings.","expected_behavior":"All tested interleavings preserve the security invariant.","failure_mode":"Testing covers only sequential behavior or one favorable interleaving.","root_cause":"Concurrency verification is treated as ordinary functional testing.","solution":"Combine controlled concurrency tests with code-level synchronization review and appropriate static/dynamic analysis.","edge_cases":["Scheduler variability.","Different CPU counts.","Database isolation differences.","Production topology differences."],"security_implications":"Insufficient race testing can leave exploitable authorization or integrity defects undetected.","performance_implications":"Stress testing can reveal contention regressions in addition to security behavior.","testing":{"method":"Controlled concurrency stress, synchronization barriers, invariant assertions, fault injection, and repeated runs.","expected_result":"No tested execution violates the invariant; residual uncertainty is explicitly recorded."},"verification":"Review both empirical test evidence and synchronization design.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Tooling and concurrency model dependent.","provenance":["CWE-362","CWE-367","OWASP Testing Guide"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B276","D40-B279"]} {"record_id":"D40-B281","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Native memory safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Buffer overflow remediation","question":"How should native software remediate a buffer overflow?","answer":"Replace unchecked memory operations with bounds-checked or size-aware APIs, validate lengths before copying or indexing, and use memory-safe abstractions where feasible.","objective":"Prevent out-of-bounds memory access that can cause crashes, data corruption, or code execution.","requirements":["Validate buffer lengths before access.","Use APIs that accept explicit destination capacities.","Avoid unchecked pointer arithmetic.","Add boundary-focused tests and sanitizer-based testing where appropriate."],"constraints":["Legacy C/C++ code may expose unsafe APIs throughout the codebase.","ABI or performance requirements can constrain migration choices."],"preconditions":["Native code performs manual memory access or fixed-size buffer operations."],"input":"Buffer and attacker-influenced length/data.","output":"Memory access remains within allocated bounds.","expected_behavior":"Oversized or malformed input is rejected or safely truncated according to the intended contract.","failure_mode":"Input causes out-of-bounds read or write.","root_cause":"Length validation is absent, incorrect, or disconnected from the actual allocation size.","solution":"Use size-aware APIs, explicit bounds checks, safer abstractions, and memory-safety testing.","edge_cases":["Integer overflow in size calculations.","Zero-length buffers.","Null pointers.","Variable-length structures."],"security_implications":"Memory corruption can compromise confidentiality, integrity, availability, and process control.","performance_implications":"Bounds checks generally add limited overhead, but implementation-specific measurements are required for performance claims.","testing":{"method":"Boundary tests plus AddressSanitizer or equivalent memory-safety tooling where supported.","expected_result":"Out-of-bounds inputs are rejected and sanitizer runs report no corresponding memory violation."},"verification":"Review allocation/access size relationships and execute memory-safety tests.","languages":["C","C++","Rust"],"version_scope":"Native-language and compiler/runtime dependent.","provenance":["CWE-119","CWE-120","CWE-787","SEI CERT C Coding Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B282","D40-B283"]} {"record_id":"D40-B282","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Native memory safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Use-after-free remediation","question":"How should native software remediate use-after-free vulnerabilities?","answer":"Ensure object lifetime is explicit and ownership is unique or otherwise synchronized. Prefer RAII, ownership-aware abstractions, reference counting where appropriate, or memory-safe language constructs instead of manual lifetime assumptions.","objective":"Prevent access to memory after its lifetime has ended.","requirements":["Define ownership and lifetime.","Invalidate or prevent use of released objects.","Use ownership-aware abstractions where possible.","Test with memory-safety instrumentation."],"constraints":["Reference counting does not solve every ownership cycle or concurrency problem.","Legacy manual-memory code may require staged refactoring."],"preconditions":["Native code manually manages object lifetime or shares pointers across asynchronous operations."],"input":"Object pointer/reference whose lifetime may end before all consumers finish.","output":"Consumers cannot dereference invalid memory.","expected_behavior":"Object lifetime extends through all valid uses and cleanup occurs exactly according to ownership rules.","failure_mode":"Code accesses freed memory.","root_cause":"Lifetime ownership is ambiguous or cleanup occurs before the final use.","solution":"Redesign ownership, use RAII/ownership-aware types, synchronize concurrent access, and add memory-safety testing.","edge_cases":["Asynchronous callbacks.","Reference cycles.","Concurrent destruction.","Error-path cleanup."],"security_implications":"Use-after-free can lead to memory corruption, information disclosure, and potentially arbitrary code execution.","performance_implications":"Ownership mechanisms can change allocation and synchronization costs; measure workload-specific effects.","testing":{"method":"AddressSanitizer or equivalent, lifetime-focused unit tests, and concurrent/asynchronous failure tests.","expected_result":"No tested execution accesses freed memory."},"verification":"Review ownership/lifetime model and run memory-safety instrumentation.","languages":["C","C++","Rust"],"version_scope":"Compiler and runtime/tooling dependent.","provenance":["CWE-416","C++ Core Guidelines","Rust ownership documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B281","D40-B283"]} {"record_id":"D40-B283","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Native memory safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Double-free remediation","question":"How should native software remediate double-free vulnerabilities?","answer":"Establish a single clear ownership model so each allocation has exactly one responsible release path, use RAII or ownership-aware abstractions where practical, and ensure error paths cannot release the same resource twice.","objective":"Prevent heap corruption caused by repeated deallocation of the same resource.","requirements":["Define resource ownership.","Centralize or structurally enforce cleanup.","Handle error paths consistently.","Use memory-safety tooling during validation."],"constraints":["Manual memory management may be required in legacy or low-level components.","Custom allocators can complicate diagnosis."],"preconditions":["Code manually releases heap or other owned resources."],"input":"Resource with multiple potential cleanup paths.","output":"Each resource is released exactly once according to its ownership contract.","expected_behavior":"All normal and failure paths converge on one valid cleanup decision.","failure_mode":"The same resource is released multiple times.","root_cause":"Ownership is duplicated or cleanup paths are not mutually exclusive.","solution":"Use ownership-aware design, RAII, explicit ownership transfer, and structured cleanup.","edge_cases":["Exceptions.","Early returns.","Callbacks.","Concurrent cleanup.","Partial initialization."],"security_implications":"Double-free can corrupt allocator metadata and cause crashes, information disclosure, or code execution.","performance_implications":"Structured ownership can reduce defensive cleanup complexity; allocator-specific performance must be measured.","testing":{"method":"Run failure-path and memory-safety tests under AddressSanitizer or equivalent tooling.","expected_result":"No double-free is detected across tested paths.","verification":"Review ownership transfer and cleanup control flow and execute instrumented tests.","languages":["C","C++","Rust"],"version_scope":"Native implementation and allocator dependent.","provenance":["CWE-415","C++ Core Guidelines","SEI CERT C Coding Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B282","D40-B284"]} {"record_id":"D40-B284","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Native memory safety","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Format-string vulnerability remediation","question":"How should native software remediate externally controlled format strings?","answer":"Use constant format strings and pass untrusted data as data arguments rather than as the formatting template. Prefer type-safe formatting APIs where available.","objective":"Prevent attackers from controlling formatting directives that can read or write unintended memory or otherwise corrupt output.","requirements":["Keep format templates under program control.","Pass external data as arguments.","Use compiler diagnostics and format-security warnings.","Test special formatting characters."],"constraints":["Legacy APIs may permit unsafe patterns.","Format-string semantics vary across libraries."],"preconditions":["User-controlled text is passed to a formatting function as the format template."],"input":"Untrusted string passed to a formatting API.","output":"The untrusted string is rendered as data rather than interpreted as formatting syntax.","expected_behavior":"Formatting directives supplied by the attacker have no special execution semantics.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled format directives access unintended arguments or memory.","root_cause":"Data and formatting instructions are not separated.","solution":"Use literal format strings and type-safe formatting interfaces.","edge_cases":["Localization strings.","Variadic C APIs.","Logging functions.","Custom formatting libraries."],"security_implications":"Format-string flaws can cause information disclosure, crashes, or memory corruption.","performance_implications":"Type-safe formatting may have different allocation or formatting costs; benchmark only where necessary.","testing":{"method":"Supply formatting directives as input and compile with format-security warnings.","expected_result":"Input is emitted literally and static diagnostics remain clean.","verification":"Inspect call sites and run negative tests with attacker-controlled format syntax.","languages":["C","C++","Rust"],"version_scope":"Library/compiler dependent.","provenance":["CWE-134","SEI CERT C Coding Standard","C++ Core Guidelines"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B281","D40-B285"]} {"record_id":"D40-B285","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Native memory safety","subtopic":"Integer arithmetic","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Integer overflow in security-sensitive size calculation","question":"How should native software remediate integer overflow that can lead to undersized allocations or bounds bypasses?","answer":"Validate arithmetic before allocation or indexing, use checked arithmetic APIs or wider validated types, and ensure multiplication/addition cannot wrap into a smaller value before memory operations.","objective":"Prevent arithmetic wraparound from invalidating memory-safety or resource-size assumptions.","requirements":["Check arithmetic overflow before use.","Use checked or saturating operations where appropriate.","Validate attacker-controlled dimensions and counts.","Keep allocation size and access calculations consistent."],"constraints":["Unsigned and signed overflow semantics differ by language.","A wider integer type does not by itself eliminate overflow."],"preconditions":["External values influence allocation sizes, lengths, indexes, or resource quotas."],"input":"Attacker-influenced integer operands.","output":"Only mathematically valid and representable sizes reach allocation or indexing operations.","expected_behavior":"Overflowing inputs are rejected before unsafe operations occur.","failure_mode":"Arithmetic wraps and produces an incorrect size or index.","root_cause":"Arithmetic is performed without overflow validation.","solution":"Use checked arithmetic and explicit maximum bounds before memory or resource operations.","edge_cases":["Zero values.","Maximum representable integers.","Signed overflow.","Multiplication followed by addition.","Architecture-dependent integer widths."],"security_implications":"Integer overflow can become buffer overflow, denial of service, quota bypass, or memory corruption.","performance_implications":"Checked arithmetic generally has low cost, but hot-path impact should be measured if relevant.","testing":{"method":"Boundary and fuzz tests around integer limits plus compiler/static diagnostics.","expected_result":"Overflowing inputs are rejected and valid maximum values remain correct.","verification":"Review arithmetic operations and execute boundary-focused tests.","languages":["C","C++","Rust","Go","Java"],"version_scope":"Language integer semantics and compiler version dependent.","provenance":["CWE-190","CWE-680","SEI CERT C Coding Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B281","D40-B284"]} {"record_id":"D40-B286","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container hardening","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Privileged container execution","question":"How should containerized workloads remediate unnecessary privileged execution?","answer":"Run containers without privileged mode unless a documented requirement exists, remove unnecessary Linux capabilities, use a non-root user, and apply restrictive security profiles and filesystem permissions.","objective":"Reduce the host-impact potential of a compromised container.","requirements":["Avoid privileged mode by default.","Drop unnecessary capabilities.","Use non-root execution where feasible.","Apply seccomp, AppArmor, SELinux, or platform-equivalent controls.","Restrict writable host mounts."],"constraints":["Some infrastructure workloads require elevated capabilities.","Container runtime and orchestrator capabilities differ."],"preconditions":["A container currently runs with excessive host privileges."],"input":"Container security configuration.","output":"Workload operates with only the privileges required by its function.","expected_behavior":"Compromise of the workload does not automatically grant broad host-level capabilities.","failure_mode":"A compromised container can perform privileged host operations.","root_cause":"Convenience-oriented deployment configuration grants excessive privileges.","solution":"Remove privileged mode and capabilities incrementally while testing required functionality.","edge_cases":["Kernel-level agents.","Storage plugins.","Host networking.","Rootless runtimes."],"security_implications":"Privileged containers significantly enlarge the impact of application compromise.","performance_implications":"Removing privileges normally has little direct performance effect, but security profiles can block required operations and require compatibility testing.","testing":{"method":"Deploy with reduced privileges and execute the complete workload test suite plus forbidden-operation tests.","expected_result":"Required functionality works while prohibited privileged operations fail.","verification":"Inspect runtime configuration and effective capabilities rather than relying solely on deployment manifests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Container runtime and orchestrator dependent.","provenance":["Docker security documentation","Kubernetes Pod Security Standards","CIS Docker/Kubernetes Benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B287","D40-B288"]} {"record_id":"D40-B287","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container hardening","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Writable container root filesystem","question":"How should containerized applications remediate unnecessary write access to the root filesystem?","answer":"Run containers with a read-only root filesystem where practical and provide narrowly scoped writable volumes or temporary filesystems only for paths that require writes.","objective":"Reduce persistence and tampering opportunities after application compromise.","requirements":["Identify required writable paths.","Use read-only root filesystem where supported.","Mount writable directories explicitly.","Apply least-privilege ownership and permissions."],"constraints":["Applications may need runtime caches, temporary files, or generated configuration.","Read-only root filesystems can expose hidden assumptions in legacy applications."],"preconditions":["A workload does not require unrestricted writes to its container image filesystem."],"input":"Container filesystem configuration.","output":"Only explicitly required paths are writable.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized modifications to application binaries and configuration fail.","failure_mode":"Compromised application modifies executable or configuration files inside the container.","root_cause":"Container filesystem permissions are broader than the workload requires.","solution":"Enable read-only root filesystem and provide controlled writable mounts for required state.","edge_cases":["Temporary directories.","Package managers.","Runtime-generated certificates.","Log files.","Native libraries requiring writable caches."],"security_implications":"Read-only filesystems can reduce persistence and tampering after compromise.","performance_implications":"Storage behavior may change depending on writable volume configuration; workload testing is required.","testing":{"method":"Run application tests with read-only root and verify only approved write paths succeed.","expected_result":"Application functions correctly without unrestricted root filesystem writes."},"verification":"Inspect effective mount permissions and attempt controlled unauthorized writes.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Container runtime/orchestrator dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes securityContext documentation","Docker security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B286","D40-B289"]} {"record_id":"D40-B288","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Host namespace exposure","question":"How should container deployments remediate unnecessary host namespace sharing?","answer":"Do not share host PID, network, IPC, or other namespaces unless the workload has a documented requirement. Use isolated namespaces and explicit service interfaces instead.","objective":"Reduce the ability of a compromised workload to observe or interact with host resources and unrelated workloads.","requirements":["Disable unnecessary host namespace sharing.","Document required namespace exceptions.","Use network policies and explicit service communication.","Test workload functionality under isolated namespaces."],"constraints":["Certain infrastructure components require host-level visibility.","Namespace controls vary by runtime and orchestrator."],"preconditions":["A container shares one or more host namespaces without a clear requirement."],"input":"Container namespace configuration.","output":"Workload receives only the namespace visibility required by its role.","expected_behavior":"The container cannot inspect or interfere with unrelated host processes or network resources beyond its authorized scope.","failure_mode":"A compromised workload gains host-level visibility or access through shared namespaces.","root_cause":"Host namespaces were enabled for convenience or inherited from a broad deployment template.","solution":"Disable unnecessary sharing and replace it with explicit, least-privilege interfaces.","edge_cases":["Node monitoring agents.","Host-network services.","Debugging containers.","Service meshes."],"security_implications":"Namespace sharing can materially reduce container isolation and increase lateral movement opportunities.","performance_implications":"Namespace isolation can affect networking and observability architecture; performance impact is deployment-specific.","testing":{"method":"Inspect effective namespaces and attempt controlled access to host resources.","expected_result":"Unauthorized host visibility and access are unavailable.","verification":"Validate runtime configuration rather than only declarative manifests.","languages":["Python","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Container runtime and orchestrator dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes securityContext documentation","Docker security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B286","D40-B287"]} {"record_id":"D40-B289","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container management interfaces","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Exposed container management endpoint","question":"How should deployments remediate an unauthenticated or unnecessarily exposed container management interface?","answer":"Remove unnecessary management endpoints, bind required administrative interfaces to protected networks, require strong authentication and authorization, and avoid exposing container runtime control sockets to application workloads.","objective":"Prevent remote or local compromise of container infrastructure through management interfaces.","requirements":["Identify all management endpoints and sockets.","Restrict network exposure.","Require authentication and authorization.","Separate application workloads from runtime control interfaces.","Audit access attempts."],"constraints":["Operational tooling may require management access.","Runtime control APIs can differ substantially between platforms."],"preconditions":["A container runtime, orchestrator API, debug endpoint, or control socket is reachable beyond its intended trust boundary."],"input":"Network or local access to a management interface.","output":"Only explicitly authorized administrative principals can invoke control operations.","expected_behavior":"Untrusted workloads and external clients cannot issue privileged runtime operations.","failure_mode":"An attacker gains container creation, host mount, execution, or other administrative capabilities.","root_cause":"Management interface is exposed without sufficient network and authorization controls.","solution":"Remove exposure where unnecessary and enforce layered access controls where required.","edge_cases":["Unix socket permissions.","Kubernetes API exposure.","Debug ports.","Remote Docker APIs.","Monitoring interfaces."],"security_implications":"Container management access can effectively provide host or cluster control.","performance_implications":"Restricting management access has negligible application performance impact but can affect operational tooling connectivity.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate management endpoints and attempt access from application and external network contexts.","expected_result":"Only authorized administrative contexts can access control functions.","verification":"Review network exposure, authentication policy, socket permissions, and audit logs.","languages":["Go","Python","Java","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Runtime/orchestrator specific.","provenance":["Docker security documentation","Kubernetes security documentation","CIS Docker/Kubernetes Benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B286","D40-B290"]} {"record_id":"D40-B290","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container secrets","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secrets embedded in container images","question":"How should container builds remediate secrets embedded in images or build layers?","answer":"Remove secrets from source and image layers, inject runtime credentials through an appropriate secret-management mechanism, rotate any exposed credentials, and prevent future secret inclusion with build-time scanning and policy checks.","objective":"Prevent credentials from becoming recoverable from container images, registries, caches, or build artifacts.","requirements":["Keep secrets outside immutable image content.","Use runtime secret injection.","Rotate credentials that may already be exposed.","Scan source and image layers for secrets.","Restrict registry access."],"constraints":["Secret-management capabilities vary by deployment environment.","Removing a secret from the final image does not necessarily remove it from historical layers or registries."],"preconditions":["Credentials or sensitive keys have been included in image build context or layers."],"input":"Container build process and image artifacts.","output":"Runtime receives required secrets without storing them in image layers.","expected_behavior":"Image inspection cannot recover active application credentials.","failure_mode":"An attacker with image access extracts credentials from layers or build artifacts.","root_cause":"Build-time configuration is incorrectly used as long-lived secret storage.","solution":"Use external secret delivery, purge exposed artifacts where possible, rotate compromised credentials, and enforce secret-scanning gates.","edge_cases":["Build caches.","Multi-stage builds.","Registry retention.","CI logs.","Copied environment variables."],"security_implications":"Image leakage can become credential compromise across every environment using the image.","performance_implications":"Secret scanning adds build time proportional to artifact size; runtime secret retrieval can add startup latency.","testing":{"method":"Inspect all image layers, build logs, caches, and manifests for known test secrets.","expected_result":"No active secret is recoverable from the image or build artifacts.","verification":"Perform artifact inspection and validate runtime secret injection separately.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Container and CI/CD platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","Docker build security documentation","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B144","D40-B289"]} {"record_id":"D40-B291","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud and IaC security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Publicly exposed cloud storage","question":"How should infrastructure remediate unintentionally public cloud object storage?","answer":"Disable public access by default, apply explicit resource policies for required sharing, use account or project-level public-access guardrails where available, and continuously detect policy drift.","objective":"Prevent unintended public disclosure of stored data.","requirements":["Classify storage resources and data.","Disable anonymous/public access unless explicitly required.","Use least-privilege resource policies.","Continuously detect configuration drift.","Test effective access from an unauthenticated context."],"constraints":["Some public content is intentionally required.","Cloud providers differ in policy evaluation and public-access controls."],"preconditions":["Object storage or equivalent cloud storage contains data that should not be publicly accessible."],"input":"Storage resource policy and object access request.","output":"Only intended principals can access protected objects.","expected_behavior":"Anonymous access to protected data is denied.","failure_mode":"A storage policy or ACL exposes sensitive objects publicly.","root_cause":"Broad default permissions, policy drift, or conflicting resource-level settings.","solution":"Disable public access, reduce permissions, add preventive policy controls, and verify effective access.","edge_cases":["Static websites.","CDN origin access.","Cross-account sharing.","Legacy ACLs.","Bucket-policy versus object-policy precedence."],"security_implications":"Public storage exposure can cause large-scale data disclosure without compromising application code.","performance_implications":"Private access through controlled identity or CDN mechanisms may change request paths; measure deployment-specific effects.","testing":{"method":"Evaluate effective anonymous, cross-account, and authorized access using cloud policy tools and controlled requests.","expected_result":"Protected objects are inaccessible to unauthorized principals.","verification":"Review effective cloud policy and perform external access tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider specific.","provenance":["CIS cloud benchmarks","NIST SP 800-53","Cloud-provider official storage security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B292","D40-B293"]} {"record_id":"D40-B292","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud IAM","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overly broad cloud IAM permissions","question":"How should cloud infrastructure remediate excessive IAM permissions?","answer":"Reduce identities and workloads to the minimum actions and resources required, separate administrative roles from runtime identities, and continuously review effective permissions rather than relying only on declared policies.","objective":"Reduce the blast radius of compromised credentials and unintended privileged actions.","requirements":["Identify required actions and resources.","Remove unused permissions.","Separate runtime and administrative identities.","Use resource-level restrictions where supported.","Review effective permissions periodically."],"constraints":["Some managed services require broad permissions for specific operations.","Policy evaluation can involve multiple inherited or resource-level policies."],"preconditions":["Cloud identities have permissions broader than their documented workload responsibilities."],"input":"Identity policy set and workload behavior.","output":"Identity can perform required operations but not unrelated privileged actions.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized cloud actions are denied.","failure_mode":"A compromised low-privilege workload can access administrative or unrelated resources.","root_cause":"IAM permissions were granted for convenience, copied from broad roles, or never reduced after implementation.","solution":"Apply least privilege, split roles, constrain resources/actions, and verify effective permissions.","edge_cases":["Cross-account roles.","Service-linked roles.","Temporary elevation.","Break-glass access.","Policy inheritance."],"security_implications":"Excessive IAM permissions can turn application compromise into broad cloud compromise.","performance_implications":"Fine-grained policy evaluation can add administrative complexity; request latency effects are generally provider-specific and must be measured.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate effective permissions and test representative allowed and denied actions.","expected_result":"Required operations succeed while unrelated privileged actions fail.","verification":"Use provider policy analysis and controlled authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider IAM semantics are version/service specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","CIS cloud benchmarks","Cloud-provider official IAM documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B291","D40-B293"]} {"record_id":"D40-B293","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud network security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overly permissive cloud security group or firewall rule","question":"How should infrastructure remediate cloud network rules that expose services unnecessarily?","answer":"Restrict ingress and egress rules to the minimum required sources, destinations, ports, and protocols; remove broad internet exposure; and verify effective network policy after deployment.","objective":"Reduce unauthorized network reachability between workloads and from external networks.","requirements":["Inventory required communication paths.","Restrict source and destination ranges.","Restrict ports and protocols.","Remove stale rules.","Test effective reachability."],"constraints":["Distributed applications may require dynamic service communication.","Cloud network policies can interact across multiple layers."],"preconditions":["A cloud security group, firewall, network ACL, or equivalent rule is broader than required."],"input":"Network policy and service communication requirements.","output":"Only authorized network flows are permitted.","expected_behavior":"Unapproved network paths are blocked.","failure_mode":"A service becomes reachable from an unintended network or workload.","root_cause":"Broad default rules, temporary exceptions, or configuration drift remain active.","solution":"Replace broad rules with least-privilege flows and continuously detect drift.","edge_cases":["IPv6 exposure.","Load balancers.","Ephemeral ports.","Cross-region traffic.","Service-to-service identity policies."],"security_implications":"Network exposure can enable exploitation, lateral movement, and data exfiltration.","performance_implications":"Overly restrictive rules can cause connection failures; controlled connectivity testing is required before rollout.","testing":{"method":"Perform authorized and unauthorized connectivity tests from relevant network zones.","expected_result":"Required flows succeed and prohibited flows fail.","verification":"Review effective network policy and observe controlled traffic results.","languages":["Python","Go","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider and networking-stack dependent.","provenance":["CIS cloud benchmarks","NIST SP 800-41","Cloud-provider official network security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B292","D40-B294"]} {"record_id":"D40-B294","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud workload identity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Static cloud credentials in application configuration","question":"How should cloud applications remediate long-lived access keys embedded in configuration?","answer":"Replace static long-lived credentials with short-lived workload identity mechanisms where supported, restrict permissions to the workload's needs, rotate exposed keys, and remove credentials from source and deployment artifacts.","objective":"Reduce credential theft and long-term unauthorized cloud access.","requirements":["Use workload identity or short-lived credentials where supported.","Remove static secrets from code and configuration.","Rotate credentials that may have been exposed.","Apply least-privilege permissions.","Audit credential use."],"constraints":["Legacy systems may not support workload identity.","External integrations may still require credentials."],"preconditions":["Application configuration contains reusable cloud access keys.",""],"input":"Cloud workload authentication configuration.","output":"Application authenticates using a controlled identity mechanism with bounded credential lifetime.","expected_behavior":"Compromising application configuration does not yield reusable long-lived cloud credentials.","failure_mode":"Attackers extract static credentials and use them outside the workload.","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle is decoupled from workload identity and deployment lifecycle.","solution":"Adopt workload identity or short-lived credentials and remove static keys from artifacts.","edge_cases":["Cross-account access.","Local development credentials.","CI/CD identities.","Credential caching."],"security_implications":"Long-lived credentials increase persistence and blast radius after theft.","performance_implications":"Token acquisition can add startup or refresh overhead; caching must respect expiration and revocation semantics.","testing":{"method":"Inspect deployment artifacts and verify credential expiration and identity bindings.","expected_result":"No reusable long-lived credential is exposed through application configuration.","verification":"Review effective workload identity and attempt controlled access after credential expiration.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider identity mechanism dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63B","Cloud-provider official workload identity documentation","OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B144","D40-B292"]} {"record_id":"D40-B295","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Cloud configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security-sensitive configuration stored as plaintext","question":"How should cloud infrastructure remediate plaintext security credentials or sensitive configuration in IaC repositories?","answer":"Move secrets and sensitive values into an approved secret-management system, reference them through runtime or deployment mechanisms that avoid source exposure, rotate any compromised values, and enforce secret-detection controls in version control and CI.","objective":"Prevent repository and build-system compromise from exposing operational credentials.","requirements":["Remove active secrets from source control.","Use managed secret storage or equivalent protected mechanisms.","Rotate exposed credentials.","Scan commits and artifacts.","Restrict secret access by workload identity."],"constraints":["Historical commits may retain removed secrets.","Some configuration values are sensitive even when not technically passwords."],"preconditions":["Infrastructure-as-code or deployment configuration contains plaintext secrets."],"input":"Repository and deployment configuration containing sensitive values.","output":"Configuration references protected secret sources rather than storing active credentials in source.","expected_behavior":"Source repository access does not grant access to active secrets.","failure_mode":"Credentials are recovered from current or historical repository content.","root_cause":"Configuration and secret storage responsibilities are mixed.","solution":"Externalize secrets, rotate exposed values, and add preventive repository/CI controls.","edge_cases":["Git history.","Forks and mirrors.","CI logs.","Rendered manifests.","State files."],"security_implications":"Repository compromise can become cloud or production compromise when secrets are stored in configuration.","performance_implications":"Secret retrieval may add deployment/runtime overhead; exact impact is environment-specific.","testing":{"method":"Scan repository history, build artifacts, state files, and deployment outputs for test credentials and validate secret injection.","expected_result":"No active credential is exposed through source or generated configuration.","verification":"Review secret-manager access logs and verify rotation invalidates previously exposed credentials.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"IaC and secret-management platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-57","CIS cloud benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B144","D40-B294"]} {"record_id":"D40-B296","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software supply chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Dependency confusion remediation","question":"How should build systems remediate dependency confusion risk?","answer":"Use explicitly configured trusted package sources, scoped package names where supported, dependency lockfiles, internal namespace controls, and repository policies that prevent untrusted public packages from satisfying private dependency names.","objective":"Prevent attackers from publishing higher-priority or similarly named packages that are accidentally selected during builds.","requirements":["Define trusted package registries.","Reserve or scope internal dependency namespaces.","Lock dependency versions and sources where supported.","Review package provenance before adoption.","Prevent unintended registry fallback."],"constraints":["Package managers differ in source-priority and namespace behavior.","Some builds intentionally combine public and private dependencies."],"preconditions":["The project consumes dependencies from multiple package repositories or uses private package names.",""],"input":"Dependency declaration and package-manager source configuration.","output":"The resolver selects only intended packages from approved sources.","expected_behavior":"A public package cannot silently satisfy an internal dependency identifier.","failure_mode":"Build resolves a malicious package with the same or similar dependency name.","root_cause":"Package source trust and namespace boundaries are ambiguous.","solution":"Configure trusted sources and namespaces explicitly and validate resolved dependency provenance.","edge_cases":["Scoped versus unscoped packages.","Registry outages.","Mirror fallback.","Transitive dependencies.","Developer-local configuration differences."],"security_implications":"Dependency confusion can introduce malicious code into build and deployment pipelines.","performance_implications":"Private registry resolution and provenance checks can add build latency but improve determinism.","testing":{"method":"Use controlled package-resolution tests with conflicting package names across trusted and untrusted sources.","expected_result":"Only the intended trusted dependency is resolved.","verification":"Inspect resolver configuration and generated dependency metadata.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Package-manager specific and version sensitive.","provenance":["OWASP Dependency-Check guidance","NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B297"]} {"record_id":"D40-B297","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software supply chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Typosquatting dependency remediation","question":"How should projects remediate accidental installation of typosquatted packages?","answer":"Use dependency review, approved package registries, exact dependency declarations, lockfiles, provenance checks, and automated detection for suspicious package names and newly introduced dependencies.","objective":"Reduce the probability that a spelling mistake or deceptive package name introduces malicious code.","requirements":["Review new dependencies.","Prefer known trusted sources.","Lock resolved versions.","Validate package metadata and provenance where available.","Require review for dependency changes."],"constraints":["Package registries contain legitimate similarly named projects.","Automated similarity detection is heuristic and requires human or policy review."],"preconditions":["Developers can add dependencies directly from public registries."],"input":"New dependency declaration.","output":"Dependency is accepted only after source, identity, and provenance checks.","expected_behavior":"Suspicious package additions are blocked or escalated for review.","failure_mode":"A malicious lookalike package is added to the build.","root_cause":"Dependency identity is trusted based only on name and successful installation.","solution":"Introduce dependency review and provenance controls and restrict unreviewed package additions.","edge_cases":["Renamed packages.","Abandoned legitimate packages.","Private/public name overlap.","Transitive typosquatting."],"security_implications":"A malicious package executes with build or runtime privileges and can compromise source, secrets, or production artifacts.","performance_implications":"Review and scanning increase build governance overhead; automated caching can reduce repeated metadata checks.","testing":{"method":"Introduce controlled suspicious dependency names into CI policy tests.","expected_result":"Policy flags or blocks the dependency before production use.","verification":"Review package source, maintainer identity where available, provenance metadata, and resolved artifact digest.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Registry/package-manager dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance","NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B296","D40-B298"]} {"record_id":"D40-B298","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Build pipeline security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Malicious dependency install or build script","question":"How should build systems remediate untrusted dependency installation scripts?","answer":"Restrict build-script capabilities where package managers permit it, isolate builds, review dependencies and lifecycle scripts, minimize build secrets, and require trusted provenance before executing unreviewed build code.","objective":"Limit the ability of malicious packages to execute arbitrary code during dependency installation or compilation.","requirements":["Identify package lifecycle scripts and build hooks.","Run builds in isolated environments.","Do not expose unnecessary secrets to dependency installation.","Use trusted provenance and review policies.","Restrict network and filesystem access where feasible."],"constraints":["Some legitimate packages require native compilation or install scripts.","Build isolation capabilities vary by package manager and CI environment."],"preconditions":["Dependencies can execute arbitrary installation or build-time code."],"input":"Dependency package and build environment.","output":"Dependency installation occurs within a constrained, auditable build boundary.","expected_behavior":"A malicious dependency cannot directly access unrelated production credentials or infrastructure.","failure_mode":"Dependency installation executes attacker-controlled code with broad CI privileges.","root_cause":"Build environment grants excessive network, filesystem, credential, or identity access.","solution":"Isolate builds, minimize privileges and secrets, review lifecycle scripts, and validate dependency provenance.","edge_cases":["Native extensions.","Private registries.","Network-required builds.","Build caches.","Compiler toolchains."],"security_implications":"Build-time compromise can steal signing credentials, source code, tokens, or poison released artifacts.","performance_implications":"Sandboxing and isolated builds can increase build duration and resource consumption.","testing":{"method":"Run controlled build jobs with restricted credentials, filesystem, and network access and inspect attempted accesses.","expected_result":"Build succeeds with required capabilities while unauthorized access is denied.","verification":"Review CI identity permissions, sandbox configuration, and build logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Package manager and CI platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA","OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B296","D40-B299"]} {"record_id":"D40-B299","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Build provenance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsigned or unverifiable build artifacts","question":"How should organizations remediate deployment of artifacts whose origin and integrity cannot be verified?","answer":"Generate provenance during controlled builds, sign or otherwise authenticate release artifacts using protected signing infrastructure, verify signatures and provenance before deployment, and prevent unverified artifacts from entering trusted environments.","objective":"Ensure deployed software can be traced to an authorized build process and its integrity can be checked.","requirements":["Define trusted build identities.","Generate provenance metadata.","Protect signing credentials.","Verify artifact signatures and provenance at deployment.","Record verification results."],"constraints":["Provenance formats and signing systems vary.","A valid signature alone does not establish that the build process was secure."],"preconditions":["Artifacts are built and deployed across a supply chain with insufficient origin or integrity verification."],"input":"Build artifact, signature, and provenance metadata.","output":"Deployment accepts only artifacts satisfying the organization's authenticity and provenance policy.","expected_behavior":"Tampered or unauthorized artifacts fail deployment verification.","failure_mode":"An attacker substitutes or modifies an artifact without detection.","root_cause":"Artifact identity, integrity, and build provenance are not enforced at the deployment boundary.","solution":"Adopt authenticated artifact signing and provenance verification with protected build identities and deployment policy enforcement.","edge_cases":["Key rotation.","Reproducible builds.","Emergency releases.","Third-party artifacts.","Multi-stage promotion."],"security_implications":"Artifact verification reduces supply-chain substitution and tampering risk but depends on protecting signing and build infrastructure.","performance_implications":"Signature and provenance verification adds deployment-time computation that is typically bounded by artifact count and metadata size.","testing":{"method":"Attempt deployment of valid, tampered, unsigned, incorrectly signed, and provenance-invalid artifacts.","expected_result":"Only policy-compliant artifacts are admitted.","verification":"Validate signatures, provenance predicates, build identity, and deployment enforcement independently.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Supply-chain tooling and deployment platform dependent.","provenance":["SLSA","in-toto","Sigstore documentation","NIST SSDF SP 800-218"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B296","D40-B298","D40-B300"]} {"record_id":"D40-B300","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software supply chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Dependency and artifact remediation closure","question":"How should a security remediation workflow verify that a dependency or build-chain vulnerability is actually closed?","answer":"Verify the dependency graph, resolved artifact versions and digests, build inputs, provenance, deployed artifact identity, and runtime deployment state. A source-level version change alone is insufficient evidence of remediation.","objective":"Prevent false closure when vulnerable dependencies remain in transitive graphs, caches, artifacts, or deployed environments.","requirements":["Recompute the resolved dependency graph.","Verify vulnerable versions are absent from deployed artifacts.","Confirm build and artifact provenance.","Verify deployed artifact identity.","Run relevant regression/security tests."],"constraints":["Vulnerability scanners can produce false positives or miss unsupported package formats.","Runtime images may differ from source dependency manifests."],"preconditions":["A dependency or software supply-chain vulnerability has been reported and remediation has been applied."],"input":"Remediated source tree, dependency resolution, build artifacts, and deployment state.","output":"Evidence showing whether the vulnerable component is absent or otherwise mitigated across the actual delivery path.","expected_behavior":"The vulnerable artifact is no longer reachable in the deployed software or the documented compensating control demonstrably prevents exploitation.","failure_mode":"Ticket is marked fixed while vulnerable transitive dependency or stale artifact remains deployed.","root_cause":"Verification stops at source modification rather than tracing the complete software supply chain.","solution":"Perform source→resolution→build→artifact→deployment verification and retain evidence with limitations.","edge_cases":["Transitive dependencies.","Container image layers.","Build caches.","Multiple deployment environments.","Emergency hotfix branches."],"security_implications":"Incomplete closure verification leaves known vulnerabilities exploitable despite apparent remediation.","performance_implications":"Full dependency and artifact verification increases CI/CD work but reduces repeated incident and remediation uncertainty.","testing":{"method":"Compare vulnerable-component identifiers against resolved dependency graphs, image contents, artifact manifests, and deployed versions; run regression tests.","expected_result":"No vulnerable artifact remains in the verified deployment scope, or the residual risk is explicitly documented.","verification":"Require evidence from each supply-chain stage and record unverified environments or artifacts separately.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Toolchain and dependency ecosystem dependent.","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA","OWASP Dependency Management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B299"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B301","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overprivileged CI/CD runner","question":"How should a CI/CD pipeline remediate runners with excessive permissions?","answer":"Assign each runner the minimum identity, filesystem, network, and repository permissions required for its jobs; isolate untrusted workloads; and prevent build jobs from inheriting production credentials unless explicitly required.","objective":"Reduce the impact of a compromised build job or malicious repository change.","requirements":["Use least-privilege runner identities.","Separate trusted and untrusted workloads.","Restrict network and filesystem access.","Avoid exposing production credentials to ordinary builds.","Rotate ephemeral credentials."],"constraints":["Some builds require privileged toolchains or deployment access.","Runner isolation depends on the CI platform and execution model."],"preconditions":["CI jobs execute with broad credentials or host-level access."],"input":"CI runner configuration and job permissions.","output":"Each job executes within a bounded trust and authorization scope.","expected_behavior":"A compromised build job cannot directly access unrelated repositories, production systems, or high-privilege credentials.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one build job provides broad organizational access.","root_cause":"Runner permissions are broader than individual job requirements.","solution":"Use ephemeral or isolated runners, scoped identities, minimal secrets, and separate deployment stages.","edge_cases":["Pull-request builds from untrusted contributors.","Self-hosted runners.","Privileged container builds.","Forked repositories."],"security_implications":"CI runners are high-value supply-chain trust boundaries because jobs can execute arbitrary code.","performance_implications":"Isolation can increase provisioning and startup overhead; measure runner lifecycle costs.","testing":{"method":"Execute controlled untrusted jobs and attempt access to protected resources.","expected_result":"Unauthorized resources remain inaccessible."},"verification":"Inspect effective runner identity, mounts, network access, and injected secrets.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"CI/CD platform and runner implementation dependent.","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA","CIS CI/CD security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B298","D40-B299"]} {"record_id":"D40-B302","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secrets exposed to untrusted pull-request builds","question":"How should CI systems remediate secret exposure to untrusted pull-request code?","answer":"Do not expose privileged secrets to jobs that can execute attacker-controlled repository changes. Separate validation jobs from trusted deployment workflows and require controlled approval or protected branches before sensitive credentials become available.","objective":"Prevent repository contributors from using CI execution to exfiltrate deployment or infrastructure credentials.","requirements":["Classify CI jobs by trust level.","Keep privileged secrets unavailable to untrusted code.","Use protected branches/environments for sensitive workflows.","Separate build and deployment credentials.","Audit secret access."],"constraints":["Some testing requires external service credentials.","Fork and pull-request security models vary by CI platform."],"preconditions":["Untrusted pull-request code can execute CI jobs.",""],"input":"Pull-request workflow and secret exposure policy.","output":"Untrusted code executes without access to high-impact secrets.","expected_behavior":"Pull-request jobs can validate code but cannot exfiltrate production credentials.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies workflow or test code to print or transmit secrets.","root_cause":"Secret availability is determined by repository membership rather than executed-code trust.","solution":"Remove privileged secrets from untrusted jobs and gate sensitive workflows behind protected execution contexts.","edge_cases":["Forked repositories.","Reusable workflows.","Self-hosted runners.","Workflow file changes.","Artifact poisoning."],"security_implications":"Secret exposure through CI can compromise source repositories, signing keys, cloud accounts, and production environments.","performance_implications":"Additional approval or staged workflows can increase delivery latency.","testing":{"method":"Run a controlled pull-request workflow containing secret-access attempts.","expected_result":"Sensitive credentials are unavailable to the untrusted job.","verification":"Inspect workflow context, environment variables, mounted files, and secret-provider permissions.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"CI platform specific.","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA","OWASP CI/CD Security Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B301","D40-B290"]} {"record_id":"D40-B303","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted CI artifact promotion","question":"How should deployment pipelines remediate promotion of artifacts that were not produced by an approved build?","answer":"Bind deployment to authenticated artifact identity and trusted provenance, and promote only artifacts produced by authorized workflows from approved source revisions.","objective":"Prevent attackers from bypassing build controls by directly supplying deployment artifacts.","requirements":["Authenticate artifact identity.","Record source revision and build identity.","Verify provenance before promotion.","Restrict who or what can promote artifacts.","Prevent arbitrary artifact uploads to trusted repositories."],"constraints":["Emergency release procedures may require exceptional controls.","Third-party artifacts need a separate trust policy."],"preconditions":["Deployment accepts artifacts from multiple producers or repositories."],"input":"Candidate release artifact and provenance metadata.","output":"Only policy-compliant artifacts are promoted.","expected_behavior":"An artifact created outside the trusted build process cannot enter the protected release path.","failure_mode":"Attacker uploads or substitutes an artifact directly into a deployment repository.","root_cause":"Deployment trusts artifact location rather than authenticated provenance.","solution":"Require signed identity/provenance verification and enforce promotion policy at the deployment boundary.","edge_cases":["Manual releases.","Artifact mirrors.","Rollback artifacts.","Multi-stage repositories."],"security_implications":"Artifact substitution can introduce malicious code without modifying the source repository.","performance_implications":"Verification adds deployment-time metadata and cryptographic checks.","testing":{"method":"Attempt promotion of valid, unsigned, incorrectly signed, and unauthorized artifacts.","expected_result":"Only authorized artifacts are promoted.","verification":"Inspect deployment admission logs and artifact provenance evidence.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Artifact repository and deployment platform dependent.","provenance":["SLSA","in-toto","Sigstore","NIST SSDF SP 800-218"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B299","D40-B300"]} {"record_id":"D40-B304","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Mutable build environment","question":"How should a build pipeline remediate non-reproducible or mutable build environments?","answer":"Pin build dependencies and toolchain versions where practical, define the build environment declaratively, isolate build inputs, and record environment and dependency provenance.","objective":"Reduce the possibility that uncontrolled build-environment changes alter released artifacts or introduce malicious components.","requirements":["Pin important toolchain and dependency versions.","Define build environment reproducibly.","Control external network inputs.","Record build metadata.","Review environment changes."],"constraints":["Some compilers or operating-system components have unavoidable environmental dependencies.","Full reproducibility may require platform-specific controls."],"preconditions":["Build output changes because build environments are mutable or insufficiently documented."],"input":"Source revision and build environment.","output":"Build process has controlled and traceable inputs.","expected_behavior":"Equivalent approved inputs produce sufficiently consistent and attributable artifacts according to the project's reproducibility requirement.","failure_mode":"Unreviewed toolchain or dependency changes alter released artifacts.","root_cause":"Build environment is treated as an implicit dependency.","solution":"Version and attest build inputs and isolate uncontrolled external dependencies.","edge_cases":["Compiler bootstrap.","Native system libraries.","Network package mirrors.","Time-dependent builds."],"security_implications":"Mutable build environments increase supply-chain uncertainty and can conceal malicious build changes.","performance_implications":"Reproducible builds may require additional caching or environment preparation.","testing":{"method":"Rebuild from the same source and compare artifact metadata or reproducibility criteria.","expected_result":"Outputs satisfy the defined reproducibility requirement.","verification":"Inspect build provenance and compare environment inputs across builds.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Build-system dependent.","provenance":["SLSA","NIST SSDF SP 800-218","Reproducible Builds project documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B299","D40-B300"]} {"record_id":"D40-B305","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Protected deployment approval bypass","question":"How should deployment systems remediate workflows that allow sensitive releases to bypass required authorization?","answer":"Enforce deployment approvals and policy checks at the protected deployment boundary rather than relying only on workflow conventions or developer instructions.","objective":"Ensure high-impact deployments cannot bypass mandatory governance controls through workflow modification.","requirements":["Define protected deployment environments.","Require authorized approval for sensitive releases.","Enforce policy independently of mutable workflow code where possible.","Audit approval identity and artifact identity."],"constraints":["Emergency procedures require explicitly documented exception paths.","Approval requirements differ by environment and organizational policy."],"preconditions":["A CI/CD workflow contains mandatory deployment approval requirements.",""],"input":"Deployment request, artifact identity, and approval context.","output":"Deployment occurs only when required policy conditions are satisfied.","expected_behavior":"A modified workflow cannot silently remove mandatory authorization.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies pipeline configuration to self-approve or bypass production controls.","root_cause":"Security policy exists only inside mutable build configuration.","solution":"Use protected environment controls and independent deployment policy enforcement.","edge_cases":["Emergency break-glass access.","Rollback deployments.","Automated low-risk deployments.","Multiple approvers."],"security_implications":"Approval bypass can convert source or CI compromise into unauthorized production changes.","performance_implications":"Approval gates can increase deployment latency but do not materially affect application runtime.","testing":{"method":"Attempt deployment with missing, unauthorized, or manipulated approval metadata.","expected_result":"Deployment is rejected.","verification":"Review protected-environment configuration and audit records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"CI/CD platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SSDF SP 800-218","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B301","D40-B303"]} {"record_id":"D40-B306","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Infrastructure as Code","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"IaC state file secret exposure","question":"How should infrastructure-as-code systems remediate secrets stored in state files?","answer":"Treat state as sensitive data, protect it with access controls and encryption, minimize secret persistence where supported, prevent state files from entering source control, and rotate secrets that have already been exposed.","objective":"Prevent infrastructure state from becoming an unintended credential repository.","requirements":["Restrict state access.","Encrypt state at rest and in transit where supported.","Protect state locking and backend credentials.","Avoid committing state files to source control.","Rotate exposed secrets."],"constraints":["Some IaC tools necessarily record sensitive values in state.","State backends and secret-handling semantics vary by tool."],"preconditions":["IaC state contains credentials, tokens, or other sensitive values."],"input":"IaC state and backend configuration.","output":"State is accessible only to authorized infrastructure operators and protected according to its sensitivity.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized repository or backend access does not expose active secrets.","failure_mode":"State file disclosure reveals credentials or sensitive infrastructure data.","root_cause":"State is treated as ordinary build output rather than security-sensitive infrastructure data.","solution":"Secure the backend, restrict access, enable encryption, minimize stored secrets, and rotate exposed credentials.","edge_cases":["Historical state versions.","State snapshots.","Remote backend logs.","State exports.","Team-shared local state."],"security_implications":"State compromise can reveal cloud credentials, network topology, resource identifiers, and sensitive configuration.","performance_implications":"Remote encrypted backends can add state access latency.","testing":{"method":"Inspect state storage, historical versions, access policy, and encryption configuration.","expected_result":"Unauthorized identities cannot retrieve protected state.","verification":"Perform controlled backend access tests and inspect state contents for active secrets.","languages":["Python","Go","Ruby"],"version_scope":"IaC-tool and backend-specific.","provenance":["Terraform security documentation","OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B295","D40-B307"]} {"record_id":"D40-B307","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Infrastructure as Code","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"IaC configuration drift","question":"How should infrastructure-as-code remediation address security configuration drift?","answer":"Continuously compare deployed infrastructure with the approved declarative configuration and security policy, detect unauthorized changes, and either reconcile automatically within an approved scope or escalate for controlled remediation.","objective":"Prevent manual or unauthorized infrastructure changes from silently weakening security controls.","requirements":["Define the authoritative configuration.","Detect differences between desired and effective state.","Classify security-sensitive drift.","Audit changes.","Control reconciliation authority."],"constraints":["Some resources are intentionally managed outside IaC.","Automatic reconciliation can overwrite legitimate emergency changes if policy boundaries are unclear."],"preconditions":["Infrastructure is managed declaratively but can be modified through other channels."],"input":"Desired IaC state and deployed infrastructure state.","output":"Security drift is detected and handled according to policy.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized security-relevant changes are visible and cannot persist unnoticed.","failure_mode":"Manual change weakens a firewall, IAM policy, or storage control and remains undetected.","root_cause":"No effective-state monitoring or ownership boundary exists.","solution":"Implement drift detection and explicit resource ownership with controlled reconciliation.","edge_cases":["Emergency changes.","Provider-generated fields.","Multiple IaC tools.","Imported resources."],"security_implications":"Undetected drift can reintroduce vulnerabilities after remediation.","performance_implications":"Continuous drift scans consume API and compute capacity proportional to resource inventory.","testing":{"method":"Introduce controlled security configuration drift and observe detection/reconciliation behavior.","expected_result":"Drift is detected and handled according to policy.","verification":"Compare effective cloud configuration against approved state and audit change history.","languages":["Python","Go","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"IaC/cloud platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","CIS cloud benchmarks","Infrastructure-as-code official documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B291","D40-B293"]} {"record_id":"D40-B308","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Kubernetes workload running as root","question":"How should Kubernetes workloads remediate unnecessary root execution?","answer":"Configure workloads to run as a non-root user with an explicit security context where the application permits it, and validate filesystem, port, and capability requirements before enforcement.","objective":"Reduce the impact of application compromise by limiting process privileges.","requirements":["Specify a non-root security context where feasible.","Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities.","Use appropriate filesystem permissions.","Validate required application operations under the reduced identity."],"constraints":["Some legacy applications assume root privileges.","Certain low-level workloads require specific capabilities."],"preconditions":["A Kubernetes workload runs as root without a documented requirement."],"input":"Pod security configuration.","output":"Application processes run with the minimum required operating-system privileges.","expected_behavior":"Required functionality succeeds while privileged operations fail.","failure_mode":"Application compromise provides unnecessary root-level process privileges within the container.","root_cause":"Container image or deployment assumes root for convenience.","solution":"Change image ownership, configure non-root execution, drop capabilities, and test the workload.","edge_cases":["Binding to privileged ports.","Filesystem ownership.","Init containers.","Native agents."],"security_implications":"Non-root execution reduces privilege available after container compromise but does not replace other isolation controls.","performance_implications":"Changing UID/GID and filesystem ownership can affect startup or initialization behavior.","testing":{"method":"Deploy under non-root identity and execute functional and privilege-negative tests.","expected_result":"Application works without root privileges.","verification":"Inspect effective UID, capabilities, mounts, and security context at runtime.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes and container-runtime dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes Pod Security Standards","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B286","D40-B287"]} {"record_id":"D40-B309","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Excessive Kubernetes RBAC permissions","question":"How should Kubernetes deployments remediate broad RBAC permissions?","answer":"Reduce Roles and ClusterRoles to the verbs, resources, and namespaces required by each workload, avoid unnecessary cluster-wide permissions, and review effective bindings.","objective":"Prevent compromised workloads or service accounts from escalating control over cluster resources.","requirements":["Use namespace-scoped permissions where possible.","Remove unused verbs and resources.","Avoid wildcard permissions.","Review service-account bindings.","Test denied operations."],"constraints":["Controllers may require cluster-scoped permissions for legitimate operation.","RBAC aggregation can make effective permissions broader than an individual manifest suggests."],"preconditions":["A workload service account has excessive Kubernetes permissions."],"input":"RBAC roles, bindings, and service-account identity.","output":"Workload can perform required Kubernetes API operations but not unrelated privileged actions.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized resource creation, modification, or secret access is denied.","failure_mode":"Compromised application uses service-account credentials to control unrelated cluster resources.","root_cause":"RBAC was granted broadly rather than derived from actual API requirements.","solution":"Apply least privilege, namespace scope, explicit resources/verbs, and continuous permission review.","edge_cases":["ClusterRole aggregation.","Custom resources.","Operators.","Cross-namespace access."],"security_implications":"Excessive RBAC can turn a single workload compromise into cluster-wide compromise.","performance_implications":"RBAC evaluation overhead is generally secondary to API/network costs; exact behavior is implementation-dependent.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate effective permissions and test representative allowed and denied API actions.","expected_result":"Only required operations succeed.","verification":"Use Kubernetes authorization checks and inspect all relevant bindings.","languages":["Python","Go","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes RBAC behavior is version dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes RBAC documentation","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B292","D40-B310"]} {"record_id":"D40-B310","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes secrets","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overexposed Kubernetes Secret access","question":"How should Kubernetes workloads remediate unnecessary access to Secrets?","answer":"Restrict RBAC access to only the secrets required by each workload, avoid mounting broad secret collections, separate namespaces and service accounts appropriately, and protect secret storage and transport according to cluster policy.","objective":"Limit credential exposure if a workload or service account is compromised.","requirements":["Grant secret access only to required identities.","Use namespace and resource scoping.","Avoid wildcard secret permissions.","Audit secret access.","Protect the cluster's secret storage according to platform capabilities."],"constraints":["Some controllers legitimately require broad secret access.","Kubernetes Secret objects are not equivalent to a complete external secret-management solution."],"preconditions":["A workload can read secrets unrelated to its function."],"input":"Secret objects and workload RBAC policy.","output":"Workload can access only authorized secret resources.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized secret retrieval is denied.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload enumerates and reads unrelated credentials.","root_cause":"Secret permissions are granted broadly to service accounts or controller roles.","solution":"Narrow RBAC, separate service accounts, reduce namespace scope, and integrate external secret management when appropriate.","edge_cases":["Operator controllers.","Cross-namespace references.","Secret rotation.","Projected volumes."],"security_implications":"Broad secret access can enable lateral movement and credential compromise across workloads.","performance_implications":"External secret retrieval may add synchronization or startup latency.","testing":{"method":"Attempt authorized and unauthorized Secret API reads using each workload identity.","expected_result":"Only explicitly permitted secrets are readable.","verification":"Inspect effective RBAC and audit logs for secret access.","languages":["Python","Go","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes version and secret-provider dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes Secrets documentation","Kubernetes RBAC documentation","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B309","D40-B290"]} {"record_id":"D40-B311","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes network security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing Kubernetes network isolation","question":"How should Kubernetes deployments remediate unrestricted pod-to-pod network access?","answer":"Define network policies that explicitly permit required ingress and egress flows and establish a default-deny posture where operationally appropriate.","objective":"Reduce lateral movement and unintended service exposure between workloads.","requirements":["Inventory legitimate service communication.","Define namespace and pod selectors precisely.","Control ingress and egress.","Test policy enforcement with authorized and unauthorized traffic."],"constraints":["NetworkPolicy support depends on the cluster network implementation.","A default-deny policy can break undocumented dependencies."],"preconditions":["Pods can communicate broadly without explicit network authorization.",""],"input":"Cluster network topology and service communication requirements.","output":"Only policy-approved network flows are allowed.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized pod-to-pod communication fails.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload connects directly to unrelated internal services.","root_cause":"Network segmentation is implicit rather than policy-enforced.","solution":"Implement least-privilege network policies and continuously test effective connectivity.","edge_cases":["DNS access.","Ingress controllers.","Egress gateways.","Host-network pods.","Cross-namespace communication."],"security_implications":"Network isolation limits lateral movement and reduces the reachable attack surface.","performance_implications":"Network policy enforcement can add processing overhead depending on the CNI implementation.","testing":{"method":"Test required service flows and prohibited cross-workload connections.","expected_result":"Required flows succeed and unauthorized flows fail.","verification":"Inspect effective network policy and observe controlled packet flows.","languages":["Python","Go","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes CNI and NetworkPolicy implementation dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes NetworkPolicy documentation","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B293","D40-B312"]} {"record_id":"D40-B312","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes admission security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted privileged workload admission","question":"How should Kubernetes clusters remediate admission of privileged or unsafe pod configurations?","answer":"Use admission controls or policy engines to reject prohibited configurations such as privileged containers, host namespace sharing, unsafe host mounts, or disallowed capabilities before scheduling.","objective":"Prevent insecure workload configurations from reaching cluster runtime.","requirements":["Define prohibited pod security properties.","Enforce policy at admission.","Separate exceptions from default policy.","Audit policy decisions.","Test bypass attempts."],"constraints":["Some system workloads require exceptions.","Admission policy capabilities vary by Kubernetes version and policy engine."],"preconditions":["Developers can deploy pods without centralized security validation."],"input":"Pod manifest submitted to the cluster.","output":"Only policy-compliant workloads are admitted.","expected_behavior":"Prohibited security configurations are rejected before runtime.","failure_mode":"A developer or compromised deployment process schedules a privileged workload.","root_cause":"Security policy is advisory rather than enforced at admission.","solution":"Use supported Pod Security controls or an appropriate admission policy mechanism and tightly govern exceptions.","edge_cases":["System namespaces.","Emergency workloads.","Policy version changes.","Mutating webhooks."],"security_implications":"Admission enforcement creates an early security boundary that prevents known-dangerous configurations.","performance_implications":"Admission checks add API request processing overhead; policy complexity should be controlled.","testing":{"method":"Submit compliant and prohibited manifests and verify admission decisions.","expected_result":"Unsafe manifests are rejected with auditable policy reasons.","verification":"Inspect active admission configuration and attempt controlled bypass paths.","languages":["Go","Python","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes version and admission mechanism dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes Pod Security Standards","Kubernetes Admission Control documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B286","D40-B311"]} {"record_id":"D40-B313","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes control plane","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthenticated Kubernetes API exposure","question":"How should a Kubernetes control plane remediate unnecessary unauthenticated or broadly reachable API access?","answer":"Require authenticated and authorized access, restrict network reachability to the API server, disable unnecessary anonymous capabilities, and monitor administrative API activity.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized cluster control through exposed control-plane interfaces.","requirements":["Require strong authentication.","Enforce authorization.","Restrict network access to trusted administrative paths.","Disable unnecessary anonymous access.","Audit administrative actions."],"constraints":["Cluster architecture and managed Kubernetes offerings differ.","Some discovery endpoints may have special authentication semantics."],"preconditions":["The Kubernetes API is reachable from an unintended network or permits excessive unauthenticated access."],"input":"API server configuration and incoming request.","output":"Only authorized identities from permitted network contexts can perform protected operations.","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized API requests are rejected.","failure_mode":"External or compromised workloads access cluster control functions.","root_cause":"Control-plane exposure is broader than the intended administrative trust boundary.","solution":"Restrict endpoint exposure, enforce authentication/authorization, and validate effective access.","edge_cases":["Private clusters.","Bastion access.","Managed control planes.","Break-glass administration."],"security_implications":"Control-plane compromise can expose secrets, workloads, identities, and cluster infrastructure.","performance_implications":"Network restrictions can alter administrative connectivity but normally do not affect application request performance.","testing":{"method":"Attempt protected API access from authorized and unauthorized network contexts.","expected_result":"Only authorized access succeeds.","verification":"Inspect API-server configuration, network controls, authentication, and audit records.","languages":["Go","Python","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes and managed-provider dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes API authentication documentation","Kubernetes authorization documentation","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B289","D40-B309"]} {"record_id":"D40-B314","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Kubernetes control plane","subtopic":"Kubernetes audit security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient Kubernetes audit coverage","question":"How should Kubernetes environments remediate missing audit coverage for sensitive administrative actions?","answer":"Configure audit policies that capture security-relevant API activity at an appropriate level, protect audit logs from unauthorized modification, and verify that critical actions produce attributable records.","objective":"Improve detection, investigation, and accountability for control-plane activity.","requirements":["Identify security-critical API operations.","Configure appropriate audit levels.","Protect and retain audit records according to policy.","Include identity, resource, action, and outcome information where available."],"constraints":["Verbose auditing can increase storage and processing costs.","Audit policy syntax and available fields vary by Kubernetes version."],"preconditions":["Sensitive cluster operations occur without sufficient audit evidence."],"input":"Kubernetes API requests and audit policy.","output":"Security-relevant control-plane activity produces protected audit records.","expected_behavior":"Administrative actions can be attributed and investigated within the configured retention scope.","failure_mode":"Privileged changes occur without sufficient evidence for investigation.","root_cause":"Audit configuration focuses on operational troubleshooting rather than security accountability.","solution":"Define security-focused audit policy and protect audit-log transport and storage.","edge_cases":["High-volume resources.","Sensitive object contents.","Multi-cluster logging.","Log retention limits."],"security_implications":"Insufficient audit evidence increases detection and forensic uncertainty after cluster compromise.","performance_implications":"Detailed auditing consumes CPU, storage, and network resources; tune policy to security requirements.","testing":{"method":"Perform controlled administrative actions and verify corresponding audit records.","expected_result":"Required actions produce complete attributable audit evidence.","verification":"Review audit policy, emitted records, transport, and retention configuration.","languages":["Go","Python"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes audit subsystem version dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes Auditing documentation","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B148","D40-B313"]} {"record_id":"D40-B315","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Kubernetes control plane","subtopic":"Kubernetes admission integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted mutating admission webhook","question":"How should clusters remediate an admission webhook that can modify security-sensitive workloads without sufficient trust controls?","answer":"Authenticate and authorize admission webhook communication, restrict webhook scope, protect webhook deployment and configuration, and validate that mutations cannot bypass mandatory security policy.","objective":"Prevent a compromised or misconfigured admission component from silently weakening cluster security.","requirements":["Secure webhook transport.","Restrict webhook permissions and scope.","Protect webhook configuration.","Audit mutations.","Use validating policy after mutation where appropriate."],"constraints":["Admission ordering and failure behavior are Kubernetes-version and configuration dependent.","Some legitimate mutating webhooks require broad namespace coverage."],"preconditions":["A mutating admission webhook changes security-sensitive workload specifications."],"input":"Workload manifest and admission mutation chain.","output":"Mutations remain within an authorized and auditable policy boundary.","expected_behavior":"A webhook cannot silently introduce prohibited privileges or bypass final security validation.","failure_mode":"Compromised webhook injects privileged mounts, capabilities, or credentials.","root_cause":"Mutation authority is broader than the security policy boundary.","solution":"Constrain webhook permissions and follow mutation with authoritative validation where required.","edge_cases":["Webhook failure policy.","Ordering between multiple webhooks.","Certificate rotation.","Namespace exclusions."],"security_implications":"Admission webhooks operate at a high-trust control point and compromise can affect many workloads.","performance_implications":"Webhook latency directly affects admission latency and availability.","testing":{"method":"Submit manifests requiring mutation and attempt prohibited mutations or webhook compromise scenarios in a test cluster.","expected_result":"Unauthorized mutations are rejected or prevented by downstream policy.","verification":"Inspect webhook scope, TLS/authentication, RBAC, and final admitted objects.","languages":["Go","Python"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes admission architecture dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes Dynamic Admission Control documentation","Kubernetes Pod Security Standards"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B312","D40-B314"]} {"record_id":"D40-B316","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"OAuth redirect URI validation weakness","question":"How should OAuth clients remediate overly broad redirect URI validation?","answer":"Register exact redirect URIs or use narrowly defined provider-supported matching rules, and reject attacker-controlled redirect destinations. Avoid wildcard matching that allows arbitrary hosts or paths.","objective":"Prevent authorization responses or authorization codes from being redirected to attacker-controlled endpoints.","requirements":["Register trusted redirect URIs.","Validate redirect_uri against the registered client configuration.","Avoid broad wildcard patterns.","Keep environment-specific redirect registrations explicit."],"constraints":["OAuth providers differ in supported registration and matching semantics.","Mobile and native applications use different redirect mechanisms from confidential web clients."],"preconditions":["An OAuth authorization request accepts a redirect URI.",""],"input":"OAuth client identifier and redirect URI.","output":"Authorization response is delivered only to an approved redirect endpoint.","expected_behavior":"Unregistered or overly broad redirect targets are rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker manipulates redirect URI and receives authorization data.","root_cause":"Redirect validation permits arbitrary or insufficiently constrained destinations.","solution":"Use exact registration or provider-supported secure matching and remove unnecessary wildcard entries.","edge_cases":["Multiple environments.","Loopback redirects for native applications.","Custom URI schemes.","Tenant-specific domains."],"security_implications":"Redirect URI weaknesses can enable authorization-code interception and token leakage depending on the OAuth flow.","performance_implications":"Registration lookup is normally negligible.","testing":{"method":"Test exact registered URI, modified path, modified host, encoded variations, and wildcard edge cases.","expected_result":"Only approved redirect URIs are accepted.","verification":"Inspect provider registration and authorization-server behavior.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"OAuth provider and authorization-server implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 6749","RFC 8252","RFC 9700"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B264","D40-B317"]} {"record_id":"D40-B317","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing OAuth PKCE protection","question":"How should public OAuth clients remediate authorization-code interception risk?","answer":"Use PKCE with a cryptographically random code verifier and the S256 challenge method for authorization-code flows involving public clients, following the authorization server's current protocol requirements.","objective":"Bind the authorization code exchange to the client instance that initiated the authorization request.","requirements":["Generate a high-entropy code verifier.","Derive the S256 code challenge correctly.","Associate verifier and authorization state securely.","Require verifier validation during token exchange."],"constraints":["Authorization server capabilities and client type affect exact configuration.","PKCE complements rather than replaces redirect URI validation and state protections."],"preconditions":["A public or otherwise applicable OAuth client uses authorization-code flow without sufficient proof-of-possession binding."],"input":"Authorization request and subsequent token request.","output":"Authorization code can be redeemed only by the party possessing the original verifier.","expected_behavior":"An intercepted authorization code cannot be redeemed without the corresponding verifier.","failure_mode":"Attacker intercepts a code and exchanges it for tokens.","root_cause":"Authorization code is not cryptographically bound to the initiating client instance.","solution":"Enable PKCE with S256 and securely retain the verifier for the transaction.","edge_cases":["Native applications.","Browser-based clients.","Concurrent login transactions.","Verifier storage lifecycle."],"security_implications":"PKCE reduces authorization-code interception risk in applicable OAuth deployments.","performance_implications":"Challenge computation is small relative to authorization-network latency.","testing":{"method":"Attempt token exchange with the correct and incorrect verifier.","expected_result":"Only the correct verifier permits code redemption.","verification":"Inspect authorization request and token exchange parameters and server enforcement.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"OAuth implementation and authorization-server dependent.","provenance":["RFC 7636","RFC 8252","RFC 9700"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B316","D40-B318"]} {"record_id":"D40-B318","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth state security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing OAuth state binding","question":"How should OAuth clients remediate authorization responses that are not bound to the initiating browser transaction?","answer":"Generate an unpredictable state value for each authorization transaction, bind it to the user's initiating session or transaction context, and reject callbacks whose state does not match the stored value.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled authorization responses from being accepted in another user's browser session.","requirements":["Generate unpredictable state values.","Bind state to the initiating transaction.","Expire state after use or a bounded lifetime.","Reject missing or mismatched state."],"constraints":["State storage must be protected against session mix-up.","Modern protocols and libraries may provide additional transaction protections."],"preconditions":["The client uses browser-mediated OAuth authorization responses."],"input":"Authorization callback containing state.","output":"Callback is accepted only for the corresponding initiated authorization transaction.","expected_behavior":"Unsolicited or mismatched authorization responses are rejected.","failure_mode":"Attacker injects an authorization response into another user's session.","root_cause":"Callback is trusted without correlation to an initiated transaction.","solution":"Implement per-transaction state binding and validate it before processing authorization results.","edge_cases":["Multiple concurrent login tabs.","Expired sessions.","Back-button navigation.","Distributed session storage."],"security_implications":"Missing state correlation can enable login CSRF and authorization-response injection.","performance_implications":"State storage introduces minimal session or cache operations.","testing":{"method":"Initiate one transaction and submit callbacks with missing, incorrect, expired, and correct state.","expected_result":"Only the matching transaction is accepted.","verification":"Inspect state generation, storage, comparison, and single-use lifecycle.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"OAuth client implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 6749","RFC 9700","OWASP OAuth Security Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B317","D40-B319"]} {"record_id":"D40-B319","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth token handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"OAuth access token overprivilege","question":"How should applications remediate OAuth tokens with broader scopes than the client actually requires?","answer":"Request and grant only the minimum scopes required for the operation, separate read and write privileges, and enforce resource-server authorization independently of the token's presence.","objective":"Reduce the impact of token theft or misuse.","requirements":["Define minimal scopes.","Separate high-impact operations where possible.","Validate token audience and issuer.","Apply resource-level authorization after token validation."],"constraints":["Scope models vary across authorization servers.","A broad scope may represent multiple resources and require additional application authorization."],"preconditions":["OAuth tokens grant more access than the client workflow requires."],"input":"Access token and requested resource/action.","output":"Only the minimum authorized operation is permitted.","expected_behavior":"A token with insufficient scope cannot perform restricted operations.","failure_mode":"Stolen token grants broad access unrelated to its intended use.","root_cause":"Scope design treats authentication as equivalent to authorization.","solution":"Reduce scopes and enforce resource-server authorization and audience restrictions.","edge_cases":["Multi-tenant APIs.","Delegated access.","Incremental authorization.","Token exchange."],"security_implications":"Least-privilege scopes reduce token blast radius.","performance_implications":"Additional authorization checks may require metadata lookup or policy evaluation.","testing":{"method":"Test tokens with exact, missing, excessive, wrong-audience, and expired claims.","expected_result":"Only appropriately scoped and authorized requests succeed.","verification":"Inspect token validation and resource authorization independently.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"OAuth/OIDC implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 6749","RFC 9700","OAuth Security Best Current Practice"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B318","D40-B320"]} {"record_id":"D40-B320","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OIDC authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient OIDC ID token validation","question":"How should an OpenID Connect relying party remediate incomplete ID token validation?","answer":"Validate the token according to the OIDC protocol and provider configuration, including signature, issuer, audience, expiration, and required claims, and apply flow-specific checks such as nonce validation where applicable.","objective":"Prevent an application from accepting a validly signed token issued for another client, issuer, or transaction.","requirements":["Validate signature using trusted issuer keys.","Validate issuer.","Validate audience and authorized party where applicable.","Validate expiration and relevant time claims.","Validate nonce when required by the flow."],"constraints":["Exact validation requirements vary by OIDC flow and client type.","Key rotation requires current issuer metadata or equivalent trusted key distribution."],"preconditions":["The application accepts OIDC ID tokens for authentication."],"input":"OIDC ID token and authentication transaction context.","output":"Authentication succeeds only when token claims satisfy the relying-party policy.","expected_behavior":"Tokens from the wrong issuer, audience, or transaction are rejected.","failure_mode":"A token that is valid elsewhere is accepted by the wrong relying party.","root_cause":"Application validates signature but omits protocol context validation.","solution":"Use a maintained OIDC library where possible and explicitly verify required claims and transaction bindings.","edge_cases":["Key rotation.","Multiple issuers.","Clock skew.","Multi-tenant issuer configuration."],"security_implications":"Incomplete ID-token validation can enable account impersonation or cross-client token acceptance.","performance_implications":"JWKS retrieval and signature verification add authentication overhead; caching must respect key rotation.","testing":{"method":"Test valid token plus wrong issuer, audience, nonce, expiration, signature, and key cases.","expected_result":"Only policy-valid tokens authenticate.","verification":"Review validation library configuration and negative test evidence.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"OIDC library/provider dependent.","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core 1.0","RFC 8725"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B319","D40-B321"]} {"record_id":"D40-B321","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"JWT algorithm confusion","question":"How should applications remediate JWT validation that trusts the token's algorithm declaration?","answer":"Configure an explicit allowed algorithm set in the verifier and reject tokens using algorithms outside that policy. Do not derive acceptable algorithms solely from attacker-controlled token headers.","objective":"Prevent attackers from changing JWT algorithm metadata to bypass signature verification assumptions.","requirements":["Define allowed algorithms in verifier configuration.","Reject unsupported or unexpected algorithms.","Validate key type against algorithm expectations.","Do not infer trust policy from untrusted token headers."],"constraints":["Supported algorithms vary by JWT library and deployment.","Algorithm migration requires coordinated issuer and verifier changes."],"preconditions":["JWT validation accepts algorithm information directly from the token without an independent allowlist."],"input":"JWT containing header, claims, and signature.","output":"Token is accepted only when its algorithm and key type satisfy the configured policy.","expected_behavior":"Unexpected algorithms are rejected before authentication succeeds.","failure_mode":"Verifier interprets attacker-controlled algorithm metadata in a way that weakens signature verification.","root_cause":"Token metadata is treated as trusted configuration.","solution":"Configure an explicit algorithm policy and test negative algorithm cases.","edge_cases":["Key rotation.","Multiple signing algorithms.","Symmetric versus asymmetric keys.","Legacy tokens."],"security_implications":"Algorithm confusion can turn token forgery into authentication bypass.","performance_implications":"Algorithm allowlisting has negligible overhead compared with cryptographic verification.","testing":{"method":"Submit tokens using unsupported algorithms, altered algorithm headers, wrong key types, and valid configured algorithms.","expected_result":"Only explicitly permitted algorithm/key combinations are accepted.","verification":"Inspect verifier configuration and negative authentication tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"JWT library dependent.","provenance":["RFC 8725","OWASP JSON Web Token Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B320","D40-B322"]} {"record_id":"D40-B322","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"JWT issuer and audience validation failure","question":"How should services remediate JWT validation that checks signatures but not issuer or audience?","answer":"Validate issuer and audience against service-specific trusted configuration in addition to signature and temporal claims. A cryptographically valid token is not automatically valid for every service.","objective":"Prevent tokens issued for one service or security domain from being accepted by another.","requirements":["Validate trusted issuer.","Validate intended audience.","Validate expiration and relevant temporal claims.","Bind token acceptance to the service's security context."],"constraints":["Multi-tenant or multi-issuer systems need explicit issuer-selection rules.","Audience semantics vary between token profiles."],"preconditions":["A resource server accepts signed JWTs without validating issuer or audience.",""],"input":"Signed JWT and target resource server identity.","output":"Token is accepted only when issuer and audience match the resource-server policy.","expected_behavior":"Valid tokens intended for other services are rejected.","failure_mode":"A token from another application or security domain authenticates successfully.","root_cause":"Signature validity is incorrectly treated as sufficient authorization context.","solution":"Add issuer and audience validation using trusted configuration.","edge_cases":["Multiple audiences.","Array-valued audience claims.","Multi-tenant issuers.","Token exchange."],"security_implications":"Missing issuer/audience validation can enable cross-service token replay and privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"Claim comparison is negligible; issuer metadata retrieval may introduce caching considerations.","testing":{"method":"Test correct issuer/audience and tokens with altered, missing, or unrelated values.","expected_result":"Only tokens matching policy are accepted.","verification":"Review verifier configuration and negative authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"JWT profile and library dependent.","provenance":["RFC 7519","RFC 8725","OWASP JWT Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B321","D40-B323"]} {"record_id":"D40-B323","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"JWT lifetime and replay exposure","question":"How should applications remediate excessively long-lived bearer JWTs?","answer":"Use appropriately short token lifetimes for access tokens, use refresh mechanisms with secure rotation and revocation controls where applicable, and avoid placing high-value authorization decisions solely on long-lived bearer tokens.","objective":"Reduce the useful lifetime of stolen bearer credentials.","requirements":["Define token lifetime based on risk.","Use secure refresh-token controls where applicable.","Validate expiration and not-before claims.","Provide revocation or session invalidation mechanisms where required."],"constraints":["Stateless JWTs do not automatically provide server-side revocation.","Short lifetimes can increase refresh traffic."],"preconditions":["Access tokens remain valid for longer than the application's threat model permits."],"input":"Bearer token and authentication request.","output":"Token acceptance is bounded by an explicit lifetime and session policy.","expected_behavior":"Expired or otherwise invalidated credentials cannot continue authenticating indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Stolen token remains usable for an excessive period.","root_cause":"Token lifetime was selected for convenience without considering credential compromise.","solution":"Reduce access-token lifetime and implement appropriate refresh/session controls.","edge_cases":["Offline clients.","Clock skew.","Long-running jobs.","Service-to-service tokens."],"security_implications":"Shorter token lifetimes reduce exposure after token theft but do not prevent initial compromise.","performance_implications":"More frequent token refresh can increase authentication traffic.","testing":{"method":"Test expiration, clock skew boundaries, refresh rotation, and invalidated sessions.","expected_result":"Expired or invalid tokens are rejected and refresh behavior follows policy.","verification":"Inspect token claims, server validation, and refresh-session state.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Authentication architecture dependent.","provenance":["RFC 7519","RFC 9700","OWASP JWT Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B322","D40-B324"]} {"record_id":"D40-B324","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT key management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Uncontrolled JWT signing-key rotation","question":"How should services remediate JWT signing-key rotation that can cause stale trust or unsafe fallback?","answer":"Use controlled key identifiers and trusted key-distribution mechanisms, support planned overlap during rotation, remove retired keys according to policy, and never disable signature validation merely because the current key is unavailable.","objective":"Maintain authentication integrity during signing-key lifecycle changes.","requirements":["Distribute trusted verification keys securely.","Support key identifiers where appropriate.","Plan overlap between old and new keys.","Retire keys according to policy.","Fail closed when no trusted verification key is available."],"constraints":["Rotation semantics differ between self-hosted and managed identity systems.","Existing tokens may remain valid until expiration depending on policy."],"preconditions":["The issuer rotates signing keys or has multiple active verification keys.",""],"input":"JWT and available trusted verification-key set.","output":"Tokens signed by currently trusted keys are accepted according to policy.","expected_behavior":"Legitimate rotation works while unknown or retired keys are rejected.","failure_mode":"Service accepts an untrusted key or disables validation after rotation failure.","root_cause":"Key lifecycle and verifier failure behavior are not explicitly designed.","solution":"Implement trusted JWKS/key distribution, bounded overlap, and fail-closed validation.","edge_cases":["Key compromise.","Cache staleness.","Network outage during key refresh.","Emergency rotation."],"security_implications":"Poor key rotation can either reject legitimate users or create authentication bypass conditions.","performance_implications":"Key retrieval and caching affect authentication latency and availability.","testing":{"method":"Simulate normal rotation, stale key cache, retired key, unknown key ID, and emergency rotation.","expected_result":"Valid current tokens authenticate and untrusted keys fail.","verification":"Review key source trust, cache policy, rotation process, and failure handling.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"JWT/OIDC implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 7517","RFC 8725","OpenID Connect Discovery"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B321","D40-B323"]} {"record_id":"D40-B325","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT key management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted remote key retrieval in JWT validation","question":"How should JWT verifiers remediate attacker-influenced remote key retrieval?","answer":"Do not allow an untrusted token field to select an arbitrary key URL or trust domain. Configure trusted issuer metadata and key sources independently of attacker-controlled token content.","objective":"Prevent token validation from fetching attacker-controlled keys or using an unauthorized trust source.","requirements":["Use trusted issuer configuration.","Restrict key-distribution endpoints.","Validate key source identity.","Do not dynamically trust arbitrary key URLs from token headers."],"constraints":["Multi-issuer applications require controlled issuer discovery and tenant configuration.","Key retrieval may be cached."],"preconditions":["JWT verification derives remote key locations from untrusted token metadata."],"input":"JWT header and configured trust policy.","output":"Verification uses only keys from an authorized trust source.","expected_behavior":"A token cannot redirect verification to an attacker-controlled key server.","failure_mode":"Verifier fetches an attacker-controlled public key and accepts a forged token.","root_cause":"Key discovery trust boundary is controlled by untrusted token metadata.","solution":"Bind issuer and key source to trusted configuration and validate issuer before key retrieval.","edge_cases":["Multi-tenant issuer selection.","Key rotation.","SSRF through key retrieval.","Unavailable key server."],"security_implications":"Untrusted key retrieval can produce direct authentication bypass and may introduce SSRF.","performance_implications":"Trusted key caching reduces repeated network lookups but must handle rotation safely.","testing":{"method":"Submit tokens containing manipulated key-source metadata and verify no untrusted endpoint is contacted.","expected_result":"Only configured trust sources are used.","verification":"Monitor outbound requests and inspect key-resolution configuration.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"JWT/OIDC library dependent.","provenance":["RFC 8725","OWASP JWT Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B320","D40-B324"]} {"record_id":"D40-B326","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API gateway security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API gateway authentication bypass through alternate route","question":"How should API platforms remediate alternate routes that reach a backend without passing through the intended authentication gateway?","answer":"Enforce authentication and authorization at the backend trust boundary as required by the architecture, restrict direct backend reachability, and ensure every exposed route has an explicit security policy.","objective":"Prevent attackers from bypassing gateway controls by reaching backend services through alternate network paths.","requirements":["Inventory all backend ingress paths.","Restrict direct backend exposure.","Enforce required authentication at the appropriate boundary.","Test every route and ingress path."],"constraints":["Internal services may rely on service-to-service identity instead of end-user authentication.","Gateway-only controls require strong network isolation."],"preconditions":["Backend services are reachable through multiple ingress paths.",""],"input":"Request entering through gateway or alternate backend route.","output":"Every reachable path enforces the required trust policy.","expected_behavior":"An alternate route cannot bypass mandatory security controls.","failure_mode":"Attacker discovers a direct backend endpoint and bypasses gateway authentication.","root_cause":"Security policy exists only at one ingress layer while network reachability is broader.","solution":"Close unintended routes or enforce equivalent controls at every legitimate trust boundary.","edge_cases":["Health endpoints.","Internal load balancers.","Debug ports.","Service mesh ingress.","IPv6 exposure."],"security_implications":"Alternate-route bypass can invalidate authentication, rate limiting, WAF, and authorization controls.","performance_implications":"Backend authentication may add processing overhead if duplicated across layers.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate DNS, load-balancer, service, and network paths and test protected endpoints through each.","expected_result":"No unintended path bypasses required controls.","verification":"Combine network enumeration with authorization testing and deployment configuration review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Gateway and deployment architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","NIST SP 800-204"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B313","D40-B327"]} {"record_id":"D40-B327","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API gateway security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"API gateway trust of spoofable client identity headers","question":"How should APIs remediate authorization based on client identity headers that external callers can spoof?","answer":"Accept identity headers only from a trusted authenticated intermediary and strip or overwrite them at the trust boundary. Direct clients must not be able to supply authoritative identity metadata.","objective":"Prevent attackers from impersonating users or services through forged proxy-added headers.","requirements":["Define the trusted proxy boundary.","Strip client-supplied identity headers before forwarding.","Authenticate the intermediary.","Authorize based on verified identity context."],"constraints":["Multi-proxy environments require explicit header trust propagation.","Some applications legitimately expose similarly named informational headers."],"preconditions":["Backend authorization trusts headers such as authenticated-user or forwarded-identity values.",""],"input":"Incoming request with identity-related headers.","output":"Authoritative identity is derived only from a trusted authentication boundary.","expected_behavior":"Client-supplied identity headers cannot change the authenticated principal.","failure_mode":"Attacker sets an identity header and gains another user's privileges.","root_cause":"Untrusted request metadata is treated as authenticated identity.","solution":"Strip spoofable headers at ingress and inject authoritative identity only after authentication.","edge_cases":["Multiple reverse proxies.","Header casing differences.","HTTP/2 header normalization.","Direct backend access."],"security_implications":"Identity-header spoofing can become direct authentication or authorization bypass.","performance_implications":"Header normalization and filtering are low-cost.","testing":{"method":"Submit protected requests with forged identity headers through every supported ingress path.","expected_result":"Forged identity values do not affect authorization.","verification":"Inspect ingress header transformation and backend authorization inputs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"HTTP proxy and framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","CWE-290"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B263","D40-B326"]} {"record_id":"D40-B328","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"API gateway security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Rate-limit bypass through inconsistent identity keys","question":"How should API systems remediate rate limiting that can be bypassed by manipulating client identity or forwarding headers?","answer":"Choose a trusted rate-limit identity source, normalize relevant request attributes consistently, and enforce limits at the boundary where the identity is authenticated.","objective":"Prevent attackers from evading abuse controls by changing spoofable identity or network metadata.","requirements":["Define trusted client identity.","Normalize forwarding metadata.","Apply limits consistently across gateway instances.","Separate tenant/user quotas from untrusted headers."],"constraints":["NAT and shared clients complicate IP-based limits.","Distributed rate limiting requires shared state or an equivalent coordination mechanism."],"preconditions":["Rate limits depend on headers or identities that callers can influence."],"input":"Authenticated request and rate-limit key.","output":"Rate limiting is applied to the intended principal or resource.","expected_behavior":"Changing untrusted headers does not create unlimited independent quota buckets.","failure_mode":"Attacker rotates spoofable identity values to bypass rate limits.","root_cause":"Rate-limit key is derived from untrusted metadata.","solution":"Use authenticated identity or trusted gateway metadata and define explicit fallback behavior.","edge_cases":["Anonymous traffic.","Proxies.","IPv6 privacy addresses.","Multi-tenant quotas.","Distributed gateways."],"security_implications":"Rate-limit bypass can facilitate credential attacks, scraping, resource exhaustion, and API abuse.","performance_implications":"Centralized or distributed rate-limit state can add latency and storage/network overhead.","testing":{"method":"Vary client-controlled headers and identity attributes while measuring effective quota enforcement.","expected_result":"Untrusted variations do not bypass the intended quota.","verification":"Inspect key derivation and gateway enforcement across multiple instances.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Gateway/rate-limit implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","CWE-770"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B129","D40-B327"]} {"record_id":"D40-B329","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"API gateway security","subtopic":"API request normalization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security policy bypass through inconsistent request normalization","question":"How should API gateways remediate differences between gateway normalization and backend request parsing?","answer":"Standardize parsing and normalization rules across gateway and backend components, reject ambiguous representations, and test the complete request-processing chain rather than validating only one layer.","objective":"Prevent attackers from presenting a request that security controls interpret differently from the application.","requirements":["Align parsing rules.","Reject ambiguous duplicate or conflicting parameters where appropriate.","Normalize before security decisions.","Test gateway-to-backend transformations."],"constraints":["HTTP and framework parsers have compatibility differences.","Legacy APIs may depend on unusual parameter behavior."],"preconditions":["Gateway security controls and backend application parse requests differently."],"input":"Ambiguous or duplicate request representation.","output":"Gateway and backend make consistent security-relevant interpretations.","expected_behavior":"A request accepted by the gateway cannot become a materially different unauthorized request at the backend.","failure_mode":"Gateway validates one representation while backend interprets another.","root_cause":"Multiple parsing layers use inconsistent normalization rules.","solution":"Centralize or standardize parsing and reject ambiguous requests at the earliest trusted boundary.","edge_cases":["Duplicate headers.","Duplicate parameters.","Encoded paths.","Case normalization.","HTTP/2 translation."],"security_implications":"Parser differentials can bypass authentication, routing, authorization, and input-validation controls.","performance_implications":"Additional normalization can increase request-processing cost slightly.","testing":{"method":"Create differential request corpus and compare gateway and backend interpretations.","expected_result":"No tested representation produces conflicting security decisions.","verification":"Capture transformed requests at each layer and compare parsed structures.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"HTTP stack and framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","RFC 9110","RFC 9112"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B261","D40-B265"]} {"record_id":"D40-B330","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"API gateway security","subtopic":"API gateway fail-open behavior","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security gateway fail-open","question":"How should an API gateway remediate authentication or policy enforcement that fails open during dependency outages?","answer":"Define explicit fail-closed behavior for security-critical decisions when the authoritative identity or policy dependency is unavailable, while separately designing bounded availability exceptions that cannot grant unintended privilege.","objective":"Prevent dependency failure from becoming an authorization bypass.","requirements":["Identify security-critical dependencies.","Fail closed when required trust evidence is unavailable.","Bound and explicitly authorize emergency exceptions.","Audit all fallback decisions."],"constraints":["Strict fail-closed behavior can reduce availability.","Some systems can safely cache short-lived authorization data with defined freshness."],"preconditions":["Gateway policy checks fall back to allow behavior when an identity, policy, or authorization service is unavailable."],"input":"Protected request during policy dependency failure.","output":"Request is denied or handled according to an explicitly bounded safe fallback policy.","expected_behavior":"Dependency outage cannot silently convert denied requests into allowed requests.","failure_mode":"Gateway allows traffic because authentication or policy backend is unavailable.","root_cause":"Availability fallback was implemented without a security boundary.","solution":"Use fail-closed decisions or tightly bounded cached policy with explicit freshness and audit semantics.","edge_cases":["Cached authorization.","Regional outage.","Emergency access.","Network partitions."],"security_implications":"Fail-open authentication or authorization can cause systemic access-control bypass.","performance_implications":"Fail-closed behavior can reduce availability during dependency outages; bounded caching may reduce latency and improve resilience but increases policy-staleness risk.","testing":{"method":"Simulate identity/policy service failures, timeouts, stale caches, and network partitions.","expected_result":"Unauthorized requests are not admitted during dependency failure.","verification":"Inspect fallback branches and execute failure-injection tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Distributed architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53","CWE-636"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B326"]} {"record_id":"D40-B331","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Service-to-service security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthenticated internal service communication","question":"How should microservices remediate implicit trust between internal services?","answer":"Authenticate service identities over protected channels and authorize service-to-service operations explicitly instead of treating network location as proof of trust.","objective":"Prevent compromised internal workloads from impersonating or freely accessing other services.","requirements":["Establish service identity.","Use authenticated transport where required.","Authorize operations based on service identity and resource policy.","Limit network reachability."],"constraints":["Service meshes and application-level identity systems implement this differently.","Legacy internal protocols may require staged migration."],"preconditions":["Internal services accept requests based only on network location or private addressing."],"input":"Inter-service request and authenticated service identity.","output":"Only authorized services can invoke protected operations.","expected_behavior":"A compromised or unauthorized internal workload cannot impersonate another service.","failure_mode":"Attacker moves laterally using an assumed trusted internal network position.","root_cause":"Network location is treated as sufficient authentication.","solution":"Introduce service identity, authenticated transport, authorization, and network segmentation.","edge_cases":["Batch jobs.","Asynchronous queues.","Cross-cluster communication.","Service account rotation."],"security_implications":"Internal trust without identity enables lateral movement after a single workload compromise.","performance_implications":"Mutual authentication adds cryptographic and identity-resolution overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt protected service calls from authorized and unauthorized workload identities.","expected_result":"Only authorized service identities succeed.","verification":"Inspect certificate/service identity bindings and authorization policy.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Service architecture dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-204","OWASP Microservices Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B255","D40-B311"]} {"record_id":"D40-B332","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Service-to-service security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Service identity confusion across environments","question":"How should systems remediate service identities that are valid across development, staging, and production?","answer":"Use environment-specific identities, trust domains, credentials, and authorization policies so that compromise of a lower-trust environment does not automatically grant production service access.","objective":"Prevent cross-environment credential reuse and lateral movement.","requirements":["Separate service identities by environment.","Separate trust roots or authorization domains where appropriate.","Prevent staging credentials from authenticating to production.","Test cross-environment access explicitly."],"constraints":["Shared infrastructure may require controlled federation.","Environment separation is partly architectural rather than purely credential-based."],"preconditions":["The same service identity or credential is accepted across multiple environments."],"input":"Service credential and target environment.","output":"Identity is accepted only within its authorized environment scope.","expected_behavior":"Development or staging identities cannot access production resources unless explicitly authorized.","failure_mode":"Compromise of a lower environment provides production access.","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle and trust boundaries span environments unnecessarily.","solution":"Create environment-specific identities and enforce environment-aware authorization.","edge_cases":["Shared CI/CD systems.","Cross-environment testing.","Disaster recovery.","Temporary migration access."],"security_implications":"Environment identity separation limits blast radius from lower-trust environments.","performance_implications":"Additional identity management has operational overhead but little runtime cost.","testing":{"method":"Attempt cross-environment authentication and authorization with each identity class.","expected_result":"Unauthorized cross-environment access is denied.","verification":"Inspect identity trust relationships and effective authorization.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Identity platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-204"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B331","D40-B294"]} {"record_id":"D40-B333","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Service-to-service security","subtopic":"Service authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Expired service credentials accepted by backend","question":"How should service authentication remediate expired or revoked service credentials?","answer":"Validate credential lifetime and revocation status according to the identity mechanism, fail closed for expired credentials, and implement controlled credential rotation without requiring permanent trust bypasses.","objective":"Prevent stale service credentials from remaining usable indefinitely.","requirements":["Validate credential expiration.","Implement rotation before expiry.","Enforce revocation where supported or use appropriately short-lived credentials.","Monitor authentication failures during rotation."],"constraints":["Some credential formats have no online revocation mechanism.","Short-lived credentials require reliable time synchronization."],"preconditions":["Service credentials remain accepted after their intended lifetime or revocation.",""],"input":"Service credential and current validation policy.","output":"Only currently valid service credentials authenticate.","expected_behavior":"Expired or revoked credentials are rejected.","failure_mode":"Compromised or retired service credential remains usable.","root_cause":"Credential lifetime is not enforced or rotation is disconnected from validation.","solution":"Use short-lived credentials where appropriate and enforce expiration/revocation semantics.","edge_cases":["Clock skew.","Grace periods.","Rotation overlap.","Network failure during revocation lookup."],"security_implications":"Stale credentials extend attacker persistence after compromise or decommissioning.","performance_implications":"Frequent credential refresh can increase identity-service traffic.","testing":{"method":"Test valid, expired, revoked, and rotated credentials under normal and failure conditions.","expected_result":"Only policy-valid credentials authenticate.","verification":"Inspect credential claims, validator configuration, and rotation logs.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Credential mechanism dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63B","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B255","D40-B332"]} {"record_id":"D40-B334","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Service-to-service security","subtopic":"Service authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Service account privilege escalation through delegation","question":"How should service architectures remediate unrestricted credential or token delegation?","answer":"Constrain which services can delegate identity or authority, define the delegated audience and scope explicitly, and prevent a service from minting or forwarding credentials with broader privileges than it possesses.","objective":"Prevent privilege escalation through service-to-service delegation chains.","requirements":["Define delegation relationships.","Constrain audience and scope.","Authenticate the delegating service.","Prevent privilege amplification.","Audit delegation events."],"constraints":["Legitimate workflows may require delegated user or service authority.","Token exchange mechanisms differ across identity systems."],"preconditions":["Services can exchange, forward, or mint credentials on behalf of other identities."],"input":"Delegation request and source identity.","output":"Delegated authority is no broader than policy permits.","expected_behavior":"A lower-privilege service cannot obtain higher-privilege authority through delegation.","failure_mode":"Service forwards or exchanges credentials into a more privileged context.","root_cause":"Delegation policy validates possession but not authority boundaries.","solution":"Use constrained delegation with explicit audience, scope, and actor validation.","edge_cases":["Nested delegation.","User impersonation.","Cross-tenant delegation.","Token exchange."],"security_implications":"Delegation flaws can produce privilege escalation and cross-service impersonation.","performance_implications":"Delegation validation may require additional token exchange or policy checks.","testing":{"method":"Attempt authorized delegation and deliberately request broader audience, scope, or privilege.","expected_result":"Only policy-compliant delegation succeeds.","verification":"Inspect delegation claims and end-to-end authorization decisions.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Identity protocol dependent.","provenance":["RFC 8693","RFC 8707","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B319","D40-B333"]} {"record_id":"D40-B335","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Service-to-service security","subtopic":"Service authorization","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Service authorization matrix verification","question":"How should service-to-service authorization remediation be verified?","answer":"Create an explicit service-to-resource authorization matrix and test allowed, denied, cross-environment, cross-tenant, expired-identity, and delegated-access cases against the deployed policy.","objective":"Verify that service identity and authorization controls enforce the intended trust boundaries.","requirements":["Enumerate service identities.","Enumerate protected resources and operations.","Define allowed and denied relationships.","Test policy under credential rotation and failure conditions."],"constraints":["Dynamic service discovery can make the matrix change over time.","A test environment may not fully reproduce production identity topology."],"preconditions":["Service authorization has been remediated from implicit trust or broad permissions."],"input":"Service identity, target resource, operation, and authorization policy.","output":"Evidence that intended authorization relationships are enforced.","expected_behavior":"Allowed calls succeed and unauthorized calls fail consistently.","failure_mode":"Tests cover only successful paths and miss lateral or privilege-escalation paths.","root_cause":"Authorization verification is not modeled as a complete relationship matrix.","solution":"Maintain a machine-readable authorization matrix and execute positive and negative regression tests.","edge_cases":["Temporary permissions.","Break-glass roles.","Cross-region services.","Identity rotation."],"security_implications":"Negative authorization tests are necessary to demonstrate that remediation did not merely preserve access while adding authentication.","performance_implications":"Matrix testing increases integration-test volume and environment setup requirements.","testing":{"method":"Automated service-to-service authorization matrix tests.","expected_result":"All defined policy relationships match observed behavior.","verification":"Compare observed decisions with the authoritative policy matrix and retain evidence.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Service identity platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-204"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B331","D40-B334"]} {"record_id":"D40-B336","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Data access security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cross-tenant data leakage through background jobs","question":"How should multi-tenant applications remediate tenant context loss in asynchronous jobs?","answer":"Persist an explicit tenant identity with each authorized job, validate it when the job executes, and scope every data-access operation to that tenant rather than relying on ambient process or queue context.","objective":"Prevent asynchronous processing from accessing or modifying another tenant's data.","requirements":["Bind job records to tenant identity.","Authorize job execution against the tenant context.","Apply tenant filters to data operations.","Prevent tenant context from being inherited from mutable global state."],"constraints":["Shared workers may process multiple tenants.","Queue systems can deliver jobs after long delays or retries."],"preconditions":["Background jobs execute outside the original authenticated request context."],"input":"Queued job containing tenant context and operation parameters.","output":"Job affects only resources belonging to its authorized tenant.","expected_behavior":"A job cannot change tenant identity merely by modifying execution context.","failure_mode":"Worker processes a job using another tenant's context or broad database query.","root_cause":"Tenant identity is implicit rather than explicitly bound to durable job authorization.","solution":"Store tenant identity with the job and enforce tenant-scoped authorization at execution time.","edge_cases":["Retries.","Dead-letter queues.","Worker reuse.","Tenant deletion.","Cross-tenant administrative jobs."],"security_implications":"Tenant-context confusion can cause direct cross-tenant confidentiality and integrity breaches.","performance_implications":"Tenant filters and authorization checks add query or policy overhead.","testing":{"method":"Queue jobs for multiple tenants and attempt tenant-context substitution during retries and concurrent execution.","expected_result":"Each job affects only its authorized tenant.","verification":"Inspect job metadata, database queries, and authorization decisions.","languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Queue/database architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","OWASP Multitenancy guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B278"]} {"record_id":"D40-B337","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Data access security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Tenant isolation bypass through shared cache","question":"How should multi-tenant applications remediate cache entries that are not tenant-scoped?","answer":"Include tenant identity in cache keys where responses are tenant-specific, or use isolated cache namespaces. Never assume application authorization will protect data already stored in a shared cache.","objective":"Prevent one tenant from receiving another tenant's cached data.","requirements":["Define tenant-aware cache keys.","Isolate tenant cache namespaces where appropriate.","Invalidate entries on authorization or tenant changes.","Test cross-tenant cache retrieval."],"constraints":["Global shared content may legitimately use shared cache entries.","Cache invalidation becomes more complex with tenant-specific keys."],"preconditions":["Tenant-specific data is stored in a shared cache.",""],"input":"Tenant identity and cache lookup key.","output":"Cache retrieval returns only data authorized for the current tenant.","expected_behavior":"Identical application resource identifiers do not cause cross-tenant cache collisions.","failure_mode":"Tenant A primes a cache and Tenant B receives the cached value.","root_cause":"Tenant identity is absent from cache isolation semantics.","solution":"Partition cache keys or namespaces by tenant and test cache behavior independently from application authorization.","edge_cases":["Tenant aliases.","Tenant migration.","Shared public resources.","Cache invalidation after tenant deletion."],"security_implications":"Cache isolation failure can directly bypass database-level tenant authorization.","performance_implications":"Tenant partitioning can reduce cache hit rates and increase memory usage.","testing":{"method":"Prime identical cache keys under multiple tenants and compare retrieval results.","expected_result":"No tenant receives another tenant's private cache entry.","verification":"Inspect actual cache keys and perform cross-tenant integration tests.","languages":["Python","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cache implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","RFC 9111"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B272","D40-B336"]} {"record_id":"D40-B338","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Data access security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Tenant isolation bypass through shared object storage paths","question":"How should applications remediate tenant data stored under predictable or insufficiently authorized shared object paths?","answer":"Use tenant-aware authorization at the storage access boundary, generate object identifiers that do not grant authority by possession of a predictable path, and prevent clients from selecting arbitrary tenant prefixes.","objective":"Prevent cross-tenant object retrieval or modification through storage path manipulation.","requirements":["Authorize tenant ownership independently of object path.","Restrict storage credentials by tenant or service role where practical.","Do not treat unpredictable identifiers as authorization.","Validate object ownership before access."],"constraints":["Shared object stores may require common service identities.","Storage provider policies vary in granularity."],"preconditions":["Object keys encode tenant information but access control does not independently enforce ownership."],"input":"Tenant identity and requested object key.","output":"Object access is permitted only when ownership and authorization match.","expected_behavior":"Changing tenant/object identifiers cannot access another tenant's object.","failure_mode":"Attacker changes an object path or prefix and reads another tenant's data.","root_cause":"Storage path is used as an implicit authorization boundary.","solution":"Enforce tenant authorization independently and constrain storage credentials or policies where possible.","edge_cases":["Object copy operations.","Presigned URLs.","Bulk listing.","Tenant migration.","Deleted tenants."],"security_implications":"Cross-tenant object access can expose sensitive files and backups at large scale.","performance_implications":"Per-object authorization can add metadata lookups; provider-native policy controls may reduce application overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt cross-tenant object read, write, list, copy, and delete operations.","expected_result":"All unauthorized operations fail.","verification":"Inspect effective storage policies and application ownership checks.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Object-storage provider dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","Cloud-provider object storage security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B291","D40-B337"]} {"record_id":"D40-B339","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Data access security","subtopic":"Presigned URLs","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overly broad presigned URL","question":"How should applications remediate presigned URLs that provide excessive access or lifetime?","answer":"Scope presigned URLs to the minimum resource, operation, and lifetime required, use the provider's strongest available constraints, and avoid embedding reusable credentials in the URL itself.","objective":"Limit exposure if a presigned URL is leaked or forwarded.","requirements":["Restrict resource scope.","Restrict allowed operation.","Use short appropriate expiration.","Validate content type or size where supported.","Protect URL distribution."],"constraints":["Presigned URLs are bearer capabilities and may be usable by anyone who obtains them until expiration.","Some storage providers expose limited policy controls."],"preconditions":["Applications issue presigned URLs for object access or upload.",""],"input":"Requested resource and presigned URL policy.","output":"URL grants only the intended temporary capability.","expected_behavior":"Leaked URL cannot provide broader or longer access than necessary.","failure_mode":"A long-lived or broadly scoped URL grants unauthorized object access.","root_cause":"Convenience-oriented URL generation ignores capability scope and leakage risk.","solution":"Reduce lifetime and scope and require application authorization before issuing the URL.","edge_cases":["Upload versus download.","One-time use requirements.","Large uploads.","Clock skew.","URL logging."],"security_implications":"Anyone possessing a bearer URL may use its granted capability until expiration or provider-specific revocation.","performance_implications":"Shorter URLs may increase URL regeneration frequency.","testing":{"method":"Inspect generated URL scope and test access after expiration or against alternate resources.","expected_result":"Only the intended temporary operation succeeds.","verification":"Decode provider parameters, inspect policy constraints, and execute expiry tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Cloud storage provider dependent.","provenance":["Cloud-provider official object-storage security documentation","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B338","D40-B340"]} {"record_id":"D40-B340","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Data access security","subtopic":"Object storage authorization","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Object-storage authorization regression verification","question":"How should remediation of object-storage access control be verified?","answer":"Test every supported operation across authorized, unauthorized, cross-tenant, expired-capability, and direct-storage-access scenarios rather than testing only the application's normal download path.","objective":"Demonstrate that storage authorization is enforced consistently across all access mechanisms.","requirements":["Test read, write, delete, list, copy, and metadata operations where applicable.","Test direct and application-mediated access.","Test expired and altered temporary capabilities.","Test tenant boundaries."],"constraints":["Provider capabilities vary.","Some operations are intentionally unavailable to application users."],"preconditions":["Object storage authorization has been tightened.",""],"input":"Storage authorization policy and test identities.","output":"Evidence that protected operations enforce the intended authorization boundary.","expected_behavior":"All unauthorized operations fail across tested access paths.","failure_mode":"Application path is secure but direct or alternate storage operations remain accessible.","root_cause":"Verification covers business endpoints but not underlying storage capabilities.","solution":"Build an operation-by-principal matrix and execute it against effective storage controls.","edge_cases":["Batch operations.","Versioned objects.","Copy between buckets.","Presigned capabilities.","Administrative identities."],"security_implications":"Storage-layer bypasses can defeat otherwise correct application authorization.","performance_implications":"Comprehensive storage testing increases integration-test duration and cloud API usage.","testing":{"method":"Automated authorization matrix across operations and principals.","expected_result":"Observed access matches the authoritative policy.","verification":"Compare provider access logs and test results against the expected matrix.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Provider specific.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","Cloud-provider storage security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B338","D40-B339"]} {"record_id":"D40-B341","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Database security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Application database account with excessive privileges","question":"How should applications remediate database accounts that have more privileges than the application requires?","answer":"Create database identities with narrowly scoped permissions for the application's required operations, separate migration or administrative identities from runtime identities, and prevent application accounts from owning or altering unrelated schemas.","objective":"Limit database compromise impact when an application is compromised.","requirements":["Separate runtime and administrative database identities.","Grant only required tables, views, procedures, and operations.","Restrict schema ownership.","Rotate credentials appropriately."],"constraints":["Some ORMs or migration systems require elevated permissions during deployment.","Database authorization models differ by engine."],"preconditions":["Application runtime connects with a highly privileged database account.",""],"input":"Database identity and required application operations.","output":"Runtime account can perform required operations but not unrelated administrative actions.","expected_behavior":"Compromised application cannot freely alter database structure or unrelated data.","failure_mode":"SQL injection or application compromise yields database administration privileges.","root_cause":"Runtime and administrative permissions are combined for convenience.","solution":"Separate identities and grant least privilege using database-native authorization.","edge_cases":["Stored procedures.","Background jobs.","Read replicas.","Multi-tenant schemas.","Migrations."],"security_implications":"Database least privilege limits blast radius of application-layer compromise.","performance_implications":"Fine-grained database authorization can increase policy complexity but normally has limited query overhead.","testing":{"method":"Execute representative application operations using the runtime identity and attempt prohibited database actions.","expected_result":"Required operations succeed and administrative actions fail.","verification":"Inspect effective database grants rather than only application configuration.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Database-engine specific.","provenance":["OWASP Database Security Cheat Sheet","CWE-250"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B129","D40-B292"]} {"record_id":"D40-B342","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Database security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Database administrative interface exposure","question":"How should organizations remediate publicly reachable database administration interfaces?","answer":"Remove unnecessary public exposure, restrict administrative interfaces to trusted management networks, require strong authentication, and use encrypted administrative connections.","objective":"Prevent direct attacks against database management surfaces.","requirements":["Restrict management network access.","Require strong authentication.","Use protected transport.","Disable unused administrative listeners.","Audit administrative access."],"constraints":["Remote administration may be required.","Managed databases may expose provider-specific management endpoints."],"preconditions":["Database management interfaces are reachable from untrusted networks.",""],"input":"Database listener and network access policy.","output":"Only authorized administrators can reach management interfaces.","expected_behavior":"Internet or untrusted network clients cannot directly access administrative functions.","failure_mode":"Attackers brute-force, exploit, or misconfigure a database management interface.","root_cause":"Management-plane access shares the application's broad network exposure.","solution":"Separate management and data planes and restrict administrative access.","edge_cases":["Bastion hosts.","Private endpoints.","Cloud-managed databases.","Emergency administration."],"security_implications":"Management interface exposure increases attack surface and can bypass application-layer controls.","performance_implications":"Private management paths can add operational networking complexity.","testing":{"method":"Network-scan authorized and unauthorized segments and attempt authenticated management access.","expected_result":"Only approved administrative paths are reachable.","verification":"Inspect firewall/security-group rules and effective listener exposure.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database and deployment environment dependent.","provenance":["CIS Benchmarks","NIST SP 800-41","OWASP Database Security Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B293","D40-B341"]} {"record_id":"D40-B343","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Database security","subtopic":"Database backup security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unprotected database backups","question":"How should systems remediate database backups that are accessible without equivalent protection to the primary database?","answer":"Apply equivalent or stronger access control, encryption, retention, and monitoring to backups and snapshots, and ensure restore operations do not create an unprotected copy.","objective":"Prevent sensitive data from escaping through backup and recovery infrastructure.","requirements":["Encrypt backups according to data-protection policy.","Restrict backup access.","Protect backup metadata and keys.","Control retention and deletion.","Secure restore destinations."],"constraints":["Backups may exist in separate accounts or storage systems.","Long retention can preserve historical sensitive data after primary deletion."],"preconditions":["Database backups or snapshots contain sensitive information.",""],"input":"Backup artifact and backup-storage policy.","output":"Only authorized principals can access protected backup data.","expected_behavior":"Backup compromise does not expose plaintext or unrestricted recovery capability.","failure_mode":"An attacker accesses backups because they have weaker controls than production data.","root_cause":"Backup infrastructure is treated as secondary operational data rather than a copy of sensitive information.","solution":"Apply data classification, encryption, access control, retention, and restore security consistently.","edge_cases":["Cross-region copies.","Offline backups.","Point-in-time recovery logs.","Third-party backup providers."],"security_implications":"Backups can contain complete historical datasets and credentials and therefore represent a high-value target.","performance_implications":"Encryption and backup validation add storage and processing cost.","testing":{"method":"Attempt unauthorized backup access and perform controlled restore into a hardened environment.","expected_result":"Unauthorized access fails and restored data retains required protections.","verification":"Review backup storage policy, key access, retention, and restore configuration.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Database/storage platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-57","CIS Benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B274","D40-B344"]} {"record_id":"D40-B344","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Database security","subtopic":"Database restore security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insecure database restore environment","question":"How should recovery workflows remediate restoration of sensitive databases into insecure environments?","answer":"Apply production-equivalent access controls, network isolation, encryption, identity restrictions, and data-minimization policies to restored databases. Do not assume a recovery environment is trusted merely because it is temporary.","objective":"Prevent backup restoration from creating an alternate path to sensitive data.","requirements":["Secure restored storage.","Restrict restore identities.","Apply network controls.","Remove or mask unnecessary sensitive data where policy permits.","Expire temporary recovery environments."],"constraints":["Forensic and disaster-recovery procedures may require full-fidelity data.","Emergency recovery can create pressure for temporary exceptions."],"preconditions":["Backups are restored into environments with weaker default security controls.",""],"input":"Backup and recovery environment configuration.","output":"Restored data remains within the intended security boundary.","expected_behavior":"Recovery environment cannot be accessed by unauthorized users or networks.","failure_mode":"Sensitive production data becomes accessible through a temporary recovery system.","root_cause":"Recovery security is treated as an operational concern separate from data security.","solution":"Apply security controls before restoring sensitive data and destroy temporary resources after use.","edge_cases":["Disaster recovery sites.","Developer restores.","Forensic copies.","Cross-account recovery."],"security_implications":"Recovery paths can bypass production controls and expose complete datasets.","performance_implications":"Security hardening may increase recovery preparation time.","testing":{"method":"Execute controlled restore and verify identity, network, storage, and deletion controls.","expected_result":"Restored environment satisfies defined security policy.","verification":"Review recovery runbooks and execute periodic recovery security tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Recovery platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B343","D40-B307"]} {"record_id":"D40-B345","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Database security","subtopic":"Database connection security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unencrypted database client connections","question":"How should applications remediate plaintext database connections carrying sensitive data?","answer":"Require authenticated encrypted database transport where the threat model requires it, validate the server identity according to the database driver's trust configuration, and remove plaintext fallback.","objective":"Protect database credentials and application data from network interception or manipulation.","requirements":["Enable encrypted database transport.","Validate server identity.","Use trusted certificate authorities or explicitly configured trust anchors.","Disable plaintext fallback.","Test invalid certificate behavior."],"constraints":["Database drivers and managed services expose different TLS controls.","Internal network placement does not inherently eliminate transport threats."],"preconditions":["Database client traffic traverses networks where confidentiality or integrity requires protection."],"input":"Database connection configuration.","output":"Client connects only through an authenticated protected channel.","expected_behavior":"Plaintext or untrusted-server connections fail.","failure_mode":"Attacker intercepts database credentials or modifies traffic.","root_cause":"Encryption is disabled, certificate verification is bypassed, or fallback is permitted.","solution":"Enable TLS, verify server identity, and enforce secure transport.","edge_cases":["Certificate rotation.","Private CA.","Connection pools.","Proxies.","Database replicas."],"security_implications":"Database transport compromise can expose credentials and sensitive queries/results.","performance_implications":"TLS adds connection setup and cryptographic processing; connection pooling can reduce repeated handshakes.","testing":{"method":"Inspect negotiated transport and force invalid certificate/plaintext scenarios.","expected_result":"Only authenticated encrypted connections succeed.","verification":"Capture connection configuration and controlled network traces.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Database engine and driver dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","Database-driver official security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B251","D40-B342"]} {"record_id":"D40-B346","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Observability security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive telemetry export","question":"How should applications remediate sensitive data being exported to third-party observability systems?","answer":"Classify telemetry fields, minimize collection, redact or tokenize sensitive values before export, secure transport and storage, and restrict telemetry access independently from application access.","objective":"Prevent observability pipelines from becoming secondary channels for sensitive-data disclosure.","requirements":["Define telemetry data classification.","Remove unnecessary sensitive fields.","Apply deterministic redaction or tokenization where appropriate.","Protect exporter credentials and transport.","Restrict telemetry access."],"constraints":["Debugging can tempt teams to collect excessive data.","Third-party observability services have provider-specific retention and access models."],"preconditions":["Logs, traces, metrics, or events contain sensitive application data and are exported externally.",""],"input":"Application telemetry event.","output":"Telemetry contains only approved information and is securely transmitted and stored.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive values are absent or transformed according to policy before export.","failure_mode":"Credentials, personal data, tokens, or secrets appear in external telemetry.","root_cause":"Telemetry schema was designed for debugging without data-classification controls.","solution":"Define telemetry schemas and redaction controls before export and audit generated telemetry.","edge_cases":["Distributed traces.","Exception payloads.","Request headers.","User identifiers.","Third-party SDK instrumentation."],"security_implications":"Telemetry systems can aggregate sensitive information across many applications and tenants.","performance_implications":"Redaction and tokenization add processing overhead proportional to telemetry volume.","testing":{"method":"Generate representative requests containing test secrets and sensitive data and inspect emitted telemetry.","expected_result":"Sensitive values are absent or safely transformed.","verification":"Inspect exporter payloads rather than relying only on application-side logging configuration.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Observability stack dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-92"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B271"]} {"record_id":"D40-B347","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Observability security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Telemetry endpoint without authentication","question":"How should systems remediate exposed metrics, tracing, or debugging endpoints?","answer":"Restrict telemetry endpoints to trusted networks or authenticated identities, disable unnecessary diagnostic endpoints in production, and prevent telemetry interfaces from exposing sensitive application state.","objective":"Prevent information disclosure or control through observability interfaces.","requirements":["Restrict network exposure.","Require authentication where supported.","Disable unnecessary debug endpoints.","Review exported metrics and diagnostics for sensitive data."],"constraints":["Monitoring infrastructure needs access to telemetry endpoints.","Some metrics endpoints are intentionally unauthenticated within a protected network."],"preconditions":["Metrics, traces, profiling, or debugging endpoints are reachable outside their intended monitoring boundary.",""],"input":"Telemetry endpoint request.","output":"Only authorized monitoring or administrative principals can access protected diagnostics.","expected_behavior":"Untrusted clients cannot retrieve sensitive metrics, traces, profiles, or debug information.","failure_mode":"External attacker accesses internal telemetry or debugging information.","root_cause":"Observability endpoint was deployed with default network exposure.","solution":"Restrict endpoint reachability and apply authentication and least privilege.","edge_cases":["Service discovery.","Prometheus-style scraping.","Debug ports.","Profiling endpoints."],"security_implications":"Diagnostic endpoints can disclose topology, credentials, request data, memory contents, or internal identifiers.","performance_implications":"Authentication on high-frequency scraping endpoints can add overhead; trusted network isolation may be preferable when appropriate.","testing":{"method":"Enumerate observability endpoints from trusted and untrusted network positions.","expected_result":"Only intended monitoring identities can access protected data.","verification":"Inspect network policy and endpoint authentication configuration.","languages":["Python","Go","Java","JavaScript","TypeScript","Rust"],"version_scope":"Observability framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Information Exposure guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B346"]} {"record_id":"D40-B348","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Error handling security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Stack trace disclosure in production responses","question":"How should applications remediate production error responses that expose stack traces or internal implementation details?","answer":"Return a generic client-safe error representation while recording detailed diagnostics in protected server-side telemetry with correlation identifiers. Ensure exception details are not copied into user-visible responses.","objective":"Prevent information disclosure through error handling while preserving diagnostic capability.","requirements":["Separate client error responses from internal diagnostics.","Use stable error identifiers or correlation IDs.","Protect detailed server-side logs.","Avoid returning credentials, paths, SQL, stack traces, or internal topology."],"constraints":["Developers need enough diagnostic evidence for incident response.","API clients may depend on structured error codes."],"preconditions":["Production responses expose raw exception details.",""],"input":"Unhandled application exception.","output":"Client receives bounded safe error information and operators receive protected diagnostics.","expected_behavior":"Internal implementation details are not disclosed to untrusted clients.","failure_mode":"Stack traces expose source paths, queries, secrets, or internal architecture.","root_cause":"Debugging output is reused as the client error response.","solution":"Implement centralized exception handling with separate diagnostic and client-facing representations.","edge_cases":["API versus browser responses.","Validation errors.","Nested exception causes.","Multi-service correlation."],"security_implications":"Error disclosure can assist exploitation and expose sensitive implementation data.","performance_implications":"Structured error handling has minimal runtime overhead.","testing":{"method":"Trigger representative exceptions in production-like mode and inspect HTTP responses and logs.","expected_result":"Responses contain safe information while protected logs retain necessary diagnostics.","verification":"Review centralized exception handling and response payloads.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust","C#"],"version_scope":"Framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Error Handling Cheat Sheet","CWE-209"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B262"]} {"record_id":"D40-B349","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Security configuration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security controls disabled by environment configuration","question":"How should applications remediate production security controls that can be disabled through ordinary configuration?","answer":"Separate security-critical configuration from ordinary feature configuration, enforce safe defaults, validate configuration at startup, and prevent unauthorized runtime changes to mandatory controls.","objective":"Prevent accidental or malicious configuration changes from disabling security protections.","requirements":["Identify mandatory security controls.","Use secure defaults.","Validate configuration before startup.","Restrict configuration write access.","Audit security-sensitive configuration changes."],"constraints":["Feature flags may legitimately alter behavior but should not silently disable mandatory controls.","Emergency configuration changes require controlled procedures."],"preconditions":["Authentication, authorization, TLS, logging, or validation controls can be disabled through ordinary configuration.",""],"input":"Application configuration and deployment environment.","output":"Mandatory security controls remain enabled unless changed through an explicitly governed security process.","expected_behavior":"Invalid or insecure production configuration prevents startup or is rejected rather than silently weakening security.","failure_mode":"Configuration drift disables a critical security control.","root_cause":"Security settings are treated as optional runtime features.","solution":"Classify security-critical settings, enforce safe defaults and startup validation, and protect configuration management.","edge_cases":["Feature-flag systems.","Blue/green deployments.","Emergency overrides.","Environment variable precedence."],"security_implications":"Configuration-driven security bypass can affect every request without requiring code modification.","performance_implications":"Startup validation adds negligible initialization cost but can prevent availability if configuration is invalid.","testing":{"method":"Start the application with prohibited security configurations and attempt runtime changes.","expected_result":"Unsafe configurations are rejected or cannot disable mandatory controls.","verification":"Inspect configuration schema, startup validation, and deployment permissions.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Application configuration architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secure Configuration Cheat Sheet","CWE-16"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B305"]} {"record_id":"D40-B350","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Security remediation closure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"End-to-end security remediation evidence chain","question":"How should a security remediation be verified before it is considered closed?","answer":"Trace the issue from original security evidence through root cause, code or configuration change, dependency and deployment state, positive and negative tests, runtime verification, and residual-risk assessment. A changed source file or passing unit test alone does not establish closure.","objective":"Prevent premature security-ticket closure when the actual deployed system remains vulnerable.","requirements":["Preserve the original vulnerability evidence.","Document root cause and affected scope.","Verify the remediation in the actual delivery path.","Run relevant negative and regression tests.","Verify deployed configuration or artifact identity.","Record residual uncertainty and exceptions."],"constraints":["Some vulnerabilities require production-like integration or external-system verification.","Complete exploit reproduction may be unsafe; controlled validation can be used."],"preconditions":["A security vulnerability has been remediated and is awaiting closure.",""],"input":"Security finding, remediation change, tests, artifacts, deployment state, and verification evidence.","output":"A structured evidence chain supporting remediation status and explicitly identifying remaining uncertainty.","expected_behavior":"Closure occurs only when the relevant security claim has corresponding evidence.","failure_mode":"Finding is marked resolved because source code changed while vulnerable runtime behavior or deployment state remains.","root_cause":"Verification scope stops at implementation rather than the deployed security boundary.","solution":"Use evidence-driven closure criteria covering implementation, dependencies, configuration, deployment, testing, and runtime behavior.","edge_cases":["Multiple deployment versions.","Partial rollout.","Compensating controls.","Unverifiable third-party components.","Emergency patches."],"security_implications":"Incomplete closure allows known vulnerabilities to remain exploitable despite apparently completed remediation.","performance_implications":"End-to-end verification increases validation effort but reduces uncertainty and repeated remediation cycles.","testing":{"method":"Execute implementation, regression, negative, integration, configuration, and deployment verification appropriate to the finding.","expected_result":"All required security claims have supporting evidence or explicit unresolved limitations.","verification":"Review evidence chain from finding to deployed state and classify each claim as verified, inferred, or unknown.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"General security-remediation lifecycle principle.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SSDF SP 800-218","OWASP Vulnerability Management guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B300","D40-B307"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B351","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted pull-request code executing with privileged CI credentials","question":"How should a CI pipeline remediate execution of untrusted pull-request code with privileged credentials?","answer":"Separate untrusted validation jobs from privileged workflows, prevent untrusted code from receiving sensitive secrets or write-capable tokens, restrict permissions by job, and require trusted workflow stages for privileged operations.","objective":"Prevent repository compromise and secret exfiltration through attacker-controlled CI execution.","requirements":["Treat external pull-request code as untrusted","Use least-privilege workflow permissions","Keep secrets unavailable to untrusted jobs","Separate build/test from release or deployment operations"],"constraints":["Some CI systems expose different secret and permission behavior for forked and same-repository pull requests","Builds may require controlled access to dependencies or artifacts"],"preconditions":["CI executes code derived from pull-request content","One or more workflows have elevated permissions or secrets"],"input":"Pull-request source code and workflow configuration","output":"CI execution model where untrusted code cannot directly exercise privileged capabilities","expected_behavior":"Untrusted jobs execute with minimal permissions and no sensitive credentials; privileged actions require a separately trusted workflow boundary.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled code accesses CI secrets or write permissions.","root_cause":"Privilege and trust boundaries are not separated between untrusted code execution and privileged automation.","solution":"Apply least-privilege job permissions, isolate secrets, use trusted workflow boundaries, validate artifacts before privileged consumption, and require protected review or deployment controls for sensitive operations.","edge_cases":["Internal pull requests may still be attacker-controlled if repository write access is compromised","Generated build scripts can become executable code","Reusable workflows can unintentionally inherit excessive permissions"],"security_implications":"Reduces CI-based credential theft, repository modification, and supply-chain compromise.","performance_implications":"Additional workflow stages can increase pipeline latency but generally provide predictable isolation.","testing":"Submit controlled untrusted changes and verify that sensitive secrets and write permissions are unavailable in the untrusted execution context.","verification":"Claim: untrusted CI code cannot directly access privileged credentials. Evidence: workflow permission configuration, secret exposure tests, and successful denial of unauthorized operations.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"CI-platform behavior varies by provider; verify against the deployed CI system version and workflow model.","provenance":["GitHub Actions security hardening documentation","OWASP CI/CD Security Risks","OpenSSF CI/CD security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B301","D40-B302"]} {"record_id":"D40-B352","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"CI/CD Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secrets exposed through CI logs","question":"How should secrets accidentally printed by build or test commands be remediated?","answer":"Prevent secret values from reaching logs, mask supported secret representations, remove or rotate exposed credentials, and treat any credential that may have been disclosed as compromised until verified otherwise.","objective":"Limit credential exposure through CI telemetry and restore trust after accidental disclosure.","requirements":["Identify potentially exposed credentials","Rotate or revoke affected credentials","Prevent commands from echoing secret material","Review log retention and downstream copies"],"constraints":["Masking mechanisms are not a substitute for preventing secret disclosure","Previously stored logs may remain accessible through caches or archival systems"],"preconditions":["Sensitive values are available to CI jobs","Build output is persisted or externally observable"],"input":"CI logs containing potentially sensitive values","output":"Remediated credential exposure and reduced probability of recurrence","expected_behavior":"Credentials are not emitted into build output, and suspected exposed credentials are revoked or rotated promptly.","failure_mode":"A valid credential remains usable after appearing in CI logs.","root_cause":"Secret values were passed through commands, debug output, error messages, or environment inspection.","solution":"Remove secret-printing behavior, restrict debug output, use secret-aware logging controls, rotate affected credentials, and audit log retention and access.","edge_cases":["Partial secret values may still enable correlation","Encoded or transformed secrets can bypass simplistic masking","Third-party actions may print environment data"],"security_implications":"Exposed CI credentials can enable repository, cloud, package, or deployment compromise.","performance_implications":"Secret scanning and log filtering add modest processing overhead.","testing":"Run controlled jobs containing non-production canary secrets and verify that logs do not expose their raw or transformed values.","verification":"Claim: sensitive CI credentials are not exposed through persisted logs. Evidence: controlled logging tests, secret-scanning results, and credential rotation records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"CI and logging behavior is platform-specific; validate against deployed runners and logging integrations.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","OWASP CI/CD Security Risks","OpenSSF CI/CD security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B351","D40-B353"]} {"record_id":"D40-B353","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Build artifact substitution","question":"How should a pipeline prevent an untrusted artifact from replacing the artifact produced by a trusted build?","answer":"Bind artifacts to authenticated build provenance, use immutable or content-addressed identifiers where supported, restrict artifact publication and promotion permissions, and verify provenance before deployment or release.","objective":"Ensure deployed artifacts originate from an authorized build process.","requirements":["Authenticate artifact origin","Protect publication and promotion permissions","Verify artifact identity before consumption","Preserve provenance metadata"],"constraints":["Artifact registries differ in immutability and provenance support","Provenance is useful only when consumers actually verify it"],"preconditions":["CI produces deployable artifacts","Artifacts move between build, storage, and deployment stages"],"input":"Build artifact, digest or immutable identifier, and provenance metadata","output":"Artifact promotion decision based on authenticated origin and integrity","expected_behavior":"Only artifacts satisfying configured identity, integrity, and provenance requirements are promoted.","failure_mode":"A malicious or unrelated artifact is accepted as the legitimate build output.","root_cause":"Artifact identity is trusted from mutable names, locations, or unverified metadata.","solution":"Use cryptographic artifact digests, authenticated provenance or attestations, protected registries, and deployment-time verification.","edge_cases":["Rebuilding identical source may produce different artifacts","Mutable tags can point to a different artifact later","Provenance must cover the relevant build inputs and process"],"security_implications":"Reduces artifact substitution and build-pipeline supply-chain attacks.","performance_implications":"Signature and provenance verification add small deployment-time processing cost.","testing":"Attempt promotion using an artifact with a mismatched digest or invalid provenance and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: deployment accepts only authorized artifacts. Evidence: failed substitution tests, verified digests, and provenance validation results.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Use the artifact registry, signing, and provenance mechanisms supported by the deployed toolchain.","provenance":["SLSA","Sigstore documentation","NIST Secure Software Development Framework SP 800-218"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B353","D40-B354"]} {"record_id":"D40-B354","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Software Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Insufficient build isolation","question":"How should a build system remediate untrusted build steps that can access unrelated credentials or host resources?","answer":"Run untrusted builds in isolated environments with minimal filesystem, network, credential, and host privileges, and provide only explicitly required inputs.","objective":"Reduce the blast radius of malicious or compromised build instructions.","requirements":["Isolate build execution","Minimize host privileges","Restrict network access where practical","Do not expose unrelated secrets"],"constraints":["Some builds require network access or specialized toolchains","Isolation strength depends on the runner and container or VM boundary"],"preconditions":["Build commands execute code from source or dependencies","Runner environment contains sensitive resources"],"input":"Build workload and runner security configuration","output":"Build execution environment with bounded privileges and resource access","expected_behavior":"A compromised build step cannot access unrelated host resources or credentials beyond its declared requirements.","failure_mode":"Malicious build code pivots from the build environment into the runner or connected systems.","root_cause":"Build isolation is treated as convenience rather than a security boundary.","solution":"Use disposable isolated runners or strong sandboxing, minimize mounted resources and credentials, constrain network access, and destroy sensitive build state after completion.","edge_cases":["Container isolation may not equal VM isolation","Privileged containers can weaken the intended boundary","Toolchains may require native host capabilities"],"security_implications":"Limits lateral movement and credential theft from compromised dependencies or build scripts.","performance_implications":"Ephemeral or stronger isolation may increase startup and build costs.","testing":"Execute a controlled build that attempts unauthorized filesystem, network, and credential access and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: build execution is isolated according to the declared threat model. Evidence: isolation tests, runner configuration, and denied-access observations.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Isolation guarantees depend on the CI runner, container runtime, VM technology, and configuration.","provenance":["SLSA","OWASP CI/CD Security Risks","NIST SP 800-190"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B351","D40-B353"]} {"record_id":"D40-B355","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"CI/CD Security","subtopic":"Build Governance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unprotected privileged deployment stage","question":"How should a CI/CD pipeline remediate automatic deployment from an insufficiently trusted branch or build?","answer":"Require protected source and promotion conditions, verify artifact provenance and required checks, restrict deployment credentials to the deployment stage, and enforce approval or policy gates where the threat model requires them.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized or compromised code from reaching sensitive environments.","requirements":["Protect release sources","Verify required security and quality checks","Restrict deployment credentials","Enforce deployment policy"],"constraints":["Approval requirements should match deployment risk","Automated deployments remain possible when policy conditions are machine-verifiable"],"preconditions":["CI can deploy to a sensitive environment","Source or artifact trust can be evaluated before deployment"],"input":"Candidate release artifact and deployment policy","output":"Authorized deployment decision","expected_behavior":"Only artifacts satisfying configured branch, provenance, verification, and authorization requirements are deployed.","failure_mode":"An unauthorized or insufficiently verified artifact reaches a sensitive environment.","root_cause":"Deployment authority is granted based on source location or pipeline completion without adequate trust checks.","solution":"Implement protected release paths, artifact verification, least-privilege deployment identities, policy gates, and auditable promotion controls.","edge_cases":["Emergency changes may require a separate controlled path","A trusted branch can still contain compromised code","Approval alone does not prove artifact integrity"],"security_implications":"Reduces unauthorized deployment and software supply-chain compromise.","performance_implications":"Policy checks can add release latency proportional to their number and complexity.","testing":"Attempt deployment with a policy violation, untrusted artifact, or missing required check and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized release artifacts cannot reach the protected environment. Evidence: negative deployment tests and deployment-policy audit records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Deployment controls vary by CI/CD platform and orchestration environment.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218","SLSA","OWASP CI/CD Security Risks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B353","D40-B354"]} {"record_id":"D40-B356","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"OAuth authorization-code interception","question":"How should an OAuth client reduce the risk of authorization-code interception?","answer":"Use PKCE for authorization-code flows, generate a high-entropy code verifier, send the corresponding challenge during authorization, and verify the returned authorization code against the verifier at the token endpoint.","objective":"Bind an authorization code to the client instance that initiated the authorization request.","requirements":["Generate an unpredictable code verifier","Use an appropriate PKCE challenge method","Keep the verifier confidential until token exchange","Reject mismatched or missing verifier values"],"constraints":["Exact OAuth deployment requirements depend on client type and authorization server","PKCE does not replace redirect URI validation or client authentication where required"],"preconditions":["OAuth authorization-code flow is used","Authorization server supports PKCE"],"input":"Authorization request, code challenge, authorization code, and code verifier","output":"Token response only when the code is bound to the correct verifier","expected_behavior":"An intercepted authorization code cannot be exchanged successfully without the associated verifier.","failure_mode":"An attacker redeems a stolen authorization code.","root_cause":"Authorization codes are not cryptographically bound to the client instance performing the exchange.","solution":"Implement PKCE using a secure verifier and challenge and enforce verifier validation at the authorization server.","edge_cases":["Do not downgrade to a weaker challenge method without a documented reason","Verifier reuse weakens the intended binding","Redirect URI validation remains necessary"],"security_implications":"Mitigates authorization-code interception attacks.","performance_implications":"PKCE adds negligible computational overhead compared with the authorization flow itself.","testing":"Attempt token exchange with an incorrect verifier and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: intercepted authorization codes cannot be redeemed without the correct verifier. Evidence: negative token-exchange tests and authorization-server configuration.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"OAuth 2.0 deployments implementing RFC 7636; verify authorization-server capabilities.","provenance":["RFC 7636"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B357"]} {"record_id":"D40-B357","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"OAuth redirect URI manipulation","question":"How should an OAuth authorization server remediate unsafe redirect URI validation?","answer":"Require redirect URIs to match pre-registered values according to the authorization server's security rules, avoid broad wildcard matching, and reject unexpected schemes, hosts, paths, or parameters.","objective":"Prevent authorization responses from being redirected to attacker-controlled endpoints.","requirements":["Register valid redirect URIs","Perform strict redirect URI validation","Avoid unsafe wildcards","Reject unregistered redirect targets"],"constraints":["Native or loopback application redirects have different deployment requirements","Exact matching rules should follow the authorization server and OAuth profile"],"preconditions":["OAuth authorization responses are redirected to client-controlled endpoints"],"input":"Requested redirect URI and registered client configuration","output":"Allow or deny decision for the redirect target","expected_behavior":"Only an authorized redirect target receives the authorization response.","failure_mode":"Authorization code or tokens are sent to an attacker-controlled endpoint.","root_cause":"Redirect URI validation accepts attacker-controlled hosts, paths, schemes, or wildcard patterns.","solution":"Use exact or narrowly defined registered redirect URIs appropriate to the client type and reject unregistered targets.","edge_cases":["Open redirectors on an otherwise trusted host can undermine redirect validation","Subdomain wildcards can unintentionally include attacker-controlled hosts","URI normalization differences can create validation inconsistencies"],"security_implications":"Reduces authorization-code theft and redirect-based OAuth attacks.","performance_implications":"Strict lookup or comparison has negligible cost for normal client registration sizes.","testing":"Test altered host, scheme, path, port, encoding, and query components and verify unauthorized redirects are rejected.","verification":"Claim: only registered redirect targets are accepted. Evidence: negative URI-variation tests and authorization-server configuration review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"OAuth authorization-server behavior should conform to the applicable OAuth specification and deployment profile.","provenance":["RFC 6749","RFC 9700","OWASP OAuth 2.0 Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B356","D40-B137"]} {"record_id":"D40-B358","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OpenID Connect","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing OIDC state and nonce validation","question":"How should an OpenID Connect client remediate missing state and nonce validation?","answer":"Generate unpredictable state and nonce values for the authorization request, bind them to the initiating browser session, and reject responses whose values do not match the expected values.","objective":"Prevent authorization-response injection and replay or mix-up conditions in browser-based authentication flows.","requirements":["Generate unpredictable state values","Generate and validate nonce values when required by the OIDC flow","Bind validation values to the initiating client context","Reject mismatches"],"constraints":["State and nonce address related but distinct protocol protections","Storage and lifetime of correlation values must prevent reuse"],"preconditions":["OIDC authorization flow is used","Client receives authentication responses asynchronously"],"input":"Authorization request and returned state or nonce","output":"Validated or rejected authentication response","expected_behavior":"Only a response correlated with the initiating authentication transaction is accepted.","failure_mode":"An attacker injects or replays an authentication response into another client session.","root_cause":"The client accepts protocol responses without verifying transaction correlation.","solution":"Generate high-entropy correlation values, bind them to the authentication transaction, validate them before processing identity data, and expire them after use.","edge_cases":["Multiple concurrent login attempts require separate correlation state","Nonce validation should be tied to the ID token according to the OIDC flow","Expired values should be rejected"],"security_implications":"Reduces login CSRF, response injection, and replay-related risks.","performance_implications":"Correlation-state lookup adds minimal application overhead.","testing":"Replay a response with altered or missing state or nonce and verify authentication is rejected.","verification":"Claim: unsolicited or mismatched authentication responses are rejected. Evidence: negative protocol tests and correlation-state validation.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"OpenID Connect Core implementations; exact requirements depend on the selected flow.","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core 1.0","OWASP OAuth 2.0 Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B356","D40-B357"]} {"record_id":"D40-B359","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"JWT algorithm confusion","question":"How should a JWT verifier remediate algorithm-confusion vulnerabilities?","answer":"Configure an explicit allowlist of accepted algorithms for each trust context, bind verification keys to those algorithms, and reject tokens using unexpected or incompatible algorithms.","objective":"Prevent attackers from changing JWT algorithm metadata to bypass signature verification assumptions.","requirements":["Explicitly configure accepted algorithms","Bind keys to expected algorithms","Reject unexpected algorithm identifiers","Do not derive verification policy solely from attacker-controlled token headers"],"constraints":["JWT libraries differ in default behavior","Key types and algorithms must be compatible with the configured verification policy"],"preconditions":["Application accepts signed JWTs","JWT header fields influence verification behavior"],"input":"JWT and verifier configuration","output":"Verified or rejected token","expected_behavior":"Only tokens using the configured algorithm and compatible verification key are accepted.","failure_mode":"A token with an unexpected algorithm is accepted as valid.","root_cause":"Verification policy is inferred from untrusted token metadata or permits unsafe algorithm combinations.","solution":"Use an explicit algorithm allowlist and independently configured verification policy; reject unsupported algorithms before trust decisions.","edge_cases":["Key rotation can temporarily require multiple explicitly allowed algorithms","Different issuers may require different algorithm policies","An algorithm allowlist does not validate issuer or audience"],"security_implications":"Prevents signature-verification bypasses caused by algorithm confusion or unsafe algorithm acceptance.","performance_implications":"Explicit algorithm selection adds negligible verification overhead.","testing":"Present tokens with unsupported or mismatched algorithms and verify deterministic rejection.","verification":"Claim: unsupported JWT algorithms cannot bypass signature verification. Evidence: negative token tests and verifier configuration review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"JWT verification behavior depends on the selected JOSE library and configured algorithms.","provenance":["RFC 8725","OWASP JSON Web Token Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B359","D40-B360"]} {"record_id":"D40-B360","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"JWT Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing JWT issuer and audience validation","question":"How should a service remediate acceptance of JWTs without validating issuer and audience?","answer":"Configure the expected issuer and audience for each trust context and reject tokens whose claims do not match the service's configured expectations.","objective":"Prevent a valid token issued for another security context from being accepted by the wrong service.","requirements":["Define trusted issuer","Define expected audience","Validate required claims before authorization","Keep trust configuration separate from token-supplied assumptions"],"constraints":["Multi-tenant or multi-issuer systems require explicit trust mappings","Audience semantics must match the identity provider's token model"],"preconditions":["Service accepts bearer JWTs","Multiple token consumers, issuers, or audiences exist or may exist"],"input":"JWT claims and service trust configuration","output":"Accepted or rejected authentication context","expected_behavior":"A cryptographically valid token intended for another issuer or audience is rejected.","failure_mode":"A valid token is replayed against a service that was not its intended audience.","root_cause":"Signature validity is incorrectly treated as sufficient authorization context.","solution":"Validate issuer, audience, signature, time claims, and other required claims according to the service's trust policy.","edge_cases":["Multiple legitimate audiences require an explicit allowlist","Clock skew affects time-based claim validation","Issuer identifiers must be compared according to the identity protocol's exact semantics"],"security_implications":"Reduces token substitution and confused-trust-boundary attacks.","performance_implications":"Claim comparison is negligible relative to cryptographic signature verification.","testing":"Use correctly signed tokens with incorrect issuer or audience and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: tokens outside the service trust context cannot authenticate. Evidence: negative issuer/audience tests and configuration inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"JWT/OIDC implementation-specific; validate against the deployed identity provider and JOSE library.","provenance":["RFC 8725","OpenID Connect Core 1.0"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B358","D40-B359"]} {"record_id":"D40-B361","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"OAuth Token Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Refresh-token replay","question":"How should an authorization system remediate refresh-token replay risk?","answer":"Use refresh-token rotation or another replay-detection mechanism where appropriate, invalidate the affected token family when reuse is detected, and securely store refresh tokens on the client.","objective":"Limit the impact of stolen long-lived refresh credentials.","requirements":["Detect reuse where the deployment supports rotation","Invalidate compromised token state according to policy","Protect refresh tokens at rest and in transit","Limit token lifetime and scope appropriately"],"constraints":["Rotation semantics differ by authorization server","Public clients require different protections from confidential clients"],"preconditions":["Refresh tokens are issued","A stolen refresh token could otherwise be reused"],"input":"Refresh-token request and server-side token-family state","output":"New token or rejection with appropriate session invalidation","expected_behavior":"Reuse of an already-consumed rotated refresh token triggers the configured replay response.","failure_mode":"An attacker continues obtaining access tokens with a stolen refresh token.","root_cause":"Refresh credentials remain reusable without replay detection or appropriate invalidation.","solution":"Implement rotation and reuse detection where supported, protect token storage, and define revocation behavior for detected compromise.","edge_cases":["Concurrent legitimate refresh requests can resemble replay","Distributed authorization servers require consistent token-family state","Revocation can affect legitimate sessions"],"security_implications":"Reduces persistence of stolen refresh credentials.","performance_implications":"Token-family state tracking introduces storage and synchronization overhead.","testing":"Use a controlled refresh token twice where rotation is enabled and verify the configured reuse response.","verification":"Claim: replay of a rotated refresh token is detected and handled. Evidence: controlled reuse tests and authorization-server state inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"OAuth deployment-specific; follow the authorization server's supported refresh-token rotation model.","provenance":["RFC 9700","OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B356","D40-B362"]} {"record_id":"D40-B362","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Service Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Confused deputy between internal services","question":"How should service-to-service authorization remediate confused-deputy behavior?","answer":"Bind each request to an authenticated caller identity and intended audience, authorize the caller for the specific downstream operation, and avoid accepting caller-controlled identity or privilege claims without trusted propagation.","objective":"Prevent one authorized service from being abused to perform unauthorized actions on behalf of another principal.","requirements":["Authenticate service callers","Define downstream authorization policy","Validate intended audience","Use trusted identity propagation"],"constraints":["Delegation chains can involve multiple identities","Infrastructure authentication alone does not establish application-level authorization"],"preconditions":["A service can invoke another privileged service","Caller identity or delegated authority crosses service boundaries"],"input":"Authenticated service identity, requested operation, resource, and delegation context","output":"Allow or deny decision","expected_behavior":"The downstream service authorizes the actual trusted caller and permitted delegation context rather than blindly trusting caller-supplied identity attributes.","failure_mode":"A lower-privileged service causes a higher-privileged service to act outside the intended authority.","root_cause":"Downstream authorization trusts an ambiguous or attacker-controlled principal representation.","solution":"Use authenticated workload identities, explicit audience and scope checks, constrained delegation, and resource-level authorization at the receiving service.","edge_cases":["Service meshes can authenticate transport without proving application intent","Forwarded identity headers must be trusted only across controlled boundaries","Background jobs may need explicit delegated authority"],"security_implications":"Reduces privilege-confusion and lateral movement across service boundaries.","performance_implications":"Additional policy evaluation and identity propagation add modest request overhead.","testing":"Invoke privileged downstream operations from identities lacking the required delegated authority and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: service callers cannot obtain authority beyond their configured delegation. Evidence: negative authorization tests and identity-policy inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Applies to service-oriented architectures; exact authentication mechanisms vary.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B362"]} {"record_id":"D40-B363","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization Architecture","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Privilege propagation without scope restriction","question":"How should delegated service credentials be constrained to prevent privilege escalation?","answer":"Propagate only the minimum identity and authority required for the downstream operation, use narrowly scoped tokens or claims, and require the receiving service to enforce resource and action authorization.","objective":"Prevent delegated credentials from carrying unnecessary authority across service boundaries.","requirements":["Define delegation scope","Minimize propagated privileges","Validate resource and action permissions downstream","Prevent privilege expansion during token exchange"],"constraints":["Some workflows legitimately require multi-hop delegation","Scope semantics must remain consistent across services"],"preconditions":["Identity or authorization context is propagated between services"],"input":"Delegated credential and requested downstream action","output":"Scoped authorization decision","expected_behavior":"A downstream service receives only the authority explicitly delegated for the requested operation.","failure_mode":"A delegated token can be reused for unrelated privileged operations.","root_cause":"Credential propagation preserves broad authority instead of task-specific authority.","solution":"Use least-privilege scopes, audience restriction, constrained delegation, and downstream authorization checks.","edge_cases":["Token exchange can accidentally broaden privileges","Scope names may be syntactically valid but semantically excessive","Caching authorization decisions can outlive delegation changes"],"security_implications":"Limits lateral privilege escalation in distributed systems.","performance_implications":"Fine-grained authorization may increase policy-evaluation cost.","testing":"Attempt to use a scoped delegated credential for an unrelated resource or action and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: delegated credentials cannot perform operations outside their authorized scope. Evidence: negative cross-scope tests and policy evaluation records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Applies to OAuth-based and other delegated service-identity architectures.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B364"]} {"record_id":"D40-B364","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization Architecture","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authorization enforced only at an API gateway","question":"How should a system remediate authorization that exists only at an API gateway?","answer":"Enforce authorization at the service or resource boundary as well as any gateway layer so that direct internal access, alternate routes, and service-to-service calls cannot bypass policy.","objective":"Ensure authorization remains effective when requests do not traverse the original perimeter component.","requirements":["Define resource-level authorization","Validate caller identity at the receiving service","Do not rely solely on network location or gateway routing","Keep policy semantics consistent across entry points"],"constraints":["Central policy systems may still provide shared decision logic","Duplicate policy implementation can drift unless policy ownership is explicit"],"preconditions":["Multiple request paths can reach protected services","Gateway policy is currently treated as the primary authorization control"],"input":"Authenticated request and target resource/action","output":"Service-level authorization decision","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized requests are rejected even when they bypass the gateway.","failure_mode":"An attacker reaches an internal endpoint through an alternate path and avoids gateway authorization.","root_cause":"Security policy is attached to one network entry point rather than the protected resource.","solution":"Move authoritative enforcement to the resource-owning service, optionally using a centralized policy decision component for consistency.","edge_cases":["Health and internal management endpoints require explicit access policies","Service meshes do not automatically provide application authorization","Network segmentation is not a substitute for authorization"],"security_implications":"Reduces authorization bypass through internal paths and compromised intermediary components.","performance_implications":"Additional authorization checks may increase request latency slightly.","testing":"Call the protected service through a non-gateway route and verify the same authorization policy is enforced.","verification":"Claim: protected resources enforce authorization independently of gateway traversal. Evidence: direct-access negative tests and service authorization logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Applies to API gateways, service meshes, microservices, and layered service architectures.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B363"]} {"record_id":"D40-B365","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Authorization Policy","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Fail-open authorization on policy-service failure","question":"How should authorization systems remediate policy evaluation failures that currently allow access?","answer":"Default to denial for security-sensitive authorization decisions when the policy authority cannot provide a trustworthy decision, while explicitly defining controlled availability exceptions where the risk assessment permits them.","objective":"Prevent infrastructure or policy-service failures from becoming authorization bypasses.","requirements":["Define failure behavior explicitly","Use deny-by-default for security-sensitive decisions","Distinguish unavailable from permitted","Monitor policy-service failures"],"constraints":["Some availability-critical systems may require carefully bounded emergency behavior","Fail-closed behavior can affect service availability"],"preconditions":["Authorization depends on a remote or failure-prone policy component"],"input":"Authorization request and policy-service result","output":"Allow or deny decision","expected_behavior":"An unavailable or invalid policy decision does not silently become authorization.","failure_mode":"A policy engine outage causes protected operations to be permitted.","root_cause":"Application treats missing or ambiguous authorization results as allow.","solution":"Use explicit decision states, deny on unavailable or invalid policy results for sensitive operations, and define separately governed emergency procedures where necessary.","edge_cases":["Cached allow decisions require expiration and scope controls","Network partitions can create inconsistent authorization views","Emergency bypasses require strong auditability"],"security_implications":"Prevents policy infrastructure failure from becoming a privilege-escalation path.","performance_implications":"Remote policy checks and conservative retries can add latency.","testing":"Simulate policy-engine timeout, malformed response, and network partition and verify the configured safe failure behavior.","verification":"Claim: policy failures cannot silently grant access. Evidence: failure-injection tests and authorization decision logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Applicable to centralized or distributed authorization systems; exact availability behavior is deployment-specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B364","D40-B365"]} {"record_id":"D40-B366","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Automatic service-account token mounting","question":"How should a Kubernetes workload remediate unnecessary service-account token exposure?","answer":"Disable automatic service-account token mounting for workloads that do not need Kubernetes API credentials and grant explicit service-account permissions only where required.","objective":"Reduce credential exposure and limit workload access to the Kubernetes API.","requirements":["Identify workloads requiring API credentials","Disable automatic token mounting where unnecessary","Use dedicated service accounts for workloads that need access","Apply least-privilege RBAC"],"constraints":["Some controllers and workloads legitimately require API access","Application behavior may depend on token availability"],"preconditions":["Kubernetes workloads use service accounts","Automatic token mounting is enabled or inherited"],"input":"Pod specification and service-account requirements","output":"Workload configuration with minimized credential exposure","expected_behavior":"Only workloads requiring Kubernetes API access receive the relevant credentials.","failure_mode":"A compromised application obtains a service-account token and uses it for unnecessary API access.","root_cause":"Credentials are mounted by default without workload-specific necessity.","solution":"Set automountServiceAccountToken appropriately, use dedicated service accounts, and restrict permissions with RBAC.","edge_cases":["Namespace defaults and service-account settings can interact","Legacy applications may expect in-cluster credentials","Token availability should be tested after hardening"],"security_implications":"Reduces credential theft and lateral movement within the cluster.","performance_implications":"No meaningful runtime performance benefit or penalty is expected from disabling unnecessary token mounts.","testing":"Deploy a workload without Kubernetes API requirements and verify no service-account token is mounted.","verification":"Claim: unnecessary workloads do not receive Kubernetes API credentials. Evidence: pod inspection and denied API-access tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes service-account and projected-token behavior varies across Kubernetes versions; validate the deployed version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Service Accounts","Kubernetes official documentation: RBAC"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B367","D40-B368"]} {"record_id":"D40-B367","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes RBAC","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Wildcard Kubernetes RBAC permissions","question":"How should Kubernetes RBAC rules with broad wildcard permissions be remediated?","answer":"Replace wildcard resource, verb, or API-group permissions with the smallest explicit set required by the workload and review permissions after API changes.","objective":"Reduce cluster privilege escalation opportunities caused by excessively broad RBAC rules.","requirements":["Inventory effective permissions","Identify wildcard grants","Replace them with explicit required permissions","Retest workload functionality"],"constraints":["Kubernetes APIs evolve and new resources may be introduced","Some controllers legitimately require broad permissions but should be isolated"],"preconditions":["RBAC roles or cluster roles contain wildcard permissions","Workloads depend on those identities"],"input":"RBAC policy and observed workload operations","output":"Least-privilege RBAC configuration","expected_behavior":"The workload retains required operations but loses unrelated permissions.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload uses broad RBAC permissions to access or modify unrelated resources.","root_cause":"RBAC grants authority beyond the workload's actual operational requirements.","solution":"Use explicit verbs, resources, API groups, and resource names where practical; separate workloads by trust boundary.","edge_cases":["ResourceNames can affect authorization semantics","Subresources such as pods/log or deployments/status require explicit consideration","Cluster-wide permissions should be justified separately from namespace permissions"],"security_implications":"Reduces blast radius from compromised pods and service accounts.","performance_implications":"RBAC evaluation remains platform-managed; narrower policies primarily improve security rather than application performance.","testing":"Attempt representative allowed operations and intentionally unauthorized cluster operations using the workload identity.","verification":"Claim: workload permissions match the declared least-privilege policy. Evidence: effective-RBAC inspection and negative authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes RBAC behavior is version-dependent; validate against the deployed API server version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: RBAC Authorization","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B366","D40-B368"]} {"record_id":"D40-B368","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Admission","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted workload admission","question":"How should a Kubernetes cluster remediate admission of workloads that violate security policy?","answer":"Enforce policy at admission using supported policy engines or built-in controls appropriate to the Kubernetes version, and reject workloads that violate defined security requirements before scheduling.","objective":"Prevent unsafe workload configurations from entering the cluster.","requirements":["Define enforceable workload security policies","Apply admission controls","Provide auditable rejection reasons","Keep policy versioned and tested"],"constraints":["Admission policy capabilities differ by Kubernetes version and selected policy engine","Overly restrictive policies can break legitimate workloads"],"preconditions":["Cluster accepts workload manifests from multiple actors","Security requirements can be represented as admission rules"],"input":"Workload manifest and cluster security policy","output":"Admission decision","expected_behavior":"Non-compliant workloads are rejected before becoming active workloads.","failure_mode":"Privileged, unsafe, or non-compliant workloads enter the cluster.","root_cause":"Security policy is enforced only after deployment or through manual review.","solution":"Use admission enforcement to validate security context, host access, capabilities, images, namespaces, and other deployment-specific controls.","edge_cases":["Policy exemptions need narrow scope and explicit governance","Policy updates can invalidate previously accepted workloads","Mutation and validation policies must not create unintended privilege"],"security_implications":"Blocks classes of insecure workload configurations before runtime.","performance_implications":"Admission evaluation adds API request latency depending on policy complexity.","testing":"Submit intentionally non-compliant manifests and verify admission rejection with an actionable policy reason.","verification":"Claim: defined insecure workload classes cannot be admitted. Evidence: negative admission tests and policy evaluation results.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes admission capabilities depend on cluster version and enabled policy mechanisms.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Admission Control","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B367","D40-B369"]} {"record_id":"D40-B369","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Excessive Linux capabilities in containers","question":"How should a container workload remediate unnecessary Linux capabilities?","answer":"Drop capabilities by default where supported and add back only the narrowly required capabilities, with security policy preventing privileged escalation through capability configuration.","objective":"Reduce the kernel-level privileges available to compromised containers.","requirements":["Inventory required capabilities","Drop unnecessary capabilities","Prevent privilege escalation where appropriate","Enforce workload security policy"],"constraints":["Some applications require specific capabilities","Capability behavior depends on container runtime and Linux configuration"],"preconditions":["Container workloads run with elevated or default capabilities","Application functionality can be tested after capability reduction"],"input":"Container security context and application requirements","output":"Minimal capability set","expected_behavior":"The workload operates with only capabilities required for its documented behavior.","failure_mode":"A compromised container uses unnecessary capabilities to escape isolation or access protected resources.","root_cause":"Container privileges are inherited broadly instead of being minimized.","solution":"Use least-privilege capability sets, explicitly drop unused capabilities, and combine with non-root execution and other isolation controls.","edge_cases":["Removing a capability can break legitimate networking or filesystem operations","Capabilities are not equivalent to full container isolation","Host-level privileges can override application-level restrictions"],"security_implications":"Reduces privilege available to an attacker after container compromise.","performance_implications":"Capability reduction generally has negligible application performance impact.","testing":"Run functional tests after removing each non-required capability and attempt controlled privileged operations.","verification":"Claim: the container has no unnecessary Linux capabilities. Evidence: runtime security-context inspection and negative privilege tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Linux capability and container-runtime behavior varies by kernel and runtime version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Security Context","Linux capabilities documentation","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B368","D40-B370"]} {"record_id":"D40-B370","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Writable hostPath volume exposure","question":"How should a Kubernetes workload remediate unnecessary hostPath access?","answer":"Remove hostPath mounts when possible; when unavoidable, restrict the host path, mount read-only where feasible, isolate the workload, and enforce admission policy against arbitrary host filesystem access.","objective":"Prevent compromised workloads from reading or modifying sensitive node filesystem content.","requirements":["Identify why hostPath is required","Use narrower paths","Prefer read-only mounts","Restrict workloads permitted to use hostPath"],"constraints":["Some node-level agents legitimately require host filesystem access","Read-only mounts still expose potentially sensitive information"],"preconditions":["Workload uses hostPath","Node filesystem contains sensitive or security-critical data"],"input":"Pod volume configuration and application requirements","output":"Minimized host filesystem exposure","expected_behavior":"The workload has no unnecessary host filesystem access and cannot modify protected node data through the configured mount.","failure_mode":"Compromised pod reads or modifies sensitive node files.","root_cause":"Host filesystem access is granted broadly without a strict operational requirement.","solution":"Replace hostPath with safer storage mechanisms where possible; otherwise restrict path, permissions, read/write mode, and workload admission.","edge_cases":["A read-only hostPath can still enable credential or configuration disclosure","Symlink behavior must be considered when exposing host directories","Node-level agents need separate trust treatment"],"security_implications":"Reduces node compromise and container-escape opportunities.","performance_implications":"Alternative storage mechanisms may have different I/O characteristics; security hardening itself is not a benchmark claim.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized host filesystem reads and writes from the workload and verify access is blocked.","verification":"Claim: workload cannot access unauthorized node filesystem content. Evidence: mount inspection and negative read/write tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes and container-runtime behavior varies by version and host configuration.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Volumes","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B369","D40-B371"]} {"record_id":"D40-B371","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Runtime Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted host namespace sharing","question":"How should a Kubernetes workload remediate unnecessary host namespace sharing?","answer":"Disable hostNetwork, hostPID, and hostIPC unless the workload has a documented requirement, and enforce policy restricting these settings to trusted workloads.","objective":"Reduce direct exposure to node networking, process, and IPC namespaces.","requirements":["Document any host namespace requirement","Disable unnecessary host namespace sharing","Restrict exceptions through admission policy","Use dedicated trusted identities for exceptions"],"constraints":["Node agents may require host namespace access","Namespace sharing can affect networking and process visibility"],"preconditions":["Pod specification permits host namespace sharing","Workload does not require node-level namespace access"],"input":"Pod security context and workload requirements","output":"Workload with isolated namespaces","expected_behavior":"Ordinary workloads remain within their own namespaces and cannot inspect or interact with node-level namespaces through configuration alone.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload gains visibility or access to node-level processes or networking.","root_cause":"Host namespace access is enabled without a justified operational requirement.","solution":"Disable unnecessary host namespace settings and enforce cluster policy against unauthorized use.","edge_cases":["A hostNetwork pod has different network behavior and exposure","Exceptions should be tightly scoped by namespace, identity, and workload","Other host integrations can provide similar exposure"],"security_implications":"Reduces container-to-node attack surface.","performance_implications":"Namespace isolation may alter networking or IPC behavior for workloads that previously relied on host namespaces.","testing":"Inspect pod namespace settings and attempt controlled access to node-level resources from an isolated workload.","verification":"Claim: untrusted workloads cannot opt into host namespaces. Evidence: admission-policy tests and runtime inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes pod security behavior varies with version and enabled policy controls.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Security Context","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B370","D40-B372"]} {"record_id":"D40-B372","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Runtime Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Missing seccomp restrictions","question":"How should a Kubernetes workload remediate unrestricted system-call access?","answer":"Use an appropriate seccomp profile, preferably a runtime or workload-specific restrictive profile where compatibility permits, and test the workload against the profile before enforcement.","objective":"Reduce the kernel system-call attack surface available to compromised containers.","requirements":["Identify required system calls","Apply an appropriate seccomp profile","Test compatibility before enforcement","Prevent untrusted workloads from disabling required restrictions"],"constraints":["Overly restrictive profiles can break applications","Seccomp availability depends on kernel and runtime support"],"preconditions":["Container runtime supports seccomp","Workload can be tested under the intended profile"],"input":"Workload system-call behavior and seccomp configuration","output":"Restricted system-call execution policy","expected_behavior":"The workload can perform required operations while unauthorized system calls are blocked.","failure_mode":"A compromised workload invokes unnecessary or dangerous system calls.","root_cause":"No effective system-call filtering is applied to container workloads.","solution":"Apply a tested seccomp profile and enforce it through workload security policy.","edge_cases":["Dynamic runtimes may require more system calls than expected","Kernel/runtime changes can alter compatibility","A seccomp profile is one layer and not a complete sandbox"],"security_implications":"Reduces kernel attack surface from compromised containers.","performance_implications":"System-call filtering can add some overhead, generally dependent on workload call frequency and runtime implementation.","testing":"Run functional tests under the profile and attempt controlled disallowed system calls.","verification":"Claim: required workload operations succeed while disallowed system calls are blocked. Evidence: functional and negative syscall tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Linux kernel, container runtime, and Kubernetes version dependent.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Restrict a Container's Syscalls with seccomp","Linux seccomp documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B371","D40-B373"]} {"record_id":"D40-B373","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Network Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted pod-to-pod network communication","question":"How should a Kubernetes cluster remediate unrestricted east-west network access?","answer":"Define network policies that allow only required ingress and egress flows, apply them consistently to protected workloads, and test both allowed and denied communication paths.","objective":"Reduce lateral movement after workload compromise.","requirements":["Identify required communication paths","Apply ingress and egress controls","Ensure policy enforcement is supported by the network plugin","Test default-deny and explicit allow behavior"],"constraints":["NetworkPolicy semantics depend on the cluster network implementation","Some system traffic must remain reachable"],"preconditions":["Cluster networking supports NetworkPolicy enforcement","Workloads have identifiable communication requirements"],"input":"Workload labels, ports, namespaces, and communication requirements","output":"Explicit network-access policy","expected_behavior":"Only documented network flows are allowed between protected workloads.","failure_mode":"A compromised workload freely connects to unrelated services.","root_cause":"Network segmentation is absent or overly broad.","solution":"Use least-privilege ingress and egress NetworkPolicies and verify enforcement with controlled connectivity tests.","edge_cases":["DNS access may require explicit egress rules","HostNetwork workloads may not be covered as expected","Label changes can unintentionally alter policy scope"],"security_implications":"Limits lateral movement and unauthorized service discovery or access.","performance_implications":"Policy enforcement can add network-processing overhead depending on the CNI implementation.","testing":"Test required flows and explicitly forbidden flows from representative workloads.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized east-west connections are blocked. Evidence: connectivity matrix and negative network tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Kubernetes NetworkPolicy enforcement depends on the installed network plugin and its version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Network Policies","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B371","D40-B372"]} {"record_id":"D40-B374","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Kubernetes Image Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Mutable container image tags in production","question":"How should production Kubernetes deployments remediate mutable image references?","answer":"Deploy using immutable image digests or an equivalent verified immutable artifact identity, and enforce policy preventing unauthorized mutable references where the environment requires deterministic provenance.","objective":"Prevent an image tag from silently resolving to a different artifact after deployment configuration was reviewed.","requirements":["Use immutable artifact identity","Verify image provenance where available","Control image update mechanisms","Keep deployment records tied to artifact identity"],"constraints":["Automated update systems may intentionally change versions","Digest pinning requires an explicit update process"],"preconditions":["Production workloads reference container images","Registry tags can be moved"],"input":"Deployment image reference and registry metadata","output":"Immutable deployment artifact reference","expected_behavior":"A deployment identifies a specific image artifact rather than trusting a mutable tag.","failure_mode":"A registry tag is changed to a malicious or unintended image and the deployment consumes it.","root_cause":"Deployment identity is based on mutable naming rather than immutable content identity.","solution":"Pin production images by digest, verify signatures or provenance where supported, and manage updates through controlled changes.","edge_cases":["Digest pinning does not by itself prove publisher identity","Multi-architecture image indexes require understanding the digest being pinned","Image updates require an auditable dependency-update process"],"security_implications":"Reduces image substitution and supply-chain drift.","performance_implications":"Digest resolution and verification have negligible runtime impact after image retrieval.","testing":"Change a registry tag while retaining the deployment manifest and verify that the digest-pinned deployment remains tied to the intended artifact.","verification":"Claim: production workload identity remains stable despite tag mutation. Evidence: registry mutation test and deployed image digest inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Container registry and Kubernetes image-resolution behavior varies by platform and version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Images","SLSA","Sigstore documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B353","D40-B375"]} {"record_id":"D40-B375","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Container Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Containers running as root unnecessarily","question":"How should a container workload remediate unnecessary root execution?","answer":"Run the application as a non-root user with a suitable filesystem and security context, and enforce non-root execution through image and orchestration policy when compatible.","objective":"Reduce the impact of application compromise by removing unnecessary root privileges.","requirements":["Define a non-root runtime identity","Ensure required files are accessible to that identity","Prevent unintended privilege escalation","Test application behavior under non-root execution"],"constraints":["Some legacy applications require root during startup or privileged operations","Host-level privileges remain a separate concern"],"preconditions":["Application does not require root privileges for normal operation"],"input":"Container image and runtime security context","output":"Non-root application execution","expected_behavior":"Application functions correctly without UID 0 privileges.","failure_mode":"Compromised application gains excessive filesystem or process privileges because it runs as root.","root_cause":"Container image or runtime configuration defaults to root without operational necessity.","solution":"Create or select a non-root runtime identity, adjust ownership and permissions, set security context appropriately, and enforce policy where possible.","edge_cases":["Startup scripts may assume root","Mounted volumes may have incompatible ownership","Dropping root privileges does not replace other container isolation controls"],"security_implications":"Reduces privilege available after application compromise.","performance_implications":"Normally negligible; filesystem permission changes may affect startup behavior rather than steady-state performance.","testing":"Run functional and negative privilege tests under the non-root identity.","verification":"Claim: application does not require root for its documented workload. Evidence: non-root execution tests and absence of privileged operations.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Container and orchestration security-context semantics vary by runtime and Kubernetes version.","provenance":["Kubernetes official documentation: Security Context","CIS Kubernetes Benchmark"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B369","D40-B374"]} {"record_id":"D40-B376","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Cloud IAM Security","subtopic":"IAM Policy","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Wildcard cloud IAM permissions","question":"How should overly broad cloud IAM permissions be remediated?","answer":"Replace wildcard actions and resources with the smallest explicit permissions required for the workload, review effective permissions, and separate administrative privileges from routine runtime identities.","objective":"Reduce cloud privilege-escalation and blast-radius risk.","requirements":["Inventory effective permissions","Identify unnecessary wildcard grants","Replace them with explicit actions and resources","Review privilege changes"],"constraints":["Cloud provider policy languages differ","Some services require permissions that are difficult to enumerate precisely"],"preconditions":["Cloud identities have broad IAM policies","Workload operations can be observed or specified"],"input":"IAM policies and workload authorization requirements","output":"Least-privilege IAM policy","expected_behavior":"The workload retains required cloud operations but cannot perform unrelated privileged actions.","failure_mode":"A compromised workload uses broad IAM permissions to access or modify unrelated cloud resources.","root_cause":"IAM permissions were granted for convenience rather than minimum required authority.","solution":"Apply least privilege, resource scoping, condition constraints where appropriate, and separate runtime identities from administrative identities.","edge_cases":["Provider-managed permissions may change over time","Resource wildcards can remain broad even when actions are narrowed","Deny policies can interact with allow policies"],"security_implications":"Limits cloud lateral movement and privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"IAM policy evaluation is provider-managed; narrower policies primarily affect security and governance.","testing":"Attempt representative required and forbidden API operations using the runtime identity.","verification":"Claim: runtime identity cannot perform unrelated privileged cloud actions. Evidence: effective-permission review and negative API authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Cloud IAM semantics are provider-specific and can change over time.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","AWS IAM documentation","Microsoft Azure RBAC documentation","Google Cloud IAM documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B377"]} {"record_id":"D40-B377","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Cloud IAM Security","subtopic":"Cross-Account Trust","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overly broad role assumption trust policy","question":"How should cross-account role assumption be remediated when too many principals can assume the role?","answer":"Restrict the trust policy to explicitly authorized principals and constrain assumption context with supported conditions such as identity, source, external identifiers, or session attributes where applicable.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized identities from obtaining a trusted cloud role.","requirements":["Identify legitimate role assumers","Restrict trust relationships","Use contextual conditions where appropriate","Audit role-assumption events"],"constraints":["Condition capabilities differ by provider","Some cross-account integrations legitimately involve multiple principals"],"preconditions":["Cloud role can be assumed across trust boundaries","Trust policy grants broad principal access"],"input":"Role trust policy and authorized principal inventory","output":"Restricted role-assumption policy","expected_behavior":"Only explicitly authorized principals satisfying the trust conditions can assume the role.","failure_mode":"An unintended principal assumes a privileged cross-account role.","root_cause":"Trust policy identifies a broad principal set without sufficient contextual constraints.","solution":"Narrow trusted principals, use appropriate conditional constraints, and continuously review cross-account trust relationships.","edge_cases":["Service principals and human identities have different trust semantics","External identity federation may require stable condition attributes","Removing a principal can break dependent automation"],"security_implications":"Reduces cross-account privilege escalation and unauthorized cloud access.","performance_implications":"Trust evaluation is provider-managed; policy complexity should remain operationally maintainable.","testing":"Attempt role assumption from authorized and unauthorized principals and verify expected allow/deny behavior.","verification":"Claim: only intended principals can assume the role. Evidence: trust-policy inspection and controlled assumption tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Provider-specific IAM trust semantics apply.","provenance":["AWS IAM documentation: IAM roles","Microsoft Entra and Azure identity documentation","Google Cloud IAM documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B376","D40-B378"]} {"record_id":"D40-B378","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Cloud Security","subtopic":"Instance Metadata","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted cloud instance metadata access","question":"How should cloud workloads remediate unnecessary access to instance metadata services?","answer":"Use the cloud provider's strongest supported metadata protection mode, restrict metadata access to workloads that require it, and prevent untrusted applications from obtaining instance credentials through metadata endpoints.","objective":"Reduce credential theft through cloud instance metadata services.","requirements":["Enable supported metadata protections","Limit workload access where feasible","Use workload identity mechanisms instead of metadata credentials when available","Test metadata access from untrusted workloads"],"constraints":["Cloud providers implement metadata services differently","Some legacy applications depend on metadata access"],"preconditions":["Workload runs in an environment exposing instance or workload metadata","Metadata can return credentials or sensitive configuration"],"input":"Cloud workload metadata configuration and application requirements","output":"Restricted metadata access","expected_behavior":"Untrusted application processes cannot retrieve privileged instance credentials through metadata access.","failure_mode":"SSRF or compromised code accesses metadata credentials and escalates cloud privileges.","root_cause":"Metadata endpoint is reachable without adequate protocol or workload-bound restrictions.","solution":"Enable provider-recommended metadata protections, use least-privilege workload identities, and enforce network/application controls against unnecessary metadata access.","edge_cases":["A local proxy can change metadata exposure semantics","SSRF defenses must complement metadata hardening","Some metadata is non-secret but still sensitive"],"security_implications":"Reduces a major path from application compromise or SSRF to cloud credential theft.","performance_implications":"Metadata protection mechanisms generally add limited request processing overhead; exact impact is provider-specific.","testing":"Attempt metadata access from an application context that should not have credentials and verify denial or non-credential response.","verification":"Claim: untrusted application code cannot obtain privileged cloud credentials from metadata. Evidence: metadata-access tests and cloud configuration inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Provider-specific; validate against the deployed cloud metadata service version and recommended configuration.","provenance":["AWS EC2 Instance Metadata Service documentation","Google Cloud metadata server security documentation","Microsoft Azure managed identity documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B377","D40-B379"]} {"record_id":"D40-B379","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Infrastructure as Code","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secrets stored directly in infrastructure-as-code state","question":"How should infrastructure-as-code remediate secrets exposed through state files?","answer":"Avoid embedding plaintext secrets in configuration when possible, use supported secret references or external secret stores, protect state with strong access controls and encryption, and rotate credentials that may already have been exposed.","objective":"Prevent infrastructure state from becoming a durable secret repository.","requirements":["Identify secret-bearing resources and attributes","Protect state storage and access","Prefer external or managed secret references","Rotate exposed credentials"],"constraints":["Some providers store sensitive values in state even when configuration marks them sensitive","State backups and replicas must receive equivalent protection"],"preconditions":["IaC state contains sensitive values","State is stored remotely or locally"],"input":"IaC configuration, state storage, and secret-management design","output":"Reduced secret exposure in configuration and state","expected_behavior":"Secret material is minimized and state access is tightly restricted and auditable.","failure_mode":"An attacker with state access obtains reusable credentials.","root_cause":"IaC state is treated as ordinary configuration rather than sensitive operational data.","solution":"Use external secret management where supported, mark sensitive values appropriately, encrypt and restrict state, protect backups, and rotate exposed credentials.","edge_cases":["State may retain historical sensitive values","Destroy operations can also read or expose secrets","Provider behavior differs regarding sensitive attributes"],"security_implications":"Reduces infrastructure credential disclosure and persistent secret exposure.","performance_implications":"External secret retrieval can add deployment latency depending on provider and architecture.","testing":"Inspect generated state and remote-state access paths using controlled secrets and verify protection and expected redaction behavior.","verification":"Claim: state access is insufficient to expose reusable production credentials without authorized access. Evidence: state inspection, access-control tests, and secret-rotation records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Terraform and other IaC state semantics are tool/provider-specific.","provenance":["Terraform official documentation: Sensitive Data in State","NIST SP 800-57","OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B352","D40-B380"]} {"record_id":"D40-B380","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Infrastructure as Code","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Infrastructure drift creating security exposure","question":"How should IaC workflows remediate security drift between declared and deployed infrastructure?","answer":"Detect configuration drift, classify security-relevant deviations, reconcile authorized changes through controlled IaC updates, and alert or block deployments when drift violates security policy.","objective":"Prevent unmanaged infrastructure changes from silently weakening security controls.","requirements":["Detect drift","Identify security-relevant changes","Define ownership for authorized exceptions","Reconcile or remediate unauthorized drift"],"constraints":["Some infrastructure changes are dynamic by design","Provider-generated attributes can produce expected non-security drift"],"preconditions":["Infrastructure is managed through declarative configuration","Deployed state can diverge from declared state"],"input":"Declared infrastructure configuration and observed deployed state","output":"Drift assessment and remediation action","expected_behavior":"Security-relevant unauthorized drift is detected and corrected or explicitly governed.","failure_mode":"Manual or automated changes weaken security controls without being detected.","root_cause":"IaC is treated as documentation rather than a continuously reconciled desired state.","solution":"Run scheduled or event-driven drift detection, classify security controls, require governed exceptions, and reconcile unauthorized changes.","edge_cases":["Emergency changes may temporarily create approved drift","Sensitive provider attributes may not be fully observable","Continuous reconciliation can conflict with manually managed resources"],"security_implications":"Reduces persistence of unauthorized security configuration changes.","performance_implications":"Drift detection consumes provider API calls and CI/CD resources proportional to infrastructure size.","testing":"Introduce a controlled security-relevant configuration drift and verify detection, alerting, and reconciliation behavior.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized security drift is detected within the defined monitoring interval. Evidence: controlled drift test and recorded detection event.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"IaC drift capabilities vary by tool and provider.","provenance":["Terraform official documentation","NIST SP 800-53 configuration management controls"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B379","D40-B381"]} {"record_id":"D40-B381","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Database Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Application database accounts with excessive privileges","question":"How should database accounts used by applications be remediated when they have excessive privileges?","answer":"Create workload-specific database roles with only the operations required by the application, separate migration or administrative identities from runtime identities, and restrict direct access to privileged schemas.","objective":"Limit database compromise impact when an application credential is stolen or the application is compromised.","requirements":["Inventory actual database operations","Separate runtime and administrative roles","Grant least privilege","Restrict sensitive schemas and operations"],"constraints":["Application ORMs may generate implicit queries","Privilege reduction must be tested against migrations and background jobs"],"preconditions":["Application uses database credentials","Current credentials have broader permissions than required"],"input":"Database role configuration and application query behavior","output":"Least-privilege runtime database role","expected_behavior":"Normal application operations succeed while unrelated administrative operations are denied.","failure_mode":"Compromised application credentials modify schema, users, or unrelated sensitive data.","root_cause":"Runtime credentials are reused for administrative or migration operations.","solution":"Separate database roles, grant only required CRUD or procedure privileges, and keep migration credentials outside ordinary runtime processes.","edge_cases":["Stored procedures can alter the effective privilege model","Connection pools may retain credentials longer than expected","Background workers may require different roles"],"security_implications":"Reduces database privilege escalation and blast radius.","performance_implications":"Role checks are database-managed; separating credentials generally has negligible query overhead.","testing":"Run representative application operations and forbidden administrative operations under the runtime role.","verification":"Claim: runtime database credentials cannot perform unauthorized administrative actions. Evidence: role inspection and negative SQL authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Database privilege semantics are engine-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Database Security guidance","NIST least-privilege principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B382"]} {"record_id":"D40-B382","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Row-Level Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Tenant isolation enforced only by application filters","question":"How should a multi-tenant application remediate tenant isolation that depends solely on application query filters?","answer":"Where supported and appropriate, enforce tenant isolation at the database authorization layer in addition to application checks, bind database policy context to an authenticated tenant identity, and test cross-tenant access explicitly.","objective":"Prevent cross-tenant data access when application logic contains a filtering defect or alternate query path.","requirements":["Define tenant identity at the database boundary","Use database-enforced row or schema isolation where appropriate","Retain application-level authorization","Test direct and indirect query paths"],"constraints":["Database row-level security semantics differ by engine","Connection pooling requires careful tenant-context handling"],"preconditions":["Application serves multiple tenants","Database supports an isolation mechanism appropriate to the design"],"input":"Authenticated tenant context and database query","output":"Tenant-scoped database result or denial","expected_behavior":"A query cannot retrieve another tenant's protected rows even when application filtering is accidentally omitted.","failure_mode":"A query path returns data belonging to another tenant.","root_cause":"Tenant isolation relies on duplicated application logic without a database-enforced security boundary.","solution":"Use database-enforced tenant policies where appropriate, ensure tenant context cannot be attacker-controlled, and maintain application authorization as defense in depth.","edge_cases":["Administrative cross-tenant operations require a separately governed identity","Connection pooling can leak tenant context if session state is not reset","Background jobs need explicit tenant context"],"security_implications":"Provides defense in depth against cross-tenant data leakage.","performance_implications":"Database policy evaluation can add query-planning or execution overhead depending on the database and policy complexity.","testing":"Attempt cross-tenant reads and writes through normal and alternate query paths and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: tenant isolation remains enforced when application-level filters are absent. Evidence: cross-tenant negative tests and database policy inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Database row-level security behavior is engine-specific.","provenance":["PostgreSQL official documentation: Row Security Policies","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B381","D40-B383"]} {"record_id":"D40-B383","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Dynamic SQL","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Dynamic SQL injection in stored procedures","question":"How should stored procedures remediate dynamic SQL constructed from untrusted input?","answer":"Use parameterized execution mechanisms supported by the database, validate identifiers through strict allowlists when dynamic identifiers are unavoidable, and avoid concatenating untrusted values into executable SQL.","objective":"Prevent injection through database-side dynamic SQL.","requirements":["Separate SQL structure from data values","Parameterize dynamic values","Allowlist dynamic identifiers","Use least-privilege database roles"],"constraints":["Identifiers cannot always be parameterized like values","Database-specific dynamic SQL APIs differ"],"preconditions":["Stored procedures construct SQL dynamically","Some dynamic component is influenced by input"],"input":"Stored-procedure input and generated SQL operation","output":"Safe database execution","expected_behavior":"Untrusted values remain data rather than changing executable SQL structure.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled input alters the database command.","root_cause":"SQL text is assembled by concatenating untrusted input.","solution":"Use parameterized dynamic execution and strict identifier allowlists; avoid dynamic SQL where static SQL can satisfy the requirement.","edge_cases":["Sort columns, table names, and schema names often require allowlists","Escaping alone is not equivalent to parameterization","Stored procedures may run with elevated definer privileges"],"security_implications":"Prevents database-side SQL injection and limits impact through least privilege.","performance_implications":"Parameterized execution can support plan reuse depending on the database engine; exact behavior is engine-specific.","testing":"Supply SQL metacharacters and crafted values to controlled procedure inputs and verify they cannot alter query structure.","verification":"Claim: untrusted procedure inputs cannot alter executable SQL structure. Evidence: negative injection tests and procedure-source inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Database-specific dynamic SQL semantics apply.","provenance":["OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet","PostgreSQL official documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B101","D40-B381"]} {"record_id":"D40-B384","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Database Credential Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Long-lived database credentials","question":"How should an application remediate long-lived static database credentials?","answer":"Use managed identity or short-lived credential mechanisms where supported; otherwise implement controlled credential rotation, secure storage, limited privileges, and connection-pool rollover procedures.","objective":"Reduce the exposure window of compromised database credentials.","requirements":["Use short-lived or managed credentials where practical","Rotate static credentials predictably","Avoid logging credentials","Handle active connection rollover safely"],"constraints":["Some database systems or drivers do not support short-lived authentication","Credential rotation can disrupt persistent connections if not designed correctly"],"preconditions":["Application uses database authentication credentials","Credentials remain valid for extended periods"],"input":"Database authentication configuration and connection lifecycle","output":"Reduced credential lifetime and controlled rotation","expected_behavior":"Credentials are rotated without exposing them or causing uncontrolled service disruption.","failure_mode":"A stolen database password remains usable for an extended period.","root_cause":"Credential lifecycle is disconnected from workload identity and operational rotation.","solution":"Prefer workload identity or managed authentication; otherwise automate secure rotation and coordinate pool recycling.","edge_cases":["Existing pooled connections may continue using old credentials","Rotation failures require safe rollback without leaving credentials exposed","Read replicas may use separate credential paths"],"security_implications":"Limits the useful lifetime of stolen database credentials.","performance_implications":"Credential refresh and connection recycling can temporarily increase connection-management overhead.","testing":"Perform controlled credential rotation while traffic is active and verify authorized requests continue while the old credential becomes unusable.","verification":"Claim: old credentials cease functioning after the defined rotation boundary. Evidence: controlled rotation test and authentication logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Credential rotation mechanisms vary by database, cloud provider, and driver.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B379","D40-B385"]} {"record_id":"D40-B385","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Security","subtopic":"Backup Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Backups accessible with production application credentials","question":"How should database backups be remediated when production application identities can access them?","answer":"Separate backup administration and storage access from application runtime identities, encrypt backups, restrict restore permissions, and audit backup reads and restore operations.","objective":"Prevent application compromise from becoming unrestricted access to historical database copies.","requirements":["Separate backup access identities","Restrict backup storage","Encrypt backup data","Audit reads and restores"],"constraints":["Operational recovery workflows require tested access","Backup services may use provider-managed identities"],"preconditions":["Database backups exist","Application credentials can currently reach backup storage or restore operations"],"input":"Backup storage policy, identities, and recovery workflow","output":"Isolated backup access model","expected_behavior":"Compromised application credentials cannot directly retrieve or restore protected backups.","failure_mode":"An attacker uses application credentials to obtain historical sensitive data.","root_cause":"Production runtime and backup administration share an authorization boundary.","solution":"Separate identities and roles, encrypt backups, restrict restore operations, and test recovery using dedicated privileged workflows.","edge_cases":["Backup copies and replicas require equivalent controls","Encryption keys must have separate access controls","Emergency recovery access should be audited"],"security_implications":"Reduces historical-data exposure after application compromise.","performance_implications":"Encryption and access-control checks may affect backup throughput, depending on provider and workload.","testing":"Attempt backup retrieval and restore operations with the application identity and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: production runtime identity cannot access protected backup data. Evidence: negative backup-access tests and identity-policy inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Backup and storage controls are database- and provider-specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-57","OWASP Database Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B384","D40-B386"]} {"record_id":"D40-B386","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Messaging Security","subtopic":"Message Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthorized producer access to security-sensitive topics","question":"How should a messaging system remediate broad producer permissions on sensitive topics?","answer":"Restrict producer permissions to explicitly authorized identities and topics, separate publish and consume roles, and validate authorization at the broker or messaging service.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized systems from injecting trusted-looking events into sensitive workflows.","requirements":["Define producer identity","Restrict topic permissions","Separate publish and consume authority","Audit authorization failures"],"constraints":["Messaging systems differ in ACL semantics","Shared topics can complicate least-privilege policy"],"preconditions":["Multiple producers can publish to shared topics","Downstream consumers trust published events"],"input":"Producer identity, topic, and publish request","output":"Broker authorization decision","expected_behavior":"Only authorized producers can publish to a protected topic.","failure_mode":"An unauthorized producer injects malicious commands or fabricated events.","root_cause":"Topic permissions are broader than the trust boundary requires.","solution":"Apply per-topic and per-producer ACLs, use separate command and event channels where appropriate, and enforce authorization at the broker.","edge_cases":["Administrative producers may require separate identities","Wildcard topic permissions can silently expand authority","Compromised legitimate producers remain a threat and require message-level controls"],"security_implications":"Reduces event injection and unauthorized command publication.","performance_implications":"Broker authorization checks generally add limited processing overhead.","testing":"Publish using authorized and unauthorized identities and verify expected broker decisions.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized identities cannot publish to protected topics. Evidence: ACL inspection and negative publish tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Messaging authorization is broker-specific.","provenance":["Apache Kafka security documentation","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B387"]} {"record_id":"D40-B387","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Messaging Security","subtopic":"Message Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted event payloads driving privileged actions","question":"How should an event-driven system remediate privileged actions triggered by unvalidated message payloads?","answer":"Authenticate the producer, validate message schema and semantic constraints, authorize the requested action independently of payload claims, and treat event contents as untrusted input until verified.","objective":"Prevent crafted messages from causing unauthorized privileged operations.","requirements":["Authenticate message origin","Validate schema and value constraints","Perform independent authorization","Reject malformed or unauthorized commands"],"constraints":["Valid schema does not establish authorization","Some event systems use multiple trusted producers with different authority levels"],"preconditions":["Messages trigger state changes or privileged operations","Consumers currently trust payload fields"],"input":"Event metadata and payload","output":"Validated and authorized action request","expected_behavior":"Only authenticated and authorized messages satisfying schema and business security constraints trigger privileged actions.","failure_mode":"An attacker crafts a valid-looking event that causes unauthorized behavior.","root_cause":"Message validity is incorrectly treated as proof of authority.","solution":"Separate message authentication, schema validation, authorization, and action execution; never derive privilege solely from payload fields.","edge_cases":["Replay protection may also be required","Producer compromise remains possible","Schema evolution can introduce unsafe optional fields"],"security_implications":"Reduces event injection and privilege escalation through message consumers.","performance_implications":"Schema and authorization checks add per-message processing overhead.","testing":"Submit malformed, semantically invalid, unauthorized, and correctly authorized events and verify distinct outcomes.","verification":"Claim: valid message syntax alone cannot trigger unauthorized privileged actions. Evidence: negative event tests and authorization decision records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Messaging framework and schema-registry behavior is implementation-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B386","D40-B388"]} {"record_id":"D40-B388","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Messaging Security","subtopic":"Message Replay","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Replayable security-sensitive messages","question":"How should an event-driven security workflow remediate replay of previously valid commands?","answer":"Use unique message identifiers or protocol-supported sequence information, track recently processed commands where required, enforce appropriate expiration, and make sensitive operations idempotent when possible.","objective":"Prevent previously authorized messages from being reused to repeat security-sensitive actions.","requirements":["Define replay identity","Apply appropriate freshness constraints","Track processed identifiers where needed","Design sensitive operations for safe idempotency"],"constraints":["Replay windows depend on system latency and threat model","Distributed consumers require consistent replay state"],"preconditions":["Messages can be captured and delivered more than once","Repeated execution can cause harm"],"input":"Message identifier, timestamp or sequence, and command payload","output":"Freshness and replay decision","expected_behavior":"A duplicate or expired sensitive command is rejected or safely treated as already processed.","failure_mode":"An attacker replays a previously valid command.","root_cause":"Message validity is checked without freshness or duplicate detection.","solution":"Implement protocol-appropriate replay protection and idempotency keys, with bounded state retention.","edge_cases":["Clock skew can affect timestamp-based validation","At-least-once delivery naturally creates duplicates","Idempotency storage must survive consumer restarts for the required window"],"security_implications":"Reduces replay attacks and duplicate execution of privileged actions.","performance_implications":"Replay-state storage adds memory or database operations proportional to the protection window and message volume.","testing":"Deliver the same controlled security-sensitive message multiple times and verify only the permitted execution occurs.","verification":"Claim: duplicate sensitive commands cannot cause unauthorized repeated effects. Evidence: replay tests and consumer state inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Messaging semantics are broker and application specific.","provenance":["OWASP Webhook Security guidance","RFC 9421","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B387","D40-B389"]} {"record_id":"D40-B389","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Messaging Security","subtopic":"Dead-Letter Queue Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive messages exposed through dead-letter queues","question":"How should a messaging system remediate sensitive or attacker-controlled messages retained in dead-letter queues?","answer":"Apply access controls and retention limits to dead-letter queues, preserve message confidentiality, validate messages before replay, and prevent arbitrary users from replaying privileged events.","objective":"Prevent dead-letter storage and replay mechanisms from becoming security bypasses.","requirements":["Restrict dead-letter queue access","Protect sensitive payloads","Control replay permissions","Validate messages before replay"],"constraints":["Operational teams may require diagnostic access","Replay can trigger side effects if authorization context has changed"],"preconditions":["Failed messages are retained","Dead-letter messages can be replayed into active consumers"],"input":"Dead-letter message and replay request","output":"Controlled inspection or replay decision","expected_behavior":"Only authorized operators can inspect or replay messages, and replay undergoes current security validation.","failure_mode":"An attacker reads sensitive dead-letter data or replays an old privileged command.","root_cause":"Dead-letter queues are treated as passive storage without equivalent security controls.","solution":"Apply least-privilege access, encryption, bounded retention, message validation, and controlled replay workflows.","edge_cases":["Expired credentials or authorization may make replay unsafe","Messages may contain secrets or personal data","Replay can create duplicate side effects"],"security_implications":"Protects retained event data and prevents replay-based privilege abuse.","performance_implications":"Replay validation and secure storage add operational overhead.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized read and replay operations and verify denial; replay controlled expired messages and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: dead-letter storage cannot bypass message security controls. Evidence: access tests and controlled replay tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Messaging platform-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B388","D40-B390"]} {"record_id":"D40-B390","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Messaging Security","subtopic":"Message Size Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unbounded message size enabling resource exhaustion","question":"How should a message consumer remediate unbounded message size?","answer":"Enforce broker and consumer message-size limits, reject or quarantine oversized payloads before expensive parsing, and use bounded resource allocation for processing.","objective":"Prevent malicious messages from exhausting memory, CPU, storage, or downstream resources.","requirements":["Define maximum accepted message size","Enforce limits at multiple relevant layers","Reject oversized messages early","Bound parser and processing resources"],"constraints":["Valid business payloads may legitimately vary in size","Broker and consumer limits must be compatible"],"preconditions":["Consumer processes externally supplied messages","Message size affects parsing or resource consumption"],"input":"Incoming message and configured size limits","output":"Accepted, rejected, or quarantined message","expected_behavior":"Messages exceeding configured limits are rejected before unbounded resource consumption occurs.","failure_mode":"Large or nested payloads exhaust consumer resources.","root_cause":"No explicit upper bound exists for message size or parsing resources.","solution":"Set broker and application limits, enforce early rejection, and test boundary and oversized inputs.","edge_cases":["Compression can hide large decompressed payloads","Chunked messages can bypass naive per-message limits","Nested formats can consume disproportionate resources"],"security_implications":"Reduces message-driven denial-of-service risk.","performance_implications":"Early size checks reduce unnecessary parsing and resource consumption.","testing":"Send messages below, at, and above configured limits and verify deterministic handling.","verification":"Claim: message size cannot exceed the resource budget defined by the security policy. Evidence: boundary and oversized-message tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Broker and serialization-library limits are implementation-specific.","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","OWASP Denial of Service guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B125","D40-B389"]} {"record_id":"D40-B391","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"Third-Party JavaScript","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unverified third-party JavaScript resources","question":"How should a web application remediate untrusted third-party JavaScript loaded from external locations?","answer":"Minimize third-party scripts, self-host when appropriate, use Subresource Integrity for supported static resources, constrain script execution with Content Security Policy, and monitor dependency changes.","objective":"Reduce supply-chain risk from modified or compromised browser scripts.","requirements":["Inventory third-party scripts","Use integrity verification where supported","Restrict script sources","Control update and dependency processes"],"constraints":["SRI requires stable resources and compatible delivery","Dynamic scripts and cross-origin resources may require different controls"],"preconditions":["Application loads JavaScript from external origins","Third-party code executes in the application security context"],"input":"HTML script references and trusted-resource policy","output":"Controlled third-party script execution","expected_behavior":"Unexpected changes to integrity-protected resources are blocked or detected according to policy.","failure_mode":"A compromised external script executes with application-origin privileges.","root_cause":"Third-party browser code is trusted solely based on URL or provider reputation.","solution":"Use SRI where applicable, restrictive CSP, minimized dependencies, and controlled resource hosting.","edge_cases":["CSP and SRI serve different security purposes","A compromised trusted dependency can still produce malicious content","Inline dynamic loading may bypass intended assumptions"],"security_implications":"Reduces browser supply-chain compromise and unauthorized script execution.","performance_implications":"Integrity checks have browser-managed overhead; self-hosting can change caching characteristics.","testing":"Modify a controlled integrity-protected resource and verify the browser refuses to execute it.","verification":"Claim: altered integrity-protected resources do not execute. Evidence: browser security tests and deployed headers/resource attributes.","languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","HTML"],"version_scope":"Browser behavior and CSP/SRI support should be validated against supported client versions.","provenance":["W3C Subresource Integrity","MDN Web Docs: Subresource Integrity","OWASP Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B392","D40-B393"]} {"record_id":"D40-B392","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"postMessage Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Wildcard postMessage target origin","question":"How should browser code remediate postMessage calls that use an unrestricted target origin?","answer":"Specify the exact intended target origin when sending sensitive messages and validate event.origin against an explicit allowlist when receiving messages before processing trusted data or commands.","objective":"Prevent sensitive browser messages from being disclosed to or accepted from unintended origins.","requirements":["Define trusted origins","Use explicit targetOrigin values","Validate sender origin before processing","Validate message structure and authority separately"],"constraints":["Multi-origin applications require explicit origin mappings","Origin validation does not authenticate message content by itself"],"preconditions":["Application uses window.postMessage or equivalent cross-origin messaging","Messages contain sensitive data or trigger privileged behavior"],"input":"Message, target origin, sender origin, and trusted-origin policy","output":"Accepted or rejected cross-origin message","expected_behavior":"Only messages from configured trusted origins are accepted for the intended privileged operation.","failure_mode":"An attacker-controlled origin receives sensitive data or sends a privileged command.","root_cause":"Cross-origin messages are trusted without origin restriction.","solution":"Use exact target origins for sensitive sends and explicit origin allowlists plus message validation on receive.","edge_cases":["Origin comparison must account for scheme, host, and port","A trusted origin may itself contain vulnerable application code","Opaque origins require special handling"],"security_implications":"Reduces cross-origin message interception and command injection.","performance_implications":"Origin and schema checks have negligible typical cost.","testing":"Send messages from allowed and disallowed origins and verify only the intended origin is accepted.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized origins cannot exchange privileged messages. Evidence: cross-origin browser tests and message-handler checks.","languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Browser API behavior follows web platform specifications and browser implementation support.","provenance":["MDN Web Docs: Window.postMessage","OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B391","D40-B393"]} {"record_id":"D40-B393","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"WebSocket Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"WebSocket endpoint without origin and authorization controls","question":"How should a WebSocket service remediate unrestricted connections?","answer":"Authenticate the connection, authorize the requested resource or channel, validate the Origin header where applicable, and enforce message-size and rate limits appropriate to the service.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized WebSocket access and browser-origin abuse.","requirements":["Authenticate connections","Authorize channels or operations","Validate browser origin when relevant","Limit message size and rate"],"constraints":["Origin validation is not a substitute for authentication","Non-browser clients may not provide browser Origin semantics"],"preconditions":["Application exposes WebSocket endpoints","Connections can trigger authenticated or privileged actions"],"input":"Connection request, identity, origin, and subsequent messages","output":"Authenticated and authorized WebSocket session","expected_behavior":"Only authorized clients establish sessions and invoke permitted operations.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized client connects or abuses a trusted browser session context.","root_cause":"WebSocket endpoint is treated as trusted after network connectivity alone.","solution":"Apply authentication, authorization, origin checks where applicable, and resource limits.","edge_cases":["Long-lived connections require credential/session expiration handling","Origin can be absent for non-browser clients","Authorization must be re-evaluated when privileges change"],"security_implications":"Reduces unauthorized real-time access and resource exhaustion.","performance_implications":"Per-message validation and rate limiting add processing overhead proportional to traffic.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized connections, cross-origin browser connections, oversized messages, and rate-limit violations.","verification":"Claim: WebSocket access is bounded by authentication, authorization, and resource policy. Evidence: connection and message-level negative tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"WebSocket framework and server behavior varies by implementation.","provenance":["OWASP WebSocket Security Cheat Sheet","RFC 6455"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B392","D40-B394"]} {"record_id":"D40-B394","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"Client-Side Storage","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive authentication data in browser local storage","question":"How should a web application remediate sensitive credentials stored in JavaScript-accessible local storage?","answer":"Avoid placing highly sensitive authentication credentials in JavaScript-accessible storage when a safer session architecture is available, use appropriately protected cookies for browser sessions, and apply XSS defenses.","objective":"Reduce credential theft after client-side script compromise.","requirements":["Classify stored authentication material","Minimize JavaScript-accessible secrets","Use appropriate cookie security attributes for browser sessions","Protect against script injection"],"constraints":["Browser application architecture determines feasible storage mechanisms","HttpOnly cookies mitigate JavaScript access but do not eliminate CSRF considerations"],"preconditions":["Browser stores reusable authentication material","JavaScript can read the storage location"],"input":"Authentication storage design and threat model","output":"Reduced client-side credential exposure","expected_behavior":"Highly sensitive reusable credentials are not unnecessarily accessible to arbitrary page scripts.","failure_mode":"Injected JavaScript reads authentication material and reuses it.","root_cause":"Reusable credentials are stored in a location directly accessible to page scripts.","solution":"Prefer appropriately scoped secure cookies or other architecture-specific protections, reduce credential lifetime, and harden against XSS.","edge_cases":["HttpOnly cookies still require Secure and appropriate SameSite configuration","Access tokens in memory can still be exposed to XSS","Mobile or desktop webviews have different storage risks"],"security_implications":"Reduces token theft through client-side script compromise.","performance_implications":"Storage architecture can affect authentication round trips but no universal performance result should be assumed.","testing":"Inject controlled test scripts in a security test environment and verify sensitive credentials are not readable through JavaScript.","verification":"Claim: page JavaScript cannot directly retrieve protected browser session credentials. Evidence: browser storage inspection and controlled XSS tests.","languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Browser cookie and storage behavior varies by supported browser versions.","provenance":["OWASP HTML5 Security Cheat Sheet","OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B394","D40-B395"]} {"record_id":"D40-B395","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Browser Security","subtopic":"Deep-Link Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unvalidated deep links carrying sensitive authorization context","question":"How should mobile or web applications remediate unsafe deep-link handling?","answer":"Allow only registered schemes, hosts, paths, and parameter forms, validate authorization context after navigation, and avoid placing reusable credentials or sensitive tokens in URLs.","objective":"Prevent malicious links from redirecting users into unauthorized application states or exposing credentials.","requirements":["Validate deep-link destination","Validate parameters","Avoid sensitive credentials in URLs","Require normal authorization after navigation"],"constraints":["Platform-specific universal/app-link mechanisms differ","Deep links can legitimately carry non-sensitive state"],"preconditions":["Application processes externally supplied deep links","Links can open privileged application routes"],"input":"External deep-link URI and current authentication context","output":"Validated navigation or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only approved routes and parameters are processed, and privileged operations still require authorization.","failure_mode":"Attacker crafts a link that opens a privileged route or leaks sensitive authorization data.","root_cause":"External URI parameters are treated as trusted navigation instructions.","solution":"Use platform-supported verified links where available, strict route validation, parameter validation, and independent authorization checks.","edge_cases":["URL encoding and normalization can alter parsing","Custom URI schemes can have ownership collisions","Tokens in query strings can leak through browser or proxy history"],"security_implications":"Reduces deep-link hijacking, token leakage, and unauthorized navigation.","performance_implications":"Validation is typically negligible compared with navigation and application startup.","testing":"Exercise malformed, unauthorized, alternate-host, encoded, and privileged deep links and verify safe rejection or authorization.","verification":"Claim: externally supplied deep links cannot bypass route authorization or expose credentials. Evidence: negative URI tests and authorization checks.","languages":["JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++"],"version_scope":"Platform-specific; validate against supported browser and mobile operating-system versions.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard","OWASP Mobile Application Security Testing Guide"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B357","D40-B394"]} {"record_id":"D40-B396","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Secrets and Key Management","subtopic":"Secret Rotation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secret rotation causing service outage","question":"How should secret rotation be remediated when changing credentials causes avoidable downtime?","answer":"Design credential rotation as an overlapping-validity transition: introduce the new credential, update consumers, verify successful use, then revoke the old credential after the required propagation and connection rollover period.","objective":"Reduce credential lifetime without creating unnecessary service interruption.","requirements":["Support staged credential introduction","Track active consumers","Validate new credential before revoking old one","Coordinate connection or process reload"],"constraints":["Some providers allow only one active credential","Rotation semantics differ by secret type"],"preconditions":["A service uses credentials that can be rotated","Consumers cannot all switch instantaneously"],"input":"Current credential, replacement credential, consumer rollout state","output":"Successfully rotated credential with controlled retirement","expected_behavior":"Consumers transition to the new credential before the old credential is revoked.","failure_mode":"Credential rotation causes authentication failures or leaves the old credential active indefinitely.","root_cause":"Rotation is implemented as an immediate replacement rather than a controlled lifecycle.","solution":"Use staged rotation where supported, monitor adoption, revoke the old credential after confirmation, and maintain a tested rollback procedure.","edge_cases":["Single-active-key systems require provider-specific rotation mechanisms","Long-lived pooled connections may retain old credentials","Failed consumers must be identified before revocation"],"security_implications":"Enables practical credential rotation while reducing pressure to keep long-lived credentials.","performance_implications":"Temporary dual-credential handling may increase operational complexity and connection management.","testing":"Perform a controlled rotation under active traffic and verify old and new credential behavior throughout the transition.","verification":"Claim: credential rotation completes without unauthorized continued use of the retired credential. Evidence: staged rotation test, authentication logs, and revocation confirmation.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Credential-provider and secret-manager specific.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B384","D40-B397"]} {"record_id":"D40-B397","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Secrets and Key Management","subtopic":"KMS Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Application identity allowed to administer encryption keys","question":"How should key-management systems remediate applications that can both use and administer encryption keys?","answer":"Separate cryptographic use permissions from key-administration permissions, assign administration to dedicated identities, and restrict applications to the minimum key operations they require.","objective":"Prevent application compromise from becoming encryption-key administration compromise.","requirements":["Separate key usage and administration","Use dedicated administrative identities","Restrict application key operations","Audit key-management events"],"constraints":["Some cloud KMS products expose different key roles and permissions","Encryption workflows may require decrypt, encrypt, or data-key operations without administrative access"],"preconditions":["Application identity can call KMS APIs","Application has both data-plane and control-plane permissions"],"input":"KMS policy and application cryptographic requirements","output":"Separated key-use and key-administration authority","expected_behavior":"Application can perform required cryptographic operations but cannot modify key policy, lifecycle, or administrative configuration.","failure_mode":"Compromised application changes key policy or destroys security controls.","root_cause":"Data-plane and control-plane key permissions share one identity.","solution":"Separate KMS administration from runtime cryptographic use and apply least-privilege key policies.","edge_cases":["Break-glass administration requires separate audited access","Key deletion and rotation permissions are especially sensitive","Provider-managed keys have different policy controls"],"security_implications":"Limits impact of application compromise on encryption infrastructure.","performance_implications":"Policy separation has negligible cryptographic runtime impact.","testing":"Attempt allowed cryptographic operations and forbidden key-administration operations using the application identity.","verification":"Claim: runtime application identity cannot administer cryptographic keys. Evidence: KMS policy inspection and negative API tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Cloud or enterprise KMS policy semantics are provider-specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","AWS KMS documentation","Google Cloud KMS documentation","Microsoft Azure Key Vault documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B376","D40-B398"]} {"record_id":"D40-B398","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Secrets and Key Management","subtopic":"Key Lifecycle","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe cryptographic key deletion","question":"How should cryptographic key deletion be remediated when accidental destruction could cause irreversible data loss?","answer":"Separate deletion authorization from ordinary key use, require explicit governance for destructive operations, use provider-supported deletion protection or recovery periods where appropriate, and verify dependent data and recovery requirements before destruction.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized or accidental irreversible cryptographic key destruction.","requirements":["Separate deletion privileges","Require deliberate approval for destructive operations","Identify key dependencies","Use supported recovery or deletion-protection mechanisms where appropriate"],"constraints":["Some systems make deletion irreversible after a provider-defined period","Recovery requirements vary by data sensitivity and retention policy"],"preconditions":["Encryption keys protect stored data","Key deletion can make ciphertext permanently inaccessible"],"input":"Key metadata, dependencies, retention requirements, and deletion request","output":"Approved or rejected key-deletion decision","expected_behavior":"Destructive key operations require authorized governance and do not occur accidentally through routine application permissions.","failure_mode":"An attacker or operator destroys a key and causes irreversible data loss.","root_cause":"Key deletion is exposed through broad runtime or administrative privileges without safeguards.","solution":"Separate deletion authority, enforce deletion protection where supported, document dependencies, and test recovery procedures.","edge_cases":["Legal retention requirements may conflict with deletion schedules","Backups can preserve ciphertext that remains unusable without the key","Emergency recovery may require separate break-glass authority"],"security_implications":"Protects availability and recoverability of encrypted data while limiting destructive privilege.","performance_implications":"Governance controls primarily affect administrative workflow rather than runtime performance.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized deletion with runtime identities and exercise the documented recovery or deletion-protection process in a controlled environment.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized identities cannot irreversibly delete production encryption keys. Evidence: negative deletion tests and key-policy review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"KMS behavior varies by provider and key-management implementation.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","AWS KMS documentation","Azure Key Vault documentation","Google Cloud KMS documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B397","D40-B399"]} {"record_id":"D40-B399","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Secrets and Key Management","subtopic":"Secret Bootstrap","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Static bootstrap secret used to retrieve all application secrets","question":"How should an application remediate a high-value static bootstrap secret used to access its entire secret store?","answer":"Replace broad bootstrap credentials with workload identity, narrowly scoped retrieval permissions, short-lived credentials, or provider-supported identity mechanisms where feasible; if a bootstrap secret remains necessary, minimize its scope and rotation exposure.","objective":"Reduce the concentration of privilege in the initial credential required to start the application.","requirements":["Define exact startup secret requirements","Use workload identity where available","Scope secret retrieval narrowly","Protect and rotate any unavoidable bootstrap credential"],"constraints":["Some deployment environments lack native workload identity","Bootstrapping inherently requires an initial trust mechanism"],"preconditions":["Application needs credentials before normal secret retrieval can occur","One static secret grants broad secret-store access"],"input":"Application bootstrap design and secret-store policy","output":"Reduced-privilege secret bootstrap mechanism","expected_behavior":"Compromise of the bootstrap mechanism does not automatically expose unrelated secrets beyond its configured scope.","failure_mode":"A stolen bootstrap secret provides broad access to the application's entire secret inventory.","root_cause":"Bootstrap trust is granted excessive authority because it is operationally convenient.","solution":"Use workload identity or narrowly scoped retrieval roles, limit secret visibility, and rotate unavoidable bootstrap credentials.","edge_cases":["Container images must not embed bootstrap secrets","Build-time and runtime identities should remain separate","Startup logs must not reveal bootstrap failures containing secret values"],"security_implications":"Reduces high-impact credential compromise during application startup.","performance_implications":"Workload-identity token acquisition can add startup latency depending on the platform.","testing":"Attempt to use the bootstrap identity to retrieve secrets outside its declared scope and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: bootstrap credentials cannot retrieve unrelated secrets. Evidence: secret-store policy inspection and negative retrieval tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Secret-management and workload-identity mechanisms vary by deployment platform.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B379","D40-B397"]} {"record_id":"D40-B400","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Secret Exposure Recovery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secret remains valid after confirmed exposure","question":"How should a confirmed production secret exposure be remediated?","answer":"Revoke or rotate the exposed secret, identify dependent systems, investigate usage during the exposure window, remove residual copies where feasible, and verify that the replacement credential is used successfully.","objective":"Restore trust after confirmed credential disclosure.","requirements":["Identify exposed credential and scope","Revoke or rotate it","Investigate possible use","Update dependent consumers","Verify old credential invalidation"],"constraints":["Immediate revocation can interrupt dependent services","Exposure may exist in logs, caches, backups, or third-party systems"],"preconditions":["Credential exposure is confirmed or sufficiently credible","Credential remains potentially usable"],"input":"Exposed secret, dependency inventory, and exposure timeline","output":"Revoked or replaced credential and documented recovery state","expected_behavior":"The exposed credential is no longer usable and dependent systems operate with the replacement credential.","failure_mode":"Exposed credential remains active because remediation focuses only on deleting the visible copy.","root_cause":"Secret exposure is treated as a storage-cleanup issue rather than a credential-compromise event.","solution":"Execute credential rotation or revocation, investigate access, update consumers, invalidate residual sessions where applicable, and verify closure.","edge_cases":["Unknown exposure duration may require broader incident analysis","Third-party systems may retain copies beyond direct control","Some credentials cannot be immediately revoked and require compensating controls"],"security_implications":"Limits continued unauthorized access after secret disclosure.","performance_implications":"Credential rotation can cause temporary operational overhead and connection churn.","testing":"Use a controlled canary credential exposure scenario and verify the complete rotation, dependency update, and invalidation workflow.","verification":"Claim: a confirmed exposed credential becomes unusable within the defined response window. Evidence: revocation tests, authentication logs, and dependency verification.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Credential lifecycle depends on the identity, secret, and provider mechanisms in use.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B352","D40-B396"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B401","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Serverless Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Overprivileged serverless function execution role","question":"How should a serverless function remediate excessive execution-role permissions?","answer":"Create a function-specific identity and grant only the cloud resources and actions required by that function, separating deployment permissions from runtime permissions.","objective":"Limit privilege escalation and resource access after serverless function compromise.","requirements":["Use function-specific identities where practical","Restrict actions and resources","Separate deployment and runtime roles","Review effective permissions"],"constraints":["Provider IAM semantics differ","Functions may invoke multiple services and require explicit cross-service permissions"],"preconditions":["Serverless functions execute with cloud identities","Current role permissions exceed observed requirements"],"input":"Function execution role and observed service operations","output":"Least-privilege execution identity","expected_behavior":"The function can perform required operations but cannot access unrelated privileged resources.","failure_mode":"Compromised function code uses broad execution permissions to access unrelated cloud resources.","root_cause":"Deployment or administrative permissions were reused as runtime permissions.","solution":"Separate identities by lifecycle, scope runtime permissions to required actions/resources, and continuously review effective permissions.","edge_cases":["Background triggers may require different permissions","Shared execution roles can unintentionally couple unrelated functions","Permission changes require regression testing"],"security_implications":"Reduces cloud blast radius from serverless compromise.","performance_implications":"IAM authorization is generally provider-managed; narrower policies primarily affect security and governance.","testing":"Execute required function paths and attempt representative unauthorized cloud operations using the function identity.","verification":"Claim: function runtime identity cannot perform unrelated privileged operations. Evidence: effective-policy inspection and negative authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider serverless and IAM behavior is provider-specific.","provenance":["AWS Lambda security documentation","Google Cloud Functions security documentation","Azure Functions security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B376","D40-B397"]} {"record_id":"D40-B402","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Serverless Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unvalidated serverless event source","question":"How should a serverless function remediate untrusted event input from external triggers?","answer":"Authenticate the event source where supported, validate event structure and semantic constraints, and perform independent authorization before executing privileged operations.","objective":"Prevent crafted external events from triggering unauthorized serverless actions.","requirements":["Authenticate trusted event sources","Validate event schema","Validate critical fields","Authorize requested operation independently"],"constraints":["Event authentication capabilities vary by trigger type","Schema validity does not establish authorization"],"preconditions":["Function is triggered by externally influenced events","Event contents affect security-sensitive operations"],"input":"Incoming event metadata and payload","output":"Validated and authorized function operation","expected_behavior":"Malformed, unauthenticated, or unauthorized events are rejected before privileged actions occur.","failure_mode":"Attacker crafts an event that appears structurally valid and triggers privileged behavior.","root_cause":"Event payload is trusted without source authentication and authorization.","solution":"Separate source authentication, input validation, authorization, and action execution.","edge_cases":["Retries can create duplicate operations","Provider-generated metadata may be trusted only within its documented boundary","Schema evolution can introduce unexpected fields"],"security_implications":"Reduces event injection and privilege escalation through serverless triggers.","performance_implications":"Validation adds per-event processing cost proportional to payload complexity.","testing":"Submit malformed, forged, and unauthorized test events and verify rejection before side effects.","verification":"Claim: untrusted events cannot trigger unauthorized privileged actions. Evidence: negative trigger tests and authorization logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Trigger authentication and event formats depend on the serverless provider.","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B387","D40-B401"]} {"record_id":"D40-B403","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Serverless Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data retained in serverless temporary storage","question":"How should serverless workloads remediate sensitive data left in temporary execution storage?","answer":"Minimize sensitive temporary data, apply appropriate filesystem permissions, securely remove data when practical, and avoid assuming that temporary storage is automatically confidential between executions.","objective":"Reduce exposure of sensitive data through reusable or inspectable temporary execution storage.","requirements":["Minimize sensitive temporary files","Restrict file permissions","Avoid unnecessary persistence","Understand provider storage lifecycle"],"constraints":["Temporary storage semantics differ across providers","Secure deletion guarantees depend on the underlying platform"],"preconditions":["Function writes sensitive data to temporary storage","Execution environments may be reused"],"input":"Temporary-file usage and provider storage model","output":"Reduced sensitive-data persistence","expected_behavior":"Sensitive temporary data is retained only as long as necessary and is not exposed to unrelated execution contexts.","failure_mode":"Later execution or compromised code accesses sensitive remnants.","root_cause":"Temporary storage is treated as guaranteed private and ephemeral without validating provider semantics.","solution":"Minimize use, restrict permissions, clear data when feasible, and avoid storing secrets or unnecessary sensitive information.","edge_cases":["Warm execution environments can persist files between invocations","Crash paths may bypass cleanup","Provider-managed isolation determines cross-tenant guarantees"],"security_implications":"Reduces data leakage through temporary execution state.","performance_implications":"Secure cleanup adds filesystem operations but should be evaluated against actual workload requirements.","testing":"Write controlled sensitive data, invoke subsequent executions, and verify that unauthorized execution paths cannot retrieve it.","verification":"Claim: temporary execution storage does not expose retained sensitive data to unrelated application paths. Evidence: reuse tests and filesystem inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Provider-specific temporary-storage semantics.","provenance":["AWS Lambda security documentation","Google Cloud Functions documentation","Azure Functions documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B402","D40-B404"]} {"record_id":"D40-B404","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Serverless Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Serverless function outbound access without restriction","question":"How should a serverless function remediate unnecessary unrestricted outbound network access?","answer":"Restrict outbound destinations where the platform supports egress controls, route sensitive calls through controlled network boundaries, and allow only destinations required by the function.","objective":"Reduce data exfiltration and command-and-control opportunities after function compromise.","requirements":["Inventory required destinations","Apply egress restrictions","Separate sensitive functions from unrestricted network paths","Monitor denied and unusual outbound connections"],"constraints":["Serverless networking capabilities differ by provider","Some functions require broad access to managed services"],"preconditions":["Function can initiate outbound connections","Sensitive resources or credentials are accessible to the function"],"input":"Function network requirements and egress policy","output":"Controlled outbound connectivity","expected_behavior":"Function traffic is limited to documented destinations and required service endpoints.","failure_mode":"Compromised code exfiltrates secrets or data to an attacker-controlled destination.","root_cause":"Outbound connectivity is unrestricted despite limited business requirements.","solution":"Use provider-supported egress controls, network policies, service endpoints, DNS controls, and monitoring appropriate to the architecture.","edge_cases":["Dynamic third-party endpoints complicate allowlisting","DNS-based controls require careful resolution handling","Provider control-plane endpoints may require special access"],"security_implications":"Reduces exfiltration and external command-and-control paths.","performance_implications":"Network routing and inspection can add latency and processing overhead.","testing":"Attempt allowed and unauthorized outbound connections from a controlled function and verify policy enforcement.","verification":"Claim: function cannot reach destinations outside its declared egress policy. Evidence: connectivity tests and network-policy logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Provider and networking implementation-specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","Cloud-provider serverless networking documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B378","D40-B405"]} {"record_id":"D40-B405","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Problem → Remediation","subtopic":"Serverless Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Serverless function authorization based only on trigger possession","question":"How should a serverless API function remediate authorization that depends only on possession of a trigger or endpoint?","answer":"Authenticate the caller independently, authorize the requested resource and operation, and avoid treating knowledge of an invocation endpoint or event format as sufficient authority.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized callers from invoking privileged serverless functionality.","requirements":["Authenticate callers","Authorize operations and resources","Validate tenant or ownership context","Keep trigger authentication separate from business authorization"],"constraints":["Public endpoints may legitimately exist but must enforce operation-specific authorization","Event-source authentication does not necessarily identify the end user"],"preconditions":["Function performs protected business operations","Invocation endpoint is reachable by untrusted clients"],"input":"Caller identity, requested resource, and operation","output":"Authorization decision","expected_behavior":"Only callers with appropriate authority can perform the protected operation.","failure_mode":"Any caller who can invoke the function can perform privileged operations.","root_cause":"Invocation capability is incorrectly treated as application authorization.","solution":"Apply normal authentication and resource-level authorization inside the protected operation path.","edge_cases":["Service-to-service invocations require workload identity","Anonymous operations require explicit policy rather than accidental authorization","Tenant context must not be accepted solely from client input"],"security_implications":"Prevents unauthorized serverless business operations.","performance_implications":"Authorization checks add normal request-processing overhead.","testing":"Invoke the function using authenticated identities with different permissions and verify resource-level authorization.","verification":"Claim: invocation alone does not grant business authority. Evidence: negative authorization tests and policy inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Applicable across serverless HTTP and event-triggered architectures.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B402","D40-B404"]} {"record_id":"D40-B406","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Privacy and Data Security","subtopic":"Data Minimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unnecessary collection of sensitive fields","question":"How should an application remediate collection of sensitive data that is not required for the stated function?","answer":"Remove unnecessary fields from collection and processing, minimize retained attributes, and document the remaining data requirements and purpose.","objective":"Reduce security impact and exposure created by unnecessary sensitive-data collection.","requirements":["Identify required data fields","Remove unnecessary collection","Minimize downstream propagation","Document purpose and retention requirements"],"constraints":["Removing fields can affect analytics or legacy integrations","Data minimization should preserve required business functionality"],"preconditions":["Application collects sensitive fields beyond documented requirements"],"input":"Data schema and documented functional requirements","output":"Minimized data model and collection path","expected_behavior":"Only data necessary for the defined purpose is collected and propagated.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary sensitive data becomes available to attackers or unauthorized internal consumers.","root_cause":"Collection is driven by convenience or future use rather than documented necessity.","solution":"Perform field-level data inventory, remove unnecessary collection, and enforce schema contracts that prevent accidental reintroduction.","edge_cases":["Derived fields can still reveal sensitive information","Logs and analytics pipelines may silently recreate removed data","Backward-compatible APIs may require temporary migration handling"],"security_implications":"Reduces breach impact and attack surface for sensitive data.","performance_implications":"Smaller payloads and records can reduce storage and processing costs, but exact gains require measurement.","testing":"Submit requests containing unnecessary sensitive fields and verify they are rejected or discarded according to policy.","verification":"Claim: unnecessary sensitive fields are not retained. Evidence: schema inspection, request tests, and storage verification.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Application-specific; privacy obligations vary by jurisdiction and data category.","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","OWASP Privacy Principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B407"]} {"record_id":"D40-B407","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Privacy and Data Security","subtopic":"Data Retention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Indefinite retention of sensitive application data","question":"How should an application remediate sensitive data retained indefinitely?","answer":"Define retention periods based on documented operational, legal, and security requirements, automate deletion or anonymization where appropriate, and ensure replicas, caches, and backups follow a governed lifecycle.","objective":"Reduce long-term exposure of sensitive information.","requirements":["Define retention requirements","Automate lifecycle controls","Include replicas and derived stores","Document backup-retention limitations"],"constraints":["Backups may have separate retention schedules","Deletion from immutable storage may require controlled expiry rather than immediate erasure"],"preconditions":["Sensitive data is retained without a bounded lifecycle"],"input":"Data inventory and retention policy","output":"Governed retention and deletion process","expected_behavior":"Data is retained only for the defined period and then securely deleted or transformed according to policy.","failure_mode":"Old sensitive records remain accessible indefinitely after operational need ends.","root_cause":"Retention lifecycle is undefined or implemented only at the primary database.","solution":"Implement data lifecycle policies across primary stores, caches, replicas, analytics systems, and backups where feasible.","edge_cases":["Legal holds can suspend deletion","Derived analytics can retain identifying information","Deletion jobs can fail and require monitoring"],"security_implications":"Reduces exposure from historical data compromise.","performance_implications":"Deletion jobs can consume database and storage resources and should be scheduled safely.","testing":"Create controlled records with known expiration times and verify deletion or anonymization across relevant stores.","verification":"Claim: expired sensitive data is removed or transformed according to policy. Evidence: lifecycle tests and deletion audit records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Storage-system and retention-policy specific.","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Privacy Principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B406","D40-B408"]} {"record_id":"D40-B408","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Privacy and Data Security","subtopic":"Data Export","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Cross-tenant data exposure during data export","question":"How should multi-tenant exports remediate cross-tenant data leakage?","answer":"Authorize the export against the requesting tenant and resource scope, generate the dataset from tenant-scoped queries, validate output ownership before release, and protect generated export files.","objective":"Prevent tenant isolation failures during bulk data export.","requirements":["Authorize export scope","Apply tenant-scoped data selection","Validate generated records","Protect export artifacts"],"constraints":["Large exports may run asynchronously","Export workers require explicit tenant context"],"preconditions":["System supports tenant-scoped exports","Export process can access large datasets"],"input":"Authenticated tenant, export request, and dataset scope","output":"Tenant-scoped export artifact","expected_behavior":"Export contains only records authorized for the requesting tenant.","failure_mode":"Export includes records belonging to another tenant.","root_cause":"Export path bypasses ordinary row-level authorization or relies on client-provided tenant identifiers.","solution":"Bind export scope to trusted tenant identity, enforce authorization in worker queries, and validate output before delivery.","edge_cases":["Asynchronous jobs must preserve trusted tenant context","Administrative exports require separate authorization","Cached export files can outlive the initiating session"],"security_implications":"Reduces bulk cross-tenant data leakage.","performance_implications":"Output validation can add processing cost for large exports; streaming and sampling should not replace complete authorization checks.","testing":"Request exports containing controlled multi-tenant records and verify each tenant receives only its authorized data.","verification":"Claim: export artifacts contain no unauthorized tenant records. Evidence: cross-tenant export tests and artifact inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Application and database architecture specific.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B382","D40-B409"]} {"record_id":"D40-B409","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Privacy and Data Security","subtopic":"Pseudonymization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Direct identifiers unnecessarily propagated to analytics systems","question":"How should an application remediate unnecessary exposure of direct identifiers in analytics pipelines?","answer":"Replace direct identifiers with appropriately protected pseudonymous identifiers where the analytical purpose permits, restrict re-identification data, and preserve authorization boundaries across analytics stores.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary exposure of identifying data while retaining legitimate analytical capability.","requirements":["Identify analytical requirements","Separate direct identifiers from analytical datasets","Protect mapping information","Restrict re-identification access"],"constraints":["Pseudonymization does not make data anonymous","Some analyses legitimately require direct identifiers"],"preconditions":["Analytics pipeline receives direct identifiers","Business analysis can operate using a pseudonymous key"],"input":"Source records and analytics requirements","output":"Pseudonymized analytical dataset","expected_behavior":"Analytics consumers receive only the identifiers required for their documented purpose.","failure_mode":"Broad analytics access exposes direct identifiers unnecessarily.","root_cause":"Source-system identifiers are propagated without evaluating analytical necessity.","solution":"Use controlled pseudonymous identifiers, isolate mapping tables, restrict re-identification authority, and secure analytical datasets.","edge_cases":["Small datasets may still permit re-identification","Joinable quasi-identifiers can reveal identity","Mapping-key compromise defeats the intended separation"],"security_implications":"Reduces direct identifier exposure but does not eliminate privacy risk.","performance_implications":"Additional mapping or tokenization operations add processing overhead.","testing":"Inspect analytics datasets and verify that unauthorized consumers cannot obtain direct identifiers or mapping data.","verification":"Claim: analytics consumers cannot re-identify records without authorized mapping access. Evidence: access tests and dataset inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Application and analytics-platform specific.","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST Privacy Risk Assessment Methodology"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B406","D40-B408"]} {"record_id":"D40-B410","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Privacy and Data Security","subtopic":"Deletion Controls","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"User deletion request bypassed by derived stores","question":"How should a system remediate deletion workflows that remove primary records but retain identifiable copies in derived systems?","answer":"Map data propagation and derived stores, propagate deletion or de-identification actions to applicable systems, and verify completion rather than assuming deletion from the primary database is sufficient.","objective":"Prevent stale identifiable copies from surviving a governed deletion process.","requirements":["Inventory data flows","Identify derived stores","Propagate deletion or de-identification","Track completion and failures"],"constraints":["Some immutable or backup stores have delayed deletion","Derived datasets may contain transformed but still identifying information"],"preconditions":["Primary records are copied to secondary systems","Deletion requirements apply to those records"],"input":"Deletion request and data-lineage map","output":"Tracked deletion state across applicable systems","expected_behavior":"Applicable copies are removed or transformed according to the governing policy.","failure_mode":"Deleted records remain identifiable in analytics, caches, replicas, or exports.","root_cause":"Deletion is implemented as a single-database operation without end-to-end data lineage.","solution":"Implement deletion orchestration tied to data lineage, monitor failures, and verify downstream completion.","edge_cases":["Backups may follow separate lifecycle policies","Search indexes can retain deleted fields","Event logs may contain identifiers independently"],"security_implications":"Reduces persistence of sensitive data after authorized deletion.","performance_implications":"Deletion propagation consumes resources across dependent systems.","testing":"Create controlled records, propagate them through the full pipeline, execute deletion, and verify all applicable stores.","verification":"Claim: governed deletion reaches all in-scope identifiable copies. Evidence: lineage-based deletion test and completion audit.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Data-platform specific; retention and deletion obligations depend on governing requirements.","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B407","D40-B409"]} {"record_id":"D40-B411","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Observability Security","subtopic":"Trace Data","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive data embedded in distributed traces","question":"How should observability systems remediate sensitive values captured in traces?","answer":"Redact or avoid collecting sensitive fields, apply attribute allowlists where supported, restrict telemetry access, and ensure exporters and retention systems receive the same protection.","objective":"Prevent observability infrastructure from becoming a secondary repository of sensitive data.","requirements":["Classify telemetry attributes","Use allowlists or redaction","Restrict telemetry access","Secure exporters and storage"],"constraints":["Debugging can benefit from detailed telemetry","Redaction must occur before data reaches uncontrolled exporters where possible"],"preconditions":["Application emits distributed traces","Trace attributes can contain user or credential data"],"input":"Trace instrumentation and telemetry policy","output":"Minimized and protected trace data","expected_behavior":"Sensitive values are not emitted into traces unless explicitly authorized and protected.","failure_mode":"Telemetry consumers gain access to credentials or sensitive business data.","root_cause":"Instrumentation captures request attributes without security classification.","solution":"Define safe telemetry schemas, redact sensitive fields at instrumentation boundaries, and restrict trace storage and export.","edge_cases":["Automatic HTTP instrumentation can capture headers or query parameters","Error attributes can contain raw exception input","Third-party exporters may have independent retention policies"],"security_implications":"Reduces sensitive-data leakage through observability systems.","performance_implications":"Redaction and attribute filtering add telemetry-processing overhead.","testing":"Send controlled sensitive values through instrumented requests and inspect traces and exported telemetry.","verification":"Claim: sensitive values are excluded from protected trace fields. Evidence: instrumentation tests and exporter inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Telemetry SDK and exporter behavior varies by implementation.","provenance":["OpenTelemetry security guidance","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B412"]} {"record_id":"D40-B412","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Observability Security","subtopic":"Telemetry Endpoint Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthenticated telemetry ingestion endpoint","question":"How should an observability system remediate an exposed telemetry ingestion endpoint?","answer":"Authenticate and authorize telemetry producers where the deployment requires it, restrict network exposure, validate payloads, and apply resource limits to prevent unauthorized injection or exhaustion.","objective":"Prevent attackers from injecting false telemetry or exhausting observability infrastructure.","requirements":["Restrict ingestion network exposure","Authenticate producers when applicable","Validate telemetry payloads","Enforce rate and size limits"],"constraints":["Some telemetry collectors operate inside trusted network boundaries","Authentication mechanisms differ by telemetry protocol"],"preconditions":["Telemetry collector accepts network traffic","Telemetry influences monitoring, alerting, or operational decisions"],"input":"Telemetry request and collector policy","output":"Accepted or rejected telemetry","expected_behavior":"Only authorized or appropriately bounded telemetry enters the observability pipeline.","failure_mode":"Attacker injects false events or floods the collector.","root_cause":"Telemetry ingestion is treated as inherently trusted because it is infrastructure traffic.","solution":"Apply transport security, producer authentication, network restrictions, schema validation, and resource limits appropriate to the deployment.","edge_cases":["Internal network location alone may not be sufficient trust","Collector-to-collector forwarding requires identity propagation","High-volume legitimate telemetry needs carefully sized limits"],"security_implications":"Protects monitoring integrity and availability.","performance_implications":"Authentication, validation, and rate limiting consume collector resources.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized telemetry submission and controlled flooding against a test collector.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized telemetry cannot materially alter protected observability state. Evidence: authentication and rate-limit tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Collector and telemetry protocol specific.","provenance":["OpenTelemetry security guidance","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B411","D40-B413"]} {"record_id":"D40-B413","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Observability Security","subtopic":"Alert Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security alerts modifiable by application identities","question":"How should security monitoring remediate alert stores that can be modified by ordinary application identities?","answer":"Separate telemetry production from security-alert administration, restrict modification and deletion privileges, preserve immutable or append-only evidence where appropriate, and audit alert-state changes.","objective":"Prevent compromised applications from suppressing or altering security detections.","requirements":["Separate producer and administrator permissions","Restrict alert mutation","Protect historical evidence","Audit state changes"],"constraints":["Operational workflows may require controlled alert updates","Immutable storage may have cost and retention implications"],"preconditions":["Application or service identities can write security-alert data","Alerts influence incident response"],"input":"Alert storage permissions and identity model","output":"Protected alert lifecycle","expected_behavior":"Ordinary workloads can emit telemetry but cannot silently delete or alter security decisions.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload suppresses evidence of its own activity.","root_cause":"Telemetry ingestion and security-control administration share the same authority.","solution":"Separate identities and permissions, preserve audit history, and require elevated authorization for alert changes.","edge_cases":["Automated enrichment may legitimately update alerts","Alert correlation systems need controlled write access","Deletion requirements may conflict with immutable retention"],"security_implications":"Improves integrity of detection and incident evidence.","performance_implications":"Immutable or append-only storage can affect write and query characteristics.","testing":"Attempt alert modification and deletion using normal workload identities and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: ordinary application identities cannot suppress security alerts. Evidence: permission tests and audit records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Observability platform specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B412","D40-B414"]} {"record_id":"D40-B414","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Observability Security","subtopic":"Log Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Log injection through untrusted message fields","question":"How should applications remediate log injection caused by attacker-controlled input?","answer":"Structure log records instead of concatenating untrusted text into control-sensitive formats, encode or sanitize fields for the selected sink, and preserve clear field boundaries for downstream processing.","objective":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from forging or corrupting security-relevant log records.","requirements":["Use structured logging","Treat logged fields as untrusted data","Encode according to output sink","Preserve event boundaries"],"constraints":["Logging formats differ between applications and collectors","Sanitization must match the actual parser or transport"],"preconditions":["User-controlled values are logged","Downstream systems parse log lines or fields"],"input":"Untrusted application value and logging operation","output":"Structured log event with unambiguous fields","expected_behavior":"Attacker input cannot create additional trusted-looking events or alter log structure.","failure_mode":"Injected newline, delimiter, or control data creates forged log records.","root_cause":"Untrusted text is concatenated directly into structured or line-oriented logs.","solution":"Use structured logging APIs, encode fields correctly, and validate collector parsing.","edge_cases":["JSON encoding alone does not solve unsafe downstream interpretation","Unicode control characters can affect display or processing","Log collectors may transform records again"],"security_implications":"Protects audit integrity and detection accuracy.","performance_implications":"Structured serialization adds processing overhead but can simplify downstream parsing.","testing":"Log controlled inputs containing newlines, delimiters, escape sequences, and control characters and inspect stored records.","verification":"Claim: untrusted input cannot forge separate trusted log events. Evidence: adversarial logging tests and parsed-record inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Logging library and collector specific.","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","OWASP Log Injection guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B413"]} {"record_id":"D40-B415","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Observability Security","subtopic":"Time Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unreliable security-event timestamps","question":"How should distributed systems remediate unreliable timestamps in security events?","answer":"Synchronize system clocks using trusted mechanisms, record authoritative event-time metadata where available, and distinguish event occurrence time from ingestion or processing time.","objective":"Preserve reliable temporal ordering for security investigation and detection.","requirements":["Use reliable clock synchronization","Record relevant time semantics","Preserve ingestion time separately","Detect significant clock drift"],"constraints":["Distributed systems cannot guarantee identical clocks","Offline systems may experience delayed synchronization"],"preconditions":["Security decisions or investigations depend on event ordering","Events originate across multiple hosts or services"],"input":"Event timestamps, ingestion timestamps, and clock synchronization state","output":"Security events with interpretable temporal metadata","expected_behavior":"Investigators can distinguish source event time from processing time and identify significant clock uncertainty.","failure_mode":"Incorrect timestamps cause events to be misordered or detection windows to fail.","root_cause":"Systems assume local timestamps are globally consistent without monitoring clock health.","solution":"Use authenticated or trusted time synchronization, monitor drift, and preserve multiple timestamp fields when necessary.","edge_cases":["Clock jumps can affect expiration logic","Leap-second handling differs by time source and platform","Delayed telemetry can make ingestion time very different from occurrence time"],"security_implications":"Improves incident reconstruction and time-based security controls.","performance_implications":"Time synchronization and metadata processing have minimal normal overhead.","testing":"Introduce controlled clock skew in test environments and verify detection, ordering, and timestamp semantics.","verification":"Claim: security systems expose sufficient timing information to reason about event order under bounded clock skew. Evidence: skew tests and timestamp metadata inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Time synchronization behavior is operating-system and infrastructure specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B414","D40-B416"]} {"record_id":"D40-B416","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"AI Application Security","subtopic":"Prompt Injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Untrusted retrieved content influencing privileged AI actions","question":"How should an AI application remediate prompt injection from retrieved or user-controlled content?","answer":"Treat retrieved and user-provided content as untrusted data, separate instructions from data, constrain model-authorized actions through deterministic policy checks, and require independent authorization before privileged tool execution.","objective":"Prevent untrusted text from overriding system policy or causing unauthorized external actions.","requirements":["Define trusted instruction boundaries","Treat retrieved content as untrusted","Authorize tools outside model output","Validate tool arguments"],"constraints":["Model behavior is probabilistic and cannot itself serve as the sole security boundary","Prompt separation reduces but does not eliminate injection risk"],"preconditions":["LLM receives external or retrieved content","Model can request tools or privileged operations"],"input":"User input, retrieved content, model-generated action request","output":"Policy-checked action decision","expected_behavior":"Untrusted content cannot directly grant the model authority to perform privileged actions.","failure_mode":"Injected content causes unauthorized tool invocation or data disclosure.","root_cause":"Model-generated instructions are treated as trusted authorization decisions.","solution":"Separate data from control instructions, enforce deterministic tool authorization, validate arguments, restrict tool scope, and require confirmation for high-impact actions.","edge_cases":["Indirect prompt injection can arrive through documents, websites, tickets, or database records","Tool descriptions can themselves become an instruction-injection surface","Multi-agent handoffs require explicit trust boundaries"],"security_implications":"Reduces prompt-injection-driven data exfiltration and unauthorized tool use.","performance_implications":"Additional policy checks and validation add orchestration latency.","testing":"Use controlled malicious instructions in retrieved content and verify that prohibited tools and data paths remain inaccessible.","verification":"Claim: untrusted model context cannot independently authorize privileged actions. Evidence: adversarial prompt tests and external policy-decision logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"AI framework and model behavior varies; security controls must remain external to probabilistic model output.","provenance":["OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","NIST AI Risk Management Framework"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B387","D40-B417"]} {"record_id":"D40-B417","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"AI Application Security","subtopic":"Tool Authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"AI-generated tool arguments executed without validation","question":"How should an AI agent remediate unsafe execution of model-generated tool arguments?","answer":"Validate tool arguments against strict schemas and business security constraints, authorize the requested resource and operation independently, and reject arguments outside the tool's allowed capability.","objective":"Prevent model errors or prompt injection from turning into unsafe tool execution.","requirements":["Define typed tool schemas","Validate values and resource scope","Authorize operations independently","Apply resource and rate limits"],"constraints":["Schema-valid arguments can still be unauthorized","Model-generated values may be syntactically correct but operationally dangerous"],"preconditions":["AI system can invoke tools or APIs","Model controls some tool arguments"],"input":"Model-generated tool call and authenticated execution context","output":"Validated and authorized tool invocation","expected_behavior":"Only tool calls satisfying schema, policy, and authorization requirements execute.","failure_mode":"A model-generated request performs an unauthorized or destructive operation.","root_cause":"Tool invocation is treated as trusted because it originated from the model.","solution":"Place deterministic authorization and validation between model output and tool execution.","edge_cases":["Path traversal can occur through syntactically valid paths","Resource identifiers must be authorized against the caller context","High-impact actions may require explicit human approval"],"security_implications":"Reduces agentic privilege escalation and unsafe automation.","performance_implications":"Validation and policy checks add tool-call latency.","testing":"Generate controlled invalid, unauthorized, and destructive tool arguments and verify they are rejected before execution.","verification":"Claim: model output alone cannot cause an unauthorized tool operation. Evidence: negative tool-call tests and authorization logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Agent framework specific; deterministic enforcement should remain outside model generation.","provenance":["OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","NIST AI RMF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B416","D40-B418"]} {"record_id":"D40-B418","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"AI Application Security","subtopic":"RAG Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthorized retrieval across tenant boundaries","question":"How should a retrieval-augmented AI system remediate cross-tenant document retrieval?","answer":"Apply tenant authorization before retrieval, store tenant or access-control metadata with indexed content, enforce authorization during candidate selection, and verify final context membership before model use.","objective":"Prevent retrieval systems from exposing one tenant's documents to another tenant.","requirements":["Bind indexed content to tenant identity","Filter retrieval by authorization context","Validate retrieved records before prompt construction","Protect vector and metadata stores"],"constraints":["Embedding similarity alone cannot enforce authorization","Cached retrieval results can leak data across users"],"preconditions":["AI system retrieves documents for multiple tenants","Shared vector or search infrastructure is used"],"input":"Authenticated tenant identity and retrieval query","output":"Authorized tenant-scoped retrieval context","expected_behavior":"Retrieved context contains only documents the requesting identity is authorized to access.","failure_mode":"Similarity search returns another tenant's document.","root_cause":"Security filtering is applied after retrieval or omitted from the retrieval index.","solution":"Enforce authorization-aware retrieval filters, tenant-scoped indexes where appropriate, cache isolation, and final authorization validation.","edge_cases":["Documents can belong to multiple authorization groups","Metadata filters must be tamper-resistant","Re-ranking can reintroduce unauthorized candidates if filtering is incomplete"],"security_implications":"Prevents cross-tenant information disclosure through AI retrieval.","performance_implications":"Authorization filtering can increase index or query complexity.","testing":"Seed controlled documents for multiple tenants and issue identical retrieval queries under different identities.","verification":"Claim: retrieval context contains no unauthorized tenant content. Evidence: cross-tenant retrieval tests and index-policy inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Vector database, search engine, and AI framework specific.","provenance":["OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B382","D40-B416"]} {"record_id":"D40-B419","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"AI Application Security","subtopic":"Model Output Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe direct use of model output in executable contexts","question":"How should an AI application remediate model output that is directly inserted into SQL, shell commands, HTML, or other executable contexts?","answer":"Treat model output as untrusted input, use parameterized APIs or typed interfaces, apply context-specific encoding, and validate against an allowlist before execution.","objective":"Prevent model-generated text from becoming an injection primitive.","requirements":["Treat model output as untrusted","Use safe APIs and parameterization","Apply context-specific encoding","Enforce authorization independently"],"constraints":["Output filtering alone is not a universal security boundary","Different execution contexts require different controls"],"preconditions":["Model output reaches an interpreter or executable API","Model output may be influenced by untrusted input"],"input":"Generated model output and execution context","output":"Validated non-injection execution request","expected_behavior":"Model output cannot alter executable structure outside explicitly allowed parameters.","failure_mode":"Model output creates SQL, shell, HTML, or code injection.","root_cause":"Model-generated content is trusted as if it were validated program structure.","solution":"Use typed tool interfaces, parameterized APIs, context-specific encoding, and strict authorization.","edge_cases":["Code generation requires separate compilation and sandboxing controls","HTML output requires context-specific XSS defenses","Natural-language values can contain valid but dangerous syntax"],"security_implications":"Reduces AI-assisted injection and unsafe code execution.","performance_implications":"Validation and safe serialization add modest processing overhead.","testing":"Provide adversarial model outputs containing injection syntax and verify they remain data rather than executable structure.","verification":"Claim: model output cannot directly alter executable syntax. Evidence: context-specific injection tests and API inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Application and model framework specific.","provenance":["OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B101","D40-B417"]} {"record_id":"D40-B420","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"AI Application Security","subtopic":"AI Supply Chain","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unverified third-party model or plugin artifact","question":"How should an AI system remediate use of untrusted model, plugin, or agent artifacts?","answer":"Verify artifact provenance and integrity, restrict execution permissions, scan or validate artifacts before deployment, and maintain an inventory of models and extensions in use.","objective":"Reduce supply-chain compromise through AI-specific artifacts and extensions.","requirements":["Track artifact identity and provenance","Verify integrity","Restrict runtime permissions","Maintain artifact inventory"],"constraints":["Model provenance can be more complex than binary provenance","Third-party extensions may execute with application privileges"],"preconditions":["Application imports external models, plugins, tools, or agent components","Artifacts can influence application behavior"],"input":"AI artifact and trust policy","output":"Verified and bounded AI component","expected_behavior":"Only approved artifacts satisfying integrity and provenance requirements are loaded with the permissions they require.","failure_mode":"A malicious or modified AI component gains application privileges.","root_cause":"AI artifacts are trusted based on source location or package name without integrity and provenance verification.","solution":"Use signed or otherwise verifiable artifacts where available, maintain provenance metadata, sandbox extensions, and restrict runtime permissions.","edge_cases":["Model weights can be large and difficult to inspect fully","Dependencies of plugins can introduce additional supply-chain risk","A verified artifact can still contain vulnerable behavior"],"security_implications":"Reduces AI component supply-chain compromise.","performance_implications":"Artifact verification and scanning increase deployment or startup processing.","testing":"Attempt deployment with an altered or unauthorized artifact and verify rejection.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized AI artifacts cannot enter the trusted runtime path. Evidence: provenance verification, integrity tests, and deployment-policy records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C++","Rust"],"version_scope":"AI framework and artifact registry specific.","provenance":["NIST AI RMF","SLSA","OpenSSF guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B353","D40-B419"]} {"record_id":"D40-B421","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Local Data Storage","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive mobile data stored without platform protection","question":"How should a mobile application remediate sensitive data stored in unprotected local files?","answer":"Use platform-provided secure storage mechanisms for secrets, minimize sensitive local persistence, enforce appropriate access controls, and avoid storing credentials in ordinary application files.","objective":"Reduce credential and sensitive-data exposure from device storage compromise.","requirements":["Classify locally stored data","Use platform secure storage for secrets","Minimize persistence","Protect backups where platform controls permit"],"constraints":["Secure-storage APIs differ across mobile platforms","Device compromise can weaken application-level storage guarantees"],"preconditions":["Mobile application stores sensitive data locally","Ordinary filesystem storage is accessible to application-level components"],"input":"Mobile storage design and data classification","output":"Protected local storage architecture","expected_behavior":"Reusable secrets are stored using the platform's appropriate protected storage mechanism.","failure_mode":"An attacker extracts credentials from ordinary application files or backups.","root_cause":"Sensitive data is stored without using available platform security controls.","solution":"Use Android Keystore, iOS Keychain, or platform-equivalent secure mechanisms as appropriate, and minimize local sensitive state.","edge_cases":["Backups may copy application data depending on platform configuration","Rooted or jailbroken devices weaken assumptions","Encryption keys stored alongside ciphertext provide little protection"],"security_implications":"Reduces local credential and sensitive-data theft.","performance_implications":"Secure storage APIs can have different latency characteristics from ordinary files; measure if relevant.","testing":"Inspect application storage in a controlled test device and verify sensitive values are not present in ordinary files.","verification":"Claim: protected secrets are not stored in unprotected application files. Evidence: filesystem inspection and secure-storage tests.","languages":["Java","C++","Swift","Kotlin"],"version_scope":"Android and iOS storage behavior varies by OS version and device security configuration.","provenance":["OWASP MASVS","OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide","Android Keystore documentation","Apple Keychain Services documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B394","D40-B422"]} {"record_id":"D40-B422","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Mobile Backup Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive application data included in device backups","question":"How should a mobile application remediate sensitive data unintentionally included in backups?","answer":"Classify backup-eligible data, exclude or protect sensitive data according to platform capabilities and requirements, and test backup and restore behavior rather than assuming local storage controls automatically cover backups.","objective":"Prevent sensitive application data from escaping through backup systems.","requirements":["Inventory backup contents","Exclude unnecessary sensitive data","Use platform backup controls","Test restored application state"],"constraints":["Backup behavior differs across operating systems and versions","Some platform-managed backup services have provider-specific controls"],"preconditions":["Application data is eligible for device or cloud backup","Sensitive data is stored locally"],"input":"Application data and backup configuration","output":"Controlled backup dataset","expected_behavior":"Sensitive data is either excluded from backup or protected according to the application's security requirements.","failure_mode":"Backup copies expose data that was otherwise protected on the device.","root_cause":"Backup storage is omitted from the threat model for local application data.","solution":"Configure platform backup exclusions or protections and verify actual backup contents.","edge_cases":["Secure-storage items can have distinct backup semantics","User migration between devices can change threat assumptions","Debug builds may use different backup configuration"],"security_implications":"Reduces data leakage through device and cloud backups.","performance_implications":"Backup exclusion or encryption can affect backup size and processing time.","testing":"Perform controlled backup and restore operations and inspect whether sensitive test data is included.","verification":"Claim: sensitive local data follows the intended backup policy. Evidence: backup inspection and restore tests.","languages":["Java","Kotlin","Swift","C++"],"version_scope":"Platform and OS-version specific.","provenance":["OWASP MASVS","Android backup documentation","Apple Platform Security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B421","D40-B423"]} {"record_id":"D40-B423","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"WebView Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe JavaScript bridge in mobile WebView","question":"How should a mobile application remediate an overly powerful JavaScript bridge exposed to WebView content?","answer":"Expose only narrowly required native methods, validate all arguments, restrict trusted content origins, and avoid exposing sensitive native capabilities to untrusted web content.","objective":"Prevent web content from invoking privileged native application functionality.","requirements":["Minimize bridge surface","Validate bridge inputs","Restrict trusted content","Require authorization for sensitive native operations"],"constraints":["WebView capabilities differ by platform","Trusted content can become untrusted if external resources are loaded dynamically"],"preconditions":["Application exposes JavaScript-to-native communication","WebView content can be influenced externally"],"input":"WebView origin and JavaScript bridge call","output":"Validated native operation or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only approved content can invoke narrowly scoped native capabilities.","failure_mode":"Injected or malicious web content invokes sensitive native APIs.","root_cause":"Bridge exposes broad native functionality without strong content trust boundaries.","solution":"Minimize bridge methods, enforce origin/content restrictions, validate parameters, and remove unnecessary native exposure.","edge_cases":["Navigation to an attacker-controlled URL can change the trust context","File URLs and local content may have different security semantics","Bridge methods can expose filesystem or credential capabilities"],"security_implications":"Reduces WebView-to-native privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"Origin and argument validation add negligible typical overhead.","testing":"Load controlled untrusted content and attempt unauthorized bridge calls.","verification":"Claim: untrusted WebView content cannot invoke protected native capabilities. Evidence: bridge enumeration and negative invocation tests.","languages":["Java","Kotlin","Swift","JavaScript","C++"],"version_scope":"Platform WebView behavior varies by OS and WebView implementation version.","provenance":["OWASP MASVS","OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B419","D40-B424"]} {"record_id":"D40-B424","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Application Components","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unprotected exported mobile components","question":"How should Android applications remediate exported components that expose privileged functionality?","answer":"Set component exposure explicitly, require appropriate permissions for components that must be externally accessible, and remove exported access when external invocation is unnecessary.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized applications or external actors from invoking privileged application components.","requirements":["Inventory exported components","Disable unnecessary external exposure","Require permissions for required external interfaces","Validate caller identity and input"],"constraints":["Some components must be exported for legitimate integrations","Platform behavior and manifest defaults vary by Android version and component type"],"preconditions":["Application contains activities, services, receivers, or providers","Components expose privileged behavior"],"input":"Component manifest and caller context","output":"Controlled component invocation","expected_behavior":"Only intended callers can invoke externally accessible privileged components.","failure_mode":"A malicious application invokes an exported component and reaches protected functionality.","root_cause":"Component exposure is broader than intended or relies on unsafe defaults.","solution":"Declare exported state explicitly, apply permissions, validate callers and inputs, and remove unnecessary exports.","edge_cases":["Intent filters do not automatically establish authorization","Pending intents have separate security considerations","Provider URI permissions can create indirect access"],"security_implications":"Reduces inter-application privilege escalation.","performance_implications":"Component permission checks are platform-managed and normally have negligible application overhead.","testing":"Attempt component invocation from an unauthorized application and verify denial.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized applications cannot invoke protected exported components. Evidence: manifest inspection and cross-application security tests.","languages":["Java","Kotlin"],"version_scope":"Android component and manifest behavior varies by API level.","provenance":["Android Developers security documentation","OWASP MASVS","OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B423","D40-B425"]} {"record_id":"D40-B425","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Clipboard Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Sensitive secrets copied to system clipboard","question":"How should mobile applications remediate copying sensitive credentials to the clipboard?","answer":"Avoid placing reusable secrets in the clipboard when possible, clearly distinguish sensitive copy operations, apply platform-supported sensitive-content protections, and clear clipboard data when appropriate and supported.","objective":"Reduce exposure of credentials and sensitive values through shared clipboard mechanisms.","requirements":["Classify copied data","Avoid unnecessary secret copying","Use platform-sensitive clipboard controls","Define expiration or clearing behavior where appropriate"],"constraints":["Clipboard behavior differs by operating system version","Users may legitimately need to copy non-secret sensitive information"],"preconditions":["Application allows copying sensitive values","Clipboard can be accessed by other applications or system services according to platform behavior"],"input":"Sensitive value and clipboard operation","output":"Controlled clipboard exposure","expected_behavior":"Reusable credentials are not unnecessarily exposed through shared clipboard state.","failure_mode":"Another application or system component obtains a copied secret.","root_cause":"Clipboard is treated as private application storage when it may be shared or observable.","solution":"Minimize sensitive clipboard use and use platform-supported sensitive-content protections.","edge_cases":["Passwords and one-time codes may have different usability requirements","Clipboard history features can extend retention","Screen capture protections do not necessarily protect clipboard contents"],"security_implications":"Reduces credential leakage through clipboard history or other consumers.","performance_implications":"No material performance impact is expected from avoiding unnecessary clipboard operations.","testing":"Copy controlled sensitive values and inspect clipboard behavior on supported platform versions.","verification":"Claim: sensitive clipboard content follows the defined exposure policy. Evidence: platform-specific clipboard tests and configuration inspection.","languages":["Java","Kotlin","Swift"],"version_scope":"Platform and OS-version specific.","provenance":["OWASP MASVS","Android Developers clipboard security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B421","D40-B422"]} {"record_id":"D40-B426","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"File and Content Security","subtopic":"Archive Processing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Archive extraction without cumulative resource limits","question":"How should archive-processing code remediate decompression-bomb risks?","answer":"Enforce limits on archive entry count, compressed size, uncompressed size, nesting depth, and cumulative extraction size before and during extraction.","objective":"Prevent archive inputs from exhausting CPU, memory, storage, or file descriptors.","requirements":["Set cumulative extraction limits","Limit entry count","Limit individual and total uncompressed size","Limit nesting or recursion where applicable"],"constraints":["Archive formats expose different metadata and compression behavior","Exact limits must reflect legitimate workload requirements"],"preconditions":["Application accepts or processes user-controlled archives","Extraction can allocate significant resources"],"input":"Archive and extraction policy","output":"Accepted extraction or controlled rejection","expected_behavior":"Archives exceeding configured resource budgets are rejected or safely processed without exhausting resources.","failure_mode":"Compressed input expands into excessive files or data and causes denial of service.","root_cause":"Only individual file size or compressed input size is limited.","solution":"Apply cumulative and format-aware resource limits before and during extraction.","edge_cases":["Compression ratios can be extreme","Nested archives can bypass single-level limits","Symbolic links and special files require separate handling"],"security_implications":"Reduces archive-based denial-of-service attacks.","performance_implications":"Pre-extraction inspection and cumulative accounting add processing overhead but can prevent expensive extraction.","testing":"Use controlled archives varying entry count, compression ratio, nesting depth, and total expansion size.","verification":"Claim: archive processing remains within the declared resource budget. Evidence: boundary and resource-exhaustion tests.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Archive-library and runtime specific.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","OWASP Denial of Service guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B125","D40-B390"]} {"record_id":"D40-B427","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"File and Content Security","subtopic":"Content-Type Validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"File type validation based only on client-supplied MIME type","question":"How should upload handling remediate reliance on client-supplied Content-Type values?","answer":"Treat client MIME types as untrusted hints, validate file signatures or content using safe parsers where appropriate, enforce allowed extensions and storage rules, and never rely on a single client-controlled field.","objective":"Prevent malicious files from bypassing upload restrictions through forged metadata.","requirements":["Treat MIME metadata as untrusted","Use content-aware validation where appropriate","Restrict allowed file classes","Store uploads safely"],"constraints":["File signatures are not sufficient for every format","Parsing untrusted content can itself create attack surface"],"preconditions":["Application accepts uploaded files","Security policy depends on file type"],"input":"Uploaded file, filename, MIME type, and content","output":"Validated upload classification","expected_behavior":"Forged MIME metadata alone cannot cause a prohibited file to be accepted.","failure_mode":"Executable or malicious content is accepted because its MIME type was altered.","root_cause":"Validation trusts attacker-controlled metadata.","solution":"Combine independent validation signals and isolate uploaded content from executable paths.","edge_cases":["Polyglot files can satisfy multiple format interpretations","Compressed formats require recursive validation","Image or document parsers may contain vulnerabilities"],"security_implications":"Reduces malicious upload and parser-exploitation risk.","performance_implications":"Content inspection increases upload processing cost.","testing":"Upload files with mismatched MIME type, extension, and content and verify policy decisions.","verification":"Claim: client-supplied MIME type cannot alone bypass file-type policy. Evidence: mismatch tests and stored-file inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"File-validation behavior depends on selected parsers and runtime.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B121","D40-B426"]} {"record_id":"D40-B428","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"File and Content Security","subtopic":"Content Rendering","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"User-uploaded active content served from a trusted origin","question":"How should an application remediate serving user-uploaded active content from the primary application origin?","answer":"Store and serve untrusted uploads from an isolated origin or otherwise prevent them from executing with the application's trusted origin privileges.","objective":"Prevent uploaded HTML, scripts, or active content from gaining the application's origin authority.","requirements":["Separate untrusted content origin where feasible","Disable or constrain active content execution","Use safe response headers","Prevent upload paths from sharing trusted authentication context"],"constraints":["Some applications need inline media rendering","Origin isolation architecture must preserve legitimate access requirements"],"preconditions":["Users can upload content","Uploaded content can be interpreted as active browser content"],"input":"Uploaded content and serving configuration","output":"Isolated untrusted content delivery","expected_behavior":"Uploaded active content cannot execute with the primary application's origin privileges.","failure_mode":"Malicious uploaded HTML or script executes in the trusted application origin.","root_cause":"Untrusted content shares the same origin and browser security context as trusted application code.","solution":"Use a separate origin, safe content-disposition policies, restrictive headers, and non-executable storage paths.","edge_cases":["SVG and HTML can contain active content","Content-type sniffing can alter browser interpretation","CDN or proxy configuration can accidentally collapse origin separation"],"security_implications":"Reduces stored XSS and origin-confusion attacks.","performance_implications":"Separate origins can affect caching, routing, and request architecture.","testing":"Upload controlled active content and verify it cannot execute with trusted application-origin privileges.","verification":"Claim: untrusted uploads are isolated from trusted origin execution. Evidence: browser-origin tests and response-header inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java"],"version_scope":"Browser and web-server behavior is implementation-specific.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B427","D40-B429"]} {"record_id":"D40-B429","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"File and Content Security","subtopic":"Document Processing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsafe document parser processing untrusted files","question":"How should applications remediate security risks from parsing attacker-controlled documents?","answer":"Use maintained parsers, isolate parsing workloads where feasible, disable unnecessary active features, enforce resource limits, and treat parser output as untrusted data.","objective":"Limit exploitation and resource exhaustion through complex document formats.","requirements":["Keep parsers maintained","Sandbox high-risk parsing where practical","Disable unnecessary active features","Apply resource limits"],"constraints":["Document formats may legitimately require complex features","Sandbox strength depends on runtime isolation"],"preconditions":["Application parses attacker-controlled documents","Parser handles complex or active content"],"input":"Untrusted document and parser configuration","output":"Safely bounded parsed representation","expected_behavior":"Malicious documents cannot gain broader application privileges or exhaust unbounded resources through parsing.","failure_mode":"Parser vulnerability or malformed document causes code execution or denial of service.","root_cause":"Untrusted parsing occurs with excessive privileges and unrestricted resources.","solution":"Patch parsers, isolate processing, restrict features, and enforce resource budgets.","edge_cases":["Nested documents can trigger recursive processing","Macros or embedded objects may execute active behavior","Parser output can contain attacker-controlled URLs or markup"],"security_implications":"Reduces parser exploitation and document-based denial of service.","performance_implications":"Sandboxing and resource accounting add processing overhead.","testing":"Process malformed and security-test documents in an isolated environment and verify resource and privilege boundaries.","verification":"Claim: document parsing cannot escape its intended privilege and resource boundary. Evidence: parser security tests and sandbox inspection.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Parser-library and operating-system specific.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","CWE-400","CWE-1395"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B426","D40-B428"]} {"record_id":"D40-B430","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"File and Content Security","subtopic":"File Permission Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Uploaded files inheriting overly broad filesystem permissions","question":"How should upload storage remediate files that inherit permissions broader than required?","answer":"Apply explicit ownership and restrictive permissions to uploaded files and directories, separate application upload identities from unrelated processes, and verify permissions after upload.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized local processes or users from reading or modifying uploaded content.","requirements":["Define required file access","Set explicit ownership and permissions","Use separate storage paths or identities","Verify permissions after creation"],"constraints":["Filesystem permission models vary","Shared service accounts can undermine separation"],"preconditions":["Application stores uploaded files locally or on permission-controlled storage","Default permissions are broader than required"],"input":"Uploaded file and storage configuration","output":"Least-privilege file access","expected_behavior":"Only intended application components can access uploaded content.","failure_mode":"Another local process reads or modifies sensitive uploads.","root_cause":"Storage relies on permissive default umasks, ACLs, or shared identities.","solution":"Use dedicated storage identities, restrictive permissions, controlled directories, and explicit permission verification.","edge_cases":["Temporary files can have different defaults","Container volume permissions may differ from host permissions","ACL inheritance can override expected modes"],"security_implications":"Reduces local data exposure and tampering.","performance_implications":"Permission checks are filesystem-managed and normally low cost.","testing":"Inspect file ownership and permissions after uploads and attempt unauthorized access using controlled identities.","verification":"Claim: uploaded files are accessible only to intended principals. Evidence: permission inspection and negative filesystem-access tests.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Operating-system and storage-platform specific.","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","NIST least-privilege principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B121","D40-B428"]} {"record_id":"D40-B431","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Network Security","subtopic":"DNS Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Application trusting mutable DNS resolution for security identity","question":"How should applications remediate security decisions based solely on a DNS name that can resolve to changing addresses?","answer":"Separate name resolution from authorization identity, validate the destination against the intended trust boundary, and avoid assuming that a DNS name alone proves ownership or authorization.","objective":"Prevent DNS changes or rebinding from bypassing network trust decisions.","requirements":["Define trusted destination identity","Resolve and validate destinations carefully","Recheck security-sensitive connections when appropriate","Avoid hostname-only authorization assumptions"],"constraints":["Cloud and service-discovery environments intentionally use dynamic addresses","DNS pinning can create availability and rotation problems"],"preconditions":["Application makes security decisions based on resolved hostnames","DNS records can change or are externally influenced"],"input":"Requested hostname, resolved address, and trust policy","output":"Validated network destination","expected_behavior":"A DNS resolution change cannot silently move a security-sensitive connection outside the authorized trust boundary.","failure_mode":"Attacker-controlled DNS resolution redirects traffic to an unauthorized destination.","root_cause":"Authorization is tied only to a mutable DNS name or stale resolution result.","solution":"Use authenticated service identity, explicit destination policies, safe resolution checks, and revalidation appropriate to the threat model.","edge_cases":["DNS rebinding requires validation at connection time","IPv4 and IPv6 resolution may produce different addresses","Private and public DNS views can differ"],"security_implications":"Reduces DNS-rebinding and destination-confusion attacks.","performance_implications":"Additional DNS or identity validation can increase connection setup latency.","testing":"Use controlled DNS changes and verify that security-sensitive requests are rejected when the resolved destination violates policy.","verification":"Claim: DNS manipulation cannot move a protected request outside its authorized trust boundary. Evidence: controlled resolution-change tests and destination logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"DNS resolver and network-stack specific.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B431","D40-B432"]} {"record_id":"D40-B432","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Network Security","subtopic":"Outbound Proxy Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unrestricted outbound proxy use","question":"How should an enterprise application remediate unrestricted access to an outbound proxy?","answer":"Require authenticated proxy access where appropriate, restrict permitted destinations and methods, prevent proxy use from bypassing application egress policy, and audit proxy requests.","objective":"Prevent compromised workloads from using a proxy as an unrestricted exfiltration channel.","requirements":["Authenticate proxy users or workloads","Restrict destinations and methods","Align proxy policy with egress controls","Monitor abnormal usage"],"constraints":["Proxy requirements differ for user and service traffic","TLS interception can introduce separate certificate and trust risks"],"preconditions":["Applications can reach an outbound proxy","Proxy can reach destinations broader than application requirements"],"input":"Proxy request and workload identity","output":"Authorized outbound request","expected_behavior":"Only approved workloads can use the proxy for permitted destinations and protocols.","failure_mode":"Compromised application uses proxy to bypass network restrictions.","root_cause":"Proxy is treated as a generic connectivity mechanism without security policy.","solution":"Apply identity-aware proxy authorization, destination restrictions, method limits, and monitoring.","edge_cases":["CONNECT tunneling can bypass application-layer assumptions","DNS resolution may occur at the proxy rather than client","Different applications may require different destination policies"],"security_implications":"Reduces proxy-based exfiltration and network-policy bypass.","performance_implications":"Proxy authentication and inspection can add latency and throughput overhead.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized destinations and protocols through the proxy using controlled workloads.","verification":"Claim: proxy access cannot bypass workload egress policy. Evidence: negative proxy tests and policy logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Proxy implementation and network architecture specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B404","D40-B431"]} {"record_id":"D40-B433","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Network Security","subtopic":"Service Discovery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unauthenticated service-discovery registration","question":"How should service discovery remediate unauthorized service registration?","answer":"Authenticate service-registration clients, authorize which identities may register specific service names or namespaces, validate registration metadata, and expire stale registrations.","objective":"Prevent attackers from impersonating trusted services through malicious service-discovery entries.","requirements":["Authenticate registrants","Authorize service ownership","Validate registration metadata","Use leases or expiration where supported"],"constraints":["Dynamic environments require automated registration","Service discovery systems differ in identity and namespace controls"],"preconditions":["Services register dynamically","Consumers trust discovered endpoints"],"input":"Service-registration request and identity","output":"Authorized service-discovery record","expected_behavior":"Only authorized workloads can register or modify service identities within protected namespaces.","failure_mode":"Attacker registers a malicious endpoint under a trusted service identity.","root_cause":"Service discovery trusts registration without authenticated ownership controls.","solution":"Use authenticated registration, namespace-scoped authorization, metadata validation, and controlled expiration.","edge_cases":["Stale registrations can create availability issues","Service names can collide across environments","Health checks do not establish service identity by themselves"],"security_implications":"Reduces service impersonation and traffic redirection.","performance_implications":"Registration authentication and policy checks add small control-plane overhead.","testing":"Attempt registration using unauthorized identities and verify rejection; test stale registration expiration.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized identities cannot impersonate protected service names. Evidence: registration tests and discovery-store inspection.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Service-discovery implementation specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","Kubernetes service-discovery documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B434"]} {"record_id":"D40-B434","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Network Security","subtopic":"Internal TLS","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Internal services trusting any valid certificate","question":"How should internal TLS clients remediate trust of certificates from unintended authorities?","answer":"Configure a controlled trust store and validate certificate chain, identity, validity period, and intended peer according to the service trust model rather than accepting any certificate trusted by a broad system store.","objective":"Prevent internal service impersonation through overly broad certificate trust.","requirements":["Define trusted certificate authorities","Validate peer identity","Use appropriate trust stores","Rotate and audit trust anchors"],"constraints":["Shared infrastructure may use multiple legitimate authorities","Trust-store updates must preserve availability"],"preconditions":["Internal service communication uses TLS","Client trusts certificates from a broad or inappropriate authority set"],"input":"Peer certificate and client trust configuration","output":"Validated peer connection","expected_behavior":"Only certificates satisfying the configured trust and identity policy are accepted.","failure_mode":"Attacker obtains a certificate from another trusted authority and impersonates an internal service.","root_cause":"Certificate validity is treated as equivalent to authorization for the specific service.","solution":"Use narrowly scoped trust stores or service identity mechanisms and validate hostname or identity according to the protocol.","edge_cases":["Private PKI rotation requires overlapping trust anchors","Certificate authority compromise can affect multiple services","mTLS adds client identity but still requires correct trust policy"],"security_implications":"Reduces internal service impersonation and trust-boundary bypass.","performance_implications":"Certificate validation is normal TLS overhead; custom trust-store handling adds configuration complexity.","testing":"Present valid certificates from unauthorized authorities or identities and verify connection rejection.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized certificate identities cannot authenticate as the protected service. Evidence: negative TLS tests and trust-store inspection.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"TLS library and PKI implementation specific.","provenance":["RFC 5280","RFC 6125","OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B431","D40-B435"]} {"record_id":"D40-B435","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Network Security","subtopic":"Network Management","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Exposed internal management interface","question":"How should applications remediate management interfaces exposed on production network paths?","answer":"Separate management interfaces from ordinary application traffic, require strong authentication and authorization, restrict network reachability, and avoid exposing administrative endpoints to untrusted clients.","objective":"Reduce unauthorized administrative access to production services.","requirements":["Separate management and application paths","Restrict network exposure","Require strong identity controls","Audit management actions"],"constraints":["Operational teams may need remote management","Health endpoints can unintentionally expose administrative information"],"preconditions":["Service exposes management or administrative endpoints","Current network path permits broader access than required"],"input":"Management endpoint and network-access policy","output":"Restricted management interface","expected_behavior":"Only authorized management identities from permitted network contexts can reach administrative operations.","failure_mode":"Attacker discovers and invokes an exposed administrative endpoint.","root_cause":"Management functionality shares an unprotected application exposure boundary.","solution":"Use dedicated management interfaces or protected routes, strong authentication, authorization, network controls, and safe endpoint design.","edge_cases":["Debug endpoints can become management surfaces","Reverse proxies may unintentionally expose internal paths","Management access should remain available for incident recovery through controlled channels"],"security_implications":"Reduces administrative attack surface.","performance_implications":"Network segmentation and authentication add modest connection overhead.","testing":"Scan and probe production-facing interfaces from untrusted network contexts and verify administrative operations are unreachable.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized network contexts cannot reach protected management operations. Evidence: network reachability tests and endpoint authorization tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure and application architecture specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207","OWASP API Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B364","D40-B412"]} {"record_id":"D40-B436","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Feature Flags","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security-sensitive feature flags writable by ordinary users","question":"How should security-sensitive feature flags be remediated when ordinary application users can modify them?","answer":"Separate security-sensitive configuration from ordinary user-controlled settings, require privileged authorization for changes, validate values, and audit all changes.","objective":"Prevent configuration changes from becoming privilege-escalation or security-control bypasses.","requirements":["Classify security-sensitive flags","Restrict write permissions","Validate configuration values","Audit changes"],"constraints":["Feature-flag systems may serve both product and security functions","Emergency changes require controlled privileged access"],"preconditions":["Feature flags influence authentication, authorization, security controls, or data access","Current write permissions are broad"],"input":"Feature-flag update request and caller identity","output":"Authorized configuration change or rejection","expected_behavior":"Only explicitly authorized identities can modify security-sensitive flags.","failure_mode":"Attacker changes a flag to disable authentication or authorization controls.","root_cause":"Configuration management does not distinguish security-sensitive state from ordinary product settings.","solution":"Use separate roles and environments, protected flag namespaces, change approvals where appropriate, and immutable audit history.","edge_cases":["Cached flags can delay security changes","Rollback mechanisms can restore an insecure value","Flag targeting rules can themselves become authorization logic"],"security_implications":"Prevents configuration-driven security bypass.","performance_implications":"Central flag authorization and retrieval may add small latency or dependency overhead.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized changes to protected flags and verify rejection and audit behavior.","verification":"Claim: ordinary users cannot modify security-sensitive configuration. Evidence: negative update tests and audit records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","Go"],"version_scope":"Feature-flag platform specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53 configuration-management principles","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B365","D40-B437"]} {"record_id":"D40-B437","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Configuration Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Unsigned security configuration accepted by production services","question":"How should applications remediate unauthorized modification of security-critical configuration files?","answer":"Protect configuration sources and deployment paths, verify integrity or provenance where supported, restrict write access, and fail safely when required configuration cannot be trusted.","objective":"Prevent attackers from altering security controls through configuration tampering.","requirements":["Protect configuration storage","Restrict writers","Verify integrity or provenance","Define safe failure behavior"],"constraints":["Not all configuration formats have native signing","Runtime-generated configuration may require a different trust mechanism"],"preconditions":["Security behavior depends on external configuration","Configuration can be modified independently of application code"],"input":"Configuration artifact and trust metadata","output":"Trusted configuration or startup rejection","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized configuration modifications are detected or blocked before security-sensitive behavior relies on them.","failure_mode":"Attacker changes configuration to weaken security controls.","root_cause":"Configuration is treated as trusted despite having a separate modification path.","solution":"Use controlled deployment, integrity verification, least-privilege storage access, and explicit validation before use.","edge_cases":["Secrets should not be embedded merely to simplify integrity checks","Configuration generated from multiple sources needs end-to-end trust analysis","Rollback artifacts can also be malicious if not protected"],"security_implications":"Protects security-control integrity.","performance_implications":"Integrity verification adds startup or configuration-refresh overhead.","testing":"Modify controlled configuration artifacts and verify detection or rejection before activation.","verification":"Claim: unauthorized configuration changes cannot silently become active. Evidence: tamper tests and configuration integrity records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Deployment and configuration-management specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B353","D40-B436"]} {"record_id":"D40-B438","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Environment Isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Production credentials available in development or test environments","question":"How should organizations remediate production credentials reused in lower-trust environments?","answer":"Separate credentials, identities, secrets, and data by environment, prohibit production credential reuse in development and testing, and continuously scan configurations for cross-environment secret references.","objective":"Prevent lower-trust environment compromise from reaching production resources.","requirements":["Use environment-specific identities","Separate secret stores","Separate data or sanitize production-derived data","Prevent cross-environment credential use"],"constraints":["Testing may require realistic integrations","Environment separation must include CI/CD and shared services"],"preconditions":["Development or test environments can access production credentials or services"],"input":"Environment configuration and identity inventory","output":"Environment-isolated credential model","expected_behavior":"Compromise of a lower-trust environment does not automatically provide production credentials.","failure_mode":"Attacker pivots from development infrastructure into production using reused credentials.","root_cause":"Operational convenience causes trust boundaries between environments to collapse.","solution":"Create separate identities and secrets, restrict network paths, sanitize test data, and enforce environment-specific policies.","edge_cases":["Staging may intentionally integrate with production-like services","CI runners can bridge multiple environments","Shared monitoring systems can become cross-environment trust paths"],"security_implications":"Reduces lateral movement from lower-trust environments to production.","performance_implications":"Separate environments can increase infrastructure and