ivileged test environment exposes production data.","root_cause":"Production data is copied for convenience without evaluating security necessity.","solution":"Use synthetic data or controlled de-identification, minimize fields and records, and restrict access to unavoidable sensitive data.","edge_cases":["Rare combinations of attributes can remain identifying","Logs and test artifacts can retain source data","Data masking can fail if referential relationships are not preserved safely"],"security_implications":"Reduces exposure of production-sensitive data in lower-trust environments.","performance_implications":"Synthetic-data generation or transformation adds test-data preparation cost.","testing":"Inspect generated test datasets and attempt re-identification or unauthorized access within the test environment.","verification":"Claim: test data does not unnecessarily expose production-sensitive information. Evidence: dataset inspection and access-control testing.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Data-governance and application specific.","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","OWASP Privacy Principles"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B406","D40-B438"]} {"record_id":"D40-B440","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Security Defaults","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Security controls disabled by default in production configuration","question":"How should software remediate insecure production defaults for authentication, encryption, or authorization controls?","answer":"Make security-preserving behavior the default, require explicit privileged configuration to weaken it, validate production configuration at startup or deployment, and prevent accidental debug or insecure modes.","objective":"Prevent configuration mistakes from silently disabling critical security controls.","requirements":["Define secure defaults","Validate production configuration","Block unsafe combinations","Separate development overrides from production settings"],"constraints":["Development workflows may legitimately require relaxed controls","Some compatibility modes cannot be eliminated immediately"],"preconditions":["Application has optional security controls","Production configuration can omit or disable them"],"input":"Production configuration and security policy","output":"Validated secure runtime configuration","expected_behavior":"Application refuses or warns according to policy when critical security controls are disabled in production.","failure_mode":"Deployment succeeds with authentication, TLS, authorization, or security validation unintentionally disabled.","root_cause":"Security controls are opt-in or configuration validation is absent.","solution":"Use secure-by-default settings, explicit production validation, and policy checks in deployment pipelines.","edge_cases":["Environment variables can override secure configuration","Feature flags can bypass startup validation","Backward compatibility can require transitional settings"],"security_implications":"Reduces configuration-driven security failures.","performance_implications":"Startup validation adds negligible normal overhead but can prevent expensive insecure deployments.","testing":"Deploy controlled insecure configurations and verify policy blocks or clearly rejects them according to the security requirement.","verification":"Claim: production deployments cannot silently activate prohibited insecure modes. Evidence: configuration-negative tests and deployment validation logs.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Application and deployment framework specific.","provenance":["OWASP Secure Configuration Guide","NIST SP 800-53","CIS Benchmarks"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B437","D40-B438"]} {"record_id":"D40-B441","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Verification","subtopic":"Policy Testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security policy changes without negative authorization tests","question":"How should a system remediate security regressions caused by policy changes that are tested only for allowed behavior?","answer":"Add explicit negative authorization tests for sensitive resources and operations, including boundary identities and bypass paths, and require them to pass before policy changes are released.","objective":"Detect privilege-expansion regressions that positive tests cannot reveal.","requirements":["Define denied operations","Test multiple identities and scopes","Include alternate access paths","Gate releases on security regression results"],"constraints":["Negative test coverage must reflect actual threat boundaries","Policy changes can affect large identity graphs"],"preconditions":["Authorization policy is changed over time","Existing tests mainly verify successful operations"],"input":"Authorization policy change and security test suite","output":"Regression assessment","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized operations remain denied after policy changes.","failure_mode":"A policy update accidentally grants access to previously protected resources.","root_cause":"Tests verify functionality without explicitly asserting security boundaries.","solution":"Maintain durable negative authorization tests and execute them against every security-relevant policy change.","edge_cases":["New legitimate roles require updating expected-denial tests","Caching can make policy changes appear inconsistent","Emergency policy changes need equivalent post-change validation"],"security_implications":"Reduces authorization regression risk.","performance_implications":"Security regression suites increase CI duration according to coverage and environment complexity.","testing":"Attempt prohibited actions before and after the policy change and compare decisions.","verification":"Claim: protected denial invariants remain true after policy changes. Evidence: automated negative authorization suite.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Testing framework and authorization architecture specific.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B365","D40-B437"]} {"record_id":"D40-B442","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Verification","subtopic":"Threat-Model Regression","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security remediation without threat-model traceability","question":"How should teams remediate security changes that are implemented without linking them to the threat they address?","answer":"Map each security control to an identified threat, affected trust boundary, expected security property, test evidence, and residual limitation so that future changes can detect control erosion.","objective":"Maintain traceability between security risks, controls, and verification evidence.","requirements":["Identify threat and trust boundary","Define intended security property","Link control to tests","Record residual risk or limitations"],"constraints":["Threat models evolve with architecture","One control can mitigate multiple threats and one threat can require multiple controls"],"preconditions":["Security controls are maintained over multiple software versions","Threat-model information exists or can be established"],"input":"Threat model, security control, and verification evidence","output":"Traceable security-control record","expected_behavior":"A reviewer can determine why a control exists and what evidence demonstrates its effectiveness.","failure_mode":"A later refactor removes or weakens a security control without recognizing its purpose.","root_cause":"Security implementation is disconnected from threat-model intent.","solution":"Maintain machine-readable traceability from threat to control to test and verification evidence.","edge_cases":["Controls may provide defense in depth rather than complete mitigation","Threat assumptions can become invalid after architecture changes","A passing test does not prove complete threat mitigation"],"security_implications":"Improves long-term security-control preservation.","performance_implications":"Traceability metadata adds documentation and review overhead rather than runtime cost.","testing":"Modify a protected component and verify that linked security tests identify any control regression.","verification":"Claim: security controls remain traceable to their intended threats and evidence. Evidence: threat-control-test mapping review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Architecture and development-process specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-154","NIST SP 800-218"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B441","D40-B443"]} {"record_id":"D40-B443","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Verification","subtopic":"Invariant Checking","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security invariant not checked after state transitions","question":"How should security-critical systems remediate state transitions that can violate authorization invariants?","answer":"Define explicit security invariants and evaluate them at relevant state-transition boundaries, rejecting transitions that would create unauthorized ownership, privilege, or resource state.","objective":"Prevent state-machine bugs from bypassing security policy.","requirements":["Define security invariants","Identify security-sensitive transitions","Check invariants before commit","Test invalid transitions"],"constraints":["Invariant evaluation must be consistent with transaction or concurrency semantics","Some invariants require database-level enforcement"],"preconditions":["System has security-sensitive state transitions","Authorization depends on current state"],"input":"Current state, requested transition, and security invariants","output":"Allowed or rejected state transition","expected_behavior":"State cannot transition into a configuration that violates defined security invariants.","failure_mode":"A race or state-transition bug creates unauthorized ownership or privilege.","root_cause":"Authorization is checked only on individual requests rather than on resulting state.","solution":"Encode critical invariants at transactional or authoritative state boundaries and verify them after relevant transitions.","edge_cases":["Concurrent transactions require isolation-aware invariant enforcement","Distributed systems may need coordination","Recovery paths can bypass ordinary transition logic"],"security_implications":"Reduces state-based authorization bypasses.","performance_implications":"Invariant checks add transaction or state-management overhead.","testing":"Attempt valid and invalid transitions, including concurrent conflicting transitions, and verify invariant preservation.","verification":"Claim: protected state invariants hold across authorized transitions. Evidence: transition tests, concurrency tests, and invariant checks.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Application state-management architecture specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B362","D40-B444"]} {"record_id":"D40-B444","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Verification","subtopic":"Concurrency Security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authorization check separated from protected state mutation","question":"How should a concurrent system remediate a time-of-check-to-time-of-use authorization race?","answer":"Make authorization validation and security-sensitive state mutation atomic with respect to the relevant state, or revalidate under the same transaction or lock before committing the operation.","objective":"Prevent authorization from becoming stale before the protected action commits.","requirements":["Identify mutable authorization state","Bind check and use to one atomic operation where possible","Use appropriate transaction or locking semantics","Test concurrent state changes"],"constraints":["Distributed transactions may require alternative coordination","Broad locks can reduce concurrency"],"preconditions":["Authorization depends on mutable state","Another actor can change that state between check and use"],"input":"Authorization state and concurrent mutation request","output":"Atomic authorization-and-state decision","expected_behavior":"An authorization decision cannot become invalid before the protected mutation commits without being detected.","failure_mode":"User loses permission after check but before protected operation executes, or attacker gains access during the race window.","root_cause":"Security check and state mutation occur in separate synchronization domains.","solution":"Use atomic transactions, conditional updates, version checks, or appropriate locking to bind authorization to the committed state.","edge_cases":["Optimistic concurrency requires handling version conflicts","Distributed caches can hold stale authorization state","Retries must not duplicate privileged effects"],"security_implications":"Reduces TOCTOU authorization bypasses.","performance_implications":"Stronger synchronization can reduce concurrency or increase contention.","testing":"Run concurrent permission-change and protected-operation scenarios and verify no unauthorized commit occurs.","verification":"Claim: authorization cannot become stale within the protected commit boundary. Evidence: concurrency stress tests and transaction traces.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Database, transaction, and concurrency implementation specific.","provenance":["CWE-367","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B443","D40-B445"]} {"record_id":"D40-B445","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Verification","subtopic":"Fuzzing Security Boundaries","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","concept":"Security parser tested only with valid inputs","question":"How should security-sensitive parsers remediate insufficient malformed-input testing?","answer":"Add structured negative and fuzz testing covering malformed encodings, boundary lengths, recursive structures, unexpected types, truncation, and resource-exhaustion inputs, then verify safe rejection and bounded resource use.","objective":"Discover parser failures that can become security vulnerabilities.","requirements":["Define malformed-input classes","Test boundary and recursive structures","Monitor resource consumption","Verify deterministic safe failure"],"constraints":["Fuzzing does not prove absence of vulnerabilities","Input grammar and parser complexity determine useful fuzz strategies"],"preconditions":["Application parses untrusted structured input","Parser behavior affects security or resource use"],"input":"Malformed and adversarial parser inputs","output":"Safe rejection or valid bounded parse","expected_behavior":"Invalid input is rejected without memory corruption, excessive resource consumption, or unintended state changes.","failure_mode":"Malformed input triggers crash, excessive allocation, parser confusion, or security bypass.","root_cause":"Testing covers normal syntax but not adversarial parser behavior.","solution":"Combine grammar-aware fuzzing, boundary testing, resource limits, and regression corpus management.","edge_cases":["Nested structures can cause stack exhaustion","Unicode and encoding ambiguities can create parser differentials","Multiple parsers interpreting the same data can disagree"],"security_implications":"Improves resilience against parser exploitation and denial of service.","performance_implications":"Fuzzing can consume substantial test infrastructure resources but does not affect production runtime.","testing":"Run malformed, boundary, and fuzz-generated inputs under resource monitoring and collect reproducible failures.","verification":"Claim: tested malformed input classes fail safely within the declared resource budget. Evidence: fuzz corpus, crash-free runs, resource observations, and regression tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Parser-library and runtime specific.","provenance":["OWASP Fuzzing guidance","CWE-400","CWE-20"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B426","D40-B429"]} {"record_id":"D40-B446","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Incident Response","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Incident response credentials shared across operators","question":"How should incident-response systems remediate shared emergency credentials?","answer":"Use individually attributable privileged identities, just-in-time or time-bounded access where supported, strong authentication, and detailed audit trails instead of shared emergency accounts.","objective":"Preserve accountability while limiting privileged emergency access.","requirements":["Use individual identities","Apply strong authentication","Limit emergency privilege duration","Audit privileged actions"],"constraints":["Break-glass procedures may be required during identity-service outages","Emergency access must remain operational under failure conditions"],"preconditions":["Operators currently share emergency credentials","Privileged incident-response actions require elevated access"],"input":"Incident operator identity and emergency authorization request","output":"Attributable time-bounded privileged session","expected_behavior":"Emergency access is granted to an identifiable operator under explicit policy and expires or is revoked after use.","failure_mode":"Shared credentials prevent attribution or remain active after an incident.","root_cause":"Operational convenience overrides identity accountability.","solution":"Implement individual break-glass identities, strong authentication, limited duration, approval or governance appropriate to risk, and immutable audit records.","edge_cases":["Identity provider outages require offline or alternate emergency procedures","Emergency credentials need periodic testing","Long incidents may require controlled extension of access"],"security_implications":"Improves accountability and reduces persistent privileged credential exposure.","performance_implications":"Additional authentication and authorization steps can slow emergency access slightly.","testing":"Execute a controlled break-glass procedure and verify attribution, expiration, and audit records.","verification":"Claim: emergency privileged actions are attributable and bounded. Evidence: controlled emergency-access test and audit review.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Identity and privileged-access-management specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-63B"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B397","D40-B447"]} {"record_id":"D40-B447","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Incident Response","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Incident evidence modified by responders","question":"How should incident-response workflows remediate the ability to alter original forensic evidence?","answer":"Preserve original evidence in access-controlled storage, record cryptographic hashes or equivalent integrity metadata where appropriate, separate collection from analysis copies, and audit evidence access and modification.","objective":"Maintain integrity and traceability of security evidence during investigation.","requirements":["Preserve originals","Restrict modification","Record integrity metadata","Separate analysis copies","Audit evidence access"],"constraints":["Some evidence sources change continuously","Hashing proves consistency with the hashed content but not that the original content was truthful"],"preconditions":["Incident response collects logs, files, memory, or other evidence","Evidence may be accessed by multiple investigators"],"input":"Collected evidence and chain-of-custody metadata","output":"Integrity-protected evidence set","expected_behavior":"Original evidence remains protected from unauthorized modification while investigators work from controlled copies.","failure_mode":"Evidence is altered or overwritten and its original state cannot be established reliably.","root_cause":"Collection and analysis operate on the same mutable evidence store.","solution":"Use controlled evidence acquisition, immutable or restricted originals, integrity metadata, and auditable access.","edge_cases":["Live systems may require volatile collection","Evidence copies need their own provenance","Cloud evidence can depend on provider retention and export semantics"],"security_implications":"Preserves investigative integrity and supports reliable incident analysis.","performance_implications":"Evidence hashing and duplication consume storage and processing resources.","testing":"Perform a controlled evidence-collection workflow and attempt unauthorized modification of the preserved original.","verification":"Claim: preserved evidence remains unchanged after acquisition. Evidence: integrity verification, access logs, and chain-of-custody records.","languages":["Python","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Forensic tooling and storage specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-86"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B413","D40-B446"]} {"record_id":"D40-B448","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Detection Integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Attackers disabling security controls through ordinary service privileges","question":"How should production systems remediate security-control disablement by application identities?","answer":"Separate security-control administration from application runtime identities, restrict control-management APIs, monitor configuration changes, and prevent ordinary workloads from disabling monitoring or protection mechanisms.","objective":"Prevent compromised applications from suppressing the controls that would detect or contain them.","requirements":["Separate security-control administration","Restrict control-management APIs","Monitor control changes","Test denial from ordinary workloads"],"constraints":["Some applications legitimately integrate with security controls","Emergency administration requires a separate privileged path"],"preconditions":["Application identities can modify monitoring, endpoint, or security configuration","Security controls are critical to detection or prevention"],"input":"Security-control API and caller identity","output":"Authorized control-management decision","expected_behavior":"Ordinary application identities cannot disable or weaken protected security controls.","failure_mode":"Compromised workload disables monitoring or protection before continuing an attack.","root_cause":"Runtime and security-administration privileges overlap.","solution":"Separate identities and permissions, restrict administrative endpoints, add change monitoring, and use protected control planes.","edge_cases":["Automated orchestration may require temporary elevated access","Security-control updates can cause transient state changes","Break-glass access must remain auditable"],"security_implications":"Improves resilience against defense-evasion after application compromise.","performance_implications":"Security-control monitoring adds small management overhead.","testing":"Attempt protected control changes using ordinary workload identities and verify denial and alert generation.","verification":"Claim: ordinary workloads cannot disable protected security controls. Evidence: negative API tests and security-control change alerts.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Security-platform and infrastructure specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B413","D40-B446"]} {"record_id":"D40-B449","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Dependency Response","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"Vulnerable dependency remediation without exposure assessment","question":"How should dependency vulnerability remediation be improved when teams update packages without assessing actual exposure?","answer":"Identify affected dependency versions, determine whether vulnerable functionality is reachable, assess exploitability and deployment exposure, apply a supported fix or mitigation, and verify the resulting dependency graph and runtime behavior.","objective":"Make dependency remediation evidence-driven rather than based solely on package-version changes.","requirements":["Identify vulnerable dependency and version range","Map dependency reachability","Assess runtime exposure","Apply supported remediation","Verify final dependency graph"],"constraints":["Reachability analysis can be incomplete","A dependency can be exploitable through transitive functionality even when not directly imported"],"preconditions":["Security advisory affects a dependency","Application uses a version within the affected range"],"input":"Dependency graph, advisory, application usage, and deployment context","output":"Exposure assessment and verified remediation state","expected_behavior":"Remediation addresses the actual vulnerable dependency path and confirms that the deployed artifact no longer contains the affected component or has an accepted mitigation.","failure_mode":"Team updates an unrelated dependency or assumes non-exploitation without evidence.","root_cause":"Vulnerability response is based only on package names rather than dependency graph and runtime exposure.","solution":"Combine advisory analysis, dependency graph inspection, reachability analysis where feasible, version remediation, and deployed-artifact verification.","edge_cases":["Vendor backports can use versions that differ from upstream version numbering","Shaded or bundled dependencies can hide vulnerable components","Container images may retain old packages after application dependency updates"],"security_implications":"Improves accuracy of vulnerability remediation and reduces residual exposure.","performance_implications":"Dependency analysis and artifact scanning add CI processing time.","testing":"Build the remediated artifact and verify affected dependency versions are absent or appropriately mitigated.","verification":"Claim: deployed artifact no longer exposes the affected dependency path. Evidence: dependency graph, artifact scan, and relevant regression tests.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Dependency ecosystem and advisory specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218","OWASP Dependency-Check guidance","CVE Program"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B353"]} {"record_id":"D40-B450","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Security Change Verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Security remediation marked complete before deployed-state verification","question":"How should teams remediate security changes that are considered complete after source-code modification but before deployment verification?","answer":"Verify the deployed artifact and effective runtime configuration, execute relevant security regression tests against the deployed environment, and distinguish source-level remediation from verified operational remediation.","objective":"Prevent false closure of security vulnerabilities that remain present in production artifacts or configuration.","requirements":["Identify deployed artifact","Verify runtime configuration","Execute relevant security tests","Record observed evidence and limitations"],"constraints":["Production testing may require controlled non-destructive checks","Source and deployment environments can differ materially"],"preconditions":["Security remediation has changed source or configuration","Deployment pipeline produces a separately identifiable artifact"],"input":"Remediated source, build artifact, deployment configuration, and security test plan","output":"Evidence-backed deployed remediation status","expected_behavior":"A vulnerability is marked remediated only after the deployed state satisfies the defined security property.","failure_mode":"Fixed source code exists while production still runs an old artifact or insecure configuration.","root_cause":"Verification stops at code review or successful build.","solution":"Trace source to artifact to deployment, verify runtime state, execute security regression tests, and record residual uncertainty.","edge_cases":["Canary deployments can create mixed-version populations","Configuration management may override source defaults","Caches or stale instances can preserve vulnerable behavior"],"security_implications":"Reduces false-positive remediation closure and residual production exposure.","performance_implications":"Deployment verification adds testing and operational overhead but avoids repeated incident cost.","testing":"Deploy a controlled security fix and verify artifact identity, runtime configuration, and exploit rejection in the target environment.","verification":"Claim: deployed environment satisfies the remediation requirement. Evidence: artifact digest, runtime configuration, security test results, and deployment records.","languages":["Python","JavaScript","TypeScript","Java","C","C++","Rust","Go"],"version_scope":"Deployment architecture and CI/CD platform specific.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218","NIST SP 800-53","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B349","D40-B353","D40-B449"]}

{"record_id":"D40-B451","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Insecure local storage","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Remediate sensitive data stored in plaintext on mobile devices","question":"How should an application remediate plaintext storage of credentials, tokens, or sensitive business data on a mobile device?","answer":"Remove plaintext persistence of sensitive values; use platform-protected secure storage for secrets and encrypt sensitive application data when local persistence is required. Minimize stored data and invalidate stored authentication material when appropriate.","objective":"Prevent local compromise from exposing reusable credentials or sensitive information.","requirements":["Classify data before persistence","Use platform secure storage for secrets","Encrypt sensitive persisted application data","Minimize local retention"],"constraints":["Platform security APIs differ","Device compromise can weaken local protections","Encryption does not replace access control"],"preconditions":["The application identifies data requiring local protection","Platform secure-storage capabilities are available"],"input":"Mobile application data persistence requirements","output":"Protected, minimized local persistence design","expected_behavior":"Sensitive values are not recoverable from ordinary application storage in plaintext.","failure_mode":"Credential or sensitive-data disclosure after device or application-storage compromise.","root_cause":"Secrets or sensitive records are written directly to ordinary files, preferences, databases, caches, or logs.","solution":"Use OS-backed key/secret storage, encrypt required local records with protected keys, avoid unnecessary persistence, and securely invalidate or remove data when its retention period ends.","edge_cases":["Offline mode requires temporary persistence","Backups may capture application data","Crash reports may expose supposedly protected values"],"security_implications":"Reduces credential theft and local data disclosure risk.","performance_implications":"Secure storage and encryption add modest I/O and CPU overhead.","testing":{"method":"Inspect storage locations and backup artifacts with a test device and verify sensitive values are not plaintext.","expected_result":"No sensitive secret is present in ordinary local storage or diagnostic output.","pass_criteria":"Sensitive data follows the documented storage-control policy."},"verification":"Verify storage implementation, backup behavior, deletion behavior, and platform security controls against requirements.","languages":["Swift","Kotlin","Java","Objective-C"],"version_scope":"Platform-dependent; validate against supported mobile OS security APIs.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard","OWASP Mobile Top 10"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B401","D40-B446"]} {"record_id":"D40-B452","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Certificate validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent disabled or weakened TLS certificate validation in mobile applications","question":"How should a mobile application remediate insecure TLS certificate validation?","answer":"Use the platform TLS validation mechanisms and reject invalid, expired, mismatched, or untrusted certificates. Do not ship permissive trust managers, hostname-verification bypasses, or debug-only validation overrides in production.","objective":"Prevent man-in-the-middle attacks against mobile network communication.","requirements":["Validate certificate chains","Validate hostnames","Reject invalid certificates","Separate development debugging from production trust behavior"],"constraints":["Enterprise interception may require controlled trust configuration","Certificate pinning introduces operational rotation considerations"],"preconditions":["The application communicates over TLS","A defined trust policy exists"],"input":"TLS client configuration","output":"Strict production certificate validation","expected_behavior":"Connections fail closed when server identity cannot be authenticated.","failure_mode":"An attacker can intercept or modify application traffic using an untrusted certificate.","root_cause":"Certificate or hostname validation is disabled, replaced with permissive validation, or incorrectly scoped.","solution":"Restore platform-standard certificate and hostname validation; isolate test certificates and debugging overrides from production builds.","edge_cases":["Private enterprise CAs","Certificate rotation","Legacy operating-system trust stores"],"security_implications":"Protects confidentiality and integrity of application traffic.","performance_implications":"Normal certificate validation adds limited connection-establishment overhead.","testing":{"method":"Present expired, mismatched, self-signed, and untrusted certificates in controlled tests.","expected_result":"The application rejects each invalid server identity.","pass_criteria":"No invalid certificate configuration permits a production connection."},"verification":"Review TLS configuration and execute negative certificate-validation tests on every supported platform.","languages":["Swift","Kotlin","Java","Objective-C"],"version_scope":"Applies to supported mobile TLS stacks.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard","RFC 5280"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B401","D40-B402"]} {"record_id":"D40-B453","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Deep-link authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Validate authorization when handling deep links and universal links","question":"How should applications remediate insecure deep-link handling?","answer":"Treat every deep-link parameter and destination as untrusted input and perform normal authentication and authorization checks before accessing protected resources or executing sensitive actions.","objective":"Prevent crafted links from bypassing authorization or triggering unintended application behavior.","requirements":["Validate link scheme and host","Parse and canonicalize parameters","Enforce authorization after routing","Restrict sensitive actions"],"constraints":["Some deep links intentionally support unauthenticated navigation","Routing frameworks may normalize URLs differently"],"preconditions":["The application accepts externally supplied links","Some routes access protected resources"],"input":"Externally supplied deep-link URI","output":"Validated route with authorization enforced","expected_behavior":"A link cannot grant privileges merely by selecting a protected route or manipulating parameters.","failure_mode":"Unauthorized resource access or sensitive action through a crafted link.","root_cause":"The router treats link selection as trusted authorization or fails to validate route parameters.","solution":"Apply allowlisted routing, canonicalize inputs, authenticate where required, and authorize the requested resource/action independently of link origin.","edge_cases":["Links received before login","App-to-app invocation","Nested or encoded URLs","Parameter duplication"],"security_implications":"Prevents authorization bypass through externally controlled navigation.","performance_implications":"Minimal routing-validation overhead.","testing":{"method":"Exercise protected routes with crafted links while authenticated as users with different privileges.","expected_result":"Access is determined by server-side or authoritative authorization, not by link construction.","pass_criteria":"No unauthorized route or action succeeds."},"verification":"Verify route authorization independently from URL parsing and test alternate encodings and duplicate parameters.","languages":["Swift","Kotlin","Java","Objective-C"],"version_scope":"Applies to mobile applications supporting external navigation.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B120"]} {"record_id":"D40-B454","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Clipboard leakage","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent sensitive values from being unnecessarily exposed through mobile clipboard mechanisms","question":"How should applications remediate sensitive-data exposure through clipboard operations?","answer":"Avoid copying secrets, authentication tokens, payment credentials, or other sensitive values to the clipboard unless the workflow explicitly requires it. Where supported, mark sensitive clipboard content appropriately and minimize its lifetime.","objective":"Reduce accidental disclosure through shared clipboard access or synchronization.","requirements":["Classify clipboard data","Avoid copying secrets by default","Limit sensitive clipboard lifetime where supported","Provide explicit user intent for sensitive copying"],"constraints":["Clipboard behavior varies by operating system","Some user workflows require copying sensitive values"],"preconditions":["The application exposes copy or paste functionality"],"input":"Application data copied to clipboard","output":"Minimized clipboard exposure","expected_behavior":"Sensitive values are not silently copied or retained longer than necessary.","failure_mode":"Other applications, clipboard synchronization, or diagnostic tooling expose sensitive information.","root_cause":"Sensitive data is copied without classification or lifetime controls.","solution":"Restrict clipboard use for sensitive fields and apply platform-supported sensitive-content protections.","edge_cases":["Password managers","One-time codes","Cross-device clipboard synchronization"],"security_implications":"Reduces unintended secret disclosure.","performance_implications":"Negligible.","testing":{"method":"Exercise copy workflows and inspect clipboard behavior on supported platforms.","expected_result":"Sensitive values are blocked or explicitly controlled according to policy.","pass_criteria":"No unintended sensitive clipboard persistence occurs."},"verification":"Review UI and clipboard APIs and test sensitive-field workflows.","languages":["Swift","Kotlin","Java","Objective-C"],"version_scope":"Platform-dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B451","D40-B446"]} {"record_id":"D40-B455","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Mobile Application Security","subtopic":"Backup exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent sensitive application data from entering insecure device backups","question":"How should sensitive mobile application data be protected from backup extraction?","answer":"Classify data that should not be backed up, exclude unnecessary sensitive artifacts from backup where supported, and ensure backed-up data is protected according to the platform and organizational security requirements.","objective":"Prevent backup archives from becoming an alternate path to sensitive-data disclosure.","requirements":["Define backup policy","Exclude unnecessary secrets","Protect required backup data","Test restoration behavior"],"constraints":["Backup capabilities differ by platform","Disabling all backup may harm legitimate recovery requirements"],"preconditions":["Application data participates in device or cloud backup mechanisms"],"input":"Application persistence and backup configuration","output":"Controlled backup exposure","expected_behavior":"Only approved data is recoverable through backup channels.","failure_mode":"Secrets or sensitive records are extracted from a backup despite being protected during normal application use.","root_cause":"Backup behavior was not included in the data-protection model.","solution":"Configure platform backup exclusion/protection controls where appropriate and minimize sensitive local persistence.","edge_cases":["Device migration","Cloud backup","Enterprise-managed devices","Application restore after reinstall"],"security_implications":"Reduces offline extraction and recovery-channel disclosure.","performance_implications":"Backup filtering may reduce backup size.","testing":{"method":"Generate representative application data, create a supported backup, inspect or restore it in a controlled environment.","expected_result":"Only policy-approved data is present and sensitive controls remain effective.","pass_criteria":"Backup contents match documented data-classification policy."},"verification":"Review backup configuration and perform controlled backup/restore security testing.","languages":["Swift","Kotlin","Java","Objective-C"],"version_scope":"Platform-dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Mobile Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B451","D40-B455"]} {"record_id":"D40-B456","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Cryptographic Remediation","subtopic":"Key lifecycle","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Replace long-lived unmanaged cryptographic keys with controlled key lifecycle management","question":"How should applications remediate cryptographic keys that are never rotated, expired, revoked, or inventoried?","answer":"Define key ownership, purpose, generation, activation, rotation, revocation, archival, destruction, and recovery requirements. Use managed key facilities when appropriate and prevent one key from serving unrelated security purposes.","objective":"Reduce compromise impact and uncontrolled cryptographic-key lifetime.","requirements":["Assign key purpose","Track key state","Define rotation and revocation","Protect key material","Separate cryptographic purposes"],"constraints":["Rotation must preserve availability","Some data requires historical decryption keys"],"preconditions":["Cryptographic keys protect application data or authentication material"],"input":"Existing key inventory and lifecycle requirements","output":"Documented and enforceable key lifecycle","expected_behavior":"Keys have controlled states and bounded operational use.","failure_mode":"A compromised or obsolete key remains usable indefinitely.","root_cause":"Key management was treated as a static configuration rather than a lifecycle.","solution":"Implement key inventory and lifecycle controls, rotate according to risk, revoke compromised keys, and preserve only necessary historical keys.","edge_cases":["Offline-encrypted data","Key rotation during active transactions","Disaster recovery"],"security_implications":"Limits compromise duration and blast radius.","performance_implications":"Rotation and re-encryption can create planned operational load.","testing":{"method":"Simulate key expiration, rotation, compromise, and revocation.","expected_result":"Old keys stop serving prohibited operations while required historical access remains controlled.","pass_criteria":"All lifecycle transitions enforce policy."},"verification":"Review key lifecycle records and test each state transition.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Cryptographic lifecycle principles are technology-independent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57 Part 1","NIST SP 800-57 Part 2"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B126","D40-B130"]} {"record_id":"D40-B457","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Cryptographic Remediation","subtopic":"Nonce reuse","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent nonce or IV reuse where cryptographic modes require uniqueness","question":"How should an application remediate nonce or IV reuse in authenticated encryption or other uniqueness-sensitive cryptographic schemes?","answer":"Use a cryptographic library that correctly generates or derives nonces according to the selected algorithm's requirements and ensure uniqueness constraints are enforced across the complete key lifetime.","objective":"Prevent confidentiality and integrity failures caused by nonce reuse.","requirements":["Identify nonce requirements for the algorithm","Guarantee required uniqueness","Keep nonce generation tied to the correct key scope","Fail safely when uniqueness cannot be guaranteed"],"constraints":["Some algorithms permit random nonces with strict probability bounds","Counter-based schemes require durable state"],"preconditions":["The application uses a nonce/IV-sensitive cryptographic construction"],"input":"Encryption operation and key/nonce lifecycle","output":"Nonce-safe cryptographic operation","expected_behavior":"The same key is never used with a prohibited repeated nonce.","failure_mode":"Confidentiality or authentication guarantees collapse because of nonce reuse.","root_cause":"Nonce generation is random without adequate bounds, reset on restart, reused across keys incorrectly, or manually managed.","solution":"Use vetted cryptographic APIs and enforce the algorithm-specific nonce requirements rather than constructing nonce logic ad hoc.","edge_cases":["Process restart","Multi-process encryption","Distributed workers","Counter persistence failure"],"security_implications":"Nonce misuse can expose plaintext relationships or permit forgery depending on the algorithm.","performance_implications":"Secure nonce generation is normally low cost; durable coordination may add overhead.","testing":{"method":"Instrument test encryption operations and deliberately attempt duplicate nonces under controlled conditions.","expected_result":"Duplicate prohibited nonces are prevented or detected.","pass_criteria":"Algorithm-specific nonce requirements remain true across restart and concurrency tests."},"verification":"Verify algorithm documentation, library behavior, nonce scope, and concurrent lifecycle handling.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Algorithm-dependent; verify against the selected cryptographic standard/library.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-38D","RFC 5116"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B130","D40-B456"]} {"record_id":"D40-B458","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Cryptographic Remediation","subtopic":"Password-derived encryption","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Use memory-hard password-based key derivation instead of direct password-to-key conversion","question":"How should password-derived encryption be remediated when an application hashes or truncates a password directly into an encryption key?","answer":"Use a password-based key derivation function with an appropriate salt and cost parameters, and keep password authentication and encryption-key derivation requirements distinct where possible.","objective":"Increase resistance to offline password guessing.","requirements":["Use a unique salt per password-derived key","Use an approved password-based KDF","Choose parameters appropriate to the deployment","Store KDF parameters with ciphertext when needed"],"constraints":["KDF cost must balance security and user experience","Legacy ciphertext may require migration"],"preconditions":["A password is used as cryptographic key material"],"input":"Password-derived encryption design","output":"Salted, cost-controlled derived key","expected_behavior":"Password guessing requires substantially more computation than direct hashing.","failure_mode":"Attackers can rapidly test password guesses against stolen ciphertext.","root_cause":"The application uses the password directly or applies a fast general-purpose hash without password-KDF parameters.","solution":"Use a standardized password-based KDF and unique salt; migrate legacy encrypted data through a controlled re-encryption process.","edge_cases":["Password changes","Forgotten passwords","Existing ciphertext migration","Low-resource devices"],"security_implications":"Improves resistance to offline guessing but does not make weak passwords safe.","performance_implications":"Intentional KDF cost increases CPU or memory use.","testing":{"method":"Inspect KDF parameters and benchmark derivation under representative device conditions.","expected_result":"Unique salts and policy-compliant cost parameters are used.","pass_criteria":"No direct password-to-key conversion remains."},"verification":"Verify KDF algorithm, salt uniqueness, parameter storage, migration logic, and failure handling.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Use current supported cryptographic libraries and algorithm guidance.","provenance":["RFC 8018","OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B126","D40-B456"]} {"record_id":"D40-B459","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Cryptographic Remediation","subtopic":"Randomness","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Replace predictable randomness in security-sensitive operations","question":"How should an application remediate predictable pseudo-random values used for tokens, keys, nonces, or security identifiers?","answer":"Use a cryptographically secure random-number generator provided by the operating system or a vetted cryptographic library for security-sensitive values. Never derive security tokens from timestamps, counters, ordinary pseudo-random generators, or predictable application state.","objective":"Prevent attackers from predicting security-sensitive values.","requirements":["Use CSPRNG-backed APIs","Keep security randomness separate from simulation randomness","Test API usage and error handling"],"constraints":["Randomness APIs differ by language and platform","Some protocols impose additional nonce or encoding requirements"],"preconditions":["The application generates security-sensitive random values"],"input":"Security-sensitive random-value generation","output":"Unpredictable security values","expected_behavior":"Generated values are not feasibly predictable from observable application state.","failure_mode":"Attackers guess authentication tokens, reset tokens, keys, or nonces.","root_cause":"A general-purpose pseudo-random generator or predictable seed is used for security purposes.","solution":"Replace it with the platform cryptographic randomness facility and remove predictable fallback paths.","edge_cases":["Early boot environments","Forked processes","Virtualized systems","Entropy API failure"],"security_implications":"Protects token and key unpredictability.","performance_implications":"Cryptographic randomness is normally inexpensive for ordinary application volumes.","testing":{"method":"Static analysis and API inspection to identify security-sensitive randomness calls.","expected_result":"All security-sensitive generation paths use approved CSPRNG APIs.","pass_criteria":"No predictable random source remains in security-sensitive code."},"verification":"Review source usage and test failure/fallback paths without claiming statistical tests prove cryptographic security.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Use current language/runtime cryptographic APIs.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-90A","OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B130","D40-B458"]} {"record_id":"D40-B460","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Cryptographic Remediation","subtopic":"Cryptographic migration","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"Migrate deprecated cryptographic algorithms without breaking protected data","question":"How should an application remediate deprecated cryptographic algorithms while preserving access to existing protected data?","answer":"Inventory algorithm usage, define a migration policy, introduce supported cryptography, re-encrypt or re-sign data through controlled migration paths, and remove deprecated algorithms after compatibility and recovery requirements are satisfied.","objective":"Eliminate obsolete cryptographic dependencies without uncontrolled data loss.","requirements":["Inventory algorithm and key usage","Define supported target algorithms","Plan backward-compatibility boundaries","Test migration and recovery","Remove obsolete paths"],"constraints":["Historical data may require temporary legacy support","Migration can be computationally expensive"],"preconditions":["Deprecated algorithms protect existing data or signatures"],"input":"Legacy cryptographic inventory","output":"Migrated cryptographic implementation and protected data","expected_behavior":"New operations use supported cryptography while legacy data follows an explicit migration path.","failure_mode":"Deprecated cryptography remains indefinitely or migration causes inaccessible protected data.","root_cause":"No lifecycle plan exists for cryptographic algorithm retirement.","solution":"Use versioned cryptographic envelopes or metadata, migrate data under controlled authorization, and retire legacy algorithms after verified coverage.","edge_cases":["Offline archives","Long-lived signatures","Disaster recovery","Partial migration"],"security_implications":"Reduces exposure to known cryptographic weaknesses.","performance_implications":"Bulk re-encryption can consume CPU, storage, and I/O.","testing":{"method":"Run migration against representative legacy datasets and perform rollback/recovery tests.","expected_result":"Migrated data remains accessible under the target security policy.","pass_criteria":"All required data has a verified migration state."},"verification":"Verify algorithm inventory, migration coverage, cryptographic metadata, recovery, and retirement controls.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Algorithm-specific; maintain explicit cryptographic version metadata.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","NIST Cryptographic Standards and Guidelines"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B456","D40-B457"]} {"record_id":"D40-B461","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Metadata service access","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Restrict workload access to cloud instance metadata services","question":"How should applications remediate unrestricted access to cloud instance metadata endpoints?","answer":"Restrict metadata access to workloads that require it, use the cloud provider's hardened metadata protocol and session controls where available, and prevent user-controlled network requests from reaching metadata endpoints.","objective":"Prevent server-side request paths from obtaining instance or workload credentials.","requirements":["Restrict metadata network access","Use hardened metadata settings","Separate application egress from metadata access","Test SSRF paths"],"constraints":["Cloud provider behavior differs","Some workloads legitimately require metadata access"],"preconditions":["The workload runs in an environment exposing metadata services"],"input":"Workload network and metadata configuration","output":"Controlled metadata access","expected_behavior":"Untrusted application input cannot retrieve metadata credentials.","failure_mode":"SSRF or compromised workload obtains cloud credentials through metadata access.","root_cause":"Metadata endpoint is reachable from untrusted request-processing paths without adequate restrictions.","solution":"Enable provider-recommended metadata protections, restrict network access, and prevent application-controlled destinations from reaching metadata services.","edge_cases":["Containers sharing host networking","IPv6 metadata endpoints","Redirect-based SSRF","Proxy environments"],"security_implications":"Reduces credential theft through SSRF and workload compromise.","performance_implications":"Network filtering adds negligible overhead for normal requests.","testing":{"method":"Attempt controlled SSRF requests against metadata addresses from application request paths.","expected_result":"Metadata credentials and sensitive metadata are inaccessible.","pass_criteria":"All tested untrusted paths are denied."},"verification":"Inspect cloud metadata settings, network controls, and application egress behavior.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Cloud-provider-specific; validate current provider metadata security controls.","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","Cloud provider official metadata security documentation"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B401","D40-B409"]} {"record_id":"D40-B462","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Network segmentation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Replace flat trusted networks with explicit workload segmentation","question":"How should an application environment remediate unrestricted lateral network connectivity?","answer":"Define trust boundaries between workloads and restrict communication to explicitly required flows using network policies, security groups, firewalls, or equivalent controls.","objective":"Limit lateral movement after compromise.","requirements":["Identify required communication flows","Deny unnecessary east-west traffic","Separate sensitive services","Monitor denied and unexpected flows"],"constraints":["Service discovery and dynamic scaling complicate static rules","Overly restrictive policies can break legitimate dependencies"],"preconditions":["Multiple workloads communicate across a network"],"input":"Service dependency graph and network policy","output":"Explicitly constrained network connectivity","expected_behavior":"A workload can communicate only with authorized destinations and ports.","failure_mode":"Compromised workloads freely access unrelated internal services.","root_cause":"Network placement is treated as sufficient authorization.","solution":"Model service dependencies and enforce least-privilege network policy at appropriate boundaries.","edge_cases":["Dynamic IPs","Service meshes","Emergency operational access","Multi-cluster communication"],"security_implications":"Reduces lateral movement and blast radius.","performance_implications":"Policy enforcement may add small network-processing overhead.","testing":{"method":"Test authorized and unauthorized service-to-service connections.","expected_result":"Required flows succeed and unauthorized flows are denied.","pass_criteria":"Observed connectivity matches the approved dependency model."},"verification":"Compare effective network policy with service dependency requirements and test deny paths.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure-platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture","CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B407","D40-B420"]} {"record_id":"D40-B463","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Management-plane exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Remove publicly reachable administrative interfaces","question":"How should an exposed administrative interface be remediated?","answer":"Remove public exposure where possible and place management interfaces behind authenticated, authorized, monitored management paths such as private networks, bastions, VPNs, identity-aware access controls, or equivalent mechanisms.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized access to privileged management functions.","requirements":["Identify management interfaces","Remove unnecessary internet exposure","Require strong authentication","Enforce authorization and audit access"],"constraints":["Remote administration may be operationally necessary","Availability requirements may require redundant management paths"],"preconditions":["An administrative service is reachable from an untrusted network"],"input":"Management endpoint exposure","output":"Restricted administrative access path","expected_behavior":"Only authorized operators can reach and use management functions.","failure_mode":"Attackers discover and attack administrative interfaces directly from untrusted networks.","root_cause":"Management-plane exposure was treated as ordinary application traffic.","solution":"Restrict network reachability, require strong identity controls, apply least privilege, and monitor administrative access.","edge_cases":["Emergency access","Break-glass accounts","Third-party support access"],"security_implications":"Reduces attack surface for privileged control planes.","performance_implications":"Additional access gateways can add latency but normally remain operationally small.","testing":{"method":"Scan from untrusted network locations and test authorized management access.","expected_result":"Administrative endpoints are inaccessible or strongly controlled from unauthorized networks.","pass_criteria":"No unintended public management path exists."},"verification":"Perform external exposure validation and inspect effective firewall/network policies.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure-dependent.","provenance":["CIS Controls","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B463","D40-B419"]} {"record_id":"D40-B464","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Time synchronization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Secure time synchronization for security-sensitive systems","question":"How should systems remediate unreliable or unauthenticated time synchronization when security decisions depend on timestamps?","answer":"Use trusted time sources, protect time-synchronization paths where required by the environment, monitor clock drift, and avoid treating an untrusted client timestamp as authoritative security evidence.","objective":"Prevent timestamp manipulation from breaking authentication, logging, certificate validation, replay protection, or audit correlation.","requirements":["Use controlled time sources","Monitor drift","Distinguish trusted server time from client-supplied timestamps","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Distributed systems can experience bounded clock skew","Some environments have intermittent time connectivity"],"preconditions":["Security controls or audit systems depend on time"],"input":"System time synchronization configuration","output":"Controlled and monitored system time","expected_behavior":"Security decisions use an appropriately trusted time source and tolerate documented skew.","failure_mode":"Clock manipulation causes replay acceptance, certificate failures, misleading audit ordering, or policy bypass.","root_cause":"System time is unmanaged or client-provided timestamps are trusted for security decisions.","solution":"Deploy controlled time synchronization and define explicit skew and failure handling.","edge_cases":["Offline systems","Virtual machines","Leap events","Disaster recovery"],"security_implications":"Improves reliability of temporal security controls and forensic timelines.","performance_implications":"Negligible normal overhead.","testing":{"method":"Simulate clock skew and synchronization loss.","expected_result":"Security controls fail or degrade according to documented policy.","pass_criteria":"No critical security decision depends solely on untrusted client time."},"verification":"Review time sources, drift monitoring, and timestamp trust boundaries.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Environment-dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","RFC 5905"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B461"]} {"record_id":"D40-B465","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Infrastructure Security","subtopic":"Secure service startup","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent insecure startup states from exposing services before security controls initialize","question":"How should services remediate startup sequences that expose network functionality before authentication, authorization, or security policy initialization?","answer":"Order initialization so security-critical controls are established before accepting untrusted traffic, or fail closed until they are ready. Health and readiness signals should distinguish process existence from security readiness.","objective":"Prevent transient startup windows from becoming exploitable security bypasses.","requirements":["Define security initialization dependencies","Fail closed before readiness","Separate liveness from readiness","Test restart and failure paths"],"constraints":["Some dependencies may be unavailable during startup","Availability requirements can pressure teams to start serving early"],"preconditions":["The service accepts traffic during initialization"],"input":"Service startup lifecycle","output":"Security-safe readiness behavior","expected_behavior":"Untrusted traffic cannot reach an incompletely initialized security boundary.","failure_mode":"Requests are accepted before authentication, policy, key loading, or configuration validation is complete.","root_cause":"Readiness is defined as process started rather than security controls ready.","solution":"Implement explicit initialization states and expose readiness only after security-critical components pass validation.","edge_cases":["Configuration service outage","Key-store outage","Rolling deployments","Fast restart"],"security_implications":"Eliminates transient security bypass windows.","performance_implications":"May delay readiness by the time required to initialize controls.","testing":{"method":"Inject startup dependency failures and send traffic during initialization.","expected_result":"Traffic is rejected or held until the security boundary is ready.","pass_criteria":"No protected endpoint is served in an insecure initialization state."},"verification":"Test cold start, restart, dependency failure, and readiness transitions.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C++"],"version_scope":"Service-runtime independent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B463"]} {"record_id":"D40-B466","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Authorization Remediation","subtopic":"Resource ownership","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Enforce server-side ownership checks for object references","question":"How should applications remediate resource-access decisions based only on identifiers supplied by clients?","answer":"Resolve the requested resource within the authenticated principal's authorized scope and enforce ownership or delegated permissions server-side. Treat identifiers as references, not authorization evidence.","objective":"Prevent object-level authorization bypass.","requirements":["Authenticate requester","Resolve resource within authorized scope","Check ownership or permission","Return consistent denial behavior"],"constraints":["Shared resources may have multiple principals","Authorization may depend on organization, role, relationship, or state"],"preconditions":["Clients submit resource identifiers"],"input":"Authenticated principal and requested resource identifier","output":"Authorized or denied resource operation","expected_behavior":"Changing an identifier cannot grant access to another principal's resource.","failure_mode":"A user accesses another user's object by modifying an ID.","root_cause":"Resource existence lookup occurs without a corresponding authorization constraint.","solution":"Bind resource retrieval and authorization to the caller's effective permissions and enforce the check server-side.","edge_cases":["Shared resources","Transferred ownership","Deleted resources","Cross-tenant administration"],"security_implications":"Prevents horizontal privilege escalation and cross-tenant access.","performance_implications":"Authorization-aware queries can often reduce unnecessary object retrieval.","testing":{"method":"Attempt access to known resources across principals and tenants.","expected_result":"Unauthorized resource access is denied.","pass_criteria":"No alternate identifier bypasses authorization."},"verification":"Review data-access queries and authorization policy and execute cross-principal negative tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Application-stack independent.","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B118","D40-B119","D40-B453"]} {"record_id":"D40-B467","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Authorization Remediation","subtopic":"State-transition authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Authorize sensitive state transitions independently of current object visibility","question":"How should applications remediate workflows where a user can view an object but can also trigger privileged state transitions?","answer":"Define authorization separately for each sensitive state transition and validate current state, actor permissions, business preconditions, and concurrency state before committing the transition.","objective":"Prevent privilege escalation through unauthorized workflow transitions.","requirements":["Define transition-specific permissions","Validate current state","Validate business preconditions","Apply authorization atomically with the transition"],"constraints":["Some transitions are role-dependent","Concurrent requests can race"],"preconditions":["The resource has security-sensitive lifecycle states"],"input":"Actor, current resource state, requested transition","output":"Authorized state transition or denial","expected_behavior":"Visibility of a resource does not imply authority to perform every transition.","failure_mode":"A low-privilege user invokes an endpoint or sequence intended for a higher-privilege state transition.","root_cause":"Authorization is checked only at object-read or endpoint level rather than transition level.","solution":"Model transitions explicitly and enforce permission plus state preconditions within the transaction.","edge_cases":["Concurrent transitions","Retry requests","Administrative override","Expired approvals"],"security_implications":"Prevents workflow-level authorization bypass.","performance_implications":"Atomic checks may require transactional locking or conditional updates.","testing":{"method":"Attempt every sensitive transition with principals of different roles and resource states.","expected_result":"Only policy-authorized transitions succeed.","pass_criteria":"Unauthorized transitions cannot be induced through alternate request sequences."},"verification":"Verify policy-to-transition mapping and concurrency behavior.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Application-stack independent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B119","D40-B120","D40-B467"]} {"record_id":"D40-B468","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Authorization Remediation","subtopic":"Policy consistency","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent authorization drift across equivalent API endpoints","question":"How should applications remediate inconsistent authorization rules across multiple endpoints exposing the same capability?","answer":"Centralize or systematically reuse authorization policy, define a single capability model, and test equivalent operations across all exposed interfaces.","objective":"Prevent weaker alternate endpoints from bypassing stronger authorization controls.","requirements":["Inventory equivalent operations","Define canonical authorization policy","Reuse policy enforcement","Test endpoint consistency"],"constraints":["Different interfaces may have legitimate policy differences","Legacy endpoints may require migration"],"preconditions":["Multiple endpoints or protocols expose related operations"],"input":"Endpoint-to-capability mapping","output":"Consistent authorization enforcement","expected_behavior":"Equivalent operations enforce equivalent authorization unless an explicit policy difference exists.","failure_mode":"A restricted operation is reachable through a less-protected endpoint.","root_cause":"Authorization logic was independently implemented across interfaces.","solution":"Map endpoints to canonical capabilities and enforce shared policy components with regression tests.","edge_cases":["GraphQL and REST equivalents","Admin APIs","Internal service APIs","Versioned APIs"],"security_implications":"Reduces authorization bypass through alternate interfaces.","performance_implications":"Central policy evaluation can add small overhead but reduces duplicated checks.","testing":{"method":"Build an authorization matrix across equivalent interfaces and roles.","expected_result":"Equivalent capabilities have consistent authorization outcomes.","pass_criteria":"No unexplained weaker endpoint exists."},"verification":"Compare policy decisions across API versions, protocols, and internal/external routes.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"API architecture independent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","OWASP API Security Top 10"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B131","D40-B466"]} {"record_id":"D40-B469","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Authorization Remediation","subtopic":"Default deny","knowledge_type":"RULE","concept":"Replace authorization default-allow behavior with explicit default deny","question":"How should a system remediate authorization paths that permit access when a policy decision is missing or unavailable?","answer":"Make protected operations deny by default when no explicit authorization decision exists. Treat policy evaluation errors, missing identities, unknown permissions, and unavailable authorization dependencies as denial unless a documented low-risk exception applies.","objective":"Prevent fail-open authorization behavior.","requirements":["Default to deny","Represent unknown policy state explicitly","Fail closed for protected actions","Monitor authorization errors"],"constraints":["Availability-sensitive operations may require carefully bounded fallback policies","Emergency access must be explicitly governed"],"preconditions":["The system makes policy decisions before sensitive actions"],"input":"Authorization request and policy state","output":"Allow or deny decision with explicit reason","expected_behavior":"Missing or failed authorization information cannot silently become permission.","failure_mode":"Policy outage or missing rule grants access.","root_cause":"Authorization code interprets absent or erroneous policy data as allow.","solution":"Implement explicit deny states and fail-closed handling for protected operations.","edge_cases":["Policy service outage","New roles without rules","Cache expiration","Break-glass access"],"security_implications":"Reduces fail-open authorization vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Deny-on-error can affect availability during policy outages but avoids unauthorized execution.","testing":{"method":"Inject policy-service failures, missing rules, and malformed decisions.","expected_result":"Protected operations are denied.","pass_criteria":"No policy uncertainty results in unauthorized allow."},"verification":"Inspect default branches and failure handling and execute policy-failure tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"General authorization principle.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B463","D40-B468"]} {"record_id":"D40-B470","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Authorization Remediation","subtopic":"Cache invalidation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent stale authorization decisions from surviving privilege changes","question":"How should applications remediate authorization caches that continue granting access after role or permission revocation?","answer":"Define authorization-cache lifetime and invalidation semantics based on risk, and ensure critical revocations propagate before subsequent sensitive actions. Do not allow stale cached permissions to outlive the documented security policy.","objective":"Prevent revoked access from remaining effective through stale authorization state.","requirements":["Define cache TTL","Invalidate on critical permission changes","Bind cache entries to identity and policy version where appropriate","Protect cache integrity"],"constraints":["Immediate global invalidation can be expensive","Distributed systems have propagation delay"],"preconditions":["Authorization decisions are cached"],"input":"Cached policy decision and current authorization state","output":"Current authorization decision","expected_behavior":"Revoked privileges cease according to the documented security bound.","failure_mode":"A revoked user continues accessing protected resources through stale cached permissions.","root_cause":"Authorization cache lacks revocation semantics or uses excessive lifetime.","solution":"Use risk-appropriate TTLs, event-driven invalidation, policy versioning, or revalidation for high-impact actions.","edge_cases":["Offline caches","Network partitions","Role changes during active sessions","Multi-region propagation"],"security_implications":"Limits authorization-revocation lag.","performance_implications":"More frequent revalidation increases authorization-service load.","testing":{"method":"Revoke permissions while cached decisions exist and test subsequent requests across instances.","expected_result":"Access stops within the documented revocation bound.","pass_criteria":"Observed revocation delay does not exceed policy."},"verification":"Test cache invalidation, failover, and multi-node propagation behavior.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Distributed application architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-207"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B116","D40-B119","D40-B469"]} {"record_id":"D40-B471","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Logging and Monitoring Security","subtopic":"Log injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent attacker-controlled input from corrupting security logs","question":"How should applications remediate log injection through attacker-controlled newline, delimiter, or structured-log fields?","answer":"Treat log fields as structured data, encode or sanitize control characters according to the logging format, and preserve event boundaries and field types. Never construct security logs by blindly concatenating untrusted input.","objective":"Preserve log integrity and prevent forged or misleading events.","requirements":["Use structured logging","Encode untrusted fields","Preserve event boundaries","Keep original evidence safely available where required"],"constraints":["Sanitization must not destroy forensic meaning","Different log formats have different escaping requirements"],"preconditions":["Untrusted input is recorded in logs"],"input":"Untrusted application value","output":"Integrity-preserving log event","expected_behavior":"One application event cannot be transformed into multiple forged log events by input content.","failure_mode":"Attackers inject fake log entries, fields, or control sequences.","root_cause":"Raw untrusted strings are concatenated into logs without format-aware encoding.","solution":"Use structured logging APIs and format-specific escaping; keep log records machine-parseable.","edge_cases":["Newline characters","JSON control characters","Terminal escape sequences","Delimiter-based SIEM ingestion"],"security_implications":"Improves forensic integrity and detection reliability.","performance_implications":"Structured encoding adds minor serialization overhead.","testing":{"method":"Inject newline, delimiter, JSON, and terminal-control characters into logged fields.","expected_result":"Each input remains a data field within one valid event.","pass_criteria":"No injected value creates an additional log event or control sequence."},"verification":"Parse produced logs with the intended collector and inspect event boundaries.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Logging-framework dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","CWE-117"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B141","D40-B143"]} {"record_id":"D40-B472","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Logging and Monitoring Security","subtopic":"Correlation integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Preserve trustworthy correlation identifiers across distributed security events","question":"How should distributed systems remediate missing or attacker-controlled correlation identifiers?","answer":"Generate or validate correlation identifiers at trusted boundaries, propagate them consistently across internal calls, and distinguish trusted trace context from user-supplied business identifiers.","objective":"Maintain reliable event correlation without allowing attackers to impersonate another transaction or trace.","requirements":["Generate trusted request identifiers","Validate inbound trace context","Separate security identity from correlation identity","Preserve identifiers across service boundaries"],"constraints":["Distributed tracing standards may permit externally supplied context","Correlation identifiers are not authentication credentials"],"preconditions":["Multiple components contribute events to one transaction"],"input":"Inbound request and trace context","output":"Trusted correlation context","expected_behavior":"Events can be correlated without allowing correlation metadata to grant identity or authorization.","failure_mode":"Attackers spoof identifiers, contaminate traces, or make investigations misleading.","root_cause":"Correlation metadata is trusted as identity or accepted without boundary validation.","solution":"Establish trusted trace context at ingress, propagate it using standard mechanisms, and keep authorization based on authenticated identity.","edge_cases":["Asynchronous queues","Retries","Cross-service fan-out","External callbacks"],"security_implications":"Improves forensic integrity while avoiding identity confusion.","performance_implications":"Trace propagation adds small metadata overhead.","testing":{"method":"Inject arbitrary trace identifiers and compare authorization and log-correlation behavior.","expected_result":"Correlation can change without changing authenticated identity or privileges.","pass_criteria":"No trace identifier influences authorization."},"verification":"Inspect ingress handling, propagation, and logging semantics across services.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Distributed tracing implementation dependent.","provenance":["W3C Trace Context","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B471"]} {"record_id":"D40-B473","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Logging and Monitoring Security","subtopic":"Alert integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent security alerts from being suppressed by attacker-controlled thresholds or fields","question":"How should systems remediate detection logic that trusts attacker-controlled event severity, suppression flags, or alert-routing metadata?","answer":"Derive security-relevant severity and routing decisions from trusted detection logic rather than client-supplied values. Treat suppression and acknowledgement as separately authorized operations with audit trails.","objective":"Prevent attackers from hiding malicious activity by manipulating detection metadata.","requirements":["Separate event data from detection decisions","Authorize alert suppression","Audit suppression changes","Protect detection configuration"],"constraints":["Some applications legitimately emit user-defined metadata","High-volume systems require controlled suppression"],"preconditions":["Security monitoring consumes application-generated metadata"],"input":"Application events and detection configuration","output":"Trusted detection and alerting decision","expected_behavior":"Untrusted event fields cannot directly disable or downgrade security detection.","failure_mode":"Attacker manipulates severity or suppression fields to prevent alert generation.","root_cause":"Monitoring pipeline treats producer-controlled metadata as authoritative security policy.","solution":"Derive detection state centrally, constrain producer metadata, and require authorization for suppression or configuration changes.","edge_cases":["Maintenance windows","Known benign high-volume events","Incident-response suppression"],"security_implications":"Protects monitoring availability and detection integrity.","performance_implications":"Central detection evaluation may increase processing load.","testing":{"method":"Inject misleading severity and suppression fields into representative events.","expected_result":"Detection policy remains controlled by trusted logic.","pass_criteria":"Untrusted metadata cannot suppress required alerts."},"verification":"Review alert-policy ownership and execute suppression-bypass tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Security-monitoring architecture dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-61"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B142","D40-B143","D40-B472"]} {"record_id":"D40-B474","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Logging and Monitoring Security","subtopic":"Clock and ordering evidence","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Preserve event ordering evidence without trusting client timestamps","question":"How should distributed systems remediate unreliable event timestamps in security investigations?","answer":"Record trusted ingestion or service timestamps in addition to client-provided timestamps, preserve clock-source metadata where necessary, and avoid using client timestamps as sole evidence of event order.","objective":"Improve forensic reliability when distributed clocks or client data are untrusted.","requirements":["Record trusted server-side time","Preserve source timestamps separately","Document clock uncertainty","Maintain event identifiers"],"constraints":["Network delay prevents perfect chronological reconstruction","Clock synchronization is imperfect"],"preconditions":["Security analysis depends on event sequence"],"input":"Distributed security events","output":"Timestamped evidence with trust distinctions","expected_behavior":"Investigators can distinguish claimed event time from trusted observation time.","failure_mode":"Attackers alter client clocks or timestamps to mislead incident reconstruction.","root_cause":"Client-provided timestamps are treated as authoritative chronology.","solution":"Capture trusted observation times and maintain explicit timestamp provenance.","edge_cases":["Offline clients","Queued events","Clock rollback","Cross-region processing"],"security_implications":"Improves incident reconstruction and evidence integrity.","performance_implications":"Additional timestamp metadata has negligible overhead.","testing":{"method":"Generate events with manipulated client timestamps and delayed delivery.","expected_result":"Trusted ingestion timestamps remain accurate and distinguishable.","pass_criteria":"Analysis does not rely solely on untrusted timestamps."},"verification":"Inspect event schema and replay delayed/out-of-order event scenarios.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Distributed systems.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","W3C Trace Context"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B464","D40-B472"]} {"record_id":"D40-B475","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Logging and Monitoring Security","subtopic":"Log integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Protect high-value security logs against unauthorized modification","question":"How should systems remediate security logs that can be modified by the same identity or host that generates them?","answer":"Separate log generation from privileged log storage where practical, restrict modification rights, retain append-oriented or immutable copies for high-value events, and monitor integrity failures.","objective":"Prevent attackers from erasing or altering evidence after compromise.","requirements":["Separate writer and administrator privileges","Restrict deletion and modification","Protect high-value audit records","Monitor integrity events"],"constraints":["Retention costs increase with stronger immutability","Operational log rotation still requires controlled lifecycle management"],"preconditions":["Logs contain security or compliance evidence"],"input":"Security-event logging architecture","output":"Tamper-resistant audit trail","expected_behavior":"Compromising an application identity does not automatically permit rewriting historical security evidence.","failure_mode":"Attacker modifies or deletes logs to conceal activity.","root_cause":"Log storage privileges are coupled to application runtime privileges.","solution":"Use least-privilege log shipping, separate storage authorization, immutable or append-only retention where appropriate, and independent monitoring.","edge_cases":["Incident response access","Retention expiration","Storage outage","Multi-region replication"],"security_implications":"Improves forensic integrity and accountability.","performance_implications":"Remote or immutable storage can add network and storage overhead.","testing":{"method":"Attempt unauthorized modification and deletion using application-level identities.","expected_result":"Historical audit records remain protected.","pass_criteria":"Application identities cannot alter protected historical evidence."},"verification":"Review storage permissions, retention policy, and integrity-monitoring behavior.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure and logging-platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B143","D40-B471"]} {"record_id":"D40-B476","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Remediation","subtopic":"Dependency provenance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Require trusted provenance for externally consumed software dependencies","question":"How should a software project remediate dependencies whose origin cannot be established?","answer":"Establish approved package sources, verify package identity and integrity, retain dependency metadata, and use provenance or attestation mechanisms where supported by the ecosystem.","objective":"Reduce the risk of consuming tampered or unauthorized software.","requirements":["Define trusted sources","Record dependency coordinates and versions","Verify integrity","Review provenance where available"],"constraints":["Ecosystems differ in provenance support","Some transitive dependencies may lack rich attestations"],"preconditions":["The build consumes external dependencies"],"input":"Dependency graph and package metadata","output":"Traceable dependency set","expected_behavior":"Each accepted dependency has an identifiable source and integrity record.","failure_mode":"A compromised or unauthorized package enters the build unnoticed.","root_cause":"Dependency identity and provenance are not controlled.","solution":"Use trusted registries, lockfiles, integrity verification, provenance attestations where available, and review policies.","edge_cases":["Private registries","Vendored source","Transitive dependencies","Mirror failures"],"security_implications":"Reduces software-supply-chain compromise risk.","performance_implications":"Metadata verification adds build-time work but can be cached.","testing":{"method":"Introduce an unapproved or provenance-deficient dependency into a controlled build.","expected_result":"Policy blocks or flags the dependency.","pass_criteria":"Build policy enforces defined provenance requirements."},"verification":"Inspect dependency manifests, lockfiles, registry configuration, and CI enforcement.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Ecosystem and package-manager dependent.","provenance":["SLSA","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B146","D40-B148"]} {"record_id":"D40-B477","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Remediation","subtopic":"Build isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Isolate software builds from untrusted build-time code","question":"How should organizations remediate builds that execute dependency or project scripts with excessive host privileges?","answer":"Run builds in isolated, least-privileged environments with controlled network access, minimal credentials, and reproducible inputs. Treat build-time scripts and dependencies as untrusted code unless independently trusted.","objective":"Limit compromise of developer machines, CI workers, signing keys, and production artifacts.","requirements":["Use isolated build environments","Minimize build credentials","Restrict network access","Separate signing from ordinary compilation","Record build inputs"],"constraints":["Some builds require network access or native tooling","Isolation must support legitimate toolchains"],"preconditions":["Build steps execute third-party or repository-controlled code"],"input":"Build pipeline and dependency execution model","output":"Constrained build environment","expected_behavior":"Compromised build-time code cannot freely access unrelated secrets or infrastructure.","failure_mode":"Malicious build code steals credentials or modifies artifacts.","root_cause":"Build execution occurs with broad host and network privileges.","solution":"Use isolated builders, short-lived credentials, restricted egress, and separate artifact-signing boundaries.","edge_cases":["Package install hooks","Native compiler toolchains","Container builds","Self-hosted runners"],"security_implications":"Reduces build-pipeline compromise blast radius.","performance_implications":"Isolation can increase startup and caching complexity.","testing":{"method":"Execute controlled untrusted build steps and inspect reachable resources.","expected_result":"Only explicitly permitted resources are accessible.","pass_criteria":"Build environment cannot access prohibited credentials or systems."},"verification":"Review runner permissions, network policy, credentials, and artifact-signing boundaries.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"CI/CD-platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","SLSA"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B148","D40-B149"]} {"record_id":"D40-B478","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Remediation","subtopic":"Dependency confusion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent public package resolution from overriding private dependency names","question":"How should projects remediate dependency confusion risk when private package names overlap with public registries?","answer":"Configure package managers to use explicit trusted registries and namespace rules, reserve internal names where ecosystem mechanisms support it, and prevent unintended fallback from private repositories to public registries.","objective":"Prevent attackers from publishing higher-priority public packages that are mistakenly consumed by private builds.","requirements":["Define registry precedence","Use private namespaces where supported","Pin dependency sources","Test resolver behavior"],"constraints":["Package-manager resolution differs","Legacy projects may contain ambiguous package names"],"preconditions":["Private dependencies are consumed by automated builds"],"input":"Package-manager registry configuration","output":"Deterministic trusted package resolution","expected_behavior":"Private dependency identifiers resolve only to authorized package sources.","failure_mode":"A malicious public package is selected instead of the intended private package.","root_cause":"Registry precedence or namespace ownership is ambiguous.","solution":"Configure explicit package sources and namespace controls and validate resolution in clean environments.","edge_cases":["Developer-local configuration","Transitive dependencies","Multiple registries","Mirror outages"],"security_implications":"Reduces dependency-confusion attacks.","performance_implications":"Private registry configuration may change cache and resolution behavior.","testing":{"method":"Use a controlled test package with an overlapping public/private name and inspect resolver selection.","expected_result":"The approved private source is selected or the build fails closed.","pass_criteria":"No unintended public fallback occurs."},"verification":"Test clean CI environments rather than relying on developer machine configuration.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Package-manager dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Dependency-Check guidance","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B146","D40-B476"]} {"record_id":"D40-B479","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Remediation","subtopic":"Artifact signing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Verify software artifact integrity before deployment","question":"How should organizations remediate deployment pipelines that trust artifacts solely because they came from a build location?","answer":"Associate artifacts with authenticated provenance or signatures, verify them before deployment, and bind verification to the intended artifact identity and release policy.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized or tampered artifacts from reaching deployment environments.","requirements":["Define artifact identity","Generate trusted signatures or attestations","Verify before deployment","Protect signing authority"],"constraints":["Signing alone does not prove source correctness","Key rotation and emergency revocation require lifecycle planning"],"preconditions":["Artifacts move from build to deployment"],"input":"Build artifact and release policy","output":"Verified deployable artifact","expected_behavior":"Deployment rejects artifacts that fail integrity or provenance requirements.","failure_mode":"A modified or unauthorized artifact is deployed because location or filename is trusted.","root_cause":"Artifact authenticity is inferred from storage path or pipeline position.","solution":"Use cryptographic signing/attestation and enforce verification at deployment boundaries.","edge_cases":["Rollback artifacts","Emergency releases","Multiple architectures","Key rotation"],"security_implications":"Strengthens software-release integrity.","performance_implications":"Signature verification adds minimal deployment-time overhead.","testing":{"method":"Modify or substitute a test artifact after signing and attempt deployment.","expected_result":"Deployment rejects the artifact.","pass_criteria":"Only policy-compliant verified artifacts are deployable."},"verification":"Test signature verification, identity binding, key rotation, and failure behavior.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"CI/CD and artifact-registry dependent.","provenance":["SLSA","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B148","D40-B477"]} {"record_id":"D40-B480","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Dependency and Supply Chain Remediation","subtopic":"SBOM validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Treat SBOM data as verifiable supply-chain evidence rather than an unvalidated inventory","question":"How should organizations remediate unreliable software bill of materials data?","answer":"Generate SBOMs from trusted build inputs, associate them with exact artifacts, validate required fields and component identities, and preserve provenance showing how the SBOM was produced.","objective":"Make component inventory useful for vulnerability response and supply-chain analysis.","requirements":["Bind SBOM to artifact identity","Validate component identifiers","Record generation provenance","Update SBOM with releases"],"constraints":["Build systems may omit dynamically obtained components","Different SBOM formats and ecosystems have different metadata quality"],"preconditions":["An organization uses SBOMs for security decisions"],"input":"Build artifact and dependency metadata","output":"Artifact-bound validated SBOM","expected_behavior":"SBOM accurately represents the analyzed artifact to the documented coverage level.","failure_mode":"Security teams make decisions using incomplete, stale, or mismatched component inventories.","root_cause":"SBOM is generated or consumed without provenance and artifact binding.","solution":"Generate SBOMs from controlled builds, validate schema/content, bind them to immutable artifact identifiers, and record limitations.","edge_cases":["Generated code","Native libraries","Vendored dependencies","Runtime-loaded modules"],"security_implications":"Improves vulnerability impact analysis but does not itself prove artifact safety.","performance_implications":"SBOM generation and validation add build-time processing.","testing":{"method":"Compare SBOM components against controlled dependency and artifact inventories.","expected_result":"Required components are represented and artifact identity matches.","pass_criteria":"SBOM passes schema, identity, provenance, and coverage checks."},"verification":"Validate SBOM against the exact release artifact and record unresolved inventory gaps.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"SBOM format and build-system dependent.","provenance":["NTIA SBOM Minimum Elements","CycloneDX","SPDX"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B147","D40-B476","D40-B479"]} {"record_id":"D40-B481","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Resilience and Recovery Security","subtopic":"Backup immutability","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Protect critical backups from ransomware-style modification and deletion","question":"How should organizations remediate backups that can be altered or deleted using the same credentials as production data?","answer":"Separate backup administration from production administration, apply least privilege, use immutable or retention-protected copies where appropriate, and test restoration independently.","objective":"Preserve recoverability after destructive compromise.","requirements":["Separate backup identities","Protect retention controls","Maintain isolated or immutable copies","Test restoration"],"constraints":["Immutability does not eliminate the need for correct backup content","Retention requirements affect storage cost"],"preconditions":["Backups are relied upon for recovery"],"input":"Backup architecture and access model","output":"Protected recoverable backup set","expected_behavior":"Compromise of production credentials cannot silently destroy every recovery copy.","failure_mode":"An attacker deletes or encrypts backups together with production data.","root_cause":"Backup controls share privileges and trust boundaries with production.","solution":"Separate administration, enforce immutable retention where appropriate, and maintain independently tested recovery copies.","edge_cases":["Emergency deletion","Retention expiry","Cross-region recovery","Backup credential compromise"],"security_implications":"Improves ransomware and destructive-attack resilience.","performance_implications":"Immutable replication increases storage and transfer requirements.","testing":{"method":"Simulate unauthorized backup deletion and perform restoration from protected copies.","expected_result":"Protected backups remain available and restoration succeeds according to documented objectives.","pass_criteria":"Recovery objectives are met using independently protected backups."},"verification":"Review backup permissions, immutability controls, retention, and restoration test evidence.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Backup-platform dependent.","provenance":["CISA ransomware guidance","NIST SP 800-34"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B120","D40-B455"]} {"record_id":"D40-B482","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Resilience and Recovery Security","subtopic":"Recovery credential separation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Separate recovery credentials from ordinary operational credentials","question":"How should recovery systems remediate the use of normal production credentials for disaster recovery administration?","answer":"Use dedicated recovery identities with tightly controlled access, strong authentication, independent storage, and audited break-glass procedures. Recovery credentials should not depend exclusively on the compromised production identity system.","objective":"Preserve recovery capability during identity or production compromise.","requirements":["Dedicated recovery identities","Independent credential protection","Strong authentication","Audited emergency access","Periodic recovery testing"],"constraints":["Independent credentials increase operational responsibility","Break-glass access must be tightly governed"],"preconditions":["Production compromise could disable normal administrative access"],"input":"Recovery access architecture","output":"Independent recovery authorization path","expected_behavior":"Authorized recovery remains possible without relying solely on compromised production controls.","failure_mode":"An incident disables both production systems and the credentials needed to recover them.","root_cause":"Recovery access is coupled to the same trust boundary being recovered.","solution":"Establish protected recovery identities and test them under controlled conditions.","edge_cases":["Identity-provider outage","Lost recovery credentials","Multi-region disaster","Insider misuse"],"security_implications":"Improves resilience against total identity-plane compromise.","performance_implications":"Operational maintenance is required for recovery credentials.","testing":{"method":"Conduct controlled recovery exercises with normal administrative paths unavailable.","expected_result":"Authorized recovery access remains possible and auditable.","pass_criteria":"Recovery procedure works without bypassing security governance."},"verification":"Review recovery identity storage, authentication, authorization, and exercise evidence.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Organization and infrastructure dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-34"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B481","D40-B463"]} {"record_id":"D40-B483","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Resilience and Recovery Security","subtopic":"Recovery-point validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Verify that backups satisfy security and recovery requirements rather than merely existing","question":"How should organizations remediate backup programs that report successful backups without verifying recoverability?","answer":"Perform controlled restoration tests, validate data integrity and application consistency, measure recovery time and recovery point results, and record failures as remediation work.","objective":"Distinguish backup creation from verified recovery capability.","requirements":["Restore representative data","Validate application consistency","Measure RTO/RPO","Record restoration evidence","Test critical dependencies"],"constraints":["Full restoration can be expensive","Representative sampling must reflect business-critical systems"],"preconditions":["Backups are considered part of the recovery strategy"],"input":"Backup set and recovery requirements","output":"Evidence-backed recovery status","expected_behavior":"A backup is considered reliable only after successful restoration validation within defined requirements.","failure_mode":"Backups exist but cannot restore required systems during an incident.","root_cause":"Backup success status is mistaken for recovery verification.","solution":"Schedule restoration exercises and validate technical and business recovery criteria.","edge_cases":["Encrypted backups","Dependent services","Schema migrations","Partial regional failure"],"security_implications":"Improves resilience against data loss and destructive attacks.","performance_implications":"Restoration tests consume infrastructure and operational resources.","testing":{"method":"Restore representative systems into an isolated environment and execute validation tests.","expected_result":"Data and dependent services recover within documented objectives.","pass_criteria":"Recovery evidence satisfies defined RTO/RPO and integrity requirements."},"verification":"Review restoration artifacts, integrity checks, timing measurements, and unresolved failures.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Recovery-platform dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B481","D40-B482"]} {"record_id":"D40-B484","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Resilience and Recovery Security","subtopic":"Configuration recovery","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Back up security-critical configuration as controlled recoverable state","question":"How should systems remediate recovery plans that restore data but omit security-critical configuration?","answer":"Treat security configuration, identity mappings, network policies, cryptographic metadata, application configuration, and infrastructure definitions as recoverable dependencies where appropriate. Store them with integrity and version controls.","objective":"Prevent incomplete recovery that restores data but leaves the system insecure or unusable.","requirements":["Identify security-critical configuration","Version configuration","Protect configuration backups","Test complete recovery"],"constraints":["Secrets require separate protection","Some configuration is environment-specific"],"preconditions":["System recovery depends on configuration beyond primary data"],"input":"Application and infrastructure configuration inventory","output":"Recoverable configuration set","expected_behavior":"Recovery restores required security controls before protected services become available.","failure_mode":"Recovered application operates with default, missing, or insecure configuration.","root_cause":"Recovery planning covers data but not configuration dependencies.","solution":"Version and protect required configuration and include it in restoration tests.","edge_cases":["Environment-specific endpoints","Rotated keys","Third-party configuration","Secrets management outages"],"security_implications":"Prevents insecure post-recovery states.","performance_implications":"Configuration versioning has low runtime cost.","testing":{"method":"Perform clean-environment recovery using only documented recovery artifacts.","expected_result":"Security controls initialize correctly before service readiness.","pass_criteria":"Recovered system meets security configuration baseline."},"verification":"Compare recovered configuration against approved security baseline.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure/application dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B465","D40-B483"]} {"record_id":"D40-B485","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Resilience and Recovery Security","subtopic":"Failover security parity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Maintain security-control parity between primary and failover environments","question":"How should organizations remediate failover environments that have weaker security controls than production?","answer":"Apply the same security baseline, identity controls, network restrictions, logging, secrets handling, and authorization requirements to failover environments unless an explicitly documented and risk-reviewed difference exists.","objective":"Prevent attackers from targeting weaker disaster-recovery infrastructure.","requirements":["Compare security baselines","Synchronize critical policies","Test failover controls","Monitor standby environments"],"constraints":["Standby environments may be scaled down","Some controls behave differently during degraded operation"],"preconditions":["A secondary environment can become operational during failure"],"input":"Primary and failover security configurations","output":"Security-equivalent failover posture","expected_behavior":"Failover does not silently remove critical security controls.","failure_mode":"Attackers compromise a weaker standby environment and pivot during or before failover.","root_cause":"Failover architecture is treated as operational infrastructure rather than production-equivalent security boundary.","solution":"Continuously compare security configuration and include failover in security testing.","edge_cases":["Cold standby","Disconnected recovery sites","Emergency scaling","Cross-region identity"],"security_implications":"Reduces alternate-path compromise risk.","performance_implications":"Continuous configuration comparison adds low operational overhead.","testing":{"method":"Execute failover and compare effective controls with production baseline.","expected_result":"Critical security controls remain enforced.","pass_criteria":"No undocumented high-risk security regression appears after failover."},"verification":"Perform configuration-drift comparison before and after failover exercises.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Resilience architecture dependent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B482","D40-B484"]} {"record_id":"D40-B486","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Secure defaults","knowledge_type":"RULE","concept":"Replace insecure application defaults with secure-by-default configuration","question":"How should software remediate security controls that are disabled or permissive by default?","answer":"Make security-preserving behavior the default for new deployments and require explicit, documented configuration to weaken controls. Validate configuration at startup and reject unsupported insecure combinations.","objective":"Reduce security failures caused by omitted configuration.","requirements":["Define secure defaults","Validate configuration","Reject invalid insecure combinations","Document intentional exceptions"],"constraints":["Legacy compatibility may require transitional settings","Defaults must not prevent legitimate deployment requirements"],"preconditions":["Security behavior is configurable"],"input":"Application configuration","output":"Validated secure configuration","expected_behavior":"A minimal deployment does not silently disable critical security controls.","failure_mode":"Operators deploy software without realizing security controls are disabled by default.","root_cause":"Security depends on optional configuration that is not validated or enforced.","solution":"Set secure defaults, validate startup configuration, and require explicit exception settings for weaker modes.","edge_cases":["Development mode","Migration from legacy versions","Environment variables overriding files"],"security_implications":"Reduces configuration-induced vulnerabilities.","performance_implications":"Startup validation has negligible runtime cost.","testing":{"method":"Start application with empty or minimal configuration and inspect effective security settings.","expected_result":"Critical protections remain enabled.","pass_criteria":"No insecure security-critical default is silently selected."},"verification":"Test default, malformed, and explicitly weakened configurations.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Application configuration dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","CIS Controls"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B145","D40-B469"]} {"record_id":"D40-B487","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Configuration precedence","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent insecure configuration overrides through ambiguous precedence","question":"How should applications remediate configuration systems where environment variables, files, command-line arguments, and remote configuration can unexpectedly override security settings?","answer":"Define and document configuration precedence, restrict which sources can modify security-critical settings, validate the final effective configuration, and record its source and version.","objective":"Prevent unauthorized or accidental weakening of security controls through configuration precedence.","requirements":["Define precedence","Restrict privileged configuration sources","Validate effective configuration","Audit security-critical overrides"],"constraints":["Deployment platforms commonly combine several configuration sources","Some operational overrides are necessary"],"preconditions":["Multiple configuration sources can affect security behavior"],"input":"All configuration sources","output":"Validated effective configuration","expected_behavior":"Only authorized configuration sources can weaken protected settings.","failure_mode":"A low-trust configuration source overrides a secure setting.","root_cause":"Configuration precedence is implicit or security-unaware.","solution":"Establish explicit precedence and source trust levels and validate the merged configuration before service readiness.","edge_cases":["Container environment variables","Command-line secrets","Remote configuration outages","Hot reload"],"security_implications":"Prevents configuration-based security bypass.","performance_implications":"Configuration validation occurs primarily at startup or controlled reload.","testing":{"method":"Set conflicting values across configuration sources and inspect the effective result.","expected_result":"The authorized source wins according to policy.","pass_criteria":"Unauthorized sources cannot override protected settings."},"verification":"Test every supported configuration source and precedence combination.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Runtime/deployment dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B486","D40-B145"]} {"record_id":"D40-B488","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Configuration drift","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Detect and remediate unauthorized security configuration drift","question":"How should systems remediate production security controls that diverge from their approved baseline?","answer":"Define a versioned security baseline, continuously or periodically compare effective configuration against it, identify authorized exceptions, and remediate unauthorized drift.","objective":"Prevent long-lived security degradation caused by manual or automated configuration changes.","requirements":["Version security baseline","Measure effective configuration","Track exceptions","Alert on unauthorized drift","Provide controlled remediation"],"constraints":["Dynamic systems legitimately change configuration","Some settings are ephemeral"],"preconditions":["Production configuration is governed by a baseline"],"input":"Effective configuration and approved baseline","output":"Drift assessment and remediation state","expected_behavior":"Unauthorized deviations are detected and either blocked or remediated within defined bounds.","failure_mode":"A temporary insecure change becomes permanent and unnoticed.","root_cause":"Configuration changes lack continuous comparison and ownership.","solution":"Implement configuration drift detection with explicit exception management and remediation workflows.","edge_cases":["Autoscaling","Emergency changes","Feature flags","Ephemeral workloads"],"security_implications":"Reduces persistence of insecure configuration.","performance_implications":"Continuous comparison consumes monitoring resources.","testing":{"method":"Introduce controlled configuration drift and measure detection/remediation.","expected_result":"Drift is detected and handled according to policy.","pass_criteria":"Unauthorized security drift is detected within the defined window."},"verification":"Compare effective runtime state, desired configuration, and approved exceptions.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Infrastructure/configuration-management dependent.","provenance":["CIS Controls","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B485","D40-B487"]} {"record_id":"D40-B489","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Feature flags","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent feature flags from bypassing security controls","question":"How should applications remediate feature flags that can disable authentication, authorization, validation, or security monitoring?","answer":"Classify security-sensitive flags separately, restrict who can modify them, require auditability and controlled rollout, and prevent ordinary feature configuration from bypassing mandatory security invariants.","objective":"Prevent operational feature management from becoming an authorization or security-control bypass.","requirements":["Identify security-sensitive flags","Restrict flag administration","Audit changes","Enforce non-bypassable security invariants"],"constraints":["Feature flags may change frequently","Emergency security changes require rapid but controlled operation"],"preconditions":["Runtime feature flags alter security-relevant behavior"],"input":"Feature flag configuration","output":"Controlled security-sensitive flag state","expected_behavior":"Feature toggles cannot disable mandatory protections without authorized governance.","failure_mode":"An attacker or low-privilege operator changes a flag to bypass security controls.","root_cause":"Feature-management permissions are broader than security-policy permissions.","solution":"Separate security flags from ordinary product flags and enforce invariant controls outside mutable feature configuration.","edge_cases":["Kill switches","Canary releases","Remote flag service compromise","Emergency disablement"],"security_implications":"Prevents feature-control abuse from weakening core security boundaries.","performance_implications":"Flag evaluation overhead is normally minimal.","testing":{"method":"Attempt unauthorized modification of security-sensitive flags and test security invariants with flags in every state.","expected_result":"Mandatory protections remain enforced.","pass_criteria":"No ordinary feature flag can bypass a mandatory security invariant."},"verification":"Review flag ownership, permissions, audit trails, and invariant enforcement.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Feature-flag platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST SP 800-53"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B486","D40-B487"]} {"record_id":"D40-B490","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Configuration Security","subtopic":"Configuration secret separation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent security secrets from being mixed with ordinary configuration distribution","question":"How should applications remediate systems that distribute credentials together with ordinary configuration files?","answer":"Separate secrets from non-secret configuration, use an appropriate secrets-management mechanism, restrict secret retrieval by workload identity, and prevent secrets from entering source control or broad configuration distribution channels.","objective":"Reduce accidental and unauthorized credential exposure.","requirements":["Classify configuration values","Separate secret delivery","Restrict retrieval","Rotate secrets","Prevent source-control exposure"],"constraints":["Applications may require secrets during startup","Secrets-management outages require explicit failure behavior"],"preconditions":["Configuration contains authentication or cryptographic secrets"],"input":"Application configuration and secret values","output":"Separated configuration and protected secret delivery","expected_behavior":"Only authorized workloads can retrieve required secrets.","failure_mode":"Secrets are copied into repositories, images, logs, or broadly readable configuration stores.","root_cause":"No distinction exists between ordinary configuration and secret material.","solution":"Use dedicated secret-management controls and least-privilege workload identities.","edge_cases":["Local development","Build-time secrets","Container image layers","Emergency rotation"],"security_implications":"Reduces credential leakage and unauthorized access.","performance_implications":"Secret retrieval may add startup latency and network dependency.","testing":{"method":"Inspect source, artifacts, configuration stores, and runtime access permissions.","expected_result":"Secrets are absent from unauthorized locations and accessible only to approved identities.","pass_criteria":"Secret exposure paths are eliminated or explicitly controlled."},"verification":"Perform repository, artifact, runtime, and access-control checks.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Deployment-platform dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-57"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B456","D40-B487"]} {"record_id":"D40-B491","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Secure Protocol and Parser Remediation","subtopic":"Protocol downgrade","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent negotiation downgrade to weaker security protocols","question":"How should systems remediate protocol negotiation that allows attackers to force weaker security versions or features?","answer":"Disable obsolete protocol versions and weak negotiation options, define an approved minimum security level, and test downgrade attempts across clients and servers.","objective":"Prevent attackers from reducing a secure connection to an exploitable protocol mode.","requirements":["Define minimum protocol version","Disable obsolete protocols","Validate negotiation outcomes","Test legacy-client behavior"],"constraints":["Legacy interoperability may require migration","Protocol support differs by implementation"],"preconditions":["A protocol negotiates versions or security capabilities"],"input":"Protocol configuration and negotiation request","output":"Policy-compliant negotiated connection","expected_behavior":"Negotiation cannot select a prohibited security level.","failure_mode":"An attacker or incompatible client forces a weaker protocol mode.","root_cause":"The server accepts legacy or weak protocol negotiation without policy enforcement.","solution":"Configure an explicit minimum protocol/security level and remove insecure fallback paths.","edge_cases":["Legacy clients","Middleboxes","Mixed-version clusters","Emergency compatibility"],"security_implications":"Prevents downgrade-based attacks.","performance_implications":"Removing legacy protocols can reduce compatibility but normally does not harm secure protocol performance.","testing":{"method":"Attempt connections using prohibited protocol versions or downgrade negotiation patterns.","expected_result":"Connections are rejected.","pass_criteria":"No prohibited protocol mode can be negotiated."},"verification":"Inspect effective protocol configuration and run negative interoperability tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Protocol implementation dependent.","provenance":["RFC 7525","RFC 8996"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B401","D40-B402"]} {"record_id":"D40-B492","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Secure Protocol and Parser Remediation","subtopic":"Parser differential behavior","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Eliminate security-sensitive parser differentials between components","question":"How should systems remediate cases where proxies, gateways, and applications parse the same request differently?","answer":"Use consistent protocol parsing, reject ambiguous or malformed representations at the earliest trusted boundary, and ensure intermediaries and applications agree on message framing and normalization.","objective":"Prevent request desynchronization and security-control bypass caused by parser disagreement.","requirements":["Use consistent parsing rules","Reject ambiguous messages","Align intermediary and backend framing","Test malformed inputs"],"constraints":["Different protocol components may have historical parsing behavior","Strict parsing can expose compatibility problems"],"preconditions":["Requests traverse multiple parsers or intermediaries"],"input":"Ambiguous or malformed protocol message","output":"Single unambiguous interpretation or rejection","expected_behavior":"All security-relevant components interpret a request consistently.","failure_mode":"One component validates a request while another interprets it differently.","root_cause":"Parser implementations disagree on framing, normalization, duplicate fields, or encoding.","solution":"Standardize parsing behavior, reject ambiguous syntax, and test intermediary-to-backend differentials.","edge_cases":["Duplicate headers","Conflicting length fields","Whitespace variations","Encoded delimiters"],"security_implications":"Reduces request-smuggling and security-boundary bypass risk.","performance_implications":"Strict parsing can reduce parser complexity and malformed-request processing.","testing":{"method":"Run protocol differential tests through each intermediary/backend combination.","expected_result":"Ambiguous inputs are rejected consistently.","pass_criteria":"No security-relevant parser disagreement remains."},"verification":"Compare effective parser behavior and execute malformed-message test suites.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"HTTP/protocol implementation dependent.","provenance":["OWASP HTTP Request Smuggling guidance","RFC 9112"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B143","D40-B491"]} {"record_id":"D40-B493","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Secure Protocol and Parser Remediation","subtopic":"Unicode normalization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent authorization and validation bypass through inconsistent Unicode normalization","question":"How should applications remediate security decisions that compare Unicode strings under inconsistent normalization rules?","answer":"Define canonicalization rules for security-sensitive identifiers, normalize consistently before validation and comparison where appropriate, and avoid relying on visually identical strings as equivalent unless the policy explicitly defines equivalence.","objective":"Prevent identity, path, or authorization confusion caused by multiple Unicode representations.","requirements":["Define canonical representation","Normalize consistently","Validate after canonicalization","Avoid visual-only equality assumptions"],"constraints":["Normalization may alter legitimate user data","Protocol and filesystem normalization rules differ"],"preconditions":["Unicode strings influence security-sensitive identifiers or comparisons"],"input":"Unicode identifier or path","output":"Canonical validated representation","expected_behavior":"Equivalent security identifiers are handled consistently and ambiguous representations are rejected when necessary.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts one representation while authorization or storage uses another.","root_cause":"Different components normalize or compare Unicode strings differently.","solution":"Define and enforce canonicalization at trust boundaries and keep security identifiers constrained where practical.","edge_cases":["Homoglyphs","Combining characters","Filesystem-specific normalization","Internationalized domain names"],"security_implications":"Reduces identity and path confusion attacks.","performance_implications":"Normalization adds modest string-processing overhead.","testing":{"method":"Test canonically equivalent and visually similar Unicode inputs through every validation and authorization layer.","expected_result":"Security decisions remain consistent.","pass_criteria":"No alternate Unicode representation bypasses validation or authorization."},"verification":"Compare normalization rules across application, database, filesystem, and protocol boundaries.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript"],"version_scope":"Unicode/runtime dependent.","provenance":["Unicode Standard","OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B122","D40-B126","D40-B493"]} {"record_id":"D40-B494","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Secure Protocol and Parser Remediation","subtopic":"Parser resource limits","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Apply bounded parsing to prevent resource-exhaustion attacks","question":"How should systems remediate parsers that accept unbounded message size, nesting, recursion, or decompression?","answer":"Define explicit limits for message size, nesting depth, element count, recursion, decompression expansion, and processing time. Reject inputs that exceed limits before excessive resource consumption occurs.","objective":"Prevent parser-driven denial of service.","requirements":["Set size limits","Set structural depth limits","Bound decompression","Bound parsing time","Fail safely"],"constraints":["Legitimate documents may be large","Limits must match application requirements"],"preconditions":["The application parses externally controlled structured data"],"input":"Potentially oversized or deeply nested input","output":"Accepted bounded document or controlled rejection","expected_behavior":"Resource consumption remains within defined limits for hostile inputs.","failure_mode":"An attacker submits pathological input that consumes excessive CPU or memory.","root_cause":"Parser accepts unbounded structural or expansion complexity.","solution":"Configure parser limits, enforce upstream request limits, and use streaming approaches when appropriate.","edge_cases":["Compressed payloads","Recursive structures","Large arrays","Entity expansion"],"security_implications":"Reduces parser-based denial-of-service risk.","performance_implications":"Limits reduce worst-case resource consumption and can improve resilience.","testing":{"method":"Generate oversized, deeply nested, and expansion-heavy test inputs.","expected_result":"Inputs are rejected within bounded resource limits.","pass_criteria":"No test causes uncontrolled CPU or memory consumption."},"verification":"Measure resource use and rejection timing under adversarial inputs.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Parser/library dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","CWE-400","OWASP XML Security guidance"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B125","D40-B494"]} {"record_id":"D40-B495","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Secure Protocol and Parser Remediation","subtopic":"Content-type confusion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","concept":"Prevent content-type confusion between validation and processing layers","question":"How should applications remediate cases where one component validates a payload as one content type while another parses it as another?","answer":"Determine the expected media type at a trusted boundary, validate the actual representation, and ensure downstream components use the same canonical interpretation. Reject mismatched or ambiguous content types where security depends on parsing behavior.","objective":"Prevent validation bypass and content interpretation attacks.","requirements":["Define accepted media types","Validate actual payload representation","Keep parser selection consistent","Reject ambiguous mismatches"],"constraints":["Browsers and legacy clients may apply content sniffing","Some APIs intentionally support multiple representations"],"preconditions":["Multiple components select parsers based on content metadata"],"input":"Request content type and body","output":"Single validated parser selection","expected_behavior":"Validation and execution operate on the same representation.","failure_mode":"A gateway validates one format while backend interprets another.","root_cause":"Content-type metadata is trusted without validating downstream parser behavior.","solution":"Use explicit media-type allowlists, disable unsafe sniffing where applicable, and bind validation to the parser actually used.","edge_cases":["Multipart boundaries","Malformed headers","Legacy clients","Compressed content"],"security_implications":"Reduces content-interpretation and validation-bypass risk.","performance_implications":"Content inspection adds modest request-processing overhead.","testing":{"method":"Send mismatched, malformed, and ambiguous content-type combinations through all processing layers.","expected_result":"Ambiguous payloads are rejected or consistently interpreted.","pass_criteria":"No parser mismatch bypasses validation."},"verification":"Trace content-type handling from ingress through final parser and execute negative tests.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"HTTP/application parser dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","RFC 9110"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B122","D40-B494"]} {"record_id":"D40-B496","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Remediation Governance","subtopic":"Security invariant enforcement","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Encode mandatory security invariants as continuously testable controls","question":"How should organizations remediate security requirements that exist only in documentation and are not enforced by engineering controls?","answer":"Translate mandatory requirements into machine-checkable policies, tests, static checks, configuration rules, or deployment gates where feasible, and preserve evidence of enforcement.","objective":"Move critical security requirements from advisory documentation into repeatable engineering controls.","requirements":["Identify mandatory invariants","Define machine-checkable conditions","Integrate checks into development or deployment","Record failures and exceptions","Continuously regress controls"],"constraints":["Not every security property is fully automatable","Automated checks must not be mistaken for complete security assurance"],"preconditions":["Critical security requirements have been identified"],"input":"Security requirements and implementation controls","output":"Enforceable security control set","expected_behavior":"Known mandatory security properties are automatically checked where technically feasible.","failure_mode":"A documented requirement silently regresses because no automated enforcement exists.","root_cause":"Security requirements are disconnected from implementation and verification pipelines.","solution":"Map requirements to deterministic controls and tests, retain traceability, and require explicit review for unautomatable properties.","edge_cases":["Exception workflows","Legacy systems","Dynamic policy","Human approval requirements"],"security_implications":"Improves repeatability and reduces regression risk.","performance_implications":"CI/CD checks increase validation time but can be optimized incrementally.","testing":{"method":"Intentionally violate each encoded invariant in a controlled branch.","expected_result":"The relevant gate detects the violation.","pass_criteria":"Mandatory invariant violations fail or are explicitly escalated."},"verification":"Trace each invariant from requirement to control, test, and evidence.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Engineering-process independent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B149","D40-B488","D40-B489"]} {"record_id":"D40-B497","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Remediation Governance","subtopic":"Exception management","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"Control security exceptions with explicit scope, expiration, ownership, and compensating controls","question":"How should organizations remediate permanent undocumented security exceptions?","answer":"Record each exception with affected assets, reason, owner, risk, compensating controls, approval authority, expiration date, and review status. Temporary exceptions should expire automatically where technically feasible.","objective":"Prevent temporary security weaknesses from becoming invisible permanent conditions.","requirements":["Unique exception identity","Explicit scope","Named owner","Expiration or review date","Compensating controls","Audit trail"],"constraints":["Some legacy constraints may persist","Exceptions do not eliminate underlying risk"],"preconditions":["A documented requirement cannot currently be met"],"input":"Security control exception request","output":"Governed exception record","expected_behavior":"Every exception is visible, bounded, owned, and periodically reviewed.","failure_mode":"A known security weakness persists indefinitely without reassessment.","root_cause":"Exception handling lacks lifecycle governance.","solution":"Implement an exception registry with approval, expiry, review, and remediation tracking.","edge_cases":["Emergency exceptions","Third-party dependencies","Expired owners","Compensating-control failure"],"security_implications":"Improves visibility and limits unmanaged security debt.","performance_implications":"Governance overhead is primarily administrative.","testing":{"method":"Create, expire, renew, and revoke controlled exception records.","expected_result":"Expired exceptions cannot silently remain active.","pass_criteria":"Lifecycle state and enforcement match policy."},"verification":"Review exception records against effective security configuration and deployment controls.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust"],"version_scope":"Governance-process independent.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B488","D40-B496"]} {"record_id":"D40-B498","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Remediation Governance","subtopic":"Security regression prevention","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Require security regression coverage for previously remediated vulnerabilities","question":"How should teams prevent previously fixed security vulnerabilities from returning?","answer":"Convert confirmed vulnerabilities into durable regression tests or equivalent automated controls, retain the original failure condition, and execute those controls in relevant validation pipelines.","objective":"Prevent recurrence of known security defects.","requirements":["Create regression artifact","Preserve vulnerable condition","Test corrected behavior","Run during relevant CI/CD stages","Track failures"],"constraints":["Some vulnerabilities require integration or deployment-level tests","Tests must avoid reproducing dangerous behavior against production systems"],"preconditions":["A vulnerability has been remediated and its failure condition is understood"],"input":"Vulnerability evidence and remediation","output":"Reusable security regression control","expected_behavior":"A future reintroduction of the vulnerable condition is detected before release where coverage permits.","failure_mode":"A previously fixed vulnerability returns unnoticed.","root_cause":"The fix was implemented without preserving a durable regression check.","solution":"Encode the vulnerability as a safe negative test, static rule, configuration check, or integration test appropriate to the defect.","edge_cases":["Environment-specific vulnerabilities","Dependency vulnerabilities","Infrastructure configuration regressions","False-positive regression tests"],"security_implications":"Reduces repeat vulnerability recurrence.","performance_implications":"Regression suites add CI cost; prioritize based on risk and execution layer.","testing":{"method":"Reintroduce the vulnerable condition in an isolated test and run the regression control.","expected_result":"The regression control fails on the vulnerable implementation and passes on the corrected implementation.","pass_criteria":"Regression test distinguishes vulnerable and corrected states."},"verification":"Review test evidence and ensure the control executes in the intended release pipeline.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"Engineering-pipeline dependent.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B496"]} {"record_id":"D40-B499","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Remediation Governance","subtopic":"Remediation verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","concept":"Verify remediation against the original security failure and plausible alternate paths","question":"How should teams verify that a security remediation actually removes the vulnerability rather than only changing the observed symptom?","answer":"Reproduce the original failure in an isolated test, verify the corrected behavior, test relevant alternate input and execution paths, and confirm that the fix does not merely block one known payload while leaving the underlying security condition intact.","objective":"Distinguish genuine remediation from superficial patching.","requirements":["Preserve original failure evidence","Test corrected behavior","Test boundary and alternate paths","Check authorization and security invariants","Record verification evidence"],"constraints":["Complete exploit-space coverage is rarely possible","Verification scope should match the vulnerability's root cause and attack surface"],"preconditions":["A security defect and remediation are identified"],"input":"Original vulnerability, root cause, corrected implementation","output":"Evidence-backed remediation status","expected_behavior":"The original security condition is eliminated or demonstrably constrained according to the remediation requirement.","failure_mode":"A known exploit is blocked while equivalent alternate paths remain vulnerable.","root_cause":"Verification tests the symptom or one payload rather than the underlying security invariant.","solution":"Derive verification from the root cause and security property, then exercise alternate representations, paths, identities, states, and failure modes relevant to that property.","edge_cases":["Multiple endpoints","Alternate encodings","Different user roles","Concurrent requests","Cached state"],"security_implications":"Improves confidence that remediation addresses the actual vulnerability.","performance_implications":"Broader security verification increases test time but reduces escaped defects.","testing":{"method":"Execute original negative test, corrected positive/negative tests, alternate-path tests, and relevant boundary cases in an isolated environment.","expected_result":"Original failure is prevented and equivalent bypass paths are also denied.","pass_criteria":"All required security invariants hold across tested paths."},"verification":"Record root cause, test inputs, expected behavior, observed result, environment, and verification status without claiming unexecuted tests passed.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"General security-remediation principle.","provenance":["OWASP Application Security Verification Standard","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B496","D40-B498"]} {"record_id":"D40-B500","dataset_id":"D40","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Remediation Governance","subtopic":"Remediation closure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","concept":"Close a security remediation only after implementation, regression, verification, and residual-risk review","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before a security remediation is considered complete?","answer":"Confirm the root cause is addressed, implementation changes are reviewed, applicable security regression tests exist, required validation has actually executed, affected deployment/configuration paths are covered, provenance and evidence are recorded, and any residual risk or exception is explicitly documented.","objective":"Prevent premature closure of security vulnerabilities.","requirements":["Root-cause remediation","Code/configuration review","Regression coverage","Executed validation where required","Deployment verification","Residual-risk assessment","Evidence retention"],"constraints":["Some verification requires production-like or staged environments","Not every control can be fully automated"],"preconditions":["A security finding has an assigned remediation"],"input":"Security finding, remediation change, tests, deployment evidence","output":"Verified remediation lifecycle state","expected_behavior":"A remediation is closed only when defined evidence supports closure; otherwise it remains open, partially verified, or explicitly excepted.","failure_mode":"A vulnerability is marked fixed based only on code change without validating deployed behavior or alternate paths.","root_cause":"Closure criteria do not distinguish implementation from verification and residual risk.","solution":"Use a structured remediation state model such as identified → fixed → tested → verified → deployed → monitored, with evidence required at each transition.","edge_cases":["Emergency patches","Third-party fixes","Compensating controls","Unverified production deployment"],"security_implications":"Improves remediation integrity and reduces false closure.","performance_implications":"Formal closure evidence adds process and test cost but prevents repeated investigation and escaped vulnerabilities.","testing":{"method":"Run a complete remediation lifecycle using a controlled security defect and verify that each closure gate requires its corresponding evidence.","expected_result":"Incomplete or unverified remediation cannot reach the final closed state.","pass_criteria":"Closure state is consistent with actual implementation, execution, verification, deployment, and residual-risk evidence."},"verification":"Audit the complete evidence chain from original finding through root-cause fix, regression validation, deployment, and final security verification.","languages":["Python","Java","Go","Rust","JavaScript","TypeScript","C","C++"],"version_scope":"General security-remediation lifecycle.","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","OWASP Application Security Verification Standard"],"truth_status":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","related_records":["D40-B150","D40-B496","D40-B497","D40-B498","D40-B499"]}

